// Generated by tools/build_fin201.py — do not edit by hand. Ids are prefixed with the course id.
export const CONTENT = {"course":"fin201","chapters":[{"chapter":8,"title":"Stocks: equity valuation","topics":[{"id":"fin201-t08-1","order":1,"title":"Returns on a stock","summary":"A share pays you in two ways: dividends and a change in its price. The holding period return rolls both into one percentage for the period. When returns vary from year to year, the arithmetic average tells you the return in a typical year, but only the geometric average tells you what your money actually compounded at.","concepts":["fin201-c08-hpr","fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","fin201-c08-compound-value"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-2","order":2,"title":"Valuation vocabulary","summary":"Before valuing a share you need the language: book, market and liquidation value; the numbers on a quote screen; how debt and equity differ; how shares are issued and traded; and what preferred stock is. The exam asks for these definitions and for reading a quote screen (sample question 9).","concepts":["fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","fin201-c08-quote-screen","fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","fin201-c08-markets-trading","fin201-c08-preferred-stock"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-3","order":3,"title":"Comparables","summary":"The fastest valuation prices a share as a multiple (P/E, price/sales, EV/EBITDA) borrowed from similar firms. It works because firms with similar growth, plowback and risk should have similar multiples. The exam tests the method (sample question 1) and when it breaks down.","concepts":["fin201-c08-pe-comparables","fin201-c08-ev-multiples","fin201-c08-why-comparables"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-4","order":4,"title":"Dividend discount model","summary":"A share is worth the present value of the dividends it will pay. A constant dividend is a perpetuity, a dividend growing at g forever is a growing perpetuity (Gordon growth), and growth for a finite number of years is a growing annuity. Rearranging the Gordon price splits the required return into a dividend yield and a capital gain rate.","concepts":["fin201-c08-constant-dividend","fin201-c08-gordon-growth","fin201-c08-required-return-components","fin201-c08-growing-annuity"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-5","order":5,"title":"Non-constant growth","summary":"Many firms pay nothing at first or grow quickly before settling down. Draw a timeline, find the horizon price one period before the first constant-growth dividend, and discount everything to today. Backwards problems ask for the growth rate or dividend that makes the price match.","concepts":["fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","fin201-c08-two-stage","fin201-c08-solve-backwards"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-6","order":6,"title":"Where growth comes from","summary":"Growth is not free: a firm grows by plowing earnings back and earning ROE on them, so g = b × ROE. The same inputs give the forward P/E, the value of growth opportunities (NPVGO) and the growth rate implied by trailing versus forward multiples.","concepts":["fin201-c08-plowback-growth","fin201-c08-forward-pe","fin201-c08-npvgo","fin201-c08-implied-growth"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-7","order":7,"title":"Free cash flow valuation","summary":"When dividends are a poor guide, value the cash the business generates. FCF (after-tax operating cash before financing) discounted at the firm's rate gives enterprise value; FCFE (cash left for shareholders) discounted gives equity directly. Working capital changes and net borrowing are the usual traps, and you must know when each model fits.","concepts":["fin201-c08-fcf-ev","fin201-c08-fcfe","fin201-c08-working-capital","fin201-c08-which-model"]},{"id":"fin201-t08-8","order":8,"title":"Market efficiency","summary":"In an efficient market prices already reflect the relevant information. The three forms say which information: past prices (weak), public information (semi-strong), all information including private (strong). We want weak and semi-strong efficiency but not strong. Inefficiencies such as momentum are how an investor might profit, and noise and behaviour explain why prices deviate.","concepts":["fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","fin201-c08-noise-behaviour"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"fin201-c08-hpr","topic":"fin201-t08-1","term":"Holding period return","definition":"The total return on a share over one period: r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1 = (P1 − P0)/P0 + D1/P0, the capital gain yield plus the dividend yield.","explain":"Treat the share as an investment of P0 that returns P1 + D1 at the end of the period. The return is the gain per dollar invested. The period is whatever time the prices and dividend cover; it need not be a year. A realised return can be negative even in a year when a dividend is paid.","example":"Buy at P0 = $40.00, receive D1 = $1.20 and sell at P1 = $43.00: capital gain yield = 3.00/40.00 = 7.50%, dividend yield = 1.20/40.00 = 3.00%, so r_E = (43.00 + 1.20)/40.00 − 1 = 10.50%.","contrast":"r_E is a realised (or forecast) return built from prices and dividends; R is the required return investors demand, used to discount. Both the dividend yield and the capital gain yield divide by today's price P0, never by P1.","trap":"Dividing by P1; leaving out the dividend; forgetting the −1 (1.105 is a growth factor, not a return); treating a six-month return as an annual rate; thinking a dividend always makes the return positive.","mnemonic":"Everything you get back, over what you paid, minus 1.","formula":"r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1 = (P1 − P0)/P0 + D1/P0","given":true,"archetypes":["A1"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","topic":"fin201-t08-1","term":"Arithmetic versus geometric average return","definition":"The arithmetic average r_A = (R1 + … + R_N)/N is the return in an average year; the geometric average R_G = [(1 + R1)…(1 + R_N)]^(1/N) − 1 is the average compound return per year. r_A ≥ R_G, with equality only if every yearly return is the same.","explain":"The arithmetic average is the simple mean and suits a forecast of a typical year. The geometric average reflects compounding, so it is 'the truth' about how fast wealth grew. The more the returns vary, the further R_G falls below r_A (roughly R_G ≈ r_A − σ²/2).","example":"The lecture example: returns of 8%, −3% and 5%. r_A = (8 − 3 + 5)/3 = 3.3333%. R_G = (1.08 × 0.97 × 1.05)^(1/3) − 1 = 1.09998^(1/3) − 1 = 3.2274% per annum.","contrast":"Arithmetic answers 'what was the return in an average year'; geometric answers 'what was the average compound return per year'. Arithmetic is for forecasting a typical year, geometric for growth already earned.","trap":"Averaging the returns when the question asks for the compound return; multiplying the factors but skipping the Nth root; forgetting the −1; entering percentages instead of decimals; using N − 1 as the root.","mnemonic":"Arithmetic = average of the numbers; geometric = average of the growth factors.","formula":"r_A = (R1 + … + R_N)/N;  R_G = [(1 + R1)(1 + R2)…(1 + R_N)]^(1/N) − 1","given":true,"archetypes":["A1"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-compound-value","topic":"fin201-t08-1","term":"Compound value and implied compound return","definition":"A holding of PV that earns R1, …, R_N is worth PV(1 + R1)…(1 + R_N) = PV(1 + R_G)^N after N years. Given the start and end values, the compound return is R_G = (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1.","explain":"Because each year's return is earned on the previous year's ending value, the growth factors multiply. This works forwards (find the ending value) and backwards (find the starting investment or the yearly rate). The order of the returns does not matter unless money is added or withdrawn.","example":"The lecture example: $1,000 in a stock returning 8%, −3% and 5% is worth 1,000 × 1.08 × 0.97 × 1.05 = $1,099.98. The compound return is (1,099.98/1,000)^(1/3) − 1 = 3.2274% per annum.","contrast":"Compounding the arithmetic average overstates the ending value whenever returns vary; adding the returns ignores compounding altogether; (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1 is the geometric average.","trap":"Turning a −9% return into a factor of 1.09 instead of 0.91; dividing the total gain by N; using N − 1 years; forgetting to take the Nth root; rounding the factors early.","mnemonic":"Multiply the factors, then take the root.","formula":"FV = PV(1 + R1)…(1 + R_N) = PV(1 + R_G)^N;  R_G = (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1","given":true,"archetypes":["A1"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","topic":"fin201-t08-2","term":"Book, market and liquidation value","definition":"Book value of equity is assets minus liabilities at historical cost on the balance sheet. Market value of equity (market capitalisation) is the share price times the shares outstanding. Liquidation value is the amount left for the owners if the assets are sold and the creditors are paid first.","explain":"Book value looks backwards at accounting costs, market value looks forwards at expected cash flows, and liquidation value is the fire-sale floor. Price-to-book compares the first two: a firm with growth opportunities and valuable intangibles can trade well above book (P/B above 1), while a P/B below 1 is a warning that the market expects poor returns on book equity or that book values are overstated. The review guide asks for typical P/E and P/B values: read them off a quote screen, such as the sample screen's P/E of 18.798 and P/B of 1.722.","example":"Sigma Tools: assets $90M, liabilities $55M, so book equity is $35M. With 4 million shares at $22.00 the market value is $88M, P/B = 88/35 = 2.51. If a sale would fetch $62M for the assets, liquidation value of equity = 62 − 55 = $7M.","contrast":"Book value is not liquidation value, and liquidation value is not 'the price to buy a bankrupt firm'. Sample question 2 asks for the definition: the amount from selling the assets and paying off the creditors.","trap":"Choosing 'equal to the book value of equity' or 'the price to buy a firm after bankruptcy' for liquidation value; inverting P/B; forgetting that creditors are paid before shareholders.","mnemonic":"Book = past costs, Market = future cash flows, Liquidation = fire sale.","formula":"Market cap = P × shares;  P/B = market cap ÷ book equity;  liquidation value = asset sale proceeds − liabilities","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Three values of a firm","head":["Value","Where it comes from","Question it answers"],"rows":[["Book","Balance sheet, historical cost","What do the accounts say equity is worth?"],["Market","Price × shares outstanding","What do investors pay for the firm as a going concern?"],["Liquidation","Asset sale proceeds minus amounts owed to creditors","What is left for owners if the firm is shut down?"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","topic":"fin201-t08-2","term":"Reading a quote screen","definition":"A quote screen shows the last price and change, bid and ask, day and year ranges, volume, dividend yield, forward dividend yield, market cap, price/sales, trailing P/E, consensus forward P/E and P/B. Missing figures follow: shares = market cap ÷ P; EPS = P ÷ (P/E); net income = EPS × shares; DPS = yield × P; expected growth = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E − 1.","explain":"Every ratio on the screen shares the same price, so ratios can be turned back into per-share quantities. The exam gives a screen and asks for derived numbers (sample question 9). Trailing means last twelve months, forward means analysts' forecast for the coming year.","example":"Sample screen: last $60.34, market cap $6,397,100, P/E 18.798, forward P/E 17.943. Shares = 6,397,100/60.34 = 106,018 (dividing by the ask, $60.35, gives exactly 106,000, so the screen's market cap seems to use that price; both answers are accepted). Expected growth = 18.798/17.943 − 1 = 4.77% per annum.","contrast":"Last price (most recent trade) versus bid (what buyers offer) versus ask (what sellers want); trailing versus forward P/E; dividend yield (D0/P) versus forward dividend yield (D1/P).","trap":"Using the bid or ask in place of the last price; dividing forward by trailing P/E (sign reverses); reading market cap in thousands of dollars; mixing trailing and forward figures; forgetting the −1 on the ratio.","mnemonic":"Same price, different earnings.","formula":"Shares = market cap ÷ P;  EPS = P ÷ (P/E);  NI = EPS × shares;  growth = (trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E) − 1","given":false,"archetypes":["A6"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","topic":"fin201-t08-2","term":"Debt versus equity","definition":"Debt is a contractual borrowing repaid with interest; equity is ownership. They differ in risk level, voting rights, predictability of income (interest versus dividends) and tax treatment (interest is deductible to the firm, dividends are not).","explain":"Lenders have a prior claim, a promised payment and no vote; shareholders have the residual claim, a discretionary dividend and a vote on directors who hire and fire management. Because equity is riskier, shareholders require a higher expected return than lenders. The review guide asks for at least four differences.","example":"A firm pays $3 million of interest and $3 million of dividends. Only the interest lowers its taxable income. If it skips the dividend, nothing happens in law; if it skips the interest, it is in default.","contrast":"Debt versus equity versus preferred stock (equity for tax and law, but with a fixed dividend and priority over common). Debt has a maturity; common shares do not.","trap":"Calling dividends tax-deductible; thinking a missed dividend is a default; saying debt holders vote; saying debt is riskier than equity for the investor.","mnemonic":"Risk, Vote, Predictable, Tax: RVPT.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Four differences between debt and equity","head":["","Debt","Equity"],"rows":[["Risk to the investor","Lower: contractual claim, paid first","Higher: residual claim, paid last"],["Voting rights","None (while paid)","Vote to elect directors"],["Predictability of income","Interest is promised","Dividends are discretionary"],["Taxation (firm)","Interest is tax-deductible","Dividends come from after-tax profit"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","topic":"fin201-t08-2","term":"Markets and trading","definition":"In the primary market a firm sells new securities (an initial public offering or a later share issue) and receives the money; in the secondary market investors trade existing securities and the firm receives nothing. Trading uses exchanges and platforms (NYSE, NASDAQ, TSX, ECNs), brokers who arrange trades and dealers who hold inventory and quote a bid and an ask.","explain":"A dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask; the spread is its profit, and an investor buys at the ask and sells at the bid. A market order trades at once at the best price available; a limit order trades only at its stated price or better. A liquid secondary market makes shares more attractive in the primary market.","example":"Bid $60.31, ask $60.35: buying 1,000 shares at the ask and selling at the bid loses (60.35 − 60.31) × 1,000 = $40.00 of spread.","contrast":"Broker (agent, earns a commission, no inventory) versus dealer (principal, earns the spread, holds inventory); primary versus secondary; NYSE (one designated market maker per stock) versus NASDAQ (competing dealers); market versus limit order.","trap":"Mixing up bid and ask; thinking the company receives money when shares trade between investors; thinking a limit order is certain to be filled; treating the TSX as an American exchange.","mnemonic":"Dealers Buy at the Bid, Sell at the Ask (B-B, S-A).","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","topic":"fin201-t08-2","term":"Preferred stock","definition":"Preferred stock is equity with a fixed stated dividend and priority over common stock for dividends and in liquidation, usually without a vote. Because the dividend is fixed (g = 0), its price is a perpetuity: P0 = D/R.","explain":"Preferred looks like a bond (fixed payment, credit ratings, priority) but is equity in law and tax: the dividend is not deductible and skipping it is not a default. Cumulative preferred dividends that are skipped build up as arrears that must be paid before common dividends.","example":"A preferred share pays $5.25 a year and investors require 6.2% per annum: P0 = 5.25/0.062 = $84.68. If the price falls to $70.00 with the same dividend, R = 5.25/70.00 = 7.50%.","contrast":"Preferred versus common (fixed versus growing dividend, priority versus residual) and preferred versus a bond (no default on a skipped dividend, no tax deduction).","trap":"Using the Gordon growth formula with g > 0; valuing a deferred first dividend at the wrong date (the perpetuity value sits one period before the first payment); thinking preferred dividends are tax-deductible.","mnemonic":"Preferred = bond-like payment, equity-like law.","formula":"P0 = D/R","given":true,"archetypes":[],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","topic":"fin201-t08-3","term":"Valuation by P/E comparables","definition":"Estimate a share's price as a benchmark multiple times a matching per-share figure: P = benchmark P/E × EPS (trailing P/E with trailing EPS, forward P/E with forward EPS), or P = benchmark P/S × sales per share for loss-making firms.","explain":"The benchmark comes from similar firms, an industry average or the firm's own history. This is relative valuation: it says what the price should be if the firm trades like its peers. A target price in t years uses EPS_t = EPS0(1 + g)^t. Sample question 1 (a broker pricing a firm off its earnings and similar firms' P/E) is comparables.","example":"EPS last year $3.10, expected growth 7%, benchmark P/E 16. Price today on trailing EPS = 16 × 3.10 = $49.60. Target price in 3 years = 16 × 3.10 × 1.07^3 = 16 × 3.7976 = $60.76.","contrast":"Comparables (relative value, multiples) versus DCF/DDM (intrinsic value from the firm's own cash flows); trailing versus forward P/E; P/E versus price/sales (used when earnings are negative).","trap":"Applying a trailing multiple to forecast EPS (that is a target price one year ahead, not today's price); averaging price over EPS wrongly (average the yearly P/Es); using total sales instead of sales per share; treating a P/E of 19 as 19%.","mnemonic":"Price = multiple × metric, same basis on both sides.","formula":"P = benchmark P/E × EPS;  P = benchmark P/S × sales per share;  EPS_t = EPS0(1 + g)^t","given":false,"archetypes":["A7"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","topic":"fin201-t08-3","term":"Enterprise value multiples","definition":"Enterprise value (EV) = market value of equity + debt − cash, the value of the operating business to all investors. An EV multiple such as EV/EBITDA gives EV = multiple × EBITDA, and equity is then Equity = EV − Debt + Cash.","explain":"EBITDA is measured before interest, so EV/EBITDA is not distorted by how a firm is financed and can compare firms with different debt. You must walk from EV to equity before dividing by shares.","example":"Peers trade at 8.5 × EBITDA. A firm has EBITDA $24.0M, debt $58.0M, cash $9.5M and 10.0M shares: EV = 204.0; Equity = 204.0 − 58.0 + 9.5 = $155.5M; value per share = $15.55.","contrast":"Equity multiple (P/E: equity value directly) versus EV multiple (EV/EBITDA: whole business, needs the debt and cash bridge).","trap":"Dividing EV by the shares; subtracting cash instead of adding it; applying an EV/EBITDA multiple to net income; forgetting debt.","mnemonic":"EV in, then −Debt +Cash, then ÷ shares.","formula":"EV = multiple × EBITDA;  Equity = EV − Debt + Cash","given":true,"archetypes":["A7"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","topic":"fin201-t08-3","term":"Why comparables work","definition":"A constant-growth firm's forward P/E is P0/EPS1 = (1 − b)/(R − g), so firms with similar growth g, ROE (hence plowback b) and risk (hence required return R) should have similar P/E ratios.","explain":"Comparables are not magic: they work to the extent that the peer firms really share the drivers of the multiple. A firm with higher growth, lower risk or a higher payout deserves a higher P/E, and a small error in the benchmark causes a large error in price.","example":"b = 40%, g = 5%, R = 11%: forward P/E = 0.60/0.06 = 10.0. If peers with the same b, g and R trade at about 10 times forward earnings, a P/E of 10 for your firm is 'normal'.","contrast":"Relative valuation (multiples of peers) versus intrinsic valuation (DDM/FCF); growth stock P/E versus income stock P/E.","trap":"Comparing a high-growth firm with mature firms; using the plowback b instead of the payout 1 − b in the numerator; ignoring that the benchmark is hard to choose.","mnemonic":"Same g, same ROE, same risk, same P/E.","formula":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g)","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","topic":"fin201-t08-4","term":"Constant dividend (perpetuity)","definition":"A share that pays the same dividend D every year forever is an ordinary perpetuity: P0 = D/R. The price stays at D/R at every date.","explain":"No growth means no capital gain, so the entire return R is the dividend yield D/P0. This is the model for preferred stock and for a firm with a fixed dividend policy. A perpetuity formula values the stream one period before the first payment.","example":"D = $2.75 each year and R = 8.5%: P0 = 2.75/0.085 = $32.35, and the price will still be $32.35 in five years. Separately, a $2.00 dividend first paid in 4 years with R = 8%: P3 = 2.00/0.08 = $25.00 and P0 = 25.00/1.08^3 = $19.85.","contrast":"Constant dividend (g = 0, D/R) versus Gordon growth (g ≠ 0, D1/(R − g)).","trap":"Growing the price at R; discounting a perpetuity value that is already at the right date; using R in percent; forgetting to discount when the first dividend is several years away.","mnemonic":"No growth, no gain: yield = R.","formula":"P0 = D/R;  R = D/P0","given":true,"archetypes":["A2"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","topic":"fin201-t08-4","term":"Gordon growth model","definition":"If dividends grow at a constant rate g forever, D_{n+1} = (1 + g)D_n, a share is worth P0 = D1/(R − g) with D1 = D0(1 + g) when D0 was just paid; the price at time t is P_t = D_{t+1}/(R − g) = P0(1 + g)^t. It requires g < R.","explain":"The formula values a growing perpetuity one period before its first payment. Use D1 in the numerator, never D0. The price grows at g because every future dividend does. It is the workhorse of the stock questions (the recommended problems 1, 4 and 19).","example":"D0 = $1.85 just paid, g = 3.5%, R = 10.5%: D1 = 1.85 × 1.035 = 1.9147; P0 = 1.9147/(0.105 − 0.035) = $27.35. In 4 years: P4 = 27.35 × 1.035^4 = $31.39.","contrast":"D0 versus D1 ('just paid' versus 'next year'); P_t uses D_{t+1}; constant dividend (g = 0) is a special case; negative g is allowed.","trap":"Using D0 instead of D1 (or growing D1 again); R plus g in the denominator; growing the dividend twice; computing P_t with D_t; g as a whole number; a negative price from g > R means the model does not apply.","mnemonic":"Next dividend over the gap: D1 over R minus g.","formula":"P0 = D1/(R − g);  D1 = D0(1 + g);  P_t = P0(1 + g)^t","given":true,"archetypes":["A2"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","topic":"fin201-t08-4","term":"Components of the required return","definition":"Rearranging the Gordon model gives R = D1/P0 + g: the dividend yield plus the capital gain rate. In the constant-growth model the capital gain rate equals g, because the price grows with the dividend.","explain":"You can read the market's required return off a price, or solve for g, D1, D0 or P0 when the other pieces are known. The split matters: growth stocks earn R mostly as capital gains, income stocks mostly as dividends, and non-payers entirely as capital gains.","example":"P0 = $41.50, D1 = $2.04, g = 4.6%: dividend yield = 2.04/41.50 = 4.9157%, R = 4.9157% + 4.6% = 9.52% per annum. If R = 9.4%, P0 = $45.00 and D1 = $1.62, then g = 9.4% − 3.6% = 5.8%.","contrast":"Dividend yield (D1/P0) versus capital gain yield (g); required return R versus holding period return r_E; the yield uses NEXT year's dividend.","trap":"Using D0 for the yield; leaving out g; adding the yield to R when solving for g; forgetting to convert D0 to D1 when the dividend 'was just paid'.","mnemonic":"R = yield + growth: income plus capital gain.","formula":"R = D1/P0 + g","given":true,"archetypes":["A2"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","topic":"fin201-t08-4","term":"Growing annuity (finite growth)","definition":"The present value of N dividends, the first being D1, each growing at g, is PV = D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N]. It is the Gordon value minus the value of the dividends after year N.","explain":"Use it for a high-growth phase that lasts N years (the first stage of a two-stage valuation) or a firm that pays and then liquidates. It works even when g > R, because the finite stream still has a finite value; when g < R and N is very large it approaches D1/(R − g).","example":"D1 = $1.80, g = 4%, R = 10%, N = 8: PV = 1.80/0.06 × [1 − (1.04/1.10)^8] = 30.00 × 0.36155 = $10.85. With g = 15% > R = 10%, D1 = $2.00 and N = 6: PV = (2.00/−0.05) × [1 − (1.15/1.10)^6] = −40.00 × (−0.30566) = $12.23.","contrast":"Growing annuity (N dividends) versus growing perpetuity (Gordon: forever) versus an ordinary annuity (g = 0).","trap":"Using g/R instead of (1 + g)/(1 + R); N one too many or too few; treating g > R as an error; dropping the sign when both factors are negative; forgetting the horizon price when the stream continues.","mnemonic":"Gordon minus the tail.","formula":"PV = D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N]","given":true,"archetypes":["A2"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","topic":"fin201-t08-5","term":"Non-constant growth timeline","definition":"When dividends are zero or growing at a changing rate early on, list the explicit dividends, find the first dividend of the constant-growth stage D_{T+1}, compute the horizon price P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) at date T (one period before it), and discount the dividends and P_T to today.","explain":"Always draw the timeline and mark the date of each dividend. The Gordon formula always gives the price one period BEFORE the first dividend used, which is where the most common mistake happens.","example":"No dividends for 4 years, D5 = $3.00 then growth of 4% forever, R = 10%: P4 = 3.00/(0.10 − 0.04) = $50.00 and P0 = 50.00/1.10^4 = $34.15.","contrast":"Horizon price P_T (a value at date T) versus the first dividend D_{T+1}; the dividend at T is still received at T and is discounted separately.","trap":"Placing the horizon price one year off; growing D_T by g1 instead of g2; discounting P_T by T + 1 periods; leaving out a dividend; adding the horizon price twice.","mnemonic":"Draw it, mark T, price one year before D_{T+1}.","formula":"P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2);  P0 = Σ D_t/(1 + R)^t + P_T/(1 + R)^T","given":false,"archetypes":["A3"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-two-stage","topic":"fin201-t08-5","term":"Two-stage growth formula","definition":"A firm growing at g1 for T years and then at g2 forever has P0 = D1/(R − g1) × [1 − ((1 + g1)/(1 + R))^T] + P_T/(1 + R)^T with P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) and D_{T+1} = D0(1 + g1)^T(1 + g2).","explain":"It is the timeline method in one line: a growing annuity for the first stage plus the discounted horizon price. The first stage may have g1 > R. Both formulas are on the sheet, but you must assemble them and pick the dates correctly.","example":"D0 = $1.25, g1 = 20% for 3 years, g2 = 5%, R = 12%: annuity part = 1.50/(0.12 − 0.20) × [1 − (1.20/1.12)^3] = 4.3117; D4 = 1.25 × 1.2^3 × 1.05 = 2.268; P3 = 2.268/0.07 = 32.40; PV of P3 = 32.40/1.12^3 = 23.0617; P0 = 27.37.","contrast":"Timeline (sum of discounted terms) versus the closed form: both give the same answer; the growing annuity covers dividends 1 to T and does not include P_T.","trap":"Using g2 in the annuity term; discounting P_T by T + 1 periods; computing D_{T+1} with the wrong number of growth steps; adding the annuity and the undiscounted horizon price.","mnemonic":"Annuity for stage one, perpetuity for stage two, discounted.","formula":"P0 = D1/(R − g1)[1 − ((1 + g1)/(1 + R))^T] + [D_{T+1}/(R − g2)]/(1 + R)^T","given":true,"archetypes":["A3"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","topic":"fin201-t08-5","term":"Solving backwards","definition":"When the price (today or at the horizon) is given, rearrange the same model to find the unknown: for example g2 from P_T = D_T(1 + g2)/(R − g2) gives g2 = (R·P_T − D_T)/(P_T + D_T); an unknown D0 enters every term linearly, so factor it out.","explain":"The structure of the valuation does not change; you just solve for the missing growth rate or dividend. Seminar 4's Giant stock finds the constant growth rate beginning in year 4 from a known year-3 price.","example":"Giant: D0 = $1.50, g1 = 7% for 3 years, P3 = $20, R = 15%. D3 = 1.50 × 1.07^3 = 1.8376; g2 = (0.15 × 20 − 1.8376)/(20 + 1.8376) = 5.32%.","contrast":"Forward valuation (inputs → price) versus implied-input valuation (price → input); implied g from a price versus implied R from a price.","trap":"Forgetting that D_T already contains g1 growth; using D1 instead of D_T in the g2 formula; solving the equation with the wrong sign; not factoring out D0.","mnemonic":"Same equation, different unknown.","formula":"g2 = (R·P_T − D_T)/(P_T + D_T);  D1 = P0 ÷ (annuity factor + discounted horizon factor)","given":false,"archetypes":["A3"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","topic":"fin201-t08-6","term":"Plowback and growth","definition":"The plowback ratio b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS = 1 − payout is the share of earnings reinvested. Growth is g = b × ROE, where ROE = EPS ÷ book equity per share at the START of the year.","explain":"Reinvested earnings earn ROE, so book equity grows at b × ROE and, if payout and ROE stay constant, so do earnings and dividends. Young firms plow back more (b near 1, g large); mature firms pay out more (b near 0, g small). Sample question 4: ROE 15%, payout 40% → b = 60%, g = 9%.","example":"EPS = $3.00, DPS = $1.80, book equity per share at start of year $15.00: b = 1.20/3.00 = 40%; ROE = 3.00/15.00 = 20%; g = 8%. With R = 12%: D1 = 1.80 × 1.08 = 1.944 and P0 = 1.944/(0.12 − 0.08) = $48.60.","contrast":"Plowback b versus payout 1 − b; ROE on start-of-year equity (the professor's convention) versus end-of-year equity; the textbook's 'sustainable growth' formula is NOT the professor's.","trap":"Using payout as b; growing EPS twice when finding D1; ROE on end-of-year equity; using the textbook sustainable growth formula; forgetting that g changes if b changes.","mnemonic":"g = b × ROE: grow by what you keep times what you earn on it.","formula":"g = b × ROE;  b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS = 1 − payout","given":true,"archetypes":["A4"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","topic":"fin201-t08-6","term":"Forward P/E and its drivers","definition":"With D1 = (1 − b)EPS1 and a Gordon price, the forward P/E is P0/EPS1 = (1 − b)/(R − g). Higher growth, lower risk (lower R) and a higher payout (lower b, with g constant) all raise the P/E.","explain":"It explains why young growth firms have high P/Es and why mature income stocks have low ones, and it underlies the comparables approach. Remember that g = b × ROE links growth and plowback: if g is fixed, a higher payout needs a higher ROE.","example":"b = 40%, g = 8%, R = 12%: forward P/E = 0.60/0.04 = 15.0. With EPS1 = 3.00 × 1.08 = 3.24, P0 = 15.0 × 3.24 = $48.60.","contrast":"Forward P/E (P0/EPS1) versus trailing P/E (P0/EPS0 = forward P/E × (1 + g)); earnings yield is the inverse of the P/E.","trap":"Using b rather than 1 − b in the numerator; using EPS0 with a forward multiple; reading a high P/E as 'expensive' without checking g and R.","mnemonic":"(1 − b) over (R − g): payout over the gap.","formula":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g)","given":false,"archetypes":["A4"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-npvgo","topic":"fin201-t08-6","term":"Net present value of growth opportunities","definition":"A firm that never grew would be worth EPS1/R. The price is P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO, so NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R, and P/E = 1/R + NPVGO/EPS1.","explain":"A positive NPVGO means the firm's growth projects create value. A negative NPVGO means either the stock is undervalued (buy) or growth is destroying value (do not buy): reinvesting at ROE below R is worse than paying the earnings out.","example":"Seminar 4's Johnson: EPS1 = $8, P0 = $72.94, R = 10% → NPVGO = 72.94 − 80 = −$7.06, so growth destroys value. Own example: EPS1 = $5.00, R = 10%, P0 = $62: no-growth value $50, NPVGO = $12.00, P/E = 10 + 2.4 = 12.4.","contrast":"NPVGO (value of the growth projects) versus total growth: a firm can grow quickly and still have NPVGO < 0; no-growth value EPS1/R versus price.","trap":"Using EPS0 instead of EPS1; subtracting in the wrong order (EPS1/R − P0); interpreting a negative NPVGO as 'no growth' rather than 'growth destroys value or the price is too low'.","mnemonic":"Price = no-growth value + growth value.","formula":"P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO","given":true,"archetypes":["A5"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","topic":"fin201-t08-6","term":"Growth implied by trailing and forward multiples","definition":"Because trailing and forward P/E share the same price, EPS1/EPS0 = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E, so the expected growth in earnings is (trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E) − 1.","explain":"A forward P/E lower than the trailing P/E means earnings are expected to rise; higher means they are expected to fall. The same trick works for dividends with the trailing and forward dividend yields (D1/D0 = forward yield ÷ trailing yield). For sample question 9(b) use the two P/E ratios: that screen's dividend yields do not give the same figure.","example":"Sample question 9(b): 18.798/17.943 − 1 = 4.77% expected growth this year. Own example: trailing 21.5, forward 19.3 → 21.5/19.3 − 1 = 11.40%.","contrast":"Growth in EPS (from P/E ratios) versus growth in the price (not the same); trailing versus forward.","trap":"Dividing forward by trailing (sign reversed); forgetting the −1; using the day's price change as growth; subtracting the P/Es.","mnemonic":"Trailing over forward, minus one.","formula":"g = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E − 1","given":false,"archetypes":["A6"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","topic":"fin201-t08-7","term":"Free cash flow and enterprise value","definition":"Free cash flow to the firm is FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC, the after-tax operating cash available to all investors. Discounting FCF at the firm's rate gives enterprise value; for a constant perpetuity EV = FCF/R, and equity follows from Equity = EV − Debt + Cash.","explain":"Start from the income statement: Revenue − Operating expenses = EBITDA; − D&A = EBIT; − Interest = EBT; − Tax = NPAT. FCF uses EBIT, not NPAT, so interest never appears. Use FCF when you want the value of the whole business independent of financing.","example":"Perfect Pizzas (lecture): sales $2.0M, costs $0.7M, D&A $0.2M, interest $0.1M, tax $0.3M (T = 30%), no CAPEX or ΔWC. EBIT = 1,100k; FCF = 1,100(0.70) + 200 = $970k; EV = 970/0.10 = $9.7M.","contrast":"FCF (firm, before interest, discounted at the firm rate → EV) versus FCFE (equity, after interest and borrowing, → equity value).","trap":"Subtracting interest in FCF; starting from NPAT; adding CAPEX instead of subtracting it; subtracting a decrease in WC; forgetting that the result is EV, not equity.","mnemonic":"EBIT times (1 − T), add back D&A, take out CAPEX and ΔWC.","formula":"FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC;  EV = FCF/R","given":true,"archetypes":["A8"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-fcfe","topic":"fin201-t08-7","term":"Free cash flow to equity","definition":"FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt, equivalently FCF − (1 − T) × Interest + ΔDebt. Discounting FCFE at the cost of equity gives the value of equity directly.","explain":"FCFE is what is left for shareholders after interest, tax and net borrowing, so ΔDebt (net borrowing) adds cash. Equity from FCFE should agree with Equity = EV − Debt + Cash from the FCF route.","example":"Perfect Pizzas: NPAT 700k + D&A 200k, no CAPEX, ΔWC or ΔDebt → FCFE = $900k; Equity = 900/0.10 = $9.0M. FCF − (1 − 0.30) × 100k = 970 − 70 = 900k confirms it, and EV − Equity = 9.7 − 9.0 = $0.7M of net debt.","contrast":"FCFE (after interest, includes ΔDebt, discount at R_E) versus FCF (before interest, discount at the firm rate); DDM values the dividends actually paid.","trap":"Dropping ΔDebt; subtracting ΔDebt; using EBIT(1 − T) instead of NPAT; subtracting interest twice; discounting FCFE at the firm rate.","mnemonic":"NPAT is already after interest; add borrowing.","formula":"FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt = FCF − (1 − T)·Interest + ΔDebt","given":true,"archetypes":["A8"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-working-capital","topic":"fin201-t08-7","term":"Working capital and its change","definition":"Working capital WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables (= current assets − current liabilities). A positive change in working capital, ΔWC > 0, is a cash outflow that is subtracted in FCF.","explain":"Money tied up in inventory and receivables, or in cash, is not available to investors; payables are financing from suppliers. So inventory up, receivables up, payables down and cash up are positive changes in WC. Sample question 6 asks which event gives a positive change in net working capital (inventory increases).","example":"Inventory rises $40k, receivables rise $25k, payables rise $15k: ΔWC = 40 + 25 − 15 = +$50k, so FCF is reduced by $50k.","contrast":"ΔWC (a balance-sheet change) versus CAPEX (long-term investment) versus salaries or taxes (income-statement items, not working capital).","trap":"Treating an increase in payables as an increase in WC; adding ΔWC instead of subtracting it; counting salaries, corporate tax or CAPEX as working capital.","mnemonic":"More stuff tied up = positive ΔWC = less cash.","formula":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables","given":true,"archetypes":["A8"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-which-model","topic":"fin201-t08-7","term":"Choosing a valuation model","definition":"Use comparables for a quick relative price when good peers exist; the DDM for a firm with stable, forecastable dividends; FCF (FCFF) to value the whole firm independent of financing; FCFE to value the equity directly when borrowing changes.","explain":"The review guide asks when to choose each. Comparables need similar firms; the DDM struggles with firms that pay no dividend or an irregular one; FCF needs a sensible discount rate for the whole firm and the debt and cash bridge; FCFE needs forecasts of net borrowing.","example":"A start-up with sales but losses: price/sales comparables. A mature utility with a steady, growing dividend: Gordon growth. A private company being bought outright: FCF to EV, then Equity = EV − Debt + Cash.","contrast":"Relative valuation (comparables) versus intrinsic valuation (DCF: DDM, FCF, FCFE); firm value (EV) versus equity value.","trap":"Using P/E on negative earnings; using the DDM for a firm with no dividends; discounting FCFE at the firm rate; stopping at EV without the bridge to equity.","mnemonic":"Comparables: peers. DDM: dividends. FCF: firm. FCFE: shareholders.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Which model when","head":["Model","Cash flows or metric","Output","Good when"],"rows":[["Comparables","Multiple × EPS, sales or EBITDA","Price (or EV)","Good peers exist; quick check"],["DDM","Dividends","Equity","Stable, forecastable dividends"],["FCF (FCFF)","EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","Enterprise value","Whole business; financing may change"],["FCFE","NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt","Equity","Equity directly; net borrowing predictable"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","topic":"fin201-t08-8","term":"Forms of market efficiency","definition":"In an efficient market prices already reflect the relevant information. Weak form: past prices (charts) are priced in. Semi-strong form: all publicly released information is priced in. Strong form: all information, including private, is priced in.","explain":"Each form includes the one before it. Weak and semi-strong efficiency are desirable: they mean public information gives no edge. Strong-form efficiency is undesirable because it would mean even private information is already in prices, i.e. insiders have been trading on it (insider trading).","example":"An earnings announcement under semi-strong efficiency: the price jumps to its new level at once and stays there. Under semi-strong inefficiency the price drifts toward the new level over days. Under strong-form efficiency the price would already have moved before the announcement.","contrast":"Efficiency (prices fair) versus inefficiency (a pattern you can exploit); weak form (past prices) versus semi-strong (public information) versus strong (private).","trap":"Choosing 'weak-form efficiency' when the stem describes buying on momentum (that relies on a weak-form INEFFICIENCY); thinking efficient markets mean prices never change; thinking strong-form efficiency is the goal.","mnemonic":"Weak = Watch the chart, Semi = Study the news, Strong = Secret information.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Forms of market efficiency","head":["Form","Information priced in","Desirable?"],"rows":[["Weak","Past history of prices","Yes"],["Semi-strong","All publicly released information","Yes"],["Strong","All information, including private","No (implies insider trading)"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","topic":"fin201-t08-8","term":"Profiting from inefficiency","definition":"An inefficiency is a predictable pattern in prices. Buying because a price has been trending upward (the momentum effect) relies on a weak-form inefficiency; a delayed price response to a public announcement is a semi-strong inefficiency; an overreaction followed by reversal is another.","explain":"If past prices predicted the future, charting would make money, which the weak form says it cannot. Sample question 3: buying a stock because its price has continued upward with momentum relies on weak-form inefficiencies. After an announcement the price may respond efficiently (once), slowly (delayed) or too much (overreaction).","example":"A stock rises for six straight months and you buy expecting more: you are betting on momentum, a weak-form inefficiency. If good news is announced and the price keeps creeping up for a week, buying on the news earns a profit because the market is semi-strong inefficient.","contrast":"Weak-form inefficiency (past prices help) versus weak-form efficiency (they do not); delayed response versus overreaction.","trap":"Choosing 'weak form efficiency' instead of 'weak-form inefficiencies' when relying on momentum; thinking semi-strong inefficiency is about private information.","mnemonic":"Momentum = the market has memory.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","topic":"fin201-t08-8","term":"Noise trading and behaviour","definition":"Prices can deviate from fair value because of noise (selling for personal reasons such as health or to pay tax bills) and behaviour (disposition effect: selling winners and holding losers; overconfidence; sentiment).","explain":"Noise traders move prices for reasons unrelated to value, and behavioural biases push many investors in the same direction, so mispricings can persist. These ideas explain how inefficiencies arise.","example":"An investor sells a winning stock early to lock in a gain but keeps a loser hoping for a recovery: the disposition effect. Another sells shares in April to pay a tax bill: noise.","contrast":"Noise (liquidity or personal reasons) versus behaviour (biases and sentiment) versus information (rational price changes).","trap":"Calling the disposition effect 'selling losers'; thinking noise trading is based on information.","mnemonic":"Noise = needs cash; behaviour = biased mind.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":1,"figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"fin201-a08-01","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","title":"Order the non-constant growth steps","prompt":"Put the steps of a non-constant growth valuation in the correct order.","items":["List the explicit dividends of the early stage","Find the first dividend of the constant-growth stage, D_{T+1}","Compute the horizon price P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2), one period before that dividend","Discount each early dividend and P_T back to today at R","Add the present values to get P0"],"explanation":"The horizon price is a value at date T, one period before the first constant-growth dividend. Only after finding it can you discount everything to today and sum."},{"id":"fin201-a08-02","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","title":"Order the growth-from-plowback steps","prompt":"Put the steps for valuing a firm from EPS, DPS and book equity in order.","items":["Find the plowback ratio b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS","Find ROE = EPS ÷ book equity at the start of the year","Compute growth g = b × ROE","Grow the dividend: D1 = DPS(1 + g)","Compute the price P0 = D1/(R − g)"],"explanation":"You need b and ROE for g, then D1 from the dividend just paid, then the Gordon price. ROE uses start-of-year equity."},{"id":"fin201-a08-03","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","title":"Positive or negative change in working capital","prompt":"Drag each event to the type of change in working capital it causes.","buckets":["Positive ΔWC (cash tied up, FCF falls)","Negative ΔWC (cash released, FCF rises)"],"items":[{"text":"Inventory increases","bucket":0},{"text":"Accounts receivable increase","bucket":0},{"text":"Accounts payable decrease","bucket":0},{"text":"Cash balance increases","bucket":0},{"text":"Inventory decreases","bucket":1},{"text":"Accounts receivable fall (customers pay faster)","bucket":1},{"text":"Accounts payable increase","bucket":1},{"text":"Cash balance falls","bucket":1}],"explanation":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables. Anything that raises a positive component or lowers payables raises WC and uses cash."},{"id":"fin201-a08-04","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","title":"Which form prices this information first?","prompt":"Drag each item of information to the weakest form of market efficiency that builds it into the price.","buckets":["Weak form","Semi-strong form","Strong form"],"items":[{"text":"Last year's closing prices and price charts","bucket":0},{"text":"A six-month price trend showing momentum","bucket":0},{"text":"A public earnings announcement","bucket":1},{"text":"A press release announcing a dividend cut","bucket":1},{"text":"A published analyst report built on public filings","bucket":1},{"text":"A board's confidential plan to bid for a rival","bucket":2},{"text":"Unreleased drug trial results known only to insiders","bucket":2},{"text":"A manager's private knowledge of a contract about to be lost","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Weak form covers past prices, semi-strong adds everything public, and strong form adds private information. Strong-form efficiency is undesirable because it implies insider trading."},{"id":"fin201-a08-05","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","title":"Match the symbol to its meaning","prompt":"Match each Gordon growth symbol to its meaning.","pairs":[{"left":"D0","right":"The dividend just paid"},{"left":"D1","right":"The dividend one year from now, D0(1 + g)"},{"left":"g","right":"The constant dividend growth rate, also the capital gain rate"},{"left":"R","right":"The required return (discount rate)"},{"left":"P_t","right":"The price at time t, found with D_{t+1}"},{"left":"D1/P0","right":"The dividend yield"}],"explanation":"The numerator is always the NEXT dividend, and the price at time t uses the dividend one period later."},{"id":"fin201-a08-06","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","title":"Match the situation to the valuation approach","prompt":"Match each situation to the most suitable valuation approach.","pairs":[{"left":"Mature firm with steady, growing dividends","right":"Gordon growth / DDM"},{"left":"Loss-making start-up that reports sales","right":"Price/sales comparables"},{"left":"Profitable firm with no dividends and many similar peers","right":"P/E comparables"},{"left":"Value the whole business regardless of how it is financed","right":"FCF discounted to enterprise value"},{"left":"Value the shares directly when borrowing changes each year","right":"FCFE discounted to equity"}],"explanation":"Match the model to what the firm reports and what you want: dividends (DDM), earnings or sales with peers (comparables), firm cash flows (FCF) or shareholder cash flows (FCFE)."}],"formulas":[{"id":"fin201-f08-hpr","name":"Holding period return","formula":"r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1 = (P1 − P0)/P0 + D1/P0","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-hpr"],"when":"One-period stock return; splits into capital gain yield plus dividend yield.","trap":"The dividend yield divides by today's price P0, not P1."},{"id":"fin201-f08-arith","name":"Arithmetic average return","formula":"r_A = (R1 + R2 + … + R_N)/N","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo"],"when":"The return in a typical year; forecasting next year.","trap":"r_A ≥ R_G; it overstates compound growth."},{"id":"fin201-f08-geo","name":"Geometric average return","formula":"R_G = [(1 + R1)(1 + R2)…(1 + R_N)]^(1/N) − 1","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","fin201-c08-compound-value"],"when":"The compound return per year actually earned.","trap":"Convert percentages to decimals, take the Nth root, then subtract 1."},{"id":"fin201-f08-compound-value","name":"Compound value of a holding","formula":"FV = PV(1 + R1)…(1 + R_N) = PV(1 + R_G)^N;  R_G = (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-compound-value"],"when":"Ending value after varying yearly returns, or the yearly rate implied by start and end values.","trap":"A −9% year is the factor 0.91; do not compound the arithmetic average."},{"id":"fin201-f08-perpetuity","name":"Perpetuity (constant dividend, preferred stock)","formula":"P0 = D/R","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-constant-dividend","fin201-c08-preferred-stock"],"when":"A fixed dividend forever; value sits one period before the first payment.","trap":"No growth means the price never changes; deferred first dividends need discounting."},{"id":"fin201-f08-gordon","name":"Gordon growth price","formula":"P0 = D1/(R − g);  D1 = D0(1 + g)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"when":"Dividends growing at a constant rate g forever with g < R.","trap":"Use D1, not D0; price at time t uses D_{t+1}."},{"id":"fin201-f08-price-t","name":"Price at time t","formula":"P_t = D_{t+1}/(R − g) = P0(1 + g)^t","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"when":"The price in the future under constant growth.","trap":"The price grows at g; the numerator is the dividend one period after t."},{"id":"fin201-f08-required-return","name":"Required return components","formula":"R = D1/P0 + g","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-required-return-components"],"when":"Read R off a price, or solve for g, D1 or P0.","trap":"The yield uses next year's dividend; g is also the capital gain rate."},{"id":"fin201-f08-growing-annuity","name":"Growing annuity","formula":"PV = D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N]","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-growing-annuity","fin201-c08-two-stage"],"when":"N dividends growing at g, such as a finite high-growth phase.","trap":"Ratio is (1 + g)/(1 + R); g may exceed R; add the discounted horizon price if the stream continues."},{"id":"fin201-f08-horizon","name":"Horizon price and timeline","formula":"P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2);  P0 = Σ D_t/(1 + R)^t + P_T/(1 + R)^T","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","fin201-c08-two-stage"],"when":"Non-constant growth: dividends early, constant growth after date T.","trap":"P_T sits one period before D_{T+1}; discount it T periods, not T + 1."},{"id":"fin201-f08-solve-g2","name":"Implied constant growth from a horizon price","formula":"g2 = (R·P_T − D_T)/(P_T + D_T)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-solve-backwards"],"when":"Find the later growth rate that makes the model reproduce a known P_T.","trap":"D_T already includes the early growth g1."},{"id":"fin201-f08-plowback","name":"Growth from plowback","formula":"g = b × ROE;  b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS = 1 − payout","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-plowback-growth"],"when":"Estimate g from accounting figures.","trap":"ROE is on start-of-year equity; payout is not b."},{"id":"fin201-f08-forward-pe","name":"Forward P/E","formula":"P0/EPS1 = (1 − b)/(R − g)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-forward-pe","fin201-c08-why-comparables"],"when":"Why growth, risk and payout drive the multiple; comparables logic.","trap":"The numerator is the payout 1 − b, not b."},{"id":"fin201-f08-npvgo","name":"Value of growth opportunities","formula":"P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO;  P/E = 1/R + NPVGO/EPS1","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-npvgo"],"when":"Split the price into a no-growth value and the value of growth.","trap":"Use EPS1; a negative NPVGO means growth destroys value or the stock is undervalued."},{"id":"fin201-f08-implied-growth","name":"Growth implied by P/E ratios","formula":"g = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E − 1  (EPS1/EPS0)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-implied-growth","fin201-c08-quote-screen"],"when":"Expected earnings growth from a quote screen.","trap":"Trailing over forward; forgetting the −1."},{"id":"fin201-f08-comparables","name":"Price from a benchmark multiple","formula":"P = benchmark P/E × EPS;  P = benchmark P/S × sales per share;  EPS_t = EPS0(1 + g)^t","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-pe-comparables"],"when":"Comparables valuation and target prices.","trap":"Match trailing with trailing EPS and forward with forward EPS."},{"id":"fin201-f08-quote","name":"Quote screen derivations","formula":"Shares = market cap ÷ P;  EPS = P ÷ (P/E);  NI = EPS × shares;  DPS = yield × P","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c08-quote-screen"],"when":"Recover per-share and total figures from the screen.","trap":"Use the last price, not the bid or ask."},{"id":"fin201-f08-wc","name":"Working capital","formula":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables = Current assets − Current liabilities","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-working-capital"],"when":"Compute ΔWC for FCF and FCFE.","trap":"A positive ΔWC is a cash outflow; payables work the other way."},{"id":"fin201-f08-fcf","name":"Free cash flow to the firm","formula":"FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-fcf-ev"],"when":"Value the whole firm: discount at the firm's rate to get enterprise value.","trap":"No interest in FCF; EBIT, not NPAT."},{"id":"fin201-f08-fcfe","name":"Free cash flow to equity","formula":"FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-fcfe"],"when":"Value the equity directly.","trap":"ΔDebt (net borrowing) adds cash."},{"id":"fin201-f08-equity-ev","name":"Equity from enterprise value","formula":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c08-ev-multiples","fin201-c08-fcf-ev","fin201-c08-which-model"],"when":"Move from an EV multiple or an FCF valuation to equity.","trap":"Subtract debt, add cash."}],"questions":[{"id":"fin201-q08-001","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which expression gives the holding period return r_E on a share bought at P0, when the share pays D1 and is worth P1 at the end of the period?","options":["(P1 + D1)/P1 − 1","(P1 + D1)/P0","(P1 − P0)/P0","(P1 + D1)/P0 − 1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1: everything you receive at the end (price plus dividend) over what you paid at the start, minus 1. Dividing by P1 measures return per dollar at the end, and dropping the −1 gives the growth factor, not the return. (P1 − P0)/P0 is only the capital gain yield: it leaves out the dividend.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s6-7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-002","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Maple Ridge Foods stock was bought at $52.00, paid a $1.30 dividend over the year and was sold at $55.00. The figure ($55.00 − $52.00)/$52.00 = 5.77% is the:","options":["required return R","capital gain yield","total holding period return","dividend yield"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The price change over the price paid, (P1 − P0)/P0, is the capital gain yield. The dividend yield is D1/P0 = 1.30/52.00 = 2.50%, and the total holding period return is their sum, 8.27%.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-003","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"In r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1, the dividend yield is D1 divided by _____.","options":["P0, the price paid at the start","D0, last year's dividend","EPS1, next year's earnings","P1, the price at the end"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A return is per dollar invested, and the dollars invested are the price paid at the start, P0. So dividend yield = D1/P0 and capital gain yield = (P1 − P0)/P0 share the same denominator, which is why they add to r_E.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s7; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-004","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts a stock's holding period return with its required return R?","options":["The HPR counts only the dividends received in the period; R counts only the change in the share price over the period.","The HPR must be measured over exactly one year; R can be measured over any period the investor chooses.","The HPR is what a holder actually earns from price change and dividends over a period; R is the rate investors demand for the stock's risk.","The HPR is the discount rate used in the Gordon growth model; R is the return a holder actually realised over the period."],"answer":2,"explanation":"r_E is computed from observed (or forecast) prices and dividends; R is the opportunity cost of capital that goes in the denominator of valuation formulas. The slides stress that a holding period need not be a year, so a claim that the HPR must be annual is wrong.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s6; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-005","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"All of the following are part of a stock's holding period return EXCEPT:","options":["the P/E ratio at the end of the period","the dividend yield D1/P0","the dividends received during the period","the capital gain yield (P1 − P0)/P0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"r_E = capital gain yield + dividend yield. The P/E ratio is a valuation multiple that may help forecast P1, but it is not a component of the return itself.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-006","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the dividend yield in a holding period return measured as D1/P0 rather than D1/P1?","options":["Because P1 is uncertain, so analysts avoid using it in return calculations altogether.","Because tax rules require the beginning price to be used when reporting dividend income.","A return is per dollar invested, and the dollars invested are the price paid at the start, P0.","Because dividends are normally paid at the very start of the holding period, before the price moves."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The return on any investment is gain divided by the amount put in. The investor put in P0, so both the dividend and the price change are scaled by P0. Dividing by P1 would measure return per dollar of ending value, which no one invested.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s6-7; TB 12.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-007","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock's holding period return for the year was −3.5% and its dividend yield was 2.0%. What does this tell you about the price?","options":["The capital gain yield was −1.5%, so the price fell only slightly and the dividend nearly covered it.","The capital gain yield was −3.5%, and the dividend was not paid during the year.","The price was unchanged, and the dividend was cut to zero during the year.","The capital gain yield was −5.5%: the price fell by more than the dividend made up."],"answer":3,"explanation":"r_E = capital gain yield + dividend yield, so capital gain yield = −3.5% − 2.0% = −5.5%. Adding the two (−3.5% + 2.0% = −1.5%) applies the identity backwards.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-008","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"A share whose price is the same at the end of the year as at the start, and which paid a dividend during the year, has a holding period return of zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With P1 = P0 the capital gain yield is zero, but r_E still equals the dividend yield D1/P0, which is positive. Only a share with no price change AND no dividend has a zero return.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-009","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A share can have a negative holding period return in a year in which it pays a dividend.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the price falls by more than the dividend yield, the capital loss outweighs the dividend. SM 12-3 shows −8.29% with a 1.84% dividend yield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-010","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"North Peace Pipelines shares were bought at $38.40. One year later they trade at $41.15 after the company paid $1.76 per share in dividends during the year. What was the holding period return for the year? Answer in % for the period.","answer":11.74,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((41.15+1.76)/38.4-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Capital gain yield (P1 − P0)/P0","value":7.1615,"check":"(41.15-38.4)/38.4*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Dividend yield D1/P0","value":4.5833,"check":"1.76/38.4*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Holding period return r_E","value":11.7448,"check":"((41.15+1.76)/38.4-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Capital gain yield = (P1 − P0)/P0 = (41.15 − 38.40)/38.40 = 7.1615%","Dividend yield = D1/P0 = 1.76/38.40 = 4.5833%","r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1 = (41.15 + 1.76)/38.40 − 1 = 11.7448% ≈ 11.74%"],"keystrokes":["(41.15 + 1.76) ÷ 38.40 − 1 = 0.117448","× 100 = 11.74%"],"errors":[{"id":"div-by-p1","label":"Divided by the ending price P1","value":10.96,"check":"((41.15-38.4+1.76)/41.15)*100","explain":"The return is per dollar invested at the start, so the denominator is P0, not P1."},{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Left out the dividend","value":7.16,"check":"(41.15-38.4)/38.4*100","explain":"That is only the capital gain yield; the holder also received the dividend."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot the −1","value":111.74,"check":"(41.15+1.76)/38.4*100","explain":"(P1 + D1)/P0 is the growth factor 1.1174; the return subtracts the dollar you started with."}],"explanation":"Holding period return = capital gain yield + dividend yield, both over P0. Dividing by P1 is the most common slip.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-1; L04 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-011","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nova Grid Utilities shares cost $64.00. A year later the price is $58.20 after a $2.10 dividend was paid during the year. What was the holding period return? Answer in % (a loss is negative).","answer":-5.78,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((58.2+2.1)/64.0-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Capital gain yield","value":-9.0625,"check":"(58.2-64.0)/64.0*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Dividend yield","value":3.2812,"check":"2.1/64.0*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Holding period return","value":-5.7812,"check":"((58.2+2.1)/64.0-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Capital gain yield = (58.20 − 64.00)/64.00 = −9.0625%","Dividend yield = 2.10/64.00 = 3.2812%","r_E = −9.0625% + 3.2812% = −5.7812% ≈ −5.78%"],"keystrokes":["(58.20 + 2.10) ÷ 64.00 − 1 = -0.057812"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Ignored the dividend","value":-9.06,"check":"(58.2-64.0)/64.0*100","explain":"The dividend cushions the loss: the holder gets 3.28% back in cash."},{"id":"dividend-sign","label":"Subtracted the dividend","value":-12.34,"check":"((58.2-2.1)/64.0-1)*100","explain":"A dividend received adds to the return; it is not a cost."},{"id":"div-by-p1","label":"Divided by P1","value":-6.36,"check":"((58.2-64.0+2.1)/58.2)*100","explain":"Return is per dollar invested at the start: divide by P0."}],"explanation":"A loss year is still total return = capital gain yield + dividend yield; the dividend reduces the loss from −9.06% to −5.78%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-012","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Granite Telecom trades at $30.00 and is expected to pay a $1.50 dividend over the next year. You want a holding period return of 12% for the year. What price P1 must the share reach at the end of the year? Answer in $.","answer":32.1,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"30.0*(1+0.12)-1.5","steps":[{"label":"Total value needed at year end P0(1 + r)","value":33.6,"check":"30.0*(1+0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Required price P1 = P0(1 + r) − D1","value":32.1,"check":"30.0*(1+0.12)-1.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1  →  P1 = P0(1 + r_E) − D1","P1 = 30.00(1 + 0.12) − 1.50 = 33.60 − 1.50 = 32.10"],"keystrokes":["30.00 × 1.12 = 33.60","− 1.50 = 32.10"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Ignored the dividend","value":33.6,"check":"30.0*(1+0.12)","explain":"The dividend supplies part of the 12%, so the price needs to rise less."},{"id":"dividend-sign","label":"Added the dividend","value":35.1,"check":"30.0*(1+0.12)+1.5","explain":"Total value needed = P1 + D1, so P1 = total − D1, not total + D1."}],"explanation":"Rearrange r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1 for P1. The required total of $33.60 is made up of the dividend and the price.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-1; L04 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-013","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A dividend fund share was bought at $20.00. Six months later it paid a $0.35 dividend and was worth $20.90. If it earns the same six-month holding period return every half-year, what effective annual return does that imply? Answer in % per annum.","answer":12.89,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(((20.9+0.35)/20.0)^2-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Six-month holding period return","value":6.25,"check":"((20.9+0.35)/20.0-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Effective annual return (1 + r)^2 − 1","value":12.8906,"check":"(((20.9+0.35)/20.0)^2-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Six-month r_E = (20.90 + 0.35)/20.00 − 1 = 6.2500%","The period need not be a year; compound it: (1 + 0.062500)^2 − 1 = 12.8906% per annum"],"keystrokes":["(20.90 + 0.35) ÷ 20.00 = 1.0625","x² = ","− 1 = 0.128906"],"errors":[{"id":"annual-periodic","label":"Doubled the half-year return","value":12.5,"check":"((20.9+0.35)/20.0-1)*200","explain":"Two half-year returns compound; doubling ignores the return earned on the first half's gain."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot the −1 after squaring","value":112.89,"check":"(((20.9+0.35)/20.0)^2)*100","explain":"Squaring gives the wealth factor 1.1289; subtract 1 to get the return."}],"explanation":"The slides note a holding period need not be a year. Compound the period return to convert to an annual effective rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-014","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Foothills Brewing shares were bought at $27.50. A year later the price is $29.00 after a $0.90 dividend. What is the holding period return?","options":["8.28%","8.73%","108.73%","5.45%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"r_E = (29.00 + 0.90)/27.50 − 1 = 8.73%. The 8.28% distractor divides by the ending price P1 (div-by-p1), 5.45% leaves out the dividend, and 108.73% forgets to subtract 1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-015","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock costs $45.00 today. Next year's dividend is expected to be $1.62 and the price is expected to reach $47.40. What is the dividend yield part of the holding period return?","options":["3.60%","5.33%","8.93%","3.42%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dividend yield = D1/P0 = 1.62/45.00 = 3.60%. Using P1 (3.42%) is the wrong denominator; 5.33% is the capital gain yield and 8.93% is the total return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-016","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock A has a dividend yield of 4.0% and a capital gain yield of 1.0%. Stock B has a dividend yield of 0.5% and a capital gain yield of 4.5%. Which statement is correct?","options":["B has the higher holding period return because its capital gain yield is larger than A's.","They cannot be compared unless the P/E ratios of the two stocks are known first.","A has the higher holding period return because dividends are more certain than price gains.","Both have a 5.0% holding period return; A pays it mostly as income, B mostly as price growth."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Total return = capital gain yield + dividend yield, and both equal 5.0%. The split matters for who prefers the stock (income versus growth), not for the size of the return.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-017","concept":"fin201-c08-hpr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put the steps for a one-period holding period return in the correct order.","options":["Add D1 to P0; divide by P1; subtract 1","Subtract 1 from P1; add D1; divide by P0","Add D1 to P1; divide by P0; subtract 1","Divide P1 by P0; add D1; subtract 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"r_E = (P1 + D1)/P0 − 1: first total what you receive (P1 + D1), then express it per dollar paid (÷ P0), then take away the original dollar (−1). Adding D1 after dividing leaves D1 unscaled.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-018","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The geometric average return R_G = [(1 + R1)(1 + R2)…(1 + R_N)]^(1/N) − 1 measures:","options":["the sum of the capital gain yield and the dividend yield in the final year","the constant yearly return that compounds the start value into the end value","the return in a typical single year, found by averaging the yearly returns","the best yearly return the investment earned in any single year of the period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R_G is the average compound return per year, so (1 + R_G)^N × PV equals the actual ending value. The return in a typical year is the arithmetic average.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-019","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A fund advertises: 'Over the last five years our clients' money compounded at X% a year.' Which average return is X?","options":["The geometric average","The average dividend yield","The last year's holding period return","The arithmetic average"],"answer":0,"explanation":"'Compounded at X% a year' is the constant yearly rate that reproduces the actual growth of wealth: the geometric average. The arithmetic average answers 'what was the return in an average year'.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5; L04 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q08-020","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement distinguishes the arithmetic and geometric average returns correctly?","options":["Arithmetic includes dividends; geometric uses only price changes.","Arithmetic answers 'what was the average compound return per year'; geometric answers 'what was the return in an average year'.","Arithmetic is used for past growth of wealth; geometric is used to forecast next year.","Arithmetic answers 'what was the return in an average year'; geometric answers 'what was the average compound return per year'."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Arithmetic is the simple mean of the returns and suits forecasting a typical year; geometric reflects compounding and is the 'truth' about what happened to wealth. The slides summarise it as R_A ≥ R_G.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5; L04 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q08-021","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of these statements about R_A and R_G is NOT true?","options":["R_A is greater than or equal to R_G.","R_G reflects the compounding of returns.","R_G exceeds R_A whenever the yearly returns vary.","R_A = R_G only when every yearly return is identical."],"answer":2,"explanation":"R_A ≥ R_G always, with equality only when all returns are equal. The wrong claim reverses the inequality.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q08-022","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the geometric average below the arithmetic average when yearly returns vary?","options":["Because the geometric average ignores dividends and so understates the return earned.","Compounding: a loss costs more than an equal gain adds, as −50% then +50% leaves 75 per 100.","Because the arithmetic average counts the first year's return twice in its calculation.","Because taking the Nth root always lowers the answer relative to the simple mean."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compounding is multiplicative: 1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75 although the simple average of +50% and −50% is 0%. The more the returns vary, the bigger the gap (R_G ≈ R_A − σ²/2).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5; L04 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q08-023","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock falls 50% in year 1 and rises 100% in year 2. Its arithmetic average return is 25% per year but its geometric average return is 0%.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"R_A = (−50% + 100%)/2 = 25%. The wealth factor is 0.5 × 2.0 = 1.0, so R_G = 1.0^(1/2) − 1 = 0%. You end where you started.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-024","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"The geometric average return of a series of yearly returns can be larger than its arithmetic average return.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R_G ≤ R_A for any series, with equality only when the returns are all the same.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q08-025","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary stock earned annual returns of 12%, −7%, 18% and 4% over four years. What was the geometric average return per year? Answer in % per annum.","answer":6.33,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((1.12*0.93*1.18*1.04)^(1/4)-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Arithmetic average (for comparison)","value":6.75,"check":"(12-7+18+4)/4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Product of (1 + R)","value":1.27825,"check":"1.12*0.93*1.18*1.04","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Geometric average R_G","value":6.3296,"check":"((1.12*0.93*1.18*1.04)^(1/4)-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Convert each return to a growth factor: 1.12, 0.93, 1.18, 1.04","Product = 1.12 × 0.93 × 1.18 × 1.04 = 1.27825","R_G = 1.27825^(1/4) − 1 = 6.3296% ≈ 6.33% per annum","(Arithmetic average = 6.75%, which is higher, as expected.)"],"keystrokes":["1.12 × 0.93 × 1.18 × 1.04 = 1.27825","y^x 0.25 (or 4 then 1/x then y^x)","= 1.06330; − 1 = 0.063296"],"errors":[{"id":"arith-for-geo","label":"Averaged the four returns","value":6.75,"check":"(12-7+18+4)/4","explain":"That is the arithmetic average, the return in an average year, not the compound return."},{"id":"geo-no-root","label":"Multiplied but skipped the root","value":27.83,"check":"(1.12*0.93*1.18*1.04-1)*100","explain":"27.83% is the total four-year return; the Nth root converts it to a per-year rate."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Took the root but forgot the −1","value":106.33,"check":"(1.12*0.93*1.18*1.04)^(1/4)*100","explain":"The root gives the yearly growth factor 1.0633; subtract 1 for the rate."}],"explanation":"R_G = [Π(1 + R)]^(1/N) − 1. Convert percentages to decimals before multiplying.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-12; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-026","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bow Valley Lumber shares cost $18.40 today. Over the next three years the year-end prices are $21.10, $19.60 and $23.50, and the dividends paid in those years are $0.55, $0.60 and $0.65. What is the geometric average return per year? Answer in % per annum.","answer":11.55,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((21.65/18.40)*(20.20/21.10)*(24.15/19.60))^(1/3)*100-100","steps":[{"label":"Year 1 return","value":17.663,"check":"((21.10+0.55)/18.40-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Year 2 return","value":-4.2654,"check":"((19.60+0.60)/21.10-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Year 3 return","value":23.2143,"check":"((23.50+0.65)/19.60-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Geometric average R_G","value":11.5467,"check":"((21.65/18.40)*(20.20/21.10)*(24.15/19.60))^(1/3)*100-100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["r1 = (21.10 + 0.55)/18.40 − 1 = 17.6630%","r2 = (19.60 + 0.60)/21.10 − 1 = −4.2654%","r3 = (23.50 + 0.65)/19.60 − 1 = 23.2143%","R_G = [(1 + r1)(1 + r2)(1 + r3)]^(1/3) − 1 = 1.38794^(1/3) − 1 = 11.5467% ≈ 11.55% per annum","(R_A = 12.20%.)"],"keystrokes":["Find each year's r_E first, using the PREVIOUS year-end price as the denominator","Product of the three factors = 1.38794","y^x (1 ÷ 3) − 1 = 0.115467"],"errors":[{"id":"arith-for-geo","label":"Averaged the three returns","value":12.2,"check":"((21.65/18.40-1)+(20.20/21.10-1)+(24.15/19.60-1))/3*100","explain":"The arithmetic average overstates compound growth."},{"id":"div-by-p1","label":"Divided each year's gain by the ending price","value":10.06,"check":"((21.10-18.40+0.55)/21.10+(19.60-21.10+0.60)/19.60+(23.50-19.60+0.65)/23.50)/3*100","explain":"Each yearly return is scaled by the price at the START of that year."},{"id":"geo-no-root","label":"Skipped the root","value":38.79,"check":"((21.65/18.40)*(20.20/21.10)*(24.15/19.60)-1)*100","explain":"That is the three-year total return, not the per-year rate."}],"explanation":"Build each yearly return from prices and dividends, then compound. Using the arithmetic mean would overstate how fast wealth grew.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-027","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock's last four annual returns were +25%, −10%, +14% and +3%. If your best estimate of next year's return is the return in a typical year, what do you forecast? Answer in % per annum.","answer":8.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(25-10+14+3)/4","steps":[{"label":"Arithmetic average r_A","value":8.0,"check":"(25-10+14+3)/4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["The return in an average (typical) year is the arithmetic average:","r_A = (25 − 10 + 14 + 3)/4 = 8.00% per annum","(R_G = 7.21% describes compound growth of past wealth, not a typical-year forecast.)"],"keystrokes":["25 − 10 + 14 + 3 = 32; ÷ 4 = 8.00"],"errors":[{"id":"geo-for-arith","label":"Used the geometric average","value":7.21,"check":"(1.25*0.9*1.14*1.03)^(1/4)*100-100","explain":"R_G answers 'what compound rate did wealth earn'; for a typical year's return use the arithmetic average."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided by N−1 = 3","value":10.67,"check":"(25-10+14+3)/3","explain":"An average of four returns divides by 4."}],"explanation":"Forecasting the return of a typical future year uses r_A; reporting past compound growth uses R_G.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10; TB 12.5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-028","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock earned +10%, −5% and +6% in three consecutive years. What is its geometric average return per year?","options":["3.67%","10.77%","103.47%","3.47%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"R_G = (1.10 × 0.95 × 1.06)^(1/3) − 1 = 1.1077^(1/3) − 1 = 3.47%. The 3.67% distractor is the arithmetic average, 10.77% skips the root, and 103.47% forgets the −1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-12"},{"id":"fin201-q08-029","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock rose 30% one year and fell 30% the next. What is its geometric average return per year?","options":["−9.00%","0.00%","−4.61%","95.39%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The wealth factor is 1.30 × 0.70 = 0.91, so R_G = 0.91^(1/2) − 1 = −4.61% per year. 0.00% is the arithmetic average; −9.00% is the two-year total with no root; 95.39% forgets the −1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-12"},{"id":"fin201-q08-030","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over six years a stock's arithmetic average return was 9.83% and its geometric average return was 8.10%. What does the 1.73-point gap tell you?","options":["Dividends were left out of the geometric average but included in the arithmetic one.","The yearly returns varied; the more they vary, the further R_G falls below R_A.","The arithmetic average was miscalculated, since it must equal the geometric average.","The investor paid fees that reduced the compound return but not the average return."],"answer":1,"explanation":"R_G ≈ R_A − σ²/2, so the gap measures the volatility drag of the series. With constant returns the two would be identical.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-12"},{"id":"fin201-q08-031","concept":"fin201-c08-arith-vs-geo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"You want (i) your best guess of next year's return for a typical year and (ii) a measure of how fast your past wealth actually grew. Which averages do you use?","options":["(i) arithmetic, (ii) geometric","(i) arithmetic, (ii) arithmetic","(i) geometric, (ii) arithmetic","(i) geometric, (ii) geometric"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A typical-year estimate is the arithmetic mean (R_A); the compound growth of wealth already earned is the geometric mean (R_G).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-032","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A holding of PV earns R1, R2, …, R_N in successive years. Its value after N years is:","options":["PV + R1 + R2 + … + R_N","PV(1 + r_A)^N, using the arithmetic average","PV(1 + R1 + R2 + … + R_N)","PV(1 + R1)(1 + R2)…(1 + R_N)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each year's return applies to the previous year's ending value, so the growth factors multiply. Adding the returns ignores compounding, and compounding the arithmetic average overstates the value whenever returns vary.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-033","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An investor's $10,000 grew to $13,310 over three years. She computes (13,310/10,000)^(1/3) − 1 = 10%. This 10% is the:","options":["geometric (compound) average return per year","dividend yield earned over the three years","holding period return for the last year","arithmetic average return per year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Solving FV = PV(1 + R_G)^N for R_G gives R_G = (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1. It is the constant yearly return that reproduces the actual growth.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-034","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You invested $2,500 in Cascade Telecom. Over three years its returns were +14%, −9% and +6%, with no money added or withdrawn. What is your holding worth after three years? Answer in $.","answer":2749.11,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2500*1.14*0.91*1.06","steps":[{"label":"Value after year 1","value":2850.0,"check":"2500*1.14","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Value after year 2","value":2593.5,"check":"2500*1.14*0.91","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Value after year 3","value":2749.11,"check":"2500*1.14*0.91*1.06","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["FV = PV(1 + R1)(1 + R2)(1 + R3)","Year 1: 2,500 × 1.14 = 2,850.00","Year 2: 2,850.00 × 0.91 = 2,593.50","Year 3: 2,593.50 × 1.06 = $2,749.11"],"keystrokes":["2500 × 1.14 × 0.91 × 1.06 = 2,749.11"],"errors":[{"id":"no-compounding","label":"Added the three returns","value":2775.0,"check":"2500*(1+0.14-0.09+0.06)","explain":"Adding returns ignores that each year's return applies to a changed base."},{"id":"arith-for-geo","label":"Compounded the arithmetic average","value":2785.21,"check":"2500*(1+(0.14-0.09+0.06)/3)^3","explain":"Compounding R_A overstates the value whenever returns vary."},{"id":"minus-sign","label":"Used 1.09 instead of 0.91 for the −9% year","value":3292.89,"check":"2500*1.14*1.09*1.06","explain":"A −9% return is the factor 0.91, not 1.09."}],"explanation":"Multiply the growth factors (1 + R) in sequence; the order does not matter but the signs and decimals do.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-035","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rocky Mountain Rail shares bought for $8,000 are worth $10,350 four years later, with no dividends. What constant compound return per year does this represent? Answer in % per annum.","answer":6.65,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((10350/8000)^(1/4)-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Growth factor FV/PV","value":1.29375,"check":"10350/8000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"R_G = (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1","value":6.6504,"check":"((10350/8000)^(1/4)-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["FV = PV(1 + R_G)^N  →  R_G = (FV/PV)^(1/N) − 1","R_G = (10,350/8,000)^(1/4) − 1 = 1.29375^0.25 − 1 = 6.6504% ≈ 6.65% per annum"],"keystrokes":["10350 ÷ 8000 = 1.29375","y^x 0.25 =","− 1 = 0.066504"],"errors":[{"id":"arith-for-geo","label":"Total gain divided by N","value":7.34,"check":"(10350/8000-1)/4*100","explain":"Dividing the total gain by 4 ignores compounding; it overstates the yearly rate."},{"id":"geo-no-root","label":"Skipped the root","value":29.38,"check":"(10350/8000-1)*100","explain":"29.38% is the four-year total return."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used N = 3 instead of 4","value":8.96,"check":"((10350/8000)^(1/3)-1)*100","explain":"Four years of growth means a 1/4 power, not 1/3."}],"explanation":"Solve FV = PV(1 + R_G)^N for R_G with the correct number of years.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-036","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary fund returned +18%, then −12%, then +9% over three years and is now worth $26,000. How much was originally invested? Answer in $.","answer":22971.12,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"26000/(1.18*0.88*1.09)","steps":[{"label":"Three-year growth factor","value":1.13186,"check":"1.18*0.88*1.09","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Original investment = FV ÷ factor","value":22971.12,"check":"26000/(1.18*0.88*1.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["FV = PV × (1 + R1)(1 + R2)(1 + R3)  →  PV = FV ÷ [(1.18)(0.88)(1.09)]","Factor = 1.13186","PV = 26,000 ÷ 1.13186 = $22,971.12"],"keystrokes":["1.18 × 0.88 × 1.09 = 1.131856","26000 ÷ 1.131856 = 22,971.12"],"errors":[{"id":"no-compounding","label":"Divided by the sum of returns","value":22608.7,"check":"26000/(1+0.18-0.12+0.09)","explain":"Returns compound; dividing by 1 + sum of returns is wrong."},{"id":"minus-sign","label":"Used 1.12 for −12%","value":18048.74,"check":"26000/(1.18*1.12*1.09)","explain":"A −12% return has factor 0.88."}],"explanation":"Work backwards by dividing the ending value by the product of the growth factors.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-037","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"An investor puts $5,000 into a volatile stock whose yearly returns are +40%, −25%, +20% and −10%. What is the holding worth after four years? Answer in $.","answer":5670.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"5000*1.40*0.75*1.20*0.90","steps":[{"label":"Four-year wealth factor","value":1.134,"check":"1.40*0.75*1.20*0.90","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Ending value","value":5670.0,"check":"5000*1.40*0.75*1.20*0.90","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Wealth factor = 1.40 × 0.75 × 1.20 × 0.90 = 1.13400","FV = 5,000 × 1.134 = $5,670.00","(Compounding the arithmetic average 6.25% would claim $6,372.15, which is wrong.)"],"keystrokes":["5000 × 1.4 × 0.75 × 1.2 × 0.9 = 5,670.00"],"errors":[{"id":"arith-for-geo","label":"Compounded the arithmetic average 6.25%","value":6372.15,"check":"5000*(1+(0.40-0.25+0.20-0.10)/4)^4","explain":"R_A applied for 4 years overstates the true ending value."},{"id":"no-compounding","label":"Added the four returns","value":6250.0,"check":"5000*(1+0.40-0.25+0.20-0.10)","explain":"Adding returns gives 1.25 instead of the compound factor 1.134."}],"explanation":"Ending value = PV × product of growth factors. Volatile returns make the arithmetic shortcuts mislead.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-038","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You invest $4,000 in a stock that returns +25%, −20% and +10% in three successive years. What is the investment worth at the end of year 3?","options":["$4,600.00","$6,600.00","$4,400.00","$4,630.50"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Value = 4,000 × 1.25 × 0.80 × 1.10 = $4,400.00. Adding the returns gives $4,600.00, compounding the arithmetic average 5% gives $4,630.50, and using 1.20 for the −20% year gives $6,600.00.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-039","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"An investor's $10,000 grew to $13,310 in three years with no deposits. What compound return per year is that?","options":["33.10%","10.00%","11.03%","15.37%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R_G = (13,310/10,000)^(1/3) − 1 = 10.00%. Dividing the 33.1% total gain by 3 gives 11.03%, using no root gives 33.10%, and using a 1/2 power (N − 1) gives 15.37%.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-040","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we multiply (1 + R) factors rather than add yearly returns to find the value of a holding?","options":["Because percentage returns can only be added when the investment is held for under a year.","Because adding returns would double count the dividends included in each year's return.","Each year's return is earned on the previous year's ending value, so gains and losses compound.","Because returns are quoted per annum, so they must be multiplied rather than added."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wealth after year t becomes the base for year t + 1. That is why 1.20 × 0.85 × 1.10 = 1.122, not 1.15.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-041","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Compounding the arithmetic average return for N years, instead of using the actual yearly returns, gives an ending value that is:","options":["wrong only when dividends are paid","too high whenever the yearly returns vary","always identical to the true value","too low whenever the yearly returns vary"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since R_A ≥ R_G, (1 + R_A)^N ≥ (1 + R_G)^N = the true wealth factor. Only constant returns make the two agree.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10; TB 12.5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-042","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"If no money is added or withdrawn, a holding's value after N years does not depend on the order in which the yearly returns occur.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The wealth factor is the product of the (1 + R) terms, and multiplication is commutative. Order matters only when cash flows are added or withdrawn.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-043","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"$1,000 is held three years with returns of +20%, −15% and +10%. Which expression does NOT equal its ending value of $1,122.00?","options":["$1,000 × (1 + R_G)^3, with R_G the geometric average","$1,000 × 1.20 × 0.85 × 1.10","$1,000 × (1.20 × 0.85) × 1.10","$1,000 × (1 + 0.20 − 0.15 + 0.10)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Only the product of the growth factors (or the equivalent (1 + R_G)^3) gives $1,122.00. Adding the returns gives $1,150.00. The expression that groups 1.20 × 0.85 = 1.02 first is also correct, because multiplication can be grouped in any order.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-044","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A holding is worth exactly what it was two years ago although the yearly returns were +40% and −28.57%. The arithmetic average is 5.71%. Which figure describes what happened to your wealth?","options":["The geometric average of 0%","The 40% return in the first year","The arithmetic average of 5.71%","The −28.57% return in the second year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1.40 × 0.7143 = 1.00, so R_G = 0%. The arithmetic average would suggest wealth grew each year on average, which it did not.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-045","concept":"fin201-c08-compound-value","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put the steps for finding the geometric average return from yearly returns in order.","options":["Add 1 to each return; multiply them; raise the product to 1/N; subtract 1","Add 1 to each return; add them; divide by N; subtract 1","Multiply the returns; add 1; raise the product to 1/N; subtract 1","Add the returns; divide by N; add 1; raise to 1/N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Convert to growth factors (decimals), multiply, take the Nth root, then subtract 1. Adding and dividing by N is the arithmetic average.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.5; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-046","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm's liquidation value is the amount:","options":["from selling its assets and paying off its creditors","to purchase a firm after it files for bankruptcy","equal to the book value of equity","to repurchase all shares of common stock","none of the above"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Liquidation value is what is left for the owners if the firm's assets are sold off and the creditors are paid first. Book value of equity is an accounting figure (assets minus liabilities at historical cost) and generally differs from it; the price to buy a bankrupt firm and the cost of repurchasing all shares are different things altogether.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-047","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Book value of equity is:","options":["the cash left after assets are sold and creditors are paid","share price multiplied by shares outstanding","total assets minus total liabilities as recorded on the balance sheet","the present value of all expected future dividends"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Book value is an accounting number built from historical costs. Price × shares is market capitalisation, the cash left after a sale is liquidation value, and the PV of dividends is an intrinsic (DDM) value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-048","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sigma Tools has 4 million shares trading at $22.00. The figure 4,000,000 × $22.00 = $88 million is Sigma's:","options":["enterprise value","book value of equity","liquidation value of equity","market value of equity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Share price times shares outstanding is market capitalisation, the market value of equity. Enterprise value would add debt and subtract cash.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s13-14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-049","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best separates book value from market value?","options":["Book value counts intangible assets such as brands at market worth; market value ignores them.","Book value records past costs from the accounts; market value is what investors pay for expected future cash flows.","Book value of equity is always larger than market value for a profitable, growing firm.","Book value is what investors would pay today; market value is taken from the balance sheet."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Balance sheets mostly show historical cost; market prices look forward. That is why market value can be far above book value for a firm with growth opportunities and valuable intangibles.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s13; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-050","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a reason a firm's market value of equity might exceed its book value?","options":["Assets are carried at depreciated historical cost.","Valuable intangible assets such as a brand are not recorded at market worth.","The firm is expected to earn less than its cost of equity on its book equity.","Investors expect profitable growth opportunities."],"answer":2,"explanation":"P/B above 1 reflects growth opportunities, intangibles and historical-cost accounting. Expected returns below the cost of equity push the market value toward or below book value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-051","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can a healthy operating firm's market value be far above its liquidation value?","options":["Market value prices the future cash flows of the assets working together; liquidation sells them piece by piece.","Because liquidation value always equals book value, which is itself far below market value.","Because liquidation value ignores the firm's debts, which market value already subtracts.","Because market value ignores the firm's assets and counts only its annual sales."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A going concern keeps earning from its assets, customers and know-how. In a liquidation, only what the assets fetch on sale remains after creditors are repaid.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-052","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A listed firm trades at a price-to-book ratio of 0.8. What might this indicate?","options":["The firm is certain to go bankrupt, because market value below book means insolvency.","The firm's dividend yield is 0.8%, which is lower than its required return.","Investors value the firm at 80% of its annual sales, which is a low price/sales ratio.","Investors expect it to earn less than its cost of equity on book equity, or book assets are overstated."],"answer":3,"explanation":"P/B below 1 means the market values equity below its accounting value. It is a warning sign about expected returns or asset values, not a guarantee of failure.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-053","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Market capitalisation equals the share price multiplied by the number of shares outstanding.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is the market value of all of a firm's equity: price per share × shares outstanding.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-054","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Print Ltd. owes its creditors $13.9 million. Its books show assets of $21.0 million, but in a liquidation the assets would fetch only $18.4 million. What is the liquidation value of its equity?","options":["$32.3 million","$4.5 million","$18.4 million","$7.1 million"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Liquidation value = sale proceeds of the assets − amounts owed to creditors = 18.4 − 13.9 = $4.5 million. The $7.1 million is book equity (it uses book assets), $18.4 million forgets to pay the creditors, and $32.3 million adds the debt.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q2; L04 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-055","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar Foods has a market capitalisation of $214 million, total assets of $300 million and book equity of $122 million. What is its price-to-book (P/B) ratio?","options":["0.57","2.46","1.75","0.71"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P/B = market value of equity ÷ book value of equity = 214/122 = 1.75. The inverted ratio is 0.57, dividing by assets gives 0.71, and assets over book equity (2.46) is a leverage measure, not P/B.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-056","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The amount left for the owners after a firm's assets are sold and its creditors are paid is its _____ value.","options":["book","liquidation","market","par"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the definition of liquidation value. Book value comes from the balance sheet and market value from share prices.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-057","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A bank wants to estimate how much it could recover from a borrower that defaults and has its assets sold. Which value is most relevant?","options":["Book value of equity","Forward P/E","Market capitalisation","Liquidation value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Recoveries depend on what the assets would fetch in a sale and on the ranking of creditors, which is liquidation value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s13; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-058","concept":"fin201-c08-book-market-liquidation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The sample exam's quote screen lists 'Price/Book 1.722' for XYZ Limited. Which comparison does this figure make?","options":["Market value of equity is about 1.7 times the book value of equity on the balance sheet","Book value of equity is about 1.7 times the market value of the shares outstanding","The share price is about 1.7 times the earnings per share of the last twelve months","Liquidation value is about 1.7 times the book value of the firm's total assets"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Price/Book (market-to-book, L04 s14) divides the market value of equity (price × shares) by the book value of equity, so investors value XYZ's equity at about 1.7 times its accounting value. Book over market is the inverted ratio, price over EPS is the P/E (18.798 on the same screen), and liquidation value never appears on a quote screen.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14; PRAC q9; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-059","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"The following market trading information is available about a particular stock: XYZ Limited, last $60.34 (down $0.83, 1.38%); Bid $60.31; Ask $60.35; Day Range 60.14 - 60.49; Volume 1300; Year Range 57.27 - 63.24; Div Yield 2.768%; Forward Div Yield 2.642%; Market Cap $6397100; Price/Sales 1.88; Price/Earnings 18.798; Consensus Forward P/E 17.943; Price/Book 1.722. (a) How many shares are outstanding? Answer in shares.","answer":106018,"unit":"shares","tolerance":{"abs":20},"check":"6397100/60.34","steps":[{"label":"Shares outstanding = market cap ÷ price","value":106018,"check":"6397100/60.34","unit":"shares","tolerance":{"abs":20}}],"solution":["Market cap = price × shares outstanding","Shares = 6,397,100 ÷ 60.34 = 106,017.6 ≈ 106,018 shares","(Dividing by the ask of $60.35 gives 106,000, within the accepted range, because the screen's market cap appears to have been built on that price.)"],"keystrokes":["6397100 ÷ 60.34 = 106,017.6"],"errors":[{"id":"bid-for-last","label":"Divided by the bid $60.31","value":106070,"check":"6397100/60.31","explain":"Market cap uses the last traded price, not the bid."},{"id":"units","label":"Read the market cap as thousands of dollars","value":106017567,"check":"6397100*1000/60.34","explain":"The screen prints market cap in dollars; scaling it to thousands overstates the shares a thousand-fold."}],"explanation":"Shares outstanding = market cap ÷ last price = 6,397,100 ÷ 60.34 = 106,017.6 ≈ 106,018 shares. Dividing by the ask ($60.35) gives exactly 106,000, which suggests the screen's market cap was built on that price, so 106,000 is also accepted; the method to show is market cap ÷ last price. Dividing by the bid (106,070) is the classic quote-screen slip.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q9a"},{"id":"fin201-q08-060","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":["fin201-c08-implied-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"The following market trading information is available about a particular stock: XYZ Limited, last $60.34 (down $0.83, 1.38%); Bid $60.31; Ask $60.35; Day Range 60.14 - 60.49; Volume 1300; Year Range 57.27 - 63.24; Div Yield 2.768%; Forward Div Yield 2.642%; Market Cap $6397100; Price/Sales 1.88; Price/Earnings 18.798; Consensus Forward P/E 17.943; Price/Book 1.722. (b) What rate of growth is expected this year? Answer in % per annum.","answer":4.77,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(18.798/17.943-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Trailing EPS0 = price ÷ trailing P/E","value":3.2099,"check":"60.34/18.798","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Forward EPS1 = price ÷ forward P/E","value":3.3629,"check":"60.34/17.943","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Growth = EPS1/EPS0 − 1","value":4.7651,"check":"(18.798/17.943-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Trailing EPS0 = 60.34/18.798 = 3.2099","Forward EPS1 = 60.34/17.943 = 3.3629","Growth = EPS1/EPS0 − 1 = (trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E) − 1 = 18.798/17.943 − 1 = 4.7651% ≈ 4.77% per annum"],"keystrokes":["18.798 ÷ 17.943 = 1.04765","− 1 = 0.04765 → 4.77%"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Divided forward by trailing","value":-4.55,"check":"(17.943/18.798-1)*100","explain":"Inverting the ratio reverses the sign: a lower forward P/E means earnings are expected to RISE."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot the −1","value":104.77,"check":"18.798/17.943*100","explain":"1.0477 is the growth factor, not the rate."},{"id":"price-change-as-growth","label":"Used the day's price change of 1.38%","value":1.38,"check":"1.38","explain":"The day's price move says nothing about expected earnings growth."}],"explanation":"Same price, different earnings: price/EPS0 = 18.798 and price/EPS1 = 17.943, so EPS1/EPS0 = 18.798/17.943. Work from the two P/E ratios: on this screen the dividend yields (2.768% trailing, 2.642% forward) do not reproduce the expected earnings growth.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q9b; L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-061","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A quote shows: last price $27.30; market cap $163,800,000; trailing P/E 14.0. What was the company's net income over the last year? Answer in $.","answer":11700000,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005},"check":"27.30/14.0*163800000/27.30","steps":[{"label":"Shares outstanding = market cap ÷ price","value":6000000,"check":"163800000/27.30","unit":"shares","tolerance":{"abs":1}},{"label":"Trailing EPS = price ÷ P/E","value":1.95,"check":"27.30/14.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Net income = EPS × shares","value":11700000,"check":"27.30/14.0*163800000/27.30","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Shares = 163,800,000 ÷ 27.30 = 6,000,000","EPS = price ÷ trailing P/E = 27.30 ÷ 14.0 = $1.95","Net income = EPS × shares = 1.95 × 6,000,000 = $11,700,000"],"keystrokes":["27.30 ÷ 14 = 1.95","163,800,000 ÷ 27.30 = 6,000,000","1.95 × 6,000,000 = 11,700,000"],"errors":[{"id":"shares-as-ni","label":"Stopped at the number of shares","value":6000000,"check":"163800000/27.30","explain":"6,000,000 is the share count; net income is EPS times that count."},{"id":"p-times-pe","label":"Multiplied price by P/E for EPS","value":2293200000,"check":"27.30*14.0*163800000/27.30","explain":"EPS = price ÷ (P/E), not price × P/E."}],"explanation":"Chain: shares = market cap ÷ price; EPS = price ÷ P/E; net income = EPS × shares (which equals market cap ÷ P/E).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-22; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-062","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A quote shows a last price of $30.00, a trailing dividend yield of 3.2% and a forward dividend yield of 3.4%. What growth in the dividend per share is expected this year? Answer in % per annum.","answer":6.25,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(0.034/0.032-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Trailing dividend D0 = yield × price","value":0.96,"check":"0.032*30.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Forward dividend D1 = yield × price","value":1.02,"check":"0.034*30.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Dividend growth D1/D0 − 1","value":6.25,"check":"(0.034/0.032-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["D0 = 3.2% × 30.00 = $0.96","D1 = 3.4% × 30.00 = $1.02","Growth = D1/D0 − 1 = 1.02/0.96 − 1 = 6.25%"],"keystrokes":["0.034 ÷ 0.032 = 1.0625","− 1 = 6.25%"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Divided trailing by forward","value":-5.88,"check":"(0.032/0.034-1)*100","explain":"Forward over trailing: D1/D0, not D0/D1."},{"id":"subtract-yields","label":"Subtracted the yields","value":0.2,"check":"(0.034-0.032)*100","explain":"0.20 percentage points is the change in the yield, not the growth in the dividend (6.25%)."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot the −1","value":106.25,"check":"0.034/0.032*100","explain":"1.0625 is the growth factor."}],"explanation":"With the same price in both yields, D1/D0 = forward yield / trailing yield.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-063","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock screen lists a trailing P/E of 21.5 and a consensus forward P/E of 19.3. By what percentage are earnings per share expected to grow this year? Answer in % per annum.","answer":11.4,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(21.5/19.3-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"EPS1/EPS0 = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E","value":1.11399,"check":"21.5/19.3","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Expected growth","value":11.399,"check":"(21.5/19.3-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Same price P in both ratios: EPS0 = P/21.5 and EPS1 = P/19.3","EPS1/EPS0 = 21.5/19.3 = 1.11399","Growth = 1.11399 − 1 = 11.40% per annum"],"keystrokes":["21.5 ÷ 19.3 = 1.11399","− 1 = 11.40%"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Divided forward by trailing","value":-10.23,"check":"(19.3/21.5-1)*100","explain":"That gives −10.23%: it treats a falling P/E as falling earnings, when it means rising earnings."},{"id":"subtract-yields","label":"Subtracted the P/Es","value":2.2,"check":"21.5-19.3","explain":"A difference of P/E multiples is not a growth rate."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot the −1","value":111.4,"check":"21.5/19.3*100","explain":"1.114 is the growth factor."}],"explanation":"Forward P/E is lower than trailing P/E exactly when expected EPS is higher than last year's EPS.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9b; L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-064","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A quote shows: last $36.00, bid $35.96, ask $36.04 and a market capitalisation of $243,000,000. How many shares are outstanding?","options":["6,750,000","8,748,000,000","6,757,508","6,742,508"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shares = market cap ÷ last price = 243,000,000 ÷ 36.00 = 6,750,000. Using the bid (6,757,508) or the ask (6,742,508) is the classic quote-screen slip, and multiplying instead of dividing gives a nonsense total.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-065","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A screen shows a last price of $48.30, a trailing P/E of 21.0 and a consensus forward P/E of 18.5. What were the company's trailing earnings per share?","options":["$1,014.30","$0.43","$2.61","$2.30"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Trailing EPS = price ÷ trailing P/E = 48.30 ÷ 21.0 = $2.30. Using the forward P/E gives next year's expected EPS ($2.61), multiplying gives $1,014.30, and inverting gives $0.43.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; SM 8-22"},{"id":"fin201-q08-066","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A screen shows a last price of $52.70, a trailing P/E of 23.5 and a consensus forward P/E of 19.8. What are the analysts' forecast earnings per share for the coming year?","options":["$2.24","$0.38","$2.66","$1,043.46"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forward EPS = price ÷ forward P/E = 52.70 ÷ 19.8 = $2.66. Dividing by the trailing P/E gives last year's EPS ($2.24), which is the trailing-forward mix-up; inverting the ratio gives $0.38; multiplying by the P/E gives $1,043.46.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; L04 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-067","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a quote screen, 'Price/Earnings' (the trailing P/E) is the price divided by:","options":["analysts' forecast earnings per share for next year","earnings per share over the last twelve months","book equity per share","dividends per share paid last year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trailing P/E uses past earnings; the consensus forward P/E uses forecast earnings. The dividend yield uses dividends, and price/book uses book equity.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14-15"},{"id":"fin201-q08-068","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A quote screen shows a last price, a bid and an ask. Which statement is correct?","options":["The bid is the price a dealer sells at, and the ask is the price a dealer buys at.","The last price is the latest trade, and the ask is never below the bid.","The last price is always the average of the bid and the ask at that moment.","Market capitalisation is calculated from the ask price rather than the last price."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bid < ask is the dealer's spread. A dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask. Market cap and the ratios on the screen use the last price.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-069","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"From a quote's last price, market capitalisation and trailing P/E alone you can find all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["the number of shares outstanding","trailing earnings per share","the company's trailing net income","next year's dividend per share"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Shares = market cap ÷ price, EPS = price ÷ P/E, and net income = EPS × shares. A dividend needs a dividend yield (or payout ratio), which these three items do not give.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-070","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is a growing company's consensus forward P/E normally lower than its trailing P/E?","options":["Because forward P/E ignores the price and is based only on forecast earnings.","Because the trailing P/E includes dividends in its numerator and the forward P/E does not.","The price is the same, but forecast earnings exceed last year's, so the denominator is larger.","Because analysts always discount their forecasts back to today at the cost of equity."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both ratios share today's price. Forward P/E = P/EPS1 and trailing P/E = P/EPS0, so EPS1 > EPS0 gives a lower forward multiple.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14, s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-071","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A screen shows a trailing P/E of 24.0 and a consensus forward P/E of 25.5. What does this imply?","options":["Earnings are expected to fall about 5.9% this year.","Earnings are expected to be unchanged.","The stock is overvalued by 1.5 P/E points.","Earnings are expected to grow about 6.3% this year."],"answer":0,"explanation":"EPS1/EPS0 = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E = 24.0/25.5 = 0.941, so expected EPS is 5.9% lower. A forward multiple above the trailing one signals falling earnings.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9b"},{"id":"fin201-q08-072","concept":"fin201-c08-quote-screen","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a quote screen, the bid price is normally higher than the ask price, because buyers compete to pay more.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask, and the spread (ask minus bid) is the dealer's profit, so the ask is at or above the bid.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-073","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Shareholders of a corporation can replace its senior managers by electing different directors; lenders cannot. Which difference between equity and debt is this?","options":["Voting rights","Predictability of income","Risk level","Taxation of interest"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equity holders vote for the board, which hires and fires management. Debt holders normally have no vote while the firm pays them.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3; TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-074","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts interest on debt with dividends on equity?","options":["Both are discretionary payments, but only interest is tax-deductible to the firm.","Interest is discretionary and paid from after-tax profit; dividends are contractual and deductible.","Interest is contractual and tax-deductible to the firm; dividends are discretionary and paid from after-tax profit.","Both are contractual obligations, but only dividends are tax-deductible to the firm."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Two of the professor's four differences are predictability of income and taxation of interest: interest is promised and reduces taxable income, dividends are declared by the board and are not deductible.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3; TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-075","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"All of the following are differences between debt and equity EXCEPT:","options":["Shareholders normally vote; lenders do not.","Interest is tax-deductible for the firm; dividends are not.","Lenders are paid before shareholders in a liquidation.","Debt is riskier to the investor than equity."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Debt is the safer claim: contractual payments and priority in liquidation. Equity is the residual and riskier claim, which is why it must offer a higher expected return.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-076","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do shareholders require a higher expected return than lenders to the same firm?","options":["Because shareholders receive their payments sooner than lenders do.","Their claim is residual and their income uncertain, while lenders have contractual payments and priority.","Because lenders can vote for directors and shareholders cannot, so shareholders carry more risk.","Because dividends are taxed at a higher rate than interest in the hands of the firm."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Higher risk requires a higher expected return. Shareholders are paid only after the creditors and only if the board declares a dividend.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3; TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-077","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"If a firm skips a dividend on its common shares, it is in default and may be forced into bankruptcy.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An undeclared dividend is not a liability; shareholders cannot force payment. Missing an interest payment on debt is a default.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-078","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dividends paid to shareholders reduce the corporation's taxable income.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dividends are paid out of after-tax profit and are not a deductible expense. Interest on debt is deductible.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-079","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm pays $3 million of interest to its lenders and $3 million of dividends to its shareholders. Which payment reduces the firm's corporate income tax?","options":["The interest, because it is deductible","Neither","Both equally","The dividends, because they are a distribution of profit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Taxation of interest is a key difference: interest is a deductible cost of financing; dividends come out of profit that has already been taxed.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-080","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retiree wants the most predictable stream of cash from a company. Which claim is best suited?","options":["Its common shares, because the price always rises","Its bonds, because interest is contractual","Its common shares, because dividends are fixed by law","Either; both promise a fixed payment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Predictability of income is a debt-versus-equity difference: interest is a promise, dividends are at the board's discretion and can be cut or raised.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-081","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lenders to a firm earn 6% on its loans, while its shareholders expect 12% on their shares. What does the difference mainly reflect?","options":["Shareholders being paid before lenders","The higher tax paid by lenders","Lenders having voting rights","The higher risk of the residual equity claim"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Lower-risk contractual claims require lower returns; the riskier residual claim requires more.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-082","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Interest is tax-deductible to the firm, whereas dividends are paid out of _____ profit.","options":["pre-tax","operating","after-tax","gross"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dividends are a distribution of profit that has already been taxed, so they do not reduce the firm's tax.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-083","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Common stock has no maturity date, whereas debt normally does.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A share is a claim on the firm's cash flows indefinitely, which is why equity is valued as a perpetuity or growing perpetuity. Debt must be repaid at maturity.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-084","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which security is the residual claimant, paid only after all contractual claims are satisfied?","options":["A bond","Accounts payable to suppliers","Common stock","A bank loan"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Common shareholders receive what remains after creditors and lenders are paid, which makes equity both the riskiest and the highest-return claim.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3; TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-085","concept":"fin201-c08-debt-vs-equity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of these is one of the four differences between debt and equity listed in the lecture recap?","options":["The size of the bid-ask spread","Predictability of income (interest versus dividends)","Whether the firm has a ticker symbol","Choice between primary and secondary markets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The recap lists risk levels, voting rights, predictability of income and taxation of interest.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s3; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-086","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a primary market transaction, who receives the money?","options":["The company issuing the shares","The investor's broker","The stock exchange","The investor selling the shares"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The primary market is where a company sells new securities to investors, so the proceeds go to the company. In the secondary market, shares change hands between investors.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s4; L01 s38"},{"id":"fin201-q08-087","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Maya buys 200 shares of an already-listed company from another investor through her broker. This is a:","options":["initial public offering","seasoned equity offering","primary market transaction","secondary market transaction"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Existing shares trading between investors are secondary-market trades; the company receives no money. Initial public offerings and later share issues are primary-market sales.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-088","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a securities dealer differ from a broker?","options":["A dealer earns a commission on each trade, while a broker earns the bid-ask spread as profit.","A broker holds an inventory and quotes the bid and ask; a dealer simply arranges trades without inventory.","A dealer holds an inventory and quotes a bid and an ask; a broker matches buyers and sellers without inventory.","A broker sets the bid and ask prices for the stock, while a dealer only matches orders at those prices."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dealers make a market and earn the bid-ask spread; brokers act as agents and earn commissions.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-089","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of these is a Canadian stock exchange?","options":["NASDAQ","TSX","ECN","NYSE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The TSX (Toronto Stock Exchange) is Canada's main exchange. The NYSE and NASDAQ are American, and ECNs are electronic trading networks rather than exchanges.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-090","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock is quoted bid $60.31, ask $60.35. An investor buys 1,000 shares at the ask and immediately sells them at the bid. Ignoring commissions, how much does she lose?","options":["$40.00","$0.04","$80.00","$0.00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"She pays 60.35 and receives 60.31 per share, so the round trip costs the spread 0.04 × 1,000 = $40.00. $0.04 is the per-share spread, $80.00 double counts, and $0 wrongly assumes you trade at the last price.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-091","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The bid price is the price at which a dealer is willing to sell a security.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bid is the price the dealer will pay (buy at); the ask is the price at which the dealer sells.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-092","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company receives no money when its shares trade between investors. Why would it still want a good secondary market?","options":["Because the company earns part of the bid-ask spread each time its shares trade between investors.","Because secondary trading cancels part of the company's outstanding debt over time.","Because secondary-market trades set the company's dividend for the following year.","Investors pay more for shares they can sell easily, so the company raises new capital on better terms."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Liquidity lowers investors' required return and supports the share price, which makes later primary-market issues cheaper.","source":"generated","ref":"L01 s41; L04 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-093","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a market order differ from a limit order?","options":["A limit order is always executed before any market order that arrives at the same time.","A market order can only be placed on the NYSE, while limit orders are used on NASDAQ.","A market order trades at once at the best available price; a limit order trades only at its price or better.","A market order fixes the price you will pay; a limit order fixes only the time of execution."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Market orders trade at the lowest ask (buys) or highest bid (sells). Limit orders wait in the book until the price is reached.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s4; L01 s42"},{"id":"fin201-q08-094","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which is NOT a feature of a limit order to buy?","options":["It guarantees immediate execution","It waits in the order book at the limit price","It sets the maximum price you will pay","It may not be filled if the price never falls to the limit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Immediate execution is the feature of a market order. A limit order trades price certainty for execution uncertainty.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-095","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which description fits NASDAQ?","options":["An exchange where only Canadian firms are listed","A computer network of dealers; several market makers compete in each active stock","A website where only individuals, not dealers, post orders","A single trading floor on which one designated person handles every stock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NASDAQ has multiple market makers per active stock and is a dealer network; the NYSE assigns each stock to one designated market maker (DMM).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-096","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The difference between the ask and the bid price is the dealer's _____.","options":["commission","premium","dividend","spread"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dealers buy at the bid and sell at the ask; the spread is their basic source of profit.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-097","concept":"fin201-c08-markets-trading","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bid $24.10, ask $24.18, last trade $24.14, day range $24.02 to $24.22. You place a market order to sell 500 shares. What price per share do you receive?","options":["$24.12, the midpoint of the day's range","$24.18, the ask","$24.14, the last price","$24.10, the bid"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A market sell hits the highest bid. You buy at the ask and sell at the bid, which is the cost of immediacy.","source":"generated","ref":"L01 s42; TB 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-098","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Preferred stock usually has:","options":["a tax-deductible dividend","a dividend that grows with earnings and a vote on every board election","a fixed dividend with priority over common dividends and in liquidation","a maturity date and a promised repayment of principal"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Preferred is a form of equity with a stated dividend, priority over common stock, and often no vote. Its dividends are not deductible and skipping them is not a default.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-099","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A share promises a fixed $4.50 dividend every year forever. The right pricing formula is:","options":["P0 = D/R, a perpetuity","P0 = D1/(1 + R)","P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO","P0 = D1/(R − g), a growing perpetuity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A fixed dividend means zero growth, so the stream is an ordinary perpetuity: P0 = D/R.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-100","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A preferred share pays a fixed annual dividend of $5.25. Investors require 6.2% per annum on such shares. What is the price?","options":["$0.33","$84.68","$89.93","$4.94"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P0 = D/R = 5.25/0.062 = $84.68. Dividing by 1.062 treats it as a one-year payment, multiplying by (1 + R) treats the dividend as growing, and D × R is not a price at all.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-8; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-101","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A preferred share paying a fixed $3.80 a year sells for $62.00. What return does it offer?","options":["6.13%","5.78%","16.32%","6.53%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"R = D/P0 = 3.80/62.00 = 6.13%. Inverting gives 16.32 (that is price over dividend, not a rate), adding the dividend to the price in the denominator gives 5.78%, and subtracting it gives 6.53%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-102","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A preferred share will pay a fixed annual dividend of $2.50, with the first payment exactly 6 years from today. Investors require 5.5% per annum. What is the price today? Answer in $.","answer":34.78,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.50/0.055/1.055^5","steps":[{"label":"Price at year 5, one year before the first dividend: D/R","value":45.4545,"check":"2.50/0.055","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price today: discount 5 years","value":34.78,"check":"2.50/0.055/1.055^5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["A perpetuity formula gives the value one period BEFORE the first payment, which is year 5","P5 = D/R = 2.50/0.055 = 45.4545","P0 = P5/(1 + R)^5 = 45.4545/1.055^5 = $34.78"],"keystrokes":["2.50 ÷ 0.055 = 45.4545","÷ 1.055 y^x 5 = 34.78"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted six years instead of five","value":32.97,"check":"2.50/0.055/1.055^6","explain":"The perpetuity value is at year 5, one year before the first dividend."},{"id":"not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the perpetuity value","value":45.45,"check":"2.50/0.055","explain":"The $45.45 is the price in year 5, not today."}],"explanation":"Same logic as SM 8-21: value the perpetuity one period before the first payment, then discount that value back.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-21; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-103","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a preferred share's dividend differ from a bond's interest?","options":["Neither a preferred dividend nor bond interest is a legal obligation of the firm, so neither can default.","Both are tax-deductible to the firm, but only a missed bond payment is treated as a default by the courts.","A skipped preferred dividend is no default and is not deductible; missed bond interest is a default, and interest is deductible.","A preferred dividend is deductible, and missing it is a default just like missing bond interest would be."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Preferred looks like debt but is equity for legal and tax purposes: the board can defer the dividend, and the firm cannot deduct it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-104","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT typical of preferred stock?","options":["A fixed stated dividend rate set when the shares are issued","Priority over common stock for dividends and in liquidation","No vote in the election of directors in the normal course","Its dividend grows in line with the firm's earnings"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Preferred pays a fixed stated dividend, which is why it is valued as a zero-growth perpetuity. Growth in dividends is a feature of common stock.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-105","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is a preferred share valued with P0 = D/R rather than D1/(R − g)?","options":["Because R equals g for preferred shares, which makes D1/(R − g) undefined.","Its dividend is fixed, so g = 0 and the payments form an ordinary perpetuity.","Because preferred shares carry no risk, so the growth rate is irrelevant.","Because preferred shares are repaid at maturity, so a perpetuity formula is needed."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Set g = 0 in the Gordon growth formula and you get D/R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-8; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-106","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"If a cumulative preferred dividend is skipped, it accumulates as an arrearage that must be paid before common shareholders receive dividends.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cumulative means unpaid dividends are carried forward; common shareholders must wait until the arrears and the current preferred dividend are paid.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-107","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Preferred dividends are tax-deductible to the corporation in the same way as interest on a bond.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Preferred stock is equity for tax purposes: dividends are paid from after-tax profit and are not deductible.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-108","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A preferred share paying a fixed $3.00 a year falls in price from $50 to $40. What does this tell you?","options":["The share has become riskless, so investors accept a lower price.","The company raised its growth rate, which is why the price fell.","The market's required return on the share rose from 6.0% to 7.5%.","The company cut the dividend, since the price of a fixed-dividend share falls only if D falls."],"answer":2,"explanation":"With D fixed at $3.00, price = D/R: $3.00/$50 = 6.0% and $3.00/$40 = 7.5%. A price fall with a fixed dividend means a higher required return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-109","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A '$5 preferred' share has a stated liquidating value of $100. Its dividend yield on stated value is:","options":["$500","5%","20%","0.05%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"$5 per year on $100 of stated value is 5%. The quoted yield on the market price depends on what the share trades for.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-110","concept":"fin201-c08-preferred-stock","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Compared with common stock, preferred stock normally has:","options":["no claim on the firm's assets in a liquidation, ranking behind common holders","dividends whose size is decided by a vote of the shareholders each year","a higher expected growth rate and more voting rights than common stock","less price growth potential but a lower risk of missed dividends"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Preferred holders are paid before common holders, usually without a vote, and the dividend is fixed. That makes preferred less risky but gives it little growth.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-5; TB 8.2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-111","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Your stockbroker advises you to buy into a firm at $4.50 per share because its earnings are $0.33 per share and it should trade at a similar P/E ratio to similar firms. This approach involves:","options":["Comparables","Gordon growth model","DCF","NPV","Cost of capital"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Valuing a firm by applying the P/E ratios of similar firms to its earnings is the comparables (multiples) approach. The Gordon growth model and DCF discount cash flows, and NPV and cost of capital are capital budgeting tools.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-112","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In valuation by comparables using P/E, the share price is estimated as:","options":["benchmark P/E × EPS, with the EPS matching the type of P/E","EPS ÷ benchmark P/E, which converts earnings into a price","benchmark P/E ÷ EPS, because P/E is price divided by earnings","benchmark P/E × dividend per share, because investors are paid dividends"],"answer":0,"explanation":"P = benchmark P/E × EPS follows from P/E = P/EPS. Dividing gives a nonsense units answer, and the dividend per share is not what the multiple is defined on.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-113","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An analyst values a profitable, dividend-free software company at 22 × its forecast earnings per share, where 22 is the average forward P/E of its peers. Which valuation approach is this?","options":["Comparables using a forward P/E","Free cash flow to equity","NPV of growth opportunities","Dividend discount model"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The price comes from a peer multiple times a metric, not from discounting cash flows. Because it uses forecast EPS and a forward P/E, it is a forward comparables valuation.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-114","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A fast-growing start-up has negative earnings and pays no dividends. Which multiple can still be used to value it by comparables?","options":["Price/earnings","Dividend yield","Earnings yield","Price/sales"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Price/sales (sales per share times a benchmark P/S) works when earnings are negative. P/E and the earnings yield are meaningless on losses, and a zero dividend gives no dividend yield.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-115","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do a trailing P/E and a forward P/E differ, and how should each be used?","options":["Trailing P/E uses forecast EPS and forward uses last year's EPS; apply each to the other's EPS.","Trailing P/E uses the last twelve months' EPS, forward uses forecast EPS; apply each to its own EPS.","Trailing P/E uses dividends per share and forward uses earnings per share, so they measure different things.","They are the same ratio; the label depends only on which exchange the stock trades on."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trailing = P/EPS0, forward = P/EPS1. Applying a trailing benchmark to forecast EPS gives a price one year ahead (a target price), not today's price.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-116","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a usual source of a benchmark P/E?","options":["An industry average P/E","The average or median P/E of similar firms","The firm's own required return R","The firm's own historical average P/E"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Benchmarks come from comparable firms, an industry average or the firm's own history. The required return R belongs in a DCF valuation, not in a multiple.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-117","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must a trailing benchmark P/E be applied to trailing EPS and a forward benchmark P/E to forecast EPS?","options":["Because the exam formula sheet requires the same EPS to be used in both places.","Because forecast EPS is always higher than trailing EPS, which would inflate the price.","The multiple was calculated on that earnings basis; mixing them values a different date or stream.","Because P/E ratios are published only once a year for each listed company."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A benchmark of 20 on last year's EPS of $2.00 gives $40 today. The same 20 on next year's EPS of $2.50 gives $50, the expected price one year from today.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-118","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Orchard Tech trades at $12.60 with trailing EPS of $0.90, a P/E of 14. Similar firms trade at an average trailing P/E of 18. What does comparables logic suggest?","options":["Orchard's fair price equals its EPS of $0.90 because the P/E is only a descriptive multiple.","Orchard is fairly priced because P/E ratios never differ across firms in the same industry.","Orchard is overvalued because its P/E of 14 is lower than the peer average P/E of 18.","Orchard looks undervalued: 18 × $0.90 = $16.20 versus $12.60, unless peers have better growth or risk."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A below-average multiple means the market values each dollar of earnings less than it does for peers. That is a buy signal only if the firm really is comparable in growth and risk.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q1; L04 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-119","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Lakeview Foods earned $2.47 per share last year. Similar firms trade at a trailing P/E of 19. What price does comparables give for Lakeview's shares today? Answer in $.","answer":46.93,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"19*2.47","steps":[{"label":"Price = benchmark P/E × EPS","value":46.93,"check":"19*2.47","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["P = benchmark P/E × EPS","P = 19 × $2.47 = $46.93"],"keystrokes":["19 × 2.47 = 46.93"],"errors":[{"id":"eps-division","label":"Divided EPS by the P/E","value":0.13,"check":"2.47/19","explain":"P/E = P/EPS, so P = P/E × EPS; dividing gives $0.13."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Treated the P/E of 19 as 19%","value":0.47,"check":"0.19*2.47","explain":"A P/E of 19 is a multiple (19 times earnings), not a percentage."}],"explanation":"Multiply the benchmark multiple by the matching earnings figure.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-120","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Evergreen Paper earned $3.10 per share last year. Earnings are expected to grow 7% per year, and similar firms trade at a P/E of 16. What target share price do you expect in 3 years? Answer in $.","answer":60.76,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"16*3.10*1.07^3","steps":[{"label":"EPS in 3 years = EPS0(1 + g)^3","value":3.7976,"check":"3.10*1.07^3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Target price P3 = P/E × EPS3","value":60.76,"check":"16*3.10*1.07^3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["EPS_t = EPS0(1 + g)^t","EPS3 = 3.10 × 1.07^3 = 3.7976","P3 = 16 × 3.7976 = $60.76"],"keystrokes":["1.07 y^x 3 × 3.10 = 3.7976","× 16 = 60.76"],"errors":[{"id":"eps0-for-eps1","label":"Applied the P/E to last year's EPS","value":49.6,"check":"16*3.10","explain":"That is today's price on trailing EPS; the target price needs EPS three years out."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Grew EPS two years instead of three","value":56.79,"check":"16*3.10*1.07^2","explain":"Three years of growth means (1 + g)^3."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew EPS four years instead of three","value":65.02,"check":"16*3.10*1.07^4","explain":"Count the years: t = 3, not 4."}],"explanation":"Target price in t years = benchmark P/E × EPS0(1 + g)^t.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-27"},{"id":"fin201-q08-121","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aspen Outdoor Gear lost money last year, so you value it with price/sales. Sales were $2,850,000 with 190,000 shares outstanding, and similar firms trade at a price/sales ratio of 3.3. What price per share do you estimate? Answer in $.","answer":49.5,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"3.3*2850000/190000","steps":[{"label":"Sales per share","value":15.0,"check":"2850000/190000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price = P/S × sales per share","value":49.5,"check":"3.3*2850000/190000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["P = benchmark P/S × sales per share","Sales per share = 2,850,000/190,000 = $15.00","P = 3.3 × 15.00 = $49.50"],"keystrokes":["2,850,000 ÷ 190,000 = 15.00","× 3.3 = 49.50"],"errors":[{"id":"total-for-per-share","label":"Applied P/S to total sales","value":9405000.0,"check":"3.3*2850000","explain":"The multiple is price per share over sales per share; convert to per-share sales first."},{"id":"ratio-inverted","label":"Divided sales per share by P/S","value":4.55,"check":"15/3.3","explain":"Price = P/S × sales per share, not sales per share ÷ P/S."}],"explanation":"Price/sales is used just like P/E: benchmark multiple times the matching per-share metric.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-12"},{"id":"fin201-q08-122","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Marten Logistics' recent year-end prices and annual EPS were: Year 1 $36.40 and $2.00; Year 2 $41.30 and $2.20; Year 3 $45.15 and $2.45; Year 4 $43.70 and $2.60. Using its average historical P/E as the benchmark, and EPS growth of 9% next year, what target price do you expect one year after Year 4? Answer in $.","answer":51.16,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(36.40/2.00+41.30/2.20+45.15/2.45+43.70/2.60)/4*2.60*1.09","steps":[{"label":"Average of the four yearly P/E ratios","value":18.0522,"check":"(36.40/2.00+41.30/2.20+45.15/2.45+43.70/2.60)/4","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Next year's EPS = 2.60 × 1.09","value":2.834,"check":"2.60*1.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Target price = average P/E × EPS1","value":51.16,"check":"(36.40/2.00+41.30/2.20+45.15/2.45+43.70/2.60)/4*2.60*1.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["P/E by year: 18.20, 18.77, 18.43, 16.81","Average P/E = 18.0522","EPS1 = 2.60 × 1.09 = 2.8340","P1 = 18.0522 × 2.8340 = $51.16"],"keystrokes":["36.40 ÷ 2.00 = 18.20; repeat for each year","Average of the four P/E = 18.0522","2.60 × 1.09 = 2.8340","× 18.0522 = 51.16"],"errors":[{"id":"eps0-for-eps1","label":"Used Year 4 EPS instead of next year's","value":46.94,"check":"(36.40/2.00+41.30/2.20+45.15/2.45+43.70/2.60)/4*2.60","explain":"A target price a year ahead needs the EPS of that year, EPS1 = EPS0(1 + g)."},{"id":"latest-pe-only","label":"Used only the Year 4 P/E","value":47.63,"check":"43.70/2.60*2.60*1.09","explain":"The benchmark is the AVERAGE of the yearly P/E ratios, not just the latest one."}],"explanation":"Compute P/E for each year, average them, grow EPS one year, then multiply.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-26"},{"id":"fin201-q08-123","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harbour Transit earned $3.20 per share last year and pays no dividend. Similar firms trade at a P/E of 18, and earnings are expected to grow 6.5% next year. Using the same P/E for both dates, what return do you expect over the year? Answer in % per annum.","answer":6.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(18*3.20*1.065/(18*3.20)-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Price today = 18 × EPS0","value":57.6,"check":"18*3.20","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Target price in one year = 18 × EPS1","value":61.344,"check":"18*3.20*1.065","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Expected return","value":6.5,"check":"(18*3.20*1.065/(18*3.20)-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["P0 = 18 × 3.20 = $57.60","EPS1 = 3.20 × 1.065 = 3.408;  P1 = 18 × 3.408 = $61.344","r = (P1 + 0)/P0 − 1 = 61.344/57.60 − 1 = 6.50%","With no dividend and a constant P/E, the return equals the EPS growth rate."],"keystrokes":["18 × 3.20 = 57.60","18 × 3.20 × 1.065 = 61.344","61.344 ÷ 57.60 − 1 = 0.065"],"errors":[{"id":"eps0-for-eps1","label":"Used last year's EPS for both prices","value":0.0,"check":"(18*3.20/(18*3.20)-1)*100","explain":"If both prices use EPS0 the return is zero: the target price needs next year's EPS."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot the −1","value":106.5,"check":"(18*3.20*1.065/(18*3.20))*100","explain":"1.065 is the growth factor, not the return."}],"explanation":"A constant P/E means price grows with earnings. With no dividend, return = EPS growth (SM 8-25).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-25; L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-124","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bright Bay Software earned $1.80 per share last year and analysts forecast $2.10 for next year. Similar firms trade at an average forward P/E of 17. What price does comparables give today?","options":["$35.70","$30.60","$33.15","$8.10"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A forward multiple belongs with forward EPS: 17 × $2.10 = $35.70. Using last year's EPS (17 × $1.80 = $30.60) is the trailing-forward mix-up, dividing gives $8.10, and averaging the two EPS figures has no basis in the method.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-125","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peers trade at an average trailing P/E of 15.5. Vista Retail earned $3.20 per share last year and is expected to earn $3.60 next year. What price does comparables give today?","options":["$0.21","$49.60","$4.84","$55.80"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A trailing multiple applies to trailing EPS: 15.5 × $3.20 = $49.60. 15.5 × $3.60 = $55.80 applies it to next year's EPS, which gives next year's price, and the other two invert the formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-126","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A broker values a company by applying the industry's trailing P/E of 20 to this year's expected EPS of $2.50 (last year's EPS was $2.00) and gets $50.00. What does the $50.00 represent?","options":["Today's fair price, because any P/E times any EPS gives today's price","A target price one year from today, because a trailing multiple on next year's EPS prices next year","The average of today's price and next year's price, since two EPS figures are involved","The value per share if earnings did not grow at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A trailing benchmark applied to next year's EPS is a target price one year from now: 20 × $2.50 = $50.00. Today's price on trailing EPS would be 20 × $2.00 = $40.00.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-127","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A benchmark based on forward P/E ratios should be applied to the firm's trailing earnings per share.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A forward multiple was calculated on forecast EPS, so apply it to forecast EPS (EPS1).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-128","concept":"fin201-c08-pe-comparables","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Price = benchmark P/E × _____ (matching the type of P/E).","options":["book value per share","dividends per share","earnings per share","sales per share"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P = benchmark P/E × EPS; with a price/sales benchmark you use sales per share instead.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-129","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Enterprise value (EV) equals:","options":["market value of equity − debt + cash","book value of equity + debt","market value of equity + debt + cash","market value of equity + debt − cash"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EV is the value of the operating business to all its investors. Rearranged, Equity = EV − Debt + Cash, the formula-sheet version.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-130","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An analyst applies the peers' average EV/EBITDA of 9.0 to a firm's EBITDA of $30 million and gets $270 million. This $270 million is the firm's:","options":["enterprise value (EV)","book value of the assets","market value of equity","liquidation value of the assets"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An EV multiple times EBITDA gives EV. To reach equity you subtract debt and add cash, then divide by shares for a price.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-131","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does an EV/EBITDA valuation differ from a P/E valuation?","options":["EV/EBITDA values the whole business and needs Equity = EV − Debt + Cash; P/E gives equity directly.","EV/EBITDA gives the equity value directly; P/E values the whole business including debt.","Both ratios give enterprise value, but P/E uses a different discount rate.","EV/EBITDA is based on net income, while P/E is based on EBITDA before interest."],"answer":0,"explanation":"P/E is an equity multiple (price over earnings to shareholders). EV/EBITDA is a firm-level multiple measured before interest, so debt and cash must be handled afterwards.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-132","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which item is NOT needed to move from an EV multiple valuation to an equity value per share?","options":["Debt","The firm's tax rate","Cash","Shares outstanding"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash, then divide by shares outstanding. A tax rate is not part of that bridge.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-133","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is EV/EBITDA better than P/E for comparing firms with very different debt levels?","options":["Because P/E cannot be calculated at all for firms that have borrowed money.","Because EV ignores debt altogether, so leverage cannot distort it.","EV and EBITDA sit before the split between lenders and shareholders, so financing does not affect the ratio.","Because EBITDA is always larger than net income, so the ratio is more stable."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Interest changes net income and the equity value but not EBITDA or EV, so EV/EBITDA is not distorted by how the firm is financed.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-134","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Peers trade at an average EV/EBITDA of 8.5. Kestrel Rail has EBITDA of $24.0 million, debt of $58.0 million, cash of $9.5 million and 10.0 million shares. What share price does this multiple imply? Answer in $.","answer":15.55,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(8.5*24.0-58.0+9.5)/10.0","steps":[{"label":"Enterprise value ($ million)","value":204.0,"check":"8.5*24.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Equity value ($ million)","value":155.5,"check":"8.5*24.0-58.0+9.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Price per share","value":15.55,"check":"(8.5*24.0-58.0+9.5)/10.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["EV = 8.5 × 24.0 = $204.0 million","Equity = EV − Debt + Cash = 204.0 − 58.0 + 9.5 = $155.5 million","Price = 155.5/10.0 = $15.55"],"keystrokes":["8.5 × 24 = 204.0","− 58 + 9.5 = 155.5","÷ 10 = 15.55"],"errors":[{"id":"ev-as-equity","label":"Divided EV by shares","value":20.4,"check":"8.5*24.0/10.0","explain":"The multiple gives EV; debt and cash must be adjusted before dividing by shares."},{"id":"cash-sign","label":"Subtracted cash instead of adding it","value":13.65,"check":"(8.5*24.0-58.0-9.5)/10.0","explain":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash: cash belongs to the owners and is added."},{"id":"debt-ignored","label":"Ignored debt but added cash","value":21.35,"check":"(8.5*24.0+9.5)/10.0","explain":"Lenders have a prior claim on EV, so debt is subtracted."}],"explanation":"EV multiple → EV → Equity = EV − Debt + Cash → ÷ shares.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-11; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-135","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ridgeline Power has a market capitalisation of $412 million, debt of $150 million and cash of $35 million. Its EBITDA last year was $52.7 million. What EV/EBITDA multiple does it trade at? Answer as a multiple (x).","answer":10.0,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(412+150-35)/52.7","steps":[{"label":"Enterprise value ($ million)","value":527.0,"check":"412+150-35","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"EV/EBITDA","value":10.0,"check":"(412+150-35)/52.7","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["EV = market cap + debt − cash = 412 + 150 − 35 = $527 million","EV/EBITDA = 527/52.7 = 10.00x"],"keystrokes":["412 + 150 − 35 = 527","÷ 52.7 = 10.00"],"errors":[{"id":"cash-ignored","label":"Forgot to subtract cash","value":10.66,"check":"(412+150)/52.7","explain":"EV nets cash against debt."},{"id":"mcap-only","label":"Used market cap alone","value":7.82,"check":"412/52.7","explain":"Market cap is the equity value only; EBITDA belongs to all investors, so use EV."},{"id":"cash-sign","label":"Added cash","value":11.33,"check":"(412+150+35)/52.7","explain":"EV = equity + debt − cash."}],"explanation":"Match the numerator to the metric: EBITDA is before interest, so divide EV, not market cap.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-136","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Highland Telecom has EBITDA of $18.0 million, debt of $20.0 million, cash of $31.0 million (a net cash position) and 7.0 million shares. Peers trade at EV/EBITDA of 7.0. What is the implied value per share? Answer in $.","answer":19.57,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(7.0*18.0-20.0+31.0)/7.0","steps":[{"label":"Enterprise value ($ million)","value":126.0,"check":"7.0*18.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Equity value ($ million)","value":137.0,"check":"7.0*18.0-20.0+31.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Value per share","value":19.57,"check":"(7.0*18.0-20.0+31.0)/7.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["EV = 7.0 × 18.0 = $126.0 million","Equity = EV − Debt + Cash = 126.0 − 20.0 + 31.0 = $137.0 million","Per share = 137.0/7.0 = $19.57"],"keystrokes":["7 × 18 = 126","126 − 20 + 31 = 137","137 ÷ 7 = 19.57"],"errors":[{"id":"cash-sign","label":"Subtracted cash and added debt","value":16.43,"check":"(7.0*18.0-31.0+20.0)/7.0","explain":"A net cash position increases equity value above EV."},{"id":"ev-as-equity","label":"Divided EV by shares","value":18.0,"check":"7.0*18.0/7.0","explain":"EV is not equity; adjust for debt and cash first."}],"explanation":"With net cash the equity is worth MORE than EV: Equity = EV − Debt + Cash.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2; L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-137","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peers trade at an average EV/EBITDA of 9.0. A firm has EBITDA of $30 million, debt of $85 million and cash of $12 million. What is the implied value of its equity?","options":["$173 million","$270 million","$343 million","$197 million"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EV = 9.0 × 30 = $270 million; Equity = 270 − 85 + 12 = $197 million. $270 million treats EV as equity, $173 million subtracts the cash, and $343 million adds the debt.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-138","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar Foods has a market capitalisation of $640 million, debt of $210 million and cash of $55 million. What is its enterprise value?","options":["$640 million","$905 million","$795 million","$485 million"],"answer":2,"explanation":"EV = market value of equity + debt − cash = 640 + 210 − 55 = $795 million. $485 million reverses both signs, $905 million adds the cash, and $640 million is only the equity.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-139","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm trades at an EV/EBITDA of 12 while its peers trade at about 8. What is the most sensible reading?","options":["It has less debt than its peers, which is the only reason its multiple could be higher.","Its EBITDA must be negative, because any multiple above the peer average signals operating losses.","It must be undervalued, because a multiple of 12 is a higher number than a multiple of 8.","The market pays more per dollar of operating profit, perhaps for higher growth or lower risk, or the shares are rich."],"answer":3,"explanation":"A multiple above the peer average means a higher price per dollar of EBITDA. Comparables logic asks whether growth and risk justify it.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18, s32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-140","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Equity value = Enterprise value − Debt + Cash.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is the formula-sheet relation. Debt holders have a claim on EV, and cash is the owners' to keep.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-141","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dividing an EV/EBITDA-based enterprise value by the number of shares gives the right price per share only if the firm's debt and cash happen to be equal, for example both zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash equals EV only when debt equals cash. Otherwise the bridge must be made before dividing by shares.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-142","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Equity = EV − Debt + _____.","options":["Cash","Capital expenditure","Interest","EBITDA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cash is added back because enterprise value is measured net of cash.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-143","concept":"fin201-c08-ev-multiples","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"You value a firm with an EV/EBITDA multiple and then want a share price. Which sequence is correct?","options":["Price = multiple × EBITDA ÷ shares","EV = multiple × EBITDA; Equity = EV − Debt + Cash; price = Equity ÷ shares","Equity = multiple × EBITDA; EV = Equity − Debt + Cash; price = EV ÷ shares","EV = multiple × net income; price = EV ÷ shares"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The multiple is applied to EBITDA to get EV, then the bridge to equity, then per share.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-144","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why should two firms in the same industry trade at similar P/E multiples?","options":["Because exchanges set the P/E ratio for each industry and publish it as a benchmark.","Because firms in the same industry report the same earnings per share each year.","Because dividends per share are identical for all firms within an industry.","P/E depends on growth, plowback and risk, and firms in one industry tend to share these."],"answer":3,"explanation":"From P0 = (1 − b)EPS1/(R − g), P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g). Similar g, ROE (hence b) and risk (hence R) mean similar multiples.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-145","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a firm with a constant plowback b, growth rate g and required return R, the forward P/E (P0/EPS1) equals:","options":["(1 − b)/(R − g)","b/(R − g)","(1 − b)/g","(1 − b)/(R + g)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"P0 = D1/(R − g) and D1 = (1 − b)EPS1, so P0/EPS1 = (1 − b)/(R − g). Using b instead of the payout 1 − b is the plowback-for-payout error.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-146","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"All else equal, which change would LOWER a firm's forward P/E?","options":["A higher growth rate g","A higher required return R","A higher payout ratio, with g held constant","A lower required return R"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g). A higher R widens the denominator and lowers the multiple. Higher g, lower R or higher payout (with g constant) raise it.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s31"},{"id":"fin201-q08-147","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an assumption behind 'similar firms should have similar P/E multiples'?","options":["Similar ROE and therefore similar plowback ratios","Similar risk, so similar required returns on equity","They pay identical dividends per share","Similar growth rates in earnings and dividends"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The logic uses growth, ROE/plowback and risk (cost of equity). Dividends per share depend on firm size and need not match.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-148","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm plows back 40% of earnings and expects to grow at 5% per year. Investors require 11%. What is its forward P/E?","options":["10.00","6.67","3.75","16.67"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g) = 0.60/0.06 = 10.0. Using b in the numerator gives 6.67 (payout-as-plowback slip), R + g gives 3.75, and ignoring the payout gives 16.67.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-149","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Firm B plows back 30% of earnings and grows 6% per year; investors require 10%. What is B's forward P/E?","options":["7.00","4.38","17.50","7.50"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g) = 0.70/0.04 = 17.5. 7.50 uses b instead of 1 − b, 4.38 adds g, and 7.00 ignores growth.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-150","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can a small error in the benchmark P/E cause a large error in an estimated share price?","options":["Because P/E ratios are measured in percent, so a small change is large in dollars.","Because the error compounds each year the benchmark is applied to later earnings.","Because EPS is always an estimate, so any error in P/E is multiplied by the forecast error.","Price = P/E × EPS, so each point of P/E is worth EPS dollars; 2 points on a P/E of 18 is an 11% price error."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The critical thinking question in the text points to this: the hardest part of price-ratio analysis is choosing the correct benchmark.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8 CTQ 12"},{"id":"fin201-q08-151","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two firms in the same industry have forward P/E ratios of 25 and 12. Which explanation fits the P/E formula (1 − b)/(R − g)?","options":["The two firms must have the same EPS, so the price gap is the whole story.","The first has higher expected growth or lower risk than the second.","The second pays no dividends, which pushes its P/E down mechanically.","The first must be overvalued, whatever the reasons for the gap."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A big gap in multiples signals a difference in g, R or payout. It is a reason to question whether the firms are truly comparable.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s31-32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-152","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"All else equal, higher expected growth, lower risk or a higher payout ratio (with growth unchanged) tend to produce a higher forward P/E.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g): g up, R down or b down all raise the ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s31"},{"id":"fin201-q08-153","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"You value a fast-growing biotech firm using the average P/E of mature utilities. What is the likely problem?","options":["There is no problem, because P/E ratios are the same in every industry.","The utilities' multiple overstates growth, so the biotech is overvalued.","The utilities' multiple reflects low growth, so it would probably undervalue the biotech.","Utilities have no earnings, so their P/E ratios cannot be calculated."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Comparables only work when growth, ROE and risk are similar. A high-growth firm deserves a higher multiple than a mature one.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-154","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which three characteristics must similar firms share for the comparables approach to work well?","options":["Growth rate, ROE (hence plowback) and risk (cost of equity)","Dividend per share, EPS and the share price itself","Book value, total assets and sales in the latest year","Name, age and country of incorporation of the firm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"These are the three drivers in the forward P/E formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q08-155","concept":"fin201-c08-why-comparables","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does comparables valuation differ from DCF valuation?","options":["Comparables use only dividends, while DCF uses only earnings per share.","Comparables price a stock off similar firms' multiples; DCF discounts its own forecast cash flows.","Comparables discount forecast cash flows at R; DCF applies multiples to earnings.","They are two names for the same method, so only the labels differ between them."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Relative valuation (multiples) versus intrinsic valuation (DDM, FCF, FCFE).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s17, s36"},{"id":"fin201-q08-156","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock pays the same dividend D every year forever. Its price today is:","options":["P0 = D × R","P0 = D/(1 + R)","P0 = D/(R − g) with g = R","P0 = D/R"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A level payment forever is an ordinary perpetuity, valued at D/R, the Gordon growth formula with g = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-157","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Paradise Prototyping pays a $10 dividend each year and its policy is to continue it indefinitely. Which model values the share?","options":["NPVGO","Perpetuity (zero-growth DDM)","Two-stage growth","Growing perpetuity with g > 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With a constant dividend the share is an ordinary perpetuity, P0 = D/R, like a preferred share.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-158","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Lakeshore Cement has a policy of paying a fixed dividend of $2.75 per share every year, and investors require 8.5% per annum. What is the share price today? Answer in $.","answer":32.35,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.75/0.085","steps":[{"label":"Price P0 = D/R","value":32.35,"check":"2.75/0.085","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Constant dividend, so P0 = D/R","P0 = 2.75/0.085 = $32.35"],"keystrokes":["2.75 ÷ 0.085 = 32.35"],"errors":[{"id":"rate-as-percent","label":"Used R = 8.5 instead of 0.085","value":0.32,"check":"2.75/8.5","explain":"Put the rate in decimals (0.085) before dividing."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Added a growth factor of (1 + R)","value":35.1,"check":"2.75*1.085/0.085","explain":"A fixed dividend does not grow, so there is no (1 + g) term."}],"explanation":"A constant dividend is an ordinary perpetuity: divide by R.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-159","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A utility share that pays a fixed annual dividend of $2.40 is priced at $31.50. What return does it offer? Answer in % per annum.","answer":7.62,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.40/31.50*100","steps":[{"label":"R = D/P0","value":7.619,"check":"2.40/31.50*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["P0 = D/R  →  R = D/P0","R = 2.40/31.50 = 7.6190% ≈ 7.62% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2.40 ÷ 31.50 = 0.0762"],"errors":[{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Divided price by dividend","value":13.13,"check":"31.50/2.40","explain":"13.13 is the price-to-dividend multiple, not a rate."},{"id":"div-by-p1","label":"Divided by price plus dividend","value":7.08,"check":"2.40/(31.50+2.40)*100","explain":"The denominator is today's price P0."}],"explanation":"With no growth, the whole return is the dividend yield: R = D/P0.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-8; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-160","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A mature firm pays a fixed dividend of $1.80 per share each year, required return 7.5% per annum. What do you expect the share price to be in 5 years? Answer in $.","answer":24.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.80/0.075","steps":[{"label":"Price today D/R","value":24.0,"check":"1.80/0.075","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price in 5 years (same)","value":24.0,"check":"1.80/0.075","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Each year the remaining dividends are the same perpetuity, so P5 = D/R","P5 = 1.80/0.075 = $24.00, the same as today"],"keystrokes":["1.80 ÷ 0.075 = 24.00"],"errors":[{"id":"grew-at-r","label":"Grew the price at R for 5 years","value":34.46,"check":"1.80/0.075*1.075^5","explain":"With no dividend growth there is no capital gain, so the price does not grow."},{"id":"discounted-twice","label":"Discounted the price for 5 years","value":16.72,"check":"1.80/0.075/1.075^5","explain":"The price at year 5 is a value at year 5; no discounting is needed."}],"explanation":"With g = 0 the capital gain yield is 0, so the price stays at D/R forever.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-161","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A new class of shares will pay a fixed dividend of $2.00 per year, with the first dividend exactly 4 years from today. Required return 8% per annum. What is the price today? Answer in $.","answer":19.85,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.00/0.08/1.08^3","steps":[{"label":"Price at year 3: D/R","value":25.0,"check":"2.00/0.08","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price today: discount 3 years","value":19.85,"check":"2.00/0.08/1.08^3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["The perpetuity formula values the stream one period before the first payment, i.e. at year 3","P3 = 2.00/0.08 = $25.00","P0 = P3/1.08^3 = $19.85"],"keystrokes":["2.00 ÷ 0.08 = 25.00","÷ 1.08 y^x 3 = 19.85"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted 4 years","value":18.38,"check":"2.00/0.08/1.08^4","explain":"The first payment is in year 4, so the perpetuity value sits at year 3."},{"id":"not-discounted","label":"Did not discount","value":25.0,"check":"2.00/0.08","explain":"$25.00 is the value at year 3, not today."}],"explanation":"Value one period before the first payment, then discount back.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-14, 8-21"},{"id":"fin201-q08-162","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company pays a fixed dividend of $4.20 per share every year. Investors require 10.5% per annum. What is the share price?","options":["$40.00","$0.44","$44.20","$3.80"],"answer":0,"explanation":"P0 = D/R = 4.20/0.105 = $40.00. Dividing by 1.105 treats it as a one-year payment, multiplying by (1 + R) adds growth that is not there, and D × R is meaningless.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-163","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A share paying a fixed $4.48 per year trades at $64.00. What is its required return?","options":["6.54%","14.29%","7.00%","7.53%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"R = D/P0 = 4.48/64.00 = 7.00%. 6.54% divides by price plus dividend, 7.53% divides by price minus dividend, and 14.29% is the price-to-dividend ratio (not a rate).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-164","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the constant-dividend model relate to the Gordon growth model?","options":["It applies only to bonds.","It uses D1 and the Gordon model uses D0.","It is the Gordon growth model with g = R.","It is the Gordon growth model with g = 0."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Setting g = 0 in P0 = D1/(R − g) gives P0 = D/R.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q08-165","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does a constant-dividend stock have a zero capital gain yield, with all of R coming from the dividend yield?","options":["The price D/R never changes, so the only return is the dividend: D/P0 = R.","Because constant dividends are exempt from tax, so no capital gain arises.","Because the share price falls each year by the amount of the dividend.","Because the dividends are reinvested, so no gain shows up in the price."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With g = 0 the price is constant, so R = D1/P0 + 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-166","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT be valued as a constant-dividend perpetuity?","options":["A common share whose policy is a fixed annual dividend","A share promising $5 a year with no end date","A common share whose dividend grows 3% a year","A preferred share with a fixed dividend"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A steady 3% growth makes it a growing perpetuity (Gordon growth). The others are level, indefinite payments.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-167","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a constant-dividend perpetuity, the price is expected to rise at the rate R each year.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P1 = D/R = P0 since there is no growth in the dividend; the whole return R is earned as dividend yield, with no price change.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-168","concept":"fin201-c08-constant-dividend","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A share paying a fixed $3.20 a year falls from $40 to $32. What happened to the market's required return?","options":["It cannot be determined.","It stayed at 8%.","It fell from 10% to 8%.","It rose from 8% to 10%."],"answer":3,"explanation":"R = D/P0: 3.20/40 = 8% and 3.20/32 = 10%. With the dividend fixed, a lower price means a higher required return.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-169","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Gordon growth model for a stock whose dividend grows at a constant rate g forever is:","options":["P0 = D1/(R + g)","P0 = D1/(R − g)","P0 = D0/(R − g)","P0 = D1/R × (1 + g)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The price is the next dividend over R − g, valid for g < R. D1 = D0(1 + g) when D0 was just paid.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19-20; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-170","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which wording tells you that the dividend given is D0 (so it must be grown once) rather than D1?","options":["The dividend expected in one year is $2.00","The company will pay $2.00 next year","The company has just paid a dividend of $2.00","The next dividend will be $2.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"'Just paid' is today's dividend D0; D1 = D0(1 + g). The other phrases already give D1.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s21; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-171","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the Gordon growth model, the price at time t is:","options":["Pt = D_{t+1}/(R − g), using the next dividend","Pt = D_{t+1}/R, since the price at t has no growth","Pt = Dt/(R − g), using the dividend at date t","Pt = D1/(R − g), the same for every date t"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The formula values the stream one period before the first dividend in the numerator, so P4 uses D5.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-172","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT required for the Gordon growth formula to apply?","options":["dividends grow at the same rate forever","g must be positive","the first dividend in the formula is one period away","g must be less than R"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Negative growth is fine (SM 8-19). The requirement is R > g and constant growth forever.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-173","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the share price grow at the dividend growth rate g in the Gordon model?","options":["Because g is a discount rate, so each year's price is discounted up by g.","Because investors raise the required return R by g each year as the firm matures.","Because the dividend yield D1/P0 equals g, so the price must grow at the yield.","Pt = D_{t+1}/(R − g) and D_{t+1} grows at g, so each price is the last one × (1 + g)."],"answer":3,"explanation":"P4 = D5/(R − g) = P0(1 + g)^4. This is why the capital gain yield equals g.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8 CTQ 7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-174","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Using the Gordon formula with R = 8% and g = 9% gives a negative number. What does it mean?","options":["The formula does not apply: perpetual growth cannot exceed R, so re-examine the inputs.","The stock is being given away, since a negative price means investors would be paid to hold it.","The stock is worth the absolute value of the result, since a negative sign is meaningless.","The stock is worth nothing, because the negative value means no dividends will be paid."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If g ≥ R the present value of the dividends is infinite. The model is only valid for g < R.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-175","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Gordon growth model cannot be used when dividends are expected to shrink at a constant percentage rate.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Use a negative g (R − g then becomes R plus the rate of decline). SM 8-19 values a stock with g = −4%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-19"},{"id":"fin201-q08-176","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Gas has just paid a dividend of $1.85 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 3.5% per annum forever, and investors require 10.5% per annum. What is the share price today? Answer in $.","answer":27.35,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.85*1.035/(0.105-0.035)","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1 = D0(1 + g)","value":1.9147,"check":"1.85*1.035","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":27.35,"check":"1.85*1.035/(0.105-0.035)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["D1 = D0(1 + g) = 1.85 × 1.035 = 1.9147","P0 = D1/(R − g) = 1.9147/(0.105 − 0.035) = 1.9147/0.070 = $27.35"],"keystrokes":["1.85 × 1.035 = 1.91475","÷ (0.105 − 0.035) = 27.35"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used D0 instead of D1","value":26.43,"check":"1.85/(0.105-0.035)","explain":"The dividend was just paid; the model needs next year's dividend."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew the dividend twice","value":28.31,"check":"1.85*1.035^2/(0.105-0.035)","explain":"D1 is one step of growth from D0."},{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R","value":13.68,"check":"1.85*1.035/(0.105+0.035)","explain":"The denominator is R minus g."}],"explanation":"Grow the just-paid dividend once, then divide by R − g.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-1; L04 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q08-177","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Red River Bank will pay a dividend of $2.85 per share one year from now. Dividends grow at 5.2% per annum, and the required return is 10.7% per annum. What is the share price today? Answer in $.","answer":51.82,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.85/(0.107-0.052)","steps":[{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":51.82,"check":"2.85/(0.107-0.052)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["The $2.85 is already D1, so do not grow it","P0 = 2.85/(0.107 − 0.052) = 2.85/0.055 = $51.82"],"keystrokes":["0.107 − 0.052 = 0.055","2.85 ÷ 0.055 = 51.82"],"errors":[{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew D1 by (1 + g) again","value":54.51,"check":"2.85*1.052/(0.107-0.052)","explain":"'Next year's dividend' is D1 already."},{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R","value":17.92,"check":"2.85/(0.107+0.052)","explain":"R minus g, not plus."}],"explanation":"When D1 is given, divide it by R − g directly.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-4; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-178","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maple Leaf Brewing has just paid a dividend of $2.20 per share. Dividends grow at 5% per annum and the required return is 12% per annum. What will the share price be in 4 years? Answer in $.","answer":40.11,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.20*1.05/(0.12-0.05)*1.05^4","steps":[{"label":"D1 = D0(1 + g)","value":2.31,"check":"2.20*1.05","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":33.0,"check":"2.20*1.05/(0.12-0.05)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price in 4 years P4 = P0(1 + g)^4","value":40.11,"check":"2.20*1.05/(0.12-0.05)*1.05^4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["D1 = 2.20 × 1.05 = 2.31","P0 = D1/(R − g) = 2.31/0.07 = 33.00","P4 = P0(1 + g)^4 = 33.00 × 1.05^4 = $40.11","Check: D5 = 2.20 × 1.05^5 = 2.8078; P4 = D5/(R − g) = 2.8078/0.07 = 40.11"],"keystrokes":["2.20 × 1.05 ÷ 0.07 = 33.00","× 1.05 y^x 4 = 40.11"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D4 instead of D5 for P4","value":38.2,"check":"2.20*1.05^4/(0.12-0.05)","explain":"The price at year 4 uses the dividend one period later, D5."},{"id":"no-price-growth","label":"Gave today's price","value":33.0,"check":"2.20*1.05/(0.12-0.05)","explain":"The price grows at g, so P4 is higher than P0."},{"id":"extra-year-of-growth","label":"Grew five years instead of four","value":42.12,"check":"2.20*1.05/(0.12-0.05)*1.05^5","explain":"Four years of price growth is (1 + g)^4."}],"explanation":"The price at time t grows at g: Pt = P0(1 + g)^t = D_{t+1}/(R − g).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-179","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A declining business has just paid a dividend of $6.80 per share. Dividends are expected to fall 3% per annum forever, and the required return is 9% per annum. What is the share price today? Answer in $.","answer":54.97,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"6.80*(1-0.03)/(0.09+0.03)","steps":[{"label":"D1 = D0(1 − 0.03)","value":6.596,"check":"6.80*(1-0.03)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g), with g = −3%","value":54.97,"check":"6.80*(1-0.03)/(0.09+0.03)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["D1 = 6.80 × (1 − 0.03) = 6.596","R − g = 0.09 − (−0.03) = 0.12","P0 = 6.596/0.12 = $54.97"],"keystrokes":["6.80 × 0.97 = 6.596","÷ (0.09 + 0.03) = 54.97"],"errors":[{"id":"g-sign","label":"Subtracted 3% from R instead of adding","value":109.93,"check":"6.80*0.97/(0.09-0.03)","explain":"g is −3%, so R − g = 0.09 + 0.03 = 0.12."},{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used D0","value":56.67,"check":"6.80/(0.09+0.03)","explain":"Use the next dividend D1 = 6.596."},{"id":"wrong-growth-sign","label":"Grew D0 by +3%","value":58.37,"check":"6.80*1.03/(0.09+0.03)","explain":"Declining dividends shrink: D1 = 6.80 × 0.97."}],"explanation":"Negative growth is entered with its sign: D1 = D0(1 + g) = D0 × 0.97 and R − g = R + 0.03 (SM 8-19).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-19"},{"id":"fin201-q08-180","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock sells for $48.00. Dividends grow at 4% per annum and the required return is 9.5% per annum. What dividend did the company just pay (D0)? Answer in $.","answer":2.54,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"48.00*(0.095-0.04)/1.04","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1 = P0(R − g)","value":2.64,"check":"48.00*(0.095-0.04)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Dividend just paid D0 = D1/(1 + g)","value":2.5385,"check":"48.00*(0.095-0.04)/1.04","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["P0 = D0(1 + g)/(R − g)  →  D0 = P0(R − g)/(1 + g)","D1 = 48.00 × 0.055 = 2.64","D0 = 2.64/1.04 = $2.54"],"keystrokes":["48 × 0.055 = 2.64","÷ 1.04 = 2.54"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Reported D1 as D0","value":2.64,"check":"48.00*(0.095-0.04)","explain":"The Gordon numerator is D1; D0 is D1 ÷ (1 + g)."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Multiplied by (1 + g) instead of dividing","value":2.75,"check":"48.00*(0.095-0.04)*1.04","explain":"D0 is the earlier dividend, so it is smaller than D1."}],"explanation":"Rearrange the Gordon formula: D1 = P0(R − g), then D0 = D1/(1 + g) (SM 8-20).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-181","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Beacon Pipelines has just paid a dividend of $1.60 per share. Dividends will grow 6% per annum and the required return is 13% per annum. What is the share price?","options":["$22.86","$25.68","$8.93","$24.23"],"answer":3,"explanation":"P0 = D0(1 + g)/(R − g) = 1.696/0.07 = $24.23. $22.86 uses D0, $25.68 grows the dividend twice, and $8.93 adds g to R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-182","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"The next dividend of Granite Mutual will be $3.30 per share. Dividends grow 4.5% per annum and the required return is 12.5% per annum. What is the share price?","options":["$43.11","$19.41","$41.25","$26.40"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P0 = D1/(R − g) = 3.30/0.080 = $41.25. $43.11 grows D1 again, $19.41 adds g to R, and $26.40 ignores growth altogether.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-183","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bluewater Utilities has just paid $2.00 per share. Dividends grow at 5% per annum and the required return is 11%. What will the share price be in 3 years?","options":["$38.59","$40.52","$42.54","$35.00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P3 = D4/(R − g) = 2.00 × 1.05^4/0.06 = $40.52 (equal to P0 × 1.05^3). $35.00 is today's price, $38.59 uses D3, and $42.54 uses D5.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-184","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"To find a stock's price at time 6 with the Gordon growth formula, which dividend goes in the numerator?","options":["D1","D6","D5","D7"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The price at time t uses the dividend one period later, D_{t+1}.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-185","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Holding D1 and g fixed, what happens to the Gordon growth price as the required return R rises?","options":["The price falls only when g also falls.","The price falls, and falls sharply when R is close to g.","The price is unchanged.","The price rises because dividends are discounted more."],"answer":1,"explanation":"P0 = D1/(R − g): a larger R widens the denominator. SM 8-9: a small change in R has a dramatic effect on the price.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-186","concept":"fin201-c08-gordon-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Put the steps for a Gordon valuation with a 'just paid' dividend in order.","options":["D1 = D0(1 + g); P0 = D1/(R − g); dividend yield = D1/P0","P0 = D1/R; D1 = D0(1 + g); yield = g","yield = D1/P0; P0 = D1/(R − g); D1 = D0(1 + g)","P0 = D0/(R − g); D1 = P0(1 + g); yield = D1/P0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Grow D0 once to get D1, divide by R − g for the price, then dividend yield = D1/P0.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q08-187","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Rearranging the Gordon growth model gives the required return R as:","options":["R = D0/P0 + g","R = g/P0 + D1","R = D1/P0 + g","R = D1/P0 − g"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P0 = D1/(R − g) rearranges to R = D1/P0 + g. D1/P0 is the dividend yield and g is the capital gain rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-188","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock sells for $20, pays a next dividend of $1 and its dividends grow 10% a year. In R = 5% + 10% = 15%, the 10% is the:","options":["payout ratio, which is 1 − b","return on equity, which drives b × ROE","dividend yield, D1/P0, which is 5%","capital gains yield, equal to g in this model"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In the constant growth model the price grows at g, so the capital gains yield equals the dividend growth rate.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-189","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"For most dividend-paying companies, which part of the required return is typically larger?","options":["They are always equal, since R is split in half","The capital gains yield; dividend yields are rarely over 5%","Neither; R has only one component, the dividend yield","The dividend yield, because dividends are the only cash return"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SM CTQ 6: the capital gains yield is typically larger; for non-payers it is the whole return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8 CTQ 6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-190","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the constant growth model is NOT correct?","options":["The dividend yield equals the dividend growth rate.","The share price grows at the same rate g as the dividends.","The required return is R = D1/P0 + g.","The capital gains yield equals the dividend growth rate g."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The dividend yield D1/P0 = R − g, which is not generally equal to g. The two components together make R.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-191","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the capital gains yield equal to g in the constant growth model?","options":["Because dividends are reinvested in the stock at the rate g each year.","Because R is constant, so the price must rise at R − g.","P0 = D1/(R − g) grows with the dividend, so Pt = P0(1 + g)^t.","Because the dividend yield is zero in the constant growth model."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A price proportional to a dividend growing at g also grows at g.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8 CTQ 7"},{"id":"fin201-q08-192","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock W has a dividend yield of 7% and growth of 10%. Stock X has a dividend yield of 17% and no growth. Both have a required return of 17%. What does this show?","options":["W cannot exist, because its growth rate exceeds its dividend yield.","X is riskier than W because its dividend yield is higher.","The same R can come mostly from price growth (W) or entirely from income (X).","W is worth more than X at any price because its growth rate is higher."],"answer":2,"explanation":"SM 8-30: high-growth stocks have an appreciable capital gains component and small current income; mature, no-growth stocks pay mostly income.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-193","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a stock that pays no dividend, the required return equals its capital gains yield.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With D1 = 0, R = 0 + g: all of the return comes from price growth.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8 CTQ 6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-194","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the constant growth model the dividend yield D1/P0 rises every year as the share price grows.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Since Pt = D_{t+1}/(R − g), the yield D_{t+1}/Pt = R − g in every year: the price and the dividend grow at the same rate g.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-195","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hudson Pipeline will pay a dividend of $2.04 per share next year. The shares trade at $41.50 and dividends are expected to grow 4.6% per annum. What return does the stock offer? Answer in % per annum.","answer":9.52,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(2.04/41.50+0.046)*100","steps":[{"label":"Dividend yield D1/P0","value":4.9157,"check":"2.04/41.50*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Required return R = D1/P0 + g","value":9.5157,"check":"(2.04/41.50+0.046)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Dividend yield = 2.04/41.50 = 4.9157%","R = D1/P0 + g = 4.9157% + 4.6% = 9.5157% ≈ 9.52% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2.04 ÷ 41.50 = 0.04916","+ 0.046 = 0.0952"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-growth","label":"Gave only the dividend yield","value":4.92,"check":"2.04/41.50*100","explain":"R also includes the capital gains yield g."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew D1 by (1 + g) again","value":9.74,"check":"(2.04*1.046/41.50+0.046)*100","explain":"The $2.04 is already next year's dividend."},{"id":"g-as-r","label":"Gave only g","value":4.6,"check":"4.6","explain":"g is just the capital gains part."}],"explanation":"R = D1/P0 + g. Use the NEXT dividend.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-196","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Skyline Rail has just paid a dividend of $1.76 per share. The stock trades at $36.00 and dividends grow 5% per annum. What return does the stock offer? Answer in % per annum.","answer":10.13,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1.76*1.05/36.00+0.05)*100","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1 = D0(1 + g)","value":1.848,"check":"1.76*1.05","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"R = D1/P0 + g","value":10.1333,"check":"(1.76*1.05/36.00+0.05)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["D1 = 1.76 × 1.05 = 1.848","R = 1.848/36.00 + 0.05 = 10.1333% ≈ 10.13% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.76 × 1.05 = 1.848","÷ 36 + 0.05 = 0.10133"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used D0 for the yield","value":9.89,"check":"(1.76/36.00+0.05)*100","explain":"The yield is next year's dividend over today's price."},{"id":"omitted-growth","label":"Left out g","value":5.13,"check":"1.76*1.05/36.00*100","explain":"R has two components."}],"explanation":"Grow the just-paid dividend to D1 before computing the yield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-2; L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-197","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock trades at $45.00 and will pay a dividend of $1.62 next year. Investors require 9.4% per annum. What constant dividend growth rate is implied? Answer in % per annum.","answer":5.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(0.094-1.62/45.00)*100","steps":[{"label":"Dividend yield D1/P0","value":3.6,"check":"1.62/45.00*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Implied growth g = R − D1/P0","value":5.8,"check":"(0.094-1.62/45.00)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["R = D1/P0 + g  →  g = R − D1/P0","Dividend yield = 1.62/45.00 = 3.60%","g = 9.4% − 3.60% = 5.80% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.62 ÷ 45 = 0.036","0.094 − 0.036 = 0.058"],"errors":[{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added the yield to R","value":13.0,"check":"(0.094+1.62/45.00)*100","explain":"g = R minus the yield."},{"id":"g-as-r","label":"Used the yield as g","value":3.6,"check":"1.62/45.00*100","explain":"3.6% is the dividend yield, not the growth rate."}],"explanation":"The capital gains yield is what is left of R after the dividend yield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-3; L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-198","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock sells for $48.00 and has a required return of 12%. Its dividend yield equals its capital gains yield. What dividend did the company just pay (D0)? Answer in $.","answer":2.72,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.06*48.00/1.06","steps":[{"label":"Dividend yield = capital gains yield = R/2","value":6.0,"check":"12/2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Next dividend D1 = yield × P0","value":2.88,"check":"0.06*48.00","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Dividend just paid D0 = D1/(1 + g)","value":2.717,"check":"0.06*48.00/1.06","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Both yields are R/2 = 6%, so g = 6%","D1 = 0.06 × 48.00 = 2.88","D1 = D0(1 + g)  →  D0 = 2.88/1.06 = $2.72"],"keystrokes":["48 × 0.06 = 2.88","÷ 1.06 = 2.72"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Reported D1","value":2.88,"check":"0.06*48.00","explain":"The dividend yield uses D1, the dividend just paid is D0 = D1/(1 + g)."},{"id":"whole-r","label":"Used the whole 12% as the dividend yield","value":5.14,"check":"0.12*48.00/1.12","explain":"The two yields share R equally here."}],"explanation":"Split R into two equal parts, get D1 from the yield, then take out one year of growth.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-199","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Investors require 10.8% per annum on a stock whose dividends grow 4.3% per annum. The next dividend is $2.99 per share. What is the share price? (Find the dividend yield R − g first.) Answer in $.","answer":46.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.99/(0.108-0.043)","steps":[{"label":"Dividend yield = R − g","value":6.5,"check":"(0.108-0.043)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Share price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":46.0,"check":"2.99/(0.108-0.043)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["R = D1/P0 + g  →  dividend yield = R − g = 10.8% − 4.3% = 6.5%","P0 = D1/yield = 2.99/0.065 = $46.00"],"keystrokes":["0.108 − 0.043 = 0.065","2.99 ÷ 0.065 = 46.00"],"errors":[{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R","value":19.8,"check":"2.99/(0.108+0.043)","explain":"The yield is R − g."},{"id":"omitted-growth","label":"Divided by R alone","value":27.69,"check":"2.99/0.108","explain":"Using R as the yield forgets the capital gains part."}],"explanation":"Dividend yield = R − g, so price = D1 ÷ yield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-5; L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-200","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Next year's dividend is $2.46, the share price is $41.00 and dividends grow 5.5% per annum. What is the required return?","options":["11.50%","6.00%","11.83%","−0.50%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"R = D1/P0 + g = 6.00% + 5.50% = 11.50%. 6.00% omits g, 11.83% grows D1 again, and −0.50% subtracts the yield from g.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-201","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock trades at $32.00, will pay $1.28 next year, and its dividends grow 7% per annum. What return does it offer?","options":["3.00%","11.00%","4.00%","7.00%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R = 4.00% + 7.00% = 11.00%. The yield alone (4.00%) or the growth alone (7.00%) is only one part, and 3.00% subtracts them.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-202","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock's required return is 9% and its dividend yield is 1.5%. If the constant growth model holds, expected dividend growth is:","options":["10.5%","9%","1.5%","7.5%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"g = R − dividend yield = 9% − 1.5% = 7.5%.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-203","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"R = D1/P0 + _____.","options":["b","D0","ROE","g"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The second component is the capital gains yield, which equals the dividend growth rate g.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-204","concept":"fin201-c08-required-return-components","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sentence about R = D1/P0 + g is correct?","options":["D1/P0 uses next year's dividend over today's price.","D1/P0 uses today's dividend over next year's price.","g is the dividend yield.","R is the holding period return."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The dividend yield D1/P0 is next year's dividend over today's price; D0 appears only when it must first be grown to D1.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-205","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The growing annuity formula PV = D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N] values:","options":["a dividend growing at g forever, with the first dividend D1","N dividends, the first D1, each growing at g, then stopping","a constant dividend D received for N years and then stopped","the share price expected N years from today, discounted at R"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bracket removes the value of the Gordon stream beyond year N.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-206","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A quarry company will pay dividends that grow 6% each year for exactly 10 years and then liquidate. Which formula values the shares?","options":["NPVGO","The constant-dividend perpetuity","The growing annuity formula","The Gordon growth model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Growing cash flows for a finite number of years are a growing annuity.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q08-207","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the growing annuity relate to the Gordon growth model as N becomes very large with g < R?","options":["The growing annuity approaches zero, since the dividends are finite.","They are unrelated; one is for dividends, the other for bonds.","The bracket approaches 1, so it approaches D1/(R − g).","The growing annuity approaches D1/R, the no-growth perpetuity."],"answer":2,"explanation":"((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N → 0 when g < R, leaving D1/(R − g).","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-208","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the growing annuity formula is NOT correct?","options":["The first cash flow in the formula is D1.","It only works when g is less than R.","The bracket is below 1 when g is less than R.","N is the number of dividends."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The formula works for g > R too (the ratio is above 1 and R − g is negative, so the product is positive). Only the perpetual Gordon model requires g < R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-17, 8-23"},{"id":"fin201-q08-209","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the term [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N] reduce the Gordon value D1/(R − g)?","options":["It removes the effect of dividend growth from the value.","It adds the cost of borrowing needed to fund the dividends.","It converts a yearly growth rate into a monthly growth rate.","It subtracts the present value of the dividends after year N."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Gordon values dividends forever; the growing annuity keeps only the first N.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-210","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm will pay dividends for 8 more years: $1.80 one year from now, then growing 4% per annum, and nothing after year 8. Required return 10% per annum. What is the present value of these dividends? Answer in $.","answer":10.85,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.80/(0.10-0.04)*(1-(1.04/1.10)^8)","steps":[{"label":"Ratio (1 + g)/(1 + R)","value":0.94545,"check":"1.04/1.10","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Bracket 1 − ratio^8","value":0.36155,"check":"1-(1.04/1.10)^8","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"PV of the growing annuity","value":10.85,"check":"1.80/(0.10-0.04)*(1-(1.04/1.10)^8)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["PV = D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N]","D1/(R − g) = 1.80/0.06 = 30.00","((1.04)/(1.10))^8 = 0.63845, so the bracket = 0.36155","PV = 30.00 × 0.36155 = $10.85"],"keystrokes":["1.04 ÷ 1.10 = 0.94545","y^x 8 = 0.63845","1 − 0.63845 = 0.36155","× 30 = 10.85"],"errors":[{"id":"gordon-for-annuity","label":"Used the perpetuity D1/(R − g)","value":30.0,"check":"1.80/(0.10-0.04)","explain":"That values dividends forever; this firm stops after year 8."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used N = 7","value":9.74,"check":"1.80/(0.10-0.04)*(1-(1.04/1.10)^7)","explain":"There are 8 dividends, so N = 8."},{"id":"g-over-r","label":"Used g/R inside the bracket","value":29.98,"check":"1.80/(0.10-0.04)*(1-(0.04/0.10)^8)","explain":"The ratio is (1 + g)/(1 + R), not g/R."}],"explanation":"Growing annuity: D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N].","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1; S4 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-211","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dividends of a growth company are expected to be $2.00 next year and then grow 15% per annum for 6 years in total, with nothing after year 6. Required return 10% per annum. What is the present value of these dividends? Answer in $.","answer":12.23,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.00/(0.10-0.15)*(1-(1.15/1.10)^6)","steps":[{"label":"D1/(R − g) with g > R","value":-40.0,"check":"2.00/(0.10-0.15)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Bracket 1 − ratio^6","value":-0.30566,"check":"1-(1.15/1.10)^6","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"PV of the growing annuity","value":12.23,"check":"2.00/(0.10-0.15)*(1-(1.15/1.10)^6)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["Here g (15%) exceeds R (10%), which is fine for a finite annuity","D1/(R − g) = 2.00/(−0.05) = −40.00","(1.15/1.10)^6 = 1.30566, so the bracket = −0.30566 (negative)","PV = −40.00 × (−0.30566) = $12.23"],"keystrokes":["1.15 ÷ 1.10 = 1.04545","y^x 6 = 1.30566","1 − 1.30566 = −0.30566","× (2 ÷ (0.10 − 0.15)) = 12.23"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used N = 5","value":9.96,"check":"2.00/(0.10-0.15)*(1-(1.15/1.10)^5)","explain":"The stream has 6 dividends."},{"id":"sign-flip","label":"Dropped the negative sign of R − g","value":-12.23,"check":"2.00/0.05*(1-(1.15/1.10)^6)","explain":"Both factors are negative, so the product is positive."}],"explanation":"With g > R both D1/(R − g) and the bracket are negative; the PV is positive (TB Example 8.5, SM 8-23).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-23; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-212","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm being wound down will pay three dividends: $2.00 next year, growing 10% per annum, for years 1 to 3 only. Required return 12% per annum. Use the growing annuity formula to find the present value. Answer in $.","answer":5.26,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.00/(0.12-0.10)*(1-(1.10/1.12)^3)","steps":[{"label":"D1/(R − g)","value":100.0,"check":"2.00/(0.12-0.10)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"Bracket 1 − ratio^3","value":0.05262,"check":"1-(1.10/1.12)^3","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"PV","value":5.26,"check":"2.00/(0.12-0.10)*(1-(1.10/1.12)^3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}}],"solution":["PV = D1/(R − g) × [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N]","D1/(R − g) = 2.00/0.02 = 100.00","Bracket = 1 − (1.10/1.12)^3 = 0.052620","PV = 100 × 0.052620 = $5.26","Check by summing: 2.00/1.12 + 2.20/1.12² + 2.42/1.12³ = $5.26"],"keystrokes":["1.10 ÷ 1.12 = 0.982143","y^x 3 = 0.947380","1 − 0.947380 = 0.052620","× 100 = 5.26"],"errors":[{"id":"gordon-for-annuity","label":"Used the perpetuity","value":100.0,"check":"2.00/(0.12-0.10)","explain":"Three dividends are worth far less than a perpetuity."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used N = 4","value":6.95,"check":"2.00/(0.12-0.10)*(1-(1.10/1.12)^4)","explain":"There are three dividends."}],"explanation":"The formula must equal the sum of the discounted dividends; summing is a good check.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-213","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm will pay dividends for 5 years: $1.50 next year, growing 8% per annum, then nothing. Required return 12% per annum. What is the present value of the dividends?","options":["$6.23","$32.56","$37.50","$5.08"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PV = 1.50/0.04 × [1 − (1.08/1.12)^5] = 37.50 × 0.1663 = $6.23. $37.50 is the perpetuity, $5.08 uses N = 4, and $32.56 uses the ratio g/R instead of (1 + g)/(1 + R).","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-214","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dividends start at $3.00 next year and grow 20% per annum for 4 years only. Required return 10% per annum. What is the present value?","options":["$8.95","$12.49","−$30.00","−$12.49"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With g > R, D1/(R − g) = −30.00 and the bracket is 1 − 1.4163 = −0.4163, so PV = $12.49. Dropping the sign of D1/(R − g) gives −$12.49, stopping at D1/(R − g) gives −$30.00, and N = 3 gives $8.95.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-23"},{"id":"fin201-q08-215","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"The growing annuity formula can only be used when the growth rate g is less than the required return R.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Because the stream is finite, the present value is finite even when g > R (both factors are negative and the product is positive). Only the Gordon perpetuity needs g < R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-17"},{"id":"fin201-q08-216","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put the steps for a growing annuity valuation in order.","options":["Raise g to the power N; subtract R; divide by D1; multiply by 1 + g","Compute D1/(R − g); add (1 + g)/(1 + R); raise the sum to N; divide by N","Multiply by D1/(R − g); identify D1; subtract from 1; form the ratio (1 + g)/(1 + R)","Identify D1, g, R, N; form (1 + g)/(1 + R) and raise it to N; take 1 minus it; multiply by D1/(R − g)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Identify the inputs, form the ratio, raise to N, take 1 minus, then scale by D1/(R − g).","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-217","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm's finite growing annuity of dividends is worth $6.23 per share, while the Gordon growth value of the same dividends continuing forever is $37.50. What does the $31.27 difference represent?","options":["The dividend yield of the share in the last year of the annuity.","The financing cost of paying the dividends in the first N years.","The present value of the dividends after the final year.","The present value of the growth rate g over the N years."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The growing annuity keeps only the first N dividends; Gordon counts the infinite stream. The bracket term [1 − ((1 + g)/(1 + R))^N] strips out the tail.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-218","concept":"fin201-c08-growing-annuity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the growing annuity formula, the term raised to the power N is:","options":["(1 + g)/(1 + R)","g/R","(R − g)/(1 + R)","(1 + R)/(1 + g)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each dividend grows by (1 + g) and is discounted by (1 + R), so the net factor per year is (1 + g)/(1 + R); raising it to N captures the first N years' worth.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-401","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a non-constant growth valuation, the horizon price P_T is:","options":["the present value today of the dividends paid during the supernormal phase","the last supernormal dividend D_T divided by (R − g2)","the value at date T of every dividend from date T+1 onward, found as D_{T+1}/(R − g2)","today's price P0 compounded forward T years at the required return R"],"answer":2,"explanation":"P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) is a value at date T that stands in for the whole infinite stream after T. It is a future dollar amount, so it still has to be discounted T periods. The tempting slip is putting D_T in the numerator: the Gordon formula always needs the NEXT dividend, one period after the date being valued.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19-20; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-402","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Boreal Biotech pays no dividends for five years. Its first dividend, $0.80, arrives at the end of year 6 and then grows 5% per year forever. Using P = D/(R − g), the Gordon formula with that $0.80 in the numerator values the stock at which date?","options":["Year 5","Year 6","Year 0","Year 7"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Gordon formula gives the value one period BEFORE the first dividend in its numerator. The first dividend is D6, so the formula values the stock at the end of year 5 (P5 = D6/(R − g)). Reading it as a year-6 price is the classic horizon-off-by-one error that also leads to discounting one period too many.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s6-13; TB 8.1 Fig 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-403","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Robotics is expected to pay $1.10, $1.90 and $2.60 at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3. From year 4 on the dividend grows 3% per year forever. Which list contains exactly the cash flows to discount back to date 0?","options":["D1, D2, D3 and the horizon price P3 = D4/(R − g)","D1, D2, D3, D4 and the horizon price P3 = D4/(R − g)","D1, D2, D3 and the horizon price P4 = D5/(R − g)","D1, D2 and the horizon price P2 = D3/(R − g)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Constant growth starts with D4, so the horizon price is P3 = D4/(R − g) and it already contains D4 and every later dividend. Adding D4 separately double-counts it. Moving the horizon to P4 or P2 puts the formula one period away from the first constant-growth dividend.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; S4 s6-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-404","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Nova Cedar Mills pays no dividend for three years. Its first dividend, D4 = $0.90, is followed by growth of 7% per year forever, and R = 12% per annum. What is the correct first computation?","options":["P4 = D4/(R − g) = $18.00, a value at the end of year 4","P3 = D4(1 + g)/(R − g) = $19.26, a value at the end of year 3","P0 = D4/(R − g) = $18.00, because the formula gives today's price","P3 = D4/(R − g) = 0.90/(0.12 − 0.07) = $18.00, a value at the end of year 3"],"answer":3,"explanation":"D4 is already the first constant-growth dividend, so it goes straight into the numerator: 0.90/0.05 = $18.00, valued one period earlier, at year 3. Multiplying by (1 + g) again ($19.26) grows the dividend twice; labelling the result P4 or P0 puts the date in the wrong place.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-14; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-405","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does valuing a non-constant-growth stock differ from applying the Gordon model at date 0?","options":["Non-constant growth ignores the irregular early dividends and values only the horizon price at date T","Non-constant growth uses a different required return for every year of the early irregular phase","Non-constant growth uses the supernormal rate g1 in the horizon-price formula, where Gordon uses g2","Gordon values the whole stream at date 0 from D1; non-constant growth applies the formula at date T and discounts back"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Gordon needs one constant g from the very first dividend. When growth changes, the formula only works from the date constant growth begins, so we value the stock there (P_T), then pull P_T and the earlier dividends back with (1 + R)^t. The horizon price uses g2, the long-run rate, never g1.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19-20; S4 s5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-406","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT part of the standard non-constant growth procedure?","options":["Listing the explicit dividends through date T on a timeline of the cash flows","Growing D_T by the supernormal rate g1 to obtain D_{T+1} for the horizon price","Finding the date of the first dividend that grows at the constant rate","Discounting the horizon price back T periods to date 0 along with the dividends"],"answer":1,"explanation":"D_{T+1} is the first dividend of the constant-growth phase, so it is D_T grown at g2, not g1 (horizon-wrong-g). The other three statements are the real steps: timeline, locate the first constant-growth dividend, discount P_T together with the early dividends.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-13; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-407","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can the first-phase growth rate g1 exceed the required return R, while the long-run rate g2 cannot?","options":["Investors apply a lower discount rate to the dividends of the first phase than to later dividends","g1 is a growth rate of earnings per share, while g2 is a growth rate of dividends per share","The Gordon formula only requires g2 to be positive; it does not need to be smaller than R","The first phase has finitely many dividends, so its value is finite; the infinite phase needs g2 < R for P = D/(R − g2)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A finite stream of fast-growing dividends always has a finite value, even when g1 > R. An infinite stream growing at g2 ≥ R would have an infinite or negative value from the formula, which is why the long-run rate must stay below R (SM 8-30 stock Y).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-408","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put the steps of a non-constant growth valuation in the correct order.","options":["Timeline and dividends → D_{T+1} → P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) → discount everything to date 0 and add","Discount the explicit dividends → P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) → find D_{T+1} → add the discounted amounts","P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) → timeline and explicit dividends → discount to date 0 → find D_{T+1}","First constant-growth dividend D_{T+1} → discount to date 0 → P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) → timeline"],"answer":0,"explanation":"You cannot compute P_T until you know which dividend starts constant growth and when it is paid, and you cannot add anything until every piece is a date-0 value. So: timeline, D_{T+1}, horizon price, discount and sum.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-13; MAP A3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-409","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: if the first constant-growth dividend is D6, the horizon price is P5 = D6/(R − g2).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The Gordon formula values the stock one period before the dividend in its numerator, so D6 gives P5. Then P5 is discounted five periods (not six).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-410","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student values a stock whose dividend growth is 18% for five years and 4% thereafter, with R = 12%. Her horizon-price step returns a NEGATIVE number. The most likely cause is that she:","options":["discounted the horizon price one period too many before adding it to the dividends","used the first-phase rate g1 = 18% in R − g, which gives 12% − 18% = −6%","forgot to add the dividends of the first phase to the discounted horizon price","put D5 instead of D6 in the numerator of the Gordon formula"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A negative price from the Gordon formula means R − g < 0, so a growth rate above R was used. The horizon price must use g2 = 4%. The other slips change the size of the answer but cannot flip its sign.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-30; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-411","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cobalt Peak Software will pay its first dividend, $2.40, at the end of year 7. Dividends then grow 6% per year forever and R = 13% per annum. What is the price today?","options":["$14.57","$16.47","$17.46","$18.61"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The first dividend is D7, so P6 = 2.40/(0.13 − 0.06) = $34.2857. Discount six years: 34.2857/1.13^6 = $16.47. Discounting seven years ($14.57) treats P6 as a year-7 value; growing D7 once more ($17.46) double-counts a growth step; discounting five years ($18.61) places the horizon too early.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-14; S4 s6-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-412","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tamarack Solar will pay $1.20, $1.80 and $2.40 at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3. From year 4 the dividend grows 4% per year forever (D4 = D3 × 1.04). R = 11% per annum. What is the price today?","options":["$26.07","$27.79","$29.37","$30.37"],"answer":3,"explanation":"D4 = 2.40 × 1.04 = 2.496, so P3 = 2.496/0.07 = $35.6571. P0 = 1.20/1.11 + 1.80/1.11² + 2.40/1.11³ + 35.6571/1.11³ = 1.0811 + 1.4609 + 1.7549 + 26.0722 = $30.37. Dropping the three dividends gives $26.07; using D3 instead of D4 in the horizon price gives $29.37; discounting P3 four years gives $27.79.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1 Fig 8.1; S4 s6-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-413","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Trillium Trucking just paid a dividend of $1.60. Dividends will grow 25% next year, 18% the year after, 12% in year 3, and 4% per year from year 4 onward. R = 11% per annum. What is the price today?","options":["$31.52","$33.26","$34.36","$36.57"],"answer":2,"explanation":"D1 = 2.0000, D2 = 2.3600, D3 = 2.6432; D4 = D3 × 1.04 = 2.7489 grows at the LONG-RUN 4%, so P3 = 2.7489/0.07 = $39.27. P0 = 1.8018 + 1.9154 + 1.9327 + 28.7142 = $34.36. Growing D4 at 12% (the year-3 rate) inflates the price to $36.57; using D3 in the numerator gives $33.26; discounting P3 four years gives $31.52.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-32; S4 s9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-414","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peace River Foods pays no dividend for the next eight years. Its first dividend, D9 = $4.75, is paid at the end of year 9 and then grows 5.5% per year forever. R = 12.5% per annum. What is the price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":26.45,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"4.75/(0.125-0.055)/1.125**8","steps":[{"label":"Horizon price P8 = D9/(R − g)","value":67.8571,"check":"4.75/(0.125-0.055)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0 = P8/(1 + R)^8","value":26.4469,"check":"4.75/(0.125-0.055)/1.125**8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["No dividends in years 1-8, first dividend D9 = $4.75 at the end of year 9, so the horizon is year 8.","P8 = D9/(R − g) = 4.75/(0.125 − 0.055) = 4.75/0.07 = $67.8571","P0 = P8/(1 + R)^8 = 67.8571/1.125^8 = 67.8571/2.5658 = $26.45"],"keystrokes":["4.75 ÷ (0.125 − 0.055) = 67.8571 (this is P8)","TVM: N = 8, I/Y = 12.5, PMT = 0, FV = 67.8571, CPT PV = −26.45"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted P8 for nine years","value":23.51,"check":"4.75/(0.125-0.055)/1.125**9","explain":"P8 is a year-8 value (one period before D9), so it is discounted eight periods, not nine."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew D9 once more before dividing","value":27.9,"check":"4.75*1.055/(0.125-0.055)/1.125**8","explain":"D9 is already the first constant-growth dividend; multiplying by (1 + g) double-counts a growth step."},{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R in the denominator","value":10.28,"check":"4.75/(0.125+0.055)/1.125**8","explain":"The denominator is R minus g."}],"explanation":"No dividends before year 9, so the Gordon formula applied to D9 gives the year-8 value, which is then discounted eight periods. Discounting nine periods is the classic horizon-off-by-one error.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-14; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-415","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Kayaks will pay $0.60, $1.10 and $1.55 at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3. From year 4 the dividend grows 6% per year forever. R = 10.5% per annum. What is the price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":29.65,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.60/1.105+1.10/1.105**2+1.55/1.105**3+1.55*1.06/(0.105-0.06)/1.105**3","steps":[{"label":"First constant-growth dividend D4 = D3 × 1.06","value":1.643,"check":"1.55*1.06","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P3 = D4/(R − g)","value":36.5111,"check":"1.55*1.06/(0.105-0.06)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of D1, D2, D3","value":2.5927,"check":"0.60/1.105+1.10/1.105**2+1.55/1.105**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the horizon price","value":27.0606,"check":"1.55*1.06/(0.105-0.06)/1.105**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":29.6533,"check":"0.60/1.105+1.10/1.105**2+1.55/1.105**3+1.55*1.06/(0.105-0.06)/1.105**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Constant growth starts with D4, so the horizon is year 3.","D4 = 1.55 × 1.06 = $1.6430; P3 = 1.6430/(0.105 − 0.06) = $36.5111","PV(D1..D3) = 0.60/1.105 + 1.10/1.105² + 1.55/1.105³ = 0.5430 + 0.9009 + 1.1488 = $2.5927","PV(P3) = 36.5111/1.105³ = $27.0606","P0 = 2.5927 + 27.0606 = $29.65"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 0.60, C02 = 1.10, C03 = 1.55 + 36.5111 = 38.0611 (each F = 1)","NPV: I = 10.5, CPT NPV = 29.65"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Left out the year-1 dividend","value":29.11,"check":"1.10/1.105**2+1.55/1.105**3+1.55*1.06/(0.105-0.06)/1.105**3","explain":"Every dividend before the horizon is a separate cash flow; the horizon price does not include D1-D3."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D3 in the horizon numerator","value":28.12,"check":"0.60/1.105+1.10/1.105**2+1.55/1.105**3+1.55/(0.105-0.06)/1.105**3","explain":"The horizon price needs the first constant-growth dividend D4 = D3(1.06), not D3."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted P3 four years","value":27.08,"check":"0.60/1.105+1.10/1.105**2+1.55/1.105**3+1.55*1.06/(0.105-0.06)/1.105**4","explain":"P3 is a year-3 value, so it is discounted three periods."}],"explanation":"Value the stock at year 3 with D4 in the numerator, discount that price and the three early dividends back, and add. The horizon price is by far the largest piece (about 91% of the total).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1 Example; S4 s6-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-416","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nova Cedar Mills pays nothing in years 1 and 2, then $0.90 at the end of year 3 and $1.40 at the end of year 4. From year 5 the dividend grows 5% per year forever. R = 12% per annum. What is the price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":14.88,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.90/1.12**3+(1.40+1.40*1.05/(0.12-0.05))/1.12**4","steps":[{"label":"First constant-growth dividend D5 = D4 × 1.05","value":1.47,"check":"1.40*1.05","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P4 = D5/(R − g)","value":21.0,"check":"1.40*1.05/(0.12-0.05)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the year-3 dividend","value":0.6406,"check":"0.90/1.12**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the year-4 dividend plus P4","value":14.2356,"check":"(1.40+1.40*1.05/(0.12-0.05))/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":14.8762,"check":"0.90/1.12**3+(1.40+1.40*1.05/(0.12-0.05))/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Constant growth begins with D5, so the horizon is year 4 and both D3 and D4 are explicit.","D5 = 1.40 × 1.05 = $1.4700; P4 = 1.4700/(0.12 − 0.05) = $21.00","PV(D3) = 0.90/1.12³ = $0.6406","PV(D4 + P4) = (1.40 + 21.00)/1.12⁴ = 22.40/1.5735 = $14.2356","P0 = 0.6406 + 14.2356 = $14.88"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 0, C02 = 0, C03 = 0.90, C04 = 1.40 + 21.00 = 22.40 (each F = 1)","NPV: I = 12, CPT NPV = 14.88"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D4 instead of D5 in the horizon numerator","value":14.24,"check":"0.90/1.12**3+1.40/1.12**4+1.40/(0.12-0.05)/1.12**4","explain":"D4 is the last irregular dividend, not the first constant-growth dividend; the numerator for P4 must be D5 = D4 × 1.05."},{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Dropped the year-4 dividend","value":13.99,"check":"0.90/1.12**3+1.40*1.05/(0.12-0.05)/1.12**4","explain":"D4 is an explicit dividend; P4 values only the dividends from year 5 onward."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted both year-4 amounts five years","value":13.35,"check":"0.90/1.12**3+(1.40+1.40*1.05/(0.12-0.05))/1.12**5","explain":"The year-4 dividend and P4 are both year-4 values: discount four periods."}],"explanation":"Zeros in the early years are still part of the timeline. The key step is spotting that constant growth starts with D5, which puts the horizon at year 4 (the last irregular dividend D4 is paid with it).","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-13; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-417","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bow Valley Utilities will pay $0.85, $1.25 and $1.70 at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3. From year 4 the dividend grows 3.5% per year forever. R = 9.5% per annum. What price do you expect the stock to trade at at the end of year 2, just after that year's dividend is paid? (Answer in $.)","answer":28.33,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1.70+1.70*1.035/(0.095-0.035))/1.095","steps":[{"label":"First constant-growth dividend D4 = D3 × 1.035","value":1.7595,"check":"1.70*1.035","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P3 = D4/(R − g)","value":29.325,"check":"1.70*1.035/(0.095-0.035)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price at year 2: P2 = (D3 + P3)/(1 + R)","value":28.3333,"check":"(1.70+1.70*1.035/(0.095-0.035))/1.095","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D4 = 1.70 × 1.035 = $1.7595; P3 = 1.7595/(0.095 − 0.035) = $29.3250","At year 2 the only remaining cash flows are D3 and P3, one period away:","P2 = (D3 + P3)/(1 + R) = (1.70 + 29.3250)/1.095 = 31.0250/1.095 = $28.33"],"keystrokes":["1.70 × 1.035 ÷ 0.06 = 29.3250 (P3)","(1.70 + 29.3250) ÷ 1.095 = 28.33"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Left out D3 and discounted only P3","value":26.78,"check":"1.70*1.035/(0.095-0.035)/1.095","explain":"The holder at year 2 still receives D3 at year 3, so D3 belongs with P3."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted P3 two years","value":24.46,"check":"1.70*1.035/(0.095-0.035)/1.095**2","explain":"From year 2 the horizon is only one period away, so P3 is discounted ONE period, not two."},{"id":"cum-dividend-price","label":"Added D2 to the year-2 price (cum-dividend)","value":29.58,"check":"1.25+(1.70+1.70*1.035/(0.095-0.035))/1.095","explain":"D2 is paid at year 2; the price just after payment excludes it, so only D3 and P3 (discounted one period) remain."}],"explanation":"A price at any date is the PV, at that date, of everything still to come. At year 2 that is D3 plus P3 discounted a single period.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-418","concept":"fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lakeshore Brewing will pay $0.95 and $1.40 at the ends of years 1 and 2. From year 3 the dividend grows 5% per year forever. R = 10% per annum. What percentage of today's price comes from the horizon price? (Answer in %.)","answer":92.32,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2)/(0.95/1.10+1.40/1.10**2+1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2)*100","steps":[{"label":"First constant-growth dividend D3 = D2 × 1.05","value":1.47,"check":"1.40*1.05","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P2 = D3/(R − g)","value":29.4,"check":"1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the horizon price","value":24.2975,"check":"1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":26.3182,"check":"0.95/1.10+1.40/1.10**2+1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Share of P0 from the horizon price (%)","value":92.32,"check":"(1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2)/(0.95/1.10+1.40/1.10**2+1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D3 = 1.40 × 1.05 = $1.4700; P2 = 1.4700/(0.10 − 0.05) = $29.40","PV(P2) = 29.40/1.10² = $24.2975","PV(D1, D2) = 0.95/1.10 + 1.40/1.10² = 0.8636 + 1.1570 = $2.0207","P0 = 2.0207 + 24.2975 = $26.32","Share from the horizon = 24.2975/26.3182 = 92.32%"],"keystrokes":["1.40 × 1.05 ÷ 0.05 = 29.40; ÷ 1.10 ÷ 1.10 = 24.2975","Divide by P0 = 26.3182 and × 100 = 92.32"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-not-discounted","label":"Used the undiscounted horizon price P2","value":111.71,"check":"1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/(0.95/1.10+1.40/1.10**2+1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2)*100","explain":"P2 is a year-2 value; it must be discounted to date 0 before comparing it with today's price."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Gave the share of the early dividends instead","value":7.68,"check":"(0.95/1.10+1.40/1.10**2)/(0.95/1.10+1.40/1.10**2+1.40*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.10**2)*100","explain":"That is the complement: the share of P0 that comes from the early dividends (about 8%)."}],"explanation":"Most of the price of a growth stock sits in the horizon price. Here 92% of today's value is the discounted P2, so small errors in g2 or R matter far more than errors in the early dividends.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; S4 s6-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-421","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the two-stage dividend formula P0 = D1/(R − g1) × [1 − ((1 + g1)/(1 + R))^N] + P_N/(1 + R)^N, the first term is:","options":["the present value of the horizon price P_N","the present value of the N dividends that grow at g1 (a growing annuity)","the price at date N, just after the last supernormal dividend","the present value of a perpetuity growing at g1 forever"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bracketed first term is the growing-annuity formula: it values only the N dividends D1 through D_N that grow at g1. Everything after year N is captured by the second term, the discounted horizon price P_N.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1; TB 8.1; L04 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q08-422","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which situation should be valued with the two-stage formula rather than the plain Gordon model?","options":["Dividends grow 3% per year from now on, forever","The dividend stays at $2.00 per share every year, forever","The firm pays no dividend and is valued from a peer's P/E ratio","Dividends grow 18% per year for 6 years and then 4% per year forever"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Two-stage growth means one growth rate g1 for a fixed number of years, then a lower constant g2 forever. Constant growth is Gordon, a flat dividend is a perpetuity, and a P/E valuation is a comparables method.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; L04 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-423","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the two-stage formula, which growth rate appears in the denominator of the horizon price P_N, and which dividend is in its numerator?","options":["g1, the first-stage rate, with D_N = D0(1 + g1)^N","g2, the long-run rate, with D_N = D0(1 + g1)^N","g1, the first-stage rate, with D_{N+1} = D0(1 + g1)^N(1 + g2)","g2, the long-run rate, with D_{N+1} = D0(1 + g1)^N(1 + g2)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"P_N values the dividends from N+1 onward, which grow at g2 forever, so it uses R − g2 and the first dividend of that stream, D_{N+1}. D_{N+1} is D_N (grown N times at g1) grown ONE more step at g2.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-17"},{"id":"fin201-q08-424","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A start-up's dividend growth is expected to be 30% per year for 5 years and then 5% forever, with R = 12%. Because g1 = 30% is above R, the analyst says the two-stage formula cannot be used. What is the right response?","options":["She is right: the model fails whenever any growth rate in the forecast is above the required return R","The formula still works: only g2 must be below R, and the two negative factors multiply to a positive value","Replace g1 with R so that the denominator R − g1 is positive and the formula can then be used","Use the Gordon model with g = 30% for the whole stream, since the growth rate is above R"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only the infinite stage needs g < R. For the first stage D1/(R − g1) and [1 − ((1 + g1)/(1 + R))^N] are both negative when g1 > R, and their product is positive (SM 8-23 uses g1 = 28% with R = 13%).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-23; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-425","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the two-stage formula relate to the timeline method (list every dividend, add the discounted horizon price)?","options":["The formula is an approximation that is usually a few dollars too high","The timeline method values only the dividends, the formula also adds the horizon price","They give the same value; the formula bundles the first N discounted dividends into one growing-annuity term","They differ because the formula discounts at g1 while the timeline discounts at R"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both methods discount the same cash flows at R: N growing dividends and the horizon price P_N. The growing-annuity term simply sums the discounted dividends in one step, which is quicker for long first stages (8 or 11 years in SM 8-23 and 8-24).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-17"},{"id":"fin201-q08-426","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an input to the two-stage dividend formula?","options":["The number of years N of first-stage growth","The long-run growth rate g2 after year N","The market price P0 (the model's output)","The current dividend D0 (or D1)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The formula needs D0 (or D1), g1, N, g2 and R, and produces the value P0. The market price is what you compare that value with afterwards; it is not an input.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-427","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the two-stage formula need the second term P_N/(1 + R)^N in addition to the growing-annuity term?","options":["The annuity term already contains all the dividends but ignores the time value of money","The second term converts the first-stage growth rate g1 into the long-run rate g2","The second term adjusts the first-stage dividends for the extra risk of fast growth","The annuity covers only N dividends; the tail from year N+1 on is worth P_N at date N and must be discounted back"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The growing-annuity formula covers exactly N dividends. The infinite tail after year N is valued by Gordon at date N (P_N), and a date-N value is brought to date 0 with (1 + R)^N.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-428","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: in the two-stage model with D0 just paid, the horizon price is P_N = D0(1 + g1)^N(1 + g2)/(R − g2).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. D_N = D0(1 + g1)^N, and the first long-run dividend is D_{N+1} = D_N(1 + g2). The Gordon formula then values it at date N with R − g2 in the denominator.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; SM 8-17"},{"id":"fin201-q08-429","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a two-stage valuation with g1 = 20% above g2 = 4% (R and D0 unchanged), what happens to P0 if the high-growth period N is lengthened from 5 to 8 years?","options":["P0 falls, because the horizon price is discounted over three more years and g1 is above R","P0 is unchanged, because the horizon price depends only on g2 and R, not on N","P0 rises: there are more years of 20% growth, and the long-run phase starts from a larger dividend","P0 rises, but only through the growing-annuity term; the horizon term is unaffected"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A longer first stage means D_{N+1} is much larger and the 20% growth lasts longer; the extra discounting is outweighed by the extra growth (when g1 > g2). The change shows up in both terms, not only in the annuity term.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 8.1; L04 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q08-430","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A two-stage valuation using your inputs gives $48.00 per share, while the stock trades at $55.00. What is the most direct reading?","options":["The stock is undervalued at your inputs, so you should buy it at the $55.00 price","The model is invalid, because a correct DCF value must always equal the market price","The market price must be wrong, because a DCF value is always the true price of the stock","At your inputs the stock looks overvalued: the market assumes more growth or a lower R than you do"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A DCF value below the quote means that the price embeds more optimistic growth or a lower discount rate than your inputs. It does not prove the market wrong, but at your assumptions you would not pay $55.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s34; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-431","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards just paid a dividend of $2.20. Dividends will grow 22% per year for 4 years and 5% per year thereafter. R = 12% per annum. What is the price today?","options":["$46.46","$55.20","$57.41","$84.05"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Annuity term = 2.684/(0.12 − 0.22) × [1 − (1.22/1.12)^4] = $10.9476. D5 = 2.20 × 1.22^4 × 1.05 = $5.1174, P4 = D5/0.07 = $73.1060, PV = $46.4602. P0 = $57.41. Using D4 in the horizon numerator gives $55.20; leaving P4 undiscounted gives $84.05; dropping the annuity term gives only $46.46.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-17; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-432","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Selkirk Seafoods just paid $2.10. Dividends will grow 10% per year for 5 years and 3.5% per year forever after that. R = 11.5% per annum. What is the price today?","options":["$32.85","$34.36","$34.56","$35.47"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Annuity term = D1/(R − g1)[1 − (1.10/1.115)^5] with D1 = 2.31: $10.0838. D6 = 2.10 × 1.10^5 × 1.035 = $3.5004; P5 = D6/(0.115 − 0.035) = $43.7555; PV = 43.7555/1.115^5 = $25.3898. P0 = $35.47. Starting the annuity from D0 gives $34.56; running the annuity formula with g2 gives $34.36; discounting P5 six years gives $32.85.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-24; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-433","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Tundra Telecom just paid $0.95. Dividends will grow 35% per year for 3 years and 6% per year forever after that. R = 13% per annum. What is the price today?","options":["$20.42","$27.25","$27.73","$28.64"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With g1 = 35% > R both R − g1 = −0.22 and the bracket are negative: annuity term = +$4.1108. D4 = 0.95 × 1.35³ × 1.06 = $2.4776; P3 = D4/0.07 = $35.3943; PV = $24.5300. P0 = $28.64. Flipping the sign of the annuity term gives $20.42; using g2 in the annuity gives $27.73; putting D3 in the horizon numerator gives $27.25.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-23; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-434","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards just paid a dividend of $1.15. Dividends will grow 26% per year for the next 6 years and 4.5% per year thereafter, forever. R = 12% per annum. Use the two-stage formula to find the price today. (Answer in $.)","answer":43.12,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)/(0.12-0.26)*(1-((1+0.26)/(1+0.12))**6)+1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)/(0.12-0.045)/(1+0.12)**6","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1 = D0(1 + g1)","value":1.449,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growing-annuity term (first 6 dividends)","value":10.6324,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)/(0.12-0.26)*(1-((1+0.26)/(1+0.12))**6)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"First long-run dividend D7 = D0(1 + g1)^6(1 + g2)","value":4.8088,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P6 = D7/(R − g2)","value":64.1174,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)/(0.12-0.045)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the horizon price","value":32.4839,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)/(0.12-0.045)/(1+0.12)**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":43.1163,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)/(0.12-0.26)*(1-((1+0.26)/(1+0.12))**6)+1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)/(0.12-0.045)/(1+0.12)**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D1 = 1.15 × 1.26 = $1.4490","Annuity term = D1/(R − g1) × [1 − ((1 + g1)/(1 + R))^6] = 1.4490/(0.12 − 0.26) × [1 − (1.26/1.12)^6] = (−10.3500) × (−1.0273) = $10.6324","D7 = 1.15 × 1.26^6 × 1.045 = $4.8088; P6 = D7/(0.12 − 0.045) = $64.1174","PV(P6) = P6/1.12^6 = $32.4839","P0 = 10.6324 + 32.4839 = $43.12"],"keystrokes":["D1: 1.15 × 1.26 = 1.4490","Bracket: (1.26 ÷ 1.12) y^x 6 = 2.0273; 1 − 2.0273 = −1.0273","Annuity = 1.4490 ÷ (0.12 − 0.26) × (−1.0273) = 10.6324","P6: 1.15 × 1.26 y^x 6 × 1.045 ÷ 0.075 = 64.1174; PV: N = 6, I/Y = 12, FV = 64.1174, CPT PV = −32.4839","Add: 10.6324 + 32.4839 = 43.12"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D6 instead of D7 in the horizon price","value":41.72,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)/(0.12-0.26)*(1-((1+0.26)/(1+0.12))**6)+1.15*1.26**6/(0.12-0.045)/1.12**6","explain":"The horizon price needs the first long-run dividend D_{N+1} = D6 × 1.045, not D6."},{"id":"horizon-not-discounted","label":"Added P6 without discounting it","value":74.75,"check":"1.15*(1+0.26)/(0.12-0.26)*(1-((1+0.26)/(1+0.12))**6)+1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)/(0.12-0.045)","explain":"P6 is a year-6 value; divide by (1 + R)^6 before adding it to a present-value term."},{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Started the annuity from D0 instead of D1","value":40.92,"check":"1.15/(0.12-0.26)*(1-(1.26/1.12)**6)+1.15*(1+0.26)**6*(1+0.045)/(0.12-0.045)/(1+0.12)**6","explain":"The growing-annuity formula takes the NEXT dividend D1 = D0(1 + g1)."}],"explanation":"A two-stage value is a growing annuity for the high-growth years plus the discounted horizon price. With g1 above R the annuity pieces are negative but their product is positive. The horizon price supplies about three quarters of the value.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-23; TB Ex 8.5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-435","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Ridge Foods just paid $1.80 per share. Dividends are expected to grow 9% per year for 6 years and then 3% per year forever. R = 11% per annum. What is the price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":30.92,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)/(0.11-0.09)*(1-((1+0.09)/(1+0.11))**6)+1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)/(1+0.11)**6","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1","value":1.962,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growing-annuity term","value":10.139,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)/(0.11-0.09)*(1-((1+0.09)/(1+0.11))**6)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P6 = D7/(R − g2)","value":38.8668,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the horizon price","value":20.7798,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)/(1+0.11)**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":30.9188,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)/(0.11-0.09)*(1-((1+0.09)/(1+0.11))**6)+1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)/(1+0.11)**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D1 = 1.80 × 1.09 = $1.9620","Annuity term = 1.9620/(0.11 − 0.09) × [1 − (1.09/1.11)^6] = 98.10 × 0.10335 = $10.1390","D7 = 1.80 × 1.09^6 × 1.03 = $3.1093; P6 = D7/(0.11 − 0.03) = $38.8668","PV(P6) = 38.8668/1.11^6 = $20.7798","P0 = 10.1390 + 20.7798 = $30.92"],"keystrokes":["1.80 × 1.09 = 1.9620","(1.09 ÷ 1.11) y^x 6 = 0.8966; 1 − 0.8966 = 0.1034 (the calculator keeps full precision)","1.9620 ÷ 0.02 × bracket = 10.1390","1.80 × 1.09 y^x 6 × 1.03 ÷ 0.08 = 38.8668; N = 6, I/Y = 11, FV = 38.8668, CPT PV = −20.7798","10.1390 + 20.7798 = 30.92"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-wrong-g","label":"Used g2 in the growing-annuity term","value":29.65,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)/(0.11-0.03)*(1-((1.03)/(1.11))**6)+1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)/(1+0.11)**6","explain":"The first-stage dividends grow at g1; g2 belongs only in the horizon price."},{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Started the annuity from D0","value":30.08,"check":"1.80/(0.11-0.09)*(1-((1.09)/(1.11))**6)+1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)/(1+0.11)**6","explain":"The annuity formula needs D1 = D0(1 + g1) = 1.962."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted P6 for seven years","value":28.86,"check":"1.8*(1+0.09)/(0.11-0.09)*(1-((1+0.09)/(1+0.11))**6)+1.8*(1+0.09)**6*(1+0.03)/(0.11-0.03)/1.11**7","explain":"P6 is a year-6 value, so it is discounted six periods."}],"explanation":"When g1 < R the pieces are all positive and the first stage contributes about a third of the value. The two methods (timeline or formula) must agree; the formula is just quicker.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-24; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-436","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Foothills Robotics just paid $0.85. Dividends will grow 21% per year for 9 years and then 4% per year forever. R = 11% per annum. What is the price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":39.52,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)/(0.11-0.21)*(1-((1+0.21)/(1+0.11))**9)+0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)/(0.11-0.04)/(1+0.11)**9","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1","value":1.0285,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growing-annuity term (9 dividends)","value":12.0695,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)/(0.11-0.21)*(1-((1+0.21)/(1+0.11))**9)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"First long-run dividend D10","value":4.915,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P9","value":70.2138,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)/(0.11-0.04)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the horizon price","value":27.4483,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)/(0.11-0.04)/(1+0.11)**9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":39.5179,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)/(0.11-0.21)*(1-((1+0.21)/(1+0.11))**9)+0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)/(0.11-0.04)/(1+0.11)**9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D1 = 0.85 × 1.21 = $1.0285","Annuity term = 1.0285/(0.11 − 0.21) × [1 − (1.21/1.11)^9] = (−10.285) × (−1.1735) = $12.0695","D10 = 0.85 × 1.21^9 × 1.04 = $4.9150; P9 = D10/(0.11 − 0.04) = $70.2138","PV(P9) = 70.2138/1.11^9 = $27.4483","P0 = 12.0695 + 27.4483 = $39.52"],"keystrokes":["D1 = 0.85 × 1.21 = 1.0285","Annuity: 1.0285 ÷ (0.11 − 0.21) × (1 − (1.21 ÷ 1.11) y^x 9) = 12.0695","P9: 0.85 × 1.21 y^x 9 × 1.04 ÷ 0.07 = 70.2138","N = 9, I/Y = 11, FV = 70.2138, CPT PV = −27.4483","12.0695 + 27.4483 = 39.52"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D9 in the horizon numerator","value":38.46,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)/(0.11-0.21)*(1-((1+0.21)/(1+0.11))**9)+0.85*1.21**9/(0.11-0.04)/1.11**9","explain":"P9 values the dividends from D10 on; the numerator must be D10 = D9(1.04)."},{"id":"horizon-not-discounted","label":"Added P9 undiscounted","value":82.28,"check":"0.85*(1+0.21)/(0.11-0.21)*(1-((1+0.21)/(1+0.11))**9)+0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)/(0.11-0.04)","explain":"Discount P9 nine periods before adding it."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used only 8 years in the annuity term","value":37.67,"check":"0.85*1.21/(0.11-0.21)*(1-(1.21/1.11)**8)+0.85*(1+0.21)**9*(1+0.04)/(0.11-0.04)/(1+0.11)**9","explain":"The exponent N counts the dividends in the high-growth stage (9 here), and must match the discounting of P9."}],"explanation":"Nine years at 21% grow the dividend more than fivefold, so by year 9 the long-run dividend is large. The horizon piece is again the larger share of value, but the long first stage adds about $12.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-24; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-437","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Chinook Chemicals just paid $0.60. Dividends will grow 32% per year for 5 years and 6% per year forever after that. R = 12% per annum. What is the price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":29.15,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)/(0.12-0.32)*(1-((1+0.32)/(1+0.12))**5)+0.6*(1+0.32)**5*(1+0.06)/(0.12-0.06)/(1+0.12)**5","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1","value":0.792,"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growing-annuity term (watch the signs)","value":5.0448,"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)/(0.12-0.32)*(1-((1+0.32)/(1+0.12))**5)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon price P5 = D6/(R − g2)","value":42.4791,"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)**5*(1+0.06)/(0.12-0.06)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the horizon price","value":24.1038,"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)**5*(1+0.06)/(0.12-0.06)/(1+0.12)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0","value":29.1486,"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)/(0.12-0.32)*(1-((1+0.32)/(1+0.12))**5)+0.6*(1+0.32)**5*(1+0.06)/(0.12-0.06)/(1+0.12)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D1 = 0.60 × 1.32 = $0.7920","R − g1 = 0.12 − 0.32 = −0.20; bracket = 1 − (1.32/1.12)^5 = 1 − 2.2739 = −1.2739; annuity term = 0.7920/(−0.20) × (−1.2739) = $5.0448","D6 = 0.60 × 1.32^5 × 1.06 = $2.5487; P5 = D6/(0.12 − 0.06) = $42.4791","PV(P5) = 42.4791/1.12^5 = $24.1038","P0 = 5.0448 + 24.1038 = $29.15"],"keystrokes":["0.60 × 1.32 = 0.7920","(1.32 ÷ 1.12) y^x 5 = 2.2739 → bracket = −1.2739","0.7920 ÷ (0.12 − 0.32) × (−1.2739) = 5.0448","0.60 × 1.32 y^x 5 × 1.06 ÷ 0.06 = 42.4791; N = 5, I/Y = 12, FV = 42.4791, CPT PV = −24.1038","5.0448 + 24.1038 = 29.15"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-error","label":"Flipped the sign of the annuity term","value":19.06,"check":"-(0.6*(1+0.32)/(0.12-0.32)*(1-((1+0.32)/(1+0.12))**5))+0.6*(1+0.32)**5*(1+0.06)/(0.12-0.06)/(1+0.12)**5","explain":"A negative R − g1 times a negative bracket is positive: the first-stage dividends add value."},{"id":"horizon-wrong-g","label":"Used g1 in the horizon price (P5 = D6/(R − g1))","value":-2.19,"check":"0.6*(1+0.32)/(0.12-0.32)*(1-((1+0.32)/(1+0.12))**5)+(0.60*1.32**5*1.06)/(0.12-0.32)/1.12**5","explain":"The long-run rate g2 = 6% belongs in the horizon price; g1 = 32% exceeds R and gives a negative price."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used 4 years in the annuity bracket but discounted P5 five years","value":27.78,"check":"0.60*1.32/(0.12-0.32)*(1-(1.32/1.12)**4)+0.6*(1+0.32)**5*(1+0.06)/(0.12-0.06)/(1+0.12)**5","explain":"The number of high-growth dividends N must be the same in the bracket and in the horizon date."}],"explanation":"Even with g1 far above R, the first-stage dividends are finite and add value (about $5). Only the infinite stage must have g2 < R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-23; SM 8-30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-438","concept":"fin201-c08-two-stage","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Laurentian Labs just paid total dividends of $6.4 million. Total dividends will grow 24% per year for 4 years and 5% per year forever after that. R = 13% per annum and there are 10 million shares. What is the value per share today? (Answer in $ per share.)","answer":15.43,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)+6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05)/(1+0.13)**4)/10","steps":[{"label":"Annuity term, total ($ millions)","value":32.4667,"check":"6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Total horizon dividend D5 ($ millions)","value":15.8875,"check":"6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon value P4 ($ millions)","value":198.594,"check":"6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of horizon value ($ millions)","value":121.8014,"check":"6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05)/(1+0.13)**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Total equity value ($ millions)","value":154.268,"check":"6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)+6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05)/(1+0.13)**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Value per share","value":15.4268,"check":"(6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)+6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05)/(1+0.13)**4)/10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Annuity term = 7.936/(0.13 − 0.24) × [1 − (1.24/1.13)^4] = $32.4667 million","D5 = 6.4 × 1.24^4 × 1.05 = $15.8875 million; P4 = D5/(0.13 − 0.05) = $198.5940 million","PV(P4) = 198.5940/1.13^4 = $121.8014 million","Equity = 32.4667 + 121.8014 = $154.2680 million; per share = 154.2680/10 = $15.43"],"keystrokes":["Work in $ millions, then divide by 10 million shares at the end","6.4 × 1.24 = 7.936; annuity as before; P4: 6.4 × 1.24 y^x 4 × 1.05 ÷ 0.08 = 198.6; discount four years"],"errors":[{"id":"units","label":"Forgot to divide by shares (answered in $ millions)","value":154.27,"check":"6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)+6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05)/(1+0.13)**4","explain":"The model values the whole equity; divide by the number of shares to get a price per share."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D4 instead of D5 in the horizon numerator","value":14.85,"check":"(6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)+6.4*1.24**4/(0.13-0.05)/1.13**4)/10","explain":"The horizon value needs the first long-run dividend D5 = D4 × 1.05."},{"id":"horizon-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the horizon value","value":23.11,"check":"(6.4*(1+0.24)/(0.13-0.24)*(1-((1+0.24)/(1+0.13))**4)+6.4*(1+0.24)**4*(1+0.05)/(0.13-0.05))/10","explain":"P4 is a year-4 amount: discount it four periods."}],"explanation":"The same two-stage machinery works on total dividends; convert to per-share only at the end (or divide D0 by shares at the start).","source":"generated","ref":"TB Ex 8.4; SM 8-17"},{"id":"fin201-q08-441","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In non-constant growth, \"solving backwards\" means:","options":["computing the horizon price first and then discounting it back to date 0 to get today's price","the price is given (P0 or a horizon price) and you solve the valuation equation for an unknown input such as g2 or a dividend","valuing the stock by working from the last dividend toward the first dividend","discounting the dividends at the required return in reverse order, last dividend first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In the usual direction the inputs (dividends, growth, R) give a price. Solving backwards flips this: the price is known and one input (g2, D1 or D0, the first dividend) is the unknown. The valuation equation is the same; you rearrange it.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-13; SM 8-18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-442","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following questions requires solving backwards?","options":["\"What is the stock worth today if dividends grow at 7% for three years and 5% afterwards forever?\"","\"What is the dividend yield if the stock trades at $20 and the next dividend D1 is $1.10 per share?\"","\"The stock should sell for $20 in three years. What growth rate after year 3 does that imply?\"","\"By how many periods is the horizon price discounted to bring it back to date 0?\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the prompt that starts from the expected $20 price in three years and asks for the growth rate that price implies solves backwards: the price is known and an input (g2) is the unknown. The others run forward from inputs to a value or a ratio. Giant's \"expected constant growth rate beginning in year 4\" is the model case (S4 s9-13).","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-443","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"The horizon price P_T = D_T(1 + g2)/(R − g2) is known, together with D_T and R. Which expression gives g2?","options":["g2 = (P_T × R − D_T)/(P_T − D_T)","g2 = R − D_T/P_T","g2 = (P_T × R + D_T)/(P_T + D_T)","g2 = (P_T × R − D_T)/(P_T + D_T)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"P_T(R − g2) = D_T(1 + g2) → P_T R − D_T = g2 (P_T + D_T). The shortcut R − D_T/P_T forgets that the next dividend is D_T(1 + g2), not D_T, so it overstates g2; the other two slip a sign when collecting the g2 terms.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-444","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"The price today and all growth rates are known, but the current dividend D0 is not. The cleanest way to find D0 is to:","options":["guess D0 and keep adjusting the required return until the price matches","find the horizon price and divide it by the number of years to the first dividend","write every discounted cash flow as a multiple of D0, add the multiples, and divide P0 by that total","average the growth rates and apply the Gordon formula backwards to D1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With R and the growth rates fixed, every term of the valuation is D0 times a known factor, so P0 = D0 × (sum of factors) and D0 = P0/(sum). SM 8-18 gets a factor of 53.75 and D0 = $76/53.75 = $1.41.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-18; S4 s9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-445","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Compare solving a non-constant growth model for D0 with solving it for the required return R. Which statement is correct?","options":["D0 appears linearly, so algebra is enough; R is in every denominator, so it needs trial and error","Both can be rearranged to a closed form in one step, since each appears once in the equation","R has a closed form, but D0 needs trial and error because it multiplies every term","Neither can be solved, because the horizon price itself depends on the unknown quantity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"P0 = D0 × (known factors) is linear in D0. But R is inside (1 + R)^t and also inside R − g2 of the horizon price, so SM 8-33 finds R = 13.43% by root-finding. (Constant growth alone gives R = D1/P0 + g, which is closed form.)","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-18; SM 8-33"},{"id":"fin201-q08-446","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following can NOT be found by simple algebra from a non-constant growth valuation?","options":["The long-run growth rate g2 implied by a given horizon price","The dividend D0 implied by today's price and the growth rates","The required return R implied by the price (needs trial and error)","The horizon price that would give a chosen target value today"],"answer":2,"explanation":"g2, D0 and the needed horizon price each appear linearly (or in a simple ratio) once the others are known. R sits in several powers of (1 + R) and in R − g2, so SM 8-33 uses a root-finder to get 13.43%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-33"},{"id":"fin201-q08-447","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is D0 easy to solve for from today's price, even when growth rates change several times?","options":["The horizon price does not depend on D0, so only the early dividends matter","Every dividend is D0 times known growth factors, so the valuation is P0 = D0 × (a known number)","Dividends cancel out of the present-value equation when growth rates change","The required return can be set equal to the growth rate, which removes D0 from the equation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each dividend is D0(1 + g1)(1 + g2)... and each discount factor is known, so the equation has a single unknown with a constant coefficient. That coefficient (e.g. 53.75 in SM 8-18) is the PV of the stream per $1 of D0.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-448","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"An analyst's target price of $20 in three years implies a long-run dividend growth rate g2 = 9.8% when R = 11%. How should you read this?","options":["The target is conservative, because g2 is comfortably less than R","The target is very optimistic: g2 is close to R, far above plausible long-run growth","The target is irrelevant, because g2 does not affect the horizon price","The implied g2 means the stock must be undervalued today, so buy it"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A horizon price P_T = D_{T+1}/(R − g2) becomes extremely sensitive when g2 approaches R. An implied g2 near R signals that the target needs near-perpetual high growth. Compare with Giant (S4 s11), where g2 = 5.32% against R = 15%.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s11-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-449","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Gatineau Glass just paid $1.20. Dividends grow 9% per year for 3 years, and the stock is expected to be worth $24.00 at the end of year 3 (R = 14% per annum). What constant growth rate g2 after year 3 does that price imply?","options":["7.07%","7.52%","8.05%","8.57%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"D3 = 1.20 × 1.09³ = $1.5540. From 24 = D3(1 + g2)/(0.14 − g2): g2 = (24 × 0.14 − 1.5540)/(24 + 1.5540) = 7.0672%. The shortcut R − D3/P3 (7.52%) treats D3 as next year's dividend; using D0 gives 8.57%; (P − D) in the denominator gives 8.05%.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-450","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harbourfront Bakeries trades at $60 today. Its dividend will grow 20% next year, 10% the year after, and 4% per year from year 3 on. R = 9% per annum. What is next year's dividend D1?","options":["$2.37","$2.84","$3.08","$32.55"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Per $1 of D0: PV = 1.2/1.09 + 1.32/1.09² + (1.32 × 1.04/0.05)/1.09² = 25.3211. D0 = 60/25.3211 = $2.3696, so D1 = 1.20 × D0 = $2.84. Reporting D0 ($2.37) answers the wrong question; dropping the horizon price gives $32.55; discounting the horizon price three years gives $3.08.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-18; S4 s9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-451","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Whitecap Wind just paid $2.40. Dividends grow 8% per year for 3 years. Analysts expect the stock to be worth $48.00 at the end of year 3, and R = 13% per annum. What constant growth rate g2 after year 3 is implied? (Answer in % per annum.)","answer":6.3,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(48*0.13-2.40*1.08**3)/(48+2.40*1.08**3)*100","steps":[{"label":"Year-3 dividend D3 = D0(1.08)^3","value":3.0233,"check":"2.40*1.08**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Implied long-run growth g2 = (P3 R − D3)/(P3 + D3) (in %)","value":6.3044,"check":"(48*0.13-2.40*1.08**3)/(48+2.40*1.08**3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D3 = 2.40 × 1.08³ = $3.0233","P3 = D4/(R − g2) = D3(1 + g2)/(R − g2) = 48","48 × (0.13 − g2) = D3 + D3 g2 → 6.24 − 3.0233 = g2 (48 + 3.0233)","g2 = 3.2167/51.0233 = 6.3044% per annum","Check: D4 = 3.2139; D4/(0.13 − 0.0630) = $48.00"],"keystrokes":["2.40 × 1.08 y^x 3 = 3.0233","(48 × 0.13 − 3.0233) ÷ (48 + 3.0233) × 100 = 6.30"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Treated D3 as the next dividend (g2 = R − D3/P3)","value":6.7,"check":"(0.13-2.40*1.08**3/48)*100","explain":"The horizon price uses D4 = D3(1 + g2), which still contains the unknown g2."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used D0 instead of D3","value":7.62,"check":"(48*0.13-2.40)/(48+2.40)*100","explain":"The dividend that grows into the long-run stream is D3, the last first-stage dividend."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Collected the g2 terms with a sign slip","value":7.15,"check":"(48*0.13-2.40*1.08**3)/(48-2.40*1.08**3)*100","explain":"48(0.13 − g2) = D3(1 + g2) gives g2 in the form (48R − D3)/(48 + D3)."}],"explanation":"With the horizon price given, the Gordon formula P3 = D3(1 + g2)/(R − g2) is one equation in one unknown. g2 = 6.30% is sensible against R = 13%.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q08-452","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rideau Rail trades at $52.00. Its dividend is expected to grow 25% next year, 15% the year after, and 5% per year from year 3 on. R = 10% per annum. What is the dividend D1 due next year? (Answer in $.)","answer":2.38,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"52/(1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2+1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.1**2)*1.25","steps":[{"label":"Horizon price per $1 of D0: P2/D0 = 1.25 × 1.15 × 1.05/(R − g)","value":30.1875,"check":"1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of everything per $1 of D0","value":27.2727,"check":"1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2+1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.1**2","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Current dividend D0 = 52/factor","value":1.9067,"check":"52/(1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2+1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.1**2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Next dividend D1 = 1.25 × D0","value":2.3833,"check":"52/(1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2+1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.1**2)*1.25","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Write each cash flow per $1 of D0: D1 = 1.25, D2 = 1.4375, D3 = 1.5094","P2 = D3/(R − g) = 1.5094/0.05 = 30.1875 (per $1 of D0)","Factor = 1.25/1.10 + 1.4375/1.10² + 30.1875/1.10² = 1.1364 + 1.1880 + 24.9483 = 27.2727","D0 = 52/27.2727 = $1.9067","D1 = 1.25 × 1.9067 = $2.38"],"keystrokes":["1.25 × 1.15 × 1.05 ÷ 0.05 = 30.1875","1.25 ÷ 1.1 + 1.4375 ÷ 1.21 + 30.1875 ÷ 1.21 = 27.2727","52 ÷ 27.2727 = 1.9067 (D0); × 1.25 = 2.38"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Reported D0 instead of D1","value":1.91,"check":"52/(1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2+1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.1**2)","explain":"The question asks for next year's dividend D1 = 1.25 D0."},{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Left the horizon price out of the factor","value":27.96,"check":"52/(1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2)*1.25","explain":"The constant-growth tail after year 2 is by far the largest part of the factor."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted the horizon price three years","value":2.6,"check":"52/(1.25/1.1+1.25*1.15/1.1**2+1.25*1.15*1.05/(0.10-0.05)/1.1**3)*1.25","explain":"D3 is the first constant-growth dividend, so P2 is a year-2 value and is discounted twice."}],"explanation":"A solve-for-the-dividend problem is a forward valuation per $1 of D0 followed by a division. The horizon price carries most of the factor.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-453","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Yukon Drillers is expected to pay $1.10, $1.30, $1.55 and $1.80 at the ends of years 1 to 4. R = 11% per annum. What price would the stock have to reach at the end of year 4 for a buyer today to be willing to pay $55.00? (Answer in $.)","answer":76.87,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(55-(1.10/1.11+1.30/1.11**2+1.55/1.11**3+1.80/1.11**4))*1.11**4","steps":[{"label":"PV of the four dividends","value":4.3652,"check":"1.10/1.11+1.30/1.11**2+1.55/1.11**3+1.80/1.11**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV that the year-4 price must supply","value":50.6348,"check":"55-(1.10/1.11+1.30/1.11**2+1.55/1.11**3+1.80/1.11**4)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required price P4","value":76.8672,"check":"(55-(1.10/1.11+1.30/1.11**2+1.55/1.11**3+1.80/1.11**4))*1.11**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV(dividends) = 1.10/1.11 + 1.30/1.11² + 1.55/1.11³ + 1.80/1.11⁴ = 4.3652","The year-4 price must supply 55.00 − 4.3652 = $50.6348 in present value","P4 = 50.6348 × 1.11⁴ = $76.87"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = −55, C01 = 1.10, C02 = 1.30, C03 = 1.55, C04 = 1.80 + P4","Or: NPV of dividends at 11% = 4.3652; (55 − 4.3652) × 1.11 y^x 4 = 76.87"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Ignored the dividends (P4 = 55 × 1.11^4)","value":83.49,"check":"55*1.11**4","explain":"The four dividends already pay back about $4.37 of the price, so P4 only has to cover the remainder."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Compounded the remainder only three years","value":69.25,"check":"(55-(1.10/1.11+1.30/1.11**2+1.55/1.11**3+1.80/1.11**4))*1.11**3","explain":"P4 is a year-4 value: compound the needed present value four periods."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Added the dividends instead of subtracting","value":90.12,"check":"(55+(1.10/1.11+1.30/1.11**2+1.55/1.11**3+1.80/1.11**4))*1.11**4","explain":"The dividends reduce, not increase, what the final price must be worth."}],"explanation":"Solving backwards for a horizon price is the same equation as the forward valuation: P0 = PV(dividends) + P4/(1 + R)^4, rearranged for P4.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s9-13; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-454","concept":"fin201-c08-solve-backwards","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Saskatoon Seed Corp trades at $18.40. It will pay no dividend for five years; its first dividend D6 arrives at the end of year 6 and then grows 6% per year forever. R = 12% per annum. What first dividend D6 is the market price implying? (Answer in $.)","answer":1.95,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"18.40*1.12**5*(0.12-0.06)","steps":[{"label":"Horizon price P5 = P0 × (1 + R)^5","value":32.4271,"check":"18.40*1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"First dividend D6 = P5 × (R − g)","value":1.9456,"check":"18.40*1.12**5*(0.12-0.06)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["P0 = P5/(1 + R)^5 and P5 = D6/(R − g)","P5 = 18.40 × 1.12⁵ = 18.40 × 1.7623 = $32.4271","D6 = P5 × (R − g) = 32.4271 × 0.06 = $1.95"],"keystrokes":["18.40 × 1.12 y^x 5 = 32.4271","32.4271 × (0.12 − 0.06) = 1.95"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Compounded six years instead of five","value":2.18,"check":"18.40*1.12**6*(0.12-0.06)","explain":"P5 (not P6) is one period before the first dividend, so compound P0 five periods."},{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Used R + g","value":5.84,"check":"18.40*1.12**5*(0.12+0.06)","explain":"The Gordon denominator is R minus g."},{"id":"not-compounded","label":"Skipped the compounding (D6 = P0 × (R − g))","value":1.1,"check":"18.40*(0.12-0.06)","explain":"Today's price is a present value; the dividend is paid years later, so you must compound P0 forward to year 5 first."}],"explanation":"Rearranging P0 = [D6/(R − g)]/(1 + R)^5 gives D6 = P0 × (1 + R)^5 × (R − g). The market is pricing in a first dividend of about $1.95.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-14; S4 s9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q08-461","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The plowback ratio b is:","options":["the fraction of earnings paid out as dividends, DPS/EPS","the fraction of earnings retained and reinvested, b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS","the growth rate of book equity per share","earnings per share divided by book equity per share"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plowback (retention) is what the firm keeps: b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS = 1 − payout. The payout ratio DPS/EPS is its complement, and EPS divided by book equity per share is ROE.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s23-26; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-462","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In g = b × ROE as used in this course, ROE is earnings per share divided by:","options":["book equity per share at the END of the year","the average of start-of-year and end-of-year book equity","book equity per share at the START of the year","the current market price per share"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retained earnings added during the year raise equity at the end of the year, so the start-of-year base is the equity that earned this year's EPS. Dividing by end-of-year equity (or market price) understates ROE and therefore g.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s27; PRAC q4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-463","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm earns EPS of $3.00 and pays DPS of $1.20. Which pair of ratios describes it?","options":["Plowback 60%, payout 40%","Plowback 40%, payout 60%","Plowback 60%, payout 60%","Plowback 40%, payout 40%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Payout = 1.20/3.00 = 40%. Plowback is the rest: b = (3.00 − 1.20)/3.00 = 60%. The two always add to 100%; swapping them is the payout-as-plowback slip that produces g = payout × ROE.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q08-464","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A mature firm with ROE = 12% raises its payout ratio from 40% to 70% and ROE does not change. Its growth rate g changes from:","options":["7.2% to 8.4%","4.8% to 8.4%","4.8% to 3.6%","7.2% to 3.6%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"g = b × ROE with b = 1 − payout: 0.60 × 12% = 7.2% before, and 0.30 × 12% = 3.6% after. Paying out more retains less, so growth falls. The other pairs use the payout ratio as if it were b.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s23-28"},{"id":"fin201-q08-465","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement distinguishes the payout ratio from the plowback ratio correctly?","options":["Payout drives growth through g = payout × ROE; plowback is paid to shareholders","Payout = DPS/EPS goes to shareholders now; plowback b = 1 − payout drives growth through g = b × ROE","Payout and plowback are the same quantity measured on different dates","Plowback is measured on book equity, payout on the market price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cash paid out today is the payout; what stays in the firm to earn ROE next year is b. Only b enters g = b × ROE. Using the payout ratio there is the single most common slip on this topic.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s23-27"},{"id":"fin201-q08-466","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT consistent with the course's growth relationship g = b × ROE?","options":["A firm that retains all earnings and earns ROE of 14% grows at 14%","A firm that pays out all earnings still grows at ROE","At a constant ROE, raising the payout ratio lowers g","Growth comes from reinvested earnings that earn ROE on equity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If b = 0 (everything paid out) then g = 0 × ROE = 0: no new equity is added, so book equity and earnings stay flat. The other statements follow directly from g = b × ROE.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s24-27"},{"id":"fin201-q08-467","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do young companies typically plow back more than mature companies?","options":["Young firms earn lower ROE, so they must reinvest more just to be able to pay dividends","Plowback is required by law for firms under five years old with no earnings history","Young firms have lower required returns, so they can afford to pay no dividends at all","They have many projects worth funding and need the cash, so b is near 1 and g is large"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Growth stocks reinvest nearly all earnings (b near 1, large g) because their opportunities are plentiful; income stocks have b near 0 and small g (L04 s29). Reinvestment is a response to opportunities, not a legal rule.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s29; REV p3; SM 8 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-468","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm with R = 12% considers raising b. When will a higher plowback ratio increase the share price, holding R fixed?","options":["Always, because a higher b raises g","When ROE is below R, because retained earnings are then cheaper than new equity","Never, because dividends are the only source of value","When ROE exceeds R: the reinvested earnings then earn more than investors require"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A higher b lowers D1 but raises g. If ROE > R each retained dollar is worth more than a dollar to shareholders (NPVGO rises); if ROE < R the growth destroys value, as in Johnson (S4 s18). If ROE = R, b makes no difference to price.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34; S4 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-469","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm with a return on equity of 15% and a payout ratio of 40% would expect a growth rate of:","options":["9%","21%","6%","24%","None of the above"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plowback b = 1 − payout = 1 − 0.40 = 0.60, so g = b × ROE = 0.60 × 15% = 9%. The tempting 6% comes from multiplying the payout ratio by ROE (0.40 × 15%: payout-as-plowback). The 21% and 24% add 15% × payout or 15% × b onto ROE, which has no basis in g = b × ROE.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-470","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tamarack Solar wants its dividends to grow 7.5% per year and earns ROE = 12.5% on start-of-year equity. What plowback ratio b does it need? (Answer as a percentage.)","options":["0.94%","40.00%","55.81%","60.00%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"g = b × ROE → b = g/ROE = 7.5%/12.5% = 60%. Reporting the payout ratio (40%) answers the complement; multiplying g by ROE gives 0.94%; solving the textbook sustainable-growth formula ROE·b/(1 − ROE·b) for b gives 55.81%, which is not the course formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s27; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-471","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rideau Rail earned $2.4 million after tax, paid $0.96 million in dividends, and had book equity of $16.0 million at the start of the year. What growth rate does the course's formula give?","options":["6.00%","8.26%","9.00%","15.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"b = (2.4 − 0.96)/2.4 = 0.60 and ROE = 2.4/16.0 = 15% on start-of-year equity, so g = 0.60 × 15% = 9.00%. Using the payout ratio (0.40) gives 6.00%; dividing by end-of-year equity (16.0 + 1.44 = 17.44) gives ROE 13.76% and g = 8.26%; and reporting ROE itself (15.00%) forgets to multiply by b.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s26-28"},{"id":"fin201-q08-472","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sun Peaks Resorts just reported EPS of $3.50 and pays out 45% of earnings; ROE is 14% and R = 11.5% per annum. Assuming payout and ROE stay constant, what is the share price?","options":["$32.20","$41.45","$44.64","$48.08"],"answer":2,"explanation":"DPS0 = 3.50 × 0.45 = $1.575, b = 0.55, g = 0.55 × 14% = 7.7%. D1 = 1.575 × 1.077 = $1.6963 and P0 = 1.6963/(0.115 − 0.077) = $44.64. Using the payout as b (g = 6.3%) gives $32.20; skipping the growth step to D1 gives $41.45; growing twice gives $48.08.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s28; SM 8-29"},{"id":"fin201-q08-473","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: if a firm pays out all of its earnings as dividends, then g = b × ROE gives a growth rate of zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. With full payout b = 0, so no earnings are retained, book equity does not grow and g = 0 × ROE = 0. This is the 'no-growth' case behind the value EPS1/R.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s24-27"},{"id":"fin201-q08-474","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cobalt Peak Software reported EPS of $4.20 and DPS of $1.68 for the year just ended; book equity per share at the start of the year was $28.00. Payout and ROE stay constant and R = 12% per annum. What is the share price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":61.04,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.68*(1+(4.20-1.68)/4.20*4.20/28)/(0.12-(4.20-1.68)/4.20*4.20/28)","steps":[{"label":"Plowback b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS","value":0.6,"check":"(4.20-1.68)/4.20","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"ROE = EPS/book equity at start of year","value":0.15,"check":"4.20/28","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growth g = b × ROE (in %)","value":9.0,"check":"(4.20-1.68)/4.20*4.20/28*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Next dividend D1 = DPS(1 + g)","value":1.8312,"check":"1.68*(1+(4.20-1.68)/4.20*4.20/28)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":61.04,"check":"1.68*(1+(4.20-1.68)/4.20*4.20/28)/(0.12-(4.20-1.68)/4.20*4.20/28)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["b = (4.20 − 1.68)/4.20 = 0.60 (payout 40%)","ROE = 4.20/28.00 = 0.15 (start-of-year equity)","g = 0.60 × 0.15 = 9%","D1 = 1.68 × 1.09 = $1.8312","P0 = 1.8312/(0.12 − 0.09) = 1.8312/0.03 = $61.04"],"keystrokes":["(4.20 − 1.68) ÷ 4.20 = 0.60","4.20 ÷ 28 = 0.15; 0.60 × 0.15 = 0.09","1.68 × 1.09 = 1.8312","1.8312 ÷ (0.12 − 0.09) = 61.04"],"errors":[{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Used the payout ratio as b (g = 6%)","value":29.68,"check":"1.68*(1+0.4*0.15)/(0.12-0.4*0.15)","explain":"Plowback is the retained share, 60%, not the 40% paid out."},{"id":"roe-end-equity","label":"Measured ROE on end-of-year equity","value":48.59,"check":"1.68*(1+0.6*4.20/(28+4.20*0.6))/(0.12-0.6*4.20/(28+4.20*0.6))","explain":"ROE uses start-of-year equity (28.00); end-of-year equity includes the earnings just retained."},{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used DPS instead of D1","value":56.0,"check":"1.68/(0.12-0.09)","explain":"The Gordon numerator is next year's dividend D1 = DPS(1 + g)."}],"explanation":"Steps: plowback → ROE on start-of-year equity → g = b × ROE → D1 = DPS(1 + g) → Gordon. The trailing P/E is about 14.5, sensible for g = 9% and R = 12%.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s28; PRAC q4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-475","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards earned net income of $14.7 million, paid dividends of $5.3 million, and had book equity of $98.0 million at the start of the year. Payout and ROE stay constant. What growth rate does the course's formula imply? (Answer in % per annum.)","answer":9.59,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1-5.3/14.7)*14.7/98*100","steps":[{"label":"Payout ratio","value":0.3605,"check":"5.3/14.7","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Plowback b = 1 − payout","value":0.6395,"check":"1-5.3/14.7","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"ROE = NI/start-of-year equity","value":0.15,"check":"14.7/98","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growth g = b × ROE (in %)","value":9.5918,"check":"(1-5.3/14.7)*14.7/98*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Payout = 5.3/14.7 = 0.3605; b = 1 − 0.3605 = 0.6395","ROE = 14.7/98.0 = 0.15","g = 0.6395 × 0.15 = 9.592% per annum"],"keystrokes":["5.3 ÷ 14.7 = 0.3605","1 − 0.3605 = 0.6395","14.7 ÷ 98 = 0.15; × 0.6395 = 0.0959 → 9.59%"],"errors":[{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Used the payout ratio as b","value":5.41,"check":"5.3/14.7*14.7/98*100","explain":"b is what is retained (about 64%), not what is paid out."},{"id":"roe-end-equity","label":"Used end-of-year equity (98 + 14.7 − 5.3)","value":8.75,"check":"(1-5.3/14.7)*14.7/(98+14.7-5.3)*100","explain":"ROE is measured on start-of-year equity."},{"id":"roe-as-g","label":"Reported ROE itself as the growth rate","value":15.0,"check":"14.7/98*100","explain":"g = b × ROE; skipping b treats every dollar of earnings as retained."}],"explanation":"Net income and dividends give b, net income and starting equity give ROE; their product is the growth rate (about 9.6%). The same answer comes from EPS and DPS per share.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s26-28; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-476","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Gatineau Glass just reported EPS of $5.00. It pays out 35% of earnings, ROE is 14% on start-of-year equity, and R = 13% per annum. Payout and ROE stay constant. What is the share price today? (Answer in $.)","answer":48.96,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"5.00*0.35*(1+0.65*0.14)/(0.13-0.65*0.14)","steps":[{"label":"Plowback b = 1 − payout","value":0.65,"check":"1-0.35","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growth g = b × ROE (in %)","value":9.1,"check":"0.65*0.14*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Next dividend D1 = EPS0 × payout × (1 + g)","value":1.9092,"check":"5.00*0.35*(1+0.65*0.14)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":48.9551,"check":"5.00*0.35*(1+0.65*0.14)/(0.13-0.65*0.14)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["b = 1 − 0.35 = 0.65; g = 0.65 × 0.14 = 9.1%","D0 = 5.00 × 0.35 = $1.75; D1 = 1.75 × 1.091 = $1.9093","P0 = 1.9093/(0.13 − 0.091) = 1.9093/0.039 = $48.96"],"keystrokes":["0.65 × 0.14 = 0.091","5.00 × 0.35 × 1.091 = 1.9093","1.9093 ÷ (0.13 − 0.091) = 48.96"],"errors":[{"id":"eps0-for-eps1","label":"Used EPS0 × payout as D1 (no growth step)","value":44.87,"check":"5.00*0.35/(0.13-0.65*0.14)","explain":"D1 = payout × EPS1 and EPS1 = EPS0(1 + g); EPS0 is last year's number."},{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Used payout as b (g = 4.9%)","value":22.66,"check":"5.00*0.35*(1+0.35*0.14)/(0.13-0.35*0.14)","explain":"g = (1 − payout) × ROE = 0.65 × 14%."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew the dividend twice","value":53.41,"check":"5.00*0.35*(1+0.65*0.14)**2/(0.13-0.65*0.14)","explain":"Only one growth step separates D0 from D1."}],"explanation":"EPS0 is already earned; the dividend investors receive next year is payout × EPS1, where EPS1 = EPS0(1 + g).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s28; SM 8-29"},{"id":"fin201-q08-477","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bow Valley Utilities reported EPS of $2.50 and DPS of $1.00. ROE is 12% on start-of-year equity, payout and ROE stay constant, and R = 10.5% per annum. What do you expect the share price to be three years from now? (Answer in $.)","answer":40.02,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.00*1.072/(0.105-0.072)*1.072**3","steps":[{"label":"Plowback b = (EPS − DPS)/EPS","value":0.6,"check":"(2.50-1.00)/2.50","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growth g = b × ROE (in %)","value":7.2,"check":"(2.50-1.00)/2.50*0.12*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0 = DPS(1 + g)/(R − g)","value":32.4848,"check":"1.00*1.072/(0.105-0.072)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price in three years P3 = P0(1 + g)^3","value":40.0189,"check":"1.00*1.072/(0.105-0.072)*1.072**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["b = (2.50 − 1.00)/2.50 = 0.60; g = 0.60 × 0.12 = 7.2%","P0 = 1.00 × 1.072/(0.105 − 0.072) = 1.072/0.033 = $32.48","With constant growth the price grows at g: P3 = 32.4848 × 1.072³ = $40.02"],"keystrokes":["0.60 × 0.12 = 0.072","1.072 ÷ 0.033 = 32.4848","1.072 y^x 3 = 1.2320; × 32.4848 = 40.02"],"errors":[{"id":"no-price-growth","label":"Stopped at today's price","value":32.48,"check":"1.00*1.072/(0.105-0.072)","explain":"The question asks for the price three years ahead; it has grown at g."},{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used D0 = 1.00 in the Gordon numerator","value":37.33,"check":"1.00/(0.105-0.072)*1.072**3","explain":"P0 needs D1 = 1.00 × 1.072."},{"id":"g-as-r","label":"Grew the price at R (10.5%) instead of g","value":43.83,"check":"1.00*1.072/(0.105-0.072)*1.105**3","explain":"Under constant growth the price rises at g, not R (R is the total return including dividends)."}],"explanation":"Constant growth means price, dividend and earnings all grow at g = b × ROE. The 7.2% growth rate is what carries today's $32.48 to $40.02.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20; SM 8-1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-478","concept":"fin201-c08-plowback-growth","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peace River Foods wants dividends to grow 8% per year while paying out 55% of earnings. What ROE (on start-of-year equity) does it need? (Answer in % per annum.)","answer":17.78,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.08/(1-0.55)*100","steps":[{"label":"Plowback b = 1 − payout","value":0.45,"check":"1-0.55","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required ROE = g/b (in %)","value":17.7778,"check":"0.08/(1-0.55)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["b = 1 − 0.55 = 0.45","g = b × ROE → ROE = g/b = 0.08/0.45 = 17.78%"],"keystrokes":["1 − 0.55 = 0.45","0.08 ÷ 0.45 = 0.1778"],"errors":[{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Divided g by the payout ratio","value":14.55,"check":"0.08/0.55*100","explain":"g = b × ROE with b = 0.45, so ROE = 0.08/0.45."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Multiplied g by b","value":3.6,"check":"0.08*0.45*100","explain":"To isolate ROE, divide g by b; multiplying gives a growth-like number."}],"explanation":"Rearranging g = b × ROE gives ROE = g/b. A high payout leaves little to reinvest, so each retained dollar must earn a lot (17.8%).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q08-481","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The forward P/E ratio is:","options":["the price divided by the earnings of the year just ended, P0/EPS0, which is the trailing ratio","forecast earnings divided by the current price, EPS1/P0, which is the earnings yield","the price expected next year divided by this year's earnings, P1/EPS0 under constant growth","the price divided by forecast earnings for the coming year, P0/EPS1 = (1 − b)/(R − g) under constant growth"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Forward P/E uses next year's earnings in the denominator; with D1 = (1 − b)EPS1 and P0 = D1/(R − g) it simplifies to (1 − b)/(R − g). Using last year's earnings gives the trailing P/E, and E/P is the earnings yield.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14, s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-482","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock trades at $42. Analysts expect earnings of $3.00 per share over the next twelve months, and the company earned $2.80 over the last twelve months. Which ratio equals 42/3.00 = 14.0?","options":["The forward P/E","The trailing P/E","The earnings yield","The dividend yield"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Price over forecast earnings is the forward P/E (42/3.00 = 14.0). The trailing P/E would use the $2.80 already earned (15.0), and the earnings yield is the inverse, E/P.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14, s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-483","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a company whose earnings are expected to grow, how do the trailing and forward P/E ratios compare?","options":["The forward P/E is higher, because the future is more uncertain than the past","They are equal, because both ratios use exactly the same current price","The forward P/E is lower only if the firm pays no dividend at all","The forward P/E is lower: the same price is divided by larger forecast earnings"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Trailing = P0/EPS0 and forward = P0/EPS1. With EPS1 > EPS0 and the same P0, forward < trailing; their ratio is 1 + g. If earnings are expected to fall the order reverses.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s18; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-484","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two firms have the same R and the same growth rate g. Firm X pays out 60% of earnings and Firm Y pays out 30%. Which has the higher forward P/E and why?","options":["Firm X: (1 − b) is larger, so more of each dollar of EPS1 is paid as dividends at the same g","Firm Y: it retains more, so each dollar of its earnings is worth more to shareholders","They have the same P/E, because R and g are the same for both firms","Firm Y: retaining more always raises growth, so it must be worth more per dollar"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g). With R and g equal, a higher payout (lower b) raises the numerator. Equal g with lower b means X's ROE is higher; X delivers the same growth while paying out more. (Raising b with ROE fixed would raise g, but here g is held equal.)","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s31"},{"id":"fin201-q08-485","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does a higher required return R lower the forward P/E?","options":["A higher R raises the growth rate g by the same amount, so the two cancel out in the ratio","A higher R lowers the forecast EPS1 that sits in the denominator of the P/E ratio","A higher R lowers the payout ratio, so the numerator (1 − b) falls as well","R − g rises, so each dollar of next year's dividend is worth fewer dollars of price"],"answer":3,"explanation":"P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g): R only appears in the denominator. A riskier stock (higher R) is discounted more heavily, so the same earnings and growth buy a lower multiple (L04 s31, point 2).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s31"},{"id":"fin201-q08-486","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Other things equal, which of the following would NOT raise a firm's forward P/E?","options":["A higher expected growth rate g in dividends","A higher required return R for a riskier firm","A lower required return R for the stock","A higher payout ratio with growth g unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The three professor's rules: high g, low R and high payout (with g constant) all raise (1 − b)/(R − g). A riskier stock has a higher R, which lowers the multiple.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s31"},{"id":"fin201-q08-487","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock has a forward P/E of 9 while similar firms trade near 15. Which interpretation is consistent with P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g)?","options":["The firm must have a higher payout ratio than its peers","The firm's earnings are overstated, so the multiple is mechanically too low","The market expects lower growth or requires a higher return for this firm (or it is mispriced)","The firm must have higher growth, since low P/E signals growth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A low multiple means a small (1 − b)/(R − g): either R − g is large (low g or high R) or the firm is simply undervalued relative to comparables. Low P/E is the signature of an income or risky stock, not a growth stock.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30-32; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-488","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: under constant growth the forward P/E equals (1 − b)/(R − g).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. P0 = D1/(R − g) and D1 = (1 − b)EPS1, so P0/EPS1 = (1 − b)/(R − g).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-489","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kodiak Fibre retains 35% of earnings, earns ROE = 16%, and investors require R = 11% per annum. What is its forward P/E?","options":["5.91","12.04","18.52","58.33"],"answer":1,"explanation":"g = b × ROE = 0.35 × 16% = 5.6%, so P/E = (1 − 0.35)/(0.11 − 0.056) = 0.65/0.054 = 12.04. The 1/(R − g) shortcut (18.52) forgets the dividend share (1 − b); treating 0.35 as the payout flips b and gives 58.33; ignoring g gives 5.91.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-490","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Trillium Trucking pays out 60% of earnings, expects dividend growth of 6% per year, and R = 10% per annum. What is its forward P/E?","options":["3.75","10.00","15.00","25.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g) = payout/(R − g) = 0.60/0.04 = 15.00. Using b = 40% in the numerator gives 10.00; adding g to R gives 3.75; dropping the payout gives 25.00.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-491","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards pays out 35% of earnings, earns ROE = 12% on start-of-year equity, and R = 10.5% per annum. Assuming constant payout and ROE, what is its forward P/E? (Answer as a multiple, 2 decimals.)","answer":12.96,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1-0.65)/(0.105-0.65*0.12)","steps":[{"label":"Plowback b = 1 − payout","value":0.65,"check":"1-0.35","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growth g = b × ROE (in %)","value":7.8,"check":"0.65*0.12*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g)","value":12.963,"check":"(1-0.65)/(0.105-0.65*0.12)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["b = 1 − 0.35 = 0.65; g = 0.65 × 0.12 = 7.8%","P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g) = 0.35/(0.105 − 0.078) = 0.35/0.027 = 12.96"],"keystrokes":["0.65 × 0.12 = 0.078","0.35 ÷ (0.105 − 0.078) = 12.96"],"errors":[{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Treated 35% as b","value":10.32,"check":"0.65/(0.105-0.35*0.12)","explain":"Payout is 35%, so b = 65%: g = 7.8% and the numerator is the payout 0.35."},{"id":"forgot-payout","label":"Dropped (1 − b)","value":37.04,"check":"1/(0.105-0.65*0.12)","explain":"P/E = P0/EPS1 and P0 only capitalises the dividend share (1 − b) of EPS1."},{"id":"forgot-g","label":"Ignored growth in the denominator","value":3.33,"check":"0.35/0.105","explain":"R − g, not R, is the capitalisation rate."}],"explanation":"A 35% payout and 7.8% growth against R = 10.5% give a forward multiple near 13. Because the denominator R − g is small, the multiple is very sensitive to g and R.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s28-31"},{"id":"fin201-q08-492","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Grain Co-op just reported EPS of $3.85. It pays out 60% of earnings, ROE is 15%, R = 10.5% per annum, and payout and ROE stay constant. Use its forward P/E to find the share price today. (Answer in $.)","answer":54.41,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1-0.40)/(0.105-0.40*0.15)*3.85*(1+0.40*0.15)","steps":[{"label":"Growth g = (1 − payout) × ROE (in %)","value":6.0,"check":"0.40*0.15*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Forward P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g)","value":13.3333,"check":"(1-0.40)/(0.105-0.40*0.15)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Next year's EPS1 = EPS0(1 + g)","value":4.081,"check":"3.85*(1+0.40*0.15)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today = forward P/E × EPS1","value":54.4133,"check":"(1-0.40)/(0.105-0.40*0.15)*3.85*(1+0.40*0.15)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["b = 1 − 0.60 = 0.40; g = 0.40 × 0.15 = 6%","Forward P/E = 0.60/(0.105 − 0.06) = 0.60/0.045 = 13.3333","EPS1 = 3.85 × 1.06 = $4.0810","P0 = 13.3333 × 4.0810 = $54.41"],"keystrokes":["0.40 × 0.15 = 0.06","0.60 ÷ 0.045 = 13.3333","3.85 × 1.06 = 4.0810","13.3333 × 4.0810 = 54.41"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Applied the forward P/E to EPS0","value":51.33,"check":"(1-0.40)/(0.105-0.40*0.15)*3.85","explain":"A forward multiple pairs with EPS1 = EPS0(1 + g), not with last year's EPS."},{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Used the payout ratio as b (g = 9%)","value":111.91,"check":"(1-0.60)/(0.105-0.60*0.15)*3.85*(1+0.60*0.15)","explain":"Payout is 60%, so b = 40% and g = 6%."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew EPS twice","value":57.68,"check":"(1-0.40)/(0.105-0.40*0.15)*3.85*(1+0.40*0.15)**2","explain":"One growth step from EPS0 gives EPS1."}],"explanation":"Forward P/E × EPS1 is the Gordon price. Mixing a forward multiple with trailing EPS understates the price by exactly the factor (1 + g).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-493","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Laurentian Labs has a forward P/E of 16.5, pays out 60% of earnings, and its dividends are expected to grow 5.5% per year. What required return R does the market price imply? (Answer in % per annum.)","answer":9.14,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(0.055+0.60/16.5)*100","steps":[{"label":"Dividend share of price, (1 − b)/PE","value":0.0364,"check":"0.60/16.5","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required return R = g + (1 − b)/PE (in %)","value":9.1364,"check":"(0.055+0.60/16.5)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["P/E = (1 − b)/(R − g) → R − g = (1 − b)/PE = 0.60/16.5 = 3.6364%","R = g + 3.6364% = 5.5% + 3.6364% = 9.14% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.60 ÷ 16.5 = 0.036364","+ 0.055 = 0.091364 → 9.14%"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-g","label":"Forgot to add g back","value":3.64,"check":"0.60/16.5*100","explain":"R − g = 3.64% is the dividend yield D1/P0; the required return is yield plus g."},{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Used b = 40% in the numerator","value":7.92,"check":"(0.055+0.40/16.5)*100","explain":"The numerator is the payout (1 − b) = 60%."},{"id":"forgot-payout","label":"Used the earnings yield 1/PE","value":11.56,"check":"(0.055+1/16.5)*100","explain":"1/PE capitalises all earnings; only the payout share becomes dividends."}],"explanation":"Solving the forward P/E equation for R is the 'R = D1/P0 + g' relation in disguise: D1/P0 = (1 − b)/PE.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20, s30"},{"id":"fin201-q08-494","concept":"fin201-c08-forward-pe","also":["fin201-c08-implied-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rideau Rail has a forward P/E of 14.2 and its earnings are expected to grow 6.5% this year. What is its trailing P/E? (Answer as a multiple, 2 decimals.)","answer":15.12,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"14.2*1.065","steps":[{"label":"Growth factor EPS1/EPS0 = 1 + g","value":1.065,"check":"1.065","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Trailing P/E = forward P/E × (1 + g)","value":15.123,"check":"14.2*1.065","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Trailing = P0/EPS0 and forward = P0/EPS1, so trailing/forward = EPS1/EPS0 = 1 + g","Trailing P/E = 14.2 × 1.065 = 15.12"],"keystrokes":["14.2 × 1.065 = 15.12"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Divided by (1 + g)","value":13.33,"check":"14.2/1.065","explain":"Dividing gives a number smaller than the forward P/E; the trailing ratio must be the larger one for a growing firm."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Subtracted growth instead of compounding","value":13.28,"check":"14.2*(1-0.065)","explain":"The link between the two is multiplicative: EPS1 = EPS0(1 + g)."}],"explanation":"Same price, smaller denominator: trailing P/E exceeds forward P/E by the growth factor.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; L04 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-501","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"NPVGO stands for the net present value of growth opportunities. It is:","options":["the net present value of the firm's single largest investment project","the present value of all future dividends, P0 = D1/(R − g)","the price the firm would have if it grew at the industry growth rate","the part of the price above the no-growth value: NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A firm with no growth has value EPS1/R (a perpetuity of its earnings). Whatever the market pays above that is the value of the growth opportunities: NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R, so P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-502","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement of the NPVGO relationship matches the formula sheet?","options":["P0 = EPS0/R + NPVGO","P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO","P0 = EPS1 × R + NPVGO","P0 = EPS1/(R − g) − NPVGO"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The no-growth value is next year's earnings capitalised at R. EPS0 is last year's number (eps0-for-eps1 error), multiplying by R inverts the perpetuity, and NPVGO is added to, not subtracted from, the no-growth value.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1; L04 s33"},{"id":"fin201-q08-503","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fundy Marine expects EPS1 = $5.10 and R = 9% per annum. What does 5.10/0.09 = $56.67 represent?","options":["The value of the firm's growth opportunities, NPVGO, per share","The value of the share if the firm paid out all earnings and never grew","The Gordon growth price of the share, P0 = D1/(R − g)","The share's book value per share taken from the balance sheet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"EPS1/R is a perpetuity of constant earnings: the no-growth value. NPVGO is the market price minus this number.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33; S4 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-504","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Your valuation shows that a stock's NPVGO is negative at your discount rate. Which conclusion matches the course?","options":["The stock must be overvalued, so it should be sold if held or avoided at this price","NPVGO can never be negative, so there must be an arithmetic mistake in the valuation","Either undervalued (buy) or growth is destroying value (do not buy); the sign alone does not say","The firm should raise its plowback ratio to repair the negative NPVGO"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Professor Godfrey: NPVGO ought to be positive; if it is negative either the company is undervalued (you should buy it) or its growth opportunities destroy value (you should not). You must look at why the price is below EPS1/R.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-505","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly relates a firm's growth rate to its NPVGO?","options":["Fast growth does not guarantee a positive NPVGO; growth adds value only if reinvestment earns more than R","Any positive growth rate produces a positive NPVGO, since earnings keep rising","NPVGO is positive exactly when the firm pays dividends to its shareholders","NPVGO depends on the growth rate but not on the required return R"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPVGO measures value created by reinvestment, so it depends on ROE versus R, not on growth alone. Johnson Company grows 1% yet has NPVGO = −$7.06 (S4 s18), while Harrison grows 8% after year 6 and has NPVGO = +$68.14.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s14-19; L04 s34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-506","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT true of the NPVGO approach?","options":["It uses last year's earnings EPS0 to value the no-growth stream","It splits price into a no-growth value and a growth component","A negative NPVGO can mean growth is destroying value","The no-growth value is EPS1/R"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first dividend of the no-growth perpetuity arrives next year, so the numerator is EPS1, the forecast. Using EPS0 shifts the perpetuity one period and is the standard error (eps0-for-eps1).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-507","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the NPVGO formula use EPS1 rather than EPS0?","options":["EPS1 is always smaller than EPS0, which keeps NPVGO positive in the formula","EPS0 already includes the growth opportunities, so it would double count them","The formula sheet uses EPS1 because dividends are paid on forecast earnings only","A perpetuity is valued one period before its first payment, and next year's earnings are the first payment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EPS1/R is the PV today of earnings EPS1, EPS1, EPS1... received at dates 1, 2, 3, ... Using EPS0 would value a stream starting at date 0 and overstate the no-growth value by one year of earnings.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33"},{"id":"fin201-q08-508","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"From NPVGO, P/E = 1/R + NPVGO/EPS1. A firm with R = 10% has a forward P/E of 9. What does this say?","options":["The firm has strongly positive growth opportunities","NPVGO is zero because P/E is close to 1/R","The P/E is below 1/R = 10, so NPVGO is negative","The required return must be exactly 9%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"1/R = 10 is the multiple of a no-growth firm. A P/E of 9 sits below it, so NPVGO/EPS1 = 9 − 10 = −1 and NPVGO is negative. Compare Johnson (P/E 9.12 < 10) with Harrison (37.26 > 10) in Seminar 4.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s20-21"},{"id":"fin201-q08-509","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm retains most of its earnings but earns only 8% on them (ROE = 8%) while shareholders require R = 12%. What does the NPVGO framework say?","options":["NPVGO is positive, because retained earnings raise the growth rate","NPVGO is zero, because any positive ROE adds value","NPVGO is negative: shareholders would be better off if the earnings were paid out","NPVGO cannot be assessed without knowing the payout ratio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each retained dollar earns 8% when investors could earn 12% elsewhere, so reinvestment destroys value and the price falls below EPS1/R. This is the logic of the Johnson example.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s34; S4 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-510","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm trades at $74.80 with EPS1 = $4.40 and R = 10%. What does it tell you that EPS1/R = $44.00?","options":["The firm is overvalued by $44.00 relative to its earnings power","The firm's book equity is $44.00 per share on the balance sheet","The market expects the firm to pay a $44.00 dividend next year","About 41% of the price pays for growth opportunities; $44.00 is the no-growth value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"No-growth value $44.00 plus NPVGO $30.80 = price $74.80. Growth opportunities are 30.80/74.80 = 41.2% of the price.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-511","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Chinook Chemicals trades at $61.50. It earned $3.40 last year and analysts forecast EPS of $3.60 for next year. R = 9% per annum. What is NPVGO per share?","options":["-$21.50","$21.50","$23.72","$57.90"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R = 61.50 − 3.60/0.09 = 61.50 − 40.00 = $21.50. Using last year's EPS ($3.40) gives $23.72; subtracting in the wrong order flips the sign (−$21.50); forgetting to divide by R gives $57.90.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s34; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-512","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm has R = 8%, EPS1 = $2.50 and NPVGO = $12.00 per share. Using P/E = 1/R + NPVGO/EPS1, what is its forward P/E?","options":["4.80","12.50","17.30","24.50"],"answer":2,"explanation":"1/R = 12.50 and NPVGO/EPS1 = 12.00/2.50 = 4.80, so P/E = 17.30. Adding the dollar NPVGO to 1/R (24.50) mixes dollars with a multiple; using only NPVGO/EPS1 (4.80) or only 1/R (12.50) leaves out one of the two pieces. Check: P0 = 17.30 × 2.50 = $43.25 = 31.25 + 12.00.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s20-21"},{"id":"fin201-q08-513","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Brewing earned $2.00 this year and expects EPS1 = $2.10. R = 7% per annum and the value of its growth opportunities is NPVGO = $9.40 per share. What is the share price?","options":["$11.50","$20.60","$37.97","$39.40"],"answer":3,"explanation":"No-growth value = EPS1/R = 2.10/0.07 = $30.00; adding NPVGO gives P0 = $39.40. Using this year's EPS gives $37.97; subtracting NPVGO gives $20.60; skipping the division by R gives $11.50.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-514","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":["fin201-c08-gordon-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Fundy Marine just paid a dividend of $3.80, which is expected to grow 2% per year forever. R = 9% per annum and next year's EPS is forecast at $5.10. What is its NPVGO per share? (Answer in $; a negative answer is possible.)","answer":-1.3,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"3.80*1.02/(0.09-0.02)-5.10/0.09","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1","value":3.876,"check":"3.80*1.02","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":55.3714,"check":"3.80*1.02/(0.09-0.02)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"No-growth value EPS1/R","value":56.6667,"check":"5.10/0.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R","value":-1.2952,"check":"3.80*1.02/(0.09-0.02)-5.10/0.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["D1 = 3.80 × 1.02 = $3.876; P0 = 3.876/(0.09 − 0.02) = $55.37","No-growth value = 5.10/0.09 = $56.67","NPVGO = 55.3714 − 56.6667 = −$1.30 per share","Negative: either the shares are undervalued or growth destroys value (the firm retains about a quarter of EPS1 yet grows only 2%)."],"keystrokes":["3.876 ÷ 0.07 = 55.3714","5.10 ÷ 0.09 = 56.6667","55.3714 − 56.6667 = −1.30"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used D0 in the Gordon numerator","value":-2.38,"check":"3.80/(0.09-0.02)-5.10/0.09","explain":"P0 needs D1 = 3.80 × 1.02 = 3.876."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":1.3,"check":"5.10/0.09-3.80*1.02/(0.09-0.02)","explain":"NPVGO is the market (model) price MINUS the no-growth value."},{"id":"forgot-r","label":"Subtracted EPS1 without dividing by R","value":50.27,"check":"3.80*1.02/(0.09-0.02)-5.10","explain":"The no-growth value is the perpetuity EPS1/R."}],"explanation":"Fundy Marine pays out about 75% of EPS1 yet grows only 2%, so its retained earnings are earning little: growth destroys a little value (-$1.30). This is the Johnson pattern in Seminar 4.","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s15-18; L04 s34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-515","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":["fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Boreal Biotech pays no dividend for six years. Its first dividend, D7 = $4.20, comes at the end of year 7 and then grows 7% per year forever. R = 11.5% per annum and EPS1 is forecast at $1.95. What is NPVGO per share? (Answer in $.)","answer":31.62,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"4.20/(0.115-0.07)/1.115**6-1.95/0.115","steps":[{"label":"Horizon price P6 = D7/(R − g)","value":93.3333,"check":"4.20/(0.115-0.07)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price today P0 = P6/(1 + R)^6","value":48.5722,"check":"4.20/(0.115-0.07)/1.115**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"No-growth value EPS1/R","value":16.9565,"check":"1.95/0.115","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R","value":31.6157,"check":"4.20/(0.115-0.07)/1.115**6-1.95/0.115","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["P6 = D7/(R − g) = 4.20/(0.115 − 0.07) = $93.3333","P0 = 93.3333/1.115⁶ = $48.57","No-growth value = 1.95/0.115 = $16.96","NPVGO = 48.5722 − 16.9565 = $31.62: growth creates value"],"keystrokes":["4.20 ÷ 0.045 = 93.3333; N = 6, I/Y = 11.5, FV = 93.3333, CPT PV = −48.5722","1.95 ÷ 0.115 = 16.9565","48.5722 − 16.9565 = 31.62"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted P6 seven years","value":26.61,"check":"4.20/(0.115-0.07)/1.115**7-1.95/0.115","explain":"P6 is a year-6 value (one period before D7)."},{"id":"horizon-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount P6 at all","value":76.38,"check":"4.20/(0.115-0.07)-1.95/0.115","explain":"P6 must be discounted to date 0 before comparing with EPS1/R."},{"id":"grew-twice","label":"Grew D7 once more before dividing","value":35.02,"check":"4.20*1.07/(0.115-0.07)/1.115**6-1.95/0.115","explain":"D7 is already the first constant-growth dividend."}],"explanation":"Almost two thirds of the price (65%) is growth value, typical of a young firm. NPVGO is positive, so growth creates value (Harrison pattern).","source":"generated","ref":"S4 s17-19; SM 8-14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-516","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":["fin201-c08-plowback-growth"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Whitecap Wind expects EPS1 = $6.00. It retains 60% of earnings, earns ROE = 10% on start-of-year equity, and R = 13% per annum. Payout and ROE stay constant. What is NPVGO per share? (Answer in $; the sign matters.)","answer":-11.87,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"6*0.4/(0.13-0.6*0.10)-6/0.13","steps":[{"label":"Growth g = b × ROE (in %)","value":6.0,"check":"0.6*0.10*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Next dividend D1 = (1 − b) × EPS1","value":2.4,"check":"6*0.4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":34.2857,"check":"6*0.4/(0.13-0.6*0.10)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"No-growth value EPS1/R","value":46.1538,"check":"6/0.13","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPVGO","value":-11.8681,"check":"6*0.4/(0.13-0.6*0.10)-6/0.13","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["g = 0.60 × 10% = 6%; D1 = 0.40 × 6.00 = $2.40","P0 = 2.40/(0.13 − 0.06) = $34.29","No-growth value = 6.00/0.13 = $46.15","NPVGO = 34.2857 − 46.1538 = −$11.87: retaining at ROE 10% < R 13% destroys value"],"keystrokes":["0.60 × 0.10 = 0.06","2.40 ÷ 0.07 = 34.2857","6 ÷ 0.13 = 46.1538","34.2857 − 46.1538 = −11.87"],"errors":[{"id":"payout-as-plowback","label":"Used 40% as b (g = 4%)","value":-6.15,"check":"6*0.6/(0.13-0.4*0.10)-6/0.13","explain":"The firm retains 60%, so b = 0.60 and the dividend is 40% of EPS1."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":11.87,"check":"6/0.13-6*0.4/(0.13-0.6*0.10)","explain":"NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R, which is negative here."},{"id":"forgot-r","label":"Compared P0 with EPS1 instead of EPS1/R","value":28.29,"check":"6*0.4/(0.13-0.6*0.10)-6","explain":"The no-growth value is the perpetuity EPS1/R = 46.15, not EPS1 itself."}],"explanation":"ROE (10%) is below R (13%), so each reinvested dollar is worth less than a dollar to shareholders: the more the firm retains, the lower NPVGO. Paying everything out would give $46.15.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s27-34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-517","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cobalt Peak Software trades at $82.40 with EPS1 = $5.20 forecast and R = 10% per annum. What percentage of its price is NPVGO? (Answer in %.)","answer":36.89,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(82.40-5.20/0.10)/82.40*100","steps":[{"label":"No-growth value EPS1/R","value":52.0,"check":"5.20/0.10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R","value":30.4,"check":"82.40-5.20/0.10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPVGO as % of price","value":36.8932,"check":"(82.40-5.20/0.10)/82.40*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["No-growth value = 5.20/0.10 = $52.00","NPVGO = 82.40 − 52.00 = $30.40","Share of price = 30.40/82.40 = 36.89%"],"keystrokes":["5.20 ÷ 0.10 = 52.00","82.40 − 52.00 = 30.40","30.40 ÷ 82.40 = 0.3689"],"errors":[{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Gave the no-growth share of the price","value":63.11,"check":"5.20/0.10/82.40*100","explain":"That is the complement (63.1%); the question asks for the growth share."},{"id":"wrong-base","label":"Divided NPVGO by the no-growth value instead of the price","value":58.46,"check":"(82.40-5.20/0.10)/(5.20/0.10)*100","explain":"The share of the PRICE uses P0 in the denominator; NPVGO/(EPS1/R) compares growth with the no-growth part (58.5%)."}],"explanation":"Over a third of the price is growth value. The rest, $52, is what the current earnings alone are worth.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34; S4 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-518","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock trades at $64.00. You estimate its growth opportunities are worth NPVGO = $14.50 per share and R = 9.5% per annum. What EPS1 does that imply? (Answer in $.)","answer":4.7,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(64-14.5)*0.095","steps":[{"label":"No-growth value P0 − NPVGO","value":49.5,"check":"64-14.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EPS1 = (P0 − NPVGO) × R","value":4.7025,"check":"(64-14.5)*0.095","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO → EPS1/R = 64.00 − 14.50 = $49.50","EPS1 = 49.50 × 0.095 = $4.70"],"keystrokes":["64 − 14.5 = 49.5","49.5 × 0.095 = 4.7025"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-error","label":"Added NPVGO instead of subtracting","value":7.46,"check":"(64+14.5)*0.095","explain":"NPVGO is part of the price, so the no-growth part is P0 − NPVGO."},{"id":"forgot-r","label":"Stopped before multiplying by R","value":49.5,"check":"64-14.5","explain":"49.50 is the no-growth value EPS1/R, not EPS1 itself."}],"explanation":"Rearranging P0 = EPS1/R + NPVGO gives EPS1 = (P0 − NPVGO) × R. It is the same relationship used backwards.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34"},{"id":"fin201-q08-519","concept":"fin201-c08-npvgo","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is a correct statement about NPVGO?","options":["NPVGO = EPS1/R − P0","NPVGO can never be negative","NPVGO uses this year's EPS0 in the no-growth value","NPVGO equals the market price of the stock","None of the above"],"answer":4,"explanation":"NPVGO = P0 − EPS1/R: the order is price minus the no-growth value (not the reverse), it can be negative (Johnson, S4 s18), the no-growth value uses next year's EPS1, and NPVGO is only the growth part of the price. None of the listed statements is correct.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s33-34; S4 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-521","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"From a quote screen, the earnings growth the market expects this year is implied by:","options":["trailing P/E divided by forward P/E, minus 1","trailing P/E minus forward P/E","forward P/E divided by trailing P/E, minus 1","the dividend yield minus the forward dividend yield"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Trailing P/E = P0/EPS0 and forward P/E = P0/EPS1, so trailing ÷ forward = EPS1/EPS0 = 1 + g. Subtract 1 for the growth rate. The price cancels, so the market's expected growth is read off the two ratios.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; MAP A6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-522","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A quote screen lists a price/earnings ratio of 21.37 and a consensus forward P/E of 19.52. Which question can be answered directly from these two numbers alone?","options":["What is the company's required return R on its shares?","How many shares does the company have outstanding?","What EPS growth does the market expect this year?","What is the company's plowback ratio this year?"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The ratio 21.37/19.52 = EPS1/EPS0 gives expected EPS growth. Shares need the market capitalisation and price, R needs a dividend model, and plowback needs payout data.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; MAP A6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-523","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock has trailing P/E of 18 and forward P/E of 16. Which expression gives the expected EPS growth?","options":["16/18 − 1","18 − 16","(18 − 16)/18","18/16 − 1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EPS1/EPS0 = (P0/16)/(P0/18) = 18/16, so g = 18/16 − 1 = 12.5%. Inverting the ratio gives −11.1%, the difference of the two multiples is not a growth rate, and dividing by the trailing multiple gives the wrong base (11.1%).","source":"generated","ref":"MAP A6; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-524","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"You know a stock's price, its trailing P/E and its forward P/E. To find next year's expected EPS you should:","options":["divide the price by the forward P/E","divide the price by the trailing P/E","multiply the price by the forward P/E","multiply the trailing EPS by the forward P/E"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forward P/E = P0/EPS1, so EPS1 = P0/forward P/E. Dividing by the trailing P/E gives EPS0, last year's earnings (a trailing-forward mix-up).","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; L04 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-525","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does the growth implied by the trailing and forward P/E differ from the growth rate g in the Gordon model?","options":["It is the same thing as Gordon's g, because both measure the market's expected growth","It measures dividend growth, whereas Gordon's g measures earnings growth","It is a long-run rate, whereas Gordon's g is a one-year growth rate","It is the expected EPS growth for one year; Gordon's g is a constant dividend growth rate forever"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A trailing/forward comparison only tells you EPS1/EPS0 for the next twelve months, which can be temporarily high or low. Gordon's g is a permanent rate used in P0 = D1/(R − g). They can be equal for a steady-growth firm but need not be.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s30; MAP A6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-526","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following can NOT be found from a quote screen's price, market capitalisation, trailing P/E and forward P/E?","options":["The number of shares outstanding","The company's required return R","Last year's EPS","Next year's expected net income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shares = market cap/price; EPS0 = price/trailing P/E; net income next year = price × shares/forward P/E. R is a property of investors' required compensation and needs a valuation model (R = D1/P0 + g), not just multiples.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; SM 8-22"},{"id":"fin201-q08-527","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the forward P/E lower than the trailing P/E for a company whose earnings are expected to rise?","options":["The price is the same, but the forward ratio divides it by a larger EPS","Forward earnings are discounted at a higher rate than trailing earnings","The price falls whenever earnings are forecast rather than reported as actual","Analysts deliberately forecast low earnings to make stocks look cheap"],"answer":0,"explanation":"P0 is the same in both ratios. EPS1 > EPS0 makes P0/EPS1 < P0/EPS0, and the gap between them is exactly the growth factor 1 + g.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s14, s18"},{"id":"fin201-q08-528","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock has trailing P/E 12.4 and forward P/E 13.6. What does the market expect for this year's earnings?","options":["Growth of about 9.7%, because the forward P/E is higher than the trailing P/E","No change, because both P/E ratios use the same price","Growth of about 1.2%, which is the difference between the two ratios","A decline of about 8.8%, because forecast EPS is below last year's EPS"],"answer":3,"explanation":"12.4/13.6 − 1 = −8.8%: the forward multiple is higher only because forecast EPS is lower than last year's. A forward P/E above the trailing P/E signals expected falling earnings.","source":"generated","ref":"MAP A6; PRAC q9"},{"id":"fin201-q08-529","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A quote screen shows a trailing P/E of 22.4 and a consensus forward P/E of 20.0. What growth in EPS does the market expect this year?","options":["−10.71%","2.40%","12.00%","112.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"EPS1/EPS0 = 22.4/20.0 = 1.12, so g = 12.00%. The difference of the multiples (2.40) is not a growth rate; inverting the ratio gives -10.71%; leaving out the −1 gives 112%.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; MAP A6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-530","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Harbourfront Bakeries trades at $36.00 and just paid a dividend of $1.44. If R = 10% per annum, what constant dividend growth rate does the price imply?","options":["5.77%","6.00%","13.46%","14.00%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"36 = 1.44(1 + g)/(0.10 − g) → 3.60 − 36g = 1.44 + 1.44g → g = (3.60 − 1.44)/(36 + 1.44) = 5.77%. The shortcut R − D0/P0 (6.00%) treats D0 as next year's dividend; adding D0 in the numerator gives 13.46%; adding the yield to R gives 14.00%.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-531","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":["fin201-c08-quote-screen"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A quote screen for Laurentian Labs shows a trailing price/earnings ratio of 21.37 and a consensus forward P/E of 19.52. What rate of growth in earnings per share is expected this year? (Answer in %.)","answer":9.48,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(21.37/19.52-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"EPS1/EPS0 = trailing P/E ÷ forward P/E","value":1.0948,"check":"21.37/19.52","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected growth (in %)","value":9.4775,"check":"(21.37/19.52-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Trailing P/E = P0/EPS0 and forward P/E = P0/EPS1","EPS1/EPS0 = 21.37/19.52 = 1.0948","g = 1.0948 − 1 = 9.48% this year"],"keystrokes":["21.37 ÷ 19.52 = 1.0948","− 1 = 0.0948 → 9.48%"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Inverted the ratio (forward ÷ trailing − 1)","value":-8.66,"check":"(19.52/21.37-1)*100","explain":"Forward EPS is the larger number, so the ratio that exceeds 1 is trailing ÷ forward."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Subtracted the two multiples","value":1.85,"check":"21.37-19.52","explain":"The multiples are ratios of the same price; the growth factor is their quotient, not their difference."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot to subtract 1","value":109.48,"check":"21.37/19.52*100","explain":"1.0948 is the growth factor 1 + g; g is the 0.0948."}],"explanation":"The price cancels out: trailing ÷ forward P/E = EPS1/EPS0. This is the calculation in part (b) of the sample-exam quote-screen question.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; MAP A6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-532","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":["fin201-c08-quote-screen"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rideau Rail trades at $45.60 with a trailing P/E of 15.2 and a forward P/E of 14.0. What EPS growth does the market expect this year? (Answer in %.)","answer":8.57,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(45.60/14.0)/(45.60/15.2)*100-100","steps":[{"label":"Last year's EPS0 = price/trailing P/E","value":3.0,"check":"45.60/15.2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected EPS1 = price/forward P/E","value":3.2571,"check":"45.60/14.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Growth g = EPS1/EPS0 − 1 (in %)","value":8.5714,"check":"(45.60/14.0)/(45.60/15.2)*100-100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["EPS0 = 45.60/15.2 = $3.0000","EPS1 = 45.60/14.0 = $3.2571","g = 3.2571/3.0000 − 1 = 8.57%"],"keystrokes":["45.60 ÷ 15.2 = 3.00","45.60 ÷ 14.0 = 3.2571","3.2571 ÷ 3.00 − 1 = 0.0857"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Reported EPS1/EPS0 as a percentage","value":108.57,"check":"(45.60/14.0)/(45.60/15.2)*100","explain":"The ratio 1.0857 includes the base; growth is the extra 8.57%."},{"id":"wrong-base","label":"Divided the increase by EPS1 instead of EPS0","value":7.89,"check":"((45.60/14.0)-(45.60/15.2))/(45.60/14.0)*100","explain":"Growth is measured on the starting EPS0."},{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Swapped trailing and forward","value":-7.89,"check":"(14.0/15.2-1)*100","explain":"Trailing P/E is the larger one for a growing firm; the ratio forward ÷ trailing gives a decline."}],"explanation":"Going through EPS0 and EPS1 gives the same answer as the ratio shortcut and is a good check.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; SM 8-22"},{"id":"fin201-q08-533","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":["fin201-c08-required-return-components"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards trades at $48.00 and just paid a dividend of $1.92. If R = 9.5% per annum, what constant dividend growth rate does the market price imply? (Answer in % per annum.)","answer":5.29,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(48*0.095-1.92)/(48+1.92)*100","steps":[{"label":"Price × R","value":4.56,"check":"48*0.095","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Implied growth g = (P0 R − D0)/(P0 + D0) (in %)","value":5.2885,"check":"(48*0.095-1.92)/(48+1.92)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["P0 = D0(1 + g)/(R − g) → P0 R − P0 g = D0 + D0 g","g = (P0 R − D0)/(P0 + D0) = (4.56 − 1.92)/(48 + 1.92) = 2.64/49.92","g = 5.29% per annum"],"keystrokes":["48 × 0.095 = 4.56","(4.56 − 1.92) ÷ (48 + 1.92) × 100 = 5.29"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used R − D0/P0","value":5.5,"check":"(0.095-1.92/48)*100","explain":"D0/P0 is not the dividend yield: the yield uses D1 = D0(1 + g)."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Added D0 to P0 R in the numerator","value":12.98,"check":"(48*0.095+1.92)/(48+1.92)*100","explain":"Collecting terms: P0 R − D0 = g (P0 + D0)."},{"id":"g-as-r","label":"Added the yield to R","value":13.5,"check":"(0.095+1.92/48)*100","explain":"R = yield + g, so g = R − yield, not R + yield."}],"explanation":"With D0 given, the algebra needs the (P0 + D0) denominator. If D1 were given, the shortcut g = R − D1/P0 would be exact.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-534","concept":"fin201-c08-implied-growth","also":["fin201-c08-quote-screen"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Gatineau Glass trades at $38.40 with a market capitalisation of $2,400 million, a trailing P/E of 17.5 and a consensus forward P/E of 15.9. What net income does the market expect next year? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":150.94,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2400/15.9","steps":[{"label":"Shares outstanding (millions) = market cap/price","value":62.5,"check":"2400/38.40","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected EPS1 = price/forward P/E","value":2.4151,"check":"38.40/15.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected net income = EPS1 × shares ($ millions)","value":150.9434,"check":"2400/15.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Shares = 2,400/38.40 = 62.5 million","EPS1 = 38.40/15.9 = $2.4151","Net income next year = 2.4151 × 62.5 = $150.94 million (also market cap ÷ forward P/E)"],"keystrokes":["2400 ÷ 38.40 = 62.5","38.40 ÷ 15.9 = 2.4151","2.4151 × 62.5 = 150.94"],"errors":[{"id":"trailing-forward-mix","label":"Used the trailing P/E (this gives last year's income)","value":137.14,"check":"2400/17.5","explain":"Dividing by the trailing P/E gives last year's net income, $137.14 million."},{"id":"units","label":"Stopped at shares outstanding","value":62.5,"check":"2400/38.40","explain":"62.5 is the number of shares in millions; net income needs EPS1 as well."}],"explanation":"Net income = market cap ÷ P/E. With the forward ratio you get next year's expected income; with the trailing ratio, last year's. The ratio of the two is the implied growth (10.1% here).","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q9; SM 8-22"},{"id":"fin201-q08-541","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Free cash flow to the firm (FCF or FCFF) is:","options":["the cash left for shareholders after interest and debt repayments have been paid","net income after tax plus depreciation, before CAPEX and working capital","the after-tax cash from operations available to lenders and shareholders, EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","operating revenue minus operating expenses, which is EBITDA"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FCF is measured BEFORE any payment to capital providers, so it is built from EBIT, not from net income. The cash left for shareholders after lenders are paid is FCFE; EBITDA ignores tax, CAPEX and working capital.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s38-40; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-542","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which expression is the free cash flow formula on the formula sheet?","options":["NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","EBIT(1 − T) + D&A + CAPEX − ΔWC","EBIT(1 − T) − Interest + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC"],"answer":3,"explanation":"FCF taxes EBIT as if the firm had no debt, adds back non-cash D&A, subtracts the cash spent on CAPEX and the cash tied up in working capital. NPAT-based and interest-based versions describe cash to shareholders, not to the whole firm; adding CAPEX has the wrong sign.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2; L04 s40"},{"id":"fin201-q08-543","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An analyst forecasts a firm's free cash flows and discounts them at the firm's overall discount rate. What does the result represent?","options":["The value of the shareholders' equity after subtracting debt","The accounting book value of the firm's total assets","The present value of next year's dividend per share","Enterprise value (EV), the value of the operations to all investors"],"answer":3,"explanation":"FCF belongs to lenders and shareholders together, so its PV is EV. Equity is found afterwards: Equity = EV − Debt + Cash.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37-42"},{"id":"fin201-q08-544","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm's FCF has just been reported at $5.2 million and is expected to grow 2.5% per year forever. R = 8.5% per annum. Which expression gives EV?","options":["EV = 5.2/(0.085 − 0.025)","EV = 5.2 × 1.025/(0.085 − 0.025)","EV = 5.2/0.085","EV = 5.2 × 1.025/(0.085 + 0.025)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Constant growing FCF is a growing perpetuity: EV = FCF1/(R − g) with FCF1 = FCF0(1 + g). Using FCF0 is the d0-for-d1 error; dividing by R alone ignores growth; adding g to R is a sign slip.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19, s37; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-545","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"How does FCF differ from net income (NPAT)?","options":["FCF is after interest, whereas NPAT is calculated before any interest expense","FCF includes only operating revenue and expenses, whereas NPAT also includes tax","They are the same figure whenever the firm pays no dividend","FCF is before interest and deducts CAPEX and ΔWC; NPAT is after interest and ignores both"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NPAT is an accounting profit after interest and tax with depreciation deducted. FCF starts from EBIT(1 − T), so lenders' interest is not deducted, then converts profit to cash by adding back D&A and deducting reinvestment (CAPEX and ΔWC).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s38-40"},{"id":"fin201-q08-546","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following does NOT appear in the FCF formula?","options":["The tax rate T applied to EBIT","Capital expenditures (CAPEX)","Interest expense and the change in debt","The change in working capital (ΔWC)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Interest and ΔDebt are payments to and from lenders; FCF is the cash generated BEFORE those flows. They appear in FCFE (through NPAT and + ΔDebt), not in FCF.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s40-41; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-547","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is FCF built from EBIT(1 − T) rather than from net income?","options":["Net income is not affected by taxes, so EBIT(1 − T) is the more accurate measure","EBIT is a cash figure while net income is a non-cash accounting figure","FCF is the cash for ALL capital providers, so it must not be reduced by interest paid to lenders","Interest is not a real expense for the firm, so it is always ignored in valuation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lenders and shareholders are both claimants on FCF. Deducting interest first would measure only what remains for shareholders (and also needs the matching ΔDebt, which is the FCFE route).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s38-40"},{"id":"fin201-q08-548","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A fast-growing retailer reports negative FCF this year because CAPEX far exceeds D&A. What does this imply for its EV?","options":["EV must be negative, because the first cash flow is negative and everything is discounted","EV equals zero until the year in which FCF first turns positive for the firm","The firm should be valued with the Gordon model using this year's FCF as D0","Nothing by itself: EV is the PV of ALL future FCFs, which may be large and positive later"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A growth firm reinvests heavily today and is valued on later cash flows (a non-constant growth timeline with horizon value). A negative first-year FCF cannot be run through FCF/R or FCF1/(R − g).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-549","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: if FCFs are discounted to give enterprise value, the equity value is EV − Debt + Cash.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. EV = Equity + Debt − Cash, so Equity = EV − Debt + Cash. Cash is added back because it is not part of the operations valued by FCF but belongs to shareholders (net debt = Debt − Cash).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s42; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-550","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A software firm has EV = $80 million, debt of $5 million and cash of $25 million. Its equity value is:","options":["$60 million, because debt and cash are both subtracted","$100 million, larger than EV because cash exceeds debt","$75 million, because debt is subtracted and cash ignored","$80 million, because equity equals EV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash = 80 − 5 + 25 = $100 million. With net cash (cash > debt), equity exceeds EV. Subtracting cash is the sign slip of confusing equity with EV.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s42; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-551","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Brewing reports (in $ thousands): revenue 3,200; operating costs excluding D&A 2,150; D&A 250; interest 90. The tax rate is 30%, CAPEX is 300 and ΔWC is 40. What is its free cash flow?","options":["$380 thousand","$407 thousand","$470 thousand","$550 thousand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"EBIT = 3,200 − 2,150 − 250 = 800; EBIT(1 − T) = 560. FCF = 560 + 250 − 300 − 40 = 470. Starting from NPAT (497) gives 407; subtracting interest from EBIT(1 − T) gives 380; adding ΔWC instead of subtracting gives 550.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s38-40; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-552","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm's free cash flows are worth an enterprise value of $120.0 million. It has debt of $45.0 million and cash of $8.0 million. What is the value of its equity? (in $ millions)","options":["$67.0 million","$75.0 million","$83.0 million","$157.0 million"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash = 120.0 − 45.0 + 8.0 = $83.0 million. Subtracting cash too gives $67.0 million; adding debt gives $157.0 million; ignoring cash gives $75.0 million.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s42; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-553","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tamarack Solar just generated FCF of $2.5 million. FCF is expected to grow 3% per year forever and the firm's discount rate is 9% per annum. What is its enterprise value? (in $ millions)","options":["$21.46 million","$28.61 million","$41.67 million","$42.92 million"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EV = FCF1/(R − g) = 2.5 × 1.03/(0.09 − 0.03) = 2.575/0.06 = $42.92 million. Using FCF0 gives $41.67 million; adding g to R gives $21.46 million; dividing by R alone gives $28.61 million.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19, s37"},{"id":"fin201-q08-554","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cascade Cabinetry's year shows (in $ millions): sales 48.0; cash operating costs 33.6; D&A 4.2; interest 1.5. The tax rate is 28%, CAPEX is 5.0 and ΔWC is 0.8. FCF is expected to stay constant forever and the firm's discount rate is 9% per annum. What is its enterprise value? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":63.82,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((48.0-33.6-4.2)*0.72+4.2-5.0-0.8)/0.09","steps":[{"label":"EBITDA = sales − operating costs","value":14.4,"check":"48.0-33.6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EBIT = EBITDA − D&A","value":10.2,"check":"48.0-33.6-4.2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EBIT(1 − T)","value":7.344,"check":"(48.0-33.6-4.2)*0.72","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","value":5.744,"check":"(48.0-33.6-4.2)*0.72+4.2-5.0-0.8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Enterprise value = FCF/R","value":63.8222,"check":"((48.0-33.6-4.2)*0.72+4.2-5.0-0.8)/0.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["EBITDA = 48.0 − 33.6 = 14.4; EBIT = 14.4 − 4.2 = 10.2","EBIT(1 − T) = 10.2 × 0.72 = 7.344","FCF = 7.344 + 4.2 − 5.0 − 0.8 = $5.744 million (interest is not deducted)","EV = 5.744/0.09 = $63.82 million"],"keystrokes":["48.0 − 33.6 − 4.2 = 10.2","10.2 × 0.72 = 7.344","7.344 + 4.2 − 5.0 − 0.8 = 5.744","5.744 ÷ 0.09 = 63.82"],"errors":[{"id":"npat-in-fcf","label":"Started from NPAT (after interest)","value":51.82,"check":"((48.0-33.6-4.2-1.5)*0.72+4.2-5.0-0.8)/0.09","explain":"NPAT is after interest; FCF starts from EBIT(1 − T) so lenders' interest is not deducted."},{"id":"interest-in-fcf","label":"Subtracted interest from EBIT(1 − T)","value":47.16,"check":"((48.0-33.6-4.2)*0.72-1.5+4.2-5.0-0.8)/0.09","explain":"Interest is a payment to a capital provider; FCF is the cash before those payments."},{"id":"added-capex","label":"Added CAPEX instead of subtracting","value":174.93,"check":"((48.0-33.6-4.2)*0.72+4.2+5.0-0.8)/0.09","explain":"CAPEX is cash spent on assets, so it reduces FCF."}],"explanation":"Income statement → EBIT → EBIT(1 − T) → FCF → EV. Interest never appears in FCF; it only matters later when debt is subtracted from EV to reach equity.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s38-43"},{"id":"fin201-q08-555","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Laurentian Labs has constant FCF of $7.2 million per year forever and a discount rate of 8.5% per annum. It has debt of $24.0 million, cash of $3.5 million and 5.0 million shares. What is the equity value per share? (Answer in $.)","answer":12.84,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(7.2/0.085-24+3.5)/5","steps":[{"label":"Enterprise value = FCF/R ($ millions)","value":84.7059,"check":"7.2/0.085","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Equity = EV − Debt + Cash ($ millions)","value":64.2059,"check":"7.2/0.085-24+3.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Equity per share","value":12.8412,"check":"(7.2/0.085-24+3.5)/5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["EV = 7.2/0.085 = $84.7059 million","Equity = 84.7059 − 24.0 + 3.5 = $64.2059 million","Per share = 64.2059/5.0 = $12.84"],"keystrokes":["7.2 ÷ 0.085 = 84.7059","84.7059 − 24.0 + 3.5 = 64.2059","64.2059 ÷ 5 = 12.84"],"errors":[{"id":"net-debt-sign","label":"Subtracted cash as well as debt","value":11.44,"check":"(7.2/0.085-24-3.5)/5","explain":"Cash is added back: net debt = Debt − Cash."},{"id":"ev-as-equity","label":"Divided EV by the share count","value":16.94,"check":"7.2/0.085/5","explain":"EV includes the lenders' claim; shareholders only own EV − Debt + Cash."},{"id":"net-debt-sign","label":"Added debt and subtracted cash","value":21.04,"check":"(7.2/0.085+24-3.5)/5","explain":"That turns equity into something larger than EV; debt is a claim to subtract."}],"explanation":"Discounting FCF gives EV; per-share value requires moving from EV to equity and then dividing by shares.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s42-43"},{"id":"fin201-q08-556","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow Valley Pipelines just produced FCF of $3.4 million, expected to grow 3.5% per year forever. The firm's discount rate is 9.5% per annum. What is its enterprise value? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":58.65,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"3.4*1.035/(0.095-0.035)","steps":[{"label":"Next FCF1 = FCF0(1 + g)","value":3.519,"check":"3.4*1.035","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Enterprise value = FCF1/(R − g)","value":58.65,"check":"3.4*1.035/(0.095-0.035)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["FCF1 = 3.4 × 1.035 = $3.519 million","EV = 3.519/(0.095 − 0.035) = 3.519/0.06 = $58.65 million"],"keystrokes":["3.4 × 1.035 = 3.519","3.519 ÷ 0.06 = 58.65"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used FCF0 in the numerator","value":56.67,"check":"3.4/(0.095-0.035)","explain":"The growing perpetuity needs next year's FCF."},{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R","value":27.07,"check":"3.4*1.035/(0.095+0.035)","explain":"The denominator is R − g."},{"id":"forgot-g","label":"Divided by R alone","value":37.04,"check":"3.4*1.035/0.095","explain":"Growth lowers the capitalisation rate to R − g."}],"explanation":"FCF valuation uses exactly the same growing-perpetuity algebra as the Gordon model, with FCF in place of dividends and the firm's rate in place of the equity rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19, s37"},{"id":"fin201-q08-557","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":["fin201-c08-nonconstant-timeline"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Whitecap Wind expects free cash flows of $2.0 million, $2.6 million and $3.1 million at the ends of years 1 to 3. From year 4 FCF grows 3% per year forever. The firm's discount rate is 10% per annum. What is its enterprise value today? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":40.57,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.0/1.1+2.6/1.1**2+(3.1+3.1*1.03/(0.10-0.03))/1.1**3","steps":[{"label":"First constant-growth FCF4 = FCF3 × 1.03","value":3.193,"check":"3.1*1.03","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Horizon value at year 3: EV3 = FCF4/(R − g)","value":45.6143,"check":"3.1*1.03/(0.10-0.03)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of FCF1 and FCF2","value":3.9669,"check":"2.0/1.1+2.6/1.1**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of FCF3 plus EV3","value":36.5998,"check":"(3.1+3.1*1.03/(0.10-0.03))/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Enterprise value today","value":40.5667,"check":"2.0/1.1+2.6/1.1**2+(3.1+3.1*1.03/(0.10-0.03))/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["FCF4 = 3.1 × 1.03 = 3.193; EV3 = 3.193/(0.10 − 0.03) = $45.6143 million","PV(FCF1, FCF2) = 2.0/1.10 + 2.6/1.10² = 1.8182 + 2.1488 = 3.9669","PV(FCF3 + EV3) = (3.1 + 45.6143)/1.10³ = 36.5998","EV0 = 3.9669 + 36.5998 = $40.57 million"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 2.0, C02 = 2.6, C03 = 3.1 + 45.6143 = 48.7143 (F = 1)","NPV: I = 10, CPT NPV = 40.57"],"errors":[{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used FCF3 instead of FCF4 in the horizon numerator","value":39.57,"check":"2.0/1.1+2.6/1.1**2+(3.1+3.1/(0.10-0.03))/1.1**3","explain":"The horizon value needs the first constant-growth FCF, FCF4 = FCF3(1.03)."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted FCF3 + EV3 four years","value":37.24,"check":"2.0/1.1+2.6/1.1**2+(3.1+3.1*1.03/(0.10-0.03))/1.1**4","explain":"EV3 is a year-3 value: discount three periods."},{"id":"omitted-dividend","label":"Left out FCF3","value":38.24,"check":"2.0/1.1+2.6/1.1**2+3.1*1.03/(0.10-0.03)/1.1**3","explain":"FCF3 is paid at year 3 along with EV3, which only covers year 4 onward."}],"explanation":"FCF valuation with a timeline is non-constant growth again: explicit flows, a horizon value at the date before constant growth begins, then discount everything.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37; TB 8.1"},{"id":"fin201-q08-558","concept":"fin201-c08-fcf-ev","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Yukon Drillers earns EBIT of $12.0 million per year forever. A tax change lowers its tax rate from 30% to 26%; CAPEX equals D&A and ΔWC is zero. The firm's discount rate is 9% per annum. By how much does enterprise value rise? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":5.33,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"12.0*(0.30-0.26)/0.09","steps":[{"label":"Increase in FCF = EBIT × (T_old − T_new)","value":0.48,"check":"12.0*(0.30-0.26)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Increase in EV = ΔFCF/R","value":5.3333,"check":"12.0*(0.30-0.26)/0.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["FCF before = 12.0 × 0.70 = 8.40 (CAPEX = D&A, ΔWC = 0); after = 12.0 × 0.74 = 8.88","ΔFCF = $0.48 million per year","ΔEV = 0.48/0.09 = $5.33 million"],"keystrokes":["12.0 × (0.30 − 0.26) = 0.48","0.48 ÷ 0.09 = 5.33"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-r","label":"Reported the annual FCF increase","value":0.48,"check":"12.0*(0.30-0.26)","explain":"A perpetual increase in FCF is capitalised at R: divide by 0.09."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Used the new tax rate rather than the change","value":34.67,"check":"12.0*0.26/0.09","explain":"The change in T is 4 percentage points, not 26%."},{"id":"level-for-change","label":"Capitalised the whole new FCF instead of the increase","value":98.67,"check":"12.0*(1-0.26)/0.09","explain":"That is the whole new FCF capitalised, not the increase."}],"explanation":"Because FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC, a cut in T raises FCF by EBIT × ΔT each year; the value created is that annual gain capitalised at R.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s40-43"},{"id":"fin201-q08-561","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Free cash flow to equity (FCFE) is:","options":["the cash generated by operations before any payment to lenders, EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","the dividends actually paid to shareholders during the year, as decided by the board","the cash available to shareholders after interest and net borrowing: NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt","net income after tax, taken from the income statement without any adjustments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FCFE is what shareholders could receive without hurting the business. It starts from NPAT (after interest), converts to cash with D&A, CAPEX and ΔWC, and adds net new borrowing. FCF (to the firm) is the pre-lender version; dividends are only what the board chooses to pay.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-562","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is the FCFE formula?","options":["NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt","NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC − ΔDebt","NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Net borrowing brings cash IN, so ΔDebt is added (and a repayment, a negative ΔDebt, reduces FCFE). Dropping it is the missing-delta-debt error; starting from EBIT(1 − T) is the FCF formula.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2; L04 s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-563","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An analyst discounts forecast cash flows to shareholders, after interest and after net borrowing, at the cost of equity. The result is:","options":["The equity value directly","Enterprise value (EV)","The value of the firm's debt","Book value of equity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FCFE is the cash for shareholders, so its PV at the cost of equity is the equity value. No EV − Debt + Cash step is needed.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37, s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-564","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"You want the equity value of a firm whose debt will rise and fall over the next five years. Which method fits best?","options":["FCFE at the cost of equity, since it includes the planned borrowing and repayment","FCFF discounted at the cost of equity, since both are cash flows of the firm","FCFE discounted at the firm's overall rate, since the firm is what is being valued","The Gordon model with last year's FCFE as D1, since debt changes cancel out"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FCFE already contains ΔDebt, so it values the equity cash flows directly at the equity rate. Discounting FCFE at the firm rate (a lower figure) is a rate mismatch; FCFF is a firm-level cash flow and needs the firm rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37-42; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-565","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How is FCFE related to FCF?","options":["FCFE = FCF − (1 − T) × Interest + ΔDebt","FCFE = FCF + (1 − T) × Interest − ΔDebt","FCFE = FCF − Interest + ΔDebt, with interest before tax","FCFE = FCF, because interest is not a cash flow"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Going from the whole firm to equity holders, subtract the lenders' after-tax interest (interest saves tax, so only (1 − T) × Interest is lost) and add net new debt. Using pre-tax interest ignores the tax shield on interest.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-566","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT true of FCFE?","options":["It is discounted at the firm's overall discount rate to give enterprise value","It can exceed net income when the firm is borrowing","It includes interest because it starts from NPAT","Its present value at the cost of equity is the equity value"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FCFE is an equity-holder cash flow, discounted at the cost of equity to give equity. Enterprise value comes from discounting FCF at the firm's rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37-42"},{"id":"fin201-q08-567","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is ΔDebt added in FCFE?","options":["New borrowing brings cash in for shareholders; repayments (negative ΔDebt) take cash away","Debt is an asset of the firm, so more debt directly increases the value of equity","Interest is only paid on new debt, so ΔDebt measures the interest bill","ΔDebt converts the book value of equity into its market value"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FCFE counts cash flows that equity holders can actually receive: operating cash after reinvestment, less what must go to lenders, plus new loans. If the firm repays $2 of principal, $2 less is available for shareholders.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-568","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm's FCFE this year is much larger than its NPAT, mainly because of a large increase in debt. How should an analyst read that?","options":["The firm has become more profitable, and the extra cash will recur each year","The firm should cut its dividends to match FCFE and keep the difference","NPAT must have been understated by the accountants in the income statement","A one-off boost from borrowing; capitalising it would overstate equity value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ΔDebt is cash from lenders, not earnings. A permanent perpetuity of FCFE that includes a big one-off ΔDebt overstates value; normalise borrowing before capitalising.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-569","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm reports (in $ thousands): NPAT 420; D&A 90; CAPEX 150; increase in working capital 20; net new borrowing 60. What is its FCFE?","options":["$280 thousand","$310 thousand","$340 thousand","$400 thousand"],"answer":3,"explanation":"FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt = 420 + 90 − 150 − 20 + 60 = 400. Leaving out net borrowing gives 340; subtracting it gives 280; forgetting the non-cash D&A add-back gives 310.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-570","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm has FCF of $5.8 million, interest of $0.9 million, a tax rate of 30%, and repays $1.2 million of debt during the year. What is its FCFE? (in $ millions)","options":["$3.70 million","$3.97 million","$5.17 million","$6.37 million"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FCFE = FCF − (1 − T) × Interest + ΔDebt = 5.80 − 0.63 + (−1.20) = $3.97 million. Using pre-tax interest gives $3.70 million; treating the repayment as borrowing gives $6.37 million; omitting ΔDebt gives $5.17 million.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-571","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":["fin201-c08-fcf-ev"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Grain Co-op has (in $ millions): sales 9.0; cash operating costs 5.4; D&A 0.9; interest 0.45; tax rate 30%; CAPEX 1.1; ΔWC 0.2; net new borrowing 0.5. FCFE is expected to stay constant forever. The firm's overall discount rate is 8% and shareholders require 11% per annum. What is the equity value? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":15.23,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45)*0.70+0.9-1.1-0.2+0.5)/0.11","steps":[{"label":"EBIT = sales − costs − D&A","value":2.7,"check":"9.0-5.4-0.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EBT = EBIT − interest","value":2.25,"check":"9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPAT = EBT × (1 − T)","value":1.575,"check":"(9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45)*0.70","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt","value":1.675,"check":"(9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45)*0.70+0.9-1.1-0.2+0.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Equity value = FCFE/R_E","value":15.2273,"check":"((9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45)*0.70+0.9-1.1-0.2+0.5)/0.11","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["EBIT = 9.0 − 5.4 − 0.9 = 2.7; EBT = 2.7 − 0.45 = 2.25; tax = 0.675; NPAT = 1.575","FCFE = 1.575 + 0.9 − 1.1 − 0.2 + 0.5 = $1.675 million","Equity = FCFE/R_E = 1.675/0.11 = $15.23 million (the 8% firm rate is not used for FCFE)"],"keystrokes":["9.0 − 5.4 − 0.9 − 0.45 = 2.25; × 0.70 = 1.575","1.575 + 0.9 − 1.1 − 0.2 + 0.5 = 1.675","1.675 ÷ 0.11 = 15.23"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-delta-debt","label":"Left out net new borrowing","value":10.68,"check":"((9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45)*0.70+0.9-1.1-0.2)/0.11","explain":"New borrowing adds cash for shareholders and belongs in FCFE."},{"id":"firm-rate-on-fcfe","label":"Discounted FCFE at the firm's 8% rate","value":20.94,"check":"((9.0-5.4-0.9-0.45)*0.70+0.9-1.1-0.2+0.5)/0.08","explain":"Equity cash flows carry the cost of equity (11%); 8% is the rate for FCF."},{"id":"fcf-for-fcfe","label":"Used FCF (before interest and borrowing) instead of FCFE","value":13.55,"check":"((9.0-5.4-0.9)*0.70+0.9-1.1-0.2)/0.11","explain":"FCF is the whole-firm cash flow; shareholders get FCFE."}],"explanation":"Income statement → NPAT → FCFE → equity at the cost of equity. Interest is deducted here (via NPAT), and net borrowing is added.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s38-43"},{"id":"fin201-q08-572","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":["fin201-c08-fcf-ev"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Chinook Chemicals has EBIT of $6.0 million, a tax rate of 25%, D&A of $1.4 million, CAPEX of $2.0 million and ΔWC of $0.3 million. It pays interest of $0.8 million and repays $0.6 million of debt during the year. What is its FCFE? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":2.4,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"6.0*0.75+1.4-2.0-0.3-0.8*0.75-0.6","steps":[{"label":"FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","value":3.6,"check":"6.0*0.75+1.4-2.0-0.3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax interest (1 − T) × Interest","value":0.6,"check":"0.8*0.75","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"FCFE = FCF − (1 − T) × Interest + ΔDebt","value":2.4,"check":"6.0*0.75+1.4-2.0-0.3-0.8*0.75-0.6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["FCF = 6.0 × 0.75 + 1.4 − 2.0 − 0.3 = 4.5 + 1.4 − 2.0 − 0.3 = $3.6 million","After-tax interest = 0.8 × 0.75 = $0.6 million","ΔDebt = −0.6 (repayment)","FCFE = 3.6 − 0.6 + (−0.6) = $2.40 million"],"keystrokes":["6.0 × 0.75 = 4.5; 4.5 + 1.4 − 2.0 − 0.3 = 3.6","0.8 × 0.75 = 0.6","3.6 − 0.6 − 0.6 = 2.40"],"errors":[{"id":"interest-in-fcf","label":"Subtracted pre-tax interest","value":2.2,"check":"6.0*0.75+1.4-2.0-0.3-0.8-0.6","explain":"Interest is tax-deductible, so the cost to shareholders is (1 − T) × Interest."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Added the repayment instead of subtracting","value":3.6,"check":"6.0*0.75+1.4-2.0-0.3-0.8*0.75+0.6","explain":"A debt repayment is a negative ΔDebt and reduces FCFE."},{"id":"missing-delta-debt","label":"Left out ΔDebt","value":3.0,"check":"6.0*0.75+1.4-2.0-0.3-0.8*0.75","explain":"Net borrowing (here negative) must be added."}],"explanation":"FCFE = FCF − (1 − T) × Interest + ΔDebt is the bridge between the two cash flows. Here the firm both pays interest and shrinks its debt, so shareholders receive $1.2 million less than FCF.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-573","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rideau Rail just generated FCFE of $4.2 million, expected to grow 4% per year forever. Shareholders require 10.5% per annum, the firm's overall discount rate is 8%, and there are 8.0 million shares. What is the equity value per share? (Answer in $.)","answer":8.4,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"4.2*1.04/(0.105-0.04)/8","steps":[{"label":"Next FCFE1 = FCFE0(1 + g)","value":4.368,"check":"4.2*1.04","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Equity value = FCFE1/(R_E − g)","value":67.2,"check":"4.2*1.04/(0.105-0.04)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Equity per share","value":8.4,"check":"4.2*1.04/(0.105-0.04)/8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["FCFE1 = 4.2 × 1.04 = $4.368 million","Equity = 4.368/(0.105 − 0.04) = 4.368/0.065 = $67.20 million","Per share = 67.20/8.0 = $8.40"],"keystrokes":["4.2 × 1.04 = 4.368","4.368 ÷ 0.065 = 67.20","67.20 ÷ 8 = 8.40"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used FCFE0 in the numerator","value":8.08,"check":"4.2/(0.105-0.04)/8","explain":"The growing perpetuity needs next year's FCFE."},{"id":"firm-rate-on-fcfe","label":"Discounted at the firm's 8% rate","value":13.65,"check":"4.2*1.04/(0.08-0.04)/8","explain":"FCFE is an equity cash flow and takes the cost of equity."},{"id":"units","label":"Forgot to divide by the number of shares","value":67.2,"check":"4.2*1.04/(0.105-0.04)","explain":"67.20 is the total equity value in $ millions."}],"explanation":"FCFE valuation is Gordon growth applied to cash flows to equity at R_E. Dividing by shares at the end gives the price per share.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s19, s41-43"},{"id":"fin201-q08-574","concept":"fin201-c08-fcfe","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards has NPAT of $1.6 million, D&A of $0.7 million, CAPEX of $1.2 million and ΔWC of $0.15 million. It wants FCFE of $2.0 million to fund a special payout to shareholders. How much net new debt must it raise? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":1.05,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.0-(1.6+0.7-1.2-0.15)","steps":[{"label":"FCFE before any borrowing","value":0.95,"check":"1.6+0.7-1.2-0.15","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required ΔDebt = target FCFE − FCFE before borrowing","value":1.05,"check":"2.0-(1.6+0.7-1.2-0.15)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["FCFE before borrowing = 1.6 + 0.7 − 1.2 − 0.15 = $0.95 million","FCFE = that + ΔDebt → ΔDebt = 2.00 − 0.95 = $1.05 million"],"keystrokes":["1.6 + 0.7 − 1.2 − 0.15 = 0.95","2.0 − 0.95 = 1.05"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-error","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":-1.05,"check":"(1.6+0.7-1.2-0.15)-2.0","explain":"ΔDebt must make up the SHORTFALL: target minus what operations already provide."},{"id":"wc-sign","label":"Added ΔWC instead of subtracting it","value":0.75,"check":"2.0-(1.6+0.7-1.2+0.15)","explain":"An increase in working capital ties up cash and reduces FCFE."},{"id":"forgot-da","label":"Forgot to add back D&A","value":1.75,"check":"2.0-(1.6-1.2-0.15)","explain":"D&A is a non-cash charge in NPAT and is added back."}],"explanation":"FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt, rearranged for ΔDebt. Borrowing is the plug that closes the gap between operating cash and the payout target.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-581","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the course's notation, working capital (WC) is:","options":["Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables, i.e. current assets − current liabilities","Inventory + Receivables only, because cash is not an operating asset","Cash + Inventory + Receivables + Payables, the sum of the current items","Long-term assets minus long-term debt on the balance sheet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Accounts Receivable − Accounts Payable = Current assets − Current liabilities. Payables are a liability, so they reduce WC. ΔWC is the CHANGE in this figure from one year to the next.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-582","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retailer builds up inventory before the holiday season and pays for it in cash. In the FCF formula this shows up as:","options":["A negative ΔWC, which raises FCF","A positive ΔWC, which reduces FCF","Higher CAPEX, which reduces FCF","Higher D&A, which raises FCF"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inventory is part of WC, so more inventory means WC and ΔWC rise. A positive ΔWC is cash tied up in the business and is subtracted in FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-583","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company's suppliers agree to wait 15 more days for payment. Holding everything else constant, what happens to ΔWC and FCF this year?","options":["Payables rise, so ΔWC rises as well and FCF falls by the increase in payables","Payables rise, so ΔWC falls (is negative) and FCF rises by that amount","Nothing happens, because payables are not part of working capital in this course","Payables rise, so CAPEX falls and FCF rises by the amount of the increase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Payables are subtracted in WC. When they rise, WC falls and ΔWC < 0; subtracting a negative ΔWC in FCF adds cash. The firm is effectively borrowing from suppliers.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-584","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the level of working capital (WC) and the change in working capital (ΔWC) differ in a free cash flow calculation?","options":["WC enters FCF each year, and ΔWC is only a memo item with no effect on cash flow","ΔWC is the sum of cash and receivables, and WC is the sum of inventory and payables","WC is a level at a date; only the yearly change ΔWC = WC_end − WC_start enters FCF","They are the same thing because working capital is already a flow, not a level"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FCF is a flow for one period, so it needs the change in WC over that period. Subtracting the whole WC level would charge the firm again every year for the same inventory and receivables.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-585","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT cause a positive change in working capital (ΔWC > 0) in a year?","options":["An increase in inventory","An increase in accounts receivable","An increase in the cash balance","An increase in accounts payable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Inventory, receivables and cash are positive items in WC, so an increase in any of them raises WC. Payables are subtracted, so higher payables lower WC. (The professor's list of positive ΔWC examples: inventory up, receivables up, payables down, cash up.)","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; L04 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q08-586","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does an increase in inventory reduce free cash flow even though inventory is an asset the firm owns?","options":["The firm paid cash for goods it has not yet sold, so cash is tied up","Inventory is a liability on the balance sheet, so it reduces cash flow","Inventory is expensed immediately for tax purposes, lowering cash flow","Inventory raises D&A, and D&A reduces FCF dollar for dollar"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FCF counts cash, not assets. Buying inventory consumes cash now and returns it only when the goods are sold. The same logic applies to receivables (sales not yet collected).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40"},{"id":"fin201-q08-587","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A mature manufacturer collects receivables faster and trims inventory, so its working capital falls by $2 million this year while EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX is $10 million. What is this year's FCF, and is it sustainable?","options":["FCF = $12 million; the extra $2 million is a one-off release of working capital","FCF = $8 million, because ΔWC is subtracted as an outflow","FCF = $10 million, because working capital does not affect FCF","FCF = $12 million and the $2 million will recur every year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ΔWC = −2, so FCF = 10 − (−2) = $12 million. Releasing working capital is a one-time gain; capitalising it forever would overstate the firm's value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40; MAP A8"},{"id":"fin201-q08-588","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: ΔWC equals working capital at the end of the year minus working capital at the start of the year, and a positive ΔWC reduces FCF.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. ΔWC = WC_end − WC_start. FCF subtracts ΔWC, so cash tied up (ΔWC > 0) lowers FCF and cash released (ΔWC < 0) raises it.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2; L04 s39-40"},{"id":"fin201-q08-589","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"During the year a firm's inventory rises by 55, receivables fall by 20, payables fall by 15 and cash rises by 10 (all in $ thousands). What is ΔWC?","options":["$30 thousand","$50 thousand","$60 thousand","$100 thousand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ΔWC = ΔCash + ΔInventory + ΔReceivables − ΔPayables = 10 + 55 + (−20) − (−15) = 60. A fall in payables RAISES WC. Treating the payables fall as a decrease gives 30; leaving out cash gives 50; treating the receivables fall as a rise gives 100.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-590","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm's current assets rise from 612 to 655 and its current liabilities fall from 340 to 312 (in $ thousands). What is ΔWC?","options":["-$28 thousand","$15 thousand","$71 thousand","$343 thousand"],"answer":2,"explanation":"WC = current assets − current liabilities: 272 at the start, 343 at the end, so ΔWC = 343 − 272 = 71. Adding the change in liabilities instead of subtracting it gives 15; the ending level is 343; looking at liabilities alone gives −28.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2; L04 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q08-591","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Robotics's balance sheet shows (in $ thousands) at the start and end of the year: cash 120 and 150; inventory 310 and 365; receivables 240 and 262; payables 205 and 248. What is ΔWC? (Answer in $ thousands.)","answer":64.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(150+365+262-248)-(120+310+240-205)","steps":[{"label":"Working capital at the start","value":465.0,"check":"120+310+240-205","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Working capital at the end","value":529.0,"check":"150+365+262-248","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"ΔWC = WC_end − WC_start","value":64.0,"check":"(150+365+262-248)-(120+310+240-205)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["WC_start = 120 + 310 + 240 − 205 = 465","WC_end = 150 + 365 + 262 − 248 = 529","ΔWC = 529 − 465 = 64 (positive: cash tied up, FCF falls by 64)"],"keystrokes":["120 + 310 + 240 − 205 = 465","150 + 365 + 262 − 248 = 529","529 − 465 = 64"],"errors":[{"id":"wc-sign","label":"Added the payables","value":150.0,"check":"(150+365+262+248)-(120+310+240+205)","explain":"Payables are subtracted in working capital."},{"id":"cash-excluded","label":"Left out cash","value":34.0,"check":"(365+262-248)-(310+240-205)","explain":"In the course's definition cash is part of WC."},{"id":"level-for-change","label":"Reported the ending WC","value":529.0,"check":"150+365+262-248","explain":"The question asks for the change over the year."}],"explanation":"Compute WC at both dates, then take the difference. Cash, inventory and receivables push WC up; payables pull it down.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-592","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":["fin201-c08-fcf-ev"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kootenay Kayaks has EBIT of 410, a tax rate of 30%, D&A of 95 and CAPEX of 120 (all in $ thousands). Current assets rose from 840 to 905 and current liabilities rose from 515 to 598. What is its free cash flow? (Answer in $ thousands.)","answer":280.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"410*0.70+95-120-((905-598)-(840-515))","steps":[{"label":"WC at the start (CA − CL)","value":325.0,"check":"840-515","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"WC at the end (CA − CL)","value":307.0,"check":"905-598","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"ΔWC","value":-18.0,"check":"(905-598)-(840-515)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","value":280.0,"check":"410*0.70+95-120-((905-598)-(840-515))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["WC_start = 840 − 515 = 325; WC_end = 905 − 598 = 307; ΔWC = −18","EBIT(1 − T) = 410 × 0.70 = 287","FCF = 287 + 95 − 120 − (−18) = 280"],"keystrokes":["410 × 0.70 = 287","287 + 95 − 120 = 262","262 − (−18) = 280"],"errors":[{"id":"wc-sign","label":"Subtracted 18 instead of adding it back","value":244.0,"check":"410*0.70+95-120-18","explain":"ΔWC is negative (working capital fell), so subtracting it adds 18 to FCF."},{"id":"omitted-wc","label":"Left ΔWC out of the calculation","value":262.0,"check":"410*0.70+95-120","explain":"ΔWC belongs in FCF; here it releases 18 of cash."},{"id":"level-for-change","label":"Subtracted the ending WC level","value":-45.0,"check":"410*0.70+95-120-(905-598)","explain":"Only the CHANGE in WC enters FCF."}],"explanation":"Liabilities grew faster than assets, so working capital fell: the business was financed partly by its suppliers and FCF is higher than the pre-WC figure.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-593","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Gatineau Glass's annual cost of goods sold is $36.5 million. It negotiates to pay suppliers 10 days later, so payables rise by 10 days of cost of goods sold (use 365 days). By how much does this one-time change raise FCF this year? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":1.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"36.5/365*10","steps":[{"label":"Daily cost of goods sold","value":0.1,"check":"36.5/365","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Increase in payables","value":1.0,"check":"36.5/365*10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Change in WC (negative = release)","value":-1.0,"check":"-36.5/365*10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Change in FCF = −ΔWC","value":1.0,"check":"36.5/365*10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Daily COGS = 36.5/365 = $0.10 million","Payables rise by 10 × 0.10 = $1.0 million","ΔWC = −1.0 (payables are subtracted in WC)","FCF changes by −ΔWC = +$1.00 million, once"],"keystrokes":["36.5 ÷ 365 = 0.10; × 10 = 1.0"],"errors":[{"id":"wc-sign","label":"Treated higher payables as an outflow","value":-1.0,"check":"-36.5/365*10","explain":"More payables lower WC, which raises FCF."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Used 10% of COGS","value":3.65,"check":"36.5*0.10","explain":"10 days is 10/365 of annual COGS (2.7%), not 10%."},{"id":"annualised","label":"Counted the gain every year","value":10.0,"check":"36.5/365*10*10","explain":"The stretch is a one-off release, not a permanent annual gain."}],"explanation":"Stretching payables is cash borrowed from suppliers: ΔWC falls and FCF rises once. It cannot be repeated each year unless terms are stretched again.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39-40; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-594","concept":"fin201-c08-working-capital","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Harbourfront Bakeries expects inventory to rise 40% from $1.25 million, receivables to rise 8% from $0.90 million, and payables to rise 15% from $0.60 million. Cash is unchanged. What is the expected ΔWC? (Answer in $ millions.)","answer":0.48,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.005},"check":"1.25*0.40+0.90*0.08-0.60*0.15","steps":[{"label":"Increase in inventory","value":0.5,"check":"1.25*0.40","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Increase in receivables","value":0.072,"check":"0.90*0.08","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Increase in payables","value":0.09,"check":"0.60*0.15","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"ΔWC = ΔInventory + ΔReceivables − ΔPayables","value":0.482,"check":"1.25*0.40+0.90*0.08-0.60*0.15","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["ΔInventory = 1.25 × 0.40 = 0.500; ΔReceivables = 0.90 × 0.08 = 0.072; ΔPayables = 0.60 × 0.15 = 0.090","ΔWC = 0.500 + 0.072 − 0.090 = $0.482 million (a cash outflow)"],"keystrokes":["1.25 × 0.40 = 0.5","0.90 × 0.08 = 0.072","0.60 × 0.15 = 0.09","0.5 + 0.072 − 0.09 = 0.482"],"errors":[{"id":"wc-sign","label":"Added the payables increase","value":0.66,"check":"1.25*0.40+0.90*0.08+0.60*0.15","explain":"Payables are subtracted: more payables offset part of the inventory build."},{"id":"omitted-wc","label":"Counted inventory only","value":0.5,"check":"1.25*0.40","explain":"Receivables and payables also change."},{"id":"level-for-change","label":"Used the ending levels instead of the changes","value":2.03,"check":"1.25*1.40+0.90*1.08-0.60*1.15","explain":"WC at the end is a level; ΔWC needs the differences."}],"explanation":"Each item's change is level × growth rate. Inventory and receivables increase WC; the payables increase offsets part of it.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s39; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-601","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The comparables approach to valuation:","options":["discounts forecast dividends at the required return to obtain the price today","applies the multiple of similar firms (P/E, P/S, EV/EBITDA) to the target's earnings, sales or EBITDA","discounts free cash flows at the firm's overall discount rate to obtain EV","adds up the values of the firm's assets as if they were sold off in liquidation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Comparables need no forecast of future cash flows: price = benchmark multiple × the firm's own metric. The other three descriptions are the DDM, FCFF valuation and liquidation value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s17-18; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-602","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A regulated electric utility has paid steadily growing dividends for decades, and its payout policy is stable. Which method is the most natural way to value its shares?","options":["Free cash flow to the firm, discounted at the cost of equity","Price/sales comparables, because dividends are not reliable","NPVGO, because the firm has no growth opportunities","The dividend discount model (Gordon growth)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Stable, predictable dividends are exactly the condition under which the DDM works well. FCFF discounted at the cost of equity mixes the wrong rate with a firm-level cash flow, and price/sales is a fallback for firms without earnings.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; L04 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q08-603","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pathfinder Fibre pays no dividends and plans to borrow heavily to finance a network build-out over the next five years. You want the value of its shares directly from forecasts of what shareholders could receive. Which method fits?","options":["FCFE discounted at the cost of equity","The Gordon growth model with D0 = 0","FCFF discounted at the cost of equity","A trailing P/E applied to this year's earnings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With no dividends the DDM has nothing to discount until payouts begin, and FCFE captures both the heavy reinvestment and the planned borrowing. FCFF would give enterprise value (and needs the firm's rate), and a trailing P/E ignores the build-out.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; L04 s37, s41"},{"id":"fin201-q08-604","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A buyer wants the value of a target's operations regardless of how the target is currently financed, because it will refinance the target with its own mix of debt and equity after the takeover. Which approach fits best?","options":["FCFE discounted at the cost of equity using current borrowing","The DDM using the target's current dividend and growth rate","The target's book value of equity from its balance sheet","FCFF at the firm's rate (EV), then Equity = EV − Debt + Cash"],"answer":3,"explanation":"FCF is independent of the financing mix (it is before interest and debt flows), so it values the operations whatever the debt level. Equity is then derived from EV with the current net debt.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; L04 s37-42"},{"id":"fin201-q08-605","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"How do FCFF and FCFE differ as valuation methods?","options":["FCFF gives the value of equity, whereas FCFE gives the value of the whole firm","FCFF is discounted at the firm's rate to give EV; FCFE at the cost of equity to give equity directly","Both are discounted at the cost of equity, but FCFE is measured after taxes","FCFF is built from dividends, whereas FCFE is built from earnings"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FCFF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC belongs to all capital providers (EV, firm rate). FCFE = NPAT + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC + ΔDebt belongs to shareholders (equity, cost of equity).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37-42"},{"id":"fin201-q08-606","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A young software company has sales but negative earnings and pays no dividend. Peers trade at about 4 times sales. Which valuation is the most workable?","options":["P/E comparables applied to this year's negative earnings per share","Price/sales comparables applied to the company's sales per share","The Gordon model using the dividend paid this year as D0","NPVGO using the no-growth value EPS1/R as the price"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A P/E on negative earnings is meaningless and the Gordon model has no dividend to grow; a sales multiple still produces a positive number (SM 8-12 values a firm at 4.3 × sales per share).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 8-12; MAP 8.3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-607","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can the dividend discount model be a poor choice for a firm that retains most of its earnings?","options":["Retained earnings are discounted at a higher rate than paid-out dividends are","The DDM cannot be used at all whenever ROE is above the required return","Firms that retain earnings have no required return on their shares","Dividends then say little about the cash the business generates, so they understate its value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The DDM values only cash paid out. If a firm reinvests most of its cash, current dividends are small or zero and say little about the value of the business; FCFE or FCFF capture the cash generated whether or not it is paid out.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; SM 8 CTQ 2-3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-608","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a reason to prefer comparables over DCF methods?","options":["They are quick and use observable market multiples","They avoid having to estimate a long-run growth rate and discount rate","They work for firms whose future cash flows are hard to forecast","Comparables require a detailed forecast of each future year's cash flows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Comparables rely on peers' multiples applied to current or next-year earnings, sales or EBITDA. The cost is the benchmark: peers must really have similar g, ROE and R (SM 8 CTQ 12). A detailed multi-year forecast is the DCF's demand, not comparables'.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s17-18, s32; SM 8 CTQ 12"},{"id":"fin201-q08-609","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"For a growing firm that pays no dividend, the DDM gives $22 per share while peers' P/E multiples imply $35. What is the most reasonable reading?","options":["The peers' P/E multiples must be wrong, because the DDM is the more reliable method","The firm is certainly overvalued at $35, because the DDM value is lower","The DDM is likely unsuitable: tiny dividends hide the cash flows; cross-check with FCFE or FCFF","The two methods must always agree, so there must be an arithmetic mistake"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When payouts are small, the DDM can understate value; the methods differ because their assumptions differ. A cash-flow model (FCFE/FCFF) fills the gap, and then you compare it with the comparables value.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-610","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: if you discount FCFF at the firm's discount rate, you must still subtract debt and add cash to reach the value of the shares.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. FCFF discounted gives enterprise value. Equity = EV − Debt + Cash. By contrast, FCFE discounted at the cost of equity gives equity directly.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s42; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-611","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"To value a firm's operations independently of how they are financed, discount _____ at the firm's overall discount rate.","options":["free cash flow to the firm (FCF)","free cash flow to equity (FCFE)","dividends per share","net income after tax"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FCF is before payments to lenders and shareholders, so it is independent of the financing mix; its PV at the firm's rate is enterprise value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37, s40"},{"id":"fin201-q08-612","concept":"fin201-c08-which-model","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two analysts value the same firm. One discounts FCF at the firm's rate and computes Equity = EV − Debt + Cash; the other discounts FCFE at the cost of equity. Their equity values differ. What is the most likely reason?","options":["Their forecasts or rates are inconsistent; built consistently, the two methods should agree","FCFF always gives a higher answer than FCFE, so the gap is expected","The DDM must be used to break the tie, since it is the only exact method","FCFE should be discounted at the firm's rate to make the two agree"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In theory both approaches describe the same business and should give the same equity value. A gap points to inconsistent assumptions, not to a rule that one method is higher. Discounting FCFE at the firm's rate would make a rate mismatch, not fix one.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s37-43; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-621","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An efficient market is one in which:","options":["security prices never change, because all information is already known","every investor earns the same return on every security held","security prices reflect the relevant information, so securities are fairly priced","security prices always equal the book value per share on the balance sheet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Efficiency is about information: if the relevant information is already in the price, there is no reason to think the price is too high or too low. Prices still move, because new information arrives constantly.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s45; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-622","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Weak-form market efficiency says that current prices already reflect:","options":["all publicly released information, including financial statements","all information, including private information held by insiders","only the information in the firm's most recent earnings announcement","the past history of the stock's own prices (so charts cannot predict)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Weak form is the mildest: the information set is the stock's past price history. If it holds, 'only the current price is important' and the path that led to it is irrelevant (L04 s47).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s46-47"},{"id":"fin201-q08-623","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which form of market efficiency says that prices reflect all publicly released information?","options":["Weak form","Strong form","Perfect form","Semi-strong form"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Semi-strong adds public information (financial statements, announcements, news) to the past price history. Strong form adds private information too.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s46"},{"id":"fin201-q08-624","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A technical analyst studies price charts for ten years and cannot beat a buy-and-hold strategy after costs. Which form of efficiency is this consistent with?","options":["Weak-form efficiency","Strong-form efficiency only","The market being inefficient in every form","Semi-strong efficiency only, not weak"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If past prices carry no exploitable information, chart reading cannot earn abnormal profits: that is the weak-form hypothesis. Note that semi-strong efficiency implies weak form, so 'semi-strong only, not weak' is impossible.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s47; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-625","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"How do the weak and semi-strong forms of efficiency differ?","options":["Weak form covers public information and semi-strong covers private information","They cover the same information, but semi-strong also applies to bonds","Semi-strong includes weak form (past prices) plus all other public information, so it is stronger","Weak form is a stronger claim than semi-strong because it applies to all stocks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The forms are nested: weak ⊂ semi-strong ⊂ strong. The information sets are past prices; all public information; all information including private.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s46; SM 12 CTQ 6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-626","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the course, which forms of market efficiency do we want and which do we not want?","options":["We want strong-form efficiency most; weak and semi-strong forms are undesirable","We want all three forms equally, since each prices in more information","We want weak form only; semi-strong and strong forms are undesirable","We want weak and semi-strong efficiency; strong-form efficiency is undesirable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Weak and semi-strong efficiency mean prices quickly absorb past and public information, which is good for fair trading. Strong form would mean even private information is already in prices, which could only happen through insider trading.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s50; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-627","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is strong-form efficiency regarded as undesirable?","options":["Strong-form efficiency makes prices far more volatile than investors want","Private information could be in prices only if insiders traded on it, which is illegal and unfair","It prevents the market from absorbing public information quickly","It requires investors to hold only index funds, removing choice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a healthy market, insiders should not be able to profit from non-public information, so prices should NOT reflect it before it is released. Strong-form efficiency would imply that insider trading is rampant (L04 s50).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q08-628","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement is NOT implied by weak-form efficiency alone?","options":["Chart patterns in past prices cannot predict future returns","Analysis of public financial statements cannot earn abnormal returns","The current price is the only price information that matters","A momentum strategy cannot earn abnormal returns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weak form concerns past prices only. Whether financial statements are already priced is the semi-strong question, so weak form alone does not rule out profits from public statement analysis.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s46-47; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-629","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A market is semi-strong form efficient. A listed firm unexpectedly announces earnings far above forecasts at 9:00 a.m. What should happen to the share price?","options":["It jumps to the new fair level almost at once, with no tendency to drift afterwards","It rises gradually over the following eight days as investors catch up","It overshoots the fair level and then falls back over the next few days","It does not change, because earnings are already known to investors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Semi-strong efficiency means public news is priced quickly and correctly: an immediate one-step adjustment. A gradual rise is a delayed reaction and an overshoot-and-correct path is an overreaction; both are inefficient (TB Fig 12.14).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s50; TB 12.6 Fig 12.14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-630","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"F-Stop Camera's shares trade at $140 until the company announces a secret R&D success worth $40 per share. At the announcement the price jumps to $180 and stays there. What does this pattern show?","options":["Strong-form efficiency, because the price reached the right level after the news","The market is not even weak-form efficient, because the price changed","The market is inefficient, because efficient prices should never jump","Consistent with semi-strong efficiency, but not strong form: the private news was not in the $140 price"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A single jump on announcement is the efficient response to public news. But before the announcement the price did not contain the private information (the R&D success), so strong form does not hold. Prices SHOULD move when information arrives.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6; L04 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q08-631","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: if a market is semi-strong form efficient, it must also be weak-form efficient.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Historical prices are public information, so any market whose prices reflect all public information also reflects past prices. The forms are nested (SM 12 CTQ 6).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12 CTQ 6; L04 s46"},{"id":"fin201-q08-632","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"True or false: if a market is weak-form efficient, it must also be semi-strong form efficient.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Weak form only says past prices are already reflected. Prices could still be slow to react to announcements and financial statements, which would make the market weak-form efficient but not semi-strong efficient.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12 CTQ 5-6; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-633","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The past history of prices is the information set for the _____ form of market efficiency.","options":["semi-strong","strong","technical","weak"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Weak form: past prices (charting). Semi-strong: all public information. Strong: all information including private.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s46"},{"id":"fin201-q08-634","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why are all investments in an efficient market zero-NPV investments?","options":["Efficient markets prevent firms from ever earning a profit on their investments","Competition drives the required return on every security down to zero","Efficient markets set every price equal to the book value of the asset","Prices are neither too high nor too low, so market value equals cost: investors get what they pay for"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NPV = value − cost. If price equals value because all relevant information is priced, the difference is zero. Investors still earn the required return for the risk taken; they just cannot expect more.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6; SM 12 CTQ 4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-635","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Order the forms of market efficiency from the smallest information set to the largest.","options":["Weak, then semi-strong, then strong","Semi-strong, then weak, then strong","Strong, then semi-strong, then weak","Weak, then strong, then semi-strong"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weak form covers past prices only; semi-strong adds all other public information; strong adds private information as well. Each claim contains the one before it.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s46"},{"id":"fin201-q08-636","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"An analyst consistently earns abnormal returns using only published financial statements, and this is widely documented. Which statement follows?","options":["The market is not weak-form efficient, so semi-strong efficiency must fail too","The market is strong-form efficient, since all information is in the price","Nothing follows, because published financial statements are private information","The market is not semi-strong efficient (so not strong either); weak form could still hold"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Published statements are public information. Profiting from them contradicts semi-strong efficiency, which in turn contradicts strong form (nested). Weak form concerns only past prices and may still be true.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12 CTQ 10b; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-637","concept":"fin201-c08-efficiency-forms","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: if prices fluctuate from day to day, the market cannot be efficient.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Prices change because new information keeps arriving, which is what an efficient market does; an absence of price movement in a fast-changing world would suggest inefficiency (TB 12.6).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-641","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":["fin201-c08-efficiency-forms"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"If you decide to buy a stock because its price has continued to move upwards with momentum, then you are relying upon:","options":["Weak-form inefficiencies","Weak form efficiency","Semi-strong form efficiency","Semi-strong form inefficiencies","None of the above"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Momentum means a past price trend predicts the subsequent movement. Using past prices to forecast is only profitable if prices are predictable from their own history, i.e. the market is NOT weak-form efficient: you rely on a weak-form inefficiency. Weak-form efficiency would say the trend carries no information, and semi-strong forms concern public announcements, not past price trends.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-642","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The momentum effect is the tendency for:","options":["a stock that has risen sharply to fall back toward its starting price","all stocks to move in the same direction on the same trading day","a stock that has recently risen (fallen) to keep rising (falling), as past trends predict","stock prices to equal the discounted value of all future dividends"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Momentum is a counter-example to weak-form efficiency: if the past trend predicts the next move, prices are predictable from their own history and an inefficiency exists (L04 s47).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s47"},{"id":"fin201-q08-643","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A fund buys stocks that rose over the past six months and sells those that fell. The fund is trying to profit from:","options":["A semi-strong inefficiency (delayed reaction to news)","Strong-form efficiency","A weak-form inefficiency (momentum)","Differences in the required return R"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The trading rule uses only past prices, so the information it exploits is the price history. It makes money only if the market is not weak-form efficient.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s47; PRAC q3"},{"id":"fin201-q08-644","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Silver Birch Mining announces a large discovery. Its share price rises 5% on the day and then creeps up another 8% over the next eight trading days. This price path shows:","options":["An efficient reaction, because the price rose","An overreaction, because the price rose by more than the news justified","A weak-form efficiency test, because only past prices matter","A delayed reaction, a semi-strong inefficiency: the news was not fully priced at once"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In a semi-strong efficient market the full adjustment happens at the announcement. A partial jump followed by drift means public information is priced slowly; buying right after the announcement would earn abnormal returns (TB Fig 12.14, broken line).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s50; TB 12.6 Fig 12.14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-645","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How do a delayed reaction and an overreaction to news differ, and what would you do in each case?","options":["Delayed: the price adjusts only partly, then drifts to fair value, so trade after the news. Overreaction: it overshoots, then corrects, so trade against the jump","Delayed: the price overshoots the fair value and then corrects; overreaction: the price adjusts too slowly; trade with the initial move in both cases","Both are efficient reactions to public news, so neither one can be exploited profitably by anyone trading after the announcement","Delayed: no tendency to keep moving after the news; overreaction: the price stays at its new level afterwards for good"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are paths in an inefficient market. The delayed-reaction path (broken line in TB Fig 12.14) is below the fair price for days after good news; the overreaction path (dotted) overshoots and then falls back to the fair price.","source":"generated","ref":"TB Fig 12.14; L04 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q08-646","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Good news pushes a stock from $140 to $220 on day 0, but analysts agree the news is worth $40 per share (fair price $180). If the market overreacts and then corrects, what trade makes money?","options":["Buy the stock after the jump and hold it until it reaches $260 per share","Do nothing, because market prices cannot be wrong even briefly","Sell (or short) after the jump, then buy back as the price falls toward $180","Buy the stock only before the announcement, then sell it on day 0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The price is $40 above fair value after the overshoot. Selling at $220 and repurchasing near $180 profits as the correction happens. This is only possible if the market is NOT semi-strong efficient.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6 Fig 12.14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-647","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do profit opportunities from an inefficiency tend to shrink once many investors discover them?","options":["Regulators ban the trading strategy once it becomes well known","The required return on the stock rises when many investors trade it","Competition: traders pushing the price toward fair value remove the opportunity","Companies change their reported earnings to remove the pattern"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Efficiency is created by competition among investors looking for mispriced stocks. As more of them exploit a pattern, price pressure removes it, so only small and uncommon inefficiencies survive (TB 12.6).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6; SM 12 CTQ 4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-648","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following does NOT rely on a market inefficiency to make money?","options":["Buying recent winners because past price trends persist","Holding a diversified portfolio to earn the market's required return for its risk","Buying immediately after good public news because prices adjust slowly","Selling a stock that has overshot after an announcement, expecting it to fall back"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Earning the required return for bearing risk needs no mispricing. The other three strategies profit only if prices are predictable (weak-form inefficiency) or react wrongly to public news (semi-strong inefficiency).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s47-50; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-649","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a semi-strong efficient market, what return should an investor expect from buying a stock just after a public announcement?","options":["An abnormal profit, because the news is good and positive for the firm","A loss, because the price has already moved before the investor could trade","A return equal to the risk-free rate regardless of the stock's risk","Only the return that compensates for the risk; the trade has zero NPV"],"answer":3,"explanation":"News is already in the price by the time you can trade, so the purchase is fairly priced: NPV = 0. You still earn the required return for the risk taken, but no abnormal return.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6; SM 12 CTQ 4"},{"id":"fin201-q08-650","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"True or false: a momentum strategy can earn abnormal profits only if the market is not weak-form efficient.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Momentum uses past prices only. If the market were weak-form efficient, the price history would carry no predictive information and the strategy could not beat the market on a risk-adjusted basis.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s47; SM 12 CTQ 5"},{"id":"fin201-q08-651","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A market is semi-strong form efficient but not strong-form efficient. Which investor could still earn abnormal profits (ignoring the law)?","options":["One who trades on published financial statements","One who studies price charts","One who trades on private information before it is announced","One who buys immediately after a press release"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Semi-strong efficiency prices past prices and all public information. Private information is not yet in prices, so trading on it can pay, which is why insider trading is prohibited (SM 12 CTQ 10c).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12 CTQ 10; L04 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q08-652","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why would chart reading (technical analysis) fail to beat the market if the market is weak-form efficient?","options":["Charts are too expensive to produce compared with the profits available","Technical analysts cannot read financial statements or earnings reports","The current price already reflects the whole price history, so patterns carry no information","Weak-form efficiency means prices follow a perfectly predictable pattern"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a weak-form efficient market the current price already embeds the past price history. 'Only the current price is important. The past history of how it got there is irrelevant' (L04 s47).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s47; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-653","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock trades at $140. News arrives showing it is worth $180, but on the announcement day the price rises only to $160, and over the next eight days it drifts up to $180. You buy at $160 on the announcement day and sell at $180 eight days later. Ignoring costs and the required return over eight days, what is your return on this trade? (Answer in %.)","answer":12.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(180-160)/160*100","steps":[{"label":"Profit per share = sale price − purchase price","value":20.0,"check":"180-160","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Return = profit/purchase price (in %)","value":12.5,"check":"(180-160)/160*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Profit = 180 − 160 = $20 per share","Return = 20/160 = 12.5% over eight days"],"keystrokes":["180 − 160 = 20; 20 ÷ 160 = 0.125"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-base","label":"Measured the gain against the pre-news price of $140","value":14.29,"check":"(180-160)/140*100","explain":"Your cost was $160, the price you actually paid."},{"id":"wrong-base","label":"Measured the gain against the sale price","value":11.11,"check":"(180-160)/180*100","explain":"A return is profit divided by the amount invested at the start."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Used the full $40 revaluation as the profit","value":25.0,"check":"(180-140)/160*100","explain":"Part of the $40 jump ($20) happened before you could trade, so only $20 is available to you."}],"explanation":"A delayed reaction leaves a profit for those who trade right after the news; in an efficient market the price would have jumped to $180 immediately and the trade would earn nothing abnormal.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.6 Fig 12.14"},{"id":"fin201-q08-654","concept":"fin201-c08-inefficiency-profit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"After a good-news announcement a stock's price rises to $215, then falls steadily back to $180 over the next two weeks, where the fair value was estimated to be. Which conclusion is best?","options":["The market is semi-strong efficient, because the price ended at the right level","The market overreacted and then corrected: a trader could profit by selling after the jump","The market is weak-form efficient, because the price returned to fair value","The news must have been wrong, because the price fell after the announcement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An efficient market goes straight to the fair price on the news. Overshoot-and-correct means the price was wrong for days, which is an inefficiency (semi-strong); ending at the right level does not make the path efficient.","source":"generated","ref":"TB Fig 12.14; L04 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q08-661","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Noise trading is trading:","options":["based on private information about the firm's future earnings and dividends","driven by reasons unrelated to information about value, such as a health expense or a tax bill","carried out only by professional market makers who quote bid and ask prices","that follows chart patterns in the stock's past prices to forecast moves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Noise traders buy or sell for personal or liquidity reasons (health, taxes, bills due), not because they have analysed value. Their trades can push prices away from fair value for a while (L04 s51).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-662","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The disposition effect is the tendency of investors to:","options":["buy stocks only after they have fallen","sell all stocks at the end of the tax year","sell winning stocks too early and hold losing stocks too long","hold diversified portfolios regardless of price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The disposition effect is a behavioural bias: realising gains feels good and realising losses feels bad, so investors sell winners and keep losers regardless of the stocks' future prospects.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-663","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Early in April a retiree sells a block of shares she still likes in order to pay her income tax bill. This is an example of:","options":["Noise trading","The disposition effect","Overconfidence","Momentum investing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"She is selling for a personal cash need (taxes due), not because she thinks the stock is overpriced. That is noise trading. The disposition effect would require selling because of gain/loss on the position.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-664","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An investor sells a stock that has doubled to 'lock in the gain' but keeps a stock that has lost 40%, saying 'I will sell when it gets back to what I paid.' This behaviour is:","options":["Noise trading","The disposition effect","Sentiment","Weak-form efficiency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selling the winner and holding the loser, anchored on the purchase price, is the textbook disposition effect.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-665","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A day trader believes she can out-guess the market and trades several hundred times a month, even though her results are no better than the index. Which behavioural factor does this illustrate?","options":["The disposition effect","Noise trading for tax reasons","Semi-strong efficiency","Overconfidence"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Overconfidence leads investors to overestimate their ability and trade too much. The excess trading costs money and adds noise to prices.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-666","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"After a long bull market, a wave of optimism lifts the prices of many tech stocks well beyond what their earnings forecasts justify. This is best described as:","options":["The disposition effect","Liquidity noise from tax selling","Sentiment among investors","Strong-form efficiency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sentiment is a shared mood (optimism or pessimism) that moves prices independently of fundamentals. It is one of the behavioural reasons prices may deviate from fair value.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-667","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do noise trading and behavioural biases differ as reasons why prices may deviate from fair value?","options":["Noise comes from personal or liquidity needs; behavioural biases are systematic psychological errors","Noise trading is illegal, whereas behavioural biases are legal, and only the first can move prices","Noise trading affects only bonds, whereas behavioural biases affect only stocks and no other securities","They are the same thing described with different names, so the distinction has no content"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The slide groups them as two sources of mispricing: Noise (selling for personal reasons such as health, selling when bills like tax are due) and Behaviour (disposition effect, overconfidence, sentiment).","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51; REV p2"},{"id":"fin201-q08-668","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can noise trading move a stock's price away from its fair value?","options":["Noise traders always possess valuable private information about the firm","Noise trading changes the firm's future dividends and therefore its true value","The pressure has nothing to do with value, so it can push the price until others step in","Noise trading forces the firm to reissue its shares at lower prices"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fair value comes from expected cash flows. A forced seller shifts supply without changing those cash flows, which temporarily lowers the price below value until buyers who notice the discount arrive.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-669","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the reasons for price deviations listed in the course?","options":["Selling for personal reasons such as health","Firms paying dividends at fixed dates every year","Selling when bills such as tax are due","Overconfidence and sentiment among investors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slide lists noise (personal and tax-driven selling) and behaviour (disposition effect, overconfidence, sentiment). A regular dividend calendar is not a source of mispricing.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-670","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Many investors sell losing stocks in late December to realise tax losses, pushing some prices below value with no news about the firms. What could a disciplined investor do?","options":["Buy at the depressed price, expecting the discount to vanish when the selling ends","Sell the same stocks short to ride the decline caused by tax-loss selling","Avoid the stocks, because in an efficient market prices are always right","Wait for the next dividend payment before deciding whether to buy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If price pressure comes from non-informational selling, the discount should reverse once it ends. Capturing it requires patience and capital, which is why such deviations can persist.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51; TB 12.6"},{"id":"fin201-q08-671","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock falls 12% on heavy volume in the last week of December with no company news, then recovers most of the loss in January. Which explanation is most consistent with the course?","options":["The market learned bad private information in December and good news in January","The market is strong-form efficient, so price changes without news are impossible","Tax-related noise selling pushed the price below fair value, and it recovered when the pressure ended","The dividend was cut in December and restored in January, moving the price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A sharp fall with no news that reverses is the signature of non-informational selling (here, tax selling). An information-driven move would not reverse on its own.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-672","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"True or false: selling a stock to pay for a medical bill, rather than because of a view about its value, is an example of noise trading.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The seller is motivated by a personal cash need (health), which is unrelated to information about the firm; the course lists 'selling for personal reasons such as health' as noise.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q08-673","concept":"fin201-c08-noise-behaviour","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Investors who hold on to losing stocks while selling their winners are showing the _____ effect.","options":["momentum","disposition","efficiency","dividend"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The disposition effect is the habit of selling winners and holding losers.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s51"}],"color":"#2E6F8E","short":"Stocks","tag":"Value a share","province":"Illyricum","numeral":"VIII"},{"chapter":9,"title":"Capital budgeting: decision rules","topics":[{"id":"fin201-t09-1","order":1,"title":"Accounting rate of return","summary":"The first of the five decision rules compares average accounting profit with the average book value of the money tied up. It is easy to compute but uses accounting numbers, not cash flows, and ignores the time value of money. Know the formula and why it can only be a relative comparison.","concepts":["fin201-c09-arr"]},{"id":"fin201-t09-2","order":2,"title":"Payback","summary":"Payback asks how many years it takes to get the initial investment back; discounted payback asks the same after discounting each flow. Both are quick screens that ignore everything after the recovery date. Practise the cumulative table and the fractional year.","concepts":["fin201-c09-payback","fin201-c09-discounted-payback"]},{"id":"fin201-t09-3","order":3,"title":"Net present value","summary":"NPV = CF0 + PV is the rule that tracks shareholder wealth, so it wins whenever rules conflict. The four-step sequence organizes the analysis from cash flows to the accept/reject decision.","concepts":["fin201-c09-npv-rule","fin201-c09-npv-steps"]},{"id":"fin201-t09-4","order":4,"title":"Internal rate of return","summary":"IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV zero, the project's own return. It matches NPV for conventional flows, but fails with several sign changes, with financing-type flows, and when ranking mutually exclusive projects. The NPV profile and crossover rate show why.","concepts":["fin201-c09-irr-rule","fin201-c09-npv-profile","fin201-c09-multiple-irr","fin201-c09-financing-flows","fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","fin201-c09-crossover","fin201-c09-mirr"]},{"id":"fin201-t09-5","order":5,"title":"Profitability index","summary":"The PI divides the PV of the inflows by the PV of the outflows, a benefit/cost ratio. It gives the same accept/reject answer as NPV but a different ranking, because it measures profit per dollar invested.","concepts":["fin201-c09-pi","fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"]},{"id":"fin201-t09-6","order":6,"title":"Project relationships and rationing","summary":"Projects can be independent, dependent or mutually exclusive, and that decides how the decision rules are used. When funds run out, positive-NPV projects must be rejected (capital rationing) and the PI ranks them.","concepts":["fin201-c09-project-relationships","fin201-c09-capital-rationing"]},{"id":"fin201-t09-7","order":7,"title":"Unequal lives","summary":"Mutually exclusive projects with different lives cannot be compared on raw NPV. Convert them with the equivalent annual value (EAV), a constant chain of replacement (CCR, NPV to infinity) or the lowest common multiple of the lives.","concepts":["fin201-c09-eav","fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"fin201-c09-arr","topic":"fin201-t09-1","term":"Accounting rate of return (ARR / ROC)","definition":"ARR (ROC) = expected average annual profit after tax divided by average investment, where average investment = (I0 + book value at the end of the project)/2. ROE and ROA use average book equity and average assets as the denominator instead.","explain":"ARR compares accounting profit with the average book value of the capital tied up in the project. It is quick to calculate, but it uses accounting figures (which may include accruals) rather than cash flows, ignores the time value of money and averages a multi-year project into one number. It is a relative comparison against the ROC of your other projects or a target, never an absolute decision on its own.","example":"A project costs $900,000 and will have a book value of $300,000 at the end. Average profit after tax is $90,000 a year. Average investment = (900,000 + 300,000)/2 = $600,000, so ARR = 90,000/600,000 = 15.00%. Sample exam Q10(a): $215,000 / [($1.2M + $0.96M)/2] = 19.91% per annum.","contrast":"ARR is a ratio of accounting numbers (profit over book value) with no discounting; IRR is the discount rate that sets the NPV of the cash flows to zero. ARR is not payback (a time in years) and not a cash yield (profit, not cash flow, over the average rather than the initial investment). ROC, ROE and ROA differ only in the denominator.","trap":"Dividing by I0 instead of the average investment (arr-divide-i0); using cash flow instead of profit after tax (arr-cash-not-profit); comparing ARR with the discount rate R and calling it an accept decision. Cue words: \"net income\", \"residual value\", \"average\", \"state the decision\" (answer: relative only; use NPV).","mnemonic":"Average profit over average investment: two averages.","formula":"ARR = avg annual profit after tax / [(I0 + book value at end)/2]","given":false,"archetypes":["A9"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-payback","topic":"fin201-t09-2","term":"Payback period","definition":"The payback period is the time, in years and part years, until the cumulative undiscounted cash flows equal the initial investment. Accept the project if its payback is shorter than a preset limit.","explain":"Cumulate the cash flows year by year, find the year in which the investment is recovered, and add the fraction of that year: amount still to recover divided by that year's cash flow. The maths is easy and quick, but payback ignores the time value of money, ignores all income after the payback date (so it is biased against long projects) and needs an arbitrary cutoff. It is a relative comparison only.","example":"Cost $120,000; cash flows $30,000, $40,000, $45,000, $50,000. Cumulative: 30,000; 70,000; 115,000. After year 3, $5,000 remains and year 4 brings $50,000: 5,000/50,000 = 0.1, so payback = 3.1 years. For level flows, payback = cost / annual flow (for example $1,370,000 / $285,000 = 4.81 years).","contrast":"Payback adds raw cash flows; discounted payback adds present values at R. A payback shorter than the life guarantees NPV > 0 only at a zero discount rate. Payback measures liquidity (time to get cash back), not value created.","trap":"Counting the whole recovery year (payback-after-recovery: 4.00 instead of 3.1); dividing the remainder by the previous year's flow; applying the cost/annual-flow shortcut to uneven flows or beyond the life (if the total inflow never reaches the cost, the project never pays back).","mnemonic":"Years before recovery + remaining over next.","formula":"Payback = k + (I0 - cumulative CF_k) / CF_(k+1);  level flows: I0 / CF","given":false,"archetypes":["A9"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","topic":"fin201-t09-2","term":"Discounted payback period","definition":"The discounted payback period is the time until the cumulative present values of the cash flows, CF_t/(1+R)^t, equal the initial investment.","explain":"It is the payback table with each flow discounted at R first, so it removes the time-value criticism. It still ignores income after the discounted payback date and still needs a cutoff. For conventional flows and R > 0 it is always longer than the ordinary payback, and if it is shorter than the project's life, the NPV is positive.","example":"Cost 100; four flows of 40; R = 10%. Ordinary payback = 2.5 years. PVs: 36.36, 33.06, 30.05, 27.32; cumulative PV after year 3 = 99.47, so 0.53 remains: 0.53/27.32 = 0.02. Discounted payback = 3.02 years (SM 9-4 style).","contrast":"Payback vs discounted payback: raw flows vs present values. Discounted payback < life implies NPV > 0; ordinary payback < life implies nothing at R > 0. Both ignore flows after the recovery date and give only a relative comparison.","trap":"Dividing the PV remainder by the undiscounted flow of the recovery year (both parts must be in PV terms); accumulating undiscounted flows by mistake; forgetting that a project can pay back in raw terms yet never in PV terms (the project then has NPV < 0).","mnemonic":"Discount first, then add up.","formula":"accumulate CF_t/(1+R)^t until = I0;  fraction = PV still to recover / PV of next flow","given":false,"archetypes":["A9"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","topic":"fin201-t09-3","term":"Net present value (NPV) rule","definition":"NPV = CF0 + PV, where PV = CF1/(1+R) + CF2/(1+R)^2 + ... and CF0 is negative (the initial outlay), so NPV = PV of future cash flows - initial investment. Accept the project if NPV > 0.","explain":"NPV is the expected change in shareholder wealth, in today's dollars, after the time value of money. It gives an absolute accept/reject decision on each project as well as a relative comparison, and it is the rule that serves shareholders, so when another rule disagrees, NPV wins. Its one weakness is that it ignores the amount of capital tied up (the PI deals with that).","example":"CF0 = -10,000, CF1 = 4,500, CF2 = 5,200, CF3 = 3,900, R = 9%: PV = 4,128.44 + 4,376.74 + 3,011.52 = 11,516.69, so NPV = +1,516.69: accept. Sample exam Q10(b): -1,200,000 + 215,000 x 4.9676 + 960,000/1.12^8 = +$255,770.45.","contrast":"NPV is a dollar amount at the required return R; IRR is a percentage you compare with R; PI is a ratio. Excel's NPV() is really a PV() and discounts the first value by one period: add the outlay separately.","trap":"Entering CF0 with the wrong sign (sign-cf0); treating the outlay as year 1 (cf0-as-period-1); forgetting to add the residual (dropped-salvage-term) or not discounting it (salvage-not-discounted); reporting the PV of the inflows as the NPV. NPV = 0 means indifferent; negative NPV projects are sometimes forced (environmental repair).","mnemonic":"NPV = what it is worth (PV) minus what it costs (CF0).","formula":"NPV = CF0 + CF1/(1+R) + CF2/(1+R)^2 + ... + CFN/(1+R)^N","given":true,"archetypes":["A10"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"The five decision rules (plus discounted payback): rule, good points, bad points","head":["Rule","Good points","Bad points"],"rows":[["ARR (ROC)","Quick and easy to calculate","Accounting figures, not cash flows (may include accruals); no time value of money; single-year averages of a multi-year project; relative comparison only"],["Payback","Easy maths, quick to calculate","No time value of money; ignores income after the payback period; arbitrary cutoff, relative comparison only"],["Discounted payback","Brings in the time value of money","Still ignores income after the discounted payback period; still only a relative comparison"],["NPV","Equals the change in shareholder wealth; uses time value of money; absolute decision and relative comparison","Does not consider the amount of capital tied up"],["IRR","Uses time value of money; shows the size of the return; agrees with NPV for conventional flows","Multiple IRRs (or none) with unconventional flows; can mis-rank mutually exclusive projects"],["PI (benefit/cost)","Same accept/reject as NPV; profit per dollar invested, so the right ranking under capital rationing","Can mis-rank mutually exclusive projects of different scale; two definitions (1-based and 0-based)"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","topic":"fin201-t09-3","term":"The four-step NPV analysis","definition":"The professor's sequence: (1) work out the expected after-tax cash flows CF0, CF1, ...; (2) decide the discount rate and discount to today; (3) if lives are unequal, equalize them with EAV or CCR; (4) decide: independent projects, accept if NPV > 0; mutually exclusive projects, accept the highest positive NPV (the least negative if you must take one).","explain":"Steps 1 and 2 are the hard parts (forecasting incremental after-tax cash flows and choosing R). Step 3 only matters when mutually exclusive projects have different lives. Step 4 is the decision: each independent project is judged on NPV > 0, while mutually exclusive projects compete and only the best NPV survives.","example":"Projects P and Q (3-year lives, R = 10%) have NPV(P) = +$15,154.02 and NPV(Q) = +$14,522.92. Same lives, so skip step 3; both positive but mutually exclusive, so accept P (the higher NPV), even though Q has the higher IRR.","contrast":"Independent vs mutually exclusive in step 4: independent means accept all with NPV > 0; mutually exclusive means accept only the best. Step 3 (equalize lives) is different from step 2 (discount to today).","trap":"Skipping step 3 when lives differ and comparing raw NPVs; rejecting a forced project because its NPV is negative; using IRR or PI to pick between mutually exclusive projects.","mnemonic":"Flows, rate, lives, decide.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"Internal rate of return (IRR) rule","definition":"The IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV = 0: 0 = CF0 + CF1/(1+IRR) + ... + CFN/(1+IRR)^N. For conventional cash flows, accept if IRR > R and reject if IRR < R.","explain":"IRR is what the project returns; R is the return you need. It is the same idea as the yield to maturity on a bond. For conventional flows (CF0 negative, all later flows positive or zero) the NPV profile crosses zero once, so IRR > R means NPV > 0 and the two rules agree. It uses the time value of money and shows the size of the return, but it can have several answers when the flows change sign.","example":"Flows -1,000, +600, +600: x = 1/(1+IRR) solves 600x^2 + 600x - 1,000 = 0, so IRR = 13.07%. With R = 10%, IRR > R: accept (NPV at 10% = +41.32). With R = 15%, IRR < R: reject. Closed forms: single payment IRR = (FV/PV)^(1/N) - 1; level perpetuity IRR = CF/cost.","contrast":"IRR vs R: the project's own return vs the return required. IRR vs ARR: discount rate vs accounting ratio. IRR vs YTM: the same calculation applied to a project instead of a bond. The IRR rule is a shortcut for NPV only when flows are conventional.","trap":"Comparing the wrong way round (irr-direction: accepting when IRR < R); using the rule with unconventional or financing-type flows; computing IRR as average gain per year (no compounding); choosing the higher IRR among mutually exclusive projects.","mnemonic":"IRR above R: go. IRR below R: no.","formula":"NPV(IRR) = 0;  accept if IRR > R (conventional flows)","given":false,"archetypes":["A10"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"NPV profile","definition":"An NPV profile is the graph of a project's NPV against the discount rate. Its vertical intercept (R = 0) is the sum of the undiscounted cash flows, it approaches CF0 as R grows without limit, and it crosses the horizontal axis at the IRR(s).","explain":"For conventional flows the profile slopes downward because a higher R shrinks the PV of the later inflows while the outlay at t = 0 stays put. Reading it: left of the IRR NPV is positive (accept), right of the IRR it is negative (reject). For financing-type flows it slopes upward, and unconventional flows can make it cross the axis more than once.","example":"Flows -1,000, +600, +600: NPV(0%) = +200 (the sum), NPV(10%) = +41.32, NPV(20%) = -83.33, and it crosses zero at the IRR = 13.07%. As R becomes very large, NPV tends to -1,000.","contrast":"NPV profile vs payback or cash-flow timeline (a profile has the discount rate on the horizontal axis). Conventional (downward) vs financing-type (upward) profiles. The IRR is read at the crossing; the required return is where you read your own NPV.","trap":"Thinking NPV approaches zero for very high R (it approaches CF0); forgetting that NPV at 0% is just the sum of the flows including the outlay; assuming every profile slopes downward.","mnemonic":"Left of the IRR positive, right of it negative.","formula":"NPV(R = 0) = sum of CF_t;  NPV(R -> infinity) -> CF0","given":false,"archetypes":["A12"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"Multiple (or no) IRRs","definition":"A project can have as many IRRs as there are sign changes in its cash flows (a rule-of-signs upper limit). With unconventional flows there may be several IRRs, or none, so the rule \"accept if IRR > R\" is not reliable and the NPV at R must decide.","explain":"Solving NPV = 0 means finding the roots of an equation, and a profile that crosses the axis several times has several roots. Unconventional flows arise from big maintenance or clean-up costs late in the life and from incremental (A minus B) streams. A calculator shows just one root, so check the NPV at R or the profile.","example":"Flows -500,000; +1,235,000; -756,000 have two IRRs, 12% and 35%. NPV is positive only between them: NPV(25%) = +4,160.00 (accept) but NPV(8%) = -4,629.63 even though both IRRs exceed 8%. The flows -500,000; +800,000; -400,000 have no real IRR at all (discriminant < 0).","contrast":"Conventional (one sign change, at most one IRR) vs unconventional (several sign changes). Multiple IRRs (several rates give NPV = 0) vs financing-type flows (one IRR but the rule reverses).","trap":"Trusting the single IRR the calculator returns; accepting because \"both IRRs exceed R\"; believing two sign changes always give exactly two IRRs. When NPV and IRR disagree, use NPV (seminar 5).","mnemonic":"Count the sign flips: that many IRRs, at most.","formula":"max number of IRRs = number of sign changes in CF0, CF1, ..., CFN","given":false,"archetypes":["A12"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"Financing-type cash flows","definition":"Financing-type flows have a cash inflow first and outflows later (CF0 positive, later flows negative). The IRR is then a rate you pay, not earn, and the rule reverses: accept only if IRR < R.","explain":"Think of borrowing: you receive money now and repay later. The NPV profile slopes upward, so NPV rises with R. Customer prepayments before the work is done are an example. The NPV at R gives the right decision and avoids the sign confusion.","example":"Receive $200 now and pay $236 in a year: IRR = 236/200 - 1 = 18%. At R = 12% the NPV is 200 - 236/1.12 = -$10.71, so reject: the money costs 18%, more than the 12% you can earn elsewhere. Accept only if R > 18%.","contrast":"Investing-type flows (outlay first): accept if IRR > R, downward profile. Financing-type flows (inflow first): accept if IRR < R, upward profile. Both give a single IRR; unlike multiple IRRs, the problem here is the direction of the rule.","trap":"Applying the standard rule (IRR > R) and accepting a costly source of funds; reading the NPV profile slope backwards (irr-direction).","mnemonic":"Money in first means you are the borrower: low IRR wins.","formula":"financing-type: accept if IRR < R","given":false,"archetypes":["A12"],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"Ranking conflict between NPV and IRR","definition":"For mutually exclusive projects, NPV and IRR can rank the projects differently because of scale (different outlays) or timing (different patterns of flows). The correct choice is the higher NPV at the required return R, equivalently the incremental IRR (A minus B, with A the bigger project) beating R.","explain":"Only one project can be taken, so the question is which adds more wealth, and that is the NPV. A smaller project can have a higher IRR but a lower NPV. Two ways to settle it: compute both NPVs at R, or compute the IRR of the difference in cash flows and compare it with R.","example":"P: -90,000; 36,000; 44,000; 48,000 and Q: -60,000; 29,000; 31,000; 30,000, R = 10%. NPV(P) = 15,154.02 > NPV(Q) = 14,522.92, but IRR(P) = 18.87% < IRR(Q) = 23.21%. Choose P. The incremental IRR of P - Q is 11.03% > 10%, which agrees.","contrast":"Independent projects: IRR and NPV agree for conventional flows. Mutually exclusive projects: they can disagree, and NPV is right. The crossover rate tells where the NPV ranking flips.","trap":"Picking the higher IRR (picked-higher-irr); comparing PI ranks across different scales; forgetting that the ranking can depend on R (it changes at the crossover rate).","mnemonic":"Pick the bigger wealth, not the bigger percentage.","formula":"choose max NPV at R;  or accept A over B if IRR(A - B) > R","given":false,"archetypes":["A11"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-crossover","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"Crossover rate and incremental IRR","definition":"The crossover rate is the discount rate at which two projects have equal NPV; it is the IRR of the difference in their cash flows (A minus B, subtracting year by year including CF0). Below it the project with the higher NPV at low rates wins; above it the other wins.","explain":"Subtract the two cash-flow lists year by year; the IRR of that incremental stream is the crossover rate. It does not matter which project you subtract from which, but the ranges must be stated carefully: compare R with the crossover to see which project is better.","example":"P - Q = -30,000; 7,000; 13,000; 18,000 has IRR 11.03%, the crossover. At R = 10% (below 11.03%) P has the higher NPV (15,154.02 vs 14,522.92); at R = 15% (above) Q does (8,383.33 vs 6,135.45).","contrast":"Crossover rate (where NPVs are equal, IRR of A - B) vs each project's own IRR (where its own NPV is zero). The difference of two conventional streams can be unconventional, so check for multiple crossovers.","trap":"Mis-stating which project wins below or above the crossover; subtracting in different years; confusing the crossover with an IRR of one project; PI ranking on different scales.","mnemonic":"Equal NPV where the difference has zero NPV.","formula":"Crossover = IRR of (A - B) cash flows","given":false,"archetypes":["A11"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-mirr","topic":"fin201-t09-4","term":"Modified IRR (MIRR)","definition":"The MIRR modifies the cash flows first (discounting outflows, compounding inflows at the required return, or a combination) so that only one sign change remains, then computes an IRR on the two modified flows.","explain":"MIRR was proposed to remove the multiple-IRR problem. It is controversial: there are several different MIRRs, it is not a true rate of return, and you must already know the required return, in which case the NPV answers the question directly. Low priority for the exam; know the idea.","example":"Flows -1,000; +600; +700 at R = 10%. Reinvestment approach: FV of inflows at the end = 600 x 1.10 + 700 = 1,360; MIRR = (1,360/1,000)^(1/2) - 1 = 16.62%, below the IRR of 18.88% because the 600 is reinvested at 10%, not at the IRR.","contrast":"MIRR (modified flows, one answer) vs IRR (the actual flows, possibly several answers). MIRR is not the same as the required return.","trap":"Calling the MIRR a true rate of return; forgetting that different MIRR methods give different numbers; thinking IRR assumes reinvestment at the IRR (the NPV and IRR are not affected by what is done with the cash).","mnemonic":"Modify the flows, then IRR.","formula":"MIRR = (FV of inflows at R / PV of outflows at R)^(1/N) - 1","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-pi","topic":"fin201-t09-5","term":"Profitability index (benefit/cost ratio)","definition":"PI = PV of cash inflows / PV of cash outflows. For conventional flows PI = PV / initial outlay = 1 + NPV/outlay. Accept if PI > 1 (NPV > 0); reject if PI < 1.","explain":"The PI is bang for the buck: the present value you get for each dollar you put in. Its accept/reject decision always matches the NPV, but it ranks by profitability per dollar, which is what matters when capital is limited. A PI of 0.98 means the project is rejected; the PI is 1-based, not 0-based.","example":"Using the NPV example above: PV of inflows = 11,516.69 and outlay = 10,000, so PI = 1.1517 (= 1 + 1,516.69/10,000). PI > 1: accept. A project with PV of inflows $38,200 and outlay $40,000 has PI = 0.955: reject.","contrast":"PI (PV/outlay, 1-based) vs the alternative 0-based version NPV/outlay: adding or subtracting 1 gives the same rankings. PI vs NPV: same decision, different ranking.","trap":"Reading a PI below 1 as acceptable (PI = 0.98 is a reject); using NPV/outlay and calling it PI > 1 (pi-zero-based); using PI to choose between mutually exclusive projects of different scale.","mnemonic":"PI above one: you get back more than you put in.","formula":"PI = PV of inflows / PV of outflows = 1 + NPV / initial outlay","given":true,"archetypes":["A10"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","topic":"fin201-t09-5","term":"PI versus NPV: the same decision, different ranking","definition":"NPV and PI always give the same accept/reject decision for a single project, but they can rank projects differently: NPV ranks on the dollar change in wealth and ignores capital tied up, while PI ranks on profit per dollar of capital.","explain":"A big project can have the larger NPV and the smaller PI. For mutually exclusive projects of different scale, use the NPV (it measures wealth). Under capital rationing, use the PI (it ranks the best use of each scarce dollar).","example":"Mutually exclusive S (outlay 40,000, PV 52,000: NPV 12,000, PI 1.30) and L (outlay 150,000, PV 175,000: NPV 25,000, PI 1.17). NPV picks L; PI picks S. If both could be done with enough funds, accept both (both NPV > 0).","contrast":"Which is best: NPV (mutually exclusive, no budget limit) vs PI (independent projects under a budget limit). PI and NPV both accept at PI > 1 / NPV > 0.","trap":"Using PI to rank mutually exclusive projects that differ in scale (the SM 9-16 trap); using NPV to rank projects when funds are limited; mixing the 1-based and 0-based PI when comparing.","mnemonic":"NPV measures size; PI measures efficiency.","formula":"PI(1-based) = 1 + NPV/outlay;  PI(0-based) = NPV/outlay","given":false,"archetypes":["A11"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","topic":"fin201-t09-6","term":"Independent, dependent and mutually exclusive projects","definition":"Independent projects can be taken in any combination without affecting each other's values; dependent projects affect each other's values (one makes the other worth more or less); mutually exclusive projects can only be taken one at a time, which includes timing and replacement decisions.","explain":"The relationship decides how the rules are used. Independent: accept each with NPV > 0. Mutually exclusive: pick the best, equalizing lives if needed. Timing decisions (when to replace, retire or close) are mutually exclusive: pick the timing with the maximum NPV.","example":"A new delivery van and a new website are independent. A new restaurant whose customers depend on a new highway exit is dependent on that exit. Machine A versus Machine B for the same job are mutually exclusive, and so are \"replace now\" versus \"replace in two years\".","contrast":"Independent (no effect on each other) vs dependent (one changes the other's cash flows) vs mutually exclusive (taking one rules out the others). Mutually exclusive projects are chosen by NPV, not by accepting everything positive.","trap":"Treating mutually exclusive projects as independent and accepting both; forgetting that timing and replacement decisions are mutually exclusive choices.","mnemonic":"Independent: all that pass. Exclusive: only the best.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","topic":"fin201-t09-6","term":"Capital rationing","definition":"Capital rationing is rejecting positive-NPV projects because of a lack of funds (or people). When projects are independent, rank them by PI and take them in rank order until the budget is used.","explain":"Without a budget limit, every independent project with NPV > 0 is accepted. With a hard limit the firm must choose, and the best choice maximizes total NPV per dollar spent, so rank by PI. It is a soft constraint when set internally and a hard one when the capital market denies funds.","example":"Budget $400,000. B (outlay 200,000, NPV 50,000, PI 1.25), D (100,000, NPV 18,000, PI 1.18), E (100,000, NPV 14,000, PI 1.14), A (300,000, NPV 36,000, PI 1.12). Rank by PI: B, D, E use $400,000 for total NPV $82,000; A is rejected despite its positive NPV. Sample exam Q5: capital rationing = \"rejecting a positive NPV project because of a lack of funds\".","contrast":"Capital rationing (a funds constraint on independent projects, rank by PI) vs mutually exclusive choices (rank by NPV) vs simply stopping to analyse projects in bad markets (not rationing).","trap":"Ranking by NPV or by IRR when funds are limited; calling a pause in project analysis \"rationing\"; forgetting the best set of projects can leave some budget unused.","mnemonic":"Limited budget? Rank by PI.","formula":"rank by PI = PV inflows / outlay, take in order until the budget is gone","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-eav","topic":"fin201-t09-7","term":"Equivalent annual value (EAV / EAA / EAB / EAC)","definition":"The equivalent annual value converts an NPV into the level annual amount over the project's life with the same present value: EAV = NPV / annuity factor(R, N), with annuity factor = [1 - (1+R)^-N]/R. For costs it is the equivalent annual cost (EAC).","explain":"Use it to compare mutually exclusive projects with unequal lives that will be repeated: compare the annual amounts, not the raw NPVs. Higher EAV (or lower EAC) wins. The annuity factor comes from the table sheet or the formula, using each project's own life N.","example":"Two maintenance programs at R = 10%: spend $2.0M for 2 years of work, or $2.7M for 3 years. Annuity factors: 1.7355 and 2.4869. EAC = 2.0/1.7355 = $1.1524M a year versus 2.7/2.4869 = $1.0857M a year. The 3-year program is cheaper per year.","contrast":"EAV (annual amount) vs CCR (NPV to infinity) vs LCM (repeat to a common horizon): all give the same ranking. EAV = NPV / annuity factor; CCR = EAV / R.","trap":"Comparing raw NPVs of unequal-life projects (raw-npv-unequal-lives); using the wrong N in the annuity factor (wrong-n-annuity); sign confusion with costs (smaller cost wins).","mnemonic":"Spread the NPV over the years: NPV over the annuity factor.","formula":"EAV = NPV / [(1 - (1+R)^-N)/R]","given":false,"archetypes":["A13"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","topic":"fin201-t09-7","term":"Constant chain of replacement and lowest common multiple","definition":"CCR (NPV to infinity) values the project replaced forever: CCR = NPV x (1+R)^N / ((1+R)^N - 1) = EAV/R. The lowest common multiple (LCM) method repeats each project until the lives meet, repeating the whole NPV at each replacement.","explain":"These are two other ways to put unequal lives on the same footing. The LCM horizon for lives of 2 and 3 years is 6 years: repeat the 2-year program three times and the 3-year program twice. All three methods (EAV, CCR, LCM) rank the projects identically.","example":"Programs of $2.0M for 2 years and $2.7M for 3 years at 10%: CCR = -2.0 x 1.21/0.21 = -$11.5238M versus -2.7 x 1.331/0.331 = -$10.8571M. LCM over 6 years: -$5.0189M versus -$4.7285M; scaling by 1.1^6/(1.1^6 - 1) gives the same CCRs. The two programs have equal annual cost at 26.27% per annum.","contrast":"CCR is EAV capitalized as a perpetuity (EAV / R); LCM stops at a common horizon. Do not compare raw NPVs or stop at the shorter life.","trap":"Repeating only the cost and not the whole NPV in the LCM method; applying (1+R)^N with the wrong N; comparing signs the wrong way for costs (the less negative CCR wins).","mnemonic":"Chain the project forever: EAV over R.","formula":"CCR = NPV x (1+R)^N / ((1+R)^N - 1) = EAV / R","given":false,"archetypes":["A13"],"weight":2,"figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"fin201-a09-01","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","title":"Order the four NPV steps","prompt":"Put the professor's four-step NPV analysis in the correct order.","items":["Work out the expected after-tax cash flows CF0, CF1, CF2, ...","Decide the discount rate and discount the cash flows back to today","If projects have unequal lives, make them equivalent with EAV or CCR","Decide: accept NPV > 0 (independent) or the highest NPV (mutually exclusive)"],"explanation":"You cannot discount before you have the cash flows, you cannot compare unequal lives before you have NPVs, and the accept/reject decision comes last."},{"id":"fin201-a09-02","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","title":"Which rule is it?","prompt":"Sort each statement under the decision rule it describes.","buckets":["ARR","Payback","NPV","IRR"],"items":[{"text":"Divides average profit after tax by average investment","bucket":0},{"text":"Built from accounting figures that may include accruals","bucket":0},{"text":"Averages a multi-year project into a single figure","bucket":0},{"text":"Needs a cutoff measured in years","bucket":1},{"text":"Ignores all income after the recovery date","bucket":1},{"text":"Cumulates the raw cash flows until the cost is back","bucket":1},{"text":"Equals the change in shareholder wealth","bucket":2},{"text":"Expressed in dollars of value added today","bucket":2},{"text":"Does not consider the amount of capital tied up","bucket":2},{"text":"Same idea as the yield to maturity on a bond","bucket":3},{"text":"Can have several answers when the cash flows change sign","bucket":3},{"text":"Accept if it is greater than R (conventional flows)","bucket":3}],"explanation":"ARR and payback are accounting/relative rules; NPV tracks shareholder wealth and ignores capital tied up; IRR is the project's own return and breaks down for unconventional flows."},{"id":"fin201-a09-03","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","title":"Match the rule to its decision criterion","prompt":"Match each decision rule to the condition under which it says accept.","pairs":[{"left":"ARR (ROC)","right":"Compares favourably with the ROC of other projects or a target (relative only)"},{"left":"Payback","right":"Shorter than a preset limit in years"},{"left":"Discounted payback","right":"Present values recover the cost sooner than the preset limit"},{"left":"NPV","right":"Greater than zero"},{"left":"IRR","right":"Greater than R, for conventional cash flows"},{"left":"PI","right":"Greater than 1"}],"explanation":"Only NPV, IRR and PI give an absolute decision against a market benchmark; ARR and the payback rules are relative comparisons against internal limits."},{"id":"fin201-a09-04","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","title":"Order the payback steps","prompt":"Put the steps for calculating a payback period with uneven cash flows in order.","items":["Write the cumulative cash flows year by year","Find the year in which the cumulative total first covers the initial investment","Divide the amount still unrecovered at the start of that year by that year's cash flow","Add the fraction to the number of whole years completed before that year"],"explanation":"Payback = whole years before recovery + (remaining / next year's flow). Reversing the divisor to the previous year's flow is a classic slip."},{"id":"fin201-a09-05","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","title":"Accept or reject?","prompt":"Sort each result into the decision it implies (conventional cash flows, firm's required return as stated).","buckets":["Accept","Reject"],"items":[{"text":"NPV = +$3,200","bucket":0},{"text":"PI = 1.08","bucket":0},{"text":"IRR = 14% with R = 11%","bucket":0},{"text":"Discounted payback 3.4 years on a 5-year life","bucket":0},{"text":"NPV = -$450","bucket":1},{"text":"PI = 0.97","bucket":1},{"text":"IRR = 9% with R = 12%","bucket":1},{"text":"Payback 4.2 years against a 3-year limit","bucket":1}],"explanation":"Accept on NPV > 0, PI > 1, IRR > R (conventional flows), or a discounted payback inside the life. A payback longer than the preset limit fails the payback screen."},{"id":"fin201-a09-06","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","title":"Cash-flow pattern and its IRR problem","prompt":"Match each cash-flow pattern to what it implies for the IRR.","pairs":[{"left":"-, +, +, +  (conventional)","right":"At most one IRR; IRR > R agrees with NPV > 0"},{"left":"-, +, -  (reclamation at the end)","right":"Up to two IRRs; NPV at R decides"},{"left":"-, +, +, -, +, +  (a maintenance year)","right":"Up to three IRRs"},{"left":"+, -  (inflow first, outflow later)","right":"Financing type: accept only if IRR < R"},{"left":"-500,000; +800,000; -400,000","right":"No real IRR; NPV is negative at every rate"}],"explanation":"The number of IRRs is limited by the sign changes; whenever the pattern is not conventional, rely on the NPV at the required return."}],"formulas":[{"id":"fin201-f09-npv","name":"Net present value","formula":"NPV = CF0 + CF1/(1+R) + CF2/(1+R)^2 + ... + CFN/(1+R)^N","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c09-npv-rule","fin201-c09-npv-steps"],"when":"Every discounted-cash-flow decision: CF0 is the (negative) outlay at t = 0 and the rest is the PV of the inflows.","trap":"CF0 is negative and is not discounted; Excel's NPV() is a PV() that discounts the first value one period."},{"id":"fin201-f09-pi","name":"Profitability index","formula":"PI = PV of inflows / PV of outflows = 1 + NPV / outlay","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c09-pi","fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","fin201-c09-capital-rationing"],"when":"To rank independent projects under capital rationing; accept if PI > 1.","trap":"It is 1-based (PI 0.98 is a reject); NPV/outlay is the 0-based variant, and PI can mis-rank mutually exclusive projects of different scale."},{"id":"fin201-f09-irr","name":"Internal rate of return","formula":"0 = CF0 + CF1/(1+IRR) + ... + CFN/(1+IRR)^N","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-irr-rule","fin201-c09-npv-profile","fin201-c09-multiple-irr","fin201-c09-financing-flows"],"when":"Find the project's own return with the calculator CF worksheet; accept if IRR > R for conventional flows.","trap":"Multiple IRRs with sign changes and a reversed rule for financing-type flows; the calculator shows just one root."},{"id":"fin201-f09-crossover","name":"Crossover rate","formula":"Crossover = IRR of the difference in cash flows (A - B)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-crossover","fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"when":"Mutually exclusive projects whose NPV ranking changes with R.","trap":"Subtract year by year including CF0; then state which project wins below and above the crossover."},{"id":"fin201-f09-payback","name":"Payback period","formula":"Payback = years before recovery + (amount still to recover) / (next year's cash flow)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-payback"],"when":"A quick screen of how fast the investment is recovered; accept if shorter than a preset limit.","trap":"Do not count the whole recovery year or divide by the previous year's flow; if the total inflow is below the cost, the project never pays back."},{"id":"fin201-f09-disc-payback","name":"Discounted payback","formula":"accumulate CF_t/(1+R)^t until the total equals I0; fraction = PV still to recover / PV of next flow","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-discounted-payback"],"when":"The payback screen with the time value of money included.","trap":"Both the numerator and the denominator of the fraction must be present values."},{"id":"fin201-f09-arr","name":"Accounting rate of return (ROC)","formula":"ARR = average annual profit after tax / [(I0 + book value at end)/2]","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-arr"],"when":"Accounting screen comparing a project's return on capital with other projects.","trap":"Use profit, not cash flow, and divide by the average investment, not I0; do not compare with the discount rate."},{"id":"fin201-f09-eav","name":"Equivalent annual value","formula":"EAV = NPV / [(1 - (1+R)^-N)/R]","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-eav"],"when":"Compare mutually exclusive projects with unequal lives that will be replaced (EAB if benefits, EAC if costs).","trap":"Use each project's own N in the annuity factor; for costs the smaller annual cost wins."},{"id":"fin201-f09-ccr","name":"Constant chain of replacement (NPV to infinity)","formula":"CCR = NPV x (1+R)^N / ((1+R)^N - 1) = EAV / R","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"when":"Same comparison as EAV, replacing each project forever; consistent with the LCM method.","trap":"In the LCM method repeat the whole NPV at each replacement; CCR and EAV always rank the projects the same way."}],"questions":[{"id":"fin201-q09-001","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"You are considering investing in a project which is expected to provide $215,000 net income per year for each of the next eight years, in return for an up-front investment of $1.2 million. You expect the residual value will be $0.96 million at the end. Your discount rate is 12% per annum. There is no tax. Determine each of the following and state the decision that arises from each figure: (a) accounting rate of return; (b) NPV. Enter part (a), the accounting rate of return, in % per annum.","answer":19.91,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"215000/((1200000+960000)/2)*100","steps":[{"label":"Average annual profit after tax","value":215000.0,"check":"215000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Average investment (I0 + residual)/2","value":1080000.0,"check":"(1200000+960000)/2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"ARR (ROC)","value":19.91,"check":"215000/((1200000+960000)/2)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Average annual profit after tax = $215,000 (there is no tax, so net income is the profit after tax).","Average investment = (I0 + book value at the end)/2 = ($1,200,000 + $960,000)/2 = $1,080,000.","ARR = ROC = $215,000 / $1,080,000 = 0.19907 = 19.91% per annum.","Decision: ARR gives only a relative signal. Accept if 19.91% beats the firm's target or the ROC of its other projects. It is a ratio of accounting figures, so it must not be compared with the 12% discount rate. The accept/reject call for this project comes from the NPV in part (b)."],"keystrokes":["1,200,000 + 960,000 = 2,160,000","2,160,000 ÷ 2 = 1,080,000","215,000 ÷ 1,080,000 = 0.19907, i.e. 19.91%"],"errors":[{"id":"arr-divide-i0","label":"Divided by the up-front investment","value":17.92,"check":"215000/1200000*100","explain":"ARR divides by the AVERAGE investment (I0 + residual)/2, not by I0 alone."},{"id":"arr-residual-only","label":"Divided by the residual value","value":22.4,"check":"215000/960000*100","explain":"The residual is only the closing book value; the average investment is the mean of the opening and closing book values."},{"id":"arr-sum-not-half","label":"Forgot to halve the sum","value":9.95,"check":"215000/(1200000+960000)*100","explain":"(I0 + residual) is the sum of two book values; divide it by 2 to get the average."}],"explanation":"ARR = average annual profit after tax / average investment, with average investment = (I0 + residual book value)/2 = $1,080,000 here. The tempting I0 denominator ignores that the asset is written down over the project.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q10a"},{"id":"fin201-q09-002","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Ridge Dairy will install a $840,000 pasteurizing line, depreciated straight-line to zero over five years. Expected profits after tax are $94,000, $131,000, $157,000, $142,000 and $104,000 in years 1 to 5. What is the project's accounting rate of return (ROC) in % per annum?","answer":29.9,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"((94000+131000+157000+142000+104000)/5)/((840000+0)/2)*100","steps":[{"label":"Average annual profit after tax","value":125600.0,"check":"(94000+131000+157000+142000+104000)/5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Average investment (I0 + 0)/2","value":420000.0,"check":"(840000+0)/2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"ARR (ROC)","value":29.9,"check":"((94000+131000+157000+142000+104000)/5)/((840000+0)/2)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Average profit = (94,000 + 131,000 + 157,000 + 142,000 + 104,000)/5 = 628,000/5 = $125,600.","The line is written down to zero, so average investment = (840,000 + 0)/2 = $420,000.","ARR = 125,600 / 420,000 = 0.29905 = 29.90% per annum."],"keystrokes":["94,000 + 131,000 + 157,000 + 142,000 + 104,000 = 628,000","628,000 ÷ 5 = 125,600","840,000 ÷ 2 = 420,000","125,600 ÷ 420,000 = 0.29905 -> 29.90%"],"errors":[{"id":"arr-divide-i0","label":"Divided by the full $840,000","value":14.95,"check":"125600/840000*100","explain":"With straight-line depreciation to zero the average book value is half the cost, $420,000."},{"id":"arr-total-profit","label":"Used total profit instead of average profit","value":149.52,"check":"628000/420000*100","explain":"ARR uses the AVERAGE annual profit; the five-year total overstates the rate fivefold."}],"explanation":"ARR averages the profit over the life and divides by the average book value. Straight-line depreciation to zero makes the average investment half of the cost.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-003","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Similkameen Winery buys a $600,000 bottling line with a residual book value of $120,000 after six years (straight-line depreciation). It adds $150,000 a year to earnings before depreciation and tax. The tax rate is 30%. Find the ARR (ROC) in % per annum.","answer":13.61,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"((150000-(600000-120000)/6)*(1-0.30))/((600000+120000)/2)*100","steps":[{"label":"Annual depreciation","value":80000.0,"check":"(600000-120000)/6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Annual profit after tax","value":49000.0,"check":"(150000-(600000-120000)/6)*(1-0.30)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Average investment (I0 + residual)/2","value":360000.0,"check":"(600000+120000)/2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"ARR (ROC)","value":13.61,"check":"((150000-(600000-120000)/6)*(1-0.30))/((600000+120000)/2)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Depreciation = (600,000 - 120,000)/6 = $80,000 a year.","Profit after tax = (150,000 - 80,000)(1 - 0.30) = $49,000. (ARR uses profit, not cash flow.)","Average investment = (600,000 + 120,000)/2 = $360,000.","ARR = 49,000/360,000 = 0.13611 = 13.61% per annum."],"keystrokes":["(600,000 − 120,000) ÷ 6 = 80,000","(150,000 − 80,000) × 0.70 = 49,000","(600,000 + 120,000) ÷ 2 = 360,000","49,000 ÷ 360,000 = 0.13611 -> 13.61%"],"errors":[{"id":"arr-cash-not-profit","label":"Used after-tax cash flow instead of profit","value":35.83,"check":"(49000+80000)/360000*100","explain":"Cash flow (profit + depreciation = $129,000) is not accounting profit; ARR uses profit after tax."},{"id":"arr-divide-i0","label":"Divided by the original $600,000","value":8.17,"check":"49000/600000*100","explain":"The denominator is the average investment, $360,000."},{"id":"arr-pretax","label":"Used profit before tax","value":19.44,"check":"70000/360000*100","explain":"The rule uses profit AFTER tax: $70,000 x (1 - 0.30) = $49,000."}],"explanation":"Build profit after tax first (EBIT x (1 - T)), then divide by the average of the opening and residual book values.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-004","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards expects a new packing line to add $64,000 of profit after tax each year for five years. The line costs $500,000 and will have a book value of $100,000 at the end of year 5. What is its ARR (ROC)?","options":["21.33%","12.80%","32.00%","16.00%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Average investment = (500,000 + 100,000)/2 = $300,000, so ARR = 64,000/300,000 = 21.33%. 12.80% divides by I0 alone; 32.00% uses (I0 - residual)/2 = $200,000, which is half the depreciable amount, not the average book value; 16.00% divides by the depreciable amount $400,000.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-005","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $800,000 sorting system at Fraser Valley Packaging is depreciated straight-line to zero over four years. Each year revenue is $520,000, cash costs are $300,000 and the tax rate is 25%. What is the ARR (ROC)?","options":["53.75%","3.75%","26.88%","1.88%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Profit after tax = (520,000 - 300,000 - 200,000)(0.75) = $15,000. Average investment = $400,000. ARR = 15,000/400,000 = 3.75%. 53.75% divides cash flow ($215,000) instead of profit; 26.88% divides cash flow by I0; 1.88% divides profit by I0.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s7"},{"id":"fin201-q09-006","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The accounting rate of return on capital (ROC) of a project is:","options":["expected average annual cash flow divided by the initial investment","the discount rate at which the NPV of the project is zero","the final-year profit divided by the cost of the asset","expected average annual profit after tax divided by average investment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ROC = average annual profit after tax / average investment (capitalization). It uses accounting profit and book values, not cash flows, and is not a discount rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-007","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The board of Banff Springs Spa compares a proposed sauna wing with its other spa projects by dividing the wing's expected average annual profit after tax by the average of its cost and its book value at the end of the project. Which decision rule is the board using?","options":["Internal rate of return","Profitability index","Accounting rate of return (ROC)","Payback period"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Average profit after tax over the average of the opening and closing book values is the ROC form of the ARR. No cash flows are discounted, so it is not IRR or PI, and nothing is measured in years, so it is not payback.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s6-8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-008","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the ARR differ from the IRR?","options":["ARR discounts the cash flows at the required return; IRR leaves them undiscounted and uses book values","ARR divides average accounting profit by average book value; IRR is the discount rate that makes the NPV zero","ARR is built from cash flows and the time value of money; IRR is built from accounting profit","ARR gives an absolute accept/reject decision against R; IRR gives only a relative comparison with other projects"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ARR = accounting profit / book value with no time value of money. IRR is built from cash flows and the time value of money and is compared with the required return R. The reverse statements swap the two rules.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s6-9, s20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-009","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"All of the following are bad points of the accounting rate of return EXCEPT:","options":["it uses accounting figures rather than cash flows","it ignores the time value of money","it is quick and easy to calculate","it uses single-year averages of a multi-year project"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quick and easy calculation is the one good point of ARR. The bad points are accounting-based inputs (possibly including accruals), no time value of money, averaging, and no mechanism for an absolute decision.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-010","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the ARR provide no mechanism for an absolute accept/reject decision on a single project?","options":["it is expressed in dollars rather than as a rate, so it cannot be compared with R","it always equals the IRR for conventional cash flows, so it adds nothing to the IRR rule","it can only be calculated for projects with several IRRs, which makes any decision ambiguous","it is an accounting ratio with no market benchmark, so it can only be judged against other projects or a target"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ARR is not a rate of return in the economic sense: book profit over book value ignores timing. Without a market benchmark such as R, the rule can only rank a project against the ROC of the firm's other projects (a relative comparison).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s8-9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-011","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's ROC is 19.9% and the firm's required return is 12%. An analyst says: \"19.9% beats 12%, so accept.\" What is the best response?","options":["ARR is an accounting ratio, so it cannot be tested against R; use NPV to decide whether to accept","Correct: any rate of return above the required return means a positive NPV","Correct, because ARR is calculated from the same discounted cash flows as the IRR","Incorrect: the project must be rejected because ARR ignores the residual value of the asset"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ARR is built from book profit and book values, with no discounting, so a number above R proves nothing about NPV. Compare ARR with other projects' ROC and make the accept decision with NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s8-9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-012","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the ROC version of the ARR, \"average investment\" is:","options":["the initial investment alone","the initial investment minus the residual value","the total of all annual profits divided by the number of years","(initial investment + book value at the end of the project)/2"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Average investment = (I0 + closing book value)/2: the mean of the opening and closing book values. With depreciation to zero it equals I0/2.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s7"},{"id":"fin201-q09-013","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"ROC, ROE and ROA are all accounting rates of return that differ only in the denominator: average capitalization, average book equity and average assets respectively.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All three divide expected average annual profit after tax by an average book figure. ROC (average investment or capitalization) is the most common because it links to the capital put into the project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-014","concept":"fin201-c09-arr","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"The most common ARR is the _____ because it links with the idea of investing capital into the project.","options":["ROE","ROA","ROC","EPS"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ROC (return on capital) divides average profit after tax by average investment or capitalization; it is the version that mirrors the capital committed to the project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-015","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Westlock Foods will spend $135,000 on a new cold-storage unit. The unit is expected to produce cash flows of $25,000, $40,000, $45,000, $60,000 and $70,000 in years 1 to 5. What is the payback period in years?","answer":3.42,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"3+(135000-(25000+40000+45000))/60000","steps":[{"label":"Cumulative cash flow after year 3","value":110000.0,"check":"25000+40000+45000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Still to recover at the end of year 3","value":25000.0,"check":"135000-(25000+40000+45000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Payback period","value":3.42,"check":"3+(135000-(25000+40000+45000))/60000","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Cumulative flows: year 1 = 25,000; year 2 = 65,000; year 3 = 110,000; year 4 = 170,000.","The $135,000 is recovered during year 4. Still to recover after year 3 = 135,000 - 110,000 = $25,000.","Fraction of year 4 needed = 25,000/60,000 (year 4's cash flow) = 0.4167.","Payback = 3 + 0.4167 = 3.42 years."],"keystrokes":["25,000 + 40,000 + 45,000 = 110,000","135,000 − 110,000 = 25,000","25,000 ÷ 60,000 = 0.4167","3 + 0.4167 = 3.42 years"],"errors":[{"id":"payback-after-recovery","label":"Counted the whole recovery year","value":4.0,"check":"4","explain":"Payback is the year before recovery plus the fraction of the recovery year actually needed, not the end of the recovery year."},{"id":"payback-wrong-flow","label":"Divided by the previous year's flow","value":3.56,"check":"3+25000/45000","explain":"The fraction is remaining ÷ the cash flow of the year in which recovery happens (year 4: $60,000)."},{"id":"payback-avg-flow","label":"Cost divided by the average annual flow","value":2.81,"check":"135000/((25000+40000+45000+60000+70000)/5)","explain":"Cost ÷ average flow is the annuity shortcut; it only works when the flows are equal."}],"explanation":"Payback = whole years before recovery + (unrecovered amount ÷ next year's cash flow).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-016","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Medicine Hat Machining is considering a $192,000 laser cutter that will return $71,500, $83,200 and $98,700 in years 1 to 3. The firm accepts projects that pay back within three years. Find the payback period in years (the project is then accepted or rejected against the cutoff).","answer":2.38,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2+(192000-(71500+83200))/98700","steps":[{"label":"Cumulative cash flow after year 2","value":154700.0,"check":"71500+83200","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Payback period","value":2.38,"check":"2+(192000-(71500+83200))/98700","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["After year 2 the firm has recovered 71,500 + 83,200 = $154,700.","Still to recover = 192,000 - 154,700 = $37,300, which comes in year 3.","Payback = 2 + 37,300/98,700 = 2 + 0.378 = 2.38 years.","2.38 years < 3-year cutoff, so the rule says accept."],"keystrokes":["71,500 + 83,200 = 154,700","192,000 − 154,700 = 37,300","37,300 ÷ 98,700 = 0.378","2 + 0.378 = 2.38 years"],"errors":[{"id":"payback-after-recovery","label":"Counted the whole recovery year","value":3.0,"check":"3","explain":"Year 3 is only partly needed; use the fraction 37,300/98,700."},{"id":"payback-wrong-flow","label":"Divided by the previous year's flow","value":2.45,"check":"2+37300/83200","explain":"Use the cash flow of the recovery year (year 3), not year 2."},{"id":"payback-fraction-only","label":"Gave only the fraction of the year","value":0.38,"check":"37300/98700","explain":"Payback includes the whole years already elapsed: 2 + 0.378."}],"explanation":"Whole years until the cumulative flow is just short of the cost, plus remaining ÷ the next year's flow. Compare with the cutoff.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s5-6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-017","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A $1,370,000 water-treatment upgrade at Red Deer Utilities is expected to save $285,000 in cash every year for 8 years. What is the payback period in years?","answer":4.81,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1370000/285000","steps":[{"label":"Payback = cost / annual flow","value":4.81,"check":"1370000/285000","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["The flows are an annuity, so payback = initial cost / annual cash flow.","Payback = 1,370,000 / 285,000 = 4.81 years (< 8-year life, so the project does pay back)."],"keystrokes":["1,370,000 ÷ 285,000 = 4.807 -> 4.81 years"],"errors":[{"id":"payback-after-recovery","label":"Rounded up to the whole year","value":5.0,"check":"5","explain":"Payback includes the fraction of the recovery year: 4.81, not 5."},{"id":"payback-inverted","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":0.21,"check":"285000/1370000","explain":"Payback is cost divided by the annual flow (years), not the flow divided by the cost (that is a yield)."}],"explanation":"For level annual flows, payback = cost ÷ annual flow. Check that the result is less than the project's life.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-018","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yellowhead Plastics pays $64,000 for a moulding press that returns $14,500, $18,200, $21,300, $24,900 and $26,000 in years 1 to 5. What is the payback period?","options":["4.00 years","3.40 years","3.47 years","0.40 years"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cumulative flows: 14,500; 32,700; 54,000; 78,900. After year 3, 64,000 - 54,000 = 10,000 remains, recovered in year 4: 10,000/24,900 = 0.40, so payback = 3.40 years. 4.00 counts the entire recovery year; 3.47 divides by the previous year's flow ($21,300); 0.40 forgets the three whole years.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-019","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A $48,000 irrigation system at Taber Growers will return $6,000 at the end of each year for 7 years and then be worthless. What is its payback period?","options":["The project never pays back","8.00 years","7.00 years","0.88 years"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Seven flows of $6,000 total $42,000, which is less than the $48,000 cost, so the investment is never recovered. 48,000/6,000 = 8.00 is the annuity shortcut applied beyond the 7-year life, where it does not make sense; 7.00 is simply the life; 0.88 divides the total inflows by the cost (an inverted ratio).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-020","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Wetaskiwin Dairy sets a 3-year payback cutoff. Project A costs $60,000 and returns $23,000, $28,000 and $21,000 in years 1 to 3. Project B costs $66,000 and returns $15,000, $18,000, $26,000 and $30,000 in years 1 to 4. Which statement is correct?","options":["A pays back in 3.00 years and is accepted; B pays back in 4.00 years and is rejected","A pays back in 2.43 years and is accepted; B pays back in 3.23 years and is rejected","A pays back in 2.32 years and is accepted; B pays back in 3.27 years and is rejected","A pays back in 0.43 years and is accepted; B pays back in 0.23 years and is accepted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A: after 2 years 51,000 is recovered, 9,000 remains, and year 3 brings 21,000: 2 + 9,000/21,000 = 2.43 < 3, accept. B: after 3 years 59,000 is recovered, 7,000 remains, and year 4 brings 30,000: 3 + 7,000/30,000 = 3.23 > 3, reject. The other options count whole recovery years, divide by the previous year's flow, or report only the fraction.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-3"},{"id":"fin201-q09-021","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The payback period of a project is:","options":["the length of time until the sum of its undiscounted cash flows equals the initial investment","the length of time until the sum of its discounted cash flows equals the initial investment","the number of years the project is expected to last","the discount rate that makes the NPV of the project zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Payback adds raw cash flows year by year until the initial outlay is recovered (part years allowed). Adding discounted flows would be the discounted payback.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s10"},{"id":"fin201-q09-022","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Mackenzie Fitness Supply funds a new machine only if the cash it generates will repay the purchase price within three years, and it does not look at anything that happens after year 3. Which decision rule is it applying?","options":["Discounted payback","Accounting rate of return","Payback period","Net present value"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Counting only whether undiscounted cash flows repay the cost within a year cutoff is the payback rule. Discounted payback would first convert each flow to its present value at R.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s10-12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-023","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"What is the key difference between the payback period and the discounted payback period?","options":["Payback uses accounting profit; discounted payback uses cash flow","Payback gives an absolute accept/reject decision; discounted payback is only relative","Payback counts the cash flows after the payback date; discounted payback ignores them","Payback adds up raw cash flows; discounted payback adds up cash flows after discounting each at R"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both rules are relative comparisons against a cutoff and both ignore flows after payback. Only the discounted version brings in the time value of money by using CF/(1+R)^t.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s12-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-024","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a bad point of the payback rule?","options":["the mathematics is difficult","it ignores the time value of money","it ignores income after the payback period","it gives only a relative comparison, not an absolute decision"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Payback's good point is easy maths and speed. Its bad points are no time value of money, no credit for cash flows after the payback date, and no absolute decision (the cutoff is arbitrary).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-025","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the payback rule bias a firm toward short-lived projects?","options":["it discounts distant cash flows at a high rate, so later inflows count for little","it ignores cash flows after the payback date, so long projects get no credit for later inflows","it uses profit after tax, which is highest in the early years of a project","it assumes the cash flows are reinvested at the IRR rather than at R"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Payback only asks how fast the investment is recovered. Any large inflows that arrive after the cutoff are invisible, so a quickly recovered but short project can beat a longer, more valuable one.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-026","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"At a 2-year payback cutoff, Cedar Valley Brewing accepts Short (cost $300,000; $120,000 then $200,000) and rejects Long (cost $300,000; $110,000 a year for 4 years). At R = 15%, NPV(Short) = -$44,423 and NPV(Long) = +$14,048. What does this show?","options":["Payback and NPV always agree when the cutoff is set at two years or less","Short is better because its NPV is closer to zero than the NPV of Long","The cutoff was too long; a one-year cutoff would remove the conflict","Payback can accept a value-losing project and reject a value-adding one"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A payback below the cutoff is not a guarantee of positive NPV. Short pays back in 1.9 years but its flows are worth less than the cost at 15%. Long's payback of 2.73 years fails the cutoff even though its fourth year of income creates value. Changing the cutoff cannot fix a rule that ignores TVM.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-027","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"When a project's cost is recovered partway through year k + 1, the fractional part of the payback period is:","options":["the amount still unrecovered at the end of year k divided by the cash flow in year k","the cash flow in year k + 1 divided by the initial investment","the amount still unrecovered at the end of year k divided by the cash flow in year k + 1","the amount still unrecovered divided by the total of all remaining cash flows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Payback = k + (remaining at end of year k)/CF(k+1). Dividing by year k's flow (the previous year) is a typical slip.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-028","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project whose payback period is shorter than its life must have a positive NPV at any positive discount rate.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A payback shorter than the life only guarantees a positive NPV at a zero discount rate. At positive rates the NPV may be positive, zero or negative depending on whether R is below, equal to, or above the IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-029","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"If the cumulative cash flows of a project never reach its initial investment, the project has no payback period (it never pays back).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Payback is the time to recover the outlay. If the inflows over the whole life total less than the outlay, recovery never happens, and no fraction of a year can be computed.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-030","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence correctly describes how to compute a payback period for uneven cash flows?","options":["Discount each cash flow; cumulate the discounted flows; find the recovery year; divide by the number of years","Average the annual cash flows; divide the investment by this average; round up to the next whole year","Find the recovery year; add its cash flow to the investment; divide the total by the project life","Cumulate the cash flows; find the recovery year; divide the unrecovered amount by that year's flow; add the whole years before it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Payback uses undiscounted flows: cumulate them, locate the year of recovery, take (remaining ÷ that year's flow) as the fraction, and add the completed years before it.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-031","concept":"fin201-c09-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two machines each cost $90,000. Machine X returns $50,000 in year 1 and $50,000 in year 2. Machine Y returns $20,000 a year for 15 years. The firm accepts anything with payback of 3 years or less. What does payback say?","options":["Accept X (1.80 years), reject Y (4.50 years), although Y's total inflow is larger","Accept Y, because its cash flows last longer and total more than X's","Accept both, because both recover their cost eventually within their lives","Reject both, because neither pays back within a single year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"X: 50,000 after year 1, 40,000 remains, 40,000/50,000 = 0.8, so 1.80 years. Y: 90,000/20,000 = 4.50 years. Payback ignores Y's 15 years of inflows, which is the bias toward short projects.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-032","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stony Plain Packaging pays $64,000 for a labelling line that will return $22,000, $26,500, $24,800, $23,000 and $21,500 in years 1 to 5. The required return is 13% per annum. What is the discounted payback period in years?","answer":3.47,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"3+(64000-(22000/1.13+26500/1.13**2+24800/1.13**3))/(23000/1.13**4)","steps":[{"label":"PV of years 1-3 flows (cumulative)","value":57410.06,"check":"22000/1.13+26500/1.13**2+24800/1.13**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Still to recover after year 3 (in PV terms)","value":6589.94,"check":"64000-(22000/1.13+26500/1.13**2+24800/1.13**3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PV of the year-4 cash flow","value":14106.33,"check":"23000/1.13**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Discounted payback period","value":3.47,"check":"3+(64000-(22000/1.13+26500/1.13**2+24800/1.13**3))/(23000/1.13**4)","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PV of year 1 = 22,000/1.13 = 19,469.03; year 2 = 26,500/1.13^2 = 20,753.39; year 3 = 24,800/1.13^3 = 17,187.64.","Cumulative PV after year 3 = 57,410.06, so 64,000 - 57,410.06 = 6,589.94 is still to recover.","PV of year 4's flow = 23,000/1.13^4 = 14,106.33.","Discounted payback = 3 + 6,589.94/14,106.33 = 3 + 0.467 = 3.47 years."],"keystrokes":["Discount each flow: 22,000 ÷ 1.13 = 19,469.03 ; 26,500 ÷ 1.13^2 = 20,753.39 ; 24,800 ÷ 1.13^3 = 17,187.64 (compute 1.13 y^x 3 = 1.442897 first, then divide)","Cumulate: 57,410.06 ; 64,000 − 57,410.06 = 6,589.94","23,000 ÷ 1.13^4 = 14,106.33 ; 6,589.94 ÷ 14,106.33 = 0.467","3 + 0.467 = 3.47 years"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-discount","label":"Used undiscounted cash flows (ordinary payback)","value":2.63,"check":"2+(64000-(22000+26500))/24800","explain":"That is the ordinary payback of 2.63 years. Discounted payback first converts each flow to its present value."},{"id":"payback-after-recovery","label":"Counted the whole recovery year","value":4.0,"check":"4","explain":"Add only the fraction of year 4 that is needed."},{"id":"payback-undiscounted-flow","label":"Divided the discounted remainder by the undiscounted year-4 flow","value":3.29,"check":"3+(64000-(22000/1.13+26500/1.13**2+24800/1.13**3))/23000","explain":"Both parts must be in present-value terms: use the PV of the year-4 flow, $14,106.33."}],"explanation":"Discount every flow at R, accumulate the PVs, and find where they reach the cost; the fraction is (PV still to recover) ÷ (PV of the next year's flow).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-033","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lacombe Brewing invests $41,000 in a bottling upgrade with cash flows of $12,800, $14,900, $17,400 and $19,100 in years 1 to 4. Its required return is 11% per annum and it accepts projects with a discounted payback of 3.5 years or less. Find the discounted payback in years.","answer":3.37,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"3+(41000-(12800/1.11+14900/1.11**2+17400/1.11**3))/(19100/1.11**4)","steps":[{"label":"Cumulative PV after year 3","value":36347.44,"check":"12800/1.11+14900/1.11**2+17400/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Discounted payback period","value":3.37,"check":"3+(41000-(12800/1.11+14900/1.11**2+17400/1.11**3))/(19100/1.11**4)","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PVs: 12,800/1.11 = 11,531.53; 14,900/1.11^2 = 12,093.17; 17,400/1.11^3 = 12,722.73; cumulative = 36,347.44.","Still to recover = 41,000 - 36,347.44 = 4,652.56. PV of year 4's flow = 19,100/1.11^4 = 12,581.76.","Discounted payback = 3 + 4,652.56/12,581.76 = 3.37 years.","3.37 < 3.5 years, so the rule says accept (the ordinary payback is 2.76 years)."],"keystrokes":["12,800 ÷ 1.11 = 11,531.53 ; 14,900 ÷ 1.11^2 = 12,093.17 ; 17,400 ÷ 1.11^3 = 12,722.73","Sum = 36,347.44 ; 41,000 − 36,347.44 = 4,652.56","19,100 ÷ 1.11^4 = 12,581.76 ; 4,652.56 ÷ 12,581.76 = 0.370","3 + 0.370 = 3.37 years"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-discount","label":"Used undiscounted flows","value":2.76,"check":"2+(41000-(12800+14900))/17400","explain":"That gives the ordinary payback, 2.76 years, which ignores the time value of money."},{"id":"payback-undiscounted-flow","label":"Divided the PV remainder by the undiscounted year-4 flow","value":3.24,"check":"3+(41000-(12800/1.11+14900/1.11**2+17400/1.11**3))/19100","explain":"The denominator must be the PV of the year-4 flow, $12,581.76, not $19,100."}],"explanation":"Discounted payback is always at least the ordinary payback for conventional flows and R > 0. Here it is 3.37 vs 2.76 years.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-4"},{"id":"fin201-q09-034","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $9,600 phone-system project at Spruce Grove Insurance returns $3,400, $3,900, $4,300 and $4,000 in years 1 to 4. The required return is 12% per annum. By how many years does the discounted payback exceed the ordinary payback?","answer":0.62,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(3+(9600-(3400/1.12+3900/1.12**2+4300/1.12**3))/(4000/1.12**4))-(2+(9600-(3400+3900))/4300)","steps":[{"label":"Ordinary payback","value":2.53,"check":"(2+(9600-(3400+3900))/4300)","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Discounted payback","value":3.16,"check":"(3+(9600-(3400/1.12+3900/1.12**2+4300/1.12**3))/(4000/1.12**4))","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Difference","value":0.62,"check":"(3+(9600-(3400/1.12+3900/1.12**2+4300/1.12**3))/(4000/1.12**4))-(2+(9600-(3400+3900))/4300)","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Ordinary payback: after two years 3,400 + 3,900 = 7,300; 2,300 remains; 2,300/4,300 = 0.535, so 2.53 years.","Discounted PVs: year 1 = 3,035.71; year 2 = 3,109.06; year 3 = 3,060.66; year 4 = 2,542.07.","Cumulative PV after year 3 = 9,205.43, which is still short of 9,600 by 394.57. Recovery occurs in year 4: 394.57/2,542.07 = 0.155.","Discounted payback = 3.16 years. Difference = 3.155 - 2.535 = 0.62 years."],"keystrokes":["Ordinary: 9,600 − 7,300 = 2,300 ; 2,300 ÷ 4,300 = 0.535 -> 2.53 years","Discounted: 3,035.71 + 3,109.06 + 3,060.66 = 9,205.43 ; 9,600 − 9,205.43 = 394.57","394.57 ÷ 2,542.07 = 0.155 -> 3.16 years","3.155 − 2.535 = 0.62 years"],"errors":[{"id":"payback-after-recovery","label":"Took the discounted recovery year as a whole year (4.00)","value":1.47,"check":"4-(2+(9600-(3400+3900))/4300)","explain":"Discounted payback of 3.16 years includes only the fraction of year 4 that is needed."},{"id":"wrong-quantity","label":"Gave the discounted payback itself, not the difference","value":3.16,"check":"(3+(9600-(3400/1.12+3900/1.12**2+4300/1.12**3))/(4000/1.12**4))","explain":"The question asks for the excess of the discounted payback over the ordinary payback."}],"explanation":"Discounting shrinks later flows, so the cost is recovered later: discounted payback > ordinary payback for conventional flows and R > 0.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 4"},{"id":"fin201-q09-035","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A $30,000 CNC upgrade returns $11,000, $12,500, $14,000 and $9,000 in years 1 to 4. The required return is 8% per annum. What is the discounted payback period?","options":["2.46 years","2.82 years","3.00 years","2.65 years"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PVs: 10,185.19; 10,716.74; 11,113.65. After 2 years 20,901.93 is recovered and 9,098.07 remains: 2 + 9,098.07/11,113.65 = 2.82 years. 2.46 uses undiscounted flows (ordinary payback); 3.00 counts the whole recovery year; 2.65 divides the PV remainder by the undiscounted year-3 flow ($14,000).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-4"},{"id":"fin201-q09-036","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project costs $9,000 and returns $2,000 at the end of each of the next 6 years. The required return is 18% per annum. What is the discounted payback period?","options":["4.50 years","6.00 years","It never pays back within the 6 years","10.03 years"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The PV of six $2,000 flows at 18% is 2,000 x 3.4976 = $6,995, which is less than $9,000, so the cost is never recovered in PV terms. 4.50 years is the ordinary payback; 6.00 is just the life; 10.03 solves the annuity equation as if the flows continued beyond year 6.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-037","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The discounted payback period is the length of time until:","options":["the sum of the undiscounted cash flows equals the initial investment","the NPV of the project first becomes zero at the IRR","the sum of the present values of the cash flows equals the initial investment","accounting profit equals the book value of the asset"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Discounted payback uses CF/(1+R)^t for each year, so it measures the time to recover the investment in economic (present-value) terms.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-038","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Athabasca Tech asks how long it takes for the PV of a server upgrade's cash flows, discounted at the firm's 10% required return, to repay the purchase price, and accepts projects that do this within four years. Which rule is this?","options":["Discounted payback","Payback period","Profitability index","Accounting rate of return"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Discounting each flow at R and then looking for the recovery year is the discounted payback. Without discounting it would be the ordinary payback.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-039","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a conventional project and a positive discount rate, how does the discounted payback compare with the ordinary payback?","options":["It is shorter, because discounting adds interest to each cash flow","It is longer, because discounting makes each future cash flow worth less","It is the same, because both use the same cash flows","It can be longer or shorter depending on the project life"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each discounted flow is smaller than the raw flow, so more time is needed to recover the same cost. At R = 0 the two are equal. Discounted payback is therefore never shorter for conventional flows.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 4c"},{"id":"fin201-q09-040","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT true of the discounted payback rule?","options":["it takes into account the time value of money","it gives only a relative comparison, not an absolute decision","it ignores income beyond the discounted payback period","it takes into account the cash flows that occur after the discounted payback period"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Discounted payback fixes the time-value criticism but still ignores every cash flow after the recovery date and still needs a cutoff. Only the claim that it counts the cash flows after the discounted payback date is false.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-041","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is the following statement true: \"If a project's discounted payback is shorter than its life, its NPV must be positive\"?","options":["discounted payback is calculated at the IRR rather than at the required return R","the ordinary payback is always shorter than the discounted payback, so NPV is positive","once the PV of the inflows has covered the cost inside the life, later discounted inflows only add to it","the discounted payback rule ignores the cost of the project and counts only the inflows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Discounted payback uses the same R as the NPV. If the discounted flows have repaid the cost within the life, the sum of all discounted flows exceeds the cost, which is NPV > 0. (Ordinary payback gives no such guarantee.)","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 1-2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-042","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project with conventional cash flows has a 5-year life, a discounted payback of 4.2 years at the firm's 14% required return and an ordinary payback of 3.1 years. What can be concluded?","options":["The NPV is negative because the discounted payback is longer than the ordinary payback","The NPV is positive at 14% and the IRR exceeds 14%","Nothing, because payback rules give no information about NPV","The IRR is less than 14% because the discounted payback exceeds 4 years"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A discounted payback shorter than the life (4.2 < 5) means the PV of the inflows exceeds the cost, so NPV > 0 at 14%, and a positive NPV means the IRR is higher than 14%. The longer discounted payback is expected for any positive R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-043","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"To find a discounted payback you accumulate which quantity year by year until it equals the initial investment?","options":["CF_t","CF_t × (1 + R)^t","CF_t/(1 + IRR)^t","CF_t/(1 + R)^t"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each flow is brought back to time 0 at the required return R: CF_t/(1+R)^t. Using raw CF_t is the ordinary payback; compounding forward or using the IRR would not give a present value at R.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-044","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"If the discount rate is 0%, a project's discounted payback period equals its ordinary payback period.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At R = 0 each discount factor is 1, so the PV of every flow equals the flow itself and the two payback periods coincide.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-045","concept":"fin201-c09-discounted-payback","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The discounted payback rule gives an absolute accept/reject decision without a cutoff period.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It still compares the discounted payback with a pre-set limit, so it is only a relative comparison (and ignores cash flows after the recovery date).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-046","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"You are considering investing in a project which is expected to provide $215,000 net income per year for each of the next eight years, in return for an up-front investment of $1.2 million. You expect the residual value will be $0.96 million at the end. Your discount rate is 12% per annum. There is no tax. Determine each of the following and state the decision that arises from each figure: (a) accounting rate of return; (b) NPV. For part (b) treat the $215,000 per year as the annual after-tax cash flow and the $0.96 million residual as cash received at the end of year 8. Enter part (b), the NPV, in $.","answer":255770.45,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1.0},"check":"-1200000+215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)+960000/(1+0.12)**8","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (12%, 8 years)","value":4.9676,"check":"((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"PV of the eight $215,000 flows","value":1068042.55,"check":"215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1.0}},{"label":"PV of the $960,000 residual","value":387727.9,"check":"960000/(1+0.12)**8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1.0}},{"label":"NPV = CF0 + PV","value":255770.45,"check":"-1200000+215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)+960000/(1+0.12)**8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1.0}}],"solution":["Annuity factor = [1 - 1.12^-8]/0.12 = 4.96764.","PV of the eight annual flows = 215,000 x 4.967640 = $1,068,042.55.","PV of the residual = 960,000/1.12^8 = 960,000/2.475963 = $387,727.90.","NPV = -1,200,000 + 1,068,042.55 + 387,727.90 = +$255,770.45.","Decision: NPV > 0, so accept. The project is expected to add about $255,770 to shareholder wealth."],"keystrokes":["CF: CF0 = -1200000, C01 = 215000, F01 = 7, C02 = 1175000 (215000 + 960000), F02 = 1","NPV: I = 12, ↓ CPT -> 255,770.45","Or: N = 8, I/Y = 12, PMT = 215000, FV = 960000, CPT PV = 1,455,770.45; minus 1,200,000 = 255,770.45"],"errors":[{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Ignored the residual value","value":-131957.45,"check":"-1200000+215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)","explain":"The $960,000 residual is a cash inflow at the end of year 8 and must be discounted and added."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Added the residual without discounting","value":828042.55,"check":"-1200000+215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)+960000","explain":"The residual arrives in year 8, so it is worth 960,000/1.12^8 today."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Discounted the up-front investment one year","value":384341.88,"check":"-1200000/(1+0.12)+215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)+960000/(1+0.12)**8","explain":"The investment occurs today (t = 0) and is not discounted."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 7-year annuity factor","value":168935.55,"check":"-1200000+215000*((1-(1+0.12)**-7)/0.12)+960000/(1+0.12)**8","explain":"Eight annual flows need the 8-year annuity factor."},{"id":"depreciation-added-back","label":"Added back $30,000 straight-line depreciation to the flows","value":404799.64,"check":"-1200000+245000*((1-(1+0.12)**-8)/0.12)+960000/(1+0.12)**8","explain":"The question states the $215,000 is the annual cash flow, so no depreciation is added."}],"explanation":"NPV = CF0 + PV of the inflows (annuity plus the discounted residual). NPV > 0, so the project is accepted.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q10b"},{"id":"fin201-q09-047","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Beaumont Freight can buy a refrigerated trailer for $148,000. It is expected to generate cash flows of $52,400, $61,900 and $78,300 in years 1 to 3. The required return is 11% per annum. What is the NPV, and should the firm accept?","answer":6698.92,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-148000+52400/(1+0.11)+61900/(1+0.11)**2+78300/(1+0.11)**3","steps":[{"label":"PV of year 1 flow","value":47207.21,"check":"52400/(1+0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of year 2 flow","value":50239.43,"check":"61900/(1+0.11)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of year 3 flow","value":57252.29,"check":"78300/(1+0.11)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV = CF0 + PV","value":6698.92,"check":"-148000+52400/(1+0.11)+61900/(1+0.11)**2+78300/(1+0.11)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PV1 = 52,400/1.11 = 47,207.21; PV2 = 61,900/1.11^2 = 50,239.43; PV3 = 78,300/1.11^3 = 57,252.29.","PV of the inflows = 154,698.92.","NPV = CF0 + PV = -148,000 + 154,698.92 = +$6,698.92. NPV > 0, so accept."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -148000, C01 = 52400, F01 = 1, C02 = 61900, F02 = 1, C03 = 78300, F03 = 1, then NPV: I = 11, ↓ CPT -> 6,698.92"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Entered the outlay as a positive number","value":302698.92,"check":"148000+52400/(1+0.11)+61900/(1+0.11)**2+78300/(1+0.11)**3","explain":"CF0 is an outflow, so it carries a minus sign: NPV = CF0 + PV with CF0 negative."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Treated the outlay as year 1 (spreadsheet NPV() on the whole list)","value":6035.06,"check":"(-148000+52400/(1+0.11)+61900/(1+0.11)**2+78300/(1+0.11)**3)/(1+0.11)","explain":"The initial outlay is at t = 0; every later flow is at its own year number. Shifting everything one period divides the NPV by 1.11."},{"id":"omitted-cf0","label":"Reported only the PV of the inflows","value":154698.92,"check":"52400/(1+0.11)+61900/(1+0.11)**2+78300/(1+0.11)**3","explain":"The PV of the inflows is not the NPV until you add the (negative) initial outlay."}],"explanation":"NPV = CF0 + sum of CF_t/(1+R)^t. A positive NPV means the project adds value at the 11% required return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-048","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cochrane Ridge Outfitters is evaluating a $420,000 store renovation with cash flows of $120,000, $135,000, $150,000 and $165,000 in years 1 to 4. Find the NPV at a required return of 9% per annum and at 16% per annum. Enter the NPV at 16%.","answer":-28998.05,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-420000+120000/(1+0.16)+135000/(1+0.16)**2+150000/(1+0.16)**3+165000/(1+0.16)**4","steps":[{"label":"NPV at 9%","value":36436.22,"check":"-420000+120000/(1+0.09)+135000/(1+0.09)**2+150000/(1+0.09)**3+165000/(1+0.09)**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"NPV at 16%","value":-28998.05,"check":"-420000+120000/(1+0.16)+135000/(1+0.16)**2+150000/(1+0.16)**3+165000/(1+0.16)**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["At 9%: NPV = -420,000 + 120,000/1.09 + 135,000/1.09^2 + 150,000/1.09^3 + 165,000/1.09^4 = +$36,436.22 -> accept.","At 16%: NPV = -420,000 + 120,000/1.16 + 135,000/1.16^2 + 150,000/1.16^3 + 165,000/1.16^4 = -$28,998.05 -> reject.","Same cash flows, different required returns: the decision flips because NPV falls as R rises."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -420000, C01 = 120000, F01 = 1, C02 = 135000, F02 = 1, C03 = 150000, F03 = 1, C04 = 165000, F04 = 1, then NPV: I = 9, CPT -> 36,436.22 ; I = 16, CPT -> -28,998.05"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Used +420,000 for the outlay","value":811001.95,"check":"420000+120000/(1+0.16)+135000/(1+0.16)**2+150000/(1+0.16)**3+165000/(1+0.16)**4","explain":"The outlay is a negative cash flow at t = 0."},{"id":"wrong-rate","label":"Used the 9% answer","value":36436.22,"check":"-420000+120000/(1+0.09)+135000/(1+0.09)**2+150000/(1+0.09)**3+165000/(1+0.09)**4","explain":"The question asks for the NPV at 16%; at 9% the NPV is positive."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Treated the outlay as year 1","value":-24998.32,"check":"(-420000+120000/(1+0.16)+135000/(1+0.16)**2+150000/(1+0.16)**3+165000/(1+0.16)**4)/(1+0.16)","explain":"Put the outlay at t = 0, with the first inflow at t = 1."}],"explanation":"Higher required returns shrink the PV of inflows, so NPV falls and a project can change from accept to reject without any change in its cash flows.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-049","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Camrose Wind Co. can build a turbine for $2,400,000. It is expected to generate $150,000 of cash flow next year, growing at 3% per annum forever. The required return is 9.5% per annum. What is the project's NPV?","answer":-92307.69,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-2400000+150000/(0.095-0.03)","steps":[{"label":"PV of the growing perpetuity D1/(R - g)","value":2307692.31,"check":"150000/(0.095-0.03)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV","value":-92307.69,"check":"-2400000+150000/(0.095-0.03)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Inflows are a growing perpetuity: PV = CF1/(R - g) = 150,000/(0.095 - 0.03) = $2,307,692.31.","NPV = -2,400,000 + 2,307,692.31 = -$92,307.69.","NPV < 0, so reject."],"keystrokes":["150,000 ÷ (0.095 − 0.03) = 2,307,692.31","2,307,692.31 − 2,400,000 = −92,307.69"],"errors":[{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R in the denominator","value":-1200000.0,"check":"-2400000+150000/(0.095+0.03)","explain":"The growing-perpetuity denominator is R - g."},{"id":"omitted-cf0","label":"Reported the PV of the inflows only","value":2307692.31,"check":"150000/(0.095-0.03)","explain":"Subtract the $2,400,000 outlay: NPV = CF0 + PV."},{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Grew the first flow once more","value":-23076.92,"check":"-2400000+150000*1.03/(0.095-0.03)","explain":"The $150,000 is next year's flow (CF1), so it is not grown again."}],"explanation":"NPV = CF0 + CF1/(R - g) for a growing perpetuity; the same R - g logic as Gordon growth.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-21"},{"id":"fin201-q09-050","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow Valley Logistics is considering a project with cash flows of -$260,000 now, then $90,000, $110,000 and $120,000 at the end of years 1 to 3. The required return is 12% per annum. What is the NPV?","options":["-$6,537.90","$513,462.10","$253,462.10","-$5,837.41"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV = -260,000 + 90,000/1.12 + 110,000/1.12^2 + 120,000/1.12^3 = -$6,537.90, so reject. $513,462.10 adds the outlay as a positive number; $253,462.10 is just the PV of the inflows (CF0 left out); -$5,837.41 is the spreadsheet NPV() applied to the whole list (every flow shifted one period).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-051","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A $52,000 machine at Lloydminster Oilfield Services returns $14,000 at the end of each of the next 5 years. The required return is 10% per annum. What is the NPV?","options":["$53,071.01","$105,071.01","$8,973.65","$973.65","None of the above"],"answer":4,"explanation":"PV of the annuity = 14,000 × 3.790787 = $53,071.01, so NPV = 53,071.01 − 52,000 = $1,071.01, which is not listed: none of the above. $53,071.01 forgets to subtract the outlay; $105,071.01 adds the outlay; $8,973.65 uses a 6-year annuity factor; $973.65 treats the outlay as a year-1 flow (the whole NPV divided by 1.10).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-052","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The net present value of a project is:","options":["the discount rate at which the present value of the future cash flows equals the initial investment","the average annual accounting profit divided by the average investment","the present value of its future cash flows minus the initial investment (NPV = CF0 + PV, with CF0 negative)","the present value of the cash inflows divided by the present value of the cash outflows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV = CF0 + PV with CF0 negative, so NPV = PV - initial investment. The rate that makes PV equal the cost is the IRR, average profit over average investment is the ARR, and PV of inflows over PV of outflows is the PI.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s15-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-053","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Camrose Fuels' CFO tells the board: \"After discounting every expected cash flow at our 10% required return and subtracting the cost, this plant is expected to add $85,000 to the wealth of our shareholders.\" Which figure is she quoting?","options":["Net present value","Internal rate of return","Profitability index","Discounted payback period"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A dollar amount equal to the change in shareholder wealth after allowing for the time value of money is the NPV. IRR and PI are not dollar amounts, and discounted payback is measured in years.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s18"},{"id":"fin201-q09-054","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts NPV with IRR?","options":["NPV is a percentage that must be compared with the required return; IRR is a dollar change in wealth","NPV is a dollar change in wealth measured at the required return; IRR is a percentage that must be compared with the required return","NPV ignores the time value of money; IRR includes it","NPV and IRR are always interchangeable, even for mutually exclusive projects"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NPV answers \"how many dollars of wealth does this add?\" IRR answers \"what rate does it earn?\". Both use the time value of money, but they can disagree on rankings and with unconventional flows.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s15-26"},{"id":"fin201-q09-055","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"All of the following are good points of the NPV rule EXCEPT:","options":["it is the change in shareholder wealth","it uses the time value of money","it tells you how much value is created per dollar of capital tied up","it gives an absolute decision on each project as well as a relative comparison"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV does NOT consider the amount of capital tied up in the project; that is its bad point (the PI deals with it). Its good points are the link to shareholder wealth, the time value of money and an absolute decision.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s18"},{"id":"fin201-q09-056","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the NPV rule ensure the firm is doing the right thing for its shareholders?","options":["NPV is the change in shareholder wealth, so NPV > 0 projects maximize the owners' value","NPV always selects the project with the highest percentage return on capital","NPV ignores uncertain future cash flows, which makes it the safest rule to use","NPV is the only rule that can be applied without forecasting any cash flows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The financial manager's aim is to maximize shareholder wealth. Because NPV is exactly the expected change in that wealth (after the time value of money), it is the rule aligned with the goal.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s3, s18"},{"id":"fin201-q09-057","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has an NPV of +$12,000 at the firm's required return of 10%. What does that mean?","options":["The project earns an accounting profit of $12,000 in each year of its life","After earning the 10% required return, the project adds $12,000 to shareholder wealth in today's dollars","The project recovers its cost from $12,000 of discounted cash flow each year","The project's IRR is 12%, which is above the 10% required return"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Positive NPV = value created beyond the required return, expressed in present-value dollars. It is not an annual profit, and NPV alone does not give the IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s17-18"},{"id":"fin201-q09-058","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which expression gives the NPV of a project with CF0 = -100, CF1 = 60 and CF2 = 70 at discount rate R?","options":["-100/(1+R) + 60/(1+R)^2 + 70/(1+R)^3","100 + 60/(1+R) + 70/(1+R)^2","60/(1+R) + 70/(1+R)^2","-100 + 60/(1+R) + 70/(1+R)^2"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CF0 occurs today so it is not discounted; CF1 is discounted once and CF2 twice. Dividing the -100 by (1+R) as well shifts everything one period (the spreadsheet NPV() trap); leaving the -100 out omits CF0; writing +100 flips its sign.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s15; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-059","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":1,"stem":"If a project's NPV is exactly zero, accepting it earns the firm exactly its required return and leaves shareholder wealth unchanged.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV = 0 means the PV of inflows equals the cost; the project earns just the required return, so it neither adds nor destroys value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s17"},{"id":"fin201-q09-060","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project with a negative NPV should never be undertaken under any circumstances.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A negative NPV is bad and the project is normally rejected, but firms are sometimes forced to carry out costly projects (for example restoring the environment after mining) where the least-negative NPV choice is taken.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s17"},{"id":"fin201-q09-061","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"An analyst types =NPV(12%, B2:B5) in a spreadsheet, where B2 holds the initial outlay of -$165,000 and B3:B5 hold the three annual inflows. What does the cell return?","options":["The correct NPV, because the function includes the outlay at time 0 in B2","The PV of the three inflows only, because B2 is ignored as a label","A PV that treats the outlay as arriving in one year, so it equals the true NPV divided by 1.12","The IRR of the project, because the first value in the range is negative"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Excel's NPV() is really a PV(): it discounts the first value in the range by one period. The correct NPV is B2 + NPV(12%, B3:B5), with the outlay entered separately at t = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p2; TB 9.1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-062","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which sequence matches the professor's four-step analysis using NPV?","options":["Choose the discount rate; decide accept or reject; estimate the cash flows; equalize unequal lives","Estimate the after-tax cash flows; choose the discount rate and discount to today; equalize unequal lives; decide accept or reject","Estimate the cash flows; compute the IRR; rank by IRR; decide accept or reject","Equalize unequal lives; estimate the cash flows; decide accept or reject; choose the discount rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 1 is the expected after-tax cash flows CF0, CF1, ...; step 2 is the discount rate R (or K) and discounting; step 3 is to make unequal lives comparable (EAV, CCR); step 4 is the accept/reject decision.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-063","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the four-step NPV analysis, step 3 (\"if comparing projects with unequal lives\") asks you to:","options":["make the lives equivalent, for example with EAV or a constant chain of replacement (CCR)","discount every cash flow at the IRR rather than at the required return R","drop the cash flows that occur after the shorter project has ended","choose the project with the longer life, because it earns more"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPVs of projects with different lives are not comparable as they stand, so step 3 puts them on a common basis (EAV, CCR/NPV to infinity or the lowest common multiple of the lives).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19, s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-064","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive machines at Drumheller Mining cost the same but one lasts 3 years and the other 5. The analyst first converts each NPV into an equivalent annual value before choosing. Which step of the NPV sequence is this?","options":["Step 1: estimate the after-tax cash flows","Step 2: choose the discount rate","Step 4(a): accept if NPV is positive","Step 3: equalize unequal lives"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Converting NPVs to EAVs (or using a constant chain of replacement) is how step 3 puts projects with unequal lives on the same footing before the accept/reject decision in step 4.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19, s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-065","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the step-4 decision differ between independent and mutually exclusive projects?","options":["Independent: accept only the highest-NPV project; mutually exclusive: accept every project with a positive NPV","Independent: accept the highest IRR; mutually exclusive: accept the shortest payback","Independent: accept every project with NPV > 0; mutually exclusive: accept only the highest NPV (least negative if one must be taken)","There is no difference: both accept the single project with the highest NPV"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Independent projects do not affect each other, so each is judged on NPV > 0 alone. Mutually exclusive projects compete: only one can be taken, so choose the largest positive NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-066","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four steps in the professor's NPV sequence?","options":["rank the projects by their accounting rate of return","work out the expected after-tax cash flows","decide the discount rate and discount the cash flows to today","decide whether to accept or reject each project"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The NPV sequence uses cash flows, a discount rate, equalized lives where needed, and the NPV-based decision. Ranking by ARR belongs to a different (accounting) rule.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-067","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is step 1, estimating the after-tax cash flows, described as \"not necessarily easy\"?","options":["the cash flows must first be converted into accounting profit before discounting","NPV is only as good as its forecasts: flows must be incremental, after tax and dated correctly","the cash flows should be measured before tax so that taxes do not distort them","the cash flows must be averaged over the life of the project before use"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forecasting incremental after-tax cash flows (and choosing R) is the hard part of NPV; the arithmetic afterwards is mechanical. Accounting profit and averaging belong to the ARR, not NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19; SM 9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-068","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A mine must choose one of three mutually exclusive plans for restoring land that it is legally required to remediate. Their NPVs are -$840,000, -$615,000 and -$1,120,000. What does the NPV sequence say?","options":["Reject all three, because every one of the NPVs is negative","Take the plan with NPV = -$1,120,000, because it spends the most money","Take all three plans, because they are independent of each other","Take the plan with NPV = -$615,000, the least negative, as one plan must be done"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Step 4(b): accept the highest NPV, or the least negative if you must take one. Forced projects (such as environmental repairs) are the case where a negative NPV is accepted.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s17, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-069","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Capilano Marine has three independent projects with NPVs of +$120,000, +$45,000 and -$30,000, and enough funds for all of them. Which should it accept?","options":["Only the +$120,000 project, because it has the highest NPV","All three, because the total NPV is positive","The +$120,000 and +$45,000 projects only","None, because they cannot all have positive NPVs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For independent projects the rule is NPV > 0 for each one separately. Accept +$120,000 and +$45,000 and reject -$30,000. (With limited funds this changes: capital rationing.)","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-070","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"For two mutually exclusive projects, NPV(P) = $15,154 and NPV(Q) = $14,523 at R = 10%, while IRR(P) = 18.9% and IRR(Q) = 23.2%. What does step 4(b) tell the firm to do?","options":["Accept Q, because it has the higher IRR and so the higher return","Accept P, because mutually exclusive projects are ranked by NPV","Accept both, because both have positive NPVs at 10%","Accept neither, because the two IRRs differ from each other"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Step 4(b) uses NPV for mutually exclusive projects. The higher IRR of Q reflects its smaller scale; P adds $631 more to shareholder wealth.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19, s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-071","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Mutually exclusive projects at Sherwood Park Autobody: P costs $90,000 and returns $36,000, $44,000 and $48,000 in years 1 to 3; Q costs $60,000 and returns $29,000, $31,000 and $30,000. Both have 3-year lives and R = 10% per annum. Enter the NPV of the project that should be accepted.","answer":15154.02,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-90000+36000/(1+0.1)+44000/(1+0.1)**2+48000/(1+0.1)**3","steps":[{"label":"NPV of P","value":15154.02,"check":"-90000+36000/(1+0.1)+44000/(1+0.1)**2+48000/(1+0.1)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Q","value":14522.92,"check":"-60000+29000/(1+0.1)+31000/(1+0.1)**2+30000/(1+0.1)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV(P) = -90,000 + 36,000/1.10 + 44,000/1.10^2 + 48,000/1.10^3 = +$15,154.02.","NPV(Q) = -60,000 + 29,000/1.10 + 31,000/1.10^2 + 30,000/1.10^3 = +$14,522.92.","Same lives, so no equalizing is needed (step 3 skipped). Step 4(b): both positive but only one can be taken, so accept the higher NPV: P, at $15,154.02."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -90000, C01 = 36000, F01 = 1, C02 = 44000, F02 = 1, C03 = 48000, F03 = 1, NPV: I = 10, CPT -> 15,154.02","CF0 = -60000, C01 = 29000, F01 = 1, C02 = 31000, F02 = 1, C03 = 30000, F03 = 1, NPV: I = 10, CPT -> 14,522.92"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-irr","label":"Reported the NPV of Q (higher IRR)","value":14522.92,"check":"-60000+29000/(1+0.1)+31000/(1+0.1)**2+30000/(1+0.1)**3","explain":"Q has the higher IRR but a smaller NPV; for mutually exclusive projects choose the higher NPV."},{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Entered P's outlay as a positive number","value":195154.02,"check":"90000+36000/(1+0.10)+44000/(1+0.10)**2+48000/(1+0.10)**3","explain":"CF0 is an outflow: NPV = CF0 + PV with CF0 negative."}],"explanation":"NPV(P) > NPV(Q) > 0 at R = 10%. When projects are mutually exclusive, the highest positive NPV wins.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19; SM 9-17"},{"id":"fin201-q09-072","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"For independent projects, the NPV rule says to accept every project whose NPV is greater than zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent projects do not affect each other's values, so each is accepted if NPV > 0 (subject only to any resource constraint, which brings in capital rationing).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-073","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"For mutually exclusive projects with equal lives, the NPV rule says to accept the project with the _____.","options":["highest IRR","shortest payback","highest ARR","highest positive NPV"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Step 4(b): choose the highest positive NPV (the least negative if all are negative and one must be taken). Highest IRR, shortest payback and highest ARR are other rules that can give conflicting answers.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-074","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-steps","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Using step 2 of the NPV sequence, a project costs $17,000 today and returns $9,000 at the end of year 1 and $12,000 at the end of year 2. The required return is 10% per annum. What is its NPV?","options":["$1,099.17","$35,099.17","$18,099.17","$999.25"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV = -17,000 + 9,000/1.10 + 12,000/1.10^2 = -17,000 + 8,181.82 + 9,917.36 = +$1,099.17. $35,099.17 adds the outlay instead of subtracting it; $18,099.17 is the PV of the inflows only; $999.25 shifts every flow one period (outlay treated as year 1).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-075","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Wildrose Farms pays $250,000 today for a grain-drying operation that will be sold for a single payment of $338,000 in exactly 4 years. What is the project's IRR in % per annum?","answer":7.83,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"((338000/250000)**(1/4)-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Growth factor FV/PV","value":1.352,"check":"338000/250000","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"IRR","value":7.83,"check":"((338000/250000)**(1/4)-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["With one inflow, NPV = 0 means 250,000 = 338,000/(1 + IRR)^4.","(1 + IRR)^4 = 338,000/250,000 = 1.352, so 1 + IRR = 1.352^(1/4) = 1.07831.","IRR = 7.83% per annum. (Check: 250,000 x 1.0783^4 = 338,000.)"],"keystrokes":["N = 4, PV = −250000, PMT = 0, FV = 338000, CPT I/Y = 7.83","Or: 1.352 y^x 0.25 = 1.0783 ; − 1 = 0.0783"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Forgot to subtract 1 after the root","value":107.83,"check":"(338000/250000)**(1/4)*100","explain":"The root gives 1 + IRR (the growth factor); subtract 1 to get the rate."},{"id":"geo-no-root","label":"Used the total gain without the root","value":35.2,"check":"(338000/250000-1)*100","explain":"35.2% is the four-year total return; the IRR is the compound annual rate, found with the 4th root."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided the total gain by 4","value":8.8,"check":"(338000/250000-1)*100/4","explain":"Dividing by N ignores compounding; use the Nth root."}],"explanation":"With one inflow, IRR = (inflow/outlay)^(1/N) - 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-076","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cold Lake Aviation will pay $55,000 for avionics that return $33,000 at the end of year 1 and $36,500 at the end of year 2. The required return is 11% per annum. Find the IRR in % per annum (solve the quadratic) and decide.","answer":16.81,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(2*36500/(-33000+sqrt(33000**2+4*55000*36500))-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"x = 1/(1+IRR)","value":0.8561,"check":"(-33000+sqrt(33000**2+4*55000*36500))/(2*36500)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"IRR","value":16.81,"check":"(2*36500/(-33000+sqrt(33000**2+4*55000*36500))-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["IRR sets NPV = 0: -55,000 + 33,000x + 36,500x^2 = 0 where x = 1/(1 + IRR).","x = [-33,000 + sqrt(33,000^2 + 4(55,000)(36,500))] / (2 x 36,500) = (-33,000 + 95,493.46)/73,000 = 0.85607.","IRR = 1/0.85607 - 1 = 0.16812 = 16.81% per annum.","Verification: NPV at 16.8122% = -55,000 + 33,000/1.168122 + 36,500/1.168122^2 = 28,250.47 + 26,749.53 - 55,000 = 0.00.","Decision: 16.81% > 11% required return, so accept (NPV at 11% = +$4,353.95, consistent)."],"keystrokes":["CF: CF0 = -55000, C01 = 33000, F01 = 1, C02 = 36500, F02 = 1","IRR, CPT -> 16.81"],"errors":[{"id":"irr-simple-average","label":"Averaged the net gain over the two years","value":13.18,"check":"((33000+36500)/55000-1)*100/2","explain":"Dividing the total net gain by the number of years ignores timing; the IRR discounts each flow."},{"id":"avg-flow-over-cost","label":"Average annual flow divided by the outlay","value":63.18,"check":"(33000+36500)/2/55000*100","explain":"That is a cash yield on the outlay, not the discount rate that sets NPV to zero."}],"explanation":"For a two-year project let x = 1/(1+IRR) and solve CF0 + CF1 x + CF2 x^2 = 0. The calculator IRR key does the same by iteration.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-23; S5 s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q09-077","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hay River Hotels pays $1,800,000 for a building that will produce net cash flow of $175,500 at the end of every year forever. The required return is 9% per annum. What is the project's IRR in % per annum?","answer":9.75,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"175500/1800000*100","steps":[{"label":"IRR = CF / cost","value":9.75,"check":"175500/1800000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["For a level perpetuity PV = CF/R. NPV = 0 means the cost equals CF/IRR.","IRR = CF/cost = 175,500/1,800,000 = 0.0975 = 9.75% per annum.","9.75% > 9%, so the rule says accept."],"keystrokes":["175,500 ÷ 1,800,000 = 0.0975 -> 9.75%"],"errors":[{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Inverted the ratio (a payback figure)","value":10.26,"check":"1800000/175500","explain":"1,800,000/175,500 = 10.26 is the payback in years, not a rate."},{"id":"added-r-to-yield","label":"Added the 9% required return to the yield","value":18.75,"check":"175500/1800000*100+9","explain":"The cash yield already is the IRR for a level perpetuity; do not add the required return."}],"explanation":"For a level perpetuity, IRR = annual cash flow / initial outlay. It must beat R for a conventional project.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-078","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kitscoty Storage pays $3,200,000 for a grain terminal expected to produce $252,000 of cash flow next year, growing at 2.5% per annum forever. What is the IRR in % per annum?","answer":10.38,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(252000/3200000+0.025)*100","steps":[{"label":"Cash yield CF1 / cost","value":7.875,"check":"252000/3200000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"IRR = CF1/cost + g","value":10.38,"check":"(252000/3200000+0.025)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["For a growing perpetuity PV = CF1/(R - g). NPV = 0 means cost = CF1/(IRR - g).","IRR = CF1/cost + g = 252,000/3,200,000 + 0.025 = 0.07875 + 0.025 = 0.10375.","IRR = 10.38% per annum (10.375%). Same structure as R = D1/P0 + g for a stock."],"keystrokes":["252,000 ÷ 3,200,000 = 0.07875 ; + 0.025 = 0.10375 -> 10.38%"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-growth","label":"Left out the growth rate g","value":7.88,"check":"252000/3200000*100","explain":"The IRR is the cash yield plus the growth rate: CF1/cost + g."},{"id":"g-as-r","label":"Subtracted g instead of adding it","value":5.38,"check":"(252000/3200000-0.025)*100","explain":"Solve cost = CF1/(IRR - g) for IRR: IRR = CF1/cost + g."}],"explanation":"Growing-perpetuity IRR = CF1/cost + g, just as a stock's required return is D1/P0 + g.","source":"generated","ref":"L04 s20; SM 9-21"},{"id":"fin201-q09-079","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project costs $38,200 now and returns $14,900, $18,300 and $20,700 at the end of years 1 to 3. The required return is 12% per annum. What is its IRR?","options":["18.26%","13.70%","47.03%","12.00%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The IRR is the rate that sets NPV to zero; the calculator CF worksheet gives 18.26% (NPV is about $0.15 at 18.26% and +$4,426.07 at 12%). 13.70% is the total net gain divided by the cost and by 3 years (no compounding); 47.03% is the average annual flow divided by the cost; 12.00% is the required return, not the project's return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-7"},{"id":"fin201-q09-080","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $96,000 project returns $31,000, $36,000, $41,000 and $27,000 at the end of years 1 to 4. The required return is 12% per annum. What is its IRR?","options":["10.16%","15.30%","35.16%","12.00%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting NPV to zero with the calculator worksheet gives 15.30% (NPV is about -$8 at 15.30% and +$6,719.53 at 12%, so IRR > R and the project is accepted). 10.16% averages the total net gain over four years; 35.16% divides the average flow by the cost; 12.00% is R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-10"},{"id":"fin201-q09-081","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The internal rate of return (IRR) of a project is:","options":["the required return the firm needs to earn on the project","the average annual accounting profit divided by the average investment","the discount rate that makes the NPV of the project equal to zero","the present value of the inflows divided by the present value of the outflows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"IRR is the project's own return: the rate at which the PV of the future flows equals the price, i.e. NPV = 0. It is the same idea as the yield to maturity on a bond.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-082","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Hay River Lumber tells its lender that a new kiln \"returns 14.2% on the money invested, the rate at which the present value of its cash flows exactly equals its cost.\" Which measure is that?","options":["Required return (R)","Accounting rate of return","Profitability index","IRR"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The discount rate that sets the present value of the future cash flows equal to the cost is the IRR, the project's analogue of a bond's yield to maturity.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-083","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How should you distinguish IRR from the required return R?","options":["IRR is what the project returns; R is the return the firm needs (its cost of capital)","IRR is the return the firm needs; R is what the project returns","IRR is an accounting measure; R is a cash-flow measure","They are the same number for any project that has a positive NPV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IRR comes from the project's cash flows alone; R comes from the capital market and the project's risk. The rule compares them: IRR > R is good for conventional flows.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s25"},{"id":"fin201-q09-084","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"All of the following are good points of the IRR rule EXCEPT:","options":["it uses the time value of money","it gives a single unambiguous rate for any pattern of cash flows","it shows the size of the return","it links with the NPV concept when the cash flows are conventional"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bad point of IRR is that it may have multiple answers (or none) when the cash flows are unconventional. Good points: time value of money, shows the size of the return, agrees with NPV for conventional flows.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s26"},{"id":"fin201-q09-085","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does IRR > R imply NPV > 0 for conventional cash flows?","options":["the IRR is always calculated using the required return R as its discount rate","IRR and NPV are both measured in the same units, so they must agree","NPV falls as R rises and is zero at the IRR, so at any R below the IRR the NPV is positive","a conventional project always has an IRR above 100%, so its NPV is always positive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For conventional flows the NPV profile slopes downward and crosses zero once, at the IRR. Any required return to the left of that crossing gives a positive NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s21, s25"},{"id":"fin201-q09-086","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project with conventional cash flows has an IRR of 17.18%. Firm A's required return is 12% and firm B's is 20%. What do the decision rules say?","options":["Both accept, because the IRR is the same for every firm","Firm A rejects and firm B accepts, because a higher required return is more demanding","Both reject, because 17.18% is not above 20% for firm A","Firm A accepts (NPV > 0); firm B rejects (NPV < 0)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The IRR is fixed by the cash flows; only R differs. Firm A has R < IRR, so NPV > 0; firm B has R > IRR, so NPV < 0. Reversing the comparison (accepting when IRR < R) swaps the decisions.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s25"},{"id":"fin201-q09-087","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which equation defines the IRR?","options":["0 = CF0 + CF1/(1+R) + CF2/(1+R)^2 + ... + CFN/(1+R)^N","IRR = (CF1 + CF2 + ... + CFN)/CF0","IRR = PV of inflows / PV of outflows - 1","0 = CF0 + CF1/(1+IRR) + CF2/(1+IRR)^2 + ... + CFN/(1+IRR)^N"],"answer":3,"explanation":"IRR is the rate that makes NPV zero, and it appears inside the discount factors. Using R in the factors gives the NPV formula, not the IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-088","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The IRR of a project is the same idea as the yield to maturity (YTM) of a bond: the discount rate that makes the present value of the future cash flows equal to the observed price.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a bond, YTM solves price = PV of coupons and face value; for a project IRR solves cost = PV of its inflows (NPV = 0).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-089","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"The rule \"accept if IRR > R\" is valid for any pattern of cash flows.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is only valid for conventional cash flows (CF0 negative, all later flows positive or zero). Unconventional flows can have several IRRs, none, or financing-type flows that reverse the rule.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s26"},{"id":"fin201-q09-090","concept":"fin201-c09-irr-rule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project with level cash flows that last forever has a payback period of 5 years. What is its IRR?","options":["5%","25%","20%","10%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For a perpetuity cost = CF/IRR, so payback = cost/CF = 1/IRR and IRR = 1/payback = 1/5 = 20%. (For long-lived projects with roughly constant flows, the shorter the payback, the higher the IRR.)","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 CTQ 9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-091","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Moraine Lake Lodge is evaluating a $61,500 renovation with cash flows of $22,400, $27,900 and $31,450 in years 1 to 3. Compute the NPV at discount rates of 0%, 10% and 20% to sketch the NPV profile. Enter the NPV at 20%.","answer":-5258.1,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-61500+22400/(1+0.2)+27900/(1+0.2)**2+31450/(1+0.2)**3","steps":[{"label":"NPV at 0% (vertical intercept)","value":20250.0,"check":"-61500+22400+27900+31450","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV at 10%","value":5550.34,"check":"-61500+22400/(1+0.1)+27900/(1+0.1)**2+31450/(1+0.1)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV at 20%","value":-5258.1,"check":"-61500+22400/(1+0.2)+27900/(1+0.2)**2+31450/(1+0.2)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["At 0% flows are simply added: -61,500 + 22,400 + 27,900 + 31,450 = +$20,250.00.","At 10%: -61,500 + 22,400/1.10 + 27,900/1.10^2 + 31,450/1.10^3 = +$5,550.34.","At 20%: -61,500 + 22,400/1.20 + 27,900/1.20^2 + 31,450/1.20^3 = -$5,258.10.","NPV falls as R rises and changes sign between 10% and 20%, so the IRR is between 10% and 20% (it is 14.77%)."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -61500, C01 = 22400, F01 = 1, C02 = 27900, F02 = 1, C03 = 31450, F03 = 1, then NPV: I = 20, CPT -> -5,258.10 (repeat with I = 10 and I = 0)"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Entered the outlay as a positive number","value":117741.9,"check":"61500+22400/(1+0.20)+27900/(1+0.20)**2+31450/(1+0.20)**3","explain":"CF0 is an outflow; with CF0 positive the NPV is nonsense."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Treated the outlay as year 1 (everything shifted one period)","value":-4381.75,"check":"(-61500+22400/(1+0.2)+27900/(1+0.2)**2+31450/(1+0.2)**3)/(1+0.20)","explain":"The outlay is at t = 0 and is not discounted; shifting every flow divides the NPV by 1.20."},{"id":"wrong-rate","label":"Used the 10% result","value":5550.34,"check":"-61500+22400/(1+0.1)+27900/(1+0.1)**2+31450/(1+0.1)**3","explain":"The question asks for the NPV at 20%; at 10% the NPV is still positive."}],"explanation":"NPV profile points: NPV(0%) = sum of the flows; NPV falls as R rises; the profile crosses zero at the IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-11; S5 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-092","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project costs $80,000 and returns $45,000 at the end of year 1 and $52,000 at the end of year 2. At what discount rate (% per annum) does its NPV profile cross the horizontal axis?","answer":13.51,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(2*52000/(-45000+sqrt(45000**2+4*80000*52000))-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"x = 1/(1+IRR)","value":0.881,"check":"(-45000+sqrt(45000**2+4*80000*52000))/(2*52000)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Discount rate where NPV = 0 (IRR)","value":13.51,"check":"(2*52000/(-45000+sqrt(45000**2+4*80000*52000))-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["The profile crosses the horizontal axis where NPV = 0, which is the IRR.","With x = 1/(1 + IRR): -80,000 + 45,000x + 52,000x^2 = 0, so x = [-45,000 + sqrt(45,000^2 + 4(80,000)(52,000))]/(2 x 52,000) = 0.88096.","IRR = 1/0.88096 - 1 = 13.51% per annum.","Check: at 10% NPV = +$3,884.30 and at 20% it is negative, so the crossing lies between them."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -80000, C01 = 45000, F01 = 1, C02 = 52000, F02 = 1, IRR, CPT -> 13.51"],"errors":[{"id":"irr-simple-average","label":"Averaged the net gain over two years","value":10.62,"check":"((45000+52000)/80000-1)*100/2","explain":"That ignores the timing of the flows; the crossing point is the rate that discounts the flows to exactly $80,000."},{"id":"avg-flow-over-cost","label":"Average annual flow over the outlay","value":60.62,"check":"(45000+52000)/2/80000*100","explain":"That is a cash yield, not the rate at which NPV = 0."}],"explanation":"The x-intercept of the NPV profile is the IRR. For a two-year project it can be found from the quadratic in x = 1/(1+IRR).","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s9; L05A s21"},{"id":"fin201-q09-093","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Peace River Mills is analyzing a $212,000 kiln with cash flows of $64,000, $71,500, $83,000 and $59,300 in years 1 to 4. What is the vertical intercept of its NPV profile (the NPV at a 0% discount rate)?","answer":65800.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-212000+64000+71500+83000+59300","steps":[{"label":"Sum of the inflows","value":277800.0,"check":"64000+71500+83000+59300","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV at R = 0%","value":65800.0,"check":"-212000+64000+71500+83000+59300","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["At a 0% discount rate (and only then) cash flows can be added across time.","Inflows = 64,000 + 71,500 + 83,000 + 59,300 = 277,800.","NPV(0%) = -212,000 + 277,800 = +$65,800.00."],"keystrokes":["-212,000 + 64,000 + 71,500 + 83,000 + 59,300 = 65,800"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-cf0","label":"Gave the sum of the inflows only","value":277800.0,"check":"64000+71500+83000+59300","explain":"The vertical intercept is NPV, so subtract the outlay."},{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Added the outlay","value":489800.0,"check":"212000+64000+71500+83000+59300","explain":"The outlay is an outflow and is negative."},{"id":"wrong-rate","label":"Discounted at 10%","value":8134.55,"check":"-212000+64000/(1+0.1)+71500/(1+0.1)**2+83000/(1+0.1)**3+59300/(1+0.1)**4","explain":"At R = 0% there is no discounting; the 10% value is a different point on the profile."}],"explanation":"The vertical intercept of an NPV profile is the sum of the undiscounted cash flows, outlay included.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-094","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Moraine Lake Lodge's renovation costs $61,500 and returns $22,400, $27,900 and $31,450 in years 1 to 3. What is its NPV at a 15% required return?","options":["$246.45","-$246.45","$5,550.34","$20,250.00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NPV(15%) = -61,500 + 22,400/1.15 + 27,900/1.15^2 + 31,450/1.15^3 = -$246.45, slightly negative because the IRR is 14.77%, just below 15%. $246.45 flips the sign; $5,550.34 is the NPV at 10%; $20,250.00 is the NPV at 0%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-095","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"For the same renovation (outlay $61,500; inflows $22,400, $27,900, $31,450), where does the NPV profile cross the vertical axis?","options":["+$20,250","+$81,750","-$61,500","+$143,250"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On the vertical axis R = 0%, so NPV is the sum of all flows: -61,500 + 81,750 = +$20,250. +$81,750 forgets to subtract the outlay; -$61,500 is where the profile heads as R approaches infinity; +$143,250 adds the outlay.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-18"},{"id":"fin201-q09-096","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An NPV profile is:","options":["a table of the project's cash flows by year","a graph of the project's cumulative undiscounted cash flows over time","a plot of the project's IRR against its payback period","a graph of a project's NPV plotted against the discount rate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The NPV profile shows how NPV changes as the discount rate varies. Its vertical intercept is the sum of the flows; where it crosses the horizontal axis is the IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s21; S5 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-097","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A graph for a conventional project shows NPV falling steadily from +$20,250 at R = 0% to -$13,445 at R = 30%, and cutting the horizontal axis at R = 14.77%. What does the crossing point represent?","options":["The payback period","The required return","The IRR","The profitability index"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV = 0 at the point where the profile cuts the axis, so the discount rate there is the IRR. The required return is where you read the NPV for your firm; it is not a feature of the curve.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s21; S5 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q09-098","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the NPV profile of a financing-type project (cash inflow first, outflows later) differ from that of a conventional investing project?","options":["It slopes downward more steeply","It slopes upward (NPV rises with R) rather than downward","It is flat, because NPV does not depend on R","It always crosses the horizontal axis twice"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With the inflow at t = 0 and outflows later, a higher discount rate shrinks the PV of the outflows, so NPV rises with R. Conventional investing projects have the inflows later, so NPV falls with R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-099","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the NPV profile of a conventional project is NOT correct?","options":["As the discount rate becomes very large, NPV approaches zero","It slopes downward as the discount rate rises","Its vertical intercept is the sum of the undiscounted cash flows","It crosses the horizontal axis once, at the IRR"],"answer":0,"explanation":"As R becomes very large the future flows are worth almost nothing, so NPV approaches CF0, the (negative) initial outlay, not zero.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-18"},{"id":"fin201-q09-100","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the NPV of a conventional project fall as the discount rate rises?","options":["the inflows come after the outlay, so a higher rate shrinks their PV while the outlay at t = 0 is unchanged","a higher rate raises the present value of the outlay at t = 0 by more than that of the inflows","a higher rate compounds the later inflows into larger amounts that must be repaid","a higher rate shortens the payback period, which lowers the project's value"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV = CF0 + sum of CF_t/(1+R)^t. CF0 is not discounted, but every later inflow is divided by a larger factor as R rises, so the NPV decreases.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-101","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A conventional project's NPV profile crosses the horizontal axis at 14.77%. Firm A's required return is 12% and firm B's is 18%. What follows?","options":["Both read a positive NPV, because the profile starts above zero","A reads a negative NPV and B a positive NPV, because the profile slopes upward","A reads a positive NPV and accepts; B reads a negative NPV and rejects","Neither can decide until the payback period is known"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The profile is above the axis to the left of the IRR (R < 14.77% gives NPV > 0) and below it to the right (R > 14.77% gives NPV < 0).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s21, s25"},{"id":"fin201-q09-102","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's NPV is +$12,307 at R = 5% and -$5,258 at R = 20%, with conventional flows. What does this tell you about its IRR?","options":["The IRR is exactly 12.5%, because it is the average of the two rates","The IRR is below 5%, because the NPV is positive at a 5% discount rate","The IRR is above 20%, because the NPV is large and positive at 5%","The IRR is between 5% and 20%, because the profile changes sign between them"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A continuous downward-sloping profile that is positive at 5% and negative at 20% must cross zero between them; the exact crossing (the IRR) needs the full calculation, not an average of the two rates.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-103","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a discount rate of 0%, the NPV of a project equals:","options":["the sum of its inflows only, with the initial outlay excluded","the sum of all its cash flows, including the negative initial outlay","its initial outlay CF0, because the inflows are worth nothing","its IRR, because the discount rate is zero at that point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With R = 0 every discount factor is 1, so NPV = CF0 + CF1 + ... + CFN. The initial outlay alone is the limit as R grows without bound.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-11"},{"id":"fin201-q09-104","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"For conventional cash flows, the NPV profile crosses the horizontal axis exactly once, at the project's IRR.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A conventional project (CF0 negative, later flows non-negative) has one sign change, so at most one IRR. The profile is downward sloping and crosses zero at that rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s21-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-105","concept":"fin201-c09-npv-profile","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"The NPV profile of every project slopes downward.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Projects with financing-type flows (inflow first, outflows later) have upward-sloping profiles, and unconventional flows can make the profile rise and then fall.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-106","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A quarry expansion at Exshaw Aggregates costs $500,000 now, generates $1,235,000 at the end of year 1, and requires $756,000 of site reclamation at the end of year 2. The cash flows change sign twice, so two IRRs exist. Find the LOWER IRR in % per annum.","answer":12.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(1512000/(1235000+sqrt(1235000**2-4*500000*756000))-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Square-root term sqrt(b^2 - 4ac)","value":115000.0,"check":"sqrt(1235000**2-4*500000*756000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1.0}},{"label":"Lower IRR","value":12.0,"check":"(1512000/(1235000+sqrt(1235000**2-4*500000*756000))-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["NPV = 0 with x = 1/(1 + IRR): -500,000 + 1,235,000x - 756,000x^2 = 0, i.e. 756,000x^2 - 1,235,000x + 500,000 = 0.","x = [1,235,000 +/- sqrt(1,235,000^2 - 4(756,000)(500,000))]/(2 x 756,000) = [1,235,000 +/- 115,000]/1,512,000.","x = 0.89286 -> IRR = 12.00%; x = 0.74074 -> IRR = 35.00%. The lower IRR is 12.00%.","Verification: at 12% NPV = -500,000 + 1,235,000/1.12 - 756,000/1.12^2 = -500,000 + 1,102,678.57 - 602,678.57 = 0.00."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -500000, C01 = 1235000, F01 = 1, C02 = -756000, F02 = 1, IRR, CPT -> 12.00 (the calculator returns only one root, here depending on its guess)"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-root","label":"Gave the higher IRR instead","value":35.0,"check":"(1512000/(1235000-sqrt(1235000**2-4*500000*756000))-1)*100","explain":"Both 12.00% and 35.00% make NPV = 0; the question asks for the lower one."},{"id":"ignored-final-flow","label":"Ignored the reclamation cost in year 2","value":147.0,"check":"(1235000/500000-1)*100","explain":"The year-2 outflow is part of the cash flows and creates the second sign change."}],"explanation":"Two sign changes can give two IRRs. Both 12% and 35% satisfy NPV = 0; the calculator CF worksheet reports only one of them.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-14; L05A s23"},{"id":"fin201-q09-107","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Using the same quarry expansion (outlay $500,000; $1,235,000 in year 1; reclamation cost $756,000 in year 2), the required return is 25% per annum. Compute the NPV, the figure that settles the decision.","answer":4160.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-500000+1235000/(1+0.25)-756000/(1+0.25)**2","steps":[{"label":"PV of the year-1 inflow","value":988000.0,"check":"1235000/(1+0.25)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of the year-2 reclamation cost","value":-483840.0,"check":"-756000/(1+0.25)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV","value":4160.0,"check":"-500000+1235000/(1+0.25)-756000/(1+0.25)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PV of year 1 = 1,235,000/1.25 = 988,000.00; PV of year 2 = -756,000/1.5625 = -483,840.00.","NPV = -500,000 + 988,000 - 483,840 = +$4,160.00.","25% lies between the two IRRs (12% and 35%), where the NPV is positive. Accept. A calculator that reports only 12% would wrongly say reject."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -500000, C01 = 1235000, F01 = 1, C02 = -756000, F02 = 1, NPV: I = 25, CPT -> 4,160.00"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Entered the outlay as a positive number","value":1004160.0,"check":"500000+1235000/(1+0.25)-756000/(1+0.25)**2","explain":"CF0 is an outflow."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Treated the outlay as year 1","value":3328.0,"check":"(-500000+1235000/(1+0.25)-756000/(1+0.25)**2)/(1+0.25)","explain":"The outlay is at t = 0; shifting all flows divides the NPV by 1.25."},{"id":"forgot-discount-last","label":"Did not discount the year-2 cost","value":-268000.0,"check":"-500000+1235000/(1+0.25)-756000","explain":"The reclamation cost occurs in year 2 and must be divided by 1.25^2."}],"explanation":"With multiple IRRs the NPV at the required return decides. The profile is positive only between the two IRRs.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-14; S5 s11-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-108","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A film-studio expansion at Okotoks Pictures costs $500,000 now, brings in $800,000 in year 1, then requires $400,000 of clean-up in year 2. The required return is 10% per annum. Find the NPV. (Its NPV is negative at every discount rate, so no real IRR exists.)","answer":-103305.79,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-500000+800000/(1+0.1)-400000/(1+0.1)**2","steps":[{"label":"PV of the year-1 inflow","value":727272.73,"check":"800000/(1+0.10)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV at 10%","value":-103305.79,"check":"-500000+800000/(1+0.1)-400000/(1+0.1)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV = -500,000 + 800,000/1.10 - 400,000/1.10^2 = -500,000 + 727,272.73 - 330,578.51 = -$103,305.79.","IRR check: 400,000x^2 - 800,000x + 500,000 = 0 has discriminant 800,000^2 - 4(400,000)(500,000) = -1.6 x 10^11 < 0, so there is no real IRR.","The profile never reaches zero (its maximum is -$100,000), so NPV < 0 at every R: reject."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -500000, C01 = 800000, F01 = 1, C02 = -400000, F02 = 1, NPV: I = 10, CPT -> -103,305.79 (IRR, CPT shows an error)"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Entered the outlay as a positive number","value":896694.21,"check":"500000+800000/(1+0.10)-400000/(1+0.10)**2","explain":"CF0 is an outflow."},{"id":"forgot-discount-last","label":"Did not discount the year-2 clean-up","value":-172727.27,"check":"-500000+800000/(1+0.10)-400000","explain":"The clean-up cost occurs in year 2 and must be divided by 1.10^2."},{"id":"dropped-final-flow","label":"Left out the clean-up cost","value":227272.73,"check":"-500000+800000/(1+0.10)","explain":"The year-2 outflow is part of the project."}],"explanation":"No real IRR does not mean the project is acceptable: always fall back on the NPV at R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-23"},{"id":"fin201-q09-109","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's cash flows are -$60,000, +$90,000, +$56,000 and -$90,000 in years 0 to 3. Its two IRRs are about 8.32% and 40.35%. The required return is 15%. Which statement is correct?","options":["Reject: the calculator's IRR of 8.32% is below the 15% required return","Accept: NPV(15%) = +$1,428.45, although the calculator's first IRR (8.32%) is below 15%","Reject: NPV(15%) = -$1,428.45, since the cash flows end with an outflow","No decision is possible: with two IRRs neither the IRR nor the NPV rule gives an answer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NPV(15%) = -60,000 + 90,000/1.15 + 56,000/1.15^2 - 90,000/1.15^3 = +$1,428.45. 15% lies between the two IRRs, where the NPV is positive. Trusting the single IRR the calculator shows (IRR below R, so reject) gives the wrong decision; having two IRRs does not stop the NPV from deciding.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s10-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-110","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"For cash flows of -$500,000, +$1,235,000 and -$756,000 (IRRs of 12% and 35%), at which required returns is the project accepted?","options":["Between 12% and 35%","Above 12%","Below 35%","Below 12%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV is positive only between the two IRRs (for example +$4,160.00 at 25%). Below 12% the NPV is negative (-$2,066.12 at 10%), and above 35% it is negative again (-$3,571.43 at 40%). The one-sided answers each apply the conventional rule to a single IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-14; TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-111","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"For the same cash flows (-$500,000, +$1,235,000, -$756,000), what is the NPV at a required return of 8%, below both IRRs?","options":["$4,629.63","$4,160.00","-$4,629.63","-$2,066.12"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV(8%) = -500,000 + 1,235,000/1.08 - 756,000/1.08^2 = -$4,629.63. It is negative: a required return below both IRRs does NOT mean accept when the flows are unconventional. $4,160.00 is the NPV at 25%; -$2,066.12 is the NPV at 10%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-112","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The \"multiple IRR\" problem refers to:","options":["the same project having a different IRR for each firm that evaluates it","a project having several payback periods, one for each positive cash flow","the IRR changing each year as new cash flows are received","more than one discount rate making the project's NPV equal to zero"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When the cash flows change sign more than once, the NPV equation can cross the horizontal axis several times, so several rates solve NPV = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-113","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A mining project costs $9 million now, earns positive cash flows for three years, and then requires a $4 million environmental clean-up in year 4. Its NPV equation crosses zero at two different rates. Which problem does this illustrate?","options":["The scale problem in ranking projects","The timing problem in ranking projects","The reinvestment-rate problem of the IRR","Multiple IRRs from unconventional flows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Flows that change sign more than once (a large cost late in the life) can produce more than one IRR. Scale and timing problems concern ranking mutually exclusive projects.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s22-23"},{"id":"fin201-q09-114","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"An analyst enters -$500,000, +$1,235,000 and -$756,000 in a financial calculator and sees a single IRR of 12.00%. She then plots the NPV profile and sees it cross the axis twice. What did the calculator do?","options":["It averaged the two IRRs","It reported only one of the two IRRs","It reported the IRR that is closest to the required return","It reported the correct IRR; the second crossing is a plotting error"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Calculators and spreadsheets iterate from a starting guess and stop at the first root they find. The other IRR (35%) is just as valid, which is why the NPV profile, or the NPV at R, must be checked.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-115","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do conventional and unconventional cash flows differ in terms of IRRs?","options":["Conventional flows (one sign change) have one IRR; unconventional flows (several sign changes) can have several or none","Conventional flows can have several IRRs, while unconventional flows always have exactly one IRR","Both always have exactly one IRR; the two kinds differ only in the size of the NPV","Unconventional flows never have an IRR, so only the NPV can be used for them"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Conventional: CF0 negative and all later flows positive or zero, so a single sign change and a single crossing. Unconventional: mixed signs, so up to as many IRRs as there are sign changes (possibly none).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s22-23"},{"id":"fin201-q09-116","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a typical reason for unconventional cash flows?","options":["an occasional year with large maintenance costs","clean-up or decommissioning costs at the end of the project","a single initial outlay followed by level positive annual inflows","the incremental cash flows of one project minus another"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A single outlay followed by positive inflows is the conventional pattern. Unconventional flows come from large later costs (maintenance, reclamation) and from incremental (A minus B) streams.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s22, s24"},{"id":"fin201-q09-117","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can a project with cash flows that change sign twice have two IRRs?","options":["the firm has two different required returns, one for each sign of cash flow","the NPV is calculated twice, once before tax and once after tax","the IRR depends on the order in which the flows are entered into the calculator","solving NPV = 0 means finding the roots of an equation, and a curve that crosses the axis twice has two roots"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The IRR is a root of the NPV equation. Mixed-sign flows can make the NPV profile cross the axis more than once, and each crossing is an IRR (up to the number of sign changes).","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q09-118","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why can the difference between two conventional cash-flow streams (project A minus project B) have more than one IRR?","options":["year-by-year subtraction can create several sign changes, i.e. an unconventional stream","subtracting always adds an extra year of cash flows to the difference","the difference is always negative in the last year of the project","the IRR of a difference between two projects is not defined at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each stream may have one sign change, but their difference can swing between positive and negative from year to year. That is why incremental IRRs (crossover rates) need care.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s24"},{"id":"fin201-q09-119","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project with cash flows of -$500,000, +$1,235,000 and -$756,000 has IRRs of 12% and 35%. The required return is 8%. A manager says: \"Both IRRs are above our 8%, so we accept.\" What is the right response?","options":["Accept: both IRRs exceed the required return, so the NPV must be positive","Reject: the project has two IRRs, so its value cannot be determined at all","Reject: NPV(8%) = -$4,629.63; with unconventional flows IRR > R no longer implies NPV > 0","Accept: the NPV is positive at every discount rate below the higher IRR of 35%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The NPV is negative below 12%, positive between 12% and 35%, and negative above 35%. R = 8% is in the negative zone even though both IRRs exceed it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-120","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's cash flows are -$10M, +$1M, +$1M, +$1M, -$3M, +$1M, +$1M. What is the maximum number of IRRs it could have?","options":["1","3","2","6"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The sign pattern -, +, +, +, -, +, + changes sign three times (- to +, + to -, - to +), so there may be up to three IRRs. Counting negative flows (two) or total flows would give the wrong bound.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s23"},{"id":"fin201-q09-121","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"The maximum number of IRRs a project can have is equal to:","options":["the number of years in the project","the number of negative cash flows","the number of positive cash flows","the number of times its cash flows change sign"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Descartes' rule of signs bounds the number of real positive roots by the number of sign changes. It is an upper limit: there may be fewer, or none.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s23; SM 9-20"},{"id":"fin201-q09-122","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project whose cash flows change sign exactly twice must have exactly two IRRs.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two sign changes allow up to two IRRs. There can be one, two, or none: for example -500,000, +800,000, -400,000 has no real IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-23"},{"id":"fin201-q09-123","concept":"fin201-c09-multiple-irr","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"When the IRR rule and the NPV rule conflict because of unconventional cash flows, the NPV rule should be used.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV is unambiguous for any pattern of cash flows because it is a single value at the required return; the IRR can be multiple or missing.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-401","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm lists four proposals with cash flows in $ thousands at years 0, 1, 2 and 3. Which proposal has financing-type cash flows?","options":["−120, +50, +50, +50","+95, −40, −40, −40","−80, +60, −25, +70","−60, 0, 0, +95"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Financing-type flows start with money coming IN (a positive CF0) and are followed by money going OUT. The proposal +95, −40, −40, −40 looks like a loan or a customer prepayment. The −120, +50, +50, +50 pattern is conventional, and −80, +60, −25, +70 changes sign twice (an unconventional mid-life cost), but neither starts positive and then only pays out.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-402","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-multiple-irr"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the professor's terms, a project has financing-type cash flows when:","options":["CF0 is negative and the later cash flows are positive","the cash flows change sign more than twice","the cash flows include interest paid on the firm's debt","CF0 is positive (money received up front) and the later cash flows are negative"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Financing-type flows reverse the usual pattern: you receive cash first and pay it back later, like borrowing. A conventional project is the other way round. Interest on the firm's own debt is a financing cost that is kept OUT of project cash flows, so it is not what makes flows 'financing-type'.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-403","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the IRR decision rule for a financing-type project differ from the rule for a conventional project?","options":["Conventional: accept if IRR > R. Financing-type: the same rule, because IRR is always a return.","Conventional: accept if IRR < R. Financing-type: accept if IRR > R.","Conventional: accept if IRR > R. Financing-type: accept if IRR < R.","Both use IRR > R, but financing-type projects must also have a payback under two years."],"answer":2,"explanation":"For a conventional project the IRR is the return you earn, so you want it above R. For a financing-type project the IRR is the rate you PAY for the money received up front, so you want it below R. Applying the conventional rule to financing-type flows is the classic wrong-direction error.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s25-26; SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-404","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-profile"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the IRR rule reverse for financing-type cash flows?","options":["Financing-type flows have two IRRs, and the lower one must always be used","The later cash flows are negative, so the IRR must itself be negative","Financing-type projects are riskier, so the firm should demand a higher IRR","The IRR is the cost of the money received, and NPV rises with R as the later outflows shrink"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With a positive CF0 and negative later flows, a higher discount rate makes the future outflows cheaper in present value, so NPV climbs with R (an upward-sloping NPV profile). NPV is positive when R is above the IRR, which is the same as IRR below R. The sign pattern has one change, so there is a single IRR, and it is usually positive.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-405","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northwind Cold Storage is paid $72,000 up front for a service contract and must spend $38,000 at the end of year 1 and $41,000 at the end of year 2. Its IRR is 6.33% per annum and the required return is 11%. What follows?","options":["Reject: the IRR of 6.33% is below the required return of 11%","Reject: the later flows are negative, so the contract cannot create value","Accept only if the IRR is above 11%, so the answer cannot be decided","Accept: the contract funds the firm at 6.33%, below the 11% it requires, and NPV is positive"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The flows are financing-type, so the rule flips: accept when IRR < R. 6.33% < 11%, and the NPV at 11% is $4,489.25, which confirms it. Rejecting because 'IRR is below R' applies the conventional rule to flows it does not fit.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-406","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project pays the firm $50,000 today and then costs $30,000 at the end of each of the next two years. The required return is 10% and the project's IRR is above 10%. The firm should accept it because the IRR exceeds the required return.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These are financing-type flows, so an IRR above R means the money costs MORE than the firm can earn elsewhere. Check with NPV: 50,000 − 30,000 × annuity factor(10%, 2) = 50,000 − 30,000 × 1.73554 = −$2,066.12, so reject.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-407","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-profile"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"All of the following are true of a project with financing-type cash flows EXCEPT:","options":["NPV falls as the required return rises","The IRR is a cost of funds rather than a return earned","The NPV profile slopes upward","The project should be accepted when IRR < R"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For financing-type flows the NPV profile slopes UP, so NPV rises (not falls) as R increases. The other three statements are the standard properties: IRR is the cost of the money received, the profile slopes upward, and the accept rule is IRR < R.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-408","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-profile"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project brings in $60,000 now and costs $35,000 at the end of each of the next two years. The NPV at 0% is −$10,000 and the IRR is 10.92%. For which required returns R should the firm accept it?","options":["Any R below 10.92%","Any R above 0%, because the NPV at 0% is the only number that matters","Any R above 10.92%","Never: the NPV at 0% is negative"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Financing-type NPV starts negative at R = 0 and rises, crossing zero at the IRR of 10.92%. So NPV is positive, and the project acceptable, for every R above the IRR. Choosing 'below' reverses the rule.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22; L05A s25"},{"id":"fin201-q09-409","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northwind Cold Storage is paid $72,000 up front and must spend $38,000 at the end of year 1 and $41,000 at the end of year 2. The required return is 11% per annum. What is the NPV? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":4489.25,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"72000-38000/1.11-41000/1.11**2","steps":[{"label":"PV of the year-1 cost","value":34234.23,"check":"38000/1.11","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of the year-2 cost","value":33276.52,"check":"41000/1.11**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV = CF0 − PV of costs","value":4489.25,"check":"72000-38000/1.11-41000/1.11**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["CF0 = +72,000 (money received) and the later flows are negative.","PV of year-1 cost = 38,000/1.11 = 34,234.23","PV of year-2 cost = 41,000/1.11² = 33,276.52","NPV = 72,000 − 34,234.23 − 33,276.52 = $4,489.25","NPV > 0, so accept (the IRR is below R, as the financing-type rule requires)."],"keystrokes":["CF, CF0 = 72000, C01 = −38000, F01 = 1, C02 = −41000, F02 = 1","NPV, I = 11, CPT → 4,489.25"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Treated the up-front $72,000 as an outflow","value":-139510.75,"check":"-72000-38000/1.11-41000/1.11**2","explain":"The $72,000 is cash received, so CF0 is +72,000; flipping its sign makes everything negative."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Discounted CF0 one period","value":4044.37,"check":"72000/1.11-38000/1.11**2-41000/1.11**3","explain":"CF0 occurs today and is not discounted; every later flow moves out one year in this error."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Added the flows without discounting","value":-7000.0,"check":"72000-38000-41000","explain":"At 0% the flows can be added, but the firm's required return is 11%, so each outflow must be discounted."}],"explanation":"Positive CF0 less the present value of the two later costs. The positive NPV says the up-front money is cheaper than the firm's 11% requirement.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-410","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A supplier offers to prepay $47,000 now in return for $52,000 of goods delivered in one year. The firm's required return is 12% per annum. First find the IRR of the deal, then its NPV. (Final answer: the NPV in $, to the cent.)","answer":571.43,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"47000-52000/1.12","steps":[{"label":"IRR = 52,000/47,000 − 1 (cost of the funds)","value":10.6383,"check":"(52000/47000-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV at 12%","value":571.43,"check":"47000-52000/1.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Flows: +47,000 at t = 0, −52,000 at t = 1 (financing-type).","IRR: 47,000 = 52,000/(1 + IRR), so IRR = 52,000/47,000 − 1 = 10.6383%.","NPV = 47,000 − 52,000/1.12 = 47,000 − 46,428.57 = $571.43.","IRR (10.64%) < R (12%) and NPV > 0, so accept: the funds cost less than the firm requires."],"keystrokes":["IRR: 52000 ÷ 47000 − 1 = 0.10638 → 10.6383%","NPV: 47000 − 52000 ÷ 1.12 = 571.43"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Used −47,000 now and +52,000 later (conventional signs)","value":-571.43,"check":"-47000+52000/1.12","explain":"That is the NPV of lending the money, not of receiving it."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Discounted both flows by a year","value":510.2,"check":"47000/1.12-52000/1.12**2","explain":"CF0 happens today (no discounting), and the repayment is one year away, not two."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Subtracted the repayment without discounting","value":-5000.0,"check":"47000-52000","explain":"The $52,000 is paid in a year, so it must be discounted at 12% before comparing."}],"explanation":"A one-period financing deal has IRR = repayment/amount received − 1, here 10.64%. Because this cost is below 12%, NPV is positive and the deal is accepted.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22; L05A s25"},{"id":"fin201-q09-411","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A co-op receives $120,000 now and repays $52,000 at the end of each of the next three years. The required return is 10% per annum. What is the NPV? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":-9316.3,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"120000-52000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (10%, 3 years)","value":2.48685,"check":"(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the three repayments","value":129316.3,"check":"52000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV = 120,000 − PV of repayments","value":-9316.3,"check":"120000-52000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Financing-type flows: +120,000 now, then −52,000 for three years.","Annuity factor = (1 − 1/1.1³)/0.10 = 2.48685","PV of repayments = 52,000 × 2.48685 = 129,316.30","NPV = 120,000 − 129,316.30 = −$9,316.30","NPV < 0 (the IRR of about 14.36% is above R = 10%), so reject: the money costs too much."],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 10, PMT = −52000, FV = 0, CPT PV → 129,316.30","120000 − 129316.30 = −9,316.30"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 4-year annuity factor","value":-44833.0,"check":"120000-52000*(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1","explain":"Three repayments need a three-year annuity factor."},{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Put the $120,000 as an outflow","value":-249316.3,"check":"-120000-52000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1","explain":"The $120,000 is received, so it enters as +120,000."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Added the undiscounted repayments","value":-36000.0,"check":"120000-52000*3","explain":"Three payments of 52,000 are 156,000 undiscounted; at 10% their present value is smaller."}],"explanation":"The repayments are an ordinary annuity discounted at R and subtracted from the cash received. NPV is negative, so the firm rejects the financing. The IRR (14.36%) is above R, which for financing-type flows signals reject.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22; TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-412","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Ridge Marinas receives a $30,000 grant now and must spend $16,000 at the end of year 1 and $17,500 at the end of year 2. At a required return of 9% per annum, what is the NPV?","options":["−$59,408.30","$542.84","−$3,500.00","$591.70"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NPV = 30,000 − 16,000/1.09 − 17,500/1.09² = 30,000 − 14,678.90 − 14,729.40 = $591.70. The wrong figures come from treating the grant as a cost, discounting CF0 a year, or adding the flows undiscounted (−$3,500).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-413","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm receives $90,000 today and pays $50,000 at the end of each of the next two years. It correctly computes the IRR of the deal and compares it with its required return. For which required returns should it accept?","options":["R above 7.32%","R below 7.32%","R above 11.11%","R below 11.11%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Solve 90,000 = 50,000/(1 + r) + 50,000/(1 + r)²: the annuity factor must be 1.8, which gives r = 7.32%. The money costs 7.32% per annum, so accept only if the firm requires more than that. 11.11% is the undiscounted gain (100,000/90,000 − 1), which ignores the timing, and 'below' flips the rule.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22"},{"id":"fin201-q09-414","concept":"fin201-c09-financing-flows","also":["fin201-c09-npv-profile"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has a positive CF0 followed by two negative cash flows. If the required return R rises from 6% to 12%, what happens to the NPV?","options":["It falls, as for every conventional project","It stays the same, because the cash flows do not change","It rises, because the later outflows are discounted more heavily","It falls until the IRR and then rises"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV = CF0 + PV(later flows). With negative later flows, a higher R shrinks the present value of those costs, so NPV goes up. Northwind-type contracts show it: NPV is −$338.91 at 6% and +$5,386.48 at 12%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-22; L05A s21"},{"id":"fin201-q09-415","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":["fin201-c09-multiple-irr"],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A modified internal rate of return (MIRR) is:","options":["the IRR after removing the project's largest cash flow","the IRR of cash flows that have first been modified using an external discount or reinvestment rate","the discount rate at which the project's discounted payback equals its life","the average of the project's IRRs when it has more than one"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An MIRR first rewrites the cash flows (discounting some to today, compounding others to the end, or both) and then takes the IRR of the modified flows. Because the modified flows have one sign change, there is a single answer.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9 App"},{"id":"fin201-q09-416","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"To find an MIRR, an analyst discounts every negative flow back to time 0 at the required return, compounds every positive flow forward to the end of the project at the same rate, and then computes the IRR of the two resulting flows. Which approach is this?","options":["The discounting approach","The reinvestment approach","The payback approach","The combination approach"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The combination approach does both things: negative flows are discounted to time 0 and positive flows are compounded to the end. The discounting approach only moves negative flows to time 0, and the reinvestment approach only compounds everything (except CF0) to the end.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-417","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly separates the ordinary IRR from an MIRR?","options":["The MIRR depends only on the project's own cash flows, while the IRR needs a required return","Both are true internal rates because both set an NPV to zero","The IRR depends only on the project's own cash flows, while an MIRR also needs an externally supplied rate","The IRR assumes cash flows are reinvested at the required return; the MIRR does not reinvest anything"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The IRR is computed from the cash flows alone. An MIRR is not truly 'internal': it uses the required return (or a reinvestment rate) to rewrite the flows first, so the result changes if that rate changes. Neither IRR nor NPV actually requires a reinvestment assumption.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-418","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":["fin201-c09-multiple-irr"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does an MIRR not suffer from the multiple-IRR problem?","options":["The MIRR ignores all negative cash flows","The MIRR is always calculated at the required return, so it is never an IRR","The modified cash flows change sign only once, so only one IRR exists","The MIRR is computed on the project's NPV instead of its cash flows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Discounting the later negative flows to time 0 (or compounding everything to the end) leaves a negative start and a positive finish, so the sign pattern is conventional and only one rate solves it. The cost is that the answer now depends on the rate used to modify the flows.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-419","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar Creek Mining expects cash flows of −$80,000 now, +$45,000, +$52,000 and +$38,000 in years 1 to 3, and a −$30,000 reclamation cost in year 4. Use the combination approach at R = 10% per annum for both discounting and compounding. What is the MIRR? (% per annum, 2 decimals.)","answer":13.1322,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((45000*1.1**3+52000*1.1**2+38000*1.1)/(80000+30000/1.1**4))**(1/4)*100-100","steps":[{"label":"Time-0 outflow: 80,000 + 30,000/1.1⁴","value":100490.4,"check":"80000+30000/1.1**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Time-4 inflow value","value":164615.0,"check":"45000*1.1**3+52000*1.1**2+38000*1.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"MIRR","value":13.1322,"check":"((45000*1.1**3+52000*1.1**2+38000*1.1)/(80000+30000/1.1**4))**(1/4)*100-100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Discount the negative flows to time 0: 80,000 + 30,000/1.1⁴ = 100,490.40.","Compound the positive flows to year 4: 45,000(1.1)³ + 52,000(1.1)² + 38,000(1.1) = 59,895 + 62,920 + 41,800 = 164,615.00.","MIRR = (164,615.00/100,490.40)^(1/4) − 1 = 1.63812^(0.25) − 1 = 13.1322%."],"keystrokes":["30000 ÷ 1.1^4 = 20,490.40; + 80000 = 100,490.40","FV: 45000 × 1.331 + 52000 × 1.21 + 38000 × 1.1 = 164,615","164615 ÷ 100490.40 = 1.63812; y^x 0.25 = 1.13132; − 1 → 13.13%"],"errors":[{"id":"mirr-method-mix","label":"Used the reinvestment approach instead","value":13.894,"check":"(((45000*1.1**3+52000*1.1**2+38000*1.1)-30000)/80000)**(1/4)*100-100","explain":"That approach leaves −$80,000 at time 0 and subtracts the −$30,000 from the year-4 value instead of discounting it, so it is a different MIRR."},{"id":"outflow-undiscounted","label":"Added the reclamation cost to CF0 without discounting","value":10.6036,"check":"((45000*1.1**3+52000*1.1**2+38000*1.1)/110000)**(1/4)*100-100","explain":"The year-4 cost must be discounted four years at R before it joins the time-0 outflow."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Took the cube root instead of the fourth root","value":17.8822,"check":"((45000*1.1**3+52000*1.1**2+38000*1.1)/(80000+30000/1.1**4))**(1/3)*100-100","explain":"The modified flows are four years apart (time 0 to time 4), so the root is 1/4."}],"explanation":"Combination approach: negatives to today, positives to the end, then a two-flow IRR. It is a two-flow problem, so a root, not a trial-and-error search, finishes it.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-420","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Ridgeline Quarry has cash flows of −$90,000 now, +$40,000 in year 1, −$15,000 in year 2, +$48,000 in year 3 and +$52,000 in year 4. Using the reinvestment approach with a reinvestment rate of 8% per annum, what is the MIRR? (% per annum, 2 decimals.)","answer":11.0216,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((40000*1.08**3-15000*1.08**2+48000*1.08+52000)/90000)**(1/4)*100-100","steps":[{"label":"Time-4 value of every flow after time 0","value":136732.48,"check":"40000*1.08**3-15000*1.08**2+48000*1.08+52000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"MIRR = (FV/90,000)^(1/4) − 1","value":11.0216,"check":"((40000*1.08**3-15000*1.08**2+48000*1.08+52000)/90000)**(1/4)*100-100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Reinvestment approach: compound every flow EXCEPT CF0 to the end (year 4) at 8%.","40,000(1.08)³ = 50,388.48;  −15,000(1.08)² = −17,496.00;  48,000(1.08) = 51,840.00;  +52,000.","Sum = 136,732.48.","MIRR = (136,732.48/90,000)^(1/4) − 1 = 1.51925^(0.25) − 1 = 11.0216%."],"keystrokes":["40000 × 1.08^3 − 15000 × 1.08^2 + 48000 × 1.08 + 52000 = 136,732.48","136732.48 ÷ 90000 = 1.51925; y^x 0.25 = 1.11023; − 1 → 11.02%"],"errors":[{"id":"mirr-method-mix","label":"Used the combination approach (negative flow discounted to time 0)","value":10.6571,"check":"((40000*1.08**3+48000*1.08+52000)/(90000+15000/1.08**2))**(1/4)*100-100","explain":"The reinvestment approach compounds the −$15,000 forward with everything else; it does not discount it to time 0."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Used a three-year root","value":14.959,"check":"((40000*1.08**3-15000*1.08**2+48000*1.08+52000)/90000)**(1/3)*100-100","explain":"From time 0 to year 4 is four years, so the root is 1/4."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Reported the growth factor 1.1102 as 111.02%","value":111.0216,"check":"((40000*1.08**3-15000*1.08**2+48000*1.08+52000)/90000)**(1/4)*100","explain":"A rate is the growth factor minus 1."}],"explanation":"Reinvestment approach: carry every later flow, positive or negative, to the project's end at the reinvestment rate, then treat the result as a single investment and a single terminal value.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-421","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cedar Creek Mining (−$80,000 now; +$45,000, +$52,000, +$38,000 in years 1 to 3; −$30,000 in year 4) uses R = 10% per annum. Using the DISCOUNTING approach (only the negative flows are moved, to time 0), what is the MIRR?","options":["13.13%","13.89%","21.17%","16.80%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Discounting approach: time-0 flow = −80,000 − 30,000/1.1⁴ = −100,490.40, with +45,000, +52,000 and +38,000 left in years 1 to 3. The IRR of those flows is 16.80%. 13.13% is the combination MIRR, 13.89% the reinvestment MIRR, and 21.17% the ordinary IRR of the original flows.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-422","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar Creek Mining's combination-approach MIRR is 13.13% against a required return of 10%, and its NPV at 10% is $11,943.86. What should the analyst conclude?","options":["Accept; MIRR and NPV agree, and if they ever disagreed the NPV rule would govern","Reject; the MIRR must be at least as high as the ordinary IRR of 21.17%","Accept only if the MIRR equals the required return exactly","No decision; an MIRR is the project's true internal return so it cannot be compared with R"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The MIRR (13.13%) exceeds R and the NPV is positive, so both say accept. The MIRR is not a true internal return, since it depends on the rate used to modify the flows, which is why NPV is the fall-back rule. The three methods give different MIRRs for the same project.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-423","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a conventional project (a negative CF0 followed only by positive cash flows), the discounting-approach MIRR equals the ordinary IRR.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The discounting approach only moves NEGATIVE flows back to time 0. A conventional project has no negative flows after time 0, so its cash flows are left unchanged and the IRR of the 'modified' flows is the ordinary IRR. The reinvestment and combination approaches do change the flows, and give a different answer.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-424","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"All of the following are common criticisms of the MIRR EXCEPT:","options":["It is not a true internal rate, because it depends on externally supplied rates","There are several ways to calculate it and no clear reason to prefer one","If the relevant discount rate is known, the NPV can be calculated directly","It cannot be calculated when the cash flows change sign more than once"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The MIRR was invented to deal with cash flows that change sign more than once, because it always gives one answer. The criticisms are the other three: it is not internal, there are several competing versions, and NPV answers the same question without the extra steps.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-425","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project costs $50,000 and returns $30,000 at the end of year 1 and $35,000 at the end of year 2. The reinvestment rate is 10% per annum. Which expression gives the reinvestment-approach MIRR?","options":["[(30,000 + 35,000 × 1.10) / 50,000]^(1/2) − 1","[(30,000 × 1.10² + 35,000 × 1.10) / 50,000]^(1/2) − 1","(30,000 + 35,000) / 50,000 − 1","[(30,000 × 1.10 + 35,000) / 50,000]^(1/2) − 1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Compound each flow after time 0 to the end of the project (year 2): the year-1 flow grows one year at 10% and the year-2 flow is already at the end. Divide by the initial outlay and take the 2nd root. Compounding the wrong flow, compounding to year 3, or skipping the root all give wrong rates.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-426","concept":"fin201-c09-mirr","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project costs $50,000 and returns $30,000 at the end of year 1 and $35,000 at the end of year 2. Using the reinvestment approach at 10% per annum, what is the MIRR?","options":["18.88%","16.62%","36.00%","17.05%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Value at year 2 = 30,000 × 1.10 + 35,000 = 68,000; MIRR = (68,000/50,000)^(1/2) − 1 = 16.62%. 18.88% is the ordinary IRR, 36.00% forgets the square root, and 17.05% compounds the wrong flow.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-427","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-project-relationships"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects have IRRs of 24% and 21%. At the firm's required return of 10% the 21% project has the higher NPV. This disagreement between the IRR and NPV rankings is called:","options":["the multiple-IRR problem","a ranking conflict between IRR and NPV","capital rationing","a financing-type cash-flow problem"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When IRR and NPV rank mutually exclusive projects differently, the firm faces a ranking conflict. It is caused by differences in scale or timing, not by unconventional cash flows (multiple IRRs), by a lack of funds (rationing), or by the sign pattern.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-428","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is NPV, not IRR, used to make the final choice between mutually exclusive projects?","options":["IRR cannot be calculated when projects are mutually exclusive","NPV measures the change in shareholder wealth in dollars, so the highest NPV adds the most value","NPV is a percentage, so it can be compared across projects of different sizes","IRR is based on accounting profit while NPV is based on cash flows"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The financial manager's aim is to maximise shareholder wealth, and NPV is that change in wealth. IRR is a rate that ignores how many dollars are invested. Both IRR and NPV use cash flows, and NPV is a dollar amount, not a percentage.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s18-19; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q09-429","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pair identifies the two ways in which the IRR and NPV rankings of mutually exclusive projects can conflict?","options":["The scale of the investment and the timing of the cash flows","The tax rate and the rate of inflation","The number of IRRs and the sign of CF0","The riskiness and the liquidity of the projects"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A bigger project can have a lower IRR yet a bigger NPV (scale), and a project with later cash flows can have a lower IRR yet a bigger NPV at low discount rates (timing). The number of IRRs is a different issue (unconventional flows).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-12, 9-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-430","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hartwell Foods must choose one of two mutually exclusive lines at R = 10%. Line S costs $35,000 and has an IRR of 28.57% and an NPV of $5,909.09. Line L costs $150,000 and has an IRR of 20.00% and an NPV of $13,636.36. What is the source of the conflict and the correct choice?","options":["Scale: choose Line S, which has the higher IRR","Scale: choose Line L, which has the higher NPV","Timing: choose Line S, which pays back sooner","No conflict: accept both lines because both NPVs are positive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Line L is more than four times as large, so its lower percentage return still earns more dollars. That is a scale conflict, and NPV settles it. Taking both is impossible because the lines are mutually exclusive, and choosing S by IRR is the mistake of picking the higher IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-16; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-431","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"For two mutually exclusive projects at R = 10%, Project A has an IRR of 28.03% and an NPV of $13,388.43, while Project B has an IRR of 22.98% and an NPV of $15,522.16. Which project should the firm take?","options":["Project B, because it has the higher NPV","Project A, because it has the higher IRR","Both, because both have positive NPVs","Neither, because the two rules disagree"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When the rules conflict for mutually exclusive projects, the NPV rule decides, because NPV measures the dollars added to shareholder wealth. Project B adds more even though its IRR is lower.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-432","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a reason why the IRR and NPV rules can rank mutually exclusive projects differently?","options":["The projects differ in the scale of the investment","The projects' cash flows arrive at different times","NPV uses cash flows while IRR uses accounting profit","IRR is a percentage that ignores how many dollars are invested"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both NPV and IRR are built from the same cash flows, so the basis of the numbers is not the cause. Conflicts come from scale (IRR ignores dollars invested) and from timing (the NPV ranking changes with the discount rate, the IRR does not).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-433","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"For independent projects with conventional cash flows, the NPV rule and the IRR rule always give the same accept or reject decision for each project.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For conventional flows NPV is positive exactly when IRR exceeds R, so accept/reject decisions match. Disagreements arise only when projects are mutually exclusive and must be RANKED.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s25-26"},{"id":"fin201-q09-434","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-crossover"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects, E and L, have IRRs of 20.50% and 17.72%. Their NPV profiles cross at 13.24%. At R = 10% the NPV of L is higher. If the firm's required return rises to 15%, what happens to the conflict?","options":["It disappears: above the crossover rate E has both the higher IRR and the higher NPV","It gets worse, because L always has the higher NPV","It stays, because the IRR ranking and the NPV ranking never agree for these projects","It disappears because both projects now have negative NPVs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Above the crossover rate (13.24%) the profile of E lies above that of L, so E wins on NPV as well as IRR and the two rules agree. At 15% both NPVs are still positive, because both IRRs exceed 15%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-435","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-npv-steps"],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put these steps for choosing between two mutually exclusive projects in the right order. (a) Pick the project with the highest positive NPV. (b) Discount each project's cash flows at R to get its NPV. (c) Forecast each project's after-tax cash flows. (d) Decide on the required return R.","options":["d, c, a, b","b, c, d, a","c, d, b, a","c, b, d, a"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The NPV sequence from the lecture is: cash flows, then the discount rate, then discount (equalising lives if needed), then decide. The IRR is not needed to make the final choice.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-436","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-crossover"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the choice between the IRR rule and the NPV rule only matter for two mutually exclusive projects when R is BELOW the crossover rate?","options":["Below the crossover both projects have negative NPVs, so neither rule applies","Below the crossover each project's IRR changes sign, so the IRRs are unreliable","Below it the lower-IRR project has the higher NPV; above it the two rankings agree","Above the crossover the projects become independent, so both can be taken"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At the crossover rate the two NPVs are equal. Below it the lower-IRR project (bigger or later flows) is ahead on NPV; above it the higher-IRR project is ahead on both measures. The rules therefore disagree only below the crossover.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-437","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Compare a project with cash flows concentrated in year 1 with a project of equal cost whose cash flows come mostly in year 5. When the required return rises, what happens to their NPVs?","options":["The earlier-payoff project's NPV falls faster, because it is discounted first","The later-payoff project's NPV falls faster, so its NPV profile is steeper","Both NPVs fall at the same speed because they have the same cost","Both NPVs rise, because a higher R means a higher required return"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cash flows far in the future are discounted by (1 + R)^t, so a higher t makes the present value more sensitive to R. That is why back-loaded projects win at low R and lose at high R, which creates the timing conflict.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-438","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"The firm must pick exactly one of three mutually exclusive projects, each with a positive NPV at its required return. P1: IRR 31%, NPV $4.1 million. P2: IRR 22%, NPV $9.8 million. P3: IRR 16%, NPV $7.4 million. Which should it take?","options":["P1, which has the highest IRR","P3, which has the lowest IRR and so the most room to grow","All three, since each NPV is positive","P2, which has the highest NPV"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With more than two mutually exclusive projects, compute the NPV of each and choose the highest (the lecture's rule). The highest IRR (P1) is the trap of picking the highest IRR; the three cannot all be undertaken because only one can be chosen.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-439","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fraser Valley Berry Co. can take only one upgrade. Plan E costs $60,000 and returns $39,000, $31,000 and $11,000 in years 1 to 3 (IRR 20.50%). Plan L costs $60,000 and returns $5,000, $28,000 and $58,000 (IRR 17.72%). At R = 10% per annum, by how many dollars does the NPV of the plan the firm should choose exceed the NPV of the other plan?","answer":1923.37,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-60000+5000/1.1+28000/1.1**2+58000/1.1**3-(-60000+39000/1.1+31000/1.1**2+11000/1.1**3)","steps":[{"label":"NPV of Plan E at 10%","value":9338.84,"check":"-60000+39000/1.1+31000/1.1**2+11000/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Plan L at 10%","value":11262.21,"check":"-60000+5000/1.1+28000/1.1**2+58000/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV(L) − NPV(E)","value":1923.37,"check":"-60000+5000/1.1+28000/1.1**2+58000/1.1**3-(-60000+39000/1.1+31000/1.1**2+11000/1.1**3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV of E = −60,000 + 39,000/1.1 + 31,000/1.1² + 11,000/1.1³ = $9,338.84","NPV of L = −60,000 + 5,000/1.1 + 28,000/1.1² + 58,000/1.1³ = $11,262.21","L has the higher NPV although E has the higher IRR. Advantage of L = $11,262.21 − $9,338.84 = $1,923.37."],"keystrokes":["E: CF0 = −60000, C01 = 39000, C02 = 31000, C03 = 11000, NPV, I = 10, CPT → 9,338.84","L: CF0 = −60000, C01 = 5000, C02 = 28000, C03 = 58000, NPV, I = 10, CPT → 11,262.21"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-irr","label":"Reported the NPV of the higher-IRR plan (E) as the answer","value":9338.84,"check":"-60000+39000/1.1+31000/1.1**2+11000/1.1**3","explain":"E has the higher IRR, but the question asks for the NPV advantage of the plan with the higher NPV."},{"id":"subtracted-wrong-order","label":"Subtracted NPV(L) from NPV(E)","value":-1923.37,"check":"-60000+39000/1.1+31000/1.1**2+11000/1.1**3-(-60000+5000/1.1+28000/1.1**2+58000/1.1**3)","explain":"The advantage of the better plan is positive; reversing the subtraction gives a negative number."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Compared undiscounted net gains (31,000 vs 21,000)","value":10000.0,"check":"(5000+28000+58000-60000)-(39000+31000+11000-60000)","explain":"Net gains ignore timing; the later cash flows of L must be discounted before comparing."}],"explanation":"At R = 10% (below the 13.24% crossover) L has the higher NPV even though E has the higher IRR. The IRR rule would have cost shareholders the difference.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-440","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hartwell Foods must choose one of two mutually exclusive lines at R = 10% per annum. Line S costs $35,000 and returns $45,000 in one year (IRR 28.57%). Line L costs $150,000 and returns $180,000 in one year (IRR 20.00%). How much shareholder wealth is lost, in NPV terms, if the firm chooses by IRR instead of by NPV?","answer":7727.27,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(180000/1.1-150000)-(45000/1.1-35000)","steps":[{"label":"NPV of Line S","value":5909.09,"check":"45000/1.1-35000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Line L","value":13636.36,"check":"180000/1.1-150000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV lost by choosing S","value":7727.27,"check":"(180000/1.1-150000)-(45000/1.1-35000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV of S = 45,000/1.1 − 35,000 = $5,909.09","NPV of L = 180,000/1.1 − 150,000 = $13,636.36","Choosing S by IRR gives up 13,636.36 − 5,909.09 = $7,727.27 of NPV."],"keystrokes":["S: 45000 ÷ 1.1 = 40,909.09; − 35000 = 5,909.09","L: 180000 ÷ 1.1 = 163,636.36; − 150000 = 13,636.36","13,636.36 − 5,909.09 = 7,727.27"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-irr","label":"Reported the NPV of Line S (the IRR choice)","value":5909.09,"check":"45000/1.1-35000","explain":"That is what the IRR rule delivers, not what it gives up."},{"id":"subtracted-wrong-order","label":"Subtracted NPV(L) from NPV(S)","value":-7727.27,"check":"(45000/1.1-35000)-(180000/1.1-150000)","explain":"The loss is how much more NPV L has, a positive number."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Compared undiscounted gains (30,000 − 10,000)","value":20000.0,"check":"(180000-150000)-(45000-35000)","explain":"The gains arrive in a year and must be discounted at 10% before comparing."}],"explanation":"This is a scale conflict: L is four times the size, so its lower percentage return earns more dollars. The NPV rule picks L.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-16; TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-441","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-crossover"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Wind Farms Ltd. has two mutually exclusive turbine projects at R = 7% per annum. Project S costs $30,000 and returns $14,000 at the end of each of the next 3 years (IRR 18.91%). Project L costs $110,000 and returns $46,000 at the end of each of the next 3 years (IRR 12.26%). What is the NPV of the project that maximises shareholder wealth?","answer":10718.54,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"46000*(1-1/1.07**3)/0.07-110000","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (7%, 3 years)","value":2.62432,"check":"(1-1/1.07**3)/0.07","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of Project S","value":6740.42,"check":"14000*(1-1/1.07**3)/0.07-30000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Project L","value":10718.54,"check":"46000*(1-1/1.07**3)/0.07-110000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor = (1 − 1/1.07³)/0.07 = 2.62432","NPV of S = 14,000 × 2.624316 − 30,000 = $6,740.42","NPV of L = 46,000 × 2.624316 − 110,000 = $10,718.54","Project L has the higher NPV (despite the lower IRR), so it is the choice and its NPV is the answer."],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 7, PMT = 14000, CPT PV → 36,740.42; − 30000 = 6,740.42","N = 3, I/Y = 7, PMT = 46000, CPT PV → 120,718.54; − 110000 = 10,718.54"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-irr","label":"Reported the NPV of Project S (higher IRR)","value":6740.42,"check":"14000*(1-1/1.07**3)/0.07-30000","explain":"S has the higher IRR, but the shareholders gain more from L."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 4-year annuity factor for L","value":45811.72,"check":"46000*(1-1/1.07**4)/0.07-110000","explain":"Three years of cash flows need an annuity factor with N = 3."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Added the flows without discounting","value":28000.0,"check":"46000*3-110000","explain":"The three payments must be discounted at 7%; 3 × 46,000 − 110,000 ignores the time value of money."}],"explanation":"Here R = 7% is below the crossover rate (9.70%), so the larger project L wins on NPV even though S has the higher IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-442","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"At R = 8% per annum, mutually exclusive Project A (−$50,000; +$38,000, +$25,000, +$8,000) has an IRR of 25.84%, and Project B (−$50,000; +$9,000, +$18,000, +$53,000) has an IRR of 20.65%. What is the NPV of the project the firm should undertake?","options":["$15,838.54","$12,969.31","$2,869.23","$14,665.32"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV(B) = −50,000 + 9,000/1.08 + 18,000/1.08² + 53,000/1.08³ = $15,838.54; NPV(A) = $12,969.31. B is chosen by NPV, even though A has the higher IRR. $2,869.23 is only the NPV advantage, and $14,665.32 comes from treating CF0 as a period-1 flow.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-443","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm picks Line S (cost $35,000, one-year payoff $45,000) over Line L (cost $150,000, one-year payoff $180,000) because S has the higher IRR. If R = 10% per annum, how much NPV does the firm give up?","options":["$13,636.36","$5,909.09","$7,727.27","$20,000.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV(L) − NPV(S) = 13,636.36 − 5,909.09 = $7,727.27. $13,636.36 is NPV(L) alone, $5,909.09 is the NPV of the project actually taken, and $20,000 compares undiscounted gains (30,000 − 10,000).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-444","concept":"fin201-c09-ranking-conflict","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"If two mutually exclusive projects have IRRs of 24% and 21%, choosing the 24% project always maximises shareholder wealth.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"IRR ignores scale and timing. The 21% project can have the higher NPV (when R is below the crossover rate, or when it is much larger), and NPV is the measure of shareholder wealth.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-445","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The crossover rate is the discount rate at which the _____ of two projects are equal.","options":["IRRs","NPVs","profitability indexes","payback periods"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At the crossover rate the two NPV profiles intersect, so both projects have the same NPV. IRRs are where each profile cuts the horizontal axis, which is a different point.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-446","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"How is the crossover rate between Project A and Project B found?","options":["It is the simple average of the two projects' IRRs","It is the IRR of the larger project, which has the larger flows","It is the IRR of the year-by-year differences in their cash flows (A − B)","It is the discount rate at which each project's NPV equals zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Subtract one project's cash flows from the other's in every period, including CF0, and find the IRR of the differences. The NPV of that difference is NPV(A) − NPV(B), which is zero exactly when the two NPVs are equal.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-447","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Wind Farms Ltd. compares Project S (−$30,000; +$14,000 in each of years 1 to 3) with Project L (−$110,000; +$46,000 in each of years 1 to 3). Which cash-flow list is used to find their crossover rate?","options":["L + S: −$140,000, +$60,000, +$60,000, +$60,000","L − S in years 1 to 3 only: +$32,000, +$32,000, +$32,000","L ÷ S: 3.67, 3.29, 3.29, 3.29","L − S: −$80,000, +$32,000, +$32,000, +$32,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Subtract the projects' flows period by period, and keep the difference in CF0 (−110,000 − (−30,000) = −80,000). Leaving CF0 out or adding the flows are the classic setup mistakes. The IRR of L − S is 9.70% (see the NPV check: −80,000 + 32,000 × annuity factor(9.701026%, 3) = $0.00).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12, 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-448","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the crossover rate of two projects differ from the IRR of one of them?","options":["An IRR sets one project's NPV to zero; the crossover sets the gap between two NPVs to zero","The IRR needs a required return to compute, but the crossover rate does not","The crossover rate is always larger than both projects' own IRRs","They are the same number whenever the projects are mutually exclusive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IRR is where one NPV profile hits the horizontal axis. The crossover rate is where two profiles meet each other, which may happen at a positive NPV. For conventional projects with a ranking conflict, the crossover is typically below both IRRs.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12; TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-449","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"Subtracting Project B's cash flows from Project A's, rather than A's from B's, gives a different crossover rate.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reversing the subtraction multiplies every difference by −1, which flips the sign of the NPV of the difference but not the discount rate at which it is zero. The textbook solution says it is irrelevant which project is subtracted from the other.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-450","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the IRR of the difference in cash flows equal to the discount rate at which two projects have equal NPVs?","options":["NPV(A) − NPV(B) equals the NPV of the difference flows, which is zero at the IRR of those flows","NPV is linear in the discount rate, so the profiles must meet at the average of the IRRs","The IRR of any project always equals the rate where its NPV is the same as another project's","Subtracting the flows removes the time value of money"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV is additive: NPV(A) − NPV(B) = NPV(A − B). Setting that to zero means finding the IRR of the difference. NPV is not linear in R, so the average of the IRRs has no special meaning.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-451","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"The NPV profiles of E and L cross at 13.24%. E has the higher IRR (20.50% against 17.72%). The firm's required return is 9%. Which project should it choose, and why?","options":["E, because 9% is below the 13.24% crossover rate and E has the higher IRR","L, because 9% is below the 13.24% crossover rate and L has the higher NPV there","E, because 9% is below both IRRs","L, because its IRR is lower and lower IRRs are safer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Below the crossover rate, the project with the later cash flows (L) has the higher NPV. Above it, E would win. IRR size does not decide the ranking. At 9%, NPV(L) − NPV(E) = $2,574.92.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-452","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fraser Valley Berry Co.'s Plan E costs $60,000 and returns $39,000, $31,000 and $11,000 in years 1 to 3. Plan L costs $60,000 and returns $5,000, $28,000 and $58,000. The crossover rate is 13.24%. At R = 12% per annum, which is below the crossover, how many dollars larger is the NPV of the better plan than the NPV of the other plan?","answer":704.95,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-60000+5000/1.12+28000/1.12**2+58000/1.12**3-(-60000+39000/1.12+31000/1.12**2+11000/1.12**3)","steps":[{"label":"NPV of Plan E at 12%","value":7364.02,"check":"-60000+39000/1.12+31000/1.12**2+11000/1.12**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Plan L at 12%","value":8068.97,"check":"-60000+5000/1.12+28000/1.12**2+58000/1.12**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV(L) − NPV(E)","value":704.95,"check":"-60000+5000/1.12+28000/1.12**2+58000/1.12**3-(-60000+39000/1.12+31000/1.12**2+11000/1.12**3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV of E at 12% = $7,364.02","NPV of L at 12% = $8,068.97","12% is below the 13.24% crossover, so L has the higher NPV by $8,068.97 − $7,364.02 = $704.95.","(As R climbs to 13.24% this gap shrinks to $0; above it E leads.)"],"keystrokes":["E: CF0 = −60000, C01 = 39000, C02 = 31000, C03 = 11000, NPV, I = 12, CPT → 7,364.02","L: CF0 = −60000, C01 = 5000, C02 = 28000, C03 = 58000, NPV, I = 12, CPT → 8,068.97"],"errors":[{"id":"subtracted-wrong-order","label":"Subtracted NPV(L) from NPV(E)","value":-704.95,"check":"-60000+39000/1.12+31000/1.12**2+11000/1.12**3-(-60000+5000/1.12+28000/1.12**2+58000/1.12**3)","explain":"The better plan's advantage is a positive amount."},{"id":"picked-higher-irr","label":"Reported the NPV of E, the higher-IRR plan","value":7364.02,"check":"-60000+39000/1.12+31000/1.12**2+11000/1.12**3","explain":"The question asks for the gap between the two NPVs, not the NPV of the higher-IRR plan."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Compared undiscounted net gains (31,000 vs 21,000)","value":10000.0,"check":"(5000+28000+58000-60000)-(39000+31000+11000-60000)","explain":"Net gains ignore timing; the later flows of L must be discounted at 12% before comparing."}],"explanation":"The gap between the two NPVs is positive below the crossover and shrinks to zero at it, which is the same as saying NPV(L − E) rises to zero as R moves up to the crossover.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5; SM 9-13"},{"id":"fin201-q09-453","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hartwell Foods compares two mutually exclusive lines. Line S costs $35,000 and returns $45,000 in one year. Line L costs $150,000 and returns $180,000 in one year. Find the cash flows of the incremental project (L − S) and then the crossover rate. (Final answer: crossover rate, % per annum.)","answer":17.3913,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(135000/115000-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Incremental CF0 (L − S)","value":-115000.0,"check":"35000-150000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Incremental CF1 (L − S)","value":135000.0,"check":"180000-45000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Crossover rate","value":17.3913,"check":"(135000/115000-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Incremental flows (L − S): CF0 = −150,000 − (−35,000) = −115,000; CF1 = 180,000 − 45,000 = +135,000.","One-period project, so IRR = 135,000/115,000 − 1 = 17.3913%.","Check: NPV of the difference at 17.391304% = −115,000 + 135,000/1.17391304 = $0.00 (to the cent).","Below 17.39% the larger line L has the higher NPV; above it the smaller line S does."],"keystrokes":["135000 ÷ 115000 = 1.173913 → − 1 = 0.173913 → 17.39%","(or CF0 = −115000, C01 = 135000, IRR CPT → 17.39)"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-irr","label":"Reported the IRR of Line S (28.57%)","value":28.5714,"check":"(45000/35000-1)*100","explain":"That is S's own IRR, not the rate where the two NPVs are equal."},{"id":"crossover-avg-irr","label":"Averaged the two IRRs (28.57% and 20.00%)","value":24.2857,"check":"((45000/35000-1)+(180000/150000-1))/2*100","explain":"NPV profiles do not cross at the average of the IRRs; the crossover is the IRR of the difference."},{"id":"crossover-added","label":"Added the flows (L + S) instead of subtracting","value":21.6216,"check":"((180000+45000)/(150000+35000)-1)*100","explain":"Adding the flows gives the IRR of a combined project, not of the incremental one."}],"explanation":"The crossover rate is the IRR of the incremental flows. For a one-year pair of projects it reduces to incremental payoff over incremental outlay, minus one.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12, 9-13; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-454","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works compares mutually exclusive Project A (−$50,000; +$38,000, +$25,000, +$8,000) with Project B (−$50,000; +$9,000, +$18,000, +$53,000). The incremental flows A − B are 0, +$29,000, +$7,000 and −$45,000 in years 0 to 3. What is the NPV of A − B at R = 11% per annum?","answer":-1096.13,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"29000/1.11+7000/1.11**2-45000/1.11**3","steps":[{"label":"PV of the year-1 difference","value":26126.13,"check":"29000/1.11","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of the year-2 difference","value":5681.36,"check":"7000/1.11**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of the year-3 difference","value":-32903.61,"check":"-45000/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of (A − B) at 11%","value":-1096.13,"check":"29000/1.11+7000/1.11**2-45000/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PV year 1 = 29,000/1.11 = 26,126.13","PV year 2 = 7,000/1.11² = 5,681.36","PV year 3 = −45,000/1.11³ = −32,903.61","NPV(A − B) = 0 + 26,126.13 + 5,681.36 − 32,903.61 = −$1,096.13 (unrounded arithmetic)","A negative NPV for A − B means NPV(A) < NPV(B): at 11% (below the 13.08% crossover) B is the better project."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 29000, C02 = 7000, C03 = −45000, NPV, I = 11, CPT → −1,096.13"],"errors":[{"id":"subtracted-wrong-order","label":"Computed B − A instead of A − B","value":1096.13,"check":"-(29000/1.11+7000/1.11**2-45000/1.11**3)","explain":"The question asks for A − B; reversing it flips the sign (although the decision is the same)."},{"id":"cf0-as-period-1","label":"Treated the year-0 difference as a period-1 flow","value":-987.5,"check":"(29000/1.11+7000/1.11**2-45000/1.11**3)/1.11","explain":"The first difference is at time 0 (here it is zero); every flow was pushed out a year in this error."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Added the differences without discounting","value":-9000.0,"check":"0+29000+7000-45000","explain":"At R = 11% each difference must be discounted before adding."}],"explanation":"NPV of the difference equals NPV(A) − NPV(B). Its sign tells you which project is better at that rate, and it crosses zero at the crossover rate.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12; L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-455","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fraser Valley Berry Co. compares Plan E (−$60,000; +$39,000, +$31,000, +$11,000) with Plan L (−$60,000; +$5,000, +$28,000, +$58,000). The IRRs are 20.50% for E and 17.72% for L. What is the crossover rate?","options":["20.50%","17.72%","13.24%","19.11%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The incremental flows E − L are 0, +34,000, +3,000, −47,000, and their IRR is 13.2445%. Check: NPV of the difference at 13.244485% = $0.0000, i.e. zero to the cent. 20.50% and 17.72% are the projects' own IRRs, and 19.11% is the average of the two IRRs, which has no special meaning.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-456","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works compares mutually exclusive Project A (−$50,000; +$38,000, +$25,000, +$8,000) with Project B (−$50,000; +$9,000, +$18,000, +$53,000). The IRRs are 25.84% for A and 20.65% for B. What is the crossover rate?","options":["22.63%","13.08%","23.25%","25.84%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The incremental flows A − B are 0, +29,000, +7,000, −45,000, and their IRR is 13.0825%. Check: NPV of the difference at 13.082548% = $0.0001, i.e. zero to the cent. The wrong answers are the IRR of A + B (22.63%, adding instead of subtracting), the average of the IRRs (23.25%), and A's own IRR (25.84%).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-457","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Wind Farms Ltd. compares Project S (−$30,000; +$14,000 in years 1 to 3; IRR 18.91%) with Project L (−$110,000; +$46,000 in years 1 to 3; IRR 12.26%). What is the crossover rate?","options":["15.58%","13.70%","12.26%","9.70%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The incremental flows L − S are −80,000, +32,000, +32,000, +32,000, an annuity whose IRR is 9.7010% (annuity factor 2.5). Check: NPV of the difference at 9.701026% = −$0.0004, i.e. zero to the cent. 15.58% is the average of the IRRs, 13.70% the IRR of L + S, and 12.26% L's own IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-12"},{"id":"fin201-q09-458","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two conventional projects have NPV profiles that cross once at a positive rate. Project X has the higher NPV at low discount rates, and Project Y has the higher NPV at high discount rates. Which statement about their IRRs and cash-flow timing is typical?","options":["X has the lower IRR and its cash flows come later or are larger overall","X has the higher IRR and its cash flows come sooner","X and Y must have the same IRR","X has the lower IRR because its cash flows are smaller overall"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Back-loaded or larger cash flows make a steeper NPV profile: the project is worth more at low R but loses value faster as R rises, so it ends up with the lower IRR. The project that wins at high R has the flatter profile and the higher IRR.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-459","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about the crossover rate is NOT correct?","options":["It is the IRR of the difference between the two projects' cash flows","It equals the average of the two projects' IRRs","At that discount rate the two projects have the same NPV","Below it and above it, the ranking of the two projects by NPV is reversed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The crossover has to be found from the incremental cash flows; there is no shortcut through the two IRRs. The other three statements describe it correctly (for profiles that cross once).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-460","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The NPVs of two mutually exclusive projects (F and G) are computed at several rates. At 10%: F $9,339, G $11,262. At 12%: F $7,364, G $8,069. At 14%: F $5,489, G $5,079. At 16%: F $3,706, G $2,277. Between which two rates does the crossover lie?","options":["Between 10% and 12%","Between 12% and 14%","Between 14% and 16%","Below 10%, because G leads at 10%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The crossover is where the NPV gap changes sign. G is ahead at 10% and 12% (gaps +1,923 and +705) and F is ahead at 14% (gap −409), so the sign change, and therefore the crossover, lies between 12% and 14% (the exact rate is 13.24%).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-461","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"For mutually exclusive Projects S and L, the IRR of the incremental flows (L − S) is 9.70%. Project L is larger. The firm's required return is 8%. What should the firm do?","options":["Take S: the incremental IRR is below the IRRs of both projects","Take S: its own IRR is higher than the incremental IRR","Take both, because the incremental IRR is positive","Take L: the incremental IRR of 9.70% exceeds the required return of 8%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The incremental project L − S earns 9.70% on the extra money invested in L. If that exceeds R, the extra investment is worthwhile and L is better; if R were above 9.70%, S would be better. Comparing the incremental IRR with the individual IRRs is not the test.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s34"},{"id":"fin201-q09-462","concept":"fin201-c09-crossover","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Project X costs $50,000 and returns $30,000 in each of years 1 and 2. Project Y also costs $50,000 and returns $25,000 in each of years 1 and 2. The firm must pick one. What can be said about a crossover rate?","options":["There is none at a positive rate: X beats Y at every discount rate","The crossover is at the IRR of X because X has the larger flows","The crossover is the average of the two IRRs","The crossover is at 0% because the outlays are equal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The incremental flows X − Y are 0, +5,000, +5,000: all non-negative, so they have no IRR. X dominates Y: it is better in every year and NPV(X) > NPV(Y) at every R, so there is no ranking conflict.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-463","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The profitability index (PI) is the present value of a project's cash _____ divided by the present value of its cash outflows.","options":["profits","inflows","tax shields","dividends"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PI = PV of cash inflows / PV of cash outflows. It is also called the benefit/cost ratio, and for a conventional project the outflow is the initial investment.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s27"},{"id":"fin201-q09-464","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which expression is the professor's profitability index (the usual, 1-based definition)?","options":["NPV ÷ initial outlay (value added per dollar)","Initial outlay ÷ PV of cash inflows (cost per dollar of benefit)","NPV ÷ PV of cash inflows (value added per dollar of benefit)","PV of cash inflows ÷ PV of cash outflows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The lecture defines PI = PV of inflows / PV of outflows and shows PI = 1 + NPV/initial outlay. NPV/outlay is the 0-based alternative, which ranks projects the same way but equals PI − 1 (easily mixed up with the accept cut-off of 1).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s27-31; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-465","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A manager says Project Z 'returns $1.18 of present value for every dollar we invest in it'. Which decision measure is she quoting?","options":["The internal rate of return","The net present value","The accounting rate of return","The profitability index (benefit/cost ratio)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PI is the 'bang for the buck': present value of benefits per dollar of cost, 1.18 here, meaning NPV is about 18% of the outlay. IRR is a percentage rate, NPV is a dollar amount, and ARR uses accounting profit.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s27-30; TB 9.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-466","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has a profitability index of 0.96 at the firm's required return. What does this say?","options":["It returns 96% per year, so it should be accepted if the required return is below 96%","NPV is positive because the PI is positive","Each dollar invested returns only 96 cents of present value, so NPV is negative and the project should be rejected","The project breaks even in present-value terms and the firm is indifferent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PI < 1 means PV of inflows is less than PV of outflows, so NPV < 0 and the project destroys value. A PI of 0.96 is not a rate of return, and a PI is always positive even when NPV is negative. Indifference occurs at PI = 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-467","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a required return of 9% per annum a conventional project has a profitability index of exactly 1.00. What are its NPV and IRR?","options":["NPV = $0 and IRR = 0%","NPV = $0 and IRR = 9%","NPV = 1 and IRR = 9%","NPV is negative and IRR is above 9%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PI = 1 means PV of inflows equals the outlay, so NPV = 0. NPV is zero exactly when the discount rate equals the IRR, so the IRR is 9%.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28-29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-468","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has an initial outlay of $120,000 and the present value of its later cash inflows is $132,000. What are its profitability index and NPV?","options":["PI = 0.10 and NPV = $12,000","PI = 0.91 and NPV = $12,000","PI = 1.10 and NPV = $132,000","PI = 1.10 and NPV = $12,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PI = PV of inflows / outlay = 132,000/120,000 = 1.10, and NPV = 132,000 − 120,000 = $12,000. 0.10 is the 0-based figure (NPV/outlay), 0.91 inverts the ratio (120/132), and $132,000 is the PV of inflows, not the NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28-29; SM 9-15"},{"id":"fin201-q09-469","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the 1-based PI (PV inflows ÷ PV outflows) and the 0-based version (NPV ÷ initial outlay) compare?","options":["The 0-based figure is the 1-based figure minus 1; they rank projects identically but have different accept cut-offs (1 and 0)","They give the same numbers, because both are called the profitability index","The 0-based figure is always larger, so it accepts more projects","They rank projects differently, so a firm must pick one definition and stay with it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lecture warns there are two definitions and that adding or subtracting one gives the same rankings. The trap is comparing a 0-based figure with the cut-off of 1: a 0-based 0.12 is a good project, not a reject.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s31"},{"id":"fin201-q09-470","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the PI rule always give the same accept or reject decision as the NPV rule for a conventional project?","options":["Because PI uses the project's IRR as its discount rate, so the two rules always agree","PI > 1 exactly when PV of inflows exceeds the outlay, which is exactly when NPV > 0","Because PI is simply the NPV expressed as a percentage of the PV of inflows","Because both rules ignore the size of the project"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NPV = PV of inflows − outlay, and PI = PV of inflows / outlay. NPV is positive when the PV exceeds the outlay, and PI is above 1 under exactly the same condition. They differ only in how they RANK projects.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-471","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A conventional project (a single outlay at time 0, inflows afterwards) with a profitability index of 1.15 has an NPV equal to 15% of its initial outlay.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PI = 1 + NPV/outlay, so NPV = outlay × (PI − 1) = 0.15 × outlay. For example, an outlay of $60,000 gives an NPV of $9,000.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28"},{"id":"fin201-q09-472","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT true of the profitability index?","options":["It is also called the benefit/cost ratio","A value above 1 means the project has a positive NPV (conventional flows)","It measures the value created per dollar invested","It is expressed in dollars, so it shows how much wealth the project creates"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PI is a ratio, so it says nothing about the dollar size of the NPV. That is why it can rank mutually exclusive projects badly. The other statements are standard.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s30; TB 9.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-473","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Brewing's project costs $26,000 and returns $11,500, $12,400 and $9,800 in years 1 to 3. Its PI is 1.16 at a required return of 6%, 1.04 at 12% and 0.99 at 15%. At which of these required returns should the firm accept it?","options":["At 15% only","At all three rates","At 6% only, because it has the highest PI there","At 6% and 12% only"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Accept when PI > 1. The PI falls as R rises and crosses 1 at the IRR (14.57%), so the project is acceptable at 6% and 12% but not at 15%. Accepting at 6% only treats the highest PI as the only acceptable one.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-474","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-npv-steps"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Brewing's project costs $26,000 now and returns $11,500, $12,400 and $9,800 at the end of years 1, 2 and 3. The required return is 9% per annum. What is the profitability index? (Two decimals.)","answer":1.1,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(11500/1.09+12400/1.09**2+9800/1.09**3)/26000","steps":[{"label":"PV of the three inflows","value":28554.69,"check":"11500/1.09+12400/1.09**2+9800/1.09**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PI = PV of inflows ÷ 26,000","value":1.098,"check":"(11500/1.09+12400/1.09**2+9800/1.09**3)/26000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of inflows = 11,500/1.09 + 12,400/1.09² + 9,800/1.09³ = 10,550.46 + 10,436.83 + 7,567.40 = 28,554.69","PI = 28,554.69/26,000 = 1.098, i.e. 1.10","PI > 1 (NPV = +$2,554.69), so accept."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 11500, C02 = 12400, C03 = 9800, NPV, I = 9, CPT → 28,554.69","÷ 26000 = 1.0983"],"errors":[{"id":"pi-zero-based","label":"Reported the 0-based index NPV ÷ outlay","value":0.1,"check":"(11500/1.09+12400/1.09**2+9800/1.09**3)/26000-1","explain":"That is PI − 1. The usual PI divides the PV of inflows by the outlay and is compared with 1."},{"id":"pi-inverted","label":"Divided the outlay by the PV of inflows","value":0.91,"check":"26000/(11500/1.09+12400/1.09**2+9800/1.09**3)","explain":"PI puts the benefits on top: PV of inflows over outlay."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Used undiscounted inflows","value":1.3,"check":"(11500+12400+9800)/26000","explain":"The inflows are in years 1 to 3 and must be discounted at 9% first."}],"explanation":"PI is PV of inflows divided by the outlay. Enter the inflows with CF0 = 0 so that the calculator's NPV is a pure present value, then divide.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-15; L05A s27"},{"id":"fin201-q09-475","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-npv-steps"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Muskoka Marine plans a boatyard. It spends $80,000 now and a further $35,000 at the end of year 1. Cash inflows of $60,000, $65,000 and $70,000 follow at the end of years 2, 3 and 4. The required return is 10% per annum. Using PI = PV of inflows ÷ PV of outflows, what is the PI? (Two decimals.)","answer":1.31,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(60000/1.1**2+65000/1.1**3+70000/1.1**4)/(80000+35000/1.1)","steps":[{"label":"PV of inflows","value":146233.18,"check":"60000/1.1**2+65000/1.1**3+70000/1.1**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of outflows","value":111818.18,"check":"80000+35000/1.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PI","value":1.308,"check":"(60000/1.1**2+65000/1.1**3+70000/1.1**4)/(80000+35000/1.1)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of inflows = 60,000/1.1² + 65,000/1.1³ + 70,000/1.1⁴ = 49,586.78 + 48,835.46 + 47,810.94 = 146,233.18","PV of outflows = 80,000 + 35,000/1.1 = 80,000 + 31,818.18 = 111,818.18","PI = 146,233.18 / 111,818.18 = 1.308, i.e. 1.31","PI > 1 and NPV = $34,415.00 > 0, so accept."],"keystrokes":["Inflows: CF0 = 0, C01 = 0, C02 = 60000, C03 = 65000, C04 = 70000, NPV, I = 10, CPT → 146,233.18","Outflows: 80000 + 35000 ÷ 1.1 = 111,818.18","146233.18 ÷ 111818.18 = 1.3078"],"errors":[{"id":"pi-later-outflow-ignored","label":"Counted only the $80,000 initial outlay as the outflow","value":1.83,"check":"(60000/1.1**2+65000/1.1**3+70000/1.1**4)/80000","explain":"The year-1 spending is also a cost; the PV of ALL outflows goes in the denominator."},{"id":"pi-netted","label":"Subtracted the PV of the year-1 spending from the inflows","value":1.43,"check":"((60000/1.1**2+65000/1.1**3+70000/1.1**4)-35000/1.1)/80000","explain":"Netting the later cost against the inflows changes the ratio. The lecture definition keeps inflows on top and ALL outflows below."},{"id":"pi-zero-based","label":"Reported PI − 1","value":0.31,"check":"(60000/1.1**2+65000/1.1**3+70000/1.1**4)/(80000+35000/1.1)-1","explain":"That is the 0-based figure, NPV ÷ PV of outflows."}],"explanation":"With a second outlay after time 0, the denominator is the PV of all outflows. Discount the year-1 cost one period; the inflows start in year 2 and are discounted two to four periods.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s27; TB 9.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-476","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has an initial outlay of $84,000 and an NPV of $17,640 at the firm's required return. What is its profitability index? (Two decimals.)","answer":1.21,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"1+17640/84000","steps":[{"label":"NPV ÷ outlay","value":0.21,"check":"17640/84000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PI = 1 + NPV ÷ outlay","value":1.21,"check":"1+17640/84000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PI = PV of inflows / outlay = (outlay + NPV)/outlay = 1 + NPV/outlay","NPV/outlay = 17,640/84,000 = 0.21","PI = 1 + 0.21 = 1.21"],"keystrokes":["17640 ÷ 84000 = 0.21; + 1 = 1.21"],"errors":[{"id":"pi-zero-based","label":"Reported NPV ÷ outlay (0.21)","value":0.21,"check":"17640/84000","explain":"That is the 0-based index. Add 1 to get the usual PI."},{"id":"pi-npv-over-pv","label":"Divided the NPV by the PV of inflows","value":0.17,"check":"17640/(84000+17640)","explain":"PV of inflows is outlay + NPV = $101,640, and PI puts it on top of the outlay, not under the NPV."},{"id":"pi-inverted","label":"Divided the outlay by the PV of inflows","value":0.83,"check":"84000/(84000+17640)","explain":"The ratio is the other way round: benefits over costs."}],"explanation":"Since NPV = PV of inflows − outlay, PV of inflows = outlay + NPV and PI = 1 + NPV/outlay.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28; SM 9-15"},{"id":"fin201-q09-477","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works is considering a project that costs $28,000 and returns $9,000, $10,500, $8,200 and $6,000 at the end of years 1 to 4. The required return is 16% per annum. What is the profitability index? (Two decimals.)","answer":0.86,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(9000/1.16+10500/1.16**2+8200/1.16**3+6000/1.16**4)/28000","steps":[{"label":"PV of the four inflows","value":24128.97,"check":"9000/1.16+10500/1.16**2+8200/1.16**3+6000/1.16**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PI","value":0.862,"check":"(9000/1.16+10500/1.16**2+8200/1.16**3+6000/1.16**4)/28000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of inflows = 9,000/1.16 + 10,500/1.16² + 8,200/1.16³ + 6,000/1.16⁴ = 24,128.97","PI = 24,128.97/28,000 = 0.862, i.e. 0.86","PI < 1 (NPV = −$3,871.03), so reject."],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 9000, C02 = 10500, C03 = 8200, C04 = 6000, NPV, I = 16, CPT → 24,128.97","÷ 28000 = 0.8617"],"errors":[{"id":"pi-zero-based","label":"Reported the 0-based index PI − 1","value":-0.14,"check":"(9000/1.16+10500/1.16**2+8200/1.16**3+6000/1.16**4)/28000-1","explain":"That is negative here, and the cut-off for the usual PI is 1."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Used undiscounted inflows","value":1.2,"check":"(9000+10500+8200+6000)/28000","explain":"Undiscounted inflows give 1.20 and wrongly suggest acceptance; at 16% the discounted PI is below 1."},{"id":"dropped-final-flow","label":"Dropped the year-4 inflow (three-year horizon)","value":0.74,"check":"(9000/1.16+10500/1.16**2+8200/1.16**3)/28000","explain":"All four inflows count."}],"explanation":"A PI below 1 at the required return means a negative NPV. The project's IRR (about 8.4%) is below 16%, so the two rules agree.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-15; L05A s29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-478","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works can buy a bottler for $45,000. It will return $19,500 at the end of each of the next 3 years, and the required return is 8% per annum. What is the profitability index?","options":["0.12","0.90","1.30","1.12"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PV of inflows = 19,500 × annuity factor(8%, 3) = 19,500 × 2.57710 = 50,253.39; PI = 50,253.39/45,000 = 1.117, i.e. 1.12. 0.12 is the 0-based figure, 0.90 inverts the ratio, and 1.30 divides the undiscounted $58,500 by the outlay.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-479","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kootenay Brewing spends $60,000 now and a further $24,000 at the end of year 2. It receives $30,000 at the end of year 1 and $45,000 at the end of each of years 3 and 4. At R = 12% per annum, using PI = PV of inflows ÷ PV of outflows, what is the PI?","options":["1.46","1.14","1.10","0.10"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PV of inflows = 87,414.14; PV of outflows = 60,000 + 24,000/1.12² = 79,132.65; PI = 1.105, i.e. 1.10. 1.46 ignores the year-2 spending, 1.14 nets that spending against the inflows, and 0.10 is the 0-based figure.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s27"},{"id":"fin201-q09-480","concept":"fin201-c09-pi","also":["fin201-c09-irr-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a conventional project, what happens to the PI as the required return R rises, and what is the PI when R equals the IRR?","options":["PI rises as R rises, and it equals 0 when R equals the IRR","PI is unaffected by R, and it equals the IRR when R equals the IRR","PI falls as R rises, and it equals 1 when R equals the IRR","PI falls as R rises, and it equals 0 when R equals the IRR"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A higher R shrinks the PV of the inflows, so PI falls. At R = IRR the PV of inflows equals the outlay, so NPV = 0 and PI = 1 (a 0-based version would show 0, which is where the wrong options come from).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28-29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-481","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects: X has a PI of 1.34 and an NPV of $6,100; Y has a PI of 1.21 and an NPV of $13,100. Which rule picks which project?","options":["PI picks Y and NPV picks X","PI picks X and NPV picks Y","Both rules pick X","Both rules pick Y"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PI ranks by value per dollar invested (X is higher), NPV ranks by total wealth created (Y is higher). Same accept/reject decision, different ranking, so for mutually exclusive projects the NPV rule decides.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s30; SM 9-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-482","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the NPV and PI rules is correct?","options":["They always agree on both the decision and the ranking","They agree on accept or reject but can rank projects differently","They can disagree on whether to accept, but never on the ranking","They disagree on accept or reject, and agree on ranking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PI > 1 exactly when NPV > 0, so decisions on a single project match. Ranking is different: NPV measures dollars, PI measures dollars per dollar invested.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s29-30"},{"id":"fin201-q09-483","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can the PI rule choose the wrong project when two projects are mutually exclusive?","options":["PI ignores the time value of money, so later flows are overvalued","PI uses accounting profit rather than cash flows, like the ARR","PI always favours the larger project, whatever its NPV","PI is a ratio that ignores scale: a small project can have a high PI but a small NPV"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PI divides by the capital invested, so it favours efficient small projects. The wealth the shareholders get is in dollars of NPV, which depends on scale too. PI does discount cash flows, and it can favour either size, though usually the small one.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.6; SM 9-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-484","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Project S costs $8 million and has a present value of $20 million (PI 2.50, NPV $12 million). Project L costs $400 million and has a present value of $520 million (PI 1.30, NPV $120 million). They are mutually exclusive. What should the firm do?","options":["Take S: its PI is nearly twice as large","Take L: it adds ten times as much wealth, even though its PI is lower","Take both, because both PIs exceed 1","Take S: a high PI means a low risk of losing money"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For mutually exclusive projects the highest NPV adds the most value. L's PI is lower because it is large, but its NPV is $120 million against $12 million. The ratio ignores scale.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-485","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about PI and NPV is NOT correct?","options":["PI and NPV can rank mutually exclusive projects differently","NPV = outlay × (PI − 1) for a conventional project","A single conventional project can have a PI above 1 and a negative NPV","PI is most useful for ranking independent projects when funds are limited"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PI > 1 means PV of inflows > outlay, which is the same as NPV > 0, so the two cannot disagree on a single conventional project. The other three statements are standard.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s29-30"},{"id":"fin201-q09-486","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"For two mutually exclusive projects of very different size, the NPV rule is more reliable than the PI rule.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV measures the change in shareholder wealth in dollars. PI measures wealth per dollar invested and can rank the smaller project first even though it adds less value in total.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-487","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":["fin201-c09-capital-rationing"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A company has a fixed capital budget this year and a list of independent projects, each with a positive NPV. Which ranking rule does the professor recommend for choosing among them?","options":["Highest NPV first, until the money runs out","Highest IRR first, until the money runs out","Shortest payback first, until the money runs out","Highest profitability index first, until the money runs out"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With a binding budget the goal is the most NPV per scarce dollar, which is what PI measures. Ranking by raw NPV would favour large projects that use up the budget.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q09-488","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects have the SAME initial outlay. What can be said about their PI and NPV rankings?","options":["They can still disagree, because PI ignores risk","The higher PI has the lower NPV, because of the ratio","They agree only if the lives are equal","They must agree, because NPV = outlay × (PI − 1) and the outlay is the same"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With equal outlays, NPV is a constant times (PI − 1), so a higher PI means a higher NPV. Disagreements arise only when the outlays (the scale) differ.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28-30"},{"id":"fin201-q09-489","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Muskoka Marine has two mutually exclusive projects at R = 9% per annum. Project X costs $18,000 and returns $8,500 at the end of each of the next 3 years. Project Y costs $70,000 and returns $32,000 at the end of each of the next 3 years. Compute each PI and decide. (Final answer: the NPV of the project the firm should choose.)","answer":11001.43,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-70000","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (9%, 3 years)","value":2.53129,"check":"(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PI of Project X","value":1.195,"check":"8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09/18000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PI of Project Y","value":1.157,"check":"32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09/70000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of the NPV-preferred project (Y)","value":11001.43,"check":"32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-70000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor (9%, 3) = 2.53129","PI of X = 8,500 × 2.53129/18,000 = 1.195; NPV of X = $3,516.00","PI of Y = 32,000 × 2.53129/70,000 = 1.157; NPV of Y = $11,001.43","PI prefers X (1.195 > 1.157) but NPV prefers Y ($11,001.43 > $3,516.00). The projects are mutually exclusive, so the NPV rule decides: choose Y, NPV = $11,001.43."],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 9, PMT = 32000, CPT PV → 81,001.43; − 70000 = 11,001.43"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-pi","label":"Reported the NPV of Project X (the higher-PI project)","value":3516.0,"check":"8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-18000","explain":"X has the higher PI, but Y creates more wealth and the projects are mutually exclusive."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 4-year annuity factor for Y","value":33671.04,"check":"32000*(1-1/1.09**4)/0.09-70000","explain":"There are three payments, so N = 3."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Added the undiscounted inflows","value":26000.0,"check":"32000*3-70000","explain":"The three inflows must be discounted at 9%."}],"explanation":"Both PIs exceed 1, so both projects are acceptable on their own; the question is which one to take. The NPV rule picks the larger-scale project Y.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-16; L05A s30"},{"id":"fin201-q09-490","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":["fin201-c09-ranking-conflict"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Using the same two mutually exclusive projects (X: cost $18,000, $8,500 a year for 3 years; Y: cost $70,000, $32,000 a year for 3 years; R = 9% per annum), how many dollars of NPV does the firm give up by choosing the project with the higher PI instead of the one with the higher NPV?","answer":7485.42,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-70000)-(8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-18000)","steps":[{"label":"NPV of Project X","value":3516.0,"check":"8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-18000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Project Y","value":11001.43,"check":"32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-70000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV given up","value":7485.42,"check":"(32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-70000)-(8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-18000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV of X = 8,500 × 2.531295 − 18,000 = $3,516.00","NPV of Y = 32,000 × 2.531295 − 70,000 = $11,001.43","Choosing X (higher PI) gives up $11,001.43 − $3,516.00 = $7,485.42."],"keystrokes":["X: PMT = 8500, N = 3, I/Y = 9, CPT PV → 21,516.00; − 18000 = 3,516.00","Y: 81,001.43 − 70000 = 11,001.43","11,001.43 − 3,516.00 = 7,485.42"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-pi","label":"Reported the NPV of the higher-PI project","value":3516.0,"check":"8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-18000","explain":"That is what the PI choice delivers, not what it gives up."},{"id":"subtracted-wrong-order","label":"Subtracted NPV(Y) from NPV(X)","value":-7485.42,"check":"(8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-18000)-(32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-70000)","explain":"The amount given up is positive: the better NPV minus the NPV actually earned."},{"id":"pv-for-npv","label":"Used the PV of inflows instead of NPV for each project","value":59485.42,"check":"32000*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09-8500*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09","explain":"The PV of inflows does not subtract the cost; the outlays differ by $52,000, which is why this figure is far too high."}],"explanation":"The ratio ranking sacrifices wealth whenever the projects have different scales.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 9-16"},{"id":"fin201-q09-491","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects: Small costs $25,000 and has a PI of 1.60. Large costs $120,000 and has a PI of 1.30. By how many dollars does the NPV of the Large project exceed the NPV of the Small project?","answer":21000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"120000*(1.30-1)-25000*(1.60-1)","steps":[{"label":"NPV of Small = outlay × (PI − 1)","value":15000.0,"check":"25000*(1.60-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Large = outlay × (PI − 1)","value":36000.0,"check":"120000*(1.30-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV(Large) − NPV(Small)","value":21000.0,"check":"120000*(1.30-1)-25000*(1.60-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV = outlay × (PI − 1) for a conventional project.","Small: 25,000 × 0.60 = $15,000. Large: 120,000 × 0.30 = $36,000.","Large exceeds Small by 36,000 − 15,000 = $21,000, even though PI ranks Small first."],"keystrokes":["25000 × 0.60 = 15,000","120000 × 0.30 = 36,000","36,000 − 15,000 = 21,000"],"errors":[{"id":"pv-for-npv","label":"Used PI × outlay (the PV of inflows) instead of NPV","value":116000.0,"check":"120000*1.30-25000*1.60","explain":"PI × outlay is the PV of inflows ($156,000 and $40,000), not the NPV. NPV subtracts the outlay."},{"id":"subtracted-wrong-order","label":"Subtracted NPV(Large) from NPV(Small)","value":-21000.0,"check":"25000*(1.60-1)-120000*(1.30-1)","explain":"The question asks how much LARGER the Large NPV is."}],"explanation":"Turning a PI back into an NPV needs the outlay: NPV = outlay × (PI − 1). A lower PI on a much larger outlay can still mean far more value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28-30"},{"id":"fin201-q09-492","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive projects have these outlays and present values of inflows: Project X, outlay $400,000, PV $520,000; Project Y, outlay $8,000, PV $20,000. What is the NPV of the project the firm should choose?","options":["$12,000","$108,000","$520,000","$120,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NPV(X) = 520,000 − 400,000 = $120,000 and NPV(Y) = 20,000 − 8,000 = $12,000. Y has the higher PI (2.50 against 1.30), but X has the higher NPV and the rule for mutually exclusive projects is NPV. $12,000 is Y's NPV, $108,000 is the gap between the two NPVs, and $520,000 is a PV, not an NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 9.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-493","concept":"fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project has an initial outlay of $400,000 and the present value of its inflows is $520,000. What are its PI and its NPV as a fraction of the outlay?","options":["PI = 0.30; NPV is 0.30 of the outlay","PI = 0.77; NPV is 0.23 of the outlay","PI = 1.30; NPV is 0.30 of the outlay","PI = 1.30; NPV is 1.30 of the outlay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PI = 520,000/400,000 = 1.30 and NPV = 120,000 = 0.30 × outlay. 0.30 as a PI is the 0-based figure, 0.77 inverts the ratio and 0.23 divides the NPV by the PV of inflows.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s28"},{"id":"fin201-q09-494","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two projects are independent when:","options":["undertaking one rules out undertaking the other","the cash flows of one depend on whether the other is undertaken","undertaking one has no effect on the cash flows or value of the other","they are financed from the same source of funds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Independent projects can be evaluated one at a time: accept each one whose NPV is positive. If taking one changes the value of another they are dependent, and if taking one rules out the other they are mutually exclusive.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-495","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A landowner can build either a hotel or a condominium tower on one downtown lot, but not both. The two proposals are:","options":["mutually exclusive","independent","dependent but not mutually exclusive","a capital rationing problem"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Only one of them can be undertaken, so they are mutually exclusive, and the choice is made by comparing NPVs. Using the same lot rules the other one out, which is different from merely affecting its value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-496","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A café chain's new drive-through window would raise the sales of its existing lunch counters. The firm can still build the window without changing anything else. The relationship between the two projects is:","options":["independent","dependent","mutually exclusive","unrelated because no cash changes hands"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Taking the drive-through changes the value of the lunch-counter business, so they are dependent. Both can be undertaken, which is why they are not mutually exclusive.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-497","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Laurentian Forestry can log its stand this year or wait five years for larger trees. It will log only once. The choice between those two dates is an example of a:","options":["mutually exclusive timing decision","pair of independent projects","dependent project","capital rationing decision"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lecture lists timing decisions (when to replace, retire or close) as mutually exclusive: each date is a separate project and only one can be chosen. The usual approach is to pick the date with the maximum NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32, s35"},{"id":"fin201-q09-498","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do dependent projects differ from mutually exclusive projects?","options":["Dependent projects can never be taken together; mutually exclusive projects always must be","Dependent projects have different discount rates; mutually exclusive projects have the same rate","With dependent projects, taking one changes the value of the other but both can be taken; with mutually exclusive projects only one can be taken","There is no difference; both terms mean the projects share a budget"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dependence is about a change in value; exclusivity is about a choice between alternatives. A project can complement another (positive dependence), substitute for it, or rule it out entirely.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-499","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of mutually exclusive projects?","options":["Replacing a delivery truck now or replacing it next year","Choosing one of three competing designs for the same warehouse","Closing a mine this year or closing it in three years","Opening a bakery in Red Deer and a bakery in Moose Jaw whose sales do not affect each other"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Two stores whose cash flows do not affect each other are independent. The other three are choices among alternatives for the same job: replacement timing, design choice and closure timing.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32, s35"},{"id":"fin201-q09-500","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the NPV rule for independent projects 'accept each project with NPV > 0', while for mutually exclusive projects it is 'accept the project with the highest NPV'?","options":["Independent projects are always riskier, so they need a stricter rule","Mutually exclusive projects always have negative NPVs, so only one is kept","For independent projects the firm uses IRR instead of NPV to decide","Independent projects are judged alone; exclusive projects compete, so only the best can be taken"],"answer":3,"explanation":"If projects do not interfere, every positive-NPV project adds wealth and should be taken. If they are alternatives, taking the second-best means giving up the best, so the highest NPV is the right pick.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19, s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-501","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"Three independent projects have NPVs of +$40,000, +$25,000 and −$10,000. The firm has plenty of funds. Which should it undertake?","options":["Only the +$40,000 project, since it has the highest NPV","The +$40,000 and +$25,000 projects","All three projects","None, since one NPV is negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent projects are judged one at a time with no budget limit, so every positive-NPV project is accepted and the negative-NPV one is rejected.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-502","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two mutually exclusive environmental clean-up options are legally required. Option 1 has an NPV of −$2.4 million and Option 2 has an NPV of −$1.9 million. What should the firm do?","options":["Choose Option 2: when a project is forced, take the least negative NPV","Reject both, because both NPVs are negative","Choose Option 1, because its cost is larger","Choose neither until the required return falls below the IRR"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lecture notes the NPV rule says to take the highest NPV 'or least negative if you must'. Doing nothing is not allowed here, so the cheapest option in present-value terms is best.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s17, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q09-503","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"The choice between keeping an old machine and replacing it with a new one is treated as a pair of mutually exclusive projects.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Only one machine will do the job, so keeping and replacing are alternatives: compare their NPVs (with equal lives, or equalised lives if they differ) and pick the better.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32, s35"},{"id":"fin201-q09-504","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Laurentian Forestry can log its stand now for net proceeds of $1,200,000, in 3 years for $1,620,000, or in 5 years for $2,050,000. The choices are mutually exclusive, and the required return is 9% per annum. What is the NPV of the best choice? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":1332359.34,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2050000/1.09**5","steps":[{"label":"NPV of logging in 3 years","value":1250937.24,"check":"1620000/1.09**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of logging in 5 years","value":1332359.34,"check":"2050000/1.09**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["NPV(now) = $1,200,000.00","NPV(3 years) = 1,620,000/1.09³ = $1,250,937.24","NPV(5 years) = 2,050,000/1.09⁵ = $1,332,359.34","Timing decisions are mutually exclusive: choose the maximum NPV, which is waiting 5 years, NPV = $1,332,359.34."],"keystrokes":["1.09 y^x 5 = 1.538624; 2050000 ÷ 1.538624 = 1,332,359.34","1.09 y^x 3 = 1.295029; 1620000 ÷ 1.295029 = 1,250,937.24"],"errors":[{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Picked the largest undiscounted amount","value":2050000.0,"check":"2050000","explain":"The $2,050,000 arrives in 5 years and must be discounted before comparing with proceeds now."},{"id":"timing-wrong-choice","label":"Chose the 3-year option","value":1250937.24,"check":"1620000/1.09**3","explain":"Both future dates beat logging now, but 5 years has the higher present value."},{"id":"horizon-off-by-one","label":"Discounted the 5-year proceeds six years","value":1222348.02,"check":"2050000/1.09**6","explain":"Year-5 proceeds are discounted five periods."}],"explanation":"A timing choice is a set of mutually exclusive projects: convert each to a present value and take the maximum.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s35"},{"id":"fin201-q09-505","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":["fin201-c09-npv-rule"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cascade Logistics can build a depot (Project A: $150,000 now, then $60,000 a year for 3 years). A tracking system (Project B: $40,000 now, then $30,000 a year for 3 years) is worth those flows only if the depot is built. At R = 10% per annum, what is the NPV of undertaking the depot and the system together? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":33816.68,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"60000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-150000+30000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-40000","steps":[{"label":"NPV of the depot (A)","value":-788.88,"check":"60000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-150000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of the system (B), given A is built","value":34605.56,"check":"30000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-40000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of the package A + B","value":33816.68,"check":"60000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-150000+30000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-40000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor (10%, 3) = 2.48685","NPV(A) = 60,000 × 2.486852 − 150,000 = −$788.88","NPV(B | A) = 30,000 × 2.486852 − 40,000 = $34,605.56","Package = −$788.88 + $34,605.56 = $33,816.68","B is dependent on A, so judge them as a package. A alone looks slightly negative, but the package is clearly positive: accept both."],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 10, PMT = 60000, CPT PV → 149,211.12; − 150000 = −788.88","PMT = 30000 → 74,605.56; − 40000 = 34,605.56","−788.88 + 34,605.56 = 33,816.68"],"errors":[{"id":"depends-ignored","label":"Evaluated only the depot (rejecting it on its own)","value":-788.88,"check":"60000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-150000","explain":"The system adds value only because the depot exists. Dependent projects must be evaluated together."},{"id":"depends-ignored","label":"Counted only the system","value":34605.56,"check":"30000*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-40000","explain":"The depot's own NPV (slightly negative) must be included in the package."},{"id":"no-discounting","label":"Added the undiscounted flows of both projects","value":80000.0,"check":"60000*3-150000+30000*3-40000","explain":"Each year's flow must be discounted at 10%."}],"explanation":"When the value of one project depends on another, evaluate the combination as a single project. Here the depot alone is marginally negative but is worth building because it enables the system.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-506","concept":"fin201-c09-project-relationships","also":["fin201-c09-capital-rationing"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A manufacturer has three possible uses for one vacant plant: warehouse it, lease it out, or retool it for a new product. It can pick only one. This set of projects is:","options":["mutually exclusive, so it should choose the one with the highest NPV","independent, so it should accept all those with a positive NPV","dependent, so it should evaluate them as a package","subject to capital rationing, so it should rank them by PI"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Competing uses for the same asset are mutually exclusive. Capital rationing is about a shortage of funds across independent projects, not about alternatives for one asset.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s32"},{"id":"fin201-q09-507","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-project-relationships"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An example of capital rationing is:","options":["Rejecting a positive NPV project because of a lack of funds","Stopping analysing new projects while market conditions are adverse","Optimising the amount of cash that the company must carry","Dividing capital between several projects","None of the above"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capital rationing means passing up positive-NPV projects because funds are limited. Pausing project analysis, managing cash balances and dividing capital among projects are all ordinary financial activities, not rationing.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-508","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Capital rationing exists when a firm:","options":["has more projects than it has managers to review","has a limit on the funds available and must pass up some positive-NPV projects","chooses to reject projects with negative NPVs","pays out most of its earnings as dividends"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The defining feature is that a funds constraint, not the project's quality, forces the firm to reject positive-NPV projects. Rejecting negative-NPV projects is just the NPV rule working.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q09-509","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm has five independent projects, all with positive NPVs, but its board has capped this year's capital spending at $250,000, which covers only three of them. The firm is facing:","options":["capital rationing","mutually exclusive projects","unequal lives","multiple IRRs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent positive-NPV projects, plus a spending cap that stops the firm taking them all, is capital rationing. The projects are not alternatives for the same job, so they are not mutually exclusive.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-510","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of capital rationing?","options":["Rejecting a project because its NPV is −$20,000","Rejecting a project with an NPV of +$20,000 because the budget is used up","Delaying a profitable project because the bank will not lend more this year","Funding only the projects with the highest PIs because the budget is capped"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A project with a negative NPV should be rejected whether or not funds are short; that is the NPV rule, not rationing. Rationing is about positive-NPV projects that cannot be funded.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q5; L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-511","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts soft and hard capital rationing?","options":["Soft rationing applies to negative-NPV projects; hard rationing applies to positive-NPV projects","Soft rationing means funds are unlimited; hard rationing means funds are limited","Soft: an internal spending limit the firm could relax; hard: the firm cannot raise the capital at all","Soft rationing ranks projects by NPV; hard rationing ranks them by payback period"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both kinds leave positive-NPV projects unfunded. With soft rationing the company as a whole is not short of capital, so the practical advice is to maximise NPV within the allocation by ranking on PI. With hard rationing DCF analysis breaks down, because the required return itself becomes ambiguous.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.6; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q09-512","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the profitability index the best way to rank independent projects when capital is rationed?","options":["It ignores the size of the projects, so small and large projects are treated alike","It is the only rule that does not require discounting","It measures value created per dollar of the scarce funds, so the budget is spent where it earns the most","It guarantees that the budget is spent exactly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When money is the constraint you want the most NPV per dollar spent, and PI = 1 + NPV/outlay is that quantity. Taking projects in order of PI until the money runs out is the lecture's rule. It does not guarantee an exact fit, and it does discount.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q09-513","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-pi-vs-npv"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why should a rationed firm NOT simply fund projects in order of NPV?","options":["NPV cannot be calculated when the funds available are limited","NPV favours small projects, so too many of them get funded","Big-NPV projects can eat the budget, giving less total NPV than ranking by NPV per dollar","NPV ignores the time value of money, unlike the PI"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV ignores the amount of capital tied up. A budget spent on one big project may produce less total NPV than the same budget spread over smaller projects with a higher PI. Example: PI-ranking gives $79,500 and NPV-ranking only $64,000 on the same $250,000 budget.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s18, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-514","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"Capital rationing is a problem for independent projects: with a funds limit, the firm takes them in rank order of PI until the money is used up.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With independent projects and unlimited funds the firm would take all positive-NPV projects. The limit forces a ranking, and PI (present value per dollar invested) is the tool.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-515","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm has $250,000 to spend on independent projects. Their PIs are: A 1.38 (cost $100,000), B 1.30 ($80,000), C 1.25 ($70,000), D 1.22 ($120,000), E 1.06 ($50,000). Taking projects in order of PI until the money runs out, which are funded?","options":["A, B and C","A and D","A, B and D","A, B, C and D"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In PI order the firm funds A ($100,000), then B ($80,000), then C ($70,000), which uses exactly $250,000. D (PI 1.22) is the next in line but nothing is left. 'A and D' is what ranking by NPV would give.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-516","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm with a $250,000 budget funds independent projects A, B and C (PIs 1.38, 1.30, 1.25; total cost $250,000) and leaves out D (cost $120,000, PI 1.22, NPV $26,000) and E (cost $50,000, PI 1.06, NPV $3,000). What does this show?","options":["D and E are bad projects and were rightly rejected","Rationing costs the firm $29,000 of NPV that it would have earned with unlimited funds","The firm made an error, because every project with a positive NPV must be funded","D and E have PIs above 1, so the budget limit does not apply to them"],"answer":1,"explanation":"D and E each have a positive NPV, so with enough money the firm would take them. Leaving them out because of the budget is the cost of capital rationing: $26,000 + $3,000 = $29,000 of NPV given up.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; PRAC q5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-517","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm has $250,000 to invest in independent projects with these outlays and present values of inflows: A $100,000 / $138,000; B $80,000 / $104,000; C $70,000 / $87,500; D $120,000 / $146,000; E $50,000 / $53,000. Projects cannot be split. Ranking by PI, what is the total NPV of the projects it funds? (Answer in $.)","answer":79500.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(138000-100000)+(104000-80000)+(87500-70000)","steps":[{"label":"PIs ranked: A 1.38, B 1.30, C 1.25, D 1.22, E 1.06. Outlay of A + B + C","value":250000.0,"check":"100000+80000+70000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of A + B + C","value":79500.0,"check":"(138000-100000)+(104000-80000)+(87500-70000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PI = PV/outlay: A 1.38, B 1.30, C 1.25, D 1.22, E 1.06.","Fund in PI order: A ($100,000), B ($80,000), C ($70,000) = $250,000, the whole budget.","NPVs: A = 38,000, B = 24,000, C = 17,500, total = $79,500."],"keystrokes":["PI: 138000 ÷ 100000 = 1.38, 104000 ÷ 80000 = 1.30, 87500 ÷ 70000 = 1.25 ...","NPV = PV − outlay for A, B and C, then add: 79,500"],"errors":[{"id":"ranked-by-npv","label":"Ranked by NPV (A and D)","value":64000.0,"check":"(138000-100000)+(146000-120000)","explain":"NPV ranking picks A ($38,000) and D ($26,000), using $220,000 and leaving too little for B or C."},{"id":"ignored-budget","label":"Added the NPVs of all five projects","value":108500.0,"check":"(138000-100000)+(104000-80000)+(87500-70000)+(146000-120000)+(53000-50000)","explain":"The budget lets the firm take only $250,000 of projects; the other NPVs are not earned."},{"id":"pv-for-npv","label":"Added the PVs of A, B and C instead of their NPVs","value":329500.0,"check":"138000+104000+87500","explain":"The PVs include the $250,000 invested; NPV is PV minus outlay."}],"explanation":"Rank by PI, fund in order until the budget is gone, and add the NPVs of the funded projects. PI-ranking beats NPV-ranking ($79,500 against $64,000) because it gets the most NPV per scarce dollar.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; PRAC q5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-518","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Using the same data (A $100,000 / $138,000; B $80,000 / $104,000; C $70,000 / $87,500; D $120,000 / $146,000; E $50,000 / $53,000) and a $250,000 budget with PI ranking, how much NPV does rationing cause the firm to give up compared with funding every positive-NPV project?","answer":29000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(146000-120000)+(53000-50000)","steps":[{"label":"NPV of D","value":26000.0,"check":"146000-120000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of E","value":3000.0,"check":"53000-50000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV forgone","value":29000.0,"check":"(146000-120000)+(53000-50000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Projects funded: A, B, C. Projects left out: D and E.","NPV(D) = 146,000 − 120,000 = $26,000; NPV(E) = 53,000 − 50,000 = $3,000.","NPV given up = 26,000 + 3,000 = $29,000 (all five NPVs total $108,500; the funded three total $79,500)."],"keystrokes":["146000 − 120000 = 26,000","53000 − 50000 = 3,000","26,000 + 3,000 = 29,000"],"errors":[{"id":"ranked-by-npv","label":"Took the total NPV of the funded set as the amount given up","value":79500.0,"check":"(138000-100000)+(104000-80000)+(87500-70000)","explain":"That is what the firm earns, not what it forgoes."},{"id":"pv-for-npv","label":"Used the PVs of D and E instead of their NPVs","value":199000.0,"check":"146000+53000","explain":"The forgone value is NPV, not PV; the projects would also cost $170,000."},{"id":"left-out-project-missed","label":"Counted only the unfunded project with the highest PI (D)","value":26000.0,"check":"146000-120000","explain":"E also has a positive NPV and is left out."}],"explanation":"Rationing means leaving out positive-NPV projects; the cost is their combined NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; PRAC q5"},{"id":"fin201-q09-519","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works has $300,000 for independent projects, each paying a level amount at the end of each of the next 4 years; R = 10% per annum. Cost / annual inflow: P $90,000 / $33,000; Q $60,000 / $23,500; R $150,000 / $51,000; S $80,000 / $30,500; T $70,000 / $24,000. Projects cannot be split. Ranking by PI, what is the total NPV of the funded projects? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":51855.06,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(23500+30500+33000+24000)*(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1-(60000+80000+90000+70000)","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (10%, 4 years)","value":3.16987,"check":"(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PI of R, the project left out","value":1.078,"check":"51000*(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1/150000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Total NPV of Q, S, P and T","value":51855.06,"check":"(23500+30500+33000+24000)*(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1-(60000+80000+90000+70000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor (10%, 4) = 3.16987","PIs: Q = 23,500 × 3.16987/60,000 = 1.2415; S = 1.2085; P = 1.1623; T = 1.0868; R = 1.0778.","Fund in PI order: Q ($60,000), S ($80,000), P ($90,000), T ($70,000) = $300,000. R ($150,000) has a positive NPV but is left out.","NPVs: Q = 14,491.84; S = 16,680.90; P = 14,605.56; T = 6,076.77; total = $51,855.06."],"keystrokes":["N = 4, I/Y = 10, PMT = 23500, CPT PV → 74,491.84; − 60000 = 14,491.84 (repeat for S, P, T)","Add the four NPVs → 51,855.06"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-budget","label":"Added the NPVs of all five projects","value":63518.2,"check":"(23500+30500+33000+24000+51000)*(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1-(60000+80000+90000+70000+150000)","explain":"Project R cannot be funded: adding it would need $450,000 against the $300,000 budget."},{"id":"pv-for-npv","label":"Added the PVs of the four funded projects","value":351855.06,"check":"(23500+30500+33000+24000)*(1-1/1.1**4)/0.1","explain":"The PVs include the $300,000 invested; NPV is PV minus outlay."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 3-year annuity factor","value":-23959.43,"check":"(23500+30500+33000+24000)*(1-1/1.1**3)/0.1-(60000+80000+90000+70000)","explain":"There are four annual inflows, so N = 4."}],"explanation":"PI ranking spends the whole $300,000 on four projects. R is a positive-NPV project (NPV $11,663.14) that rationing forces the firm to pass up.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33; TB 11.6"},{"id":"fin201-q09-520","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works' project P costs $90,000 and pays $33,000 at the end of each of the next 4 years. The required return is 10% per annum. What is its PI, the figure used to rank it when funds are rationed?","options":["0.16","1.16","0.86","1.47"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PV of inflows = 33,000 × 3.16987 = 104,605.56; PI = 104,605.56/90,000 = 1.162, i.e. 1.16. 0.16 is the 0-based figure, 0.86 inverts the ratio, and 1.47 forgets to discount the inflows.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s27, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-521","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A rationed firm ranks its projects by PI. Project R costs $150,000 and pays $51,000 a year for 4 years; the required return is 10% per annum. What is R's NPV, which the firm gives up if the budget does not allow it?","options":["$11,663.14","$161,663.14","$54,000.00","$1.08"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV(R) = 51,000 × 3.16987 − 150,000 = $11,663.14. $161,663.14 is the PV of the inflows; $54,000 adds the four payments without discounting (204,000 − 150,000); and 1.08 is R's PI, not a dollar NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-522","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm ranks independent projects by PI: A (cost $100,000, PI 1.38), B ($80,000, 1.30), C ($70,000, 1.25), D ($120,000, 1.22) and E ($50,000, 1.06). With a $250,000 budget it funds A, B and C. If the budget rises to $370,000, which project is funded in addition?","options":["D","E","None, because the PI of D is below that of C","D and E, because the extra money is enough for both"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Next in PI order after C is D ($120,000), which fits exactly in the extra $120,000. E would need a further $50,000, so it is still left out. Ranking continues in PI order, not NPV order, and not by what is cheapest.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-523","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put the steps for choosing projects under capital rationing in order. (a) Fund projects from the top of the list until the budget is used up. (b) Compute each project's PI. (c) Confirm each funded project has NPV > 0. (d) Rank the projects from highest PI to lowest.","options":["d, b, a, c","b, a, d, c","b, d, a, c","a, b, c, d"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Compute PI for every project, rank from highest to lowest, fund in that order until the money runs out, and check that each funded project has a positive NPV (PI > 1).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-524","concept":"fin201-c09-capital-rationing","also":["fin201-c09-pi"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Under capital rationing, which quantity should a firm maximise per dollar of capital spent?","options":["IRR, because it is a percentage return","Payback, because it recovers the money fastest","NPV per dollar invested, which is PI − 1 (so ranking by PI is the same)","Accounting profit per dollar of book assets"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PI = 1 + NPV/outlay, so ranking by PI is ranking by NPV per dollar of outlay. IRR can mislead in rankings, and payback and accounting profit ignore the time value of money.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s30, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q09-525","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The equivalent annual value (EAV) of a project is its NPV divided by the _____ factor for the project's own life and the required return.","options":["perpetuity","growth","annuity","compounding"],"answer":2,"explanation":"EAV = NPV ÷ annuity factor(R, N): it converts the NPV into the level annual amount, for N years at R, that has the same present value. EAA, EAB and EAC are the same idea for annuity, benefit and cost.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-526","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm compares two machines that do the same job. It converts each machine's present value of costs into a level cost per year over that machine's life and picks the machine with the lower figure. This is the:","options":["equivalent annual cost (EAC) method","accounting rate of return method","profitability index method","discounted payback method"],"answer":0,"explanation":"EAC (also called EAV for a cost) puts different lives on a common annual footing. The other three measures do not address unequal lives.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36; S6 s11-14"},{"id":"fin201-q09-527","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the EAV a better basis than the raw NPV for choosing between a 2-year project and a 3-year project that can each be repeated?","options":["The EAV ignores the time value of money, so it is easier to compare","The raw NPV ignores the required return, so it cannot be compared","The EAV is a per-year amount, while the raw NPVs cover different spans of time","The EAV and the raw NPV always rank projects the same way"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A 3-year project's NPV covers three years of service and a 2-year project's NPV covers two, so comparing them is unfair. The EAV spreads each NPV over its own life as an annuity. The raw NPV does use the required return; it simply covers different periods.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-528","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must a firm equalise the lives of mutually exclusive projects before choosing the cheapest one?","options":["Because the shorter project must be replaced sooner, so its costs recur","Because NPV can only be calculated for projects with equal lives","Because the required return is higher for longer projects","Because the longer project always has the higher NPV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both options provide the same service, so to compare like with like, assume each is replaced again and again. EAV, CCR and the LCM chain all do this. NPV can be computed for any life, but raw NPVs of unequal lives mislead.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36; SM 10-27"},{"id":"fin201-q09-529","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements about EAV is NOT correct?","options":["It is also called EAA, and EAB or EAC for benefits or costs","It uses the annuity factor for the project's own life","For two cost projects it favours the one with the lower annual cost","It can only be used when the projects being compared have the same life"],"answer":3,"explanation":"EAV is designed for UNEQUAL lives: each project's NPV is converted to a per-year amount over its own life. For equal lives you can just compare NPVs.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-530","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"When choosing between two cost-only machines with different lives, the firm should pick the one with the lower (less negative) equivalent annual cost.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Equivalent annual cost is the yearly cost of owning and replacing the machine forever. The machine with the lower annual cost gives the same service for less money.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-27; S6 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q09-531","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"At R = 10%, Bus A (a 4-year life) costs −$41,730 per year on an equivalent annual basis and Bus B (a 6-year life) costs −$44,285 per year. Both buses do the same job. What should the transit firm conclude?","options":["Buy Bus B: its figure is larger in absolute terms and so must be better","They are equally attractive, since both are negative","Buy Bus A: its equivalent annual cost is lower","Neither: negative equivalent annual values mean both should be rejected"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For cost projects the EAV is negative and the better option is the one that is less negative. Both options do the same job, so the service is needed either way; the decision is only about which is cheaper per year.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s14; SM 10-28"},{"id":"fin201-q09-532","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Put the steps for comparing two cost projects of different lives with the EAC method in order. (a) Choose the project with the lower EAC. (b) Divide each present value of costs by the annuity factor for that project's own life. (c) Compute each project's present value of costs. (d) Fix the required return R.","options":["c, d, a, b","d, c, b, a","d, b, c, a","c, b, d, a"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fix R, find each project's PV of costs, divide by the annuity factor (R, own N), then compare the yearly costs and pick the lowest.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s19, s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-533","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A machine with a 5-year life has a present value of costs of −$11,706 at R = 9%. To find its EAC, which annuity factor should be used?","options":["The 3-year factor at 9%, because the other machine lasts 3 years","The 5-year factor at 0%, which is just 5","The 5-year factor at 9%, (1 − 1/1.09⁵)/0.09 = 3.88965","The 9-year factor at 5%, because the rate and life can be swapped"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each project's EAC uses its OWN life N and the required return R. Using another project's life, ignoring R, or swapping N and R all give wrong results.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q09-534","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Seeds Co. can buy Machine X for $5,800 with operating costs of $1,100 at the end of each of 3 years, no salvage. The required return is 9% per annum. What is the equivalent annual value (EAV) of the machine? (Enter a negative number, in $, to the cent.)","answer":-3391.32,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (9%, 3 years)","value":2.53129,"check":"(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of costs","value":-8584.42,"check":"-(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"EAV = PV of costs ÷ annuity factor","value":-3391.32,"check":"-(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor = (1 − 1/1.09³)/0.09 = 2.53129","PV of costs = −(5,800 + 1,100 × 2.53129) = −$8,584.42","EAV = −$8,584.42 / 2.53129 = −$3,391.32 per year"],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 9, PMT = −1100, CPT PV → −2,784.42; − 5800 = −8,584.42","÷ 2.53129 = −3,391.32"],"errors":[{"id":"raw-npv-unequal-lives","label":"Reported the PV of costs without annualising","value":-8584.42,"check":"-(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","explain":"The PV of costs is a lump sum. The EAV divides it by the annuity factor to get a yearly amount."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided the PV of costs by 3","value":-2861.47,"check":"-(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/3","explain":"Dividing by N ignores the time value of money; the annuity factor (2.53129) is smaller than N because of discounting."},{"id":"eac-sign","label":"Reported the cost as a positive number","value":3391.32,"check":"(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","explain":"The professor's convention keeps costs negative, so the EAV of a cost is negative."}],"explanation":"The EAV turns a lump-sum PV into the level annual amount over the machine's own life. Costs stay negative, so the better machine has the less negative EAV.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-27; S6 s11-14"},{"id":"fin201-q09-535","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Seeds Co. compares Machine X ($5,800 now, $1,100 a year for 3 years) with Machine Y ($8,400 now, $850 a year for 5 years). No salvage and R = 9% per annum. Each can be replaced indefinitely. By how many dollars per year is the cheaper machine's equivalent annual cost lower than the other's? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":381.74,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)-(8400+850*(1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)","steps":[{"label":"EAC of Machine X","value":3391.32,"check":"(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"EAC of Machine Y","value":3009.58,"check":"(8400+850*(1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Annual saving of the cheaper machine","value":381.74,"check":"(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)-(8400+850*(1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)/((1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["X: PV of costs = 8,584.42; annuity factor (9%, 3) = 2.53129; EAC = 3,391.32","Y: PV of costs = 11,706.20; annuity factor (9%, 5) = 3.88965; EAC = 3,009.58","Y is cheaper per year even though its PV of costs is higher (raw PV favours X): saving = 3,391.32 − 3,009.58 = $381.74."],"keystrokes":["X: PV costs 8,584.42 ÷ 2.53129 = 3,391.32","Y: 8400 + 850 × 3.88965 = 11,706.20; ÷ 3.88965 = 3,009.58","3,391.32 − 3,009.58 = 381.74"],"errors":[{"id":"raw-npv-unequal-lives","label":"Compared the raw PVs of costs (11,706.20 − 8,584.42)","value":3121.78,"check":"(8400+850*(1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)-(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)","explain":"Raw PVs favour X, but Y covers five years of service against three. Annualise first."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided each PV of costs by its number of years","value":520.23,"check":"(5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)/3-(8400+850*(1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)/5","explain":"Dividing by N ignores discounting and understates the annual cost."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used the 5-year factor for both machines","value":-802.59,"check":"((5800+1100*(1-1/1.09**3)/0.09)-(8400+850*(1-1/1.09**5)/0.09))/((1-1/1.09**5)/0.09)","explain":"Machine X lasts 3 years, so its own annuity factor has N = 3."}],"explanation":"The machine with the higher present value of costs can still be cheaper per year because it lasts longer. EAC is the right comparison for unequal lives.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-27; L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-536","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Laurentian Dairy is considering Mixer 1: cost $640,000, saving $255,000 a year for 6 years. The required return is 11% per annum. What is the equivalent annual benefit (EAB) of Mixer 1? (Answer in $, to the cent.)","answer":103719.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(255000*(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11-640000)/((1-1/1.11**6)/0.11)","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (11%, 6 years)","value":4.23054,"check":"(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of Mixer 1","value":438787.15,"check":"255000*(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11-640000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"EAB = NPV ÷ annuity factor","value":103719.0,"check":"(255000*(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11-640000)/((1-1/1.11**6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor (11%, 6) = 4.23054","NPV = 255,000 × 4.230538 − 640,000 = $438,787.15","EAB = $438,787.15 / 4.23054 = $103,719.00 per year"],"keystrokes":["N = 6, I/Y = 11, PMT = 255000, CPT PV → 1,078,787.15; − 640000 = 438,787.15","÷ 4.23054 = 103,718.99"],"errors":[{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided the NPV by 6","value":73131.19,"check":"(255000*(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11-640000)/6","explain":"The annual equivalent uses the annuity factor, not the number of years."},{"id":"raw-npv-unequal-lives","label":"Reported the NPV itself","value":438787.15,"check":"255000*(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11-640000","explain":"The NPV is a lump sum; the EAB is the level annual equivalent."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 10-year annuity factor (the other mixer's life)","value":74506.68,"check":"(255000*(1-1/1.11**6)/0.11-640000)/((1-1/1.11**10)/0.11)","explain":"Mixer 1 lasts 6 years, so its annuity factor has N = 6."}],"explanation":"Mixer 1's EAB is $103,719.00. Mixer 2 (cost $910,000, saving $240,000 for 10 years) has EAB $85,480.70, so Mixer 1 is better even though Mixer 2 has the higher NPV ($503,415.68 against $438,787.15).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-45; L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-537","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project with a 4-year life has a net present value of costs of −$21,430 at the required return of 11% per annum. It will be replaced at the end of each life. What is its equivalent annual cost (EAC)? (Enter a negative number, in $, to the cent.)","answer":-6907.45,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-21430/((1-1/1.11**4)/0.11)","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (11%, 4 years)","value":3.10245,"check":"(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EAC","value":-6907.45,"check":"-21430/((1-1/1.11**4)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor (11%, 4) = (1 − 1/1.11⁴)/0.11 = 3.10245","EAC = −21,430 / 3.10245 = −$6,907.45 per year"],"keystrokes":["N = 4, I/Y = 11, PV = 21430, CPT PMT → −6,907.45","or −21430 ÷ 3.10245 = −6,907.45"],"errors":[{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided by 4","value":-5357.5,"check":"-21430/4","explain":"That ignores discounting; use the annuity factor (3.10245), which is below 4."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 5-year annuity factor","value":-5798.32,"check":"-21430/((1-1/1.11**5)/0.11)","explain":"The project lives 4 years, so N = 4."},{"id":"eac-sign","label":"Reported a positive number","value":6907.45,"check":"21430/((1-1/1.11**4)/0.11)","explain":"A cost stays negative in the EAV convention."}],"explanation":"When the NPV is already known, the EAV only needs the annuity factor for the project's life: EAV = NPV ÷ annuity factor(R, N).","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s14; SM 10-29"},{"id":"fin201-q09-538","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Option X costs $80,000 and lasts 1 year; Option Y costs $150,000 and lasts 2 years. Each is replaced at the end of its life, with no other costs. At what required return are the two options equally costly on an equivalent annual basis? (% per annum, 2 decimals.)","answer":14.2857,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1/(150000/80000-1)-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Y cost ÷ X cost","value":1.875,"check":"150000/80000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"1/(1 + R) = (Y ÷ X) − 1","value":0.875,"check":"150000/80000-1","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Break-even R","value":14.2857,"check":"(1/(150000/80000-1)-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["EAC(X) = 80,000/annuity factor(R, 1) = 80,000(1 + R).","EAC(Y) = 150,000/[1/(1 + R) + 1/(1 + R)²].","Setting them equal: 80,000[1 + 1/(1 + R)] = 150,000, so 1/(1 + R) = 150,000/80,000 − 1 = 0.875 and R = 14.2857%.","Check: EAC(X) = 80,000 × 1.142857 = 91,428.57; EAC(Y) = 150,000/(0.875 + 0.765625) = 150,000/1.640625 = 91,428.57. Equal to the cent."],"keystrokes":["150000 ÷ 80000 = 1.875; − 1 = 0.875; 1/x = 1.142857; − 1 = 0.142857 → 14.29%"],"errors":[{"id":"rate-from-cost-ratio","label":"Reported Y/X − 1 as the rate","value":87.5,"check":"(150000/80000-1)*100","explain":"That is 87.5%, the difference in costs, not a discount rate. It equals 1/(1 + R), not R."},{"id":"forgot-minus-one","label":"Reported 1/(Y/X − 1) = 1.1429 as 114.29%","value":114.2857,"check":"(1/(150000/80000-1))*100","explain":"1/(1 + R) = 0.875 gives 1 + R = 1.1429, and R is that minus 1."}],"explanation":"Below the break-even rate the longer, costlier option is cheaper per year; above it, the cheaper short-lived option wins. The lecture finds such a rate with a calculator; here a one- and two-year pair gives a closed form.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-539","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":["fin201-c09-ccr-lcm"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow River Packaging can run Maintenance Program A: $480,000 up front for 2 years of service, or Program B: $660,000 up front for 3 years. Either is repeated indefinitely, and R = 9% per annum. What is the equivalent annual value (EAV) of Program A?","options":["−$480,000.00","−$272,865.07","−$240,000.00","−$189,626.28"],"answer":1,"explanation":"EAV = −480,000 / annuity factor(9%, 2) = −480,000 / 1.75911 = −$272,865.07. −$480,000 is the raw cost, −$240,000 divides by N = 2, and −$189,626.28 uses Program B's 3-year factor. Program B's EAV is −$260,736.14, so B is cheaper per year.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-540","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Laurentian Dairy compares Mixer 1 (NPV $438,787.15, 6-year life) with Mixer 2 (cost $910,000, saving $240,000 a year for 10 years). The required return is 11% per annum. What is the EAB of Mixer 2?","options":["$85,480.70","$503,415.68","$50,341.57","$118,995.67"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV of Mixer 2 = 240,000 × 5.88923 − 910,000 = $503,415.68; EAB = $503,415.68 / 5.88923 = $85,480.70. The wrong values are the raw NPV, NPV ÷ 10 and the NPV over the other mixer's 6-year factor. Mixer 1's EAB is $103,719.00, so Mixer 1 is better.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-45"},{"id":"fin201-q09-541","concept":"fin201-c09-eav","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Machine X has a present value of costs of $8,584 over 3 years. Machine Y has a present value of costs of $11,706 over 5 years. Each is repeated indefinitely at R = 9% and does the same job. Which should be bought?","options":["Machine X: its present value of costs is lower","Machine Y: its equivalent annual cost is lower ($3,010 against $3,391)","Machine X: it has the shorter life and so is less risky","They cannot be compared because the lives differ"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Raw present values mislead because Y buys two more years of service. Annual equivalents are $8,584/2.53129 = $3,391 for X and $11,706/3.88965 = $3,010 for Y, so Y is cheaper per year.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-27, 10-28"},{"id":"fin201-q09-542","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The constant chain of replacement (CCR) is also called the NPV to _____ because the project is replaced forever.","options":["perpetuity growth","payback","maturity","infinity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CCR assumes the project is repeated at the end of each life forever, and sums the present values of all repeats. The result is NPV × (1+R)^N / ((1+R)^N − 1).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-543","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm assumes each of its two machines will be replaced with an identical machine at the end of its life, again and again, and computes the present value of all those cash flows to infinity. This is the:","options":["payback method","equivalent annual value method with no replacement","profitability index method","constant chain of replacement (CCR) method"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The CCR (NPV to infinity) values the endless chain of replacements. EAV is the annual equivalent of the same chain, and CCR = EAV ÷ R.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-544","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has an EAV of −$272,865.07 per year at R = 9%. What is its CCR (NPV to infinity)?","options":["−$3,031,834.13, which is EAV ÷ R","−$24,557.86, which is EAV × R","−$272,865.07, because the EAV already includes all replacements","−$2,455,785.65, which is EAV × 9"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The EAV is the level payment of the endless chain, so CCR = EAV / R = −272,865.07 / 0.09 = −$3,031,834.1 (−$3,031,834.13 with the unrounded EAV). Multiplying by R (or by 9) inverts the perpetuity formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36-37; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-545","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the lowest-common-multiple (LCM) method and the CCR method differ?","options":["LCM repeats the projects until their lives end together; CCR repeats them forever","The LCM method ignores replacements, while the CCR includes them","The LCM method uses payback periods, while the CCR uses NPV","The LCM method can only be used when the two lives are equal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"LCM stops at the first year in which both chains end together (6 years for lives of 2 and 3). CCR carries on forever. Both give the same ranking as the EAV, since each is a multiple of the EAV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36"},{"id":"fin201-q09-546","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the LCM chain method, why is the project's NPV repeated at each replacement date rather than just once?","options":["Because the NPV rises with each replacement as the firm learns","Each replacement is a new copy of the project, so its NPV recurs, discounted to today","Because the discount rate falls with each later replacement","Because only the initial cost repeats, not the inflows"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A replacement project has the same NPV as the original but arrives later, so its NPV is discounted to today by (1 + R)^(replacement date). A chain of NPV, NPV/(1+R)^N, NPV/(1+R)^2N and so on gives the LCM total.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36; SM 10-44"},{"id":"fin201-q09-547","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a correct statement about the LCM, CCR and EAV methods?","options":["They are three ways of comparing projects with unequal lives","They all assume each project is replaced by an identical copy","They can rank unequal-life projects differently, so use all three","The CCR equals the EAV divided by the required return"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because CCR = EAV/R and the LCM chain is the EAV times the annuity factor over the LCM years, all three give the same ranking. Using one of them is enough.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36-37; SM 10-44"},{"id":"fin201-q09-548","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"The CCR method and the EAV method always choose the same project.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CCR = EAV / R, so the two differ only by the constant factor 1/R. Whichever project has the higher (less negative) EAV also has the higher (less negative) CCR.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36-37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-549","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow River Packaging's Maintenance Program A costs $480,000 up front and gives 2 years of service. It is repeated forever. The required return is 9% per annum. What is its CCR (NPV to infinity)? (Enter a negative number, in $, to the cent.)","answer":-3031834.13,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-480000*1.09**2/(1.09**2-1)","steps":[{"label":"(1 + R)^N","value":1.1881,"check":"1.09**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"CCR = NPV × (1+R)^N / ((1+R)^N − 1)","value":-3031834.13,"check":"-480000*1.09**2/(1.09**2-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["(1 + R)^N = 1.09² = 1.1881","CCR = −480,000 × 1.1881 / (1.1881 − 1) = −480,000 × 1.1881 / 0.1881","= −$3,031,834.13","Cross-check: EAV = −$272,865.07 and EAV / R = −$3,031,834.13."],"keystrokes":["1.09 y^x 2 = 1.1881","1.1881 ÷ 0.1881 = 6.31632; × 480000 = 3,031,834.13; enter as negative"],"errors":[{"id":"raw-npv-unequal-lives","label":"Reported the single NPV of −480,000","value":-480000.0,"check":"-480000","explain":"That counts only the first purchase; the chain includes every replacement."},{"id":"ccr-factor-inverted","label":"Used ((1+R)^N − 1)/(1+R)^N instead","value":-75993.6,"check":"-480000*(1.09**2-1)/1.09**2","explain":"The chain multiplies the NPV by (1+R)^N/((1+R)^N − 1), which is greater than 1, not by its reciprocal."},{"id":"eav-for-ccr","label":"Stopped at the EAV (forgot to divide by R)","value":-272865.07,"check":"-480000/((1-1/1.09**2)/0.09)","explain":"That is the equivalent annual value; the CCR is EAV ÷ R."}],"explanation":"CCR = NPV × (1+R)^N/((1+R)^N − 1). It is the sum of an NPV that recurs every N years forever, which is the geometric series NPV × [1 + 1/(1+R)^N + 1/(1+R)^2N + ...].","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36-37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-550","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bow River Packaging compares Program A ($480,000 now, 2 years of service) with Program B ($660,000 now, 3 years of service) using a lowest-common-multiple chain at R = 9% per annum. Program A is bought at years 0, 2 and 4; Program B at years 0 and 3. What is the NPV of the cheaper chain? (Enter a negative number, in $, to the cent.)","answer":-1169641.1,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-660000-660000/1.09**3","steps":[{"label":"LCM of the two lives (years)","value":6.0,"check":"6","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of Program A's chain","value":-1224050.5,"check":"-480000-480000/1.09**2-480000/1.09**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV of Program B's chain","value":-1169641.1,"check":"-660000-660000/1.09**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["LCM of 2 and 3 years = 6 years. Over 6 years Program A is bought 3 times (years 0, 2, 4) and Program B twice (years 0, 3).","Chain A = −480,000 − 480,000/1.09² − 480,000/1.09⁴ = −$1,224,050.50","Chain B = −660,000 − 660,000/1.09³ = −$1,169,641.10","B is cheaper (less negative). Cross-check with EAV: −$260,736.14 × annuity factor(9%, 6) = −$260,736.14 × 4.48592 = −$1,169,641.10."],"keystrokes":["A: −480000 − 480000 ÷ 1.1881 − 480000 ÷ 1.41158 = −1,224,050.50","B: −660000 − 660000 ÷ 1.295029 = −1,169,641.10"],"errors":[{"id":"raw-npv-unequal-lives","label":"Compared single purchases and reported the cheaper one (A, −480,000)","value":-480000.0,"check":"-480000","explain":"One purchase of A covers only 2 years and one of B covers 3; the chains must cover the same 6 years."},{"id":"chain-too-long","label":"Repeated B a third time at year 6","value":-1563177.53,"check":"-660000-660000/1.09**3-660000/1.09**6","explain":"Year 6 is the END of the common span; B's second life runs from year 3 to year 6, so no further purchase is made."},{"id":"chain-missing-repeat","label":"Left out A's third purchase (year 4) and treated the cheaper-looking chain as the answer","value":-884006.4,"check":"-480000-480000/1.09**2","explain":"A is bought three times in 6 years (years 0, 2 and 4)."}],"explanation":"The LCM chain repeats the full NPV at each replacement date within the common period. Program B's chain is the cheaper (less negative) one, which agrees with the EAV and CCR rankings.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s37; SM 10-44"},{"id":"fin201-q09-551","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A machine's CCR at R = 9% per annum is −$2,897,068.22. What is its equivalent annual value (EAV)? (Enter a negative number, in $, to the cent.)","answer":-260736.14,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-2897068.22*0.09","steps":[{"label":"EAV = CCR × R","value":-260736.14,"check":"-2897068.22*0.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["CCR = EAV / R, so EAV = CCR × R","EAV = −2,897,068.22 × 0.09 = −$260,736.14"],"keystrokes":["2897068.22 × 0.09 = 260,736.14 → −260,736.14"],"errors":[{"id":"ccr-eav-inverted","label":"Divided the CCR by R","value":-32189646.89,"check":"-2897068.22/0.09","explain":"That multiplies the figure by about 11; the EAV is smaller than the CCR."},{"id":"ccr-for-eav","label":"Left the CCR unchanged","value":-2897068.22,"check":"-2897068.22","explain":"The CCR is not an annual figure; it is the infinite-horizon PV."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Multiplied by 9 instead of 0.09","value":-26073613.98,"check":"-2897068.22*9","explain":"R = 9% must be entered as 0.09."}],"explanation":"CCR and EAV are linked by the perpetuity formula: CCR = EAV / R, so EAV = CCR × R.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s37; FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q09-552","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Program B costs $660,000 up front, gives 3 years of service and is repeated forever. The required return is 9% per annum. What is its CCR (NPV to infinity)?","options":["−$660,000.00","−$2,897,068.22","−$260,736.14","−$150,358.90"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCR = −660,000 × 1.09³ / (1.09³ − 1) = −660,000 × 1.295029 / 0.295029 = −$2,897,068.22. The wrong options are the single-purchase NPV (−$660,000), the EAV (−$260,736.14, forgetting ÷ R) and the reciprocal factor.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36-37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-553","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Using a lowest-common-multiple chain at R = 9% per annum, Program A ($480,000, 2-year life, bought at years 0, 2 and 4) has what chain NPV over 6 years?","options":["−$1,224,050.50","−$884,006.40","−$1,440,000.00","−$272,865.07"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chain A = −480,000 − 480,000/1.09² − 480,000/1.09⁴ = −480,000 − 404,006.40 − 340,044.10 = −$1,224,050.50. The other options leave out the third purchase, add the three purchases without discounting (−$1,440,000) or divide by the 6-year annuity factor, which would give an annual figure.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-554","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":["fin201-c09-eav"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Using CCR at R = 9% per annum, Program A scores −$3,031,834.13 and Program B scores −$2,897,068.22. Both provide the same service. What should the firm do?","options":["Choose Program B: its CCR is less negative, so it costs less","Choose Program A: its CCR is larger in absolute value","Choose Program A: its life is shorter, so it is less risky","Neither: CCR cannot be used when costs are negative"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CCRs are comparable because each is the value of an endless chain of the same service. Costs are negative, so the less negative CCR (B) is better. The same ranking comes from the EAV (−$260,736 against −$272,865).","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36-37"},{"id":"fin201-q09-555","concept":"fin201-c09-ccr-lcm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two machines last 4 years and 6 years. For a lowest-common-multiple comparison, how many years should the replacement chains cover? (Years.)","answer":12.0,"unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"12","steps":[{"label":"LCM of 4 and 6","value":12.0,"check":"12","unit":"years","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Multiples of 4: 4, 8, 12, 16 ... Multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18 ...","The lowest common multiple is 12 years: the 4-year machine is bought 3 times (0, 4, 8) and the 6-year machine twice (0, 6)."],"keystrokes":["No calculator needed: smallest number divisible by both 4 and 6 = 12"],"errors":[{"id":"lcm-product","label":"Multiplied the lives (4 × 6)","value":24.0,"check":"24","explain":"24 is a common multiple, but not the lowest; 12 already works."},{"id":"lcm-sum","label":"Added the lives (4 + 6)","value":10.0,"check":"10","explain":"A sum is not a multiple of both lives; the chain needs a year where both machines end together."}],"explanation":"The LCM is the shortest span after which both chains end on the same date, so the comparison covers equal time.","source":"generated","ref":"L05A s36"}],"color":"#8A2A1E","short":"Decision rules","tag":"NPV, IRR and friends","province":"Pannonia","numeral":"IX"},{"chapter":10,"title":"Capital budgeting: cash flows","topics":[{"id":"fin201-t10-1","order":1,"title":"Relevant cash flows","summary":"A project is judged only on the cash flows that change because we take it. This topic teaches what to count (opportunity costs, erosion, extra working capital), what to ignore (sunk costs, allocated overhead, financing) and the five tax categories that tell you what each cash flow is multiplied by.","concepts":["fin201-c10-incremental","fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","fin201-c10-five-categories"]},{"id":"fin201-t10-2","order":2,"title":"Operating cash flow","summary":"Operating cash flow is the yearly cash a project generates before capital spending and working capital. You will meet four equivalent ways to compute it and see why CCA is added back even though it lowers taxes.","concepts":["fin201-c10-ocf"]},{"id":"fin201-t10-3","order":3,"title":"Net working capital","summary":"Projects tie up cash in inventory and receivables, partly funded by payables. A positive change in NWC is a cash outflow counted in full, and the investment comes back when the project ends.","concepts":["fin201-c10-nwc"]},{"id":"fin201-t10-4","order":4,"title":"CCA and the tax shield","summary":"Canadian tax depreciation (CCA) is declining balance on the undepreciated capital cost (UCC), with a first-year multiplier of 0.5, 1.5 or 1. Each year's CCA saves tax (the shield), and the whole stream can be valued at once with the PV of CCA tax shield formula.","concepts":["fin201-c10-cca-schedule","fin201-c10-half-year-aii","fin201-c10-dts","fin201-c10-pvcca","fin201-c10-net-acquisitions"]},{"id":"fin201-t10-5","order":5,"title":"Disposal of assets","summary":"When a depreciated asset is sold and its class closes, the proceeds are compared with UCC and the original cost (ACB). The excess up to ACB is recaptured CCA taxed at the full rate, any amount above ACB is a capital gain taxed on half, and a shortfall below UCC is a terminal loss that saves tax. If other assets remain in the class, the sale is not taxed at once.","concepts":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"]},{"id":"fin201-t10-6","order":6,"title":"Assembling an NPV","summary":"Put the pieces together: after-tax operating flows, CAPEX, working capital, salvage and the PV of the CCA tax shield. The same machinery handles expansions, replacements, cost-cutting proposals, bid prices and the choice of when to sell.","concepts":["fin201-c10-expansion-npv","fin201-c10-replacement-npv","fin201-c10-cost-cutting","fin201-c10-bid-price","fin201-c10-timing-of-sale"]},{"id":"fin201-t10-7","order":7,"title":"EAC with taxes, nominal vs real","summary":"Mutually exclusive projects with unequal lives are compared by their equivalent annual cost, computed after tax with PVCCA and salvage. Nominal cash flows are discounted at nominal rates and real cash flows at real rates.","concepts":["fin201-c10-eac-taxes","fin201-c10-nominal-real"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"fin201-c10-incremental","topic":"fin201-t10-1","term":"Incremental cash flows and the stand-alone principle","definition":"Incremental cash flows are all the changes in the firm's future after-tax cash flows that are a direct consequence of taking the project: cash flow with the project minus cash flow without it. The stand-alone principle treats them as the cash flows of an imaginary separate firm and values that firm.","explain":"Ask one question of every item: does the firm's cash flow change because we accept this project? If yes, count it after tax and in the year the cash actually moves; if no, ignore it. Once the incremental flows sit on a timeline you discount them like any other cash flows, and a positive NPV means the project adds wealth for the owners.","example":"Prairie Mill's new line adds $600,000 of sales and $280,000 of cash costs but cuts profits on existing products by $120,000 (erosion). Head-office overhead of $90,000 is paid either way. At T = 30% and ignoring CCA: (600,000 − 280,000 − 120,000)(1 − 0.30) = $140,000 per year.","contrast":"Incremental cash flow is not accounting profit: profit contains accruals, allocations and non-cash charges and ignores CAPEX and working capital. It is also not total firm cash flow, which includes flows that happen with or without the project.","trap":"Cue words 'allocated', 'already paid', 'last year', 'head office', 'would have happened anyway' mean drop the item; 'lost sales', 'could be sold or rented', 'extra inventory' mean add it. You can lose marks in both directions: counting what you should not and missing what you should.","mnemonic":"With minus without.","formula":"Incremental CF = CF(with project) − CF(without project)","given":false,"archetypes":["A14"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","topic":"fin201-t10-1","term":"Sunk, allocated and opportunity costs","definition":"A sunk cost has already been paid (or committed) and cannot change with the decision, so it is counted at zero. An allocated cost is a share of overhead the firm pays anyway and is also counted at zero. An opportunity cost is the most valuable alternative given up, measured at what an owned asset is worth today after tax, and it is counted as an outflow.","explain":"Owned does not mean free. If a project uses land or space the firm could sell or rent, charge the project today's after-tax market value (or the after-tax rent forgone), never the price paid years ago. Money already spent on a study, even a non-refundable one, is gone whatever you decide, so it does not belong in the analysis.","example":"Marlow Aggregates' gravel plant will use land bought nine years ago for $2.8M, now worth $3.4M (tax-free sale). With plant $12.6M and site preparation $0.65M, year 0 = −(3.4 + 12.6 + 0.65) = −$16.65M. The $0.2M environmental assessment paid last year is sunk and ignored.","contrast":"Sunk (past, cannot change, count 0) versus opportunity (a future benefit given up, count at today's value) versus allocated (overhead that does not change, count 0). All three sound like 'costs of the project' but only the opportunity cost is incremental.","trap":"'Bought six years ago for' is bait: use today's market value. 'Spent last year', 'already paid' or 'non-refundable' means sunk. 'Could otherwise be rented out' means add the after-tax rent forgone. If the sale would be taxed, the opportunity cost is the after-tax amount.","mnemonic":"Sunk is gone; owned is not free.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":["A14"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","topic":"fin201-t10-1","term":"Side effects and financing costs","definition":"Side effects are changes in the cash flows of the firm's other products caused by the project: erosion (a loss) is subtracted and a synergy or spillover (a gain) is added, both after tax. Financing costs such as interest, dividends and principal repaid are never part of project cash flows.","explain":"Erosion counts only when the lost sales would not have been lost anyway, for example to a competitor. Financing is analyzed separately: the discount rate already charges for the cost of capital, so subtracting interest as well would double count the cost of funds.","example":"A premium cold brew takes $85,000 a year of pre-tax profit from regular iced coffee. At T = 30% the after-tax erosion is −85,000(1 − 0.30) = −$59,500 a year. The interest on the bank loan used to buy the equipment never appears.","contrast":"Erosion (a loss on other products caused by the project) versus an opportunity cost (the best alternative use of an owned asset) versus a financing effect (excluded). Erosion belongs inside the operating cash flow; financing stays outside it.","trap":"Words like 'interest', 'loan' or 'dividend' in the stem are decoys: they never enter the cash flows. 'A competitor would have taken the sales anyway' removes the erosion. Remember to tax-effect side effects.","mnemonic":"Interest never appears.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-five-categories","topic":"fin201-t10-1","term":"The five tax categories of cash flows","definition":"Every project cash flow falls into one of five categories: (a) not counted (× 0): sunk and allocated costs; (b) counted in full (× 1): capital expenditure and the change in net working capital; (c) multiplied by (1 − T): revenues and cash expenses; (d) multiplied by T: the CCA tax shield; (e) other tax flows: the sale of depreciated assets (recapture, terminal loss, capital gain).","explain":"The category tells you what a dollar of that item is worth after tax. A dollar of revenue becomes (1 − T) because the firm pays tax on it; a dollar of expense costs only (1 − T) because it is deductible; a dollar of CCA is not paid in cash so it is worth only the tax it saves, T. CAPEX and working capital are cash moved through the balance sheet, so they count in full.","example":"At T = 30%: extra sales of $100,000 are worth $70,000; $150,000 of advertising paid last year is $0 (sunk); selling an asset for $197,000 (cost $590,000, UCC $155,566.41) gives 197,000 − 0.30(197,000 − 155,566.41) = $184,569.92 after tax.","contrast":"Multiplying by (1 − T) versus T is the classic confusion: a cash item (revenue or expense) is multiplied by (1 − T), a non-cash deduction (CCA) by T. CAPEX and NWC look like costs but are not tax-adjusted.","trap":"Taxing CAPEX or NWC; counting a sunk study; using (1 − T) on CCA; forgetting NWC recovery and the tax effects of selling an asset. The sample exam asks for the after-tax value of a revenue, a sunk advertising cost and an asset sale in one question.","mnemonic":"Zero, one, one-minus-T, T, and the sale.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":["A14"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"The five tax categories","head":["Category","Multiplier","Examples","Why"],"rows":[["(a) Not counted","× 0","Sunk costs (a study paid last year), allocated overhead","The decision cannot change them"],["(b) Counted in full","× 1","CAPEX; change in NWC and its recovery","Cash moved through the balance sheet; no immediate tax effect"],["(c) After-tax","× (1 − T)","Revenues, cash expenses, cost savings","Each $1 changes tax by $T"],["(d) Tax shield","× T","CCA (depreciation)","Non-cash; the only cash effect is the tax saved"],["(e) Other tax flows","CF_AT formula","Sale of depreciated assets: recapture, terminal loss, capital gain","POD − T[min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 0.5 T max(POD − ACB, 0)"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c10-ocf","topic":"fin201-t10-2","term":"Operating cash flow (OCF)","definition":"OCF = EBIT + D − Tax, where D is the CCA claimed. Equivalent forms: EBIT(1 − T) + D; Net income + D (bottom-up); S − C − Tax (top-down); and (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D (the tax-shield approach).","explain":"OCF is the cash a project generates in a year from running it, before capital spending and working capital. CCA is deducted to find taxable income but added back because no cash left the firm, and it still helps through the tax it saves. Interest never appears. Use whichever form fits the data, since all four must agree.","example":"S = $812,000, C = $455,000, CCA = $96,000, T = 32%. EBIT = 261,000; tax = 0.32 × 261,000 = 83,520; OCF = 261,000 + 96,000 − 83,520 = $273,480. Check: (357,000)(0.68) + 0.32(96,000) = 242,760 + 30,720 = $273,480.","contrast":"OCF versus net income (OCF adds back D), versus free cash flow (FCF = OCF − CAPEX − change in NWC) and versus EBITDA (before tax). The tax-shield form shows the effect of CCA directly: each extra $1 of CCA raises OCF by T.","trap":"Forgetting to add D back; taxing S − C instead of EBIT; subtracting interest; using D instead of T × D in the shield form; mixing pre-tax and after-tax terms. When the stem gives EBIT, CCA and tax paid, use OCF = EBIT + D − Tax.","mnemonic":"Shield form: after-tax profit plus T times CCA.","formula":"OCF = EBIT + D − Tax = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D = NI + D","given":false,"archetypes":["A14"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-nwc","topic":"fin201-t10-3","term":"Net working capital (NWC) in a project","definition":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables (current assets minus current liabilities). A project's change in NWC is NWC_t − NWC_(t-1); a positive change is a cash outflow counted in full (× 1), and the NWC is recovered as an inflow when the project ends.","explain":"A project needs cash on hand, inventory and credit sales, partly financed by suppliers' credit (payables). The firm supplies the difference up front and gets it back when inventory is sold, receivables collected and payables paid. So NWC behaves like a loan to the project, with no tax effect and no CCA.","example":"NWC is $20,000 at t = 0, $25,000 at t = 1 and $22,000 at t = 2, and all of it is released at t = 3. Cash flows from NWC: −20,000, −5,000, +3,000, +22,000. Sample exam Q6: inventory increasing gives a positive change in NWC.","contrast":"Change in NWC versus an expense versus CAPEX: the change in NWC is not tax-deductible and not depreciated (× 1), but unlike CAPEX it is recovered in full at the end. A rise in payables lowers NWC, so it is a cash inflow in the year.","trap":"Positive change in NWC is an outflow, so inventory up, receivables up or cash balances up all use cash; payables up releases cash. Do not multiply NWC by (1 − T) and do not forget the recovery at the end.","mnemonic":"Inventory up, cash out.","formula":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables; CF_t = −ΔNWC_t","given":true,"archetypes":["A14"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"list","title":"Direction of the change in NWC","items":["Inventory increases: positive change in NWC, cash outflow","Accounts receivable increase (longer credit terms): positive change, outflow","Cash balance held in the project increases: positive change, outflow","Accounts payable increase: NWC falls, cash inflow","Salaries, taxes and capital expenditure are not working-capital accounts"]}},{"id":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","topic":"fin201-t10-4","term":"CCA schedule and UCC","definition":"CCA is tax depreciation on a declining balance at the class rate d: CCA_1 = m × d × I0 (m = 0.5, 1.5 or 1), CCA_t = d × UCC_(t-1) for later years, and UCC_t = UCC_(t-1) − CCA_t. The UCC never reaches zero, and the tax saved each year is DTS_t = CCA_t × T_C.","explain":"Build the schedule year by year: claim a percentage of what is left, subtract it from the balance, repeat. Year 1 is special because of the multiplier m; from year 2 on there is no multiplier at all. The UCC at the date of sale is needed for recapture or a terminal loss.","example":"Lecture example: $7,500, d = 30%, AII. CCA_1 = 3,375, UCC_1 = 4,125; CCA_2 = 1,237.50, UCC_2 = 2,887.50; CCA_3 = 866.25; CCA_4 = 606.375; UCC at the start of year 5 = 1,414.875. Closed form: UCC_n = I0(1 − m d)(1 − d)^(n-1).","contrast":"Declining-balance CCA versus straight-line depreciation: CCA each year is d × the remaining UCC, not d × the original cost, so the amounts shrink. CCA versus accounting depreciation: only CCA changes the tax bill.","trap":"Applying 0.5 or 1.5 in every year; using d × I0 every year (straight-line); forgetting that UCC rolls forward; multiplying by (1 − T) instead of T when converting to a shield. Under the half-year rule year 2 has the largest CCA.","mnemonic":"Claim a slice of what is left.","formula":"CCA_1 = m d I0; CCA_t = d × UCC_(t-1); UCC_t = UCC_(t-1) − CCA_t","given":false,"archetypes":["A16"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"CCA schedule: $7,500 asset, d = 30%, AII (m = 1.5), T = 38%","head":["Year","Opening UCC","CCA","Closing UCC","DTS = CCA × T"],"rows":[["1","7,500.00","3,375.00 (1.5 × 0.30 × 7,500)","4,125.00","1,282.50"],["2","4,125.00","1,237.50 (0.30 × 4,125)","2,887.50","470.25"],["3","2,887.50","866.25","2,021.25","329.18"],["4","2,021.25","606.38","1,414.88","230.42"],["5","1,414.88","sold during year 5 for $3,000","disposal: recapture 1,585.13","tax 602.35"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","topic":"fin201-t10-4","term":"Half-year rule, AII and the 100% allowance","definition":"The first-year multiplier m scales the CCA on a new asset: 0.5 under the half-year rule, 1.5 under the Accelerated Investment Incentive (AII), and 1.0 for an asset already being depreciated or when the question states a full first-year rate. In the PV of CCA tax shield it becomes the factor (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k) or 1.","explain":"Tax law changes, and the professor says to be capable of handling any rules: read the question and use the multiplier it states, defaulting to the half-year rule when nothing is said. The multiplier acts on d × I0 in year 1 only. A faster first-year claim brings the tax saving forward, so the PV is larger.","example":"I0 = $120,000, d = 30%, T = 25%. Half-year: CCA_1 = 18,000, DTS = 4,500. AII: CCA_1 = 54,000, DTS = 13,500. With k = 10% the PVCCA factors are 0.9545 (half-year), 1.0000 (100%) and 1.0455 (AII).","contrast":"Half-year (m = 0.5) versus AII (m = 1.5): AII front-loads CCA and gives the higher PV. An asset the firm already owns has already used its first-year adjustment, so its factor is 1.","trap":"Applying m every year, picking the wrong factor in PVCCA (0.5 versus 1.5 regime), or applying m to a continuing asset. Check whether the asset is new this year or already depreciating.","mnemonic":"Half: 0.5. Incentive: 1.5. Old asset: 1.","formula":"m = 0.5 (half-year), 1.5 (AII), 1.0; factors (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k), 1","given":false,"archetypes":["A16"],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"The three first-year regimes","head":["Regime","m","CCA in year 1","PVCCA first-year factor"],"rows":[["Half-year rule (new asset)","0.5","0.5 × d × I0","(1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k)"],["Accelerated Investment Incentive","1.5","1.5 × d × I0","(1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)"],["100% (already owned, or stated)","1.0","d × I0 (or d × UCC)","1"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c10-dts","topic":"fin201-t10-4","term":"CCA tax shield (DTS)","definition":"The depreciation tax shield in year t is DTS_t = CCA_t × T_C, the tax saved because CCA is deductible. It is called a shield because it shields income from tax, reducing the tax bill, provided the firm has enough other taxable income.","explain":"CCA is not paid in cash, so its only cash effect is the tax it saves: T cents per dollar of CCA. Because CCA declines with UCC, the DTS shrinks every year after the first-year adjustment (under the half-year rule year 2 is the largest). The size of the shield rises with the tax rate and with how quickly CCA is claimed.","example":"I0 = $180,000, d = 20%, half-year rule, T = 40%. CCA_1 = 18,000; UCC_1 = 162,000; CCA_2 = 0.20 × 162,000 = 32,400; DTS_2 = 32,400 × 0.40 = $12,960.","contrast":"DTS versus CCA: CCA is a deduction measured in dollars of income, the DTS is the cash tax it saves (CCA × T). A cash expense is multiplied by (1 − T) because cash is paid; CCA by T because nothing is paid.","trap":"Forgetting to multiply CCA by T; using (1 − T); reusing year-1 CCA in later years; assuming a shield exists even though the firm has no other taxable income.","mnemonic":"Shield: CCA times T.","formula":"DTS_t = CCA_t × T_C","given":false,"archetypes":["A16"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-pvcca","topic":"fin201-t10-4","term":"PV of the CCA tax shield (PVCCA)","definition":"PV_CCA = (I0 d T_C)/(k + d) × {1, (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)} − (S_N d T_C)/(k + d) × 1/(1 + k)^N. The first factor is chosen by the regime (100%, half-year, AII); the last term is subtracted whenever the asset is sold in year N for S_N, whether or not its class stays open, and is dropped only when the asset is not sold (or S_N = 0).","explain":"The shield CCA × T_C shrinks at rate d each year, so it is a growing perpetuity with g = −d: PV = first shield/(k − g) = CCA_1 T_C/(k + d). The half-year and AII factors adjust for the first year's different claim. If the asset is sold in year N, CCA on it stops, so subtract the PV of the tail lost: S_N d T_C/(k + d) discounted N years. This is just the PV of the CCA expected: the proceeds S_N are a separate cash flow (received untaxed when the class stays open), and any capital gain above ACB is taxed separately.","example":"Seminar 5: I0 = $110,000 (equipment plus installation), d = 20%, T = 40%, k = 10%, AII, sold in 6 years for $17,000. PVCCA = 30,666.67 − 2,558.95 = $28,107.72.","contrast":"k + d versus k − d: the shield declines, so it is d added to k. S_N versus UCC_N in the last term: use the sale price S_N. Sold versus kept: subtract the last term whenever the asset is sold, even if other assets remain in the class; omit it only when the asset is never sold (or has no salvage value).","trap":"Using k − d; wrong first-year factor; salvage term not discounted; UCC instead of S_N; dropping the term because 'the pool stays open' (the sale still takes S_N out of the pool) or keeping it when the asset is never sold. Also never discount PVCCA again: it is already a present value.","mnemonic":"Shield over (k plus d), minus the tail after the sale.","formula":"PV_CCA = (I0 d T_C)/(k + d) × {1 | (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k) | (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)} − (S_N d T_C)/(k + d)/(1 + k)^N","given":true,"archetypes":["A17"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","topic":"fin201-t10-4","term":"Net acquisitions rule and net salvage","definition":"When assets are bought and sold in the same CCA class in the same year, the first-year rule applies to net acquisitions = additions − min(original cost, selling price) of the disposals. In a replacement analysis the PVCCA uses the net acquisition cost (new cost − old resale today) as I0 and the net salvage (new salvage − old salvage) as S_N.","explain":"Selling the old machine takes its capital cost out of the pool, so the pool grows only by the net amount. If the net acquisition is positive, apply the half-year or 1.5 rule to it; if it is negative, no adjustment is made. Net salvage can be negative, in which case the salvage term adds to PVCCA.","example":"Dickerson: new machine $280,000, old resale $60,000: net acquisition = $220,000. Salvage in 4 years $18,000 (new) and $10,000 (old): net salvage = $8,000. With d = 25%, T = 40%, k = 11%, AII: PVCCA = $62,675.29.","contrast":"Net acquisition cost (the I0 of the PVCCA) versus net salvage (the S_N): one is today, one is at the end. Net acquisition versus gross cost of the new asset: using the full $280,000 overstates the shield.","trap":"Using the full purchase price; using the original cost or UCC of the old asset instead of the lesser of cost and proceeds; subtracting salvages in the wrong direction; forgetting that net salvage may be negative.","mnemonic":"New minus old, today and at the end.","formula":"Net acquisitions = additions − min(original cost, POD); net salvage = S_N(new) − S_N(old)","given":false,"archetypes":["A17"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","topic":"fin201-t10-5","term":"Recapture of CCA and terminal loss","definition":"At disposal, compare the proceeds of disposition (POD) with UCC and ACB (the adjusted cost base, i.e. cost plus acquisition expense). The amount min(ACB, POD) − UCC is taxed at the full rate T when positive (recapture of CCA) and is a tax saving T × (UCC − POD) when negative (terminal loss).","explain":"Recapture means we claimed too much CCA relative to what the asset really lost, so CRA takes the extra back as income. A terminal loss means we claimed too little, so the unclaimed balance is deducted. Either way the tax flow is T times the difference between the lesser of ACB and POD and UCC.","example":"Lecture example: sell the $7,500 asset in year 5 for $3,000 at T = 38%; UCC = 1,414.875. Recapture = 3,000 − 1,414.875 = 1,585.125; tax = 0.38 × 1,585.125 = 602.35; after-tax proceeds = $2,397.65. Check: total tax saved = T × (cost − proceeds) = 0.38 × 4,500 = $1,710.","contrast":"Recapture (POD above UCC, taxed at T) versus terminal loss (POD below UCC, tax saved) versus capital gain (POD above ACB, taxed on half). Only the part of POD up to ACB can be recapture.","trap":"Taxing the whole POD, using POD − ACB as the recapture, getting the sign of a terminal loss wrong (it is a refund), or forgetting that recapture uses min(ACB, POD). A pool that stays open has no immediate recapture or terminal loss.","mnemonic":"Compare POD with UCC: above is tax, below is refund.","formula":"CF_AT = POD − T[min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 0.5 T max(POD − ACB, 0)","given":true,"archetypes":["A15"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Disposal cases when the class closes: POD versus UCC versus ACB (tax rate T)","head":["Case","Condition","Tax effect","CF_AT"],"rows":[["Capital gain","POD > ACB","Recapture ACB − UCC at T plus half of (POD − ACB) at T","POD − T(ACB − UCC) − 0.5T(POD − ACB)"],["Recapture only","UCC < POD <= ACB","Recapture POD − UCC taxed at T; no capital gain","POD − T(POD − UCC)"],["No tax","POD = UCC","None","POD"],["Terminal loss","POD < UCC (class closes)","Tax saved on the shortfall","POD + T(UCC − POD)"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","topic":"fin201-t10-5","term":"Capital gain and the after-tax disposal cash flow CF_AT","definition":"Any proceeds above the ACB are a capital gain, and only 50% of the gain is taxable. The after-tax disposal cash flow is CF_AT = POD − T × [min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 0.5 × T × max(POD − ACB, 0).","explain":"Work in three steps: the recapture or terminal loss relative to UCC (capped at ACB), the capital gain relative to ACB, then subtract the tax from POD. The 50% inclusion applies only to the gain above cost. Many exam assets are sold below cost, so the capital-gain term is zero.","example":"Sample exam Q11(c): POD $197,000, ACB $590,000, UCC $155,566.41, T = 30%. min(ACB, POD) = 197,000; recapture = 41,433.59; gain = 0. CF_AT = 197,000 − 0.30(41,433.59) = $184,569.92.","contrast":"Capital gain (above ACB, half taxed) versus recapture (between UCC and ACB, fully taxed). Do not compute a gain relative to UCC.","trap":"Taxing recapture at 50%, taxing a gain that is not there because POD < ACB, or measuring the gain from UCC. The formula is on the sheet; the work is in finding min and max correctly.","mnemonic":"Gain above cost: half-taxed.","formula":"CF_AT = POD − T[min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 0.5 T max(POD − ACB, 0)","given":true,"archetypes":["A15"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","topic":"fin201-t10-5","term":"Pool stays open versus pool closes","definition":"If other assets remain in the CCA class (the pool stays open), selling an asset generally triggers no recapture or terminal loss: the proceeds are received untaxed (apart from any capital gain above ACB), the pool's UCC falls by the proceeds (capped at cost) and CCA continues on the smaller pool. If the sale leaves the class empty (the pool closes), CCA stops and the gap between UCC and POD is settled at once with the CF_AT formula (recapture, terminal loss or capital gain).","explain":"Either way the sold asset's CCA stream ends, so the PVCCA formula subtracts the salvage term whenever the asset is sold for S_N (Seminar 6 tractor and Dickerson, SM 10-8 and 10-36); the term is dropped only when the asset is not sold. With an open pool, the formula plus the untaxed proceeds is exact. With a closed pool, the exact route runs the CCA schedule to the sale and applies CF_AT; its PV differs slightly from the formula value because the formula spreads the UCC − POD gap over future CCA instead of settling it at once. Settle the gap only once: never add a CF_AT recapture or terminal-loss tax on top of the formula's salvage term.","example":"A $20,000 machine is sold for $4,000 from a class that still holds other assets: the firm receives $4,000 untaxed, the pool's UCC falls by $4,000 and the PVCCA formula subtracts the shield on $4,000. If it were the last asset in the class with UCC $9,000, the $5,000 terminal loss would save tax at once: at T = 30%, CF_AT = 4,000 + 0.30 × 5,000 = $5,500.","contrast":"Open pool (proceeds untaxed, CCA continues on a smaller base, salvage term subtracted in PVCCA) versus closed pool (immediate recapture, terminal loss or capital gain via CF_AT; CCA stops). Sold versus not sold decides the salvage term; open versus closed decides whether the sale is taxed at once.","trap":"Dropping the salvage term because 'the pool stays open' (the sale still removes S_N from the pool); applying recapture or terminal loss to an asset whose class stays open; ignoring the terminal loss when the class closes; adding a CF_AT tax on top of the formula's salvage term (double counting). Look for 'last asset', 'no assets left in the class' or 'other assets remain'.","mnemonic":"Last one out turns off the lights.","formula":"Open: CF = POD (untaxed), UCC falls by POD, PVCCA subtracts S_N d T/(k + d)/(1 + k)^N. Closed: CF_AT = POD − T[min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 0.5T max(POD − ACB, 0)","given":false,"archetypes":["A15"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","topic":"fin201-t10-6","term":"Expansion project NPV","definition":"NPV of an expansion = CF0 + PV of after-tax operating flows + PV of NWC and salvage flows + PV_CCA, where CF0 = −(CAPEX + initial NWC + opportunity costs), operating flows are (S − C)(1 − T) including erosion, and PV_CCA is added (not discounted again). If the equipment is sold for S_N, add S_N at year N (received untaxed when the class stays open) and subtract the salvage term inside PV_CCA; drop the term only if it is not sold.","explain":"Follow the order: (1) incremental cash flows (drop sunk costs, add land and erosion); (2) after-tax revenue less costs each year; (3) CAPEX and NWC at t = 0, changes in NWC after, recovery at the end; (4) salvage proceeds if the equipment is sold; (5) discount at k; (6) add PVCCA with the right first-year factor and the salvage term; (7) accept if NPV > 0.","example":"Seminar 6 tractor plant: CAPEX $170M, land $10M, NWC $3M then 5% of sales, sales $100M less $10M erosion, costs $45M, T = 35%, d = 10%, k = 12%, sold for $100M at year 10 (received untaxed; salvage term subtracted), AII. NPV = 14.29 + 28.49 − 5.12 = $37.66M, so take the project.","contrast":"Expansion versus replacement: an expansion adds a new asset and has no old asset to sell, so there is no net-acquisition adjustment. The PVCCA is added once, undiscounted again.","trap":"Counting the sunk feasibility study; forgetting the land opportunity cost; recovering NWC twice; discounting PVCCA again; using interest. Dropping the salvage term because 'the pool stays open'. Also check the erosion: the lost $10M of sales reduces after-tax income.","mnemonic":"Flows, NWC, salvage, then shield.","formula":"NPV = CF0 + PV(after-tax flows, NWC, salvage) + PV_CCA","given":false,"archetypes":["A18"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","topic":"fin201-t10-6","term":"Replacement project NPV","definition":"A replacement NPV uses incremental flows: the net acquisition cost (new cost − old resale today) at t = 0, the incremental savings or sales after tax, the change in NWC, the net salvage (new − old) at the end, and PV_CCA computed on the net acquisition cost and net salvage. Both machines are in the same class, which stays open, so the old machine's sale has no immediate tax; the net salvage is received untaxed and its salvage term is subtracted in PV_CCA (a negative net salvage makes it an addition).","explain":"Selling the old machine today means we do not get its future salvage, so the old machine's end-of-life value is an opportunity cost netted against the new machine's salvage. Everything is a difference between the 'replace' and 'keep' worlds.","example":"Dickerson (Seminar 6): net acquisition $220,000, extra sales $85,000 to $70,000, NWC 10% of sales, T = 40%, k = 11%, d = 25%, AII, net salvage $8,000. NPV = −$2,075, so do not replace. Simple replacement: −85,000 + savings + net salvage −10,000 + PVCCA gives NPV = $47,867.15, so replace.","contrast":"Replacement versus expansion: a replacement has an old asset whose resale and salvage change the numbers (net acquisition, net salvage). Replacement versus cost cutting: savings are the revenue-like benefit of the new machine.","trap":"Using the full price of the new machine; forgetting that net salvage may be negative; treating the old machine's book value as relevant; ignoring the old machine's lost CCA.","mnemonic":"New minus old, everywhere.","formula":"NPV = −net acquisition + PV(after-tax savings) + PV(net salvage) + PV_CCA(net acquisition, net salvage)","given":false,"archetypes":["A18"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","topic":"fin201-t10-6","term":"Cost-cutting proposals and required savings","definition":"A cost-cutting proposal has no sales: the benefit is the after-tax saving, (saving)(1 − T) per year. The required pre-tax saving sets NPV = 0: required after-tax annual saving = (outlay − PV_CCA − PV of other flows) / annuity factor, then pre-tax saving = that / (1 − T).","explain":"Treat a cost saving like revenue: it is taxed, so only (1 − T) of it reaches the firm. To find the break-even saving, collect every known present value, back out the annuity that makes NPV zero, and then un-tax it.","example":"Equipment $500,000, d = 20%, T = 35%, k = 10%, 5 years, half-year rule, scrapped at the end with no salvage (so no salvage term). PVCCA = $111,363.64; required after-tax saving = (500,000 − PVCCA)/3.790787 = $102,521.29; required pre-tax saving = 102,521.29/0.65 = $157,725.07.","contrast":"Cost cutting versus expansion: no revenue, so the whole benefit is the tax-adjusted saving. Required saving (NPV = 0 solve) versus NPV of a given saving.","trap":"Forgetting to divide by (1 − T); dividing by N instead of the annuity factor; forgetting the CCA shield, the salvage, or the salvage term when the machine is sold; multiplying the pre-tax saving by T.","mnemonic":"Back out the annuity, then un-tax.","formula":"Required pre-tax saving = [(outlay − PV_CCA − other PVs) / annuity factor] / (1 − T)","given":false,"archetypes":["A19"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-bid-price","topic":"fin201-t10-6","term":"Bid price and break-even revenue","definition":"The minimum bid price makes NPV = 0: PV the known pieces (CAPEX, NWC, salvage, PV_CCA), solve the required after-tax operating cash flow as an annuity, divide by (1 − T), add back costs and divide by the number of units.","explain":"A contractor asks: what is the lowest price at which this job still earns the required return? Treat NPV = 0 as the break-even, back out the after-tax net revenue each year, then un-tax it and add the costs to reach the sales dollars and the price per unit.","example":"SM 10-24 (which applies no first-year factor): initial cost $1,300,000, salvage $650,000 in 3 years, NWC $340,000 recovered, d = 20%, T = 36%, k = 20%, 89 systems a year with variable cost $96,000. Required after-tax operating income = $427,628.57 per year; sales = $9,212,169.64; price at least $103,507.52 per system.","contrast":"Bid price versus NPV of a given price: the bid price is the zero-NPV price, a minimum, not a forecast. Accounting break-even would ignore the time value of money and the required return.","trap":"Forgetting to divide by (1 − T); dividing by N rather than the annuity factor; leaving out the CCA shield or the NWC recovery; stopping at net revenue and not adding back costs.","mnemonic":"NPV zero, un-tax, add costs, divide by units.","formula":"Required after-tax OCF = −(PV of other flows)/annuity factor; price = [OCF/(1 − T) + costs]/Q","given":false,"archetypes":["A19"],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","topic":"fin201-t10-6","term":"Timing of sale and the opportunity cost of the lost shield","definition":"To decide whether to sell an asset now or keep it, compute the NPV at the decision date of selling. When the sale closes the class: the after-tax proceeds CF_AT minus the present value (at that date) of the CCA tax shields we would have kept, d × UCC × T/(k + d). When other assets remain, the proceeds are untaxed and the shield lost is the one on the amount sold, POD × d × T/(k + d).","explain":"Selling stops the CCA tax shield, so the lost shield is an opportunity cost of the sale. Value the remaining shield as a growing perpetuity declining at d from the date of sale, subtract it from the after-tax cash we receive, and sell only if the result is positive.","example":"Lecture example: sell the $7,500 asset in year 5 for $3,000 (T = 38%, k = 10%, d = 30%). CF_AT = $2,397.65; the lost shield at year 5 has PV = 0.38 × 0.30 × 1,414.875/(0.10 + 0.30) = $403.24; NPV at t = 5 is $1,994.41, so selling is better. (The slide prints $403.27, a typo: 2,397.65 − 403.24 = 1,994.41, the slide's own NPV.)","contrast":"Selling versus holding: the same asset, two worlds. Compare the after-tax cash received with the PV of the lost future tax shields (and any future salvage).","trap":"Ignoring the lost shield; using the original cost instead of UCC at the sale date (closed class) or UCC instead of POD (open pool); forgetting the disposal tax.","mnemonic":"Sale cash minus lost shield.","formula":"Closed class: NPV_N(sell) = CF_AT − d × UCC_N × T/(k + d); open pool: NPV_N(sell) = POD − d × POD × T/(k + d)","given":false,"archetypes":["A18"],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","topic":"fin201-t10-7","term":"Equivalent annual cost with taxes","definition":"EAC = (PV of costs, after tax, including PV_CCA and salvage) / annuity factor(k, N). An asset sold at the end of its life adds its salvage as an inflow and its PVCCA subtracts the salvage term. With unequal lives and replacement, the project with the lower EAC (equivalently, the higher EAV) is preferred.","explain":"Compute each option's total PV of costs: initial outlay, after-tax operating costs, salvage, and the PVCCA benefit. Divide by the annuity factor for its own life to get an annual cost. Pick the lowest annual cost.","example":"Seminar 6 cars (each sold at the end of its life, so both PVCCAs subtract the salvage term): Civil PV of costs −$17,663.79 over 4 years gives EAC −$5,815; Chevalier −$15,547 over 3 years gives EAC −$6,473. Choose the Civil.","contrast":"EAC versus raw NPV: comparing PVs with unequal lives is wrong. EAC versus CCR (NPV to infinity): same ranking, different scale.","trap":"Comparing PVs of different lives; using the wrong annuity factor N; leaving the tax shield out; wrong sign (costs are negative).","mnemonic":"Annualize before comparing.","formula":"EAC = PV(costs) / [(1 − (1 + k)^(-N))/k]","given":false,"archetypes":["A13"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","topic":"fin201-t10-7","term":"Nominal versus real cash flows and rates","definition":"Discount nominal cash flows at the nominal rate and real cash flows at the real rate. The rates are linked by (1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real), and a nominal cash flow in year i converts to real by dividing by (1 + R_inf)^i.","explain":"Inflation inflates the cash flows as well as the required return. If the cash flows already include inflation they are nominal and need a nominal rate; if they are in today's dollars they are real and need the real rate. Never mix them.","example":"Nominal rate 13%, inflation 3%: real rate = 1.13/1.03 − 1 = 9.7087%. Discounting the real cash flows at 9.7087% gives the same PV as discounting the nominal cash flows at 13%.","contrast":"Nominal versus real: the PV is the same if the pairing is consistent, but EACs differ because the annuity factors use different rates.","trap":"Discounting real flows at nominal rates (or the reverse); subtracting inflation from the rate instead of dividing; forgetting to convert a CPI-linked flow.","mnemonic":"Nominal with nominal, real with real.","formula":"(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real); CF_real,i = CF_nom,i/(1 + R_inf)^i","given":true,"archetypes":["A10","A13"],"weight":1,"figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"fin201-a10-01","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","title":"Sort the cash flow by its tax treatment","prompt":"Drag each item to the multiplier that gives its after-tax effect.","buckets":["× 0 (not counted)","× 1 (counted in full)","× (1 − T)","× T (tax shield)"],"items":[{"text":"A feasibility study paid last year","bucket":0},{"text":"Head-office overhead allocated to the project but paid anyway","bucket":0},{"text":"Purchase of equipment (CAPEX)","bucket":1},{"text":"Increase in inventory at project start (change in NWC)","bucket":1},{"text":"Recovery of NWC at the end of the project","bucket":1},{"text":"Additional sales revenue","bucket":2},{"text":"Extra wages paid to project staff","bucket":2},{"text":"Annual labour savings from a new machine","bucket":2},{"text":"CCA claimed on the equipment","bucket":3},{"text":"Depreciation (CCA) on a delivery truck bought for the project","bucket":3},{"text":"Market study fee that is non-refundable and already paid","bucket":0},{"text":"Cost of materials used in production","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Sunk and allocated costs are × 0, CAPEX and NWC are × 1 (already after-tax money moved through the balance sheet), revenues and cash expenses or savings are × (1 − T), and CCA is × T because it is a non-cash deduction whose only cash effect is the tax saved."},{"id":"fin201-a10-02","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","title":"Order the steps of a project NPV","prompt":"Put the steps of the NPV of an expansion project in the right order.","items":["List the incremental cash flows: drop sunk and allocated costs, add opportunity costs and erosion","Compute after-tax operating flows each year: (S − C)(1 − T)","Add CAPEX and initial NWC at t = 0, the changes in NWC each year and the NWC recovery at the end","Add the salvage or disposal cash flow at the end","Discount all of these flows at k to get their PV","Add the PV of the CCA tax shield (with the right first-year factor and salvage term) without discounting it again","Sum to NPV and accept the project if NPV > 0"],"explanation":"First decide what counts, then put each category on the timeline, discount, add the already-discounted PVCCA once, and apply the NPV rule."},{"id":"fin201-a10-03","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","title":"Match the symbol to its meaning","prompt":"Match each term from the disposal and tax shield formulas with its meaning.","pairs":[{"left":"ACB","right":"Adjusted cost base: cost plus acquisition expense"},{"left":"POD","right":"Proceeds of disposition: the sale value"},{"left":"UCC","right":"Undepreciated capital cost: cost less CCA claimed"},{"left":"DTS","right":"Depreciation tax shield: CCA × T_C"},{"left":"AII","right":"Accelerated Investment Incentive: 1.5 × d × I0 in year 1"},{"left":"CCA","right":"Capital cost allowance: tax depreciation"},{"left":"Terminal loss","right":"POD below UCC when the class closes: tax saved"},{"left":"Recapture","right":"POD above UCC (up to ACB): taxed at the full rate"}],"explanation":"These are the Canadian disposal and CCA terms from the review list; ACB, POD and UCC drive the CF_AT formula and DTS drives the PVCCA."},{"id":"fin201-a10-04","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","title":"Order the disposal calculation","prompt":"Put the steps of the after-tax cash flow from selling a depreciated asset in order.","items":["Roll the CCA schedule forward to find UCC at the date of sale","Compare POD with ACB and compute min(ACB, POD)","Recapture (or terminal loss) = min(ACB, POD) − UCC","Capital gain = max(POD − ACB, 0), of which 50% is taxable","Tax = T × recapture + 0.5 × T × capital gain","CF_AT = POD − tax"],"explanation":"UCC comes first because everything is measured against it; the capital gain only exists above ACB; CF_AT subtracts the total tax from the proceeds."},{"id":"fin201-a10-05","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","title":"Pick the first-year regime","prompt":"Drag each situation to the first-year multiplier (or PVCCA factor) it calls for.","buckets":["0.5 (half-year rule)","1.5 (AII)","1.0 (100% / already depreciating)"],"items":[{"text":"A new press bought this year; the question says 'half-year rule'","bucket":0},{"text":"A new van; the question says 'Accelerated Investment Incentive applies'","bucket":1},{"text":"A machine the firm has owned and depreciated for three years (remaining shield)","bucket":2},{"text":"A new asset where no rule is mentioned (default)","bucket":0},{"text":"PVCCA first-year factor (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)","bucket":1},{"text":"PVCCA first-year factor (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k)","bucket":0},{"text":"A question that says the full first-year rate is allowed","bucket":2},{"text":"CCA_1 = 1.5 × d × I0","bucket":1}],"explanation":"The multiplier m is set by the rule the question states: 0.5 by default, 1.5 under the AII, and 1.0 for an asset that has already used its first-year adjustment."},{"id":"fin201-a10-06","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","title":"Match the disposal situation to its tax result","prompt":"Match each sale situation with its tax treatment.","pairs":[{"left":"POD above ACB","right":"Recapture up to ACB at full rate, plus a capital gain taxed on half"},{"left":"UCC < POD <= ACB","right":"Recapture of POD − UCC at the full rate, no capital gain"},{"left":"POD < UCC and the class closes","right":"Terminal loss: tax saved on the shortfall"},{"left":"POD = UCC","right":"No tax effect"},{"left":"Asset sold, other assets remain in the class","right":"No immediate recapture or loss; UCC falls by POD"}],"explanation":"Compare POD with UCC and ACB. A pool that stays open absorbs the sale; a pool that closes settles the difference at once."}],"formulas":[{"id":"fin201-f10-wc","name":"Working capital","formula":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables = Current assets − Current liabilities","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-nwc"],"when":"To find NWC balances and the change in them.","trap":"Payables subtract: a rise in payables lowers NWC."},{"id":"fin201-f10-delta-nwc","name":"Cash flow from NWC","formula":"ΔNWC_t = NWC_t − NWC_(t-1); project cash flow = −ΔNWC_t; recovery at the end = +NWC","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-nwc","fin201-c10-five-categories"],"when":"Whenever NWC is invested up front or as a % of sales.","trap":"A positive change is an outflow, counted in full, and recovered at the end."},{"id":"fin201-f10-ocf-ebit","name":"OCF from EBIT","formula":"OCF = EBIT + D − Tax","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-ocf"],"when":"When EBIT, CCA and tax are known.","trap":"Add D back; tax is on EBIT, not on S − C."},{"id":"fin201-f10-ocf-shield","name":"OCF tax-shield form","formula":"OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-ocf","fin201-c10-dts"],"when":"When sales, costs, CCA and T are known; shows the CCA shield directly.","trap":"The shield is T × D, not D and not (1 − T) × D."},{"id":"fin201-f10-ocf-ni","name":"OCF bottom-up and top-down","formula":"OCF = NI + D = S − C − Tax","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-ocf"],"when":"When net income or the tax paid is the starting point.","trap":"Interest never appears in project cash flows."},{"id":"fin201-f10-fcf","name":"Free cash flow","formula":"FCF = EBIT(1 − T) + D&A − CAPEX − ΔWC","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-ocf","fin201-c10-nwc","fin201-c10-five-categories"],"when":"Yearly project cash flow after CAPEX and working capital.","trap":"FCF = OCF − CAPEX − ΔNWC; do not subtract interest."},{"id":"fin201-f10-cca","name":"CCA schedule","formula":"CCA_1 = m d I0 (m = 0.5, 1.5, 1.0); CCA_t = d UCC_(t-1); UCC_t = UCC_(t-1) − CCA_t","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-cca-schedule","fin201-c10-half-year-aii"],"when":"Year-by-year CCA and UCC at the sale date.","trap":"m applies in year 1 only; later years use d × the opening UCC, not d × I0."},{"id":"fin201-f10-dts","name":"CCA tax shield","formula":"DTS_t = CCA_t × T_C","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-dts"],"when":"Cash tax saved in year t.","trap":"Multiply by T, not (1 − T)."},{"id":"fin201-f10-pvcca","name":"PV of the CCA tax shield","formula":"PV_CCA = (I0 d T_C)/(k + d) × {1 | (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k) | (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)} − (S_N d T_C)/(k + d) × 1/(1 + k)^N","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-pvcca","fin201-c10-half-year-aii","fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"],"when":"Lump the whole stream of tax savings into one present value.","trap":"k + d not k − d; choose the first-year factor; S_N is the sale price and the term is discounted N years; subtract it whenever the asset is sold, even if the pool stays open; omit it only if nothing is sold."},{"id":"fin201-f10-pvcca-base","name":"Base PVCCA (growing perpetuity)","formula":"PV_CCA = CCA_1 T_C/(k + d) = I0 d T_C/(k + d)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-pvcca","fin201-c10-timing-of-sale"],"when":"100% case: asset already being depreciated; also the PV at a sale date of the shield lost.","trap":"It is Gordon growth with g = −d."},{"id":"fin201-f10-net-acq","name":"Net acquisitions and net salvage","formula":"Net acquisitions = additions − min(original cost, POD); net salvage = S_N(new) − S_N(old)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","fin201-c10-replacement-npv"],"when":"Replacement in the same class in the same year.","trap":"Use net amounts in PVCCA; net salvage can be negative."},{"id":"fin201-f10-cfat","name":"After-tax disposal cash flow","formula":"CF_AT = POD − T × [min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 50% × T × max(POD − ACB, 0)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat"],"when":"After-tax cash from selling a depreciated asset.","trap":"min(ACB, POD) − UCC may be negative (terminal loss refund); the capital gain exists only above ACB and only half is taxed."},{"id":"fin201-f10-npv-assembly","name":"Assembled project NPV","formula":"NPV = CF0 + PV(after-tax flows) + PV(NWC, salvage) + PV_CCA","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-expansion-npv","fin201-c10-replacement-npv"],"when":"Expansion and replacement projects.","trap":"PVCCA is already a present value; do not discount it again."},{"id":"fin201-f10-required-saving","name":"Required saving and bid price","formula":"Required after-tax OCF = −(PV of all other flows)/annuity factor; pre-tax saving = OCF/(1 − T); price = [OCF/(1 − T) + costs]/Q","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-cost-cutting","fin201-c10-bid-price"],"when":"Set NPV = 0 and solve for the saving or the price.","trap":"Divide by (1 − T) and by the annuity factor, not by N."},{"id":"fin201-f10-timing","name":"Value at the sale date of the lost shield","formula":"Closed class: NPV_N(sell) = CF_AT − d × UCC_N × T/(k + d); open pool: POD − d × POD × T/(k + d)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-timing-of-sale"],"when":"Sell now or hold?","trap":"The lost shield is an opportunity cost of selling."},{"id":"fin201-f10-eac","name":"Equivalent annual cost","formula":"EAC = PV of costs / annuity factor(k, N), annuity factor = [1 − (1 + k)^(-N)]/k","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c10-eac-taxes"],"when":"Unequal lives with replacement.","trap":"Use each option's own N; costs are negative; include PVCCA and salvage."},{"id":"fin201-f10-fisher","name":"Nominal and real rates","formula":"(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-nominal-real"],"when":"Convert between nominal and real discount rates.","trap":"Divide, do not subtract."},{"id":"fin201-f10-real-cf","name":"Real cash flow","formula":"CF_real,i = CF_nom,i/(1 + R_inf)^i","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c10-nominal-real"],"when":"Convert a nominal flow to today's dollars.","trap":"Real flows pair with the real rate only."}],"questions":[{"id":"fin201-q10-001","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An incremental cash flow for a project is best described as:","options":["the change in the firm's total after-tax cash flows that results directly from accepting the project","the project's accounting net income after a share of head-office costs","all after-tax cash flows the firm will receive in the years the project is running","the project's revenue less its cash expenses, before tax"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Incremental cash flow is cash flow with the project minus cash flow without it, after tax. Net income mixes accruals and allocations, total firm cash flow includes flows that would happen anyway, and revenue less cost before tax ignores the tax that really leaves the firm.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10; S5 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-002","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"To value a proposed brewery expansion, an analyst puts only the cash flows that the expansion causes on a timeline and values them as if they belonged to a separate, imaginary firm. This approach is called the:","options":["net acquisitions rule","stand-alone principle","equivalent annual cost method","capital rationing rule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The stand-alone principle says that once the incremental cash flows are identified you can treat them as the cash flows of a stand-alone firm and value that firm with NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s14; TB 10.1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-003","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which list contains only incremental cash flows for Cascade Cycles' new e-bike line?","options":["Revenue from the new e-bike, the e-bike's share of head-office rent, cost of the extra assembly staff","Revenue from the new e-bike, the market-study fee paid last year, cost of the extra assembly staff","Revenue from the new e-bike, lost sales of the older model, cost of the extra assembly staff","Revenue from the new e-bike, interest on the loan funding the line, cost of the extra assembly staff"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lost sales of the older model are erosion, which changes the firm's cash flow. The other lists contain an allocated cost that is paid anyway, a sunk cost already paid, and a financing cost; none of these changes with the decision.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-11; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-004","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do a project's incremental cash flows differ from its accounting net income?","options":["Incremental flows are measured before tax, while net income is measured after tax and interest","Incremental flows exclude working capital and capital spending, while net income includes both","They are the same figure, except that incremental flows are reported at year-end","Incremental flows count only after-tax cash changes caused by the project; net income includes accruals, non-cash charges and allocations"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Incremental cash flows are after tax, include CAPEX and working capital, and ignore allocations. Net income is an accrual measure that includes depreciation and allocated costs and ignores CAPEX and working capital.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s4; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-005","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an incremental cash flow of launching a new product line?","options":["An allocated share of the CEO's salary, paid whether or not the line launches","Extra inventory and receivables the new line requires","The after-tax value of an owned building that could otherwise be sold","Profit lost on an existing product that customers switch away from"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An allocated cost does not change with the decision, so its incremental cost is zero. Extra inventory (working capital), the opportunity cost of the building and erosion all change the firm's cash flow.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-006","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must each incremental flow be recorded in the year the cash actually moves rather than the year it is earned in the accounts?","options":["Tax law requires every project to be reported on a cash basis","NPV discounts cash by its date, so timing changes present value","Accrual dates are always unknown for new projects","Discount rates apply only to accounting profit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The time value of money works on dates of cash, so a sale booked in year 1 but collected in year 2 must be placed in year 2 (or its receivable treated as working capital).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-007","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Incremental cash flows are measured after tax.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Taxes are a real cash outflow, so every revenue, expense and saving is stated after tax: revenues and expenses at (1 − T), CCA at T.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-008","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The relevant cash flows for a project are the _____ cash flows: the changes in the firm's future cash flows that are a direct consequence of taking the project.","options":["accounting","average","incremental","allocated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Incremental cash flows are the with-versus-without changes. Accounting and allocated figures are not cash changes caused by the project.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-009","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"An analyst reports a project NPV of +$40,000. It already deducts the present value ($120,000, after tax) of head-office overhead that the firm pays in full whether or not the project goes ahead. What is the correct reading?","options":["The NPV is correct, because a project must cover its share of every firm cost","Removing the overhead gives NPV = −$80,000, so the project should be rejected","Removing the overhead changes the NPV only if the project is financed with debt","Overhead is not incremental; removing it gives NPV = +$160,000, so the project looks better"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Allocated overhead that does not change with the decision is not incremental. NPV rises by the $120,000 that was wrongly charged: 40,000 + 120,000 = $160,000. The decision (accept) is unchanged but the value added is understated.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-010","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Without a proposed project a firm expects after-tax cash flows of $1,150,000 a year; with it, $1,420,000 a year. Which annual figure should be discounted in the project's NPV?","options":["$270,000, the difference between the two","$1,420,000, the cash flow with the project","$1,150,000, the cash flow without the project","$2,570,000, the two added together"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Incremental cash flow is with minus without: 1,420,000 − 1,150,000 = $270,000. Only that change is caused by the project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10; TB 10.1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-011","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Mill's new oat-flour line adds $600,000 of annual sales and $280,000 of cash costs. It also cuts profit on existing products by $120,000 a year (erosion). Head-office overhead of $90,000 a year is allocated to the line but is paid whether or not it launches. The tax rate is 30%; ignore CCA. What is the line's annual incremental after-tax cash flow?","answer":140000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(600000-280000-120000)*(1-0.3)","steps":[{"label":"Incremental pre-tax cash flow = 600,000 − 280,000 − 120,000","value":200000.0,"check":"600000-280000-120000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"After-tax = pre-tax × (1 − T)","value":140000.0,"check":"(600000-280000-120000)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Allocated overhead is not incremental: ignore the $90,000.","Pre-tax incremental flow = 600,000 − 280,000 − 120,000 = 200,000","After tax: 200,000 × (1 − 0.30) = $140,000"],"keystrokes":["600000 − 280000 − 120000 = 200,000","× (1 − 0.30) = 140,000"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Charged the allocated overhead","value":77000.0,"check":"(600000-280000-120000-90000)*(1-0.3)","explain":"The overhead is paid either way, so it is not incremental."},{"id":"ignored-erosion","label":"Ignored the lost profit on existing products","value":224000.0,"check":"(600000-280000)*(1-0.3)","explain":"Erosion is a real loss of cash flow caused by the project and must be subtracted."},{"id":"pretax-not-aftertax","label":"Left the flow before tax","value":200000.0,"check":"600000-280000-120000","explain":"Every incremental flow is stated after tax: multiply by (1 − T)."}],"explanation":"Count the new sales, the new costs and the erosion; drop the allocated overhead; then apply (1 − T) to the net operating flow.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-13; SM 10-2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-012","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kestrel Foods will buy a $480,000 packaging line, pay $20,000 to have it delivered and installed (capitalized into its cost) and tie up $35,000 of extra working capital at t = 0. A $12,000 feasibility study was paid last quarter. The line goes into floor space the firm owns and has no other use for. What is the incremental cash flow at t = 0?","answer":-535000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"-(480000+20000+35000)","steps":[{"label":"Installed cost of the line","value":500000.0,"check":"480000+20000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"t = 0 incremental cash flow = −(installed cost + NWC)","value":-535000.0,"check":"-(480000+20000+35000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Installed cost = 480,000 + 20,000 = 500,000 (installation is capitalized).","The $12,000 study is sunk; the unused space has no opportunity cost.","CF0 = −(500,000 + 35,000) = −$535,000"],"keystrokes":["480000 + 20000 + 35000 = 535,000","+/− = −535,000"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Included the feasibility study","value":-547000.0,"check":"-(480000+20000+35000+12000)","explain":"The study was paid last quarter; the decision cannot change it."},{"id":"missed-installation","label":"Left out the installation cost","value":-515000.0,"check":"-(480000+35000)","explain":"Delivery and installation are part of the capital cost of the line."},{"id":"forgot-nwc","label":"Left out the extra working capital","value":-500000.0,"check":"-(480000+20000)","explain":"Working capital tied up at t = 0 is a cash outflow counted in full."}],"explanation":"At t = 0 the incremental outlay is the installed cost plus the working capital; the sunk study and the free-to-use space contribute nothing.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-12; SM 10-1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-013","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Kestrel Foods project needs equipment costing $400,000 and $30,000 of working capital at t = 0. It then yields incremental after-tax operating cash flow of $190,000 a year for 3 years (already net of erosion; a $15,000 study paid last month is excluded). The working capital is recovered at t = 3. Ignore CCA and salvage. At 12% per annum, what is the NPV?","answer":47701.35,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"-(400000+30000)+190000*(1-1.12**-3)/0.12+30000/1.12**3","steps":[{"label":"CF0 = −(equipment + NWC)","value":-430000.0,"check":"-(400000+30000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PV of the three operating flows","value":456347.94,"check":"190000*(1-1.12**-3)/0.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PV of the NWC recovery at t = 3","value":21353.41,"check":"30000/1.12**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"NPV","value":47701.35,"check":"-(400000+30000)+190000*(1-1.12**-3)/0.12+30000/1.12**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CF0 = −400,000 − 30,000 = −430,000","PV(operating) = 190,000 × [1 − 1.12^−3]/0.12 = 190,000 × 2.401831 = 456,347.94","PV(NWC recovery) = 30,000/1.12^3 = 21,353.41","NPV = −430,000 + 456,347.94 + 21,353.41 = $47,701.35"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = −430,000; C01 = 190,000, F01 = 2; C02 = 220,000 (190,000 + 30,000), F02 = 1","NPV, I = 12, CPT"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Subtracted the sunk study","value":32701.35,"check":"-(400000+30000+15000)+190000*(1-1.12**-3)/0.12+30000/1.12**3","explain":"The $15,000 study is already paid and is not incremental."},{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Never recovered the working capital","value":26347.94,"check":"-(400000+30000)+190000*(1-1.12**-3)/0.12","explain":"The working capital is released when the project ends, an inflow at t = 3."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 4-year annuity","value":168449.78,"check":"-(400000+30000)+190000*(1-1.12**-4)/0.12+30000/1.12**3","explain":"The operating flows last 3 years, so the annuity factor has N = 3."}],"explanation":"Discount the incremental flows: outlay and NWC now, a 3-year annuity of after-tax operating flow, and the NWC recovery at the end.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s17-18; L05B s3"},{"id":"fin201-q10-014","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Cycles' new e-bike line will sell 4,000 units at $2,400. About 1,100 of those buyers would otherwise have bought the older model at $1,800. The line also lifts accessory sales by $150,000. What is the line's incremental annual revenue?","options":["$9,750,000","$7,770,000","$7,110,000","$7,620,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incremental revenue = new sales 9,600,000 − lost older-model sales 1,980,000 (1,100 × $1,800, the old price) + accessories 150,000 = $7,770,000. $9,750,000 ignores erosion; $7,110,000 values the lost sales at the new price; $7,620,000 ignores the accessory spill-over.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10-2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-015","concept":"fin201-c10-incremental","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hollis Hardware's new tool line adds $850,000 of annual sales and $410,000 of cash operating costs. Head-office overhead of $70,000 is allocated to the line but is paid in full either way. The tax rate is 35%; ignore CCA. What is the incremental annual after-tax cash flow?","options":["$240,500","$440,000","$286,000","$142,500"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Incremental flow = (850,000 − 410,000)(1 − 0.35) = $286,000. $240,500 charges the allocated overhead; $440,000 is the pre-tax figure; $142,500 applies (1 − T) to revenue but not to the deductible costs.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13; SM 10-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-016","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A sunk cost is a cost that:","options":["is spent on equipment that cannot be resold","is the benefit given up by using an asset the firm already owns","is spread over the life of the project as depreciation","has already been incurred and cannot be changed by accepting or rejecting the project"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A sunk cost is already paid (or committed) and will be incurred whatever the decision, so it is irrelevant. The benefit given up by using an owned asset is an opportunity cost, which is relevant.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-017","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In capital budgeting, the opportunity cost of using an asset the firm already owns is:","options":["what the asset could be sold or rented for today, after tax","what the firm originally paid for the asset","the asset's accounting book value on the balance sheet","zero, because no cash leaves the firm when an owned asset is used"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The firm gives up the best alternative use of the asset, measured at today's market value after tax. The original price is sunk and the book value is an accounting number.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10 CTQ1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-018","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Ridgeline Brewing paid a consultant $85,000 last month, non-refundable, for a market study of a proposed taproom. In the taproom's cash-flow analysis the fee is a:","options":["year-0 cash outflow of $85,000","sunk cost, counted at zero","capital expenditure eligible for CCA","financing cost excluded only for tax reasons"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fee is already paid and cannot be recovered whether or not the taproom proceeds, so it is a sunk cost and is counted at zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-019","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Dental owns an empty unit that it could lease for $4,000 a month. A new sterilization lab would use that unit. The rent forgone is a(n):","options":["sunk cost, counted at zero","financing cost, excluded","opportunity cost, counted after tax","allocated cost, counted at zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By using the unit the firm gives up rent, so the after-tax rent is an incremental cost of the project even though no cash leaves the firm.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-020","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A division is charged $30,000 a year by head office for utilities and administration. The charge would be the same whether or not the division launches a new product. For the new product the charge is a(n):","options":["opportunity cost, counted after tax","financing cost, excluded because of debt","erosion, subtracted from revenue","allocated cost, counted at zero"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An allocated cost is overhead that does not change with the decision, so the incremental cost is zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-021","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts a sunk cost with an opportunity cost?","options":["A sunk cost is already paid and excluded; an opportunity cost is a benefit given up and is included at today's value","A sunk cost is included at its historical cost; an opportunity cost is excluded because no cash is paid","Both are excluded because neither involves a cash payment to an outside party in the year","Both are included, a sunk cost at its book value and an opportunity cost at its market value today"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The difference is whether the decision can still change the cash flow. Sunk costs cannot be changed; opportunity costs arise only if the project is taken.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-022","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT part of the year-0 outflow for a new plant built on land the firm already owns?","options":["The construction cost of the plant","The price paid for the land six years ago","Today's after-tax market value of the land","Site-preparation cost incurred only if the project goes ahead"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The old purchase price is a sunk cost. What matters is today's value of the land, which is given up if the project uses it.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-023","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the price paid for land years ago irrelevant to whether the land should be used in a project?","options":["Land is never depreciated, so its original cost can never affect the firm's taxes","The historical cost of land is always lower than its market value, so it is ignored","It cannot be changed by today's decision; the cost given up is the land's sale value now","Accounting standards prohibit the use of historical cost in any forecast of cash flows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The only relevant question is what the firm gives up by using the land today, namely its current after-tax market value. The old price is sunk.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-1, 10-10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-024","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A manager on a $70,000 salary (unchanged whether or not the project proceeds) will spend half her time on the project, and accounting allocates $35,000 a year to it. What should the analyst do?","options":["Include $35,000 after tax, that is at (1 − T)","Include $35,000 before tax because salaries are not deductible","Include $70,000 because her full pay is committed","Exclude the $35,000: it is an allocated cost that does not change with the decision"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Her pay is the same with or without the project, so the incremental cost is zero. Allocated costs are in the 'not counted' category.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-025","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"A consulting fee already paid to study a project must be deducted from the project's NPV if the project is accepted.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The fee is sunk: it has been paid and cannot be changed by accepting or rejecting the project, so it is excluded.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-026","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"If a project uses a machine the firm already owns, the cost charged to the project is the machine's current market value (after tax), not its original price.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The opportunity cost is what the firm gives up today, which is the value it could realize now; the original price is sunk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-027","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project is rejected with NPV = −$20,000. The calculation includes $50,000 (present value, after tax) for a study fee that was paid before the analysis began. What is the right interpretation?","options":["Excluding the sunk fee gives NPV = +$30,000, so the project should be accepted","The fee is relevant, so the rejection stands","Excluding the fee gives NPV = −$70,000, so the rejection is even clearer","NPV cannot change, because sunk costs are part of the initial investment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The study fee is sunk and must be removed: −20,000 + 50,000 = +$30,000. The wrong inclusion turned an acceptable project into a rejection.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-028","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Marlow Aggregates is considering a gravel plant on land it bought nine years ago for $2.8 million; the land could be sold today for $3.4 million (assume the sale triggers no tax). The plant costs $12.6 million and site preparation is $0.65 million. A $0.2 million environmental assessment was paid last year. What is the relevant year-0 cash flow?","answer":-16650000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"-(3400000+12600000+650000)","steps":[{"label":"Opportunity cost of the land (today's value)","value":3400000.0,"check":"3400000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Year-0 cash flow = −(land + plant + site preparation)","value":-16650000.0,"check":"-(3400000+12600000+650000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Land: charge today's value $3,400,000, not the $2,800,000 paid long ago.","The $200,000 assessment is sunk.","CF0 = −(3,400,000 + 12,600,000 + 650,000) = −$16,650,000"],"keystrokes":["3400000 + 12600000 + 650000 = 16,650,000","+/− = −16,650,000"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Included the sunk assessment","value":-16850000.0,"check":"-(3400000+12600000+650000+200000)","explain":"The assessment was paid last year and cannot be changed by the decision."},{"id":"historic-cost-for-opportunity","label":"Charged the original land price","value":-16050000.0,"check":"-(2800000+12600000+650000)","explain":"The opportunity cost is what the land is worth today, not what was paid for it."},{"id":"land-treated-as-free","label":"Treated the owned land as free","value":-13250000.0,"check":"-(12600000+650000)","explain":"Owned land is not free: the firm gives up its sale value."}],"explanation":"Year 0 includes the plant, the site preparation and the land at its current market value; the assessment is sunk.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-029","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sterling Foods owns land bought for $1,000,000 that is now worth $1,600,000. Land is not depreciable. If it were sold, 50% of the capital gain would be taxable at 30%. A proposed warehouse would use this land. What opportunity cost belongs in the project's year-0 cash flow?","answer":1510000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"1600000-0.5*0.3*(1600000-1000000)","steps":[{"label":"Capital gain = 1,600,000 − 1,000,000","value":600000.0,"check":"1600000-1000000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Tax = 0.5 × T × gain","value":90000.0,"check":"0.5*0.3*(1600000-1000000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"After-tax opportunity cost = 1,600,000 − tax","value":1510000.0,"check":"1600000-0.5*0.3*(1600000-1000000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Capital gain = 1,600,000 − 1,000,000 = 600,000","Tax on the gain = 0.5 × 0.30 × 600,000 = 90,000","After-tax value given up = 1,600,000 − 90,000 = $1,510,000"],"keystrokes":["600000 × 0.5 × 0.3 = 90,000","1600000 − 90000 = 1,510,000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Used the pre-tax market value","value":1600000.0,"check":"1600000","explain":"The firm would only receive the after-tax proceeds if it sold, so that is the amount given up."},{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed the whole capital gain","value":1420000.0,"check":"1600000-0.3*(1600000-1000000)","explain":"Only 50% of a capital gain is taxable."},{"id":"historic-cost-for-opportunity","label":"Used the original price","value":1000000.0,"check":"1000000","explain":"The original price is sunk; the opportunity cost is today's after-tax value."}],"explanation":"The opportunity cost of land is the after-tax cash the firm would get from selling it now: market value less tax on half of the gain.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-19; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-030","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Westmount Clinic owns space it could lease for $60,000 a year (pre-tax) for the next 6 years. A project will use the space. The tax rate is 35% and the discount rate is 10%. What is the present value of the after-tax opportunity cost of the rent forgone?","answer":169855.17,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"60000*(1-0.35)*(1-1.1**-6)/0.1","steps":[{"label":"After-tax rent forgone each year","value":39000.0,"check":"60000*(1-0.35)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"6-year annuity factor at 10%","value":4.3553,"check":"(1-1.1**-6)/0.1","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Present value","value":169855.17,"check":"60000*(1-0.35)*(1-1.1**-6)/0.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Rent is income, so the forgone rent after tax = 60,000 × (1 − 0.35) = 39,000 a year.","Annuity factor = [1 − 1.10^−6]/0.10 = 4.355261","PV = 39,000 × 4.355261 = $169,855.17"],"keystrokes":["6 N, 10 I/Y, 39000 PMT, 0 FV, CPT PV = −169,855.17 (ignore the sign)"],"errors":[{"id":"pretax-not-aftertax","label":"Used the pre-tax rent","value":261315.64,"check":"60000*(1-1.1**-6)/0.1","explain":"The rent forgone is taxable income, so only 65% of it is given up after tax."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 5-year annuity","value":147840.68,"check":"60000*(1-0.35)*(1-1.1**-5)/0.1","explain":"The space is unavailable for 6 years, so N = 6."},{"id":"forgot-pv","label":"Added the six rents without discounting","value":234000.0,"check":"60000*(1-0.35)*6","explain":"Rents received in the future must be discounted at 10%."}],"explanation":"An opportunity cost can be a stream: the after-tax rent forgone each year, valued as an annuity.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s27-29"},{"id":"fin201-q10-031","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new line would cost $750,000 for equipment and would use a lot the firm bought five years ago for $400,000; the lot could be sold today for $520,000 (after tax). A $45,000 design fee was paid last month. What is the relevant year-0 outlay?","options":["$1,315,000","$1,270,000","$1,150,000","$750,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Relevant outlay = equipment 750,000 + land at today's after-tax value 520,000 = $1,270,000. $1,315,000 adds the sunk design fee; $1,150,000 charges the lot's old price; $750,000 treats the owned lot as free.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-1; SM 10-19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-032","concept":"fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A junior analyst reports an NPV of +$25,000. It subtracts a $30,000 market study already paid and ignores that the project would use a machine the firm owns, which could be sold for $90,000 after tax. What is the corrected NPV?","options":["$55,000","$145,000","−$35,000","−$95,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Corrected NPV = 25,000 + 30,000 (remove the sunk study) − 90,000 (charge the opportunity cost) = −$35,000, so reject. $55,000 removes only the sunk cost; $145,000 adds the opportunity cost instead of subtracting it; −$95,000 subtracts the sunk cost a second time.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s11; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-033","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In capital budgeting, erosion is:","options":["the decline in a project's equipment value due to wear and obsolescence","the loss in value of inventory and receivables as the project ages","a tax deduction for the cost of capital assets used up each year","a loss of cash flow on the firm's existing products caused by launching the new project"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Erosion (also called cannibalization) is cash flow the new product takes away from the firm's existing products. Wear of equipment is depreciation, not erosion.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10 CTQ5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-034","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A positive side effect (spillover or synergy) of a project is an incremental cash flow that:","options":["arises on the firm's other products because of the project and is added to the project","is ignored because the project itself did not directly produce the cash flow","is counted only if the firm has guaranteed it in a signed contract","is financed with debt and is therefore treated as a financing flow"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spillovers change the firm's cash flow because the project is accepted, so they belong in the analysis, after tax.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-035","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeside Coffee launches a premium cold brew. Analysts estimate that 15% of its buyers would otherwise have bought Lakeside's regular iced coffee. The lost iced-coffee profit is an example of:","options":["a sunk cost","erosion","the opportunity cost of an owned asset","a financing cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Profit lost on an existing product because of the new product is erosion and is subtracted (after tax) from the new product's cash flows.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-036","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A printer maker introduces a cheap new printer, and its sales of ink cartridges (a different product line) rise as a result. For the printer project, the extra ink profit is:","options":["erosion that should be subtracted","a sunk cost that should be ignored","a positive side effect that should be added to the project","a financing effect that is excluded from cash flows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The extra ink profit changes the firm's cash flow because the printer is launched, so it is an incremental spillover and is added after tax.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-037","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does erosion differ from an opportunity cost?","options":["Erosion is stated before tax in the analysis, while an opportunity cost is stated after tax","Erosion is a financing cost excluded from the analysis, while an opportunity cost is an operating cost","They are the same idea: both are costs that the firm has already incurred before the decision","Erosion is lost cash flow on other products; an opportunity cost is the value of an owned asset's best alternative use"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both are incremental and both are after tax, but erosion arises on other product lines while an opportunity cost arises from using an owned asset.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-038","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT included in a project's incremental cash flows?","options":["Interest paid on the bank loan raised for the project","Profit lost on an existing product that the project displaces","Extra profit on a complementary product caused by the project","Working capital the project requires"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Financing costs such as interest, dividends and principal repaid are never part of project cash flows. The other three change the firm's operating cash flow.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10-18"},{"id":"fin201-q10-039","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why are interest payments left out of a project's cash flows?","options":["Interest is not tax deductible, so it would only distort the after-tax flows","Financing is analyzed separately; the discount rate already charges for debt and equity, so interest would double count","Interest is a sunk cost that the firm pays whatever it decides about the project","Interest is always so small relative to operating cash flow that it can be ignored"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The project's cash flows are discounted at a rate that already reflects the cost of funds. Subtracting interest as well would charge for financing twice.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-18; TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-040","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Zenith Bikes expects a rival to launch an equivalent e-bike next year whether or not Zenith does, so Zenith's older model will lose those sales anyway. How should the lost sales be treated in the e-bike analysis?","options":["Deduct them in full, because any decline in existing sales counts","Deduct half of them, because the loss is partly caused by the project","Ignore them: erosion is relevant only when the sales would not otherwise be lost","Add them as a sunk cost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With or without the e-bike, the older model loses those sales, so the project does not change the firm's cash flow there. Erosion is relevant only if the sales would not be lost anyway.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10 CTQ5-7"},{"id":"fin201-q10-041","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Interest on the loan used to fund a project is deducted when computing the project's incremental cash flows.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Interest is a financing cost: it is part of the cash flow to creditors, not of the cash flow from the project's assets. The discount rate accounts for it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10-18"},{"id":"fin201-q10-042","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's NPV is +$12,000 when financing is left to the discount rate. An analyst who also subtracts the loan's after-tax interest from the cash flows reports −$30,000. Which interpretation is right?","options":["The −$30,000 is more conservative because it deducts interest, and is therefore correct","Both are correct; the sign depends on how the lender structures the loan","Only the −$30,000 captures the tax shield on interest, so it is the better figure","The −$30,000 double counts the cost of debt; the +$12,000 figure is the correct one"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The discount rate already charges for the cost of capital, so removing interest from the cash flows and discounting gives the right NPV. Subtracting interest as well counts the cost of funds twice.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-18"},{"id":"fin201-q10-043","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A negative impact on the cash flows of an existing product from introducing a new product is called _____.","options":["erosion","synergy","depreciation","recapture"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Erosion is the effect of a new product on the cash flows of the firm's existing products.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-044","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project should always deduct the profit it takes from existing products, even if a competitor would have taken those sales anyway.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Erosion is relevant only when the sales would not otherwise be lost; if they would be lost anyway, the project does not change the cash flow.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-045","concept":"fin201-c10-side-effects-financing","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sunridge's new snack takes sales from an existing snack, costing $85,000 a year of pre-tax profit. The tax rate is 30%. What is the annual after-tax cash-flow effect of this erosion?","options":["−$85,000","−$59,500","−$25,500","$59,500"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lost profit is a pre-tax loss, so the after-tax effect is −85,000(1 − 0.30) = −$59,500. −$85,000 leaves it before tax; −$25,500 multiplies by T instead of (1 − T); $59,500 has the wrong sign (erosion lowers cash flow).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-046","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which pairing of cash-flow type and tax multiplier is correct?","options":["Revenues and expenses: both multiplied by T","Cash expenses and revenues: counted in full (× 1)","Capital expenditure and the change in NWC: counted in full (× 1)","CCA and sunk costs: multiplied by (1 − T)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CAPEX and NWC are cash moved through the balance sheet, counted at 100%. Revenues and cash expenses are × (1 − T) and CCA is × T.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-047","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which cash flows are multiplied by (1 − T) to find their after-tax effect?","options":["Sunk costs and allocated costs","CAPEX and changes in NWC","CCA only","Revenues and cash expenses"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each $1 of revenue attracts $T of tax, leaving (1 − T); each $1 of deductible expense saves $T of tax, so it costs (1 − T).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-048","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A $20,000 delivery truck bought for a project falls into which category of cash flow?","options":["× 1: capital expenditure","× (1 − T): an expense","× T: a tax shield","× 0: a sunk cost"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The purchase price is cash paid out of after-tax funds with no immediate tax effect, so it is counted at 100%. The tax relief arrives later as CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-049","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"The tax cash flows from selling a depreciated machine (recapture, terminal loss or capital gain) belong to which category?","options":["Revenue-type flows, multiplied by (1 − T)","Other tax flows, handled by the CF_AT formula","Tax shields, like CCA, multiplied by T","Capital expenditure, counted in full (× 1)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fifth category is salvage with tax effects. It does not reduce to a simple multiplier; the sheet formula CF_AT = POD − T[min(ACB, POD) − UCC] − 0.5T max(POD − ACB, 0) is used.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10, s18"},{"id":"fin201-q10-050","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is a $10,000 increase in cash expenses multiplied by (1 − T) while $10,000 of CCA is multiplied by T?","options":["Expenses are subject to tax, but CCA is exempt from tax, so the multiplier reverses","CCA is usually much smaller than cash expenses, so it is scaled down by T","The expense is cash paid, partly offset by tax saved; CCA involves no cash, so only the tax saved counts","Both are multiplied by T, but CCA is reported on a separate line of the income statement"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A cash expense of $1 costs $1 but saves $T of tax, net (1 − T). CCA costs nothing in cash but still saves $T of tax, net T.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13-14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-051","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of these items is NOT counted at 100% (× 1) in the analysis?","options":["Capital expenditure","An increase in net working capital","Recovery of net working capital at the end","Revenue"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Revenue is taxed, so it is × (1 − T). CAPEX, ΔNWC and the NWC recovery are counted in full.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-052","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is capital expenditure counted at 100% rather than at (1 − T)?","options":["It is cash paid from already-taxed funds with no immediate tax effect; tax relief comes later through CCA","Capital assets are exempt from tax, so no multiplier applies to their cost","CAPEX is a sunk cost, since the money is committed once the project is approved","The tax saving is received immediately as a full deduction in the year of purchase"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The purchase price itself is not an expense, so it does not reduce tax when it is paid. It reduces tax gradually through CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-053","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"At T = 30%, which ordering shows the after-tax value of $1 of each item from smallest to largest?","options":["Sunk cost ($0) < revenue ($0.70) < CCA ($0.30) < CAPEX ($1.00)","Sunk cost ($0) < CCA ($0.30) < revenue ($0.70) < CAPEX ($1.00)","CCA ($0.30) < sunk cost ($0) < CAPEX ($1.00) < revenue ($0.70)","Sunk cost ($0) < CAPEX ($1.00) < CCA ($0.30) < revenue ($0.70)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multipliers: sunk × 0, CCA × T = 0.30, revenue × (1 − T) = 0.70, CAPEX × 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-054","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A dollar of additional revenue adds $(1 − T) to after-tax cash flow.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The firm pays $T of tax on each extra dollar of revenue and keeps $(1 − T).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-055","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"CCA is a cash flow that is multiplied by (1 − T).","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. CCA is a non-cash deduction; its cash effect is the tax it saves, so it is multiplied by T.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-056","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Revenues and cash expenses are multiplied by _____ to get their after-tax effect.","options":["T","(1 + T)","(1 − T)","1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each dollar of revenue or deductible expense changes tax by T, leaving (1 − T) of the pre-tax amount.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-057","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"With T = 30%, what is the cash effect of $1 of CCA?","options":["+$0.70: a tax saving","−$0.30: a tax payment","$0: CCA is non-cash","+$0.30: a tax saving"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CCA reduces taxable income by $1, which saves $0.30 of tax. It is a non-cash deduction but not a zero-cash item.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-058","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"For each of the following, determine the after-tax cash flow, assuming the tax rate is 30%: (a) an increase in sales revenue of $100,000.","answer":70000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"100000*(1-0.3)","steps":[{"label":"After-tax revenue = 100,000 × (1 − T)","value":70000.0,"check":"100000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Revenue is category (c): multiply by (1 − T).","100,000 × (1 − 0.30) = $70,000"],"keystrokes":["100000 × (1 − 0.3) = 70,000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-after-tax","label":"Left the revenue before tax","value":100000.0,"check":"100000","explain":"Extra revenue is taxed; only (1 − T) of it is kept."},{"id":"wrong-multiplier","label":"Multiplied by T instead of (1 − T)","value":30000.0,"check":"100000*0.3","explain":"T is the tax paid; the after-tax amount is (1 − T)."}],"explanation":"Revenue is multiplied by (1 − T): $100,000 × 0.70 = $70,000.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q11a"},{"id":"fin201-q10-059","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"For each of the following, determine the after-tax cash flow, assuming the tax rate is 30%: (b) $150,000 spent last year on advertising the benefits of this project.","answer":0.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"150000*0","steps":[{"label":"Sunk cost counted at zero","value":0.0,"check":"150000*0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["The advertising was spent last year and cannot be changed by the decision: it is a sunk cost.","Sunk costs are multiplied by 0: after-tax cash flow = $0"],"keystrokes":["Enter 0: the cost is sunk"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Treated the advertising as a cost after tax","value":-105000.0,"check":"-150000*(1-0.3)","explain":"A sunk cost is not incremental, whatever its tax treatment."},{"id":"counted-sunk-pretax","label":"Treated the advertising as a pre-tax outflow","value":-150000.0,"check":"-150000","explain":"The money was spent last year; it is not changed by the decision."}],"explanation":"Sunk costs belong to category (a) and count at zero.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q11b"},{"id":"fin201-q10-060","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":["fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"For each of the following, determine the after-tax cash flow, assuming the tax rate is 30%: (c) selling an asset for $197,000 which you had bought for $590,000 and had depreciated for tax purposes to an undeducted capital cost of $155,566.41.","answer":184569.92,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"197000-0.3*(197000-155566.41)-0.5*0.3*0","steps":[{"label":"Recapture = min(ACB, POD) − UCC = 197,000 − 155,566.41","value":41433.59,"check":"197000-155566.41","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Capital gain = max(POD − ACB, 0) = max(197,000 − 590,000, 0)","value":0.0,"check":"0*197000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"CF_AT = POD − T × recapture − 0.5 × T × gain","value":184569.92,"check":"197000-0.3*(197000-155566.41)-0.5*0.3*0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["POD = 197,000; ACB = 590,000; UCC = 155,566.41; T = 0.30","min(ACB, POD) = 197,000, so recapture = 197,000 − 155,566.41 = 41,433.59","POD is below ACB, so there is no capital gain: max(197,000 − 590,000, 0) = 0","CF_AT = 197,000 − 0.30(41,433.59) − 0.5(0.30)(0) = 197,000 − 12,430.08 = $184,569.92"],"keystrokes":["197000 − 155566.41 = 41,433.59","× 0.3 = 12,430.08","197000 − 12430.08 = 184,569.92"],"errors":[{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole proceeds","value":137900.0,"check":"197000*(1-0.3)","explain":"Only the excess over UCC is recaptured, not the whole sale price."},{"id":"acb-not-min","label":"Used ACB instead of min(ACB, POD) in the recapture","value":66669.92,"check":"197000-0.3*(590000-155566.41)","explain":"The sale price is below cost, so recapture cannot exceed POD − UCC."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Used POD − ACB as the tax base","value":314900.0,"check":"197000-0.3*(197000-590000)","explain":"POD − ACB is negative here, which wrongly creates a tax refund."},{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Ignored tax on the sale","value":197000.0,"check":"197000","explain":"The recapture of CCA is taxable income and must be taxed."}],"explanation":"The asset sold below its cost, so there is no capital gain; the only tax is on the recapture of CCA (POD − UCC).","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q11c; L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-061","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"This year, Mid-Prairie Dairy's cheese project has revenue of $420,000, labour of $150,000, materials of $95,000 and CCA of $28,000. It also buys $200,000 of equipment and adds $18,000 to working capital this year, and a $25,000 study was paid earlier. The tax rate is 30%. What is the project's net after-tax cash flow for this year?","answer":-87100.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(420000-150000-95000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*28000-200000-18000","steps":[{"label":"Revenue less cash expenses, after tax (× (1 − T))","value":122500.0,"check":"(420000-150000-95000)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA tax shield (× T)","value":8400.0,"check":"0.3*28000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"CAPEX and ΔNWC (× 1)","value":-218000.0,"check":"-(200000+18000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Net after-tax cash flow","value":-87100.0,"check":"(420000-150000-95000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*28000-200000-18000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Operating: (420,000 − 150,000 − 95,000)(1 − 0.30) = 175,000 × 0.70 = 122,500","CCA shield: 0.30 × 28,000 = 8,400","CAPEX and NWC counted in full: −200,000 − 18,000 = −218,000","The earlier study is sunk: 0","Net = 122,500 + 8,400 − 218,000 = −$87,100"],"keystrokes":["175000 × 0.7 = 122,500","+ 0.3 × 28000 = 130,900","− 218000 = −87,100"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Deducted the sunk study","value":-104600.0,"check":"(420000-150000-95000-25000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*28000-200000-18000","explain":"A study paid earlier is sunk and counts at zero."},{"id":"taxed-capex","label":"Multiplied CAPEX by (1 − T)","value":-27100.0,"check":"(420000-150000-95000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*28000-200000*(1-0.3)-18000","explain":"Capital expenditure is counted in full; its tax relief comes through CCA."},{"id":"taxed-nwc","label":"Multiplied the NWC change by (1 − T)","value":-81700.0,"check":"(420000-150000-95000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*28000-200000-18000*(1-0.3)","explain":"Changes in working capital are counted in full."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":-95500.0,"check":"(420000-150000-95000)*(1-0.3)-200000-18000","explain":"CCA saves T × 28,000 of tax even though it is non-cash."}],"explanation":"Run each item through its category: expenses and revenue at (1 − T), CCA at T, CAPEX and NWC at 1, the sunk study at 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-14; S5 s15-17"},{"id":"fin201-q10-062","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project raises annual cash expenses by $64,000. The tax rate is 35%. What is the after-tax cash-flow effect each year?","options":["−$41,600","−$64,000","−$22,400","$41,600"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A deductible expense costs (1 − T) of its amount: −64,000(1 − 0.35) = −$41,600. −$64,000 ignores the tax saving; −$22,400 multiplies by T instead of (1 − T); $41,600 has the wrong sign.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-063","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a year the firm claims $36,000 of CCA on a project asset. The tax rate is 30%. What is the cash effect of the CCA that year?","options":["$36,000","$10,800","$25,200","$0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCA is non-cash, but it saves T × CCA of tax: 0.30 × 36,000 = $10,800. $36,000 counts the CCA itself as a cash inflow; $25,200 multiplies by (1 − T); $0 treats non-cash as no effect at all.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-064","concept":"fin201-c10-five-categories","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gordon Gears' project has year-1 revenue of $200,000 and cash costs of $120,000. A $25,000 study was paid last year. The tax rate is 30%; ignore CCA and capital spending. What is the year-1 after-tax operating cash flow?","options":["$38,500","$80,000","$56,000","$20,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Operating flow = (200,000 − 120,000)(1 − 0.30) = $56,000. $38,500 subtracts the sunk study; $80,000 is before tax; $20,000 taxes the revenue but not the deductible costs.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-065","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Operating cash flow (OCF) for a project year is:","options":["net income minus depreciation","EBIT − taxes − capital expenditure","revenue minus total expenses including CCA","EBIT + depreciation (CCA) − taxes"],"answer":3,"explanation":"OCF = EBIT + D − Tax: start from EBIT, add back the non-cash CCA and subtract the tax actually paid. Net income minus depreciation moves the wrong way; capital expenditure belongs to free cash flow, not OCF.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15; TB 10.5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-066","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which expression equals OCF, where S is sales, C is cash costs, D is CCA and T is the tax rate?","options":["(S − C)(1 − T) + T × D","(S − C)(1 − T) + (1 − T) × D","(S − C)(1 − T) − T × D","(S − C − D)(1 − T)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"After-tax operating profit is (S − C)(1 − T), and CCA adds a tax shield of T × D. (S − C − D)(1 − T) is net income, which omits the add-back of D; (1 − T) × D or − T × D misstates the shield.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15; SM 10-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-067","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An analyst computes OCF as net income plus CCA. This is the:","options":["top-down approach","bottom-up approach","tax-shield approach","free-cash-flow approach"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bottom-up starts from net income and adds back depreciation. Top-down is S − C − Tax; the tax-shield approach is (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-068","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"An analyst writes OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D. This is called the:","options":["bottom-up approach","top-down approach","tax-shield approach","stand-alone approach"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The T × D term is the depreciation (CCA) tax shield, so this form is the tax-shield approach. All four forms give the same OCF.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15; SM 10-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-069","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a project's OCF differ from its free cash flow in the same year?","options":["FCF is OCF with the tax shield removed","FCF is OCF with interest added back","FCF includes CCA but OCF does not","FCF is OCF less CAPEX and the increase in NWC"],"answer":3,"explanation":"OCF is the cash generated by operations. Free cash flow also subtracts the cash invested in assets (CAPEX) and in working capital: FCF = OCF − CAPEX − ΔNWC.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s5; S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-070","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following does NOT enter a project's operating cash flow?","options":["Interest paid on the project's financing","Sales revenue from the project's output","Cash operating expenses of running it","Income tax on the project's EBIT"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interest is a financing cost and never appears in project cash flows. Revenue, cash expenses and the tax on EBIT are all part of OCF.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.5; SM 10-18"},{"id":"fin201-q10-071","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is depreciation (CCA) added back when OCF is computed from EBIT?","options":["It is a tax payment that must be reversed to find cash","It is non-cash: it reduced EBIT but no cash left the firm","It is financed with debt, so its cash cost is already counted","It measures the fall in the asset's market value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCA is subtracted to find taxable income, so EBIT is understated relative to cash. Adding it back recovers the cash, and the lower tax it caused is already in the Tax term.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15; SM 10-4"},{"id":"fin201-q10-072","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Holding sales, costs and the tax rate (30%) fixed, CCA rises by $10,000. What happens to OCF?","options":["It rises by $10,000, the full increase in CCA","It falls by $7,000, the after-tax cost of the CCA","It rises by $3,000, because taxes fall by that amount","It is unchanged, because CCA is non-cash"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D, only the T × D term changes: 0.30 × 10,000 = $3,000. The extra CCA is non-cash but lowers the tax bill.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-4"},{"id":"fin201-q10-073","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Interest expense is subtracted when computing a project's operating cash flow.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Interest is a financing cost; it is excluded from project OCF and the discount rate carries the cost of capital.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-074","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Starting from the same sales, costs, CCA and tax rate, the bottom-up form (NI + D) and the tax-shield form can give different OCF values.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. All the forms are algebraically identical: NI + D = (S − C − D)(1 − T) + D = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-075","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × _____ , where the missing term is the CCA claimed.","options":["I0","ΔNWC","interest","D"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The tax shield on CCA is T × D, where D is the CCA (depreciation) claimed in the year.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-076","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A problem gives EBIT, the CCA claimed and the tax paid for the year. Which formula gives OCF directly?","options":["OCF = EBIT + D − Tax","OCF = EBIT(1 − T) − D","OCF = EBIT − Tax − D","OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) − D"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Add back the non-cash CCA to EBIT and subtract the tax paid: OCF = EBIT + D − Tax. The other options subtract D, which is wrong because CCA is non-cash.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-077","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sales, cash costs and CCA are fixed, and EBIT is positive. If the corporate tax rate rises, what happens to OCF?","options":["It rises, because the larger CCA shield outweighs everything else","It falls: the lost after-tax operating profit exceeds the larger CCA shield","It is unchanged, because the tax rate only affects accounting profit","It rises, because costs become cheaper after tax at the higher rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × D, so dOCF/dT = −(S − C) + D = −EBIT. With positive EBIT, a higher T lowers OCF even though the shield is larger.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-078","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A project's EBIT is $120,000, the CCA claimed is $45,000 and the tax paid is $36,000. What is the operating cash flow?","answer":129000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"120000+45000-36000","steps":[{"label":"OCF = EBIT + D − Tax","value":129000.0,"check":"120000+45000-36000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["OCF = EBIT + D − Tax","OCF = 120,000 + 45,000 − 36,000 = $129,000"],"keystrokes":["120000 + 45000 − 36000 = 129,000"],"errors":[{"id":"ocf-as-net-income","label":"Did not add CCA back","value":84000.0,"check":"120000-36000","explain":"That is net income; CCA is non-cash and must be added back."},{"id":"tax-added","label":"Added the tax instead of subtracting it","value":201000.0,"check":"120000+45000+36000","explain":"Tax paid is a cash outflow and is subtracted."}],"explanation":"Start from EBIT, add the non-cash CCA and subtract the cash tax paid.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15; SM 10-4"},{"id":"fin201-q10-079","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm expects sales of $812,000, cash costs of $455,000 and CCA of $96,000. The tax rate is 32%. What is the operating cash flow?","answer":273480.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"812000-455000-0.32*(812000-455000-96000)","steps":[{"label":"EBIT = S − C − D","value":261000.0,"check":"812000-455000-96000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Tax = T × EBIT","value":83520.0,"check":"0.32*(812000-455000-96000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"OCF = S − C − Tax","value":273480.0,"check":"812000-455000-0.32*(812000-455000-96000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["EBIT = 812,000 − 455,000 − 96,000 = 261,000","Tax = 0.32 × 261,000 = 83,520","OCF = EBIT + D − Tax = 261,000 + 96,000 − 83,520 = $273,480","Check: (357,000)(0.68) + 0.32(96,000) = 242,760 + 30,720 = 273,480"],"keystrokes":["812000 − 455000 = 357,000","− 0.32 × 261000 = 273,480"],"errors":[{"id":"ocf-as-net-income","label":"Stopped at net income","value":177480.0,"check":"(812000-455000-96000)*(1-0.32)","explain":"Net income is after the non-cash CCA; add CCA back to get OCF."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Taxed S − C and ignored the CCA shield","value":242760.0,"check":"(812000-455000)*(1-0.32)","explain":"CCA lowers tax by T × D; leaving it out understates OCF."},{"id":"pretax-not-aftertax","label":"Left out tax","value":357000.0,"check":"812000-455000","explain":"OCF is after tax; subtract the tax paid."}],"explanation":"Any of the equivalent OCF forms gives $273,480; the top-down version is S − C − Tax.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-5; S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-080","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"An asset costs $500,000 and is in a 20% CCA class; the half-year rule applies. In year 2 the project's sales less cash costs are $190,000 and the tax rate is 30%. What is the year-2 operating cash flow?","answer":160000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"190000*(1-0.3)+0.3*(0.2*500000*(1-0.5*0.2))","steps":[{"label":"CCA in year 1 = 0.5 × 0.20 × 500,000","value":50000.0,"check":"0.5*0.2*500000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA in year 2 = d × UCC_1","value":90000.0,"check":"0.2*(500000-0.5*0.2*500000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"After-tax operating profit","value":133000.0,"check":"190000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"OCF_2 = (S − C)(1 − T) + T × CCA_2","value":160000.0,"check":"190000*(1-0.3)+0.3*(0.2*500000*(1-0.5*0.2))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.20 × 500,000 = 50,000; UCC_1 = 450,000","CCA_2 = 0.20 × 450,000 = 90,000","(S − C)(1 − T) = 190,000 × 0.70 = 133,000","T × CCA_2 = 0.30 × 90,000 = 27,000","OCF_2 = 133,000 + 27,000 = $160,000"],"keystrokes":["190000 × 0.7 = 133,000","+ 0.3 × 90000 = 160,000"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-year-cca","label":"Used year-1 CCA","value":148000.0,"check":"190000*(1-0.3)+0.3*(0.5*0.2*500000)","explain":"Year 2 uses d × UCC_1, not the year-1 amount."},{"id":"straight-line-cca","label":"Used d × I0 for year 2","value":163000.0,"check":"190000*(1-0.3)+0.3*(0.2*500000)","explain":"Declining balance applies d to the UCC, not to the original cost."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Added the whole CCA","value":223000.0,"check":"190000*(1-0.3)+(0.2*500000*(1-0.5*0.2))","explain":"The CCA is worth only T × CCA after tax."}],"explanation":"Find year-2 CCA from the UCC schedule, then add its tax shield to the after-tax operating profit.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s15, s19-20; SM 10-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-081","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project will sell 40,000 units at $6.50. Variable cost is $3.90 per unit and fixed cash costs are $28,000. CCA is $52,000 and the tax rate is 34%. What is the operating cash flow?","answer":67840.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000)-0.34*(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000-52000)","steps":[{"label":"Sales less cash costs, S − C","value":76000.0,"check":"40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"EBIT = S − C − D","value":24000.0,"check":"40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000-52000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Tax = T × EBIT","value":8160.0,"check":"0.34*(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000-52000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"OCF = S − C − Tax","value":67840.0,"check":"(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000)-0.34*(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000-52000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["S = 40,000 × 6.50 = 260,000; C = 40,000 × 3.90 + 28,000 = 184,000; S − C = 76,000","EBIT = 76,000 − 52,000 = 24,000; Tax = 0.34 × 24,000 = 8,160","OCF (top-down) = 76,000 − 8,160 = $67,840","Check: 76,000(0.66) + 0.34(52,000) = 50,160 + 17,680 = 67,840"],"keystrokes":["260000 − 156000 − 28000 = 76,000","− 0.34 × 24000 = 67,840"],"errors":[{"id":"ebit-as-ocf","label":"Reported EBIT as the cash flow","value":24000.0,"check":"40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000-52000","explain":"EBIT is after the non-cash CCA; OCF adds CCA back and subtracts tax."},{"id":"ocf-as-net-income","label":"Net income without adding CCA back","value":15840.0,"check":"(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000-52000)*(1-0.34)","explain":"Net income is not cash flow; add the CCA back."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Ignored the CCA tax shield","value":50160.0,"check":"(40000*6.5-40000*3.9-28000)*(1-0.34)","explain":"Taxing S − C instead of EBIT overlooks the shield T × D."}],"explanation":"Top-down: S − C − Tax with tax charged on EBIT (after CCA).","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s16-17; SM 10-26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-082","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has sales of $640,000, cash costs of $360,000 and CCA of $75,000. The tax rate is 28%. Using the tax-shield approach, what is OCF?","options":["$201,600","$276,600","$222,600","$147,600"],"answer":2,"explanation":"OCF = (640,000 − 360,000)(0.72) + 0.28(75,000) = 201,600 + 21,000 = $222,600. $201,600 leaves out the CCA shield; $276,600 adds the whole CCA instead of T × CCA; $147,600 is net income, which does not add CCA back.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-5; S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-083","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Last year the tax rate was 30%; this year it is 40%. Sales less cash costs are $200,000 and CCA is $50,000. What is this year's OCF?","options":["$155,000","$120,000","$150,000","$140,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"OCF = 200,000(0.60) + 0.40(50,000) = 120,000 + 20,000 = $140,000. $155,000 still uses last year's 30% rate; $120,000 omits the CCA shield; $150,000 multiplies the CCA by (1 − T) instead of T.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-5, 10-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-084","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"There will be a positive change in net working capital in a year when:","options":["Accounts payable increase","Salaries increase","Corporate tax increases","Inventory increases","Capital expenditure increases"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net working capital = cash + inventory + receivables − payables. An increase in inventory raises current assets and so raises NWC, a positive change and a cash outflow. Payables increasing lowers NWC; salaries, tax and capital expenditure are not working-capital accounts.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-085","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Net working capital is:","options":["cash + inventory + receivables − payables","cash + inventory + receivables + payables","fixed assets − long-term debt","receivables + payables − inventory"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WC = Cash + Inventory + Receivables − Payables, equal to current assets minus current liabilities. Payables are subtracted because suppliers finance part of the investment.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-086","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dunmore Toys must carry extra inventory and give customers 60 days to pay for a new product. These requirements will cause:","options":["a negative change in NWC and a cash inflow","a positive change in NWC and a cash outflow at launch","a tax deduction for the extra inventory in year 1","an increase in the CCA claimed on the product"],"answer":1,"explanation":"More inventory and receivables raise current assets, so NWC increases. A positive change in NWC uses cash and is counted in full.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.4; L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-087","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's suppliers agree to longer payment terms, so payables rise. In that year the effect on net working capital and cash flow is:","options":["NWC rises, a cash outflow","NWC is unchanged because payables are not working capital","NWC falls, a cash inflow","NWC rises, a cash inflow"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Payables are subtracted in NWC. When they rise, NWC falls (a negative change), so cash is released for the project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-088","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How is the change in NWC treated differently from capital expenditure?","options":["NWC is taxed at (1 − T) as an expense, but CAPEX is counted in full","NWC earns CCA over the project's life but CAPEX earns none","NWC is treated as a sunk cost once invested, but CAPEX is not sunk","Both are counted in full, but NWC is recovered at the end while CAPEX earns CCA shields"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both are cash invested out of after-tax funds. CAPEX is recovered slowly through CCA (and salvage); NWC is recovered in full when inventory is sold, receivables collected and payables paid.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12; SM 10 CTQ2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-089","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an example of a positive change in NWC?","options":["Accounts payable rise as suppliers extend credit","Inventory rises as the project stocks up","Receivables rise as customers are given credit","The project's cash balance rises as cash is held back"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A rise in payables lowers NWC. Rising inventory, receivables or cash all increase current assets and so increase NWC.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; PRAC q6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-090","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is NWC invested at the start of a project assumed to be recovered at the end?","options":["NWC earns CCA over the project's life and is written off at the end","Inventory is sold, receivables collected and payables paid, releasing the cash tied up","Tax law refunds the tax paid on NWC when the project's assets are sold","NWC becomes salvage value that is taxed as a capital gain at the end"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NWC behaves like a loan to the project: it is supplied at the start and returned at the end as the working-capital accounts unwind.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.2; SM 10 CTQ2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-091","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the change in NWC not multiplied by (1 − T)?","options":["Working capital is exempt from income tax by law, so no adjustment is needed","It is recovered at the end, so the tax effects cancel out over the project","It is cash moved in and out of balance-sheet accounts, not a revenue or expense, so no tax effect","NWC is an allocated cost, and allocated costs are exempt from the (1 − T) rule"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Building inventory or granting credit is not deductible when it happens, so the NWC investment is counted at 100%.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-092","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A positive change in net working capital is a cash outflow in that year.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. NWC rising means more cash tied up in inventory and receivables, so project cash flow = −ΔNWC is negative.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12; S6 s5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-093","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"At the end of a project the net working capital that was invested is _____.","options":["depreciated for tax","expensed immediately","taxed as a capital gain","recovered as a cash inflow"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Working capital is released when the project ends and counts as a cash inflow in the final year.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s5; TB 10.3"},{"id":"fin201-q10-094","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's NWC is 12% of sales. Sales fall in year 4 from $800,000 to $700,000. How does this show up in the year-4 cash flow?","options":["As a $12,000 cash inflow, because NWC falls","As a $12,000 cash outflow","As an $84,000 cash inflow","It has no effect until the final year"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NWC falls by 12% × 100,000 = $12,000, a negative change in NWC and so a cash inflow of $12,000 in year 4.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s5; SM 10-35"},{"id":"fin201-q10-095","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm offers longer credit terms to win a project's customers, so its receivables rise. All else equal, what happens to the project's NPV?","options":["NPV rises, because sales rise","NPV falls: a larger NWC is invested earlier than it is recovered","NPV is unchanged, because NWC is fully recovered","NPV falls, because receivables are taxed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Extra NWC is paid out early and returned later, so its present value cost is positive even though the amount is fully recovered.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.4"},{"id":"fin201-q10-096","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"The NWC recovered at the end of a project is a taxable capital gain.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Recovery is the release of cash that was invested; it is an inflow counted in full with no tax.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-097","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Dunmore Toys' project needs net working capital of $20,000 at t = 0, $25,000 at t = 1 and $22,000 at t = 2; at t = 3 all NWC is released. The tax rate is 30% and the discount rate is 10%. What is the present value of the cash flows from NWC?","answer":-5537.19,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-20000-(25000-20000)/1.1+(25000-22000)/1.1**2+22000/1.1**3","steps":[{"label":"Cash flow at t = 1 = −(25,000 − 20,000)","value":-5000.0,"check":"-(25000-20000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Cash flow at t = 2 = −(22,000 − 25,000)","value":3000.0,"check":"-(22000-25000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Cash flow at t = 3 (recovery)","value":22000.0,"check":"22000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PV of the NWC flows","value":-5537.19,"check":"-20000-(25000-20000)/1.1+(25000-22000)/1.1**2+22000/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["t = 0: −20,000 (NWC invested)","t = 1: −(25,000 − 20,000) = −5,000","t = 2: −(22,000 − 25,000) = +3,000","t = 3: +22,000 (recovery)","PV = −20,000 − 5,000/1.1 + 3,000/1.1² + 22,000/1.1³ = −$5,537.19"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = −20000; C01 = −5000; C02 = 3000; C03 = 22000","NPV, I = 10, CPT = −5,537.19"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Left out the recovery at t = 3","value":-22066.12,"check":"-20000-(25000-20000)/1.1+(25000-22000)/1.1**2","explain":"NWC comes back at the end of the project."},{"id":"nwc-sign-reversed","label":"Treated increases as inflows","value":5537.19,"check":"20000+(25000-20000)/1.1-(25000-22000)/1.1**2-22000/1.1**3","explain":"A positive change in NWC is an outflow."},{"id":"taxed-nwc","label":"Multiplied by (1 − T)","value":-3876.03,"check":"(-20000-(25000-20000)/1.1+(25000-22000)/1.1**2+22000/1.1**3)*(1-0.3)","explain":"NWC is counted in full; no tax adjustment."}],"explanation":"The cash flow in each year is minus the change in NWC, and the final balance is released at the end.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s5; SM 10-12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-098","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over a year a project's receivables fall by $9,200, inventory rises by $14,500 and payables rise by $4,100 (cash balances are unchanged). What is the cash-flow effect of the change in net working capital?","answer":-1200.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-((-9200+14500)-4100)","steps":[{"label":"Change in current assets = −9,200 + 14,500","value":5300.0,"check":"-9200+14500","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Change in current liabilities (payables)","value":4100.0,"check":"4100","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Cash flow = −ΔNWC","value":-1200.0,"check":"-((-9200+14500)-4100)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["ΔCA = −9,200 + 14,500 = +5,300","ΔCL = +4,100","ΔNWC = 5,300 − 4,100 = +1,200","Cash flow = −ΔNWC = −$1,200 (an outflow)"],"keystrokes":["−9200 + 14500 − 4100 = 1,200","+/− = −1,200"],"errors":[{"id":"payables-sign-error","label":"Added the payables","value":-9400.0,"check":"-(-9200+14500+4100)","explain":"Payables are subtracted in NWC."},{"id":"receivables-sign","label":"Treated the fall in receivables as an increase","value":-19600.0,"check":"-(9200+14500-4100)","explain":"Receivables fell, which lowers current assets."},{"id":"nwc-sign-reversed","label":"Reported the change as an inflow","value":1200.0,"check":"(-9200+14500)-4100","explain":"A positive change in NWC is an outflow."}],"explanation":"ΔNWC = Δcurrent assets − Δcurrent liabilities = +1,200, so cash falls by $1,200.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-099","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's sales are forecast at $400,000, $460,000, $520,000 and $500,000 in years 1 to 4. Net working capital held at the end of each year, including t = 0, equals 15% of the following year's sales, and all NWC is recovered at t = 4. The tax rate is 35% and the discount rate is 12%. What is the present value of the NWC cash flows?","answer":-25411.26,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"-0.15*400000-0.15*(460000-400000)/1.12-0.15*(520000-460000)/1.12**2-0.15*(500000-520000)/1.12**3+0.15*500000/1.12**4","steps":[{"label":"NWC invested at t = 0 (15% of year-1 sales)","value":60000.0,"check":"0.15*400000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Cash flow at t = 3 = −(75,000 − 78,000)","value":3000.0,"check":"-0.15*(500000-520000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Recovery at t = 4 = NWC_3","value":75000.0,"check":"0.15*500000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PV of the NWC flows at 12%","value":-25411.26,"check":"-0.15*400000-0.15*(460000-400000)/1.12-0.15*(520000-460000)/1.12**2-0.15*(500000-520000)/1.12**3+0.15*500000/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["NWC_0 = 15% × 400,000 = 60,000; NWC_1 = 15% × 460,000 = 69,000; NWC_2 = 15% × 520,000 = 78,000; NWC_3 = 15% × 500,000 = 75,000","Cash flows: t0 −60,000; t1 −9,000; t2 −9,000; t3 +3,000; t4 +75,000 (recovery)","PV = −60,000 − 9,000/1.12 − 9,000/1.12² + 3,000/1.12³ + 75,000/1.12⁴ = −$25,411.26"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = −60000; C01 = −9000; C02 = −9000; C03 = 3000; C04 = 75000","NPV, I = 12, CPT"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Left out the t = 4 recovery","value":-73075.12,"check":"-0.15*400000-0.15*(460000-400000)/1.12-0.15*(520000-460000)/1.12**2-0.15*(500000-520000)/1.12**3","explain":"NWC is recovered when the project ends."},{"id":"taxed-nwc","label":"Multiplied the NWC flows by (1 − T)","value":-16517.32,"check":"(-0.15*400000-0.15*(460000-400000)/1.12-0.15*(520000-460000)/1.12**2-0.15*(500000-520000)/1.12**3+0.15*500000/1.12**4)*(1-0.35)","explain":"NWC flows are counted in full."},{"id":"nwc0-omitted","label":"Ignored the initial NWC at t = 0","value":34588.74,"check":"-0.15*(460000-400000)/1.12-0.15*(520000-460000)/1.12**2-0.15*(500000-520000)/1.12**3+0.15*500000/1.12**4","explain":"The up-front NWC is the largest outflow."}],"explanation":"Build the NWC balance for each date, take minus the change as the cash flow, and discount including the final recovery.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-35; S6 s2-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-100","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over a year a project's cash balance rises by $2,000, receivables by $6,000, inventory by $9,000 and payables by $11,000. What is the cash-flow effect of the change in NWC?","options":["−$28,000","$6,000","−$6,000","−$4,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ΔNWC = 2,000 + 6,000 + 9,000 − 11,000 = +6,000, so the cash flow is −$6,000. −$28,000 adds the payables; $6,000 has the sign reversed; −$4,000 leaves out the cash balance.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q6; SM 10-12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-101","concept":"fin201-c10-nwc","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project invests $30,000 of NWC at t = 0 and a further $4,000 at the end of each of years 1, 2 and 3. All NWC is recovered at the end of year 4, and the tax rate is 35%. How much NWC is recovered?","options":["$30,000","$27,300","$46,000","$42,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Recovery = initial 30,000 + three additions of 4,000 = $42,000. $30,000 recovers only the initial amount; $27,300 taxes the recovery; $46,000 counts a fourth addition that was never made.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-34"},{"id":"fin201-q10-102","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Under declining-balance CCA, the CCA in any year after the first equals:","options":["d × the UCC at the end of the previous year","d × the original capital cost","d × (UCC − salvage value)","(capital cost − salvage value) ÷ N"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CCA_t = d × UCC_(t−1) for t ≥ 2. Applying d to the original cost is straight-line thinking, and (capital cost − salvage) ÷ N is the straight-line formula for accounting depreciation.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8; S5 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-103","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Undepreciated capital cost (UCC) is:","options":["the asset's current market value","the capital cost less the CCA claimed so far","the capital cost less the accounting depreciation","the expected salvage value of the asset"],"answer":1,"explanation":"UCC is the tax book value: cost less CCA claimed. It generally differs from market value and from accounting book value, and it is the starting point for the next year's CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-20; REV p1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-104","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm claims 25% of the opening balance of an asset class each year, and the balance never reaches zero. This describes:","options":["straight-line depreciation","the net acquisitions rule","declining-balance CCA","the capital gains inclusion rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Claiming a fixed percentage of what is left leaves a balance forever: the hallmark of declining-balance CCA. Straight-line depreciation writes the cost off in equal amounts to zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s6-8; S5 s25"},{"id":"fin201-q10-105","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does CCA differ from accounting depreciation in a capital-budgeting analysis?","options":["Accounting depreciation determines cash taxes, while CCA only affects the income reported to shareholders","CCA is a cash outflow each year, while accounting depreciation is a non-cash charge","They are identical because Canadian tax law requires straight-line depreciation for all assets","CCA follows the tax schedule and determines cash taxes; accounting depreciation does not change the cash flows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cash taxes are computed using CCA, the depreciation allowed for tax. Accounting depreciation is not the tax deduction, so it is irrelevant to the cash flows.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s6; S5 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-106","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about declining-balance CCA is NOT correct?","options":["CCA is the same dollar amount every year","UCC is reduced by each year's CCA","After year 1, CCA falls each year because it is a percentage of a shrinking UCC","The first year may use a multiplier such as 0.5 or 1.5"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Declining balance means the dollar CCA falls each year. A constant dollar amount describes straight-line depreciation.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-107","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we track UCC rather than just the original cost?","options":["UCC equals the market resale value of the asset, so it is needed to price a sale","CCA after year 1 is a percentage of the balance, and UCC at sale sets recapture or terminal loss","UCC is the asset's cost adjusted for inflation each year using the CPI","UCC is the cumulative amount of tax paid on the asset to date"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCA after year 1 is d × UCC, so the balance must be rolled forward. At disposal the UCC is also compared with the proceeds.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-108","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"CCA is a non-cash expense that reduces taxable income.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. No cash leaves the firm when CCA is claimed, but it is deducted in computing taxable income and so saves tax.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-109","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"With d = 20%, CCA in year 2 is 20% of the original capital cost.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. After year 1 the CCA is 20% of the UCC, which is smaller than the original cost, so year-2 CCA is less than 20% of cost.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-110","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"After year 1, declining-balance CCA falls each year because it is a fixed percentage of the shrinking _____.","options":["salvage value","sales","UCC","tax rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CCA_t = d × UCC_(t−1), and the UCC shrinks by each year's CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-111","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"With d = 30% and the half-year rule, year-1 CCA is 15% of cost and year-2 CCA is 25.5% of cost. What does this show?","options":["The class rate itself rises to 25.5% once the first year has passed","CCA switches to straight-line once the first year has passed","The rule increases the total CCA that can ever be claimed on the asset","The half-year rule defers part of year 1's claim, so year-2 CCA is larger"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Year 1: 0.5 × 0.30 = 15% of cost. Year 2: 0.30 × (1 − 0.15) = 25.5% of cost. Half of year 1's claim is simply postponed; the rate d is unchanged and the total claimable is still the full cost.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7"},{"id":"fin201-q10-112","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Under the half-year rule, for any CCA rate d between 0 and 1, in which year is the CCA claim largest?","options":["Year 2","Year 1","Year 3","It depends on whether the asset is sold"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CCA_1 = 0.5 d I0 and CCA_2 = d(1 − 0.5d) I0, which is larger because 1 − 0.5d > 0.5. After year 2 the claims fall by the factor (1 − d) each year. (Under AII, year 1 is the largest.)","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-113","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"For a continuing asset (t ≥ 2), UCC at the end of year t in terms of UCC at the end of year t − 1 is:","options":["UCC_t = UCC_(t−1) − d × I0","UCC_t = UCC_(t−1) × (1 − d)","UCC_t = UCC_(t−1)/(1 + d)","UCC_t = UCC_(t−1) × (1 − d)^t"],"answer":1,"explanation":"UCC_t = UCC_(t−1) − CCA_t = UCC_(t−1) − d × UCC_(t−1) = UCC_(t−1)(1 − d). Subtracting d × I0 is the straight-line mistake.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-114","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Beacon Printing buys a $250,000 press in a 30% declining-balance class and the half-year rule applies. What is the UCC at the end of year 3?","answer":104125.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"250000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2","steps":[{"label":"CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.30 × 250,000","value":37500.0,"check":"0.5*0.3*250000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"UCC_1","value":212500.0,"check":"250000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_2 = 0.30 × UCC_1","value":63750.0,"check":"0.3*250000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"UCC_2","value":148750.0,"check":"250000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_3 = 0.30 × UCC_2","value":44625.0,"check":"0.3*250000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"UCC_3","value":104125.0,"check":"250000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.30 × 250,000 = 37,500; UCC_1 = 212,500","CCA_2 = 0.30 × 212,500 = 63,750; UCC_2 = 148,750","CCA_3 = 0.30 × 148,750 = 44,625; UCC_3 = $104,125","Check: 250,000(1 − 0.15)(1 − 0.30)² = 104,125"],"keystrokes":["250000 × (1 − 0.15) × 0.7 × 0.7 = 104,125"],"errors":[{"id":"half-year-every-year","label":"Applied the half-year rate every year","value":153531.25,"check":"250000*(1-0.5*0.3)**3","explain":"The multiplier applies in year 1 only; later years use the full d."},{"id":"straight-line-cca","label":"Took d × original cost in years 2 and 3","value":62500.0,"check":"250000-0.5*0.3*250000-2*0.3*250000","explain":"CCA after year 1 is d × UCC, not d × cost."},{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Ignored the half-year rule","value":85750.0,"check":"250000*(1-0.3)**3","explain":"Year 1 CCA is 0.5 × d × I0 under the half-year rule."}],"explanation":"Roll the schedule forward year by year: CCA is a percentage of the opening UCC after the first-year adjustment.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7; SM 10-11, 10-14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-115","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Pinecrest Logistics buys a $125,000 vehicle in a 20% declining-balance class and the Accelerated Investment Incentive (1.5 × in year 1) applies. What is the CCA in year 4?","answer":11200.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"0.2*125000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**2","steps":[{"label":"CCA_1 = 1.5 × 0.20 × 125,000","value":37500.0,"check":"1.5*0.2*125000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"UCC_1","value":87500.0,"check":"125000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"UCC_3","value":56000.0,"check":"125000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_4 = 0.20 × UCC_3","value":11200.0,"check":"0.2*125000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 1.5 × 0.20 × 125,000 = 37,500; UCC_1 = 87,500","CCA_2 = 17,500; UCC_2 = 70,000","CCA_3 = 14,000; UCC_3 = 56,000","CCA_4 = 0.20 × 56,000 = $11,200"],"keystrokes":["125000 × 0.7 × 0.8 × 0.8 × 0.2 = 11,200"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-aii","label":"Used no first-year multiplier","value":12800.0,"check":"0.2*125000*(1-1*0.2)*(1-0.2)**2","explain":"The question states the 1.5 incentive, so year-1 CCA is 1.5 × d × I0."},{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year multiplier","value":14400.0,"check":"0.2*125000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**2","explain":"The question states AII, so m = 1.5, not 0.5."},{"id":"straight-line-cca","label":"Used d × original cost","value":25000.0,"check":"0.2*125000","explain":"CCA in year 4 is d × the UCC at the end of year 3."}],"explanation":"UCC after year 3 is 125,000 × (1 − 0.30) × 0.8² = 56,000; CCA in year 4 is 20% of that.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s20; L05B s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-116","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Verity Foods buys $92,000 of equipment in a 25% declining-balance class, with the half-year rule. How much total CCA is claimed over the first three years?","answer":46718.75,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"92000-92000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2","steps":[{"label":"CCA_1","value":11500.0,"check":"0.5*0.25*92000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_2","value":20125.0,"check":"0.25*92000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_3","value":15093.75,"check":"0.25*92000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Cumulative CCA = I0 − UCC_3","value":46718.75,"check":"92000-92000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.25 × 92,000 = 11,500; UCC_1 = 80,500","CCA_2 = 0.25 × 80,500 = 20,125; UCC_2 = 60,375","CCA_3 = 0.25 × 60,375 = 15,093.75; UCC_3 = 45,281.25","Total = 11,500 + 20,125 + 15,093.75 = $46,718.75 (= 92,000 − 45,281.25)"],"keystrokes":["92000 − 92000 × 0.875 × 0.75 × 0.75 = 46,718.75"],"errors":[{"id":"straight-line-cca","label":"Used d × cost in years 2 and 3","value":57500.0,"check":"0.5*0.25*92000+2*0.25*92000","explain":"After year 1, CCA is d × UCC, which is smaller than d × cost."},{"id":"half-year-every-year","label":"Applied the half-year rate every year","value":30367.19,"check":"92000-92000*(1-0.5*0.25)**3","explain":"The half-year adjustment is made in year 1 only."},{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Ignored the half-year rule","value":53187.5,"check":"92000-92000*(1-0.25)**3","explain":"Year-1 CCA is half of d × I0."}],"explanation":"Cumulative CCA after n years is the original cost less the UCC at the end of year n.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8; SM 10-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-117","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A $200,000 asset is in a 40% declining-balance class, and the half-year rule applies. What is the CCA in year 2?","options":["$80,000","$32,000","$64,000","$48,000"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.40 × 200,000 = 40,000; UCC_1 = 160,000; CCA_2 = 0.40 × 160,000 = $64,000. $80,000 applies d to the original cost; $32,000 halves the rate again; $48,000 ignores the half-year rule in year 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7; SM 10-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-118","concept":"fin201-c10-cca-schedule","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $150,000 asset is in a 20% class and the Accelerated Investment Incentive applies (1.5 × in year 1). What is the UCC at the end of year 2?","options":["$96,000","$75,000","$108,000","$84,000"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CCA_1 = 1.5 × 0.20 × 150,000 = 45,000; UCC_1 = 105,000; CCA_2 = 21,000; UCC_2 = $84,000. $96,000 leaves out the 1.5 multiplier; $75,000 takes d × cost in year 2; $108,000 uses the half-year multiplier.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s20; L05B s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-119","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Under the half-year rule, the first-year CCA on a new asset costing I0 in a class with rate d is:","options":["0.5 × d × I0","d × I0","1.5 × d × I0","0.5 × I0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The half-year rule halves the first-year allowance: m = 0.5. A full-rate claim is d × I0, and 1.5 × d × I0 is the Accelerated Investment Incentive.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7"},{"id":"fin201-q10-120","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Accelerated Investment Incentive (AII) lets a firm claim in the first year:","options":["d × I0","1.5 × d × I0","0.5 × d × I0","1.5 × I0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AII raises the first-year multiplier to m = 1.5, after which CCA is the usual d × UCC. The multiplier applies to d × I0, not to I0 itself.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s8; S5 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-121","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A problem states that the first-year multiplier for a new asset is 1.5. Which rule is in use?","options":["The half-year rule","The net acquisitions rule","Accelerated Investment Incentive (AII)","A 100% first-year allowance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A multiplier of 1.5 is the Accelerated Investment Incentive; 0.5 is the half-year rule and 1.0 is a full-rate first-year claim.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-122","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gradient Labs bought a machine three years ago and has been claiming CCA on it since. For the PV of its remaining tax shield, the first-year factor is:","options":["(1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), the half-year factor","(1.5k + 1)/(1 + k), the AII factor","0.5, because the asset is half depreciated","1: the first-year adjustment was already used"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For something already owned and depreciating, the first-year adjustment is history. From now on it is the regular 100%: PV = (UCC × d × T)/(k + d).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16"},{"id":"fin201-q10-123","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement compares the two first-year regimes correctly?","options":["AII claims more CCA in year 1, so its PV of the tax shield is larger","The half-year rule claims more CCA in year 1, so its PV is larger","The two give the same PV because total CCA is the same","AII applies the multiplier to every year of the asset's life"],"answer":0,"explanation":"m = 1.5 front-loads the deductions compared with m = 0.5. Earlier tax savings are worth more in present value terms.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16-17"},{"id":"fin201-q10-124","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the first-year multiplier is NOT correct?","options":["It is 0.5 under the half-year rule","It is applied to the opening UCC in every year of the asset's life","It is 1.5 under the Accelerated Investment Incentive","It changes the first-year CCA but not the rate d"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The multiplier applies in year 1 only. From year 2, CCA is d × opening UCC.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-125","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the multiplier m applied only in the first year?","options":["Tax law drops the class rate d once the first year is over","The multiplier is replaced by the tax rate in later years","It adjusts only the first claim; from year 2, CCA is d times the opening UCC","The asset is assumed to be sold at the end of its first year"],"answer":2,"explanation":"UCC rolls forward after the adjusted first-year claim, so later claims use the plain rate d.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-126","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"The professor expects you to apply the first-year multiplier the question states (0.5, 1.5 or 1.0) rather than assume a single fixed rule.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Tax rules change, and the multiplier is a parameter m in CCA_1 = m × d × I0. When nothing is stated, the half-year rule applies.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-127","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Under the half-year rule, the first-year CCA is _____ the normal declining-balance amount d × I0.","options":["one and a half times","double","equal to","half of"],"answer":3,"explanation":"m = 0.5 halves the first-year claim; m = 1.5 multiplies it by one and a half.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7"},{"id":"fin201-q10-128","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"At k = 10%, the first-year PVCCA factors are 0.9545 (half-year), 1.0000 (100%) and 1.0455 (AII). What does this ordering show?","options":["A faster first-year claim brings savings forward, so PV rises from half-year to 100% to AII","The half-year rule gives the largest total CCA over the asset's life","The factors differ because the tax rate is different under each regime","The factors rank the three regimes by the risk of the asset's cash flows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"(1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k) < 1 < (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k). Earlier deductions are worth more, so the PV is highest under AII.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16-17"},{"id":"fin201-q10-129","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Pinecrest Logistics buys a $300,000 truck in a 30% class, and the question states that the Accelerated Investment Incentive applies. What is the first-year CCA?","answer":135000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"1.5*0.3*300000","steps":[{"label":"CCA_1 = 1.5 × d × I0","value":135000.0,"check":"1.5*0.3*300000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = m × d × I0 with m = 1.5","CCA_1 = 1.5 × 0.30 × 300,000 = $135,000"],"keystrokes":["1.5 × 0.3 × 300000 = 135,000"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year multiplier","value":45000.0,"check":"0.5*0.3*300000","explain":"The question states AII, so m = 1.5."},{"id":"missing-aii","label":"Used no multiplier","value":90000.0,"check":"0.3*300000","explain":"AII multiplies the normal first-year claim by 1.5."},{"id":"forgot-d","label":"Multiplied the cost by 1.5 only","value":450000.0,"check":"1.5*300000","explain":"The claim is on d × I0, not on I0."}],"explanation":"First-year CCA = m × d × I0 with m = 1.5 under AII.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-130","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":["fin201-c10-dts"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm buys $120,000 of equipment in a 30% class and its tax rate is 25%. By how much does the first-year tax shield under the Accelerated Investment Incentive exceed the first-year tax shield under the half-year rule?","answer":9000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.5*0.3*120000*0.25-0.5*0.3*120000*0.25","steps":[{"label":"AII first-year CCA","value":54000.0,"check":"1.5*0.3*120000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Half-year first-year CCA","value":18000.0,"check":"0.5*0.3*120000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Difference in DTS = ΔCCA × T","value":9000.0,"check":"(1.5*0.3*120000-0.5*0.3*120000)*0.25","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["AII: CCA_1 = 1.5 × 0.30 × 120,000 = 54,000; DTS = 13,500","Half-year: CCA_1 = 18,000; DTS = 4,500","Difference = 13,500 − 4,500 = $9,000"],"keystrokes":["54000 − 18000 = 36,000","× 0.25 = 9,000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Reported the difference in CCA","value":36000.0,"check":"1.5*0.3*120000-0.5*0.3*120000","explain":"The shield is the CCA multiplied by T."},{"id":"wrong-multiplier","label":"Multiplied by (1 − T)","value":27000.0,"check":"(1.5*0.3*120000-0.5*0.3*120000)*(1-0.25)","explain":"The CCA tax shield is CCA × T."}],"explanation":"The difference in first-year CCA is (1.5 − 0.5) × d × I0 = 36,000; the tax saved on it is 25%.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8, s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-131","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"An asset costs $200,000 (no sale planned), d = 30%, T = 35% and k = 10%. How much larger is the PV of its CCA tax shield under the Accelerated Investment Incentive than under the half-year rule?","answer":4772.73,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","steps":[{"label":"Perpetuity base I0 d T/(k + d)","value":52500.0,"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PVCCA under the half-year rule","value":50113.64,"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"PVCCA under AII","value":54886.36,"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Difference","value":4772.73,"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Base = 200,000 × 0.30 × 0.35/(0.10 + 0.30) = 52,500","Half-year factor = (1 + 0.05)/1.10 = 0.954545 → PVCCA = 50,113.64","AII factor = (0.15 + 1)/1.10 = 1.045455 → PVCCA = 54,886.36","Difference = 54,886.36 − 50,113.64 = $4,772.73"],"keystrokes":["200000 × 0.3 × 0.35 ÷ 0.4 = 52,500","× (1.15 ÷ 1.1 − 1.05 ÷ 1.1) = 4,772.73"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Compared AII with the 100% case","value":2386.36,"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)","explain":"The comparison asked for is AII versus the half-year rule."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Left the tax rate out of the base","value":13636.36,"check":"(200000*0.3)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))-(200000*0.3)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","explain":"Each PVCCA includes the factor T."},{"id":"sign-reversed","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":-4772.73,"check":"(200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)) - (200000*0.3*0.35)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))","explain":"AII has the larger PV, so the difference is positive."}],"explanation":"Only the first-year factor differs: (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k) versus (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), applied to the same perpetuity base.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16-17; S5 s21-23"},{"id":"fin201-q10-132","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"An $84,000 asset is in a 30% class and the half-year rule applies. What is the first-year CCA?","options":["$25,200","$12,600","$37,800","$6,300"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.30 × 84,000 = $12,600. $25,200 claims the full rate; $37,800 uses the AII multiplier of 1.5; $6,300 halves the half-year rule a second time.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7"},{"id":"fin201-q10-133","concept":"fin201-c10-half-year-aii","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"At k = 10%, what first-year factor goes into the PV of the CCA tax shield for a new asset under the Accelerated Investment Incentive?","options":["0.9545","1.3636","1.0455","1.1500"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The AII factor is (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k) = 1.15/1.10 = 1.0455. 0.9545 is the half-year factor; 1.3636 puts 1.5 over (1 + k) without the k term; 1.1500 forgets to divide by (1 + k).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-134","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The CCA tax shield (DTS) in a year is:","options":["CCA × (1 − T_C)","the CCA itself, because it is a cash inflow","UCC × T_C","CCA × T_C, the tax saved because CCA is deductible"],"answer":3,"explanation":"DTS_t = CCA_t × T_C. CCA is non-cash; its cash effect is the tax it saves.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14-15; S5 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-135","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the CCA tax shield called a 'tax shield'?","options":["It shields part of the firm's income from tax, cutting the bill by T × CCA, if other income exists","It protects the asset's value from recapture by CRA at the time of sale","It shields the firm's cash flows from the effects of inflation","It is a refund paid by the government to the firm regardless of its income"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The deduction shields income from tax. The saving assumes the firm has enough other income against which to claim it.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3; L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-136","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new press lets Delta Plastics deduct $30,000 of CCA this year. At a 30% tax rate its tax bill falls by $9,000. The $9,000 is the:","options":["recapture of CCA","CCA tax shield (DTS)","after-tax salvage value","operating cash flow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DTS = CCA × T = 30,000 × 0.30 = $9,000, the cash tax saved by the non-cash deduction.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-137","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the DTS differ from the CCA itself?","options":["The DTS is the CCA after tax, that is CCA × (1 − T), a cash inflow","CCA is the tax saved by the deduction; the DTS is the reduction in UCC","CCA is a deduction from income; the DTS is the cash tax it saves (CCA × T)","They are the same amount, reported in different years for tax and accounting"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A deduction of CCA reduces taxable income by CCA and reduces tax by T × CCA. Only the saving is a cash flow.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14-15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-138","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the CCA tax shield is NOT correct?","options":["It rises when the tax rate rises","From year 2 on, it falls each year as UCC declines","It is realized only if the firm has enough taxable income","It is a cash outflow because depreciation is a cost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CCA is non-cash, and its tax shield is a reduction in taxes paid, a cash saving, not an outflow.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10 CTQ4; L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-139","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's CCA deduction can only be used against taxable income. If the firm has no other taxable income and cannot carry losses forward, what happens to the CCA tax shield?","options":["It cannot be used, so the saving is lost","It is still received in full as a refund","It is received at (1 − T) instead of T","It is added back to the asset's UCC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The textbook shield assumes the firm has enough other income (the professor says 'assuming you have enough other income'). Without it there is nothing to shield.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-140","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A higher corporate tax rate increases the value of the CCA tax shield.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. DTS = CCA × T_C, so each dollar of CCA saves more tax at a higher rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-141","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"For a $7,500 asset the DTS is $1,282.50 in year 1 and $470.25 in year 2 (T = 38%). Why does it shrink?","options":["The tax rate falls each year as the firm's income falls","CCA is declining balance, so each year's CCA, and its tax saving, is smaller","The firm's taxable income falls each year as the asset ages","The discount rate rises each year, so later savings are worth less"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCA_1 = 3,375 and CCA_2 = 1,237.50 under the lecture's AII example; DTS = CCA × 0.38 declines in proportion.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s24"},{"id":"fin201-q10-142","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"DTS_t = CCA_t × _____.","options":["(1 − T_C)","d","T_C","k"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The tax shield multiplies CCA by the corporate tax rate T_C.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-143","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"If the tax rate rises from 30% to 40% with CCA unchanged, the DTS:","options":["rises by 10% (× 1.10)","is unchanged, since CCA is unchanged","falls, because more tax is paid","rises by one-third (× 40/30)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"DTS = CCA × T, so it is proportional to T: 0.40/0.30 = 1.333, a one-third increase. A 10-point rise in T is not a 10% rise in the shield.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-144","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A firm buys $180,000 of equipment in a 20% class (half-year rule) and its tax rate is 40%. What is the CCA tax shield in year 2?","answer":12960.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"0.2*180000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**0*0.4","steps":[{"label":"CCA_1","value":18000.0,"check":"0.5*0.2*180000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_2 = 0.20 × UCC_1","value":32400.0,"check":"0.2*180000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"DTS_2 = CCA_2 × T","value":12960.0,"check":"0.2*180000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**0*0.4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.20 × 180,000 = 18,000; UCC_1 = 162,000","CCA_2 = 0.20 × 162,000 = 32,400","DTS_2 = 32,400 × 0.40 = $12,960"],"keystrokes":["0.2 × 162000 = 32,400","× 0.4 = 12,960"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Reported the CCA, not the saving","value":32400.0,"check":"0.2*180000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**0","explain":"The cash effect is T × CCA."},{"id":"wrong-year-cca","label":"Used year-1 CCA","value":7200.0,"check":"0.5*0.2*180000*0.4","explain":"Year 2 uses d × UCC_1."},{"id":"wrong-multiplier","label":"Multiplied by (1 − T)","value":19440.0,"check":"0.2*180000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**0*(1-0.4)","explain":"CCA is multiplied by T, not (1 − T)."}],"explanation":"Find year-2 CCA from the schedule, then multiply by the tax rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7, s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-145","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm buys $60,000 of equipment in a 30% class under the Accelerated Investment Incentive. The tax rate is 35%. What is the total CCA tax shield over years 1 to 3?","answer":15340.5,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"0.35*(1.5*0.3*60000+0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0+0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1)","steps":[{"label":"CCA_1","value":27000.0,"check":"1.5*0.3*60000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"CCA_2","value":9900.0,"check":"0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"CCA_3","value":6930.0,"check":"0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"DTS_1 + DTS_2 + DTS_3","value":15340.5,"check":"0.35*(1.5*0.3*60000+0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0+0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 1.5 × 0.30 × 60,000 = 27,000; UCC_1 = 33,000","CCA_2 = 0.30 × 33,000 = 9,900; UCC_2 = 23,100","CCA_3 = 0.30 × 23,100 = 6,930","DTS = 0.35 × (27,000 + 9,900 + 6,930) = 0.35 × 43,830 = $15,340.50"],"keystrokes":["27000 + 9900 + 6930 = 43,830","× 0.35 = 15,340.50"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year multiplier","value":12253.5,"check":"0.35*(0.5*0.3*60000+0.3*60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0+0.3*60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1)","explain":"The question states AII, so m = 1.5."},{"id":"straight-line-cca","label":"Used d × cost after year 1","value":22050.0,"check":"0.35*(1.5*0.3*60000+2*0.3*60000)","explain":"CCA after year 1 is d × UCC."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Added up the CCA, not the savings","value":43830.0,"check":"1.5*0.3*60000+0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**0+0.3*60000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**1","explain":"The shield is T × CCA."}],"explanation":"Compute the three CCA amounts, add them and multiply by T (or multiply each year and add).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7-8, s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-146","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $100,000 asset is in a 25% class with the half-year rule. The tax rate is 30% and the discount rate is 10%. What is the present value of the CCA tax shields in years 1 and 2 only?","answer":8832.64,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.3*0.5*0.25*100000/1.1+0.3*0.25*100000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0/1.1**2","steps":[{"label":"DTS_1","value":3750.0,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.25*100000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"DTS_2","value":6562.5,"check":"0.3*0.25*100000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV = DTS_1/1.10 + DTS_2/1.10²","value":8832.64,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.25*100000/1.1+0.3*0.25*100000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0/1.1**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["CCA_1 = 0.5 × 0.25 × 100,000 = 12,500; DTS_1 = 3,750","UCC_1 = 87,500; CCA_2 = 21,875; DTS_2 = 6,562.50","PV = 3,750/1.10 + 6,562.50/1.21 = 3,409.09 + 5,423.55 = $8,832.64"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0; C01 = 3750; C02 = 6562.5; NPV, I = 10, CPT = 8,832.64"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Discounted the CCA, not the shield","value":29442.15,"check":"0.5*0.25*100000/1.1+0.25*100000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0/1.1**2","explain":"Discount the tax savings (CCA × T), not the CCA."},{"id":"not-discounted","label":"Added the shields without discounting","value":10312.5,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.25*100000+0.3*0.25*100000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0","explain":"Savings in years 1 and 2 must be discounted."},{"id":"discounted-wrong-year","label":"Discounted both shields one year","value":9375.0,"check":"(0.3*0.5*0.25*100000+0.3*0.25*100000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**0)/1.1","explain":"The year-2 saving is discounted two years."}],"explanation":"DTS_t = CCA_t × T, and each is discounted at (1 + k)^t.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15-17; S5 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-147","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A $96,000 asset is in a 20% class and the Accelerated Investment Incentive applies. The tax rate is 28%. What is the first-year CCA tax shield?","options":["$8,064","$2,688","$5,376","$28,800"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DTS_1 = 1.5 × 0.20 × 96,000 × 0.28 = $8,064. $2,688 uses the half-year rule; $5,376 uses no multiplier; $28,800 is the CCA itself, not the tax saved.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s20; L05B s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-148","concept":"fin201-c10-dts","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"An asset's UCC at the start of year 3 is $48,000. It is in a 25% class and the tax rate is 40%. What is the year-3 CCA tax shield?","options":["$12,000","$4,800","$7,200","$19,200"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CCA_3 = 0.25 × 48,000 = 12,000; DTS_3 = 12,000 × 0.40 = $4,800. $12,000 is the CCA itself; $7,200 multiplies by (1 − T); $19,200 applies T to the UCC instead of to the CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s7, s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-149","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The PV of the CCA tax shield (PVCCA) is:","options":["the present value of the CCA deductions claimed, before any tax effect","the tax payable on any recapture when the asset is sold","the present value of all future CCA × T_C, valued as a perpetuity growing at −d","the salvage value of the asset discounted back N years to today"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The shields CCA_t × T_C shrink at the rate d, so the stream is a growing perpetuity with g = −d. PVCCA is the present value of the tax saved, not of the CCA itself.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15-17"},{"id":"fin201-q10-150","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which expression is the base PVCCA (no first-year adjustment and no sale)?","options":["I0 × d × T_C/(k − d)","I0 × T_C/(k + d)","I0 × d × (1 − T_C)/(k + d)","I0 × d × T_C/(k + d)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Gordon growth with CCA_1 × T_C = I0 d T_C as the first flow and g = −d gives I0 d T_C/(k + d). Using k − d treats the shield as growing; omitting d drops the first-year CCA; (1 − T_C) confuses a shield with an after-tax expense.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15; FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-151","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which first-year factor goes into PVCCA for a new asset under the Accelerated Investment Incentive?","options":["(1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)","(1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k)","1","1.5/(1 + k)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"AII front-loads the claim: 1.5 of the perpetuity starting now less 0.5 of it starting a year later gives 1.5 − 0.5/(1 + k) = (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16-17"},{"id":"fin201-q10-152","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"In PVCCA, the term [S_N × d × T_C/(k + d)] × 1/(1 + k)^N represents:","options":["the tax on the capital gain when the asset is sold","the shield lost because CCA stops after the sale in year N","the after-tax proceeds of the sale in year N","the CCA tax shield actually received in year N"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After the sale, CCA is no longer claimed on the asset's S_N. The PV at year N of that lost stream is S_N d T_C/(k + d); discounting it N years gives its value today.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-153","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does PVCCA differ for an asset sold in year N versus an asset that is kept and never sold (its CCA continues indefinitely)?","options":["Sold: add the lost-tail term; not sold: subtract the lost-tail term","Sold: replace k with N in the denominator; not sold: leave k unchanged","Sold: subtract the lost tail S_N d T_C/(k + d)/(1 + k)^N; not sold: no subtraction","There is no difference; the lost-tail term is always included"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the asset is sold, CCA on it stops, so that stream is removed. If nothing is sold, CCA continues forever and the base formula applies.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q10-154","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an input to the PVCCA formula?","options":["The sale price S_N in year N","The CCA rate d of the class","The corporate tax rate T_C","The asset's UCC at the date of sale"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The salvage term uses the sale price S_N, not the UCC. UCC at sale matters for recapture and terminal loss, not for PVCCA.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22; S5 s21-23"},{"id":"fin201-q10-155","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is the denominator in PVCCA (k + d) rather than (k − d)?","options":["The shield declines at rate d, so it is a growing perpetuity with g = −d and k − (−d) = k + d","Because tax is paid at the end of each year, which adds d to the rate","Because the half-year rule shifts the discount rate up by d","Because d is added to k to reflect the added risk of the asset"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gordon growth gives PV = first flow/(k − g). The CCA shield shrinks, so g = −d, and the denominator becomes k + d.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15, s17"},{"id":"fin201-q10-156","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why is the salvage term in PVCCA multiplied by 1/(1 + k)^N?","options":["The salvage proceeds arrive at year N and are taxed then","The lost shields begin after year N; their value at year N is discounted to today","The asset's UCC is reduced by a factor of (1 + k) each year after the sale","It adjusts the first-year factor for the half-year rule and AII regime"],"answer":1,"explanation":"S_N d T_C/(k + d) is a value at year N (a perpetuity valued one period before its first payment). Dividing by (1 + k)^N converts it to today's dollars.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-157","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"With k = 10%, the CCA rate doubles from 10% to 20% (all else equal). What happens to the base PVCCA, I0 d T_C/(k + d)?","options":["It doubles, since d doubles","It is unchanged, because d cancels out of the ratio","It rises by one-third (0.20/0.30 versus 0.10/0.20)","It rises by 50%, because the denominator also grows"],"answer":2,"explanation":"d/(k + d) goes from 0.10/0.20 = 0.5 to 0.20/0.30 = 0.667, a rise of one-third. A faster write-off brings tax savings forward, but the total undiscounted shield is the same, so the PV does not double.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-158","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the salvage term of PVCCA, S_N is the asset's UCC at the time of sale.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. S_N is the sale price (salvage value). The lost stream is the CCA the firm would have claimed on that amount of the pool; UCC does not appear in the formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-159","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"The salvage term in PVCCA is discounted by (1 + k) raised to the power _____.","options":["N − 1","N + 1","d","N"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The value at year N of the lost shield is brought back to today with (1 + k)^N.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-160","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Granger Foods owns a line it has been depreciating for years (UCC today $50,000, d = 20%, T = 30%, k = 10%) and will keep it and never sell it. Which expression gives the PV of its remaining tax shield?","options":["50,000 × 0.20 × 0.30/(0.10 + 0.20), with no first-year factor and no salvage term","50,000 × 0.20 × 0.30/(0.10 + 0.20) × (1.5 × 0.10 + 1)/(1 + 0.10)","50,000 × 0.20 × 0.30/(0.10 + 0.20) × (1 + 0.5 × 0.10)/(1 + 0.10)","50,000 × 0.20 × 0.30/(0.10 − 0.20)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An asset already depreciating has used its first-year adjustment (factor 1), and with no sale there is no lost-tail term. Applying the AII or half-year factor wrongly treats the line as new, and k − d treats a shrinking shield as a growing one.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16"},{"id":"fin201-q10-161","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Alder Machining buys a $100,000 mill in a 30% class under the half-year rule and expects to sell it in 5 years for $20,000; other assets remain in the class, so the pool stays open. The tax rate is 25% and k = 10%. What is the PV of the CCA tax shield?","answer":15569.27,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)) - (20000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5","steps":[{"label":"Perpetuity base I0 d T/(k + d)","value":18750.0,"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Half-year factor (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k)","value":0.9545,"check":"(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Salvage term S_N d T/(k + d)/(1 + k)^N","value":2328.45,"check":"(20000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PVCCA","value":15569.27,"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)) - (20000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Base = 100,000 × 0.30 × 0.25/(0.10 + 0.30) = 18,750","Half-year factor = (1 + 0.05)/1.10 = 0.954545; first term = 17,897.73","Salvage term = 20,000 × 0.30 × 0.25/0.40 × 1/1.10⁵ = 3,750/1.61051 = 2,328.45","PVCCA = 17,897.73 − 2,328.45 = $15,569.27"],"keystrokes":["100000 × 0.3 × 0.25 ÷ (0.1 + 0.3) = 18,750","× 1.05 ÷ 1.1 = 17,897.73","− 3750 ÷ 1.1^5 = 15,569.27"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the AII factor","value":17273.82,"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (20000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5","explain":"The question states the half-year rule, so the factor is (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k)."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the salvage term","value":14147.73,"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)) - (20000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)","explain":"The lost-tail term is a value at year 5 and must be discounted 5 years."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Ignored the sale","value":17897.73,"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","explain":"The asset is sold in year 5, so CCA on it stops and its tail must be subtracted."},{"id":"ucc-for-salvage","label":"Used UCC in place of the sale price","value":15521.71,"check":"(100000*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)) - (100000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**4*0.3*0.25)/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5","explain":"The salvage term uses the sale price S_N, not the UCC."}],"explanation":"Perpetuity base, adjusted for the half-year rule, less the PV of the CCA stream lost after the sale.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16-17, s22; S5 s22-23"},{"id":"fin201-q10-162","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm buys $250,000 of equipment in a 20% class and the Accelerated Investment Incentive applies. It will sell the equipment after 6 years for $60,000; other assets remain in the class. The tax rate is 35% and k = 12%. What is the PV of the CCA tax shield?","answer":50967.65,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(250000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)*((1.5*0.12+1)/(1+0.12)) - (60000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**6","steps":[{"label":"First term with AII factor","value":57617.19,"check":"(250000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)*((1.5*0.12+1)/(1+0.12))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Salvage term","value":6649.53,"check":"(60000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PVCCA","value":50967.65,"check":"(250000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)*((1.5*0.12+1)/(1+0.12)) - (60000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Base = 250,000 × 0.20 × 0.35/(0.12 + 0.20) = 54,687.50","AII factor = (1.5 × 0.12 + 1)/1.12 = 1.053571; first term = 57,617.19","Salvage term = 60,000 × 0.20 × 0.35/0.32 / 1.12⁶ = 13,125/1.973823 = 6,649.53","PVCCA = 57,617.19 − 6,649.53 = $50,967.65"],"keystrokes":["250000 × 0.2 × 0.35 ÷ 0.32 × 1.18 ÷ 1.12","− 60000 × 0.2 × 0.35 ÷ 0.32 ÷ 1.12^6"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year factor","value":45108.28,"check":"(250000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)) - (60000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**6","explain":"The question states AII, so the factor is (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the salvage term","value":44492.19,"check":"(250000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)*((1.5*0.12+1)/(1+0.12)) - (60000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)","explain":"Discount the lost tail N = 6 years."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Ignored the sale","value":57617.19,"check":"(250000*0.2*0.35)/(0.12+0.2)*((1.5*0.12+1)/(1+0.12))","explain":"The sale ends CCA on that amount, so subtract the lost tail."}],"explanation":"Same structure as the half-year case; only the first-year factor changes.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16-17, s22; S5 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-163","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Granger Foods owns a packaging line it has depreciated for several years. The UCC today is $240,000 (CCA class rate 10%). The tax rate is 30%, k = 15%, and the line will never be sold (it stays in its class). What is the PV of the remaining CCA tax shield?","answer":28800.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(240000*0.1*0.3)/(0.15+0.1)","steps":[{"label":"Perpetuity PV = UCC × d × T/(k + d)","value":28800.0,"check":"(240000*0.1*0.3)/(0.15+0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Already depreciating: first-year factor = 1. Not sold: no salvage term.","PVCCA = 240,000 × 0.10 × 0.30/(0.15 + 0.10) = 7,200/0.25 = $28,800"],"keystrokes":["240000 × 0.1 × 0.3 ÷ (0.15 + 0.1) = 28,800"],"errors":[{"id":"k-minus-d","label":"Used k − d in the denominator","value":144000.0,"check":"(240000*0.1*0.3)/(0.15-0.1)","explain":"The shield declines at d, so the denominator is k + d."},{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Applied the half-year factor to an old asset","value":26921.74,"check":"(240000*0.1*0.3)/(0.15+0.1)*((1+0.5*0.15)/(1+0.15))","explain":"A continuing asset has already used its first-year adjustment; the factor is 1."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Left the tax rate out","value":96000.0,"check":"(240000*0.1)/(0.15+0.1)","explain":"The shield is CCA × T_C."}],"explanation":"100% regime with no sale: the plain growing-perpetuity value of the shields.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16; S5 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-164","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Marsh Bakery buys equipment for $150,000 and pays $10,000 for delivery and installation (capitalized). The class rate is 20%, the tax rate 40%, k = 10%, and the Accelerated Investment Incentive applies. The equipment will be sold in 5 years for $24,000; other assets remain in the class. What is the PV of the CCA tax shield?","answer":40632.16,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (24000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","steps":[{"label":"Capital cost I0 = purchase + installation","value":160000.0,"check":"150000+10000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Perpetuity base I0 d T/(k + d)","value":42666.67,"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"First term with AII factor","value":44606.06,"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Salvage term","value":3973.9,"check":"(24000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PVCCA","value":40632.16,"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (24000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["I0 = 150,000 + 10,000 = 160,000 (installation is capitalized)","Base = 160,000 × 0.20 × 0.40/0.30 = 42,666.67; AII factor = 1.15/1.10 = 1.045455 → 44,606.06","Salvage term = 24,000 × 0.20 × 0.40/0.30 / 1.10⁵ = 6,400/1.61051 = 3,973.90","PVCCA = 44,606.06 − 3,973.90 = $40,632.16"],"keystrokes":["160000 × 0.2 × 0.4 ÷ 0.3 × 1.15 ÷ 1.1 = 44,606.06","− 24000 × 0.2 × 0.4 ÷ 0.3 ÷ 1.1^5 = 40,632.16"],"errors":[{"id":"missed-installation","label":"Left installation out of the capital cost","value":37844.29,"check":"(150000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (24000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","explain":"Installation is capitalized into I0 and earns CCA."},{"id":"ucc-for-salvage","label":"Used UCC at year 5 as the salvage","value":37010.09,"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (160000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**4*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","explain":"Use the sale price of $24,000."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the salvage term","value":38206.06,"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (24000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)","explain":"Discount the lost tail 5 years."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Ignored the sale","value":44606.06,"check":"(160000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))","explain":"The sale stops CCA on that value."}],"explanation":"Capital cost includes installation; AII factor; subtract the discounted lost tail from the sale.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s22-23; L05B s16-17, s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-165","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $600,000 structure is in a 4% CCA class. The tax rate is 35%, k = 11%, the half-year rule applies and the structure will never be sold. What is the PV of its CCA tax shield?","options":["$58,775","$53,225","$56,000","$120,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Base = 600,000 × 0.04 × 0.35/0.15 = 56,000; half-year factor = 1.055/1.11 = 0.9505; PVCCA = $53,225. $58,775 uses the AII factor; $56,000 ignores the half-year adjustment; $120,000 uses k − d.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16; SM 10-31"},{"id":"fin201-q10-166","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $180,000 asset in a 30% class qualifies for the Accelerated Investment Incentive. It will be sold after 4 years for $20,000, and other assets stay in the class. The tax rate is 40% and k = 12%. What is the PV of its CCA tax shield?","options":["$48,469","$48,018","$50,552","$54,184"],"answer":2,"explanation":"First term = 180,000 × 0.30 × 0.40/0.42 × 1.18/1.12 = 54,183.67; lost tail = 20,000 × 0.30 × 0.40/0.42/1.12⁴ = 3,631.53; PVCCA = $50,552. $48,469 fails to discount the tail; $48,018 uses the UCC at year 4 instead of the sale price; $54,184 ignores the sale.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22; S5 s21-23"},{"id":"fin201-q10-167","concept":"fin201-c10-pvcca","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rowan Tools owns an asset with a UCC of $90,000 today (20% class), T = 30% and k = 10%. It will sell the asset in 3 years for $15,000; other assets stay in the class. What is the PV of its remaining CCA tax shield?","options":["$18,000","$16,564","$15,000","$15,746"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The asset is already depreciating, so the factor is 1: 90,000 × 0.20 × 0.30/0.30 = 18,000 less the lost tail 15,000 × 0.20 × 0.30/0.30 / 1.1³ = 2,253.94 gives $15,746. $18,000 ignores the sale; $16,564 applies the AII factor to an old asset; $15,000 does not discount the tail.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16, s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-168","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When assets are bought and sold in the same CCA class in the same year, net acquisitions equal:","options":["total additions minus the lesser of original cost and sale price of each disposal","total additions plus the proceeds of the disposals, since both are cash flows","total additions minus the UCC of the disposed assets at the date of sale","total additions minus the original cost of the disposals, always"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Seminar 5: Net acquisition = total acquisitions − min(original cost, selling price). The lesser-of rule prevents a sale above cost from reducing the pool by more than cost.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-169","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm buys a new machine and sells an old one in the same class in the same year. Which rule determines the amount to which the 0.5 or 1.5 first-year multiplier is applied?","options":["The stand-alone principle","The net acquisitions rule","The capital gains inclusion rule","The salvage-term rule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The multiplier applies to net acquisitions (a positive net amount). The sale of the old asset reduces the amount that qualifies.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-170","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a replacement analysis, how do the net acquisition cost and the net salvage differ?","options":["Net acquisition arises at the end of the project, while net salvage is a cost today","Net acquisition is an after-tax figure while net salvage is stated before tax","Net acquisition = new cost − old resale today (the I0 in PVCCA); net salvage = new − old salvage at the end (S_N)","They are the same number, but net salvage is measured in a later year"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One is the net outlay on the pool today (I0), the other is the net amount leaving the pool at the end (S_N).","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-171","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT used in the PVCCA of a replacement project?","options":["New machine cost minus old machine resale value today","Net salvage at the end of the project","The tax rate on the shield","The old machine's book value as the I0"],"answer":3,"explanation":"I0 in the PVCCA is the net acquisition cost, not the book value of the old machine. The old machine's UCC is irrelevant to the replacement PVCCA.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8-10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-172","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the PVCCA of a replacement use the net acquisition rather than the full cost of the new machine?","options":["Selling the old machine removes its cost from the pool, so the pool grows by the net amount","Because the old machine's CCA continues forever and must be netted out","Because the tax rate applies only to the difference in prices","Because tax law forbids any CCA claim on assets bought as replacements"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Additions less the disposal proceeds is the net amount added to the pool, and that is what earns CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26; S6 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-173","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm's disposals in a class exceed its additions in the year, so net acquisitions are negative. How is the first-year multiplier (0.5 or 1.5) applied?","options":["The multiplier is applied to the negative amount, reducing UCC further","No adjustment: the multiplier is applied only to a positive net acquisition","The multiplier is applied to the larger of additions and disposals","The multiplier is replaced by T_C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Seminar 5: if net acquisition is greater than zero apply the 1.5 times rule to the amount; if less than zero there is no adjustment.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-174","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a replacement problem, the net salvage value (new salvage minus old salvage) can be negative.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. If the old machine would have been worth more at the end than the new one, as in the simple replacement example (0 − 10,000), net salvage is −$10,000.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-175","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a replacement, net salvage is −$10,000 (the old machine would have sold for $10,000 in 5 years; the new machine has no salvage). What does this do to the PVCCA salvage term?","options":["The term is dropped because net salvage cannot be negative in a replacement","The term is doubled because there are two machines to account for","The term is subtracted with a negative S_N, so it adds to PVCCA; the −$10,000 is also an outflow","The term is discounted at the real rate instead of the nominal rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PVCCA = main term − (S_N d T/(k + d))/(1 + k)^N; with S_N = −10,000 the subtraction becomes an addition. Giving up the old machine's $10,000 resale is also a cash outflow at year 5.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-176","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a firm buys and sells in the same class in the same year, the first-year multiplier applies to the _____ acquisitions.","options":["gross","average","total","net"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net acquisitions = additions − min(original cost, sale price) of the disposals.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-177","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fairview Haulage buys a new truck for $340,000 and, in the same year, sells an old truck from the same CCA class for $95,000. The old truck's original cost was $210,000. What is the net acquisition amount?","answer":245000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"340000-95000","steps":[{"label":"min(original cost, selling price) = min(210,000, 95,000)","value":95000.0,"check":"95000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Net acquisition = additions − min(...)","value":245000.0,"check":"340000-95000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["min(210,000, 95,000) = 95,000","Net acquisition = 340,000 − 95,000 = $245,000"],"keystrokes":["340000 − 95000 = 245,000"],"errors":[{"id":"gross-cost-for-net","label":"Used the full cost of the new truck","value":340000.0,"check":"340000","explain":"The pool grows only by the net amount after the sale."},{"id":"cost-for-min","label":"Subtracted the old truck's original cost","value":130000.0,"check":"340000-210000","explain":"The offset is the lesser of original cost and selling price: here the price, $95,000."},{"id":"sign-proceeds","label":"Added the proceeds","value":435000.0,"check":"340000+95000","explain":"Proceeds reduce the amount added to the pool."}],"explanation":"Offset the additions by the lesser of the original cost and the sale price.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-178","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hartwell Metals will buy a new machine for $210,000 and sell its old machine (same class, original cost $120,000) today for $70,000. The new machine will be sold in 4 years for $25,000; kept, the old machine would have sold then for $9,000. Other assets remain in the class. The class rate is 30%, T = 40%, k = 12%, and the half-year rule applies to net acquisitions. What is the PV of the CCA tax shield?","answer":34951.92,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(140000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)) - (16000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**4","steps":[{"label":"Net acquisition cost = 210,000 − 70,000","value":140000.0,"check":"210000-70000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Net salvage = 25,000 − 9,000","value":16000.0,"check":"25000-9000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"First term with half-year factor","value":37857.14,"check":"(140000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Salvage term","value":2905.23,"check":"(16000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PVCCA","value":34951.92,"check":"(140000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)) - (16000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Net acquisition = 210,000 − 70,000 = 140,000 (the sale price is below the original cost)","Net salvage = 25,000 − 9,000 = 16,000","Base = 140,000 × 0.30 × 0.40/0.42 = 40,000; half-year factor = 1.06/1.12 = 0.946429; first term = 37,857.14","Salvage term = 16,000 × 0.30 × 0.40/0.42 / 1.12⁴ = 4,571.43/1.573519 = 2,905.23","PVCCA = 37,857.14 − 2,905.23 = $34,951.92"],"keystrokes":["140000 × 0.3 × 0.4 ÷ 0.42 × 1.06 ÷ 1.12 = 37,857.14","− 16000 × 0.3 × 0.4 ÷ 0.42 ÷ 1.12^4 = 34,951.92"],"errors":[{"id":"gross-cost-for-net","label":"Used the full cost of the new machine","value":53880.49,"check":"(210000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)) - (16000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**4","explain":"I0 is the net acquisition cost, not the gross price."},{"id":"ignored-old-salvage","label":"Used the new machine's salvage only","value":33317.73,"check":"(140000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)) - (25000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**4","explain":"The old machine's lost salvage nets against the new machine's: S_N = 16,000."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the salvage term","value":33285.71,"check":"(140000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)*((1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)) - (16000*0.3*0.4)/(0.12+0.3)","explain":"The lost-tail term must be discounted 4 years."}],"explanation":"Replace I0 by the net acquisition cost and S_N by the net salvage in the PVCCA formula.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8-10, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-179","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca","fin201-c10-replacement-npv"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm replaces an old machine with a new one costing $180,000 in the same CCA class, which stays open. The old machine, whose original cost was $120,000, sells today for $75,000; kept, it would sell for $12,000 in 5 years. The new machine would have no salvage value. The class rate is 20%, T = 40%, k = 10% and the Accelerated Investment Incentive applies to net acquisitions. What is the PV of the CCA tax shield?","answer":31259.68,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5},"check":"(105000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (-12000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","steps":[{"label":"Net acquisition cost = 180,000 − 75,000","value":105000.0,"check":"180000-75000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Net salvage = 0 − 12,000","value":-12000.0,"check":"0-12000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"First term with AII factor","value":29272.73,"check":"(105000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}},{"label":"Salvage term (a negative S_N)","value":-1986.95,"check":"(-12000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PVCCA","value":31259.68,"check":"(105000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (-12000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.5}}],"solution":["Net acquisition = 180,000 − 75,000 = 105,000","Net salvage = 0 − 12,000 = −12,000","Base = 105,000 × 0.20 × 0.40/0.30 = 28,000; AII factor = 1.15/1.10 = 1.045455; first term = 29,272.73","Salvage term = (−12,000) × 0.20 × 0.40/0.30 / 1.10⁵ = −3,200/1.61051 = −1,986.95","PVCCA = 29,272.73 − (−1,986.95) = $31,259.68"],"keystrokes":["105000 × 0.2 × 0.4 ÷ 0.3 × 1.15 ÷ 1.1 = 29,272.73","+ 12000 × 0.2 × 0.4 ÷ 0.3 ÷ 1.1^5 = 31,259.68"],"errors":[{"id":"net-salvage-sign","label":"Used +12,000 as the net salvage","value":27285.78,"check":"(105000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (12000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","explain":"New minus old is 0 − 12,000 = −12,000; subtracting a negative term adds to PVCCA."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Ignored the net salvage","value":29272.73,"check":"(105000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1))","explain":"The net salvage of −12,000 still changes the shield."},{"id":"gross-cost-for-net","label":"Used the full cost of the new machine","value":52168.77,"check":"(180000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)*((1.5*0.1+1)/(1+0.1)) - (-12000*0.2*0.4)/(0.1+0.2)/(1+0.1)**5","explain":"I0 is the net acquisition cost of $105,000."}],"explanation":"Net acquisition cost is I0 and net salvage (new minus old, here negative) is S_N; subtracting a negative salvage term adds to the PV.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s16-19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-180","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm buys new equipment for $400,000 and, in the same year, sells old equipment from the same class for $62,000. The old equipment originally cost $50,000. What is the net acquisition amount?","options":["$350,000","$338,000","$400,000","$450,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The offset is the lesser of original cost ($50,000) and selling price ($62,000): net acquisition = 400,000 − 50,000 = $350,000. $338,000 uses the selling price although it exceeds cost; $400,000 ignores the sale; $450,000 adds instead of subtracting.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-181","concept":"fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a replacement analysis the new machine can be sold for $30,000 at the end of the project; if kept, the old machine would be sold then for $12,000. What net salvage value goes into the PVCCA?","options":["$42,000","$18,000","$12,000","$30,000"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Net salvage = new − old = 30,000 − 12,000 = $18,000. $42,000 adds the two; $12,000 uses only the old machine's value; $30,000 ignores the old machine's lost resale.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-182","concept":"fin201-c10-ocf","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project has sales of $500,000, cash costs of $300,000 and CCA of $50,000. The tax rate is 30%. What is the operating cash flow?","options":["$105,000","$140,000","$190,000","$200,000","None of the above"],"answer":4,"explanation":"OCF = (500,000 − 300,000)(0.70) + 0.30(50,000) = 140,000 + 15,000 = $155,000, which is not listed. $105,000 is net income (CCA not added back); $140,000 omits the CCA tax shield; $190,000 adds the whole CCA instead of T × CCA; $200,000 is S − C before any tax.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-5; S5 s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-401","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":["fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Foothills Machining sells the last lathe in its Class 8 pool for $9,000. The lathe's UCC at the time of sale is $14,000 and it originally cost $30,000. How does the tax system treat the $5,000 shortfall of proceeds below UCC?","options":["Recapture of CCA, taxed at the full corporate rate","A terminal loss, fully deductible against other income","A capital loss, only half of which is deductible","A capital gain, only half of which is taxable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Proceeds ($9,000) are below UCC ($14,000) and the class closes, so the $5,000 difference is a terminal loss. It is deducted in full at T, which creates a tax refund. Depreciable property gets no capital loss treatment, so 'a capital loss, half deductible' does not apply.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-402","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow River Marine sells its only dredge for $41,000. The dredge cost $60,000 and its UCC is $23,000. Which description of the tax result is correct?","options":["A capital gain of $18,000, only half of which is taxable","A terminal loss of $19,000 (ACB less POD)","No tax effect, because the proceeds are below the original cost","Recapture of CCA of $18,000, taxed at the full rate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"POD ($41,000) lies between UCC ($23,000) and ACB ($60,000), so the whole excess over UCC is recapture: 41,000 - 23,000 = $18,000, taxed at the full rate. A capital gain needs POD above ACB; the Australian-style 'gain over book value' rule is not the Canadian one.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-403","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The part of the proceeds that lies between UCC and ACB is taxed at the full corporate rate as _____.","options":["recapture of CCA","a capital gain","a terminal loss","a CCA tax shield"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Proceeds above UCC (up to the original cost, ACB) simply take back CCA that was claimed too generously, so it is recapture and is fully taxed. Only proceeds above ACB are a capital gain, and only half of that gain is taxed.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-404","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":["fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"A terminal loss can arise only when the sale of the asset leaves no assets in its CCA class.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"While other assets remain in the class the pool stays open: the proceeds just reduce UCC and CCA carries on, so there is no immediate recapture or terminal loss. Only when the last asset is sold does the class close and the difference between POD and UCC hit the tax return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-405","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts recapture of CCA with a terminal loss on the sale of the last asset in a class?","options":["Recapture happens when POD is below UCC and adds tax; a terminal loss happens when POD is above UCC and saves tax","Recapture is taxed at half the corporate rate; a terminal loss is deducted at the full rate","Recapture happens when POD is above UCC and adds tax; a terminal loss happens when POD is below UCC and saves tax","Recapture happens when POD is above ACB; a terminal loss happens when POD is below ACB"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both are measured against UCC. POD above UCC (to a maximum of ACB) means too much CCA was claimed and the excess is recaptured and taxed at T. POD below UCC means too little was claimed and the shortfall is a terminal loss that reduces tax by T times the loss. ACB only matters for the capital gain.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-406","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about a terminal loss is NOT correct?","options":["It is the amount by which UCC exceeds POD when the class closes","The tax saving equals T times the terminal loss","Only 50% of the terminal loss can be deducted","The after-tax cash from the sale is larger than the proceeds of disposition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The 50% inclusion rate applies to capital gains, not to CCA recapture or a terminal loss; a terminal loss is deducted in full. The other three statements are all true: CF_AT = POD + T(UCC - POD) exceeds POD.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-407","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the tax system collect tax on recapture even though the firm sold the asset for less than it originally paid?","options":["UCC fell below the sale price, so the CCA deductions already claimed turned out to be larger than the real loss in value","Sale proceeds are always taxed in full whenever a depreciable asset is sold","Recapture replaces the capital gains tax that would otherwise apply to the sale price","The firm must repay the CCA tax shield with interest, whatever the sale price"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CCA is a prepayment of the deduction for the expected loss of value. If the asset sells for more than UCC, the firm over-deducted by POD - UCC, and recapture claws that back at the full rate T. Selling below cost does not change this: recapture is measured against UCC, not against cost.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-408","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":["fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a disposal, a student finds that min(ACB, POD) - UCC = -$6,000, with T = 30%. What does this tell the firm?","options":["There is recapture of $6,000, costing $1,800 of tax","There is a capital gain of $6,000, of which $3,000 is taxable","The sale has no tax effect because the bracket is negative","There is a terminal loss of $6,000, giving a $1,800 tax refund on top of the sale proceeds"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A negative bracket means POD is below UCC, i.e. a terminal loss. In CF_AT = POD - T(-6,000) the minus sign turns into a refund: the firm collects POD plus $1,800.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-409","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":["fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In CF_AT = POD - T[min(ACB, POD) - UCC] - 0.5T max(POD - ACB, 0), why is ACB capped inside the first bracket?","options":["A sale price above ACB is never taxed","Recapture cannot exceed the CCA claimed (ACB − UCC); the rest is a capital gain","ACB is the value of the asset at which the pool closes","The half-year rule limits recapture to 50% of ACB"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total CCA claimed is ACB - UCC, so the most that can be recaptured is that amount. Any proceeds above ACB are a true capital gain, picked up by the last term at the 50% inclusion rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-410","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Pallet Co. sells its last wrapping machine for exactly its UCC. What are the tax consequences of the disposal?","options":["A terminal loss equal to the proceeds","Recapture equal to the original cost","A capital gain equal to the proceeds less the original cost","None: no recapture or terminal loss, so CF_AT equals the proceeds"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Recapture and terminal loss both equal the gap between POD and UCC. With POD = UCC the gap is zero, so CF_AT = POD.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-411","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":["fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Fruit Packers closes its Class 8 pool by selling the last packing line for $62,000. The line cost $130,000 and its UCC is $84,500. The tax rate is 30%. What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","options":["$43,400.00","$55,250.00","$62,000.00","$68,750.00","$82,400.00"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Terminal loss = UCC - POD = 84,500 - 62,000 = 22,500, so the refund is 0.30 x 22,500 = 6,750 and CF_AT = 62,000 + 6,750 = $68,750. Subtracting the refund gives $55,250 (sign error); taxing the whole POD gives $43,400; and using POD - ACB as the base gives $82,400.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-412","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Ridge Printing closes a CCA class by selling its last press for $19,500. The press cost $26,000 and has a UCC of $7,200. The tax rate is 35%. What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","options":["$12,675.00","$12,920.00","$15,195.00","$19,500.00","$21,775.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Recapture = POD - UCC = 19,500 - 7,200 = 12,300 (POD is below ACB, so there is no capital gain). Tax = 0.35 x 12,300 = 4,305 and CF_AT = 19,500 - 4,305 = $15,195. Taxing the whole $19,500 gives $12,675, and using ACB - UCC as the base gives $12,920.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-413","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lakeshore Dairy bought a bottling line for $50,000, claimed CCA on it for years, and then sold it as the last asset in its class for $20,000. The tax rate is 40%. Counting every CCA tax shield and the tax effect of the sale, how much tax did the line save in total?","options":["$8,000.00","$12,000.00","$20,000.00","$28,000.00","$30,000.00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Whatever the CCA schedule, the lifetime deductions equal cost less proceeds once recapture or terminal loss settles the account: total tax saved = T x (cost - proceeds) = 0.40 x 30,000 = $12,000. Using T x cost ($20,000) ignores the sale; using T x POD ($8,000) measures the wrong thing.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s25"},{"id":"fin201-q10-414","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Tooling bought a $85,000 machine (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule applies in year 1). It sells the machine, its last asset in the class, for $31,000 at the end of year 3, after claiming year 3 CCA. The tax rate is 40%. What after-tax cash flow does the sale produce?","answer":32761.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"31000+0.4*(85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-31000)","steps":[{"label":"UCC after year 1 CCA","value":72250.0,"check":"85000*(1-0.5*0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"UCC after year 2 CCA","value":50575.0,"check":"85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"UCC after year 3 CCA","value":35402.5,"check":"85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Terminal loss = UCC - POD","value":4402.5,"check":"85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-31000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax refund = T x terminal loss","value":1761.0,"check":"0.4*(85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-31000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax cash flow CF_AT","value":32761.0,"check":"31000+0.4*(85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-31000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Year 1 CCA = 0.5 x 0.30 x 85,000 = 12,750, so UCC1 = 72,250","UCC2 = 72,250 x 0.70 = 50,575; UCC3 = 50,575 x 0.70 = 35,402.50","POD = 31,000 < UCC = 35,402.50, so terminal loss = 4,402.50","Refund = 0.40 x 4,402.50 = 1,761.00","CF_AT = 31,000 + 1,761 = $32,761.00"],"keystrokes":["85000 x (1 - 0.15) = 72250","x 0.7 = 50575","x 0.7 = 35402.5","- 31000 = 4402.5","x 0.4 = 1761","+ 31000 = 32761"],"errors":[{"id":"terminal-loss-sign","label":"Subtracted the refund","value":29239.0,"check":"31000-0.4*(85000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-31000)","explain":"A terminal loss saves tax: the refund is added to the proceeds, not subtracted."},{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole sale price","value":18600.0,"check":"31000*(1-0.4)","explain":"Tax applies to the gap between POD and UCC, not to the whole proceeds."},{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Ignored the half-year rule in year 1","value":30262.0,"check":"31000-0.4*(31000-85000*(1-0.3)**3)","explain":"Year 1 CCA is only half the normal amount, so UCC is higher than 85,000 x 0.7^3; without it the sale looks like a small recapture instead of a loss."}],"explanation":"Build the UCC from the half-year schedule, compare it with the proceeds, and add the refund because POD is below UCC.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11, L05B s7, s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-415","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Niagara Glassworks buys a $120,000 furnace (CCA rate 20%) under the Accelerated Investment Incentive (1.5 x d in year 1). It sells the furnace, the last asset in the class, for $90,000 at the end of year 2 after claiming year 2 CCA. T = 38%. Find the after-tax cash flow from the sale.","answer":81336.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"90000-0.38*(90000-120000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2))","steps":[{"label":"UCC after year 1","value":84000.0,"check":"120000*(1-1.5*0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"UCC after year 2","value":67200.0,"check":"120000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Recapture = POD - UCC","value":22800.0,"check":"90000-120000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax on recapture","value":8664.0,"check":"0.38*(90000-120000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax cash flow","value":81336.0,"check":"90000-0.38*(90000-120000*(1-1.5*0.2)*(1-0.2))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Year 1 CCA = 1.5 x 0.20 x 120,000 = 36,000, so UCC1 = 84,000","UCC2 = 84,000 x 0.80 = 67,200","POD 90,000 is above UCC 67,200 but below ACB 120,000: recapture = 22,800, no capital gain","Tax = 0.38 x 22,800 = 8,664","CF_AT = 90,000 - 8,664 = $81,336.00"],"keystrokes":["120000 x (1 - 0.3) = 84000","x 0.8 = 67200","90000 - 67200 = 22800","x 0.38 = 8664","90000 - 8664 = 81336"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year rule instead of AII","value":88632.0,"check":"90000-0.38*(90000-120000*0.9*0.8)","explain":"Under AII the first-year CCA is 1.5 x d x I0, not 0.5 x d x I0; the wrong factor leaves UCC too high."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Used POD - ACB as the base","value":101400.0,"check":"90000-0.38*(90000-120000)","explain":"The recapture base is POD - UCC (capped at ACB - UCC); POD - ACB is the capital-gain test and here it is negative."},{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole sale price","value":55800.0,"check":"90000*(1-0.38)","explain":"Only the excess over UCC is taxed, not the whole proceeds."}],"explanation":"UCC after two AII years is 67,200, below the $90,000 price, so the sale creates $22,800 of recapture (and no capital gain because POD is below ACB).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11, L05B s8, s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-416","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Red Deer Crane Services sells a crane for $143,000. The crane's original cost was $410,000 and its undepreciated capital cost is $96,420. It is the last asset in its class and T = 28%. What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","answer":129957.6,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"143000-0.28*(143000-96420)","steps":[{"label":"Recapture = POD - UCC","value":46580.0,"check":"143000-96420","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax on recapture","value":13042.4,"check":"0.28*(143000-96420)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"CF_AT","value":129957.6,"check":"143000-0.28*(143000-96420)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["POD 143,000 is above UCC 96,420 and below ACB 410,000: recapture = 46,580","Tax = 0.28 x 46,580 = 13,042.40","CF_AT = 143,000 - 13,042.40 = $129,957.60"],"keystrokes":["143000 - 96420 = 46580","x 0.28 = 13042.4","143000 - 13042.4 = 129957.6"],"errors":[{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole sale price","value":102960.0,"check":"143000*(1-0.28)","explain":"Tax is only on POD - UCC."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Used POD - ACB as the base","value":217760.0,"check":"143000-0.28*(143000-410000)","explain":"POD - ACB is negative here; it is not the recapture amount."},{"id":"ignored-sale-tax","label":"Ignored the recapture tax","value":143000.0,"check":"143000","explain":"The sale price is above UCC, so CCA is clawed back at the full rate."}],"explanation":"One case only: POD between UCC and ACB means recapture of POD - UCC at the full rate and nothing else.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q11c, L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-417","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Fraser Valley Packaging buys a $40,000 machine (CCA rate 25%, half-year rule) and sells it for $12,000 at the end of year 3 after claiming year 3 CCA; the class then closes. T = 40%. Find the total tax saved: the three CCA tax shields plus the tax effect of the sale.","answer":11200.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.4*(0.5*0.25*40000+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)*(1-0.25))+0.4*(40000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2-12000)","steps":[{"label":"CCA years 1 to 3 in total","value":20312.5,"check":"0.5*0.25*40000+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)*(1-0.25)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"UCC after year 3","value":19687.5,"check":"40000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax shields from CCA","value":8125.0,"check":"0.4*(0.5*0.25*40000+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)*(1-0.25))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Terminal-loss refund","value":3075.0,"check":"0.4*(40000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2-12000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Total tax saved","value":11200.0,"check":"0.4*(0.5*0.25*40000+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)*(1-0.25))+0.4*(40000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2-12000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CCA1 = 0.5 x 0.25 x 40,000 = 5,000 (UCC1 = 35,000); CCA2 = 8,750 (UCC2 = 26,250); CCA3 = 6,562.50 (UCC3 = 19,687.50)","Tax shields = 0.40 x (5,000 + 8,750 + 6,562.50) = 8,125.00","Terminal loss = 19,687.50 - 12,000 = 7,687.50; refund = 0.40 x 7,687.50 = 3,075.00","Total = 8,125 + 3,075 = $11,200.00","Cross-check: T x (cost - proceeds) = 0.40 x (40,000 - 12,000) = 11,200"],"keystrokes":["Shields: 0.4 x 20312.5 = 8125","Refund: 0.4 x (19687.5 - 12000) = 3075","Total = 11200","Check: 0.4 x 28000 = 11200"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-terminal-loss","label":"Counted only the three CCA shields","value":8125.0,"check":"0.4*(0.5*0.25*40000+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)*(1-0.25))","explain":"The sale closes the class and produces a refund on the terminal loss; that refund is part of the total tax saved."},{"id":"terminal-loss-sign","label":"Subtracted the refund","value":5050.0,"check":"0.4*(0.5*0.25*40000+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)+0.25*(40000-0.5*0.25*40000)*(1-0.25))-0.4*(40000*(1-0.5*0.25)*(1-0.25)**2-12000)","explain":"A terminal loss reduces taxes, so it adds to the tax saved."},{"id":"ignored-sale-proceeds","label":"Applied T to the full cost","value":16000.0,"check":"0.4*40000","explain":"The asset is sold for $12,000, so only cost less proceeds is ever deducted: T x (40,000 - 12,000)."}],"explanation":"The cross-check T x (cost - proceeds) is a quick way to catch errors in a long schedule.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s25"},{"id":"fin201-q10-418","concept":"fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Athabasca Freight buys a $64,000 trailer (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule). It plans to sell it for $21,000 at the end of year 4, after claiming year 4 CCA, closing the class. T = 30% and the discount rate is 9% per annum. What is the present value today of the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","answer":16681.54,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(21000+0.3*(64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3-21000))/1.09**4","steps":[{"label":"UCC after year 4","value":29491.2,"check":"64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Terminal loss","value":8491.2,"check":"64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3-21000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax cash flow in year 4","value":23547.36,"check":"21000+0.3*(64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3-21000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Present value at 9%","value":16681.54,"check":"(21000+0.3*(64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3-21000))/1.09**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["UCC1 = 64,000 x 0.90 = 57,600; then x 0.80 three times: UCC4 = 29,491.20","POD 21,000 < UCC: terminal loss = 8,491.20, refund = 0.30 x 8,491.20 = 2,547.36","CF_AT in year 4 = 21,000 + 2,547.36 = 23,547.36","PV = 23,547.36 / 1.09^4 = $16,681.54"],"keystrokes":["64000 x 0.9 = 57600","x 0.8 x 0.8 x 0.8 = 29491.2","29491.2 - 21000 = 8491.2","x 0.3 + 21000 = 23547.36","N = 4, I/Y = 9, FV = 23547.36, CPT PV"],"errors":[{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Left the year-4 cash flow undiscounted","value":23547.36,"check":"21000+0.3*(64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3-21000)","explain":"The sale happens at the end of year 4; bring it back four years at 9%."},{"id":"terminal-loss-sign","label":"Subtracted the refund before discounting","value":13072.32,"check":"(21000-0.3*(64000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3-21000))/1.09**4","explain":"UCC is above POD, so the refund is added."},{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Skipped the half-year rule","value":15985.13,"check":"(21000+0.3*(64000*(1-0.2)**4-21000))/1.09**4","explain":"Without the half-year rule UCC after four years is too low (26,214.40) and the refund too small."}],"explanation":"A terminal loss on a future sale: compute UCC from the schedule, add the refund to the price, then discount.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21-22, SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-421","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Barge Ltd. sells its last barge in the class for $520,000. The barge cost $400,000 and its UCC is $150,000. What taxable items does the sale create?","options":["Recapture of CCA of $370,000 and no capital gain","Recapture of CCA of $250,000 and a capital gain of $120,000, of which half is taxable","A capital gain of $370,000, of which half is taxable","Recapture of CCA of $250,000 only, because the rest is a return of cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ACB ($400,000) minus UCC ($150,000) = $250,000 is recapture of all the CCA claimed. The $120,000 above ACB is a real gain, and only 50% of it is added to income. Putting the whole excess over UCC into recapture ignores the ACB cap.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-422","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Three sales each close their CCA class. (i) POD $55,000, UCC $62,000, ACB $90,000. (ii) POD $80,000, UCC $30,000, ACB $90,000. (iii) POD $120,000, UCC $30,000, ACB $90,000. Which sale creates a capital gain?","options":["Only (ii)","(ii) and (iii)","Only (iii)","(i) and (iii)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A capital gain requires POD above ACB. Sale (i) has a terminal loss, (ii) has recapture of $50,000 and no gain, and only (iii) exceeds its ACB, giving $30,000 of capital gain plus $60,000 of recapture.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-423","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"On the sale of depreciable property above its original cost, only 50% of the capital gain is included in taxable income.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The course formula carries 0.5 x T x max(POD - ACB, 0): half of the capital gain is taxed at T, so the effective rate on the gain is T/2. The recapture part, by contrast, is taxed in full.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-424","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The capital gain on the sale of a depreciable asset is the proceeds of disposition minus the _____.","options":["ACB (adjusted cost base)","UCC (undepreciated capital cost)","salvage value","present value of the CCA shield"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capital gain = POD - ACB, and only when this is positive. Gaps between UCC and ACB are recapture, not gain. Note that the Australian rule in the lecture measures gain against book value instead; for Canada the benchmark is ACB.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q10-425","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"At a 30% tax rate, how is $1 of recapture of CCA taxed compared with $1 of capital gain on the same sale?","options":["Recapture costs 15 cents of tax; the capital gain costs 30 cents","Recapture costs 30 cents of tax; the capital gain costs 15 cents","Both cost 30 cents of tax","Both cost 15 cents because only half of any sale gain is taxable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recapture takes back CCA that was deducted at the full rate, so it is taxed in full: T x $1. A capital gain is only 50% included: 0.5 x T x $1. The inclusion rate never applies to recapture.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-426","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"A firm closes a CCA class and sells its last asset for more than its original cost. Which item is NOT part of the after-tax cash flow in that case?","options":["The proceeds of disposition","Tax at the full rate on ACB minus UCC","Tax at half the rate on the proceeds above ACB","A terminal-loss refund"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With POD above ACB, the cash flow is POD - T(ACB - UCC) - 0.5T(POD - ACB). A terminal loss needs POD below UCC, which cannot be true when POD exceeds ACB.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-427","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the extra tax on proceeds above ACB lower per dollar than the tax on the recapture below it?","options":["Only half of a capital gain is added to taxable income, while recapture reverses deductions that were claimed in full","Capital gains are taxed in a separate lower tax bracket for corporations","Proceeds above ACB are not income because the asset has already been paid for","Recapture is taxed twice: once as income and once when the cash is distributed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CCA deductions saved tax at the full rate T, so recapture reverses them at T. A gain above cost is a capital gain and the tax system includes only 50% of it, an effective rate of T/2.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-428","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In CF_AT = POD - T[min(ACB, POD) - UCC] - 0.5T max(POD - ACB, 0), what is the value of the last term when POD is below ACB?","options":["Negative, giving a tax refund on the shortfall below ACB","0.5T x (ACB - POD), a capital loss deduction","Zero, because max(POD - ACB, 0) = 0","T x (POD - UCC), because the bracket switches to UCC"],"answer":2,"explanation":"max(POD - ACB, 0) is zero for any sale at or below ACB, so no capital gain is taxed. Below ACB the only tax effect is the first bracket, which is recapture if POD > UCC or a terminal loss if POD < UCC.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-429","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okotoks Stone Works sells its only quarry loader for $92,500. The loader cost $75,000 and has a UCC of $21,300. Which tax items must be computed?","options":["Recapture of $71,200 and no capital gain","A capital gain of $71,200 only","Recapture of $53,700 and a capital gain of $17,500","Recapture of $17,500 and a capital gain of $53,700"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Recapture = ACB - UCC = 75,000 - 21,300 = $53,700 because POD is above ACB. Capital gain = POD - ACB = 92,500 - 75,000 = $17,500, of which half is taxed. The recapture is not POD - UCC once POD exceeds ACB.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-430","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Haida Ferries Ltd. sells its last ferry for $610,000 (cost $480,000, UCC $205,000, tax rate 28%). What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","options":["$439,200.00","$478,400.00","$496,600.00","$514,800.00","$591,800.00"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Recapture = 480,000 - 205,000 = 275,000 (tax 77,000); capital gain = 610,000 - 480,000 = 130,000, half taxed (tax 18,200). CF_AT = 610,000 - 77,000 - 18,200 = $514,800. Taxing the gain in full gives $496,600; taxing the whole price gives $439,200; counting POD - UCC as recapture and also taxing the gain double counts ($478,400); skipping recapture gives $591,800.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-431","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kamloops Quarry Co. closes its class by selling a crusher for $118,000 (cost $96,000, UCC $37,400, tax rate 32%). What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","options":["$80,240.00","$88,688.00","$92,208.00","$95,728.00","$114,480.00"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Recapture 58,600 x 0.32 = 18,752; capital gain 22,000 x 0.5 x 0.32 = 3,520. CF_AT = 118,000 - 18,752 - 3,520 = $95,728. Taxing the gain at the full rate gives $92,208; taxing the whole sale price gives $80,240; measuring recapture up to POD and also taxing the gain gives $88,688; skipping recapture gives $114,480.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-432","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Coastal Tug and Barge has sold its last asset in the class for more than ACB. T = 30%. If the sale price rises by another $10,000, still above ACB, by how much does the after-tax cash flow rise?","options":["$1,500.00","$3,000.00","$7,000.00","$8,500.00","$10,000.00"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each extra dollar above ACB is a capital gain, taxed at 0.5T = 15%. After-tax cash rises by 10,000 x (1 - 0.15) = $8,500. Using the full rate gives $7,000 (the forgotten half-gain error); ignoring tax gives $10,000; $1,500 is only the tax on the extra gain, not the cash that is kept.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-433","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Barge Ltd. sells its last barge, which cost $400,000 and has a UCC of $150,000, for $520,000. The tax rate is 30%. Calculate the after-tax cash flow from the sale.","answer":427000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"520000-0.3*(400000-150000)-0.5*0.3*(520000-400000)","steps":[{"label":"Recapture = ACB - UCC","value":250000.0,"check":"400000-150000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Capital gain = POD - ACB","value":120000.0,"check":"520000-400000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax on recapture","value":75000.0,"check":"0.3*(400000-150000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax on capital gain","value":18000.0,"check":"0.5*0.3*(520000-400000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"CF_AT","value":427000.0,"check":"520000-0.3*(400000-150000)-0.5*0.3*(520000-400000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Since POD 520,000 > ACB 400,000: recapture = ACB - UCC = 250,000 and capital gain = 120,000","Tax on recapture = 0.30 x 250,000 = 75,000","Tax on gain = 0.5 x 0.30 x 120,000 = 18,000","CF_AT = 520,000 - 75,000 - 18,000 = $427,000.00"],"keystrokes":["400000 - 150000 = 250000","x 0.3 = 75000","520000 - 400000 = 120000","x 0.15 = 18000","520000 - 75000 - 18000 = 427000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed the gain at the full rate","value":409000.0,"check":"520000-0.3*(400000-150000)-0.3*(520000-400000)","explain":"Only 50% of the capital gain is taxed."},{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole sale price","value":364000.0,"check":"520000*(1-0.3)","explain":"Tax is not charged on the whole proceeds."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Used POD - UCC as recapture and also taxed the gain","value":391000.0,"check":"520000-0.3*(520000-150000)-0.5*0.3*(520000-400000)","explain":"Recapture stops at ACB; the part above ACB is the capital gain, not more recapture."}],"explanation":"Three pieces: the proceeds, a full-rate tax on ACB - UCC, and a half-rate tax on POD - ACB.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q10-434","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okotoks Stone Works closes its CCA class by selling its only loader for $92,500. The loader cost $75,000 and has a UCC of $21,300. The tax rate is 35%. Find the after-tax cash flow from the sale.","answer":70642.5,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"92500-0.35*(75000-21300)-0.5*0.35*(92500-75000)","steps":[{"label":"Tax on recapture","value":18795.0,"check":"0.35*(75000-21300)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax on capital gain","value":3062.5,"check":"0.5*0.35*(92500-75000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"CF_AT","value":70642.5,"check":"92500-0.35*(75000-21300)-0.5*0.35*(92500-75000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["POD 92,500 > ACB 75,000, so recapture = 75,000 - 21,300 = 53,700 and capital gain = 17,500","Tax = 0.35 x 53,700 + 0.5 x 0.35 x 17,500 = 18,795.00 + 3,062.50 = 21,857.50","CF_AT = 92,500 - 21,857.50 = $70,642.50"],"keystrokes":["75000 - 21300 = 53700","x 0.35 = 18795","(92500 - 75000) x 0.175 = 3062.5","92500 - 18795 - 3062.5 = 70642.5"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed the gain at the full rate","value":67580.0,"check":"92500-0.35*(75000-21300)-0.35*(92500-75000)","explain":"A capital gain is only 50% taxable."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Recapture measured to POD, then the gain taxed again","value":64517.5,"check":"92500-0.35*(92500-21300)-0.5*0.35*(92500-75000)","explain":"Recapture is capped at ACB - UCC."},{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole sale price","value":60125.0,"check":"92500*(1-0.35)","explain":"Only recapture and the half-taxed gain are taxed."}],"explanation":"Same three-part formula as before, with smaller numbers: the sale price exceeds ACB so both taxes apply.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-435","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kootenay Rail Services buys a $200,000 locomotive (CCA rate 30%, Accelerated Investment Incentive: 1.5 x d in year 1). Strong demand lets it sell the locomotive, the last asset in the class, for $230,000 at the end of year 2, after claiming year 2 CCA. T = 40%. What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","answer":174800.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"230000-0.4*(200000-200000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))-0.5*0.4*(230000-200000)","steps":[{"label":"UCC after year 1","value":110000.0,"check":"200000*(1-1.5*0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"UCC after year 2","value":77000.0,"check":"200000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Recapture = ACB - UCC","value":123000.0,"check":"200000-200000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Capital gain = POD - ACB","value":30000.0,"check":"230000-200000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"CF_AT","value":174800.0,"check":"230000-0.4*(200000-200000*(1-1.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))-0.5*0.4*(230000-200000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CCA1 = 1.5 x 0.30 x 200,000 = 90,000, UCC1 = 110,000; UCC2 = 110,000 x 0.70 = 77,000","POD 230,000 > ACB 200,000: recapture = 200,000 - 77,000 = 123,000; capital gain = 30,000","Tax = 0.40 x 123,000 + 0.5 x 0.40 x 30,000 = 49,200 + 6,000 = 55,200","CF_AT = 230,000 - 55,200 = $174,800.00"],"keystrokes":["200000 x (1 - 0.45) = 110000","x 0.7 = 77000","200000 - 77000 = 123000","x 0.4 = 49200","30000 x 0.2 = 6000","230000 - 49200 - 6000 = 174800"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed the gain at the full rate","value":168800.0,"check":"230000-0.4*123000-0.4*30000","explain":"Only half of the capital gain is taxed."},{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year rule instead of AII","value":191600.0,"check":"230000-0.4*(200000-200000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))-0.5*0.4*(230000-200000)","explain":"The question specifies AII, so year 1 CCA is 1.5 x d x I0."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Taxed POD - UCC as recapture","value":162800.0,"check":"230000-0.4*(230000-77000)-0.5*0.4*30000","explain":"Recapture is limited to ACB - UCC; the remainder is the capital gain."}],"explanation":"Combine the schedule (AII) with the three-part disposal formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s8, s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-436","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A mining contractor will close its CCA class by selling its last rig, which cost $240,000 and has a UCC of $110,000, for a price above cost. T = 30%. What sale price leaves exactly $300,000 after tax?","answer":356470.59,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(300000+0.3*(240000-110000)-0.5*0.3*240000)/(1-0.5*0.3)","steps":[{"label":"Tax on recapture (fixed)","value":39000.0,"check":"0.3*(240000-110000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Equation: 0.85 x POD - 3,000 = 300,000, so POD = 303,000 / 0.85","value":356470.59,"check":"(300000+0.3*(240000-110000)-0.5*0.3*240000)/(1-0.5*0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CF_AT = POD - 0.30(240,000 - 110,000) - 0.15(POD - 240,000) = POD - 39,000 - 0.15 POD + 36,000","CF_AT = 0.85 POD - 3,000 = 300,000","POD = 303,000 / 0.85 = $356,470.59","Check: POD > ACB (240,000), as assumed"],"keystrokes":["Each extra dollar of price above ACB nets 0.85","(300000 + 3000) / 0.85 = 356470.59"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed every extra dollar at the full rate","value":381428.57,"check":"(300000+0.3*(240000-110000)-0.3*240000)/(1-0.3)","explain":"The gain is half-taxed, so each dollar above ACB is worth 85 cents, not 70."},{"id":"ignored-sale-tax","label":"Left out the recapture tax","value":310588.24,"check":"(300000-0.5*0.3*240000)/(1-0.5*0.3)","explain":"The CCA recapture tax is a fixed cost of selling above UCC."}],"explanation":"Because the gain is half-taxed, every dollar of price above ACB nets 85 cents after the fixed recapture tax.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-437","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Wetaskiwin Grain Systems plans to sell its last silo-loading rig at the end of year 4 for $185,000, closing the class. The rig cost $150,000 and its UCC at that time will be $58,000. T = 35% and the discount rate is 10% per annum. What is the present value today of the after-tax sale proceeds?","answer":100181.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(185000-0.35*(150000-58000)-0.5*0.35*(185000-150000))/1.1**4","steps":[{"label":"Tax on recapture","value":32200.0,"check":"0.35*(150000-58000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax on capital gain","value":6125.0,"check":"0.5*0.35*(185000-150000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"CF_AT in year 4","value":146675.0,"check":"185000-0.35*(150000-58000)-0.5*0.35*(185000-150000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Present value at 10%","value":100181.0,"check":"(185000-0.35*(150000-58000)-0.5*0.35*(185000-150000))/1.1**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Recapture = 150,000 - 58,000 = 92,000, tax = 32,200; gain = 35,000, tax = 0.5 x 0.35 x 35,000 = 6,125","CF_AT = 185,000 - 32,200 - 6,125 = 146,675","PV = 146,675 / 1.10^4 = $100,181.00"],"keystrokes":["185000 - 32200 - 6125 = 146675","N = 4, I/Y = 10, FV = 146675, CPT PV"],"errors":[{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the year-4 cash flow","value":146675.0,"check":"185000-0.35*(150000-58000)-0.5*0.35*(185000-150000)","explain":"The sale occurs in four years; discount it."},{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed the gain at the full rate","value":95997.54,"check":"(185000-0.35*(150000-58000)-0.35*(185000-150000))/1.1**4","explain":"Only half of the capital gain is taxed."}],"explanation":"Compute the year-4 after-tax cash flow with the three-part formula and discount it four years.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21-22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-438","concept":"fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the sale of Prairie Barge Ltd.'s last barge (POD $520,000, ACB $400,000, UCC $150,000, T = 30%), the firm pays recapture tax and capital gains tax. What percentage of the $520,000 sale price goes to tax? Answer in % to two decimals.","answer":17.88,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(0.3*(400000-150000)+0.5*0.3*(520000-400000))/520000*100","steps":[{"label":"Total tax","value":93000.0,"check":"0.3*(400000-150000)+0.5*0.3*(520000-400000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Tax as % of POD","value":17.8846,"check":"(0.3*(400000-150000)+0.5*0.3*(520000-400000))/520000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Tax on recapture = 0.30 x 250,000 = 75,000; tax on gain = 0.15 x 120,000 = 18,000","Total tax = 93,000","93,000 / 520,000 = 17.88%"],"keystrokes":["93000 / 520000 = 0.1788 -> 17.88%"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-half-gain","label":"Taxed the gain at the full rate","value":21.35,"check":"(0.3*(400000-150000)+0.3*(520000-400000))/520000*100","explain":"The gain portion carries only half the rate."},{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Used the full tax rate on the whole price","value":30.0,"check":"0.3*100","explain":"Tax applies only to recapture and the gain, and the gain is half-taxed."}],"explanation":"The effective rate is below 30% because 120,000 of the 520,000 is taxed at only 15%.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-441","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Prairie Haulage owns 14 trailers in the same CCA class and sells one of them for less than its share of UCC. The other 13 trailers stay in service. What is the status of the pool?","options":["The pool stays open: there is no immediate recapture or terminal loss","The pool closes: the loss is a terminal loss deducted this year","The pool closes: the sale price is recapture","The pool is frozen: no CCA can be claimed until the trailer is replaced"],"answer":0,"explanation":"As long as other assets remain in the class, the pool remains open. The sale proceeds just reduce the pool's UCC and CCA keeps being claimed on what is left, so there is no immediate recapture or terminal loss (SM 10-11).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-442","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss","fin201-c10-capital-gain-cfat"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Marine sells the only vessel it has ever owned in its CCA class and has no plans to buy another. Which treatment applies to the sale?","options":["The pool stays open, so the proceeds simply reduce UCC and nothing is taxed","The pool stays open, but only half of any recapture is taxed","The pool closes, but the tax effect is spread over the following years by continued CCA","The pool closes, so the full CF_AT formula applies: recapture, terminal loss or capital gain are taxed now"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With no assets left in the class, no CCA can be claimed in later years, so the gap between POD and UCC has to be settled immediately as recapture, terminal loss or capital gain.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11, L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-443","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When a sale leaves other assets in the CCA class, the pool is said to remain _____ and the proceeds only reduce its UCC.","options":["closed","open","terminated","capitalized"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An open pool keeps generating CCA. The sale shrinks the UCC base, so the tax effect arrives gradually through lower future CCA instead of through an immediate recapture or terminal-loss entry.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-444","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A machine is sold for less than its UCC. How does the tax outcome differ between an open pool and a closed pool?","options":["Open pool: a terminal loss is deducted at once. Closed pool: no immediate effect","Open pool: a capital loss, half deductible. Closed pool: a terminal loss, fully deductible","There is no difference; both give a terminal loss, but the open pool defers the refund one year","Open pool: no immediate effect, lower UCC carries forward. Closed pool: a terminal loss is deducted at once"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A terminal loss only exists when the class closes. In an open pool the shortfall stays inside the pool's UCC and is deducted gradually as CCA on the remaining balance.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-445","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a consequence of selling the last asset in a CCA class?","options":["No further CCA tax shields are available from that class","CCA continues to be claimed each year on the remaining UCC","The difference between POD and UCC is settled as recapture or terminal loss","The after-tax salvage uses the CF_AT formula"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Once the class is empty there is no UCC left and no further CCA. Recapture or terminal loss settles the account immediately, and the CF_AT formula gives the cash result.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11, L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-446","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does selling one asset out of a larger pool not create a terminal loss, even if the price is below the asset's cost?","options":["Because a terminal loss only exists when the sale price is below ACB","Because assets sold below cost are treated as capital losses","The pool's remaining UCC stays in the pool and keeps being written off through future CCA","Because the Canada Revenue Agency does not allow any tax effect on the sale of used equipment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CCA is calculated on the whole pool balance. The sale price just reduces that balance, and the part of the original cost that was not recovered is still depreciated in later years. Only an empty pool needs an immediate settlement.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-447","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"For an asset sold at time N from an open pool, a student computes S_N x d x T / (k + d) x 1/(1 + k)^N. What does the result represent?","options":["The PV of the CCA tax shield lost because the proceeds leave the pool","The after-tax value of the sale proceeds","The present value of the tax paid on recapture at the sale","The present value of the capital gains tax at the sale"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is the salvage term of the PV of CCA tax shield. Selling at S_N removes S_N from the UCC base, so the CCA shields that would have come from it forever after year N are lost; the term values that lost stream at time 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-448","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-net-acquisitions"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Parcel replaces a sorting machine and buys the new machine for the same CCA class in the same year as it sells the old one. How should the sale of the old machine be treated?","options":["As a sale that closes the class and triggers a terminal loss","As a sale from an open pool, with no immediate recapture or terminal loss","As a capital transaction taxed at half the corporate rate","As a sale whose proceeds are ignored for tax purposes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The new machine goes into the same class, so the class is not emptied. The old machine's proceeds are netted against the purchase (the net acquisitions idea) and the pool carries on.","source":"generated","ref":"S5 s26, SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-449","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the PV of CCA tax shield formula, the salvage term exists because the sale reduces the pool's UCC; if the asset were kept in the pool and never sold, the term would be dropped.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first term values CCA on the whole cost forever. The second term removes the shields attached to the salvage value when the asset leaves the pool. With no sale there is nothing to remove, so the first term stands alone.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-450","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which comparison of the course's two treatments of the sale proceeds is correct?","options":["Closed pool: proceeds received untaxed and the tax effect arrives as lost future CCA. Open pool: tax effect at the sale via CF_AT","Both: proceeds received untaxed, and the salvage term is used only for assets that are not sold","Closed pool: the salvage term is added to the shield. Open pool: the salvage term is subtracted","Closed pool: tax effect at the sale via CF_AT and no further CCA. Open pool: proceeds received untaxed and the tax effect arrives as lost future CCA via the salvage term"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The two are alternative ways to account for the same fact. Emptying the pool settles everything now (CF_AT); keeping it open pushes the settlement into future CCA, which the PV formula captures by subtracting the shield on S_N.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11, L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-451","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow Valley Plastics sells the last press in its CCA class for $24,000. The press cost $52,000 and its UCC is $31,000. The tax rate is 30%. What is the after-tax cash flow from the sale?","options":["$16,800.00","$21,700.00","$21,900.00","$24,000.00","$26,100.00"],"answer":4,"explanation":"The class closes, so the terminal loss UCC - POD = 7,000 saves 0.30 x 7,000 = 2,100 of tax: CF_AT = 24,000 + 2,100 = $26,100. $24,000 is the answer only if the pool stayed open; $21,900 subtracts the refund; $21,700 taxes the UCC instead of using the terminal loss; $16,800 taxes the whole price.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11, L05B s21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-452","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Fraser Cold Storage sells equipment from an open pool (CCA rate 10%) for $60,000 at the end of year 5. T = 35% and the discount rate is 14% per annum. The sale triggers no immediate tax. What is the present value of the CCA tax shield that the firm gives up?","options":["$3,986.38","$4,544.48","$8,750.00","$12,984.22","$27,266.85"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lost shield = S_N d T / (k + d) x 1/(1+k)^N = 60,000 x 0.10 x 0.35 / 0.24 / 1.14^5 = 8,750 / 1.92541 = $4,544.48. Not discounting gives $8,750; k - d in the denominator gives a hugely inflated value; leaving out T overstates it by 1/0.35.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-453","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Hydraulics sells a machine for $48,000. Its UCC is $66,500 and its ACB was $120,000. T = 32%. How much more after-tax cash does the firm receive if the sale closes the CCA class than if other assets stay in the class (and the sale triggers no immediate tax)?","answer":5920.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.32*(66500-48000)","steps":[{"label":"After-tax cash if the pool stays open","value":48000.0,"check":"48000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax cash if the pool closes","value":53920.0,"check":"48000+0.32*(66500-48000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Difference = T x terminal loss","value":5920.0,"check":"0.32*(66500-48000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Open pool: no tax effect at the sale, so cash = POD = 48,000","Closed pool: terminal loss = 66,500 - 48,000 = 18,500; refund = 0.32 x 18,500 = 5,920","Closed-pool cash = 48,000 + 5,920 = 53,920.00; difference = $5,920.00"],"keystrokes":["66500 - 48000 = 18500","x 0.32 = 5920"],"errors":[{"id":"terminal-loss-sign","label":"Subtracted instead of adding the refund","value":-5920.0,"check":"-0.32*(66500-48000)","explain":"A terminal loss produces a refund: the closed-pool cash flow is higher, not lower."},{"id":"pod-minus-acb","label":"Used ACB - POD as the loss","value":23040.0,"check":"0.32*(120000-48000)","explain":"The terminal loss is measured against UCC, not ACB."},{"id":"ignored-sale-tax","label":"Used the full tax rate on UCC","value":21280.0,"check":"0.32*66500","explain":"Only the gap between UCC and POD is deductible."}],"explanation":"The difference is purely the immediate terminal-loss refund; in an open pool the same shortfall is claimed slowly as CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-454","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nechako Lumber sells a mill component from an open pool (CCA rate 20%) for $45,000 at the end of year 6. The sale triggers no immediate tax. T = 38% and the discount rate is 12% per annum. What is the present value today of the CCA tax shield given up?","answer":5414.62,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"45000*0.2*0.38/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**6","steps":[{"label":"Perpetuity of lost shields at year 6: S d T / (k + d)","value":10687.5,"check":"45000*0.2*0.38/(0.12+0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Discount factor 1/(1.12)^6","value":0.5066,"check":"1/1.12**6","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Present value today","value":5414.62,"check":"45000*0.2*0.38/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Lost shield stream starts with 0.20 x 45,000 x 0.38 = 3,420 in year 7 and falls 20% a year","Value at year 6 = 3,420 / (0.12 + 0.20) = 10,687.50","PV today = 10,687.50 / 1.12^6 = $5,414.62"],"keystrokes":["45000 x 0.2 x 0.38 / 0.32 = 10687.5","/ 1.12^6 = 5414.62"],"errors":[{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount for six years","value":10687.5,"check":"45000*0.2*0.38/(0.12+0.2)","explain":"The lost shield stream starts at year 6; bring it back to today."},{"id":"k-minus-d","label":"Used k - d in the denominator","value":21658.48,"check":"45000*0.2*0.38/(0.2-0.12)/1.12**6","explain":"The CCA shield declines at d, so the denominator is k + d."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Left out the tax rate","value":14249.0,"check":"45000*0.2/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**6","explain":"A CCA deduction saves T dollars per dollar claimed, so T belongs in the numerator."}],"explanation":"Open-pool sale: the proceeds shrink the UCC base, so the future shields on that amount are lost; value them as a declining perpetuity.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-455","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cascade Tooling buys a $60,000 machine (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule). It sells the machine for $20,000 at the end of year 3 after claiming year 3 CCA. The sale empties the class. T = 30% and k = 10% per annum. Find the present value today of all the tax effects: the three CCA tax shields plus the terminal-loss refund.","answer":9786.63,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*60000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)*(1-0.3)/1.1**3+0.3*(60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-20000)/1.1**3","steps":[{"label":"DTS year 1","value":2700.0,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*60000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"DTS year 2","value":4590.0,"check":"0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"DTS year 3","value":3213.0,"check":"0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the three tax shields","value":8661.91,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*60000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)*(1-0.3)/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Terminal-loss refund in year 3","value":1497.0,"check":"0.3*(60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-20000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of all tax effects","value":9786.63,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*60000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)*(1-0.3)/1.1**3+0.3*(60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-20000)/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CCA1 = 9,000 (UCC1 = 51,000); CCA2 = 15,300 (UCC2 = 35,700); CCA3 = 10,710 (UCC3 = 24,990)","Tax shields: 2,700; 4,590; 3,213","PV of shields = 2,700/1.1 + 4,590/1.1^2 + 3,213/1.1^3 = 8,661.91","Terminal loss = 24,990 - 20,000 = 4,990; refund = 0.30 x 4,990 = 1,497.00","PV of refund = 1,497 / 1.1^3 = 1,124.72; total = $9,786.63"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 2700, C02 = 4590, C03 = 3213 + 1497 = 4710, I = 10, CPT NPV"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Counted only the terminal-loss refund","value":1124.72,"check":"0.3*(60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-20000)/1.1**3","explain":"The three years of CCA shields are part of the tax effect and must be added."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the refund","value":10158.91,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*60000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)*(1-0.3)/1.1**3+0.3*(60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-20000)","explain":"The refund arrives at the end of year 3."},{"id":"terminal-loss-sign","label":"Subtracted the refund","value":7537.19,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*60000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(60000-0.5*0.3*60000)*(1-0.3)/1.1**3-0.3*(60000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2-20000)/1.1**3","explain":"A terminal loss saves tax; its refund adds to the shields."}],"explanation":"Closed pool, explicit method: claim CCA each year, then settle the remaining UCC against the price in year 3.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s23-25, SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-456","concept":"fin201-c10-pool-open-closed","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Take Cascade Tooling's machine ($60,000 cost, CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, sold for $20,000 at the end of year 3, T = 30%, k = 10%). The PV of all tax effects if the class closes is $9,786.63. If instead the class stays open, the PV of the tax effects is the PV of CCA tax shield formula with the salvage term. How much larger is the closed-pool value?","answer":281.18,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"9786.63-(60000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/1.1**3)","steps":[{"label":"Perpetual shield with half-year factor","value":12886.36,"check":"60000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Salvage term","value":3380.92,"check":"20000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield, pool open","value":9505.45,"check":"60000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Closed minus open","value":281.18,"check":"9786.63-(60000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/1.1**3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["First term: 60,000 x 0.30 x 0.30 / 0.40 = 13,500; x (1 + 0.05)/1.10 = 12,886.36","Salvage term: 20,000 x 0.30 x 0.30 / 0.40 / 1.10^3 = 3,380.92","Open-pool PV = 12,886.36 - 3,380.92 = 9,505.45","Closed - open = 9,786.63 - 9,505.45 = $281.18"],"keystrokes":["13500 x 1.05 / 1.1 = 12886.36","- 4500 / 1.331 = 9505.45"],"errors":[{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Compared with the formula without the salvage term","value":-3099.74,"check":"9786.63-60000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","explain":"The asset is sold at year 3, so the salvage term must be subtracted in the open-pool formula."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the salvage term","value":1400.26,"check":"9786.63-(60000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3))","explain":"The salvage term is discounted three years."}],"explanation":"The closed pool collects the terminal-loss refund immediately, while the open pool takes the same deduction slowly, so the closed pool is worth slightly more in present value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22-25, SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-461","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Foothills Implements will build a new plant on land it bought years ago for $90,000 and that could be sold today for $150,000 after tax. Which amount belongs in the year-0 cash flow of the expansion project?","options":["$150,000, the after-tax value of the land if it were sold today","$90,000, the price originally paid for the land","$0, because the firm already owns the land","$60,000, the gain on the land since it was bought"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The relevant cost of using land is what the firm gives up by not selling it: its current after-tax market value, which is an opportunity cost. The original price is sunk, and owning the land does not make it free.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-11, S6 s2-4"},{"id":"fin201-q10-462","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-side-effects-financing"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new line of lightweight balers will cost the firm $40,000 of contribution margin a year on its existing heavyweight line. How does this erosion enter the expansion project's cash flows?","options":["It is ignored because the heavyweight line is a separate product","As a one-time cost at time 0 of $40,000","As a lost after-tax cash flow, $40,000 x (1 - T) every year, subtracted from the project","As a gain, because the lost sales are replaced by new sales"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Erosion is an incremental effect caused by taking the project, so it belongs in the project's cash flows. Lost revenue is taxed like revenue, so it reduces cash flow by 40,000 x (1 - T) in each year it lasts.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s3-5, TB 10.1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-463","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In an expansion project, the net working capital invested at time 0 is recovered at the _____ of the project.","options":["start","midpoint","end","end of year 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NWC is tied up while the project operates and is released when the project ends, so the recovery appears as a positive cash flow in the last year. It is not taxed because it is a return of capital already counted at 100%.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s12, S6 s5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-464","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-replacement-npv"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the cash flows of an expansion project differ from those of a replacement project?","options":["An expansion uses its own flows; a replacement uses new-minus-old differences (net acquisition, net salvage, savings)","An expansion is taxed at the full rate; a replacement is taxed at half the rate","An expansion ignores CCA; a replacement includes CCA","An expansion is discounted at the cost of capital; a replacement is discounted at the cost of the old machine"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both use the same NPV machinery, but a replacement is evaluated incrementally: sell the old, buy the new, and count only what changes. An expansion adds a stand-alone set of flows.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s2-10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-465","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following should NOT be included in the cash flows of an expansion project?","options":["The after-tax value of land the project will use","The initial investment in working capital","The after-tax revenue lost on an existing product","The $25,000 feasibility study that has already been paid"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The feasibility study is a sunk cost: it has been spent whether or not the project goes ahead, so its incremental cash flow is zero. The land, working capital and erosion are all caused by the decision to go ahead.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s10-11, S6 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q10-466","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the NPV of an expansion add the PV of the CCA tax shield as a separate item rather than including CCA in the annual operating cash flow?","options":["CCA is a cash outflow that must be discounted at a higher rate","CCA is not cash; its only value is the tax it saves, which the PV formula values once","CCA is already included in revenue, so it would be double counted","CCA is the only item taxed at the rate T rather than (1 − T)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The operating cash flow (S - C)(1 - T) deliberately leaves CCA out. The shield T x CCA is a separate stream of declining flows, valued by the formula I0 d T/(k + d) x first-year factor, less the salvage term. Counting CCA in both places would double count.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s15-17, S6 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-467","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"An expansion project has an NPV of -$31,400 at the firm's 12% cost of capital. What does this result say?","options":["The project earns exactly 12% and the firm should be indifferent","The project loses $31,400 of accounting profit in year 1","The project should be accepted because its cash flows are positive after year 0","The project earns less than 12% and destroys $31,400 of value, so reject it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"NPV is the value added in today's dollars, after paying investors their required return. A negative NPV means the PV of inflows is less than the PV of outflows, so the IRR is below 12%.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s6, L05A"},{"id":"fin201-q10-468","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which items make up the time-0 cash flow of an expansion NPV?","options":["Capital expenditure, sunk feasibility costs, and interest on the financing","Capital expenditure, initial NWC, and the opportunity cost of assets the project uses","Capital expenditure and the first year's CCA tax shield","Capital expenditure and the expected salvage value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CF0 = -CAPEX - NWC0 - opportunity cost. Sunk costs and financing costs are not incremental, the CCA shield is a later-year item and salvage arrives at the end.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s4-5"},{"id":"fin201-q10-469","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca","fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"If an expansion project's equipment is not sold at the end and stays in a pool that holds other assets, the PV of the CCA tax shield has no salvage term.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The salvage term removes the shields lost when the proceeds of a sale reduce the pool. With no sale there is no reduction, so only the first term, I0 d T/(k + d) x first-year factor, remains.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16, s22"},{"id":"fin201-q10-470","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Red Deer Welding will add a $150,000 line (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule). It earns $80,000 a year before tax for 3 years, needs $10,000 of working capital recovered at the end, and will be sold for $30,000 in year 3 (the pool stays open). A $5,000 feasibility study was already paid. T = 30%, k = 10%. What is the NPV?","options":["$28,947.69","$31,460.84","$36,460.84","$37,994.93","$41,532.21"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CF0 = -160,000; after-tax operating flow = 80,000 x 0.70 = 56,000 for 3 years (PV 139,263.71); year-3 salvage plus NWC = 40,000 (PV 30,052.59); PV of CCA shield with salvage term = 27,144.53. NPV = $36,460.84. Counting the sunk study lowers it by $5,000; forgetting the NWC recovery, dropping the salvage term or omitting the half-year factor each give a different wrong value.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-8, S6 s5-6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-471","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Saskatoon Seed Co. buys $300,000 of equipment (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule) for an expansion and sells it for $60,000 at the end of year 5; the pool stays open. T = 40% and k = 12%. What is the present value of the CCA tax shield?","options":["$55,982.14","$62,470.74","$66,488.60","$70,506.45","$70,982.14"],"answer":1,"explanation":"First term: 300,000 x 0.20 x 0.40/(0.12 + 0.20) = 75,000, times (1 + 0.06)/1.12 = 70,982.14. Salvage term: 60,000 x 0.20 x 0.40/0.32 / 1.12^5 = 8,511.40. PV = $62,470.74. Using the AII factor, no first-year factor, an undiscounted salvage term or no salvage term each gives one of the other values.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16, s22, SM 10-8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-472","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Brew Co. will build a canning line costing $480,000 (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule). It adds $520,000 of revenue and $310,000 of cash costs a year for 4 years, needs $40,000 of working capital up front (recovered at the end), and will be sold for $60,000 at the end of year 4; the CCA pool stays open. A $25,000 market study was already paid. T = 30% and the discount rate is 12%. What is the NPV?","answer":68071.15,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-(480000+40000)+(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12+(60000+40000)/1.12**4+480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**4","steps":[{"label":"Time-0 cash flow: CAPEX + NWC","value":-520000.0,"check":"-(480000+40000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax operating cash flow per year","value":147000.0,"check":"(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Annuity factor, 12%, 4 years","value":3.0373,"check":"(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"PV of operating cash flows","value":446490.35,"check":"(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of salvage + NWC recovery","value":63551.81,"check":"(60000+40000)/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield (with salvage term)","value":78028.99,"check":"480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":68071.15,"check":"-(480000+40000)+(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12+(60000+40000)/1.12**4+480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Sunk market study = 0. CF0 = -480,000 - 40,000 = -520,000","Annual after-tax operating flow = (520,000 - 310,000)(1 - 0.30) = 147,000","PV = 147,000 x [(1 - 1/1.12^4)/0.12] = 147,000 x 3.0373 = 446,490.35","Year 4: salvage 60,000 + NWC 40,000 = 100,000; PV = 63,551.81","PV of CCA = 480,000 x 0.2 x 0.3/0.32 x 1.06/1.12 - 60,000 x 0.2 x 0.3/0.32 / 1.12^4 = 85,178.57 - 7,149.58 = 78,028.99","NPV = -520,000 + 446,490.35 + 63,551.81 + 78,028.99 = $68,071.15"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -520000, C01 = 147000 (F01 = 3), C02 = 247000 (147000 + 100000), I = 12, NPV CPT","then add PV of CCA = 78028.99 (computed separately)"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Included the sunk market study","value":43071.15,"check":"-(480000+40000+25000)+(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12+(60000+40000)/1.12**4+480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**4","explain":"The market study is already paid; it is the same with or without the project."},{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Forgot to recover NWC at the end","value":42650.43,"check":"-(480000+40000)+(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12+60000/1.12**4+480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**4","explain":"Working capital comes back when the project ends; leaving it out charges the project for $40,000 forever."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Dropped the salvage term from PV_CCA","value":75220.73,"check":"-(480000+40000)+(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12+(60000+40000)/1.12**4+480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)","explain":"Equipment is sold at year 4, so the shields on the salvage value are lost and must be subtracted."},{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Applied no first-year factor","value":72892.58,"check":"-(480000+40000)+(520000-310000)*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12+(60000+40000)/1.12**4+480000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)-60000*0.2*0.3/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**4","explain":"In year 1 only half the usual CCA may be claimed; the factor (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k) captures that."}],"explanation":"Assemble CF0, the annual after-tax operating flow, terminal flows and the CCA shield, then add.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-8, S6 s5-6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-473","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Foothills Implements will build a baler plant on land it owns (worth $150,000 after tax now and in 4 years). Equipment costs $700,000 (CCA rate 20%, AII applies) and sells for $120,000 at the end of year 4; the pool stays open. Sales are $560,000 a year, costs $240,000, and the plant erodes $40,000 of existing sales a year. NWC is $40,000 at time 0, then 10% of sales, recovered at the end. A $12,000 study is sunk. T = 35%, k = 11%. Find the NPV.","answer":23024.06,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-(700000+150000+40000)+(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11+(120000+0.1*560000+150000)/1.11**4+700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","steps":[{"label":"Time-0 cash flow: equipment + land + NWC","value":-890000.0,"check":"-(700000+150000+40000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax operating flow per year","value":182000.0,"check":"(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NWC cash flow in year 1","value":-16000.0,"check":"-(0.1*560000-40000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of operating flows, years 1-4","value":564645.12,"check":"(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the year-1 NWC flow","value":-14414.41,"check":"-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of salvage + NWC recovery + land at year 4","value":214746.3,"check":"(120000+0.1*560000+150000)/1.11**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield (AII, with salvage term)","value":148047.06,"check":"700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":23024.06,"check":"-(700000+150000+40000)+(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11+(120000+0.1*560000+150000)/1.11**4+700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Sunk study = 0. Land enters at its after-tax value, -150,000 now and +150,000 back in year 4","CF0 = -700,000 - 150,000 - 40,000 = -890,000","Operating flow = (560,000 - 40,000 - 240,000)(1 - 0.35) = 182,000 a year","NWC level = 10% x 560,000 = 56,000, so year 1 investment = 56,000 - 40,000 = 16,000; NWC recovered at year 4 = 56,000","PVs: operating 564,645.12; year-1 NWC -14,414.41; year-4 (120,000 + 56,000 + 150,000) 214,746.30","PV CCA (AII) = 700,000 x 0.2 x 0.35/0.31 x (1 + 0.165)/1.11 - 120,000 x 0.2 x 0.35/0.31 / 1.11^4 = 148,047.06","NPV = -890,000 + 564,645.12 - 14,414.41 + 214,746.30 + 148,047.06 = $23,024.06"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -890000, C01 = 166000 (182000 - 16000), C02 = 182000, C03 = 182000, C04 = 508000, I = 11, NPV CPT","add PV of CCA computed separately"],"errors":[{"id":"counted-sunk","label":"Included the sunk study","value":11024.06,"check":"-(700000+150000+40000+12000)+(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11+(120000+0.1*560000+150000)/1.11**4+700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","explain":"The $12,000 study is already paid and has no incremental effect."},{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year factor although AII applies","value":7360.0,"check":"-(700000+150000+40000)+(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11+(120000+0.1*560000+150000)/1.11**4+700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","explain":"The question says AII, so the first-year factor is (1 + 1.5k)/(1 + k)."},{"id":"ignored-erosion","label":"Ignored the lost sales on the existing line","value":103687.64,"check":"-(700000+150000+40000)+(560000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11+(120000+0.1*560000+150000)/1.11**4+700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","explain":"Erosion is a real incremental cost: the lost after-tax cash flow reduces the project's value."},{"id":"land-excluded","label":"Left out the land opportunity cost and its return","value":74214.41,"check":"-(700000+40000)+(560000-40000-240000)*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.11**4)/0.11-(0.1*560000-40000)/1.11+(120000+0.1*560000)/1.11**4+700000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-120000*0.2*0.35/(0.11+0.2)/1.11**4","explain":"Land the firm already owns still has an opportunity cost: the after-tax proceeds it gives up."}],"explanation":"Everything the project causes is included: land, erosion, NWC and the shield; nothing already spent is.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s2-6, SM 10-16"},{"id":"fin201-q10-474","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okotoks Sheet Metal plans a $640,000 expansion (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule) that adds $215,000 a year to pre-tax operating income for 5 years. The equipment will be worthless at the end and there is no working capital. T = 32% and the discount rate is 13%. What is the NPV?","answer":8883.96,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-640000+215000*(1-0.32)*(1-1/1.13**5)/0.13+640000*0.3*0.32/(0.13+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.13)/(1+0.13)","steps":[{"label":"After-tax operating cash flow","value":146200.0,"check":"215000*(1-0.32)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of operating cash flows","value":514219.21,"check":"215000*(1-0.32)*(1-1/1.13**5)/0.13","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield (no salvage term)","value":134664.75,"check":"640000*0.3*0.32/(0.13+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.13)/(1+0.13)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":8883.96,"check":"-640000+215000*(1-0.32)*(1-1/1.13**5)/0.13+640000*0.3*0.32/(0.13+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.13)/(1+0.13)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["After-tax operating flow = 215,000 x 0.68 = 146,200","PV = 146,200 x 3.5172 = 514,219.21","PV CCA = 640,000 x 0.30 x 0.32/0.43 x 1.065/1.13 = 134,664.75 (the equipment is scrapped, so no salvage term)","NPV = -640,000 + 514,219.21 + 134,664.75 = $8,883.96"],"keystrokes":["N = 5, I/Y = 13, PMT = 146200, CPT PV; add PV of CCA; subtract 640000"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":-125780.79,"check":"-640000+215000*(1-0.32)*(1-1/1.13**5)/0.13","explain":"The shield on $640,000 of equipment is worth far more than the NPV margin."},{"id":"k-minus-d","label":"Used k - d in the CCA denominator","value":214841.8,"check":"-640000+215000*(1-0.32)*(1-1/1.13**5)/0.13+640000*0.3*0.32/(0.3-0.13)*(1+0.5*0.13)/(1+0.13)","explain":"CCA declines at rate d, so the denominator is k + d."},{"id":"taxed-capex","label":"Reduced the capital cost by (1 - T)","value":213683.96,"check":"-640000*(1-0.32)+215000*(1-0.32)*(1-1/1.13**5)/0.13+640000*0.3*0.32/(0.13+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.13)/(1+0.13)","explain":"Capital expenditure is counted at 100%; its tax benefit comes only through CCA."}],"explanation":"No salvage and no NWC: the NPV is CF0 plus the operating annuity plus the CCA shield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-7, S6 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-475","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okanagan Orchard Supply buys $390,000 of equipment (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule) that adds $130,000 a year to pre-tax operating income for 5 years. At the end of the project the equipment is not sold: it stays in a pool that holds other assets and keeps earning CCA. T = 30% and k = 10%. What is the NPV?","answer":29416.14,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-390000+130000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+390000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","steps":[{"label":"After-tax operating cash flow","value":91000.0,"check":"130000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of operating cash flows","value":344961.6,"check":"130000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield, no salvage term","value":74454.55,"check":"390000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":29416.14,"check":"-390000+130000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+390000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["After-tax operating flow = 130,000 x 0.70 = 91,000","PV = 91,000 x 3.7908 = 344,961.60","PV CCA = 390,000 x 0.2 x 0.3/0.3 x 1.05/1.10 = 74,454.55. No sale, no salvage term","NPV = -390,000 + 344,961.60 + 74,454.55 = $29,416.14"],"keystrokes":["N = 5, I/Y = 10, PMT = 91000, CPT PV = 344,961.60; add 74,454.55; subtract 390000"],"errors":[{"id":"kept-salvage-term","label":"Subtracted a salvage term as if the equipment were sold for its UCC after 5 years","value":11562.22,"check":"-390000+130000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+390000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-390000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**4*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","explain":"The salvage term only applies when a sale takes the asset out of the pool; here it stays in the pool and the shield runs on."},{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Applied no first-year factor","value":32961.6,"check":"-390000+130000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+390000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)","explain":"Year 1 CCA is limited to half the normal amount."},{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the AII factor instead of the half-year rule","value":36507.05,"check":"-390000+130000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+390000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","explain":"The question specifies the half-year rule."}],"explanation":"If the equipment is never sold out of the pool, the whole perpetual shield counts.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s16, S5 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-476","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-half-year-aii"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow Valley Fabricators buys $850,000 of equipment (CCA rate 30%, T = 35%, k = 10%) and will keep it in the pool indefinitely. By how much does the PV of the CCA tax shield, and so the project NPV, rise if the Accelerated Investment Incentive (1.5 x d in year 1) applies instead of the half-year rule?","answer":20284.09,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"850000*0.3*0.35/(0.1+0.3)*((1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","steps":[{"label":"Perpetuity term I0 d T/(k + d)","value":223125.0,"check":"850000*0.3*0.35/(0.1+0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA shield, half-year rule","value":212982.95,"check":"850000*0.3*0.35/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA shield, AII","value":233267.05,"check":"850000*0.3*0.35/(0.1+0.3)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Difference","value":20284.09,"check":"850000*0.3*0.35/(0.1+0.3)*((1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["I0 d T/(k + d) = 850,000 x 0.30 x 0.35/0.40 = 223,125","Half-year: 223,125 x 1.05/1.10 = 212,982.95; AII: 223,125 x 1.15/1.10 = 233,267.05","Difference = 223,125 x 0.10/1.10 = $20,284.09"],"keystrokes":["223125 x (1.15 - 1.05) / 1.1 = 20284.09"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Compared AII with a factor of 1","value":10142.05,"check":"850000*0.3*0.35/(0.1+0.3)*((1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-1)","explain":"The comparison is against the half-year rule, whose factor is (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), not 1."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Left out the tax rate","value":57954.55,"check":"850000*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*0.1/(1+0.1)","explain":"The shield is CCA x T; leaving T out overstates the gain by 1/0.35."}],"explanation":"Only the first-year factor changes: the NPV gain is I0 d T/(k + d) x k/(1 + k).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s8, s16"},{"id":"fin201-q10-477","concept":"fin201-c10-expansion-npv","also":["fin201-c10-side-effects-financing"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"The Prairie Brew canning line (NPV $68,071.15 before this adjustment: 4 years, T = 30%, k = 12%) will also erode $28,000 a year of pre-tax contribution from the company's bottling line for the same 4 years. What is the project's NPV after allowing for the erosion?","answer":8539.1,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"68071.15-28000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","steps":[{"label":"After-tax erosion per year","value":19600.0,"check":"28000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of erosion","value":59532.05,"check":"28000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Adjusted NPV","value":8539.1,"check":"68071.15-28000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Lost after-tax contribution = 28,000 x 0.70 = 19,600 a year","PV = 19,600 x 3.0373 = 59,532.05","Adjusted NPV = 68,071.15 - 59,532.05 = $8,539.10"],"keystrokes":["N = 4, I/Y = 12, PMT = 19600, CPT PV = 59,532.05; 68071.15 - 59532.05"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-erosion-tax","label":"Did not tax-effect the erosion","value":-16974.63,"check":"68071.15-28000*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","explain":"Lost revenue also lowers taxes, so only (1 - T) of it is lost."},{"id":"erosion-sign","label":"Added the erosion instead of subtracting it","value":127603.2,"check":"68071.15+28000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.12**4)/0.12","explain":"Erosion is a cost caused by the project."},{"id":"one-year-only","label":"Treated erosion as a one-year loss","value":48471.15,"check":"68071.15-28000*0.7","explain":"The loss lasts 4 years; discount all four annual amounts."}],"explanation":"Erosion = after-tax lost contribution each year, discounted like any other operating flow.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s3-5, TB 10.1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-481","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dunvegan Printing will buy a new press for $190,000 and sell its old press today for $45,000. What is the relevant time-0 outlay for the replacement analysis?","options":["$190,000, the full price of the new press","$235,000, the new press plus the old press","$145,000, the net acquisition cost","$45,000, the amount received for the old press"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In a replacement only the differences count: the firm pays 190,000 and receives 45,000, so the net acquisition cost is 145,000. This net figure is also the amount that goes into the PV of CCA tax shield.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s7-8, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-482","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow River Dairy could sell its old pasteurizer for $8,000 in five years if it keeps it. If it replaces the machine today, the new unit will be worth $20,000 in five years. How is the salvage handled in the replacement NPV?","options":["Net salvage of $12,000 (20,000 - 8,000) at year 5, because the old machine's future sale is given up","Salvage of $20,000 at year 5, because the old machine is already sold","Salvage of $28,000 at year 5, the sum of both machines","No salvage, because salvage values are not incremental"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Replacing now means we do not sell the old machine in year 5, so its $8,000 is forgone. The incremental salvage is the new machine's value minus the old machine's: 20,000 - 8,000 = 12,000.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s16-19, SM 10-36"},{"id":"fin201-q10-483","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a replacement project the net acquisition cost equals the cost of the new machine minus the _____ of the old machine today.","options":["UCC","resale value","original cost","salvage value in five years"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cash the firm gets by selling the old machine now reduces the net outlay. The old machine's UCC and original cost are accounting history; they only matter through tax, and in an open pool the sale has no immediate tax effect.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-484","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts net acquisition cost with net salvage value in a replacement NPV?","options":["Net acquisition cost is taxed at (1 − T); net salvage is taxed at T","Net acquisition cost uses the old machine's UCC; net salvage uses its ACB","Both occur at time 0 but net salvage is discounted for the old machine's remaining life","Net acquisition is a time-0 flow (new cost − old resale now); net salvage is a time-N flow (new − old salvage)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each is a difference between new and old, but they occur at different dates: the acquisition difference now and the salvage difference at the end of the horizon. Both then feed the PV of CCA tax shield formula.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8-10, s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-485","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":["fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity"],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a relevant cash flow when deciding whether to replace an old machine?","options":["The price paid for the old machine five years ago","The resale value of the old machine today","The after-tax cost savings from the new machine","The salvage value the old machine would fetch if kept"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The old machine's original price is a sunk cost; nothing the firm decides now can change it. The resale value today, the extra savings and the forgone future salvage are all caused by the replacement decision.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s7, SM 10-1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-486","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the old machine's future salvage subtracted from the new machine's salvage in the replacement NPV?","options":["Old equipment is always worth less than new equipment","Salvage is a tax expense that offsets the CCA shield","Selling the old machine now gives up its future salvage","Net salvage is always negative in a replacement"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Incremental analysis compares the world with replacement to the world without. Without replacement the firm sells the old machine at N; with replacement it sells the new one. The difference S_new - S_old is the incremental cash flow.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s18-19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-487","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A replacement project has NPV = +$47,867 at the firm's required return. What does this mean?","options":["The new machine will earn $47,867 of accounting profit in its first year","The old machine is worth $47,867 more than the new one","Replacing the old machine now adds $47,867 of value in today's dollars compared with keeping it","The firm saves exactly $47,867 a year over the life of the new machine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replacement NPV is the PV of the incremental flows. A positive value means the savings, salvage differences and CCA shield outweigh the net outlay, so replace.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-488","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":["fin201-c10-net-acquisitions","fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Ridge Moulding replaces a machine with UCC of $63,000 by a new one in the same CCA class. The old machine is sold for $60,000, and other assets stay in the class. What tax effect does the $3,000 shortfall have at the sale?","options":["None at the sale: the pool stays open and the net acquisition enters PVCCA","A $3,000 terminal loss, which saves 3,000 × T of tax right now","A $3,000 capital loss, only half of which is deductible","A $3,000 recapture of CCA, taxed at the full corporate rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because the class stays open, the sale just reduces the pool's UCC by the proceeds. The seminar's treatment uses the net acquisition cost in the CCA formula and ignores any immediate recapture or terminal loss.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s8-10, SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-489","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca","fin201-c10-net-acquisitions"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a replacement NPV, which investment amount multiplies d x T/(k + d) in the PV of CCA tax shield?","options":["The full purchase price of the new machine","The old machine's UCC","The new machine's cost plus the old machine's resale value","The net acquisition cost: new cost minus old resale today"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The sale of the old machine reduces the pool, so only the net addition to the pool generates new shields. The seminar uses 220,000 = 280,000 - 60,000 in its worked example.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-490","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new machine will raise annual sales by $85,000 compared with the old machine. How is this revenue treated in the replacement NPV?","options":["The full revenue of the new machine is counted, after tax","Only the increase of $85,000 is counted, after tax at (1 - T)","It is counted before tax, because replacement revenue is not taxed","It is ignored, because the old machine already produced the base revenue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Replacement is evaluated incrementally. The relevant revenue is the difference between what the firm earns with the new machine and what it would earn with the old one, and that difference is taxed like any revenue.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s7-9"},{"id":"fin201-q10-491","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Moulding will replace a machine. New cost $120,000; old machine sells for $40,000 now and for $6,000 in 5 years; the new one sells for $15,000 in 5 years. The new machine saves $41,000 a year before tax for 5 years. CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, pool open. T = 35%, k = 10%. What is the NPV of replacing?","options":["$14,892.34","$26,612.76","$43,127.01","$45,983.24","$51,166.81"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Net acquisition = 80,000; net salvage = 9,000; after-tax savings = 26,650 a year (PV 101,024.47); PV of net salvage = 5,588.29; PV of CCA = 16,514.25. NPV = -80,000 + 101,024.47 + 5,588.29 + 16,514.25 = $43,127.01. $14,892.34 ignores the old machine everywhere (gross cost and salvage); $51,166.81 computes the CCA shield on the gross $120,000 and $15,000; $45,983.24 uses the new machine's $15,000 salvage instead of the $9,000 net salvage; $26,612.76 leaves out the CCA shield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-36, S6 s16-19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-492","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Fabricators replaces a machine in a class with CCA rate 25%. New cost $260,000; old resale today $55,000; salvage in 4 years $20,000 (new) and $9,000 (old). AII applies, pool open, T = 40%, k = 10%. What is the present value of the CCA tax shield on the replacement?","options":["$53,762.48","$59,087.15","$61,233.77","$63,380.38","$73,759.40"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Use the net acquisition 205,000 and net salvage 11,000: 205,000 x 0.25 x 0.40/0.35 x (1 + 0.15)/1.10 = 61,233.77; minus 11,000 x 0.25 x 0.40/0.35 / 1.10^4 = 2,146.61. PV = $59,087.15. Using gross figures (260,000 and 20,000), leaving out the salvage term, using the half-year rule or flipping the sign of the salvage term each gives a different value.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-493","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nechako Sawmills will replace an old planer. The new planer costs $185,000 and sells for $22,000 in 5 years; the old planer sells for $55,000 now and $8,000 in 5 years. The new planer saves $52,000 a year before tax for 5 years. CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, pool open. T = 38%, k = 12%. What is the NPV of replacing?","answer":25401.15,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-130000+52000*(1-0.38)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+14000/1.12**5+130000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-14000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)/1.12**5","steps":[{"label":"Net acquisition cost","value":130000.0,"check":"185000-55000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Net salvage value at year 5","value":14000.0,"check":"22000-8000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax savings per year","value":32240.0,"check":"52000*(1-0.38)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of savings","value":116217.98,"check":"52000*(1-0.38)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of net salvage","value":7943.98,"check":"14000/1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":31239.19,"check":"130000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-14000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)/1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":25401.15,"check":"-130000+52000*(1-0.38)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+14000/1.12**5+130000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-14000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)/1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Net acquisition = 185,000 - 55,000 = 130,000; net salvage = 22,000 - 8,000 = 14,000","After-tax savings = 52,000 x 0.62 = 32,240; PV = 32,240 x 3.6048 = 116,217.98","PV of net salvage = 14,000/1.12^5 = 7,943.98","PV CCA = 130,000 x 0.3 x 0.38/0.42 x 1.06/1.12 - 14,000 x 0.3 x 0.38/0.42 / 1.12^5 = 31,239.19","NPV = -130,000 + 116,217.98 + 7,943.98 + 31,239.19 = $25,401.15"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -130000, C01 = 32240 (F01 = 4), C02 = 46240 (32240 + 14000), I = 12, NPV CPT, then add PV of CCA"],"errors":[{"id":"net-acquisition-ignored","label":"Used the gross cost of $185,000 and the new salvage only","value":-12162.74,"check":"-185000+52000*(1-0.38)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+22000/1.12**5+185000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-22000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)/1.12**5","explain":"Replacement is incremental: use new minus old for both the outlay and the salvage."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the PV of the CCA tax shield","value":-5838.04,"check":"-130000+52000*(1-0.38)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+14000/1.12**5","explain":"The new equipment's CCA shield is a large part of the value."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Took the savings before tax","value":96631.52,"check":"-130000+52000*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+14000/1.12**5+130000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-14000*0.3*0.38/(0.12+0.3)/1.12**5","explain":"Cost savings raise taxable income, so only (1 - T) of them is kept."}],"explanation":"Net acquisition, net salvage, after-tax savings, and the CCA shield on the net amounts.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-37, S6 s16-19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-494","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Pallet replaces an old stacker (CCA rate 25%, half-year rule, pool open). The new stacker costs $150,000; the old sells for $40,000 now. In 3 years the new sells for $20,000 and the old would sell for $5,000. The new stacker adds sales of $70,000, $80,000 and $60,000 (no extra costs). NWC is $6,000 now, then 10% of each year's sales, all recovered in year 3. T = 40%, k = 11%. Find the NPV.","answer":28218.39,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(-110000-6000)+(70000*0.6-1000)/1.11+(80000*0.6-1000)/1.11**2+(60000*0.6+8000+15000)/1.11**3+110000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-15000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)/1.11**3","steps":[{"label":"Net acquisition cost","value":110000.0,"check":"150000-40000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Net salvage at year 3","value":15000.0,"check":"20000-5000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NWC cash flow at time 0","value":-6000.0,"check":"-6000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Total cash flow in year 1","value":41000.0,"check":"70000*0.6-1000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Total cash flow in year 3","value":59000.0,"check":"60000*0.6+8000+15000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of cash flows excluding CCA","value":2223.48,"check":"(-110000-6000)+(70000*0.6-1000)/1.11+(80000*0.6-1000)/1.11**2+(60000*0.6+8000+15000)/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":25994.91,"check":"110000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-15000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":28218.39,"check":"(-110000-6000)+(70000*0.6-1000)/1.11+(80000*0.6-1000)/1.11**2+(60000*0.6+8000+15000)/1.11**3+110000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-15000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Net acquisition = 110,000; net salvage = 15,000; after-tax sales = 42,000, 48,000, 36,000","NWC levels: 6,000 now; 7,000; 8,000; 6,000. Flows: -6,000 (t0), -1,000 (t1), -1,000 (t2), +8,000 (t3: -(6,000 - 8,000) + 6,000 = +8,000)","Total flows: t0 = -116,000; t1 = 41,000; t2 = 47,000; t3 = 36,000 + 8,000 + 15,000 = 59,000","PV excluding CCA = 2,223.48","PV CCA = 110,000 x 0.25 x 0.4/0.36 x 1.055/1.11 - 15,000 x 0.25 x 0.4/0.36 / 1.11^3 = 25,994.91","NPV = $28,218.39"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -116000, C01 = 41000, C02 = 47000, C03 = 59000, I = 11, NPV CPT = 2223.48, then add PV of CCA 25994.91"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Did not recover the NWC in year 3","value":23831.25,"check":"(-110000-6000)+(70000*0.6-1000)/1.11+(80000*0.6-1000)/1.11**2+(60000*0.6+8000+15000)/1.11**3-6000/1.11**3+110000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-15000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)/1.11**3","explain":"NWC tied up in the project is released at the end."},{"id":"net-acquisition-ignored","label":"Used the full cost of $150,000 and ignored the old machine","value":1419.37,"check":"-(150000+6000)+(70000*0.6-1000)/1.11+(80000*0.6-1000)/1.11**2+(60000*0.6+8000+20000)/1.11**3+150000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.25*0.4/(0.11+0.25)/1.11**3","explain":"A replacement uses the net amounts: sell the old machine and count only the difference."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":2223.48,"check":"(-110000-6000)+(70000*0.6-1000)/1.11+(80000*0.6-1000)/1.11**2+(60000*0.6+8000+15000)/1.11**3","explain":"The shield on the net acquisition is worth about $26,000 here."}],"explanation":"Dickerson-style layout: net acquisition, net salvage, incremental sales after tax, incremental NWC, then the PV of CCA.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s7-10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-495","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Wetaskiwin Grain buys a $175,000 dryer (new salvage $0 in 5 years) to replace an old dryer that sells for $60,000 now or $12,000 in 5 years. The new dryer saves $48,000 a year before tax for 5 years. CCA rate 20%, AII applies, pool open. T = 40% and k = 10%. What is the NPV of replacing?","answer":20771.16,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-115000+48000*(1-0.4)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(-12000)/1.1**5+115000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(-12000)*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","steps":[{"label":"Net acquisition cost","value":115000.0,"check":"175000-60000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Net salvage (negative)","value":-12000.0,"check":"0-12000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of savings","value":109174.66,"check":"48000*(1-0.4)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of net salvage","value":-7451.06,"check":"(-12000)/1.1**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield (the salvage term adds)","value":34047.55,"check":"115000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(-12000)*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":20771.16,"check":"-115000+48000*(1-0.4)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(-12000)/1.1**5+115000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(-12000)*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Net acquisition = 115,000; net salvage = 0 - 12,000 = -12,000 (the old dryer's future sale is given up)","PV of savings = 28,800 x 3.7908 = 109,174.66; PV of net salvage = -12,000/1.1^5 = -7,451.06","PV CCA = 115,000 x 0.2 x 0.4/0.3 x 1.15/1.10 - (-12,000) x 0.2 x 0.4/0.3 / 1.1^5 = 32,060.61 + 1,986.95 = 34,047.55","NPV = -115,000 + 109,174.66 - 7,451.06 + 34,047.55 = $20,771.16"],"keystrokes":["N = 5, I/Y = 10, PMT = 28800, CPT PV; FV = -12000, CPT PV; add PV of CCA; subtract 115000"],"errors":[{"id":"salvage-sign","label":"Treated the forgone old salvage as an inflow","value":31699.37,"check":"-115000+48000*(1-0.4)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+12000/1.1**5+115000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-12000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","explain":"The old dryer's future sale is lost, so net salvage is negative."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Dropped the salvage term from PV_CCA","value":18784.21,"check":"-115000+48000*(1-0.4)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(-12000)/1.1**5+115000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)*(1+1.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","explain":"A negative net salvage still enters the CCA term; here it raises the PV of the shield."},{"id":"wrong-first-year-factor","label":"Used the half-year rule instead of AII","value":17983.28,"check":"-115000+48000*(1-0.4)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(-12000)/1.1**5+115000*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(-12000)*0.2*0.4/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","explain":"The question states AII."}],"explanation":"Negative net salvage: the old machine's future sale is given up. It lowers the cash flow but raises the PV of the CCA shield.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s16-19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-496","concept":"fin201-c10-replacement-npv","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lakeshore Cold Storage is considering a $210,000 compressor (salvage $30,000 in 5 years) to replace one that sells for $50,000 now or $10,000 in 5 years. The new unit saves $38,000 a year before tax for 5 years; CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, pool open, T = 30%, k = 10%. At $50,000 the NPV is -$18,684.88. What is the lowest resale price for the old compressor that makes replacing worthwhile (NPV = 0)?","answer":73093.67,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(-(-210000+38000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(30000-10000)/1.1**5+210000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(30000-10000)*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/(1-0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","steps":[{"label":"Value of each extra $1 of resale: 1 minus the CCA shield per dollar","value":0.8091,"check":"1-0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"NPV if the old machine fetched $0","value":-59139.42,"check":"-210000+38000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(30000-10000)/1.1**5+210000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(30000-10000)*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Break-even resale price","value":73093.67,"check":"(-(-210000+38000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(30000-10000)/1.1**5+210000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(30000-10000)*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/(1-0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Resale R lowers the net acquisition cost one-for-one but also shrinks the CCA shield, so NPV(R) = NPV(0) + R x (1 - 0.2 x 0.3/0.3 x 1.05/1.1)","1 - 0.190909 = 0.809091","NPV(0) = -59,139.42","R* = 59,139.42 / 0.809091 = $73,093.67","Check: base NPV at R = 50,000 is -18,684.88, matching A + 50,000 x 0.8091"],"keystrokes":["Compute NPV with old resale = 0, then divide its absolute value by 1 - 0.190909"],"errors":[{"id":"net-acquisition-ignored","label":"Ignored the effect on the CCA shield","value":59139.42,"check":"-(-210000+38000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+(30000-10000)/1.1**5+210000*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-(30000-10000)*0.2*0.3/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5)","explain":"Each dollar of resale also removes shield on that dollar, so it adds only about 81 cents of value."},{"id":"resale-not-scaled","label":"Added the base shortfall to $50,000 without scaling","value":68684.88,"check":"50000+18684.88","explain":"The gap must be divided by 0.809 because resale value reduces the shield."}],"explanation":"NPV is linear in the old machine's resale price; solve it for zero.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-36, S6 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-501","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fraser Bakery is considering a new oven that earns no extra revenue but cuts energy and labour costs by $36,000 a year before tax. What kind of project is this?","options":["An expansion project, whose benefit is new revenue","A replacement project with no relevant tax effect","A bid-price problem, whose benefit is the contract price","A cost-cutting proposal, whose benefit is the after-tax cost saving"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A cost-cutting project has no new sales. Its operating benefit is the saving in costs, which raises taxable income, so the cash flow each year is saving x (1 - T), plus the CCA shield from the new equipment.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6, SM 10-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-502","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the after-tax cash flows of a cost-cutting project differ from those of an expansion project?","options":["Cost savings are not taxed because no cash is received","The benefit is saving × (1 − T) instead of (revenue − costs)(1 − T); the rest is the same","Cost-cutting projects are discounted at a lower rate because they are safer","Cost-cutting projects have no CCA tax shield because they add no revenue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A dollar saved in costs raises pre-tax income by a dollar just like a dollar of new revenue, so it is taxed at T. The equipment still qualifies for CCA, so the shield stays.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6, SM 10-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-503","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT part of the NPV of a cost-cutting investment?","options":["The after-tax annual saving","The extra inventory (NWC) the project requires, recovered at the end","Revenue from additional sales","The PV of the CCA tax shield on the new equipment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"By definition a cost-cutting project does not change sales. Its benefits are lower costs, plus the usual items: CAPEX, NWC, salvage and the CCA shield.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-34"},{"id":"fin201-q10-504","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is a $50,000 annual cost saving multiplied by (1 - T) when evaluating the project?","options":["Lower costs raise taxable income, so the firm pays tax on the saving and keeps only (1 - T) of it","Because the saving is a non-cash item that only appears on the income statement","Because cost savings are a capital expenditure","Because the tax rate on savings is always the capital gains rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Expenses are deductible, so reducing them leaves less to deduct. The extra taxable income costs T per dollar saved, and the net benefit is saving x (1 - T).","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s13, SM 10-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-505","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A cost-cutting analysis finds that the required pre-tax saving for NPV = 0 is $110,211 a year, and engineers forecast savings of $95,000. What should the firm conclude?","options":["Accept: the forecast saving is large enough to cover the cost of the equipment","Reject: forecast savings are below the break-even level, so the NPV is negative","Accept: any positive saving makes the NPV positive","The project breaks even because savings are 86% of the requirement"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The required saving is the level at which the NPV is exactly zero. Forecasts below it give a negative NPV; forecasts above it give a positive NPV. The 86% is irrelevant because NPV is linear in the saving, not proportional.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-23, 10-48"},{"id":"fin201-q10-506","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"To find the pre-tax annual saving that makes a cost-cutting project break even, which sequence is correct?","options":["Divide the equipment cost by the number of years, then multiply by (1 - T)","Divide the equipment cost by the annuity factor, then multiply by (1 - T)","Set NPV = 0, solve for the PV of savings, divide by N, then divide by T","Set NPV = 0, solve for the PV of after-tax savings, divide by the annuity factor, then divide by (1 - T)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With CF0, salvage and the CCA shield known, the PV of the after-tax savings must make up the rest. Dividing by the annuity factor gives the after-tax annual saving; dividing by (1 - T) un-taxes it.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-23, A19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-507","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a cost-cutting NPV, the CCA tax shield is valued only on the cost of the new equipment, not on the cost savings.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The shield arises from deductions for depreciating the asset: CCA x T. Cost savings are an operating item taxed at (1 - T) and carry no shield of their own.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s14-16"},{"id":"fin201-q10-508","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":["fin201-c10-nwc"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A cost-cutting machine requires a $15,000 increase in inventory that is released when the machine is retired. How is this treated?","options":["As a $15,000 outflow at time 0 and a $15,000 inflow at the end, neither taxed","As a tax-deductible expense in year 1","As a $15,000 reduction of the CCA base","As an outflow at time 0 only, because inventory is sold during the project"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NWC is counted at 100% when it is invested and again when it is recovered. It does not enter the CCA calculation, and it is not taxed.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-34, L05B s12"},{"id":"fin201-q10-509","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okotoks Bottling is buying a $120,000 filler (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule) that saves $38,000 a year before tax for 4 years. It needs $8,000 more inventory, recovered at the end, and sells for $20,000 in year 4 (pool open). T = 35%, k = 9%. What is the NPV?","options":["-$9,460.71","-$3,793.30","-$2,597.48","-$373.32","$39,294.97"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CF0 = -128,000. After-tax saving = 38,000 x 0.65 = 24,700 (PV 80,021.08). Year-4 salvage + NWC = 28,000 (PV 19,835.91). PV CCA = 24,349.71. NPV = -$3,793.30, so reject. Leaving savings untaxed makes the NPV look positive.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-34, 10-21"},{"id":"fin201-q10-510","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Saskatoon Metalworks can buy a $250,000 machine (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, salvage $35,000 at year 4, pool open) that cuts costs for 4 years. T = 30% and k = 12%. What annual pre-tax saving makes the NPV exactly zero?","options":["$59,861.97","$64,936.33","$83,275.31","$85,517.10","$107,122.01"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Known PV to cover = 250,000 - 35,000/1.12^4 - PV CCA (45,935.15) = 181,821.72. Annuity factor (12%, 4) = 3.0373, so the after-tax saving is 59,861.97; un-taxing by 0.70 gives $85,517.10.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-23, 10-48"},{"id":"fin201-q10-511","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Grain Handling can install a $310,000 sorting system (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule) that saves $95,000 a year before tax for 5 years. It needs $15,000 more inventory (recovered at the end) and sells for $40,000 in year 5; the pool stays open. T = 30%, k = 11%. What is the NPV?","answer":12883.18,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-(310000+15000)+95000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.11**5)/0.11+(40000+15000)/1.11**5+310000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-40000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/1.11**5","steps":[{"label":"Time-0 cash flow: CAPEX + inventory","value":-325000.0,"check":"-(310000+15000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax saving per year","value":66500.0,"check":"95000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of after-tax savings","value":245777.15,"check":"95000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.11**5)/0.11","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of salvage + inventory recovery","value":32639.82,"check":"(40000+15000)/1.11**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":59466.2,"check":"310000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-40000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/1.11**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV","value":12883.18,"check":"-(310000+15000)+95000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.11**5)/0.11+(40000+15000)/1.11**5+310000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-40000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/1.11**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CF0 = -310,000 - 15,000 = -325,000. After-tax saving = 95,000 x 0.70 = 66,500","PV of savings = 66,500 x 3.6959 = 245,777.15","Year 5: 40,000 + 15,000 = 55,000; PV = 32,639.82","PV CCA = 310,000 x 0.3 x 0.3/0.41 x 1.055/1.11 - 40,000 x 0.3 x 0.3/0.41 / 1.11^5 = 59,466.20","NPV = -325,000 + 245,777.15 + 32,639.82 + 59,466.20 = $12,883.18"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = -325000, C01 = 66500 (F01 = 4), C02 = 121500 (66500 + 55000), I = 11, NPV CPT, then add PV of CCA"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Used the untaxed savings","value":118216.24,"check":"-(310000+15000)+95000*(1-1/1.11**5)/0.11+(40000+15000)/1.11**5+310000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-40000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/1.11**5","explain":"Cost savings raise taxable income, so only (1 - T) is kept."},{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Did not recover the inventory","value":3981.41,"check":"-(310000+15000)+95000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.11**5)/0.11+40000/1.11**5+310000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-40000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/1.11**5","explain":"Inventory is released when the system is retired."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Dropped the salvage term","value":18093.97,"check":"-(310000+15000)+95000*(1-0.3)*(1-1/1.11**5)/0.11+(40000+15000)/1.11**5+310000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)","explain":"The system is sold at year 5, which removes the shield attached to the salvage value."}],"explanation":"No new sales: the operating benefit is the taxed saving; everything else is as in an expansion.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-34, 10-21, 10-16"},{"id":"fin201-q10-512","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kootenay Packaging is considering a $380,000 packing line (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule) that sells for $50,000 at the end of year 5 (pool open). It will cut costs for 5 years; there is no working capital. T = 35% and k = 10%. What constant annual pre-tax cost saving makes the NPV zero?","answer":110211.2,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(380000-50000/1.1**5-(380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/((1-1/1.1**5)/0.1)/(1-0.35)","steps":[{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":77392.28,"check":"380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV the after-tax savings must cover = CAPEX - PV salvage - PV CCA","value":271561.65,"check":"380000-50000/1.1**5-(380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Annuity factor (10%, 5 years)","value":3.7908,"check":"(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Required after-tax saving","value":71637.28,"check":"(380000-50000/1.1**5-(380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/((1-1/1.1**5)/0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required pre-tax saving","value":110211.2,"check":"(380000-50000/1.1**5-(380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/((1-1/1.1**5)/0.1)/(1-0.35)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV CCA = 380,000 x 0.2 x 0.35/0.3 x 1.05/1.10 - 50,000 x 0.2 x 0.35/0.3 / 1.1^5 = 77,392.28","NPV = 0: the after-tax savings annuity must be worth 380,000 - 50,000/1.1^5 - 77,392.28 = 271,561.65","After-tax saving = 271,561.65 / 3.7908 = 71,637.28","Pre-tax saving = 71,637.28 / 0.65 = $110,211.20"],"keystrokes":["Compute the three known PVs, then divide by 3.7908 and by 0.65"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide by (1 - T)","value":71637.28,"check":"(380000-50000/1.1**5-(380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/((1-1/1.1**5)/0.1)","explain":"The annuity found is after tax. The pre-tax saving is larger by 1/(1 - T)."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided by N instead of the annuity factor","value":83557.43,"check":"(380000-50000/1.1**5-(380000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-50000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5))/5/(1-0.35)","explain":"A level annuity has to be discounted: use the annuity factor, not N."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA shield","value":141620.26,"check":"(380000-50000/1.1**5)/((1-1/1.1**5)/0.1)/(1-0.35)","explain":"The shield covers a large share of the cost, so the required saving falls a lot when it is counted."}],"explanation":"Break-even savings: PV of the known pieces, divide by the annuity factor, divide by (1 - T).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-23, 10-48, A19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-513","concept":"fin201-c10-cost-cutting","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A cost-cutting machine saves $70,000 a year before tax for 5 years and sells for $25,000 at the end of year 5 (pool open). CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, T = 35%, k = 10%. There is no working capital. What is the most the firm should pay for the machine, i.e. the cost at which NPV = 0?","answer":237216.34,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(70000*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+25000/1.1**5-25000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5)/(1-0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","steps":[{"label":"Benefits that do not depend on cost (savings + salvage - salvage term)","value":184381.79,"check":"70000*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+25000/1.1**5-25000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Shield per dollar of cost: d T/(k + d) x first-year factor","value":0.2227,"check":"0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Maximum cost = benefits / (1 - shield per dollar)","value":237216.34,"check":"(70000*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+25000/1.1**5-25000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5)/(1-0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV savings = 45,500 x 3.7908 = 172,480.80; PV salvage = 15,523.03; salvage term = 3,622.04","Benefits independent of cost = 184,381.79","NPV = -I0 + 184,381.79 + I0 x 0.22273 = 0, so I0 = 184,381.79 / 0.77727 = $237,216.34"],"keystrokes":["Compute the constant, then divide by 1 - 0.22273"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Ignored the CCA shield on the machine","value":184381.79,"check":"70000*(1-0.35)*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+25000/1.1**5-25000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5","explain":"Each dollar of cost generates about 22 cents of tax shield, so the machine can cost more than the other benefits."},{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Used savings before tax","value":356703.71,"check":"(70000*(1-1/1.1**5)/0.1+25000/1.1**5-25000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)/1.1**5)/(1-0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))","explain":"Savings are taxed at T."}],"explanation":"NPV is linear in the cost, so the break-even cost can be solved directly.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-24, 10-48"},{"id":"fin201-q10-521","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Foothills Machining must submit a fixed price per unit for a five-year supply contract. It wants the lowest price that still earns its required return on the investment. What is this problem called, and what is the benchmark?","options":["A bid-price problem: choose the price that makes accounting profit equal to zero","An equivalent annual cost problem: choose the price that equals the EAC","A bid-price problem: choose the price that makes the project's NPV equal to zero","A sensitivity analysis: choose the price that is 10% above cost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At the bid price the project earns exactly the required return, so NPV = 0. Any lower price loses value (the winner's curse); any higher price risks losing the contract.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6, SM 10-24"},{"id":"fin201-q10-522","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a bid-price calculation, the minimum acceptable price is the one that sets the project's NPV equal to _____.","options":["the CCA tax shield","the initial investment","zero","the profit margin"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NPV = 0 means the project earns exactly the discount rate: it covers all costs and pays investors their required return. That is the lowest price the firm can charge without destroying value.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-523","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the bid price based on NPV = 0 differ from the price at which accounting profit is zero?","options":["The NPV price also covers the investors' required return on the capital tied up, so it is higher","The accounting price is higher because it includes depreciation","They are always identical when the tax rate is zero","The NPV price ignores the cost of equipment because it is already spent"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Accounting break-even only covers expenses including depreciation; it ignores the time value of money. NPV break-even makes the PV of after-tax flows equal the investment, which requires a larger annual net revenue.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6, L05C"},{"id":"fin201-q10-524","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a step in finding a bid price?","options":["Set NPV = 0 and compute the PV of the known flows (CAPEX, NWC, salvage, CCA shield)","Divide the remaining PV by the annuity factor to get the required after-tax annual amount","Divide by (1 - T) and add the cash costs","Divide the required after-tax annuity by the number of years N"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A level annual amount that has a given present value is found by dividing by the annuity factor, not by N, because the payments occur in the future. The other three steps are the standard sequence.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-24, A19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-525","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must the required after-tax annual amount be divided by (1 - T) when finding the bid price?","options":["The tax rate applies only to the first year","Net revenue is taxed, so the pre-tax amount must exceed the after-tax need","(1 − T) converts a nominal flow into a real flow","The CCA shield must be added back first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The after-tax annuity equals [(P - v)Q - FC](1 - T). To recover the pre-tax figure we undo the tax by dividing by (1 - T), then add the cash costs to get total revenue.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-24"},{"id":"fin201-q10-526","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okotoks Steel bids exactly the NPV = 0 price and wins the contract. What does that imply about the project?","options":["It earns a 0% return, since its NPV is zero","It creates a positive NPV equal to the CCA tax shield","It breaks even on accounting profit but not on cash flow","It earns exactly its required return (IRR = k): no value is added or lost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"At the break-even price the PV of the inflows equals the PV of the outflows at the required rate, so the IRR equals the required return. The firm is indifferent; to add value it would need a price above that level.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-527","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the bid-price equation, after-tax net revenue = [(P - v)Q - FC](1 - T). Which item is NOT inside the bracket?","options":["Variable cost per unit v (a cash cost)","CCA (a non-cash deduction)","Fixed cash costs FC (a cash cost)","Price per unit P (cash revenue)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bracket contains cash revenue and cash costs only. CCA is not a cash cost; its tax saving is counted once in the PV of CCA tax shield, so including it in the bracket would double count.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-33, 10-52"},{"id":"fin201-q10-528","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"If the required return rises and nothing else changes, the bid price per unit rises.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A higher k lowers the PV of salvage and tax shields and raises the annuity needed to repay the investment at that rate; the required after-tax net revenue grows and so does the price.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-529","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":["fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"To submit a bid, a firm must put land it owns at the project's disposal, with an after-tax market value of $300,000 now and $320,000 in five years. Which cash flows go into the bid calculation?","options":["Nothing, because the firm already owns the land","-$300,000 at time 0 only","-$300,000 at time 0 and +$320,000 at year 5","The land's original purchase price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Land has an opportunity cost: the project gives up the after-tax sale proceeds today, and the land comes back at its after-tax value at the end. Both flows are in the NPV = 0 equation.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-43"},{"id":"fin201-q10-530","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bow Valley Plastics will bid on a 4-year contract for 25,000 units a year. Equipment costs $620,000 (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, sold for $70,000 at year 4, pool open). NWC of $60,000 is recovered at the end. Variable cost is $18.40 a unit, fixed cash costs $150,000 a year, T = 30%, k = 14%. What is the lowest price per unit that gives NPV = 0?","options":["$28.06","$31.16","$31.44","$34.06","$36.23"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Known PV = -680,000 + 110,553.59 (CCA) + 76,970.44 (salvage + NWC) = -492,475.98. After-tax annuity = 492,475.98/2.9137 = 169,020.11; pre-tax = 241,457.30; revenue = 241,457.30 + 150,000 + 18.40 x 25,000 = 851,457.30; price = $34.06.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-33, 10-24"},{"id":"fin201-q10-531","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Wetaskiwin Data Services must set annual contract revenue for 3 years. Computer equipment costs $275,000 (CCA rate 45%, AII applies, worthless at the end, pool open) and $20,000 of NWC is recovered at the end. Annual cash costs are $190,000, T = 28%, k = 15%. What annual revenue gives NPV = 0?","options":["$134,029.08","$286,500.94","$292,006.19","$324,029.08","$328,611.18"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Known PV = -295,000 + PV CCA (AII) 61,516.30 + NWC 13,150.32 = -220,333.37. After-tax annuity = 220,333.37/2.2832 = 96,500.94; pre-tax = 134,029.08; revenue = pre-tax net revenue + 190,000 = $324,029.08.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-24, 10-50"},{"id":"fin201-q10-532","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nechako Components bids on a 5-year contract for 30,000 units a year. Equipment costs $540,000 (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, sold for $60,000 in year 5, pool open); NWC of $45,000 is recovered at the end. Variable cost is $22.60 a unit and fixed cash costs are $210,000 a year. T = 35%, k = 12%. What is the lowest price per unit that gives NPV = 0?","answer":35.59,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","steps":[{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":104349.4,"check":"540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of everything except operations","value":-421070.78,"check":"(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required after-tax net revenue per year","value":116809.13,"check":"((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required pre-tax net revenue = after-tax / (1 - T)","value":179706.36,"check":"((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required annual revenue = pre-tax + FC + v x Q","value":1067706.36,"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price per unit","value":35.59,"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV CCA = 540,000 x 0.2 x 0.35/0.32 x 1.06/1.12 - 60,000 x 0.2 x 0.35/0.32 / 1.12^5 = 104,349.40","Known PV = -585,000 + 104,349.40 + 59,579.82 = -421,070.78","After-tax annuity = 421,070.78 / 3.6048 = 116,809.13","Pre-tax net revenue = 116,809.13 / 0.65 = 179,706.36","Revenue = 179,706.36 + 210,000 + 22.60 x 30,000 = 1,067,706.36","Price = 1,067,706.36 / 30,000 = $35.59 per unit"],"keystrokes":["Compute the PV of the known flows, divide by 3.6048, divide by 0.65, add costs, divide by 30000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide by (1 - T)","value":33.49,"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","explain":"The annuity found is after tax. Revenue minus costs must be larger by 1/(1 - T)."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided the PV by N instead of the annuity factor","value":33.92,"check":"(-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5)/5/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","explain":"Use the annuity factor, not N."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA shield","value":37.07,"check":"((540000+45000-(60000+45000)/1.12**5)/((1-1/1.12**5)/0.12)/0.65+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","explain":"The shield lowers the net cost of the equipment and so lowers the price needed."}],"explanation":"NPV = 0: PV of known pieces, then the required annuity, then un-tax, add costs and divide by quantity.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-33, A19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-533","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Switchgear bids on a 3-year order of 12,000 units a year. Equipment costs $350,000 (CCA rate 25%, half-year rule, sold for $40,000 at year 3, pool open); $30,000 of NWC is recovered at the end. Variable cost is $31.50 a unit, fixed cash costs $95,000 a year, T = 32%, k = 10%. Find the lowest price per unit that makes NPV = 0.","answer":52.13,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(((0)-(-(350000+30000+0)+(350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3)+(40000+30000+0)/(1+0.1)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.1)**3)/0.1)/(1-0.32)+95000+31.5*12000)/12000","steps":[{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":69494.47,"check":"350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required after-tax net revenue per year","value":103710.83,"check":"((0)-(-(350000+30000+0)+(350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3)+(40000+30000+0)/(1+0.1)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.1)**3)/0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required annual revenue","value":625515.93,"check":"(((0)-(-(350000+30000+0)+(350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3)+(40000+30000+0)/(1+0.1)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.1)**3)/0.1)/(1-0.32)+95000+31.5*12000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price per unit","value":52.13,"check":"(((0)-(-(350000+30000+0)+(350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3)+(40000+30000+0)/(1+0.1)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.1)**3)/0.1)/(1-0.32)+95000+31.5*12000)/12000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV CCA = 69,494.47; known PV = -380,000 + 69,494.47 + 52,592.04 = -257,913.49","After-tax annuity = 257,913.49 / 2.4869 = 103,710.83; pre-tax = 152,515.93","Revenue = 152,515.93 + 95,000 + 31.50 x 12,000 = 625,515.93; price = $52.13"],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 10, PV = known PV, CPT PMT"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide by (1 - T)","value":48.06,"check":"(((0)-(-(350000+30000+0)+(350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3)+(40000+30000+0)/(1+0.1)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.1)**3)/0.1)+95000+31.5*12000)/12000","explain":"Net revenue is taxed, so divide the after-tax need by (1 - T)."},{"id":"forgot-fixed-costs","label":"Left out the fixed cash costs","value":44.21,"check":"(((0)-(-(350000+30000+0)+(350000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-40000*0.25*0.32/(0.1+0.25)/(1+0.1)**3)+(40000+30000+0)/(1+0.1)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.1)**3)/0.1)/(1-0.32)+31.5*12000)/12000","explain":"The price must also cover FC."}],"explanation":"Same five steps as always; the quantity is only used at the end.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-33, A19"},{"id":"fin201-q10-534","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Fraser Valley Labels will print 50,000 packs a year for 5 years. Equipment costs $480,000 (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, sold for $30,000 at year 5, pool open) and sits on land the firm owns, worth $300,000 after tax now and $320,000 after tax in 5 years. NWC of $40,000 is recovered at the end. Variable cost is $2.70 a pack, fixed cash costs $160,000, T = 34%, k = 12%. What is the lowest price per pack for NPV = 0?","answer":10.04,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(((0)-(-(480000+40000+300000)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+320000)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.34)+160000+2.7*50000)/50000","steps":[{"label":"Time-0 outflow: equipment + land + NWC","value":-820000.0,"check":"-(480000+300000+40000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of everything except operations","value":-492511.11,"check":"(-(480000+40000+300000)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+320000)/(1+0.12)**5)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required after-tax net revenue per year","value":136627.37,"check":"((0)-(-(480000+40000+300000)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+320000)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required annual revenue","value":502011.17,"check":"(((0)-(-(480000+40000+300000)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+320000)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.34)+160000+2.7*50000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price per pack","value":10.04,"check":"(((0)-(-(480000+40000+300000)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+320000)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.34)+160000+2.7*50000)/50000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Time-0 flows: -480,000 - 300,000 - 40,000 = -820,000. Year 5: 30,000 + 40,000 + 320,000 = 390,000 (PV 221,296.47)","PV CCA = 106,192.42; known PV = -820,000 + 221,296.47 + 106,192.42 = -492,511.11","After-tax annuity = 492,511.11 / 3.6048 = 136,627.37; pre-tax = 207,011.17","Revenue = 207,011.17 + 160,000 + 2.70 x 50,000 = 502,011.17; price = $10.04 per pack"],"keystrokes":["Compute the known PV with land in and out, then CPT PMT with N = 5, I/Y = 12"],"errors":[{"id":"land-excluded","label":"Left out the land's opportunity cost and return","value":9.04,"check":"(((0)-(-(480000+40000+0)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.34)+160000+2.7*50000)/50000","explain":"The land has an opportunity cost: the firm gives up its after-tax sale value now and gets it back later."},{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide by (1 - T)","value":8.63,"check":"(((0)-(-(480000+40000+300000)+(480000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-30000*0.3*0.34/(0.12+0.3)/(1+0.12)**5)+(30000+40000+320000)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)+160000+2.7*50000)/50000","explain":"Divide the after-tax requirement by (1 - T)."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA shield","value":10.93,"check":"((480000+40000+300000-(30000+40000+320000)/1.12**5)/((1-1/1.12**5)/0.12)/0.66+160000+2.7*50000)/50000","explain":"The CCA shield reduces the price needed."}],"explanation":"Land goes in at its after-tax opportunity value both ways; the rest of the five steps are unchanged.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-43"},{"id":"fin201-q10-535","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nechako Components (equipment $540,000, CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, sold for $60,000 at year 5, pool open; NWC $45,000; 30,000 units a year; variable cost $22.60; fixed cash costs $210,000; T = 35%; k = 12%) wants its 5-year contract to add $75,000 of NPV instead of zero. What price per unit does it need?","answer":36.66,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(((75000)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","steps":[{"label":"Required after-tax net revenue","value":137614.86,"check":"((75000)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required annual revenue","value":1099715.17,"check":"(((75000)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price per unit","value":36.66,"check":"(((75000)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Set NPV = 75,000: PV of operations must equal 75,000 - known PV = 75,000 + 421,070.78 = 496,070.78","After-tax annuity = 496,070.78 / 3.6048 = 137,614.86; pre-tax = 211,715.17","Revenue = 211,715.17 + 210,000 + 678,000 = 1,099,715.17; price = $36.66"],"keystrokes":["Same steps as the zero-NPV bid with 75,000 added to the PV that operations must supply"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Subtracted the target NPV instead of adding it","value":34.52,"check":"(((-75000)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","explain":"A positive target NPV means operations must generate more PV, so the price rises."},{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide by (1 - T)","value":34.19,"check":"(((75000)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)+210000+22.6*30000)/30000","explain":"Divide by (1 - T)."}],"explanation":"A required NPV simply raises the PV that the operating annuity must supply.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-24, TB 10.6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-536","concept":"fin201-c10-bid-price","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Nechako Components (equipment $540,000, CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, sold for $60,000 at year 5, pool open; NWC $45,000; variable cost $22.60; fixed cash costs $210,000; T = 35%; k = 12%) can sell its product at $38.50. How many units a year must it sell for NPV = 0? Answer in units, to the nearest unit.","answer":24510.0,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":1.0},"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000)/(38.5-22.6)","steps":[{"label":"Required pre-tax net revenue","value":179706.3584,"check":"((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Contribution per unit = P - v","value":15.9,"check":"38.5-22.6","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Break-even quantity","value":24509.8339,"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35)+210000)/(38.5-22.6)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}}],"solution":["Required after-tax net revenue = 116,809.13; pre-tax = 179,706.36","(P - v)Q - FC = pre-tax net revenue, so Q = (pre-tax + FC)/(P - v)","Q = (179,706.36 + 210,000)/15.90 = 24,510 units"],"keystrokes":["Compute the pre-tax requirement, add 210000, divide by 15.9"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide by (1 - T)","value":20554.03,"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)+210000)/(38.5-22.6)","explain":"Net revenue is taxed: divide by (1 - T) first."},{"id":"forgot-fixed-costs","label":"Left out the fixed cash costs","value":11302.29,"check":"(((0)-(-(540000+45000+0)+(540000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-60000*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)+(60000+45000+0)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)/(1-0.35))/(38.5-22.6)","explain":"Fixed costs must be covered before profit."}],"explanation":"Same NPV = 0 requirement, solved for quantity instead of price.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-52"},{"id":"fin201-q10-541","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Rail owns an old locomotive, the last asset in its CCA class, that earns nothing more. It is deciding whether to sell it at the end of year 5 or keep it and keep claiming CCA. What is the opportunity cost of selling at year 5?","options":["The present value of the CCA tax shields it gives up on the remaining UCC","The original purchase price of the locomotive","The CCA already claimed in years 1 to 5","The recapture tax that would be due on the sale"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Selling ends the CCA stream. At year 5 that stream is worth UCC x d x T/(k + d). Past CCA is history, and recapture tax is already inside the after-tax sale proceeds.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-542","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":["fin201-c10-sunk-opportunity"],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Clean Air Industries owns land it could use for a new sensor line. Which three choices should it compare?","options":["Build the project, sell the project, or abandon the project","Sell the land now and buy equivalent land back later; keep renting it out; use it for the new project","Sell the land, keep the land idle, or give the land away","Use the land now, use the land in a year, or use the land in two years"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The land has alternative uses and each is a different set of cash flows. The lecture compares the two existing uses first (sell and rebuy versus rent) and then tests the winner against the project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s27-31"},{"id":"fin201-q10-543","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When comparing a sale at year N with continuing to hold the asset, the sale gives up the present value of the remaining CCA _____.","options":["recapture","terminal loss","capital gain","tax shield"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Continuing to hold keeps the declining stream of CCA deductions going. Their tax saving, T x CCA, is the shield the sale gives up, valued as a declining perpetuity.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-544","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the cash flows from selling at year N differ from the cash flows from holding the asset?","options":["Selling brings after-tax cash now but ends the CCA shield; holding keeps the shield","Selling gives the CCA shield now; holding gives the proceeds","Selling is taxed; holding is not taxed at all","Selling and holding give the same cash flows except for timing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Selling swaps a lump of after-tax cash (POD less tax on recapture or plus the terminal-loss refund) for the loss of all later CCA shields. Holding does the opposite.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-545","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"When valuing 'sell at year 5 versus hold', which of the following should NOT enter the analysis?","options":["The after-tax proceeds from the sale","The tax effect (recapture or terminal loss) of the sale","The original purchase price of the asset","The CCA tax shields that would continue if the asset is held"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The purchase price is a sunk cost. Only what changes between selling and holding matters: the proceeds, their tax effect and the lost future shields.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-546","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can a firm be better off selling an asset at year N even though the sale ends its CCA deductions?","options":["CCA deductions are no longer tax deductible after year N","The sale proceeds are never taxed when an asset is sold","Cash from the sale arrives now; the lost shields arrive slowly and shrink","UCC always exceeds the sale price, creating a tax refund"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The lost shield is worth UCC x d x T/(k + d), a fraction of UCC. The sale delivers POD plus a tax effect immediately. Without other benefits from holding, selling usually wins.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-547","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A sell-versus-hold analysis at year 4 gives an NPV of +$31,005 for selling. What does this mean?","options":["Selling beats holding by $31,005 in year-4 dollars","Holding the asset adds $31,005 of value","The asset's sale price at year 4 is $31,005","The asset will produce $31,005 of profit before tax"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The NPV compares the two strategies at a common date: after-tax proceeds less the PV of the shield lost. A positive value favours the sale.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-548","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence correctly compares the three choices for the land in the Clean Air example?","options":["Compare all three using each choice's accounting profit","Evaluate the project alone and ignore the land","Discount all three choices at different rates and pick the largest cash flow","Compare 'rent' with 'sell and buy back', then the winner with 'use for the project'"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each comparison uses incremental NPV. The rent-versus-sell step shows what the land is worth in its best alternative use, which is the opportunity cost charged to the project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s28-31"},{"id":"fin201-q10-549","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":3,"stem":"Other things equal, a higher discount rate makes selling the asset at year N more attractive relative to holding it.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lost shield is UCC x d x T/(k + d); a higher k reduces it, while the sale proceeds do not depend on k. The comparison tilts further towards selling.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-550","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":["fin201-c10-recapture-terminal-loss"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Rail will sell its last idle crane at the end of year 5 for $24,000 and close the class. The crane cost $40,000, its UCC after year 5 CCA is $18,400, CCA rate 30%, T = 36% and k = 10%. If it keeps the crane it keeps claiming CCA on the UCC. What is the NPV, at year 5, of selling instead of holding?","options":["$15,360.00","$17,016.00","$19,032.00","$21,984.00","$26,952.00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After-tax proceeds = 24,000 - 0.36 x (24,000 - 18,400) = 21,984.00. Lost shield = 18,400 x 0.30 x 0.36/(0.10 + 0.30) = 4,968.00. NPV = 21,984.00 - 4,968.00 = $17,016.00. Ignoring the lost shield overstates the gain; adding it instead of subtracting is a sign error.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s25-26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-551","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"An idle machine (CCA rate 20%) has a UCC of $33,177.60 after year 4 CCA. T = 35% and k = 12%. What is the value at year 4 of the CCA tax shields the firm gives up if it sells the machine, closing the class?","options":["$2,322.43","$6,480.00","$7,257.60","$20,736.00","$29,030.40"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Value of the declining shield stream = UCC x d x T/(k + d) = 33,177.60 x 0.20 x 0.35/0.32 = $7,257.60 at year 4. Using k - d gives a nonsensical value; omitting T or the (k + d) divisor gives other wrong answers.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26, s15"},{"id":"fin201-q10-552","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Bow Valley Aggregates owns an idle machine bought for $72,000 (CCA rate 20%, half-year rule). At the end of year 4, after claiming year 4 CCA, it can sell it for $41,000, closing the class, or keep it and keep claiming CCA. T = 35% and k = 12%. What is the NPV at year 4 of selling instead of holding?","answer":31004.56,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"41000-0.35*(41000-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3)-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)","steps":[{"label":"UCC after year 4","value":33177.6,"check":"72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"After-tax proceeds CF_AT","value":38262.16,"check":"41000-0.35*(41000-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV at year 4 of the shield given up","value":7257.6,"check":"72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of selling vs holding","value":31004.56,"check":"41000-0.35*(41000-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3)-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["UCC1 = 72,000 x 0.9 = 64,800; UCC4 = 64,800 x 0.8^3 = 33,177.60","POD 41,000 > UCC and < ACB 72,000: recapture = 7,822.40, tax = 2,737.84, CF_AT = 38,262.16","Lost shield = 33,177.60 x 0.20 x 0.35/(0.12 + 0.20) = 7,257.60","NPV = 38,262.16 - 7,257.60 = $31,004.56"],"keystrokes":["Compute UCC; CF_AT = 41000 - 0.35(41000 - UCC); lost shield = UCC x 0.2 x 0.35 / 0.32; subtract"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Ignored the shield given up","value":38262.16,"check":"41000-0.35*(41000-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3)","explain":"Selling ends the CCA stream; its PV is the opportunity cost of selling."},{"id":"recapture-full-pod","label":"Taxed the whole proceeds","value":19392.4,"check":"41000*(1-0.35)-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3*0.2*0.35/(0.12+0.2)","explain":"Tax is only on POD - UCC (recapture)."},{"id":"k-minus-d","label":"Used k - d in the lost-shield denominator","value":9231.76,"check":"41000-0.35*(41000-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3)-72000*(1-0.5*0.2)*(1-0.2)**3*0.2*0.35/(0.2-0.12)","explain":"The shield declines at d, so the discount denominator is k + d."}],"explanation":"Net the after-tax cash from selling against the PV of the shield you would otherwise keep.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s25-26, SM 10-11"},{"id":"fin201-q10-553","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Pallet has an idle machine bought for $50,000 (CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, T = 30%, k = 10%). It will be sold either at the end of year 3 for $22,000 or at the end of year 4 for $17,500; either sale (after that year's CCA) closes the class and the machine earns nothing meanwhile. How much more is the present value today of selling at year 3 than selling at year 4? Count the CCA tax shields and the tax effect of the sale.","answer":3630.05,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(22000-0.3*(22000-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2))/1.1**3-(0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(0.3*0.3*50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2+17500-0.3*(17500-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**3))/1.1**4)","steps":[{"label":"UCC after year 3","value":20825.0,"check":"50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"UCC after year 4","value":14577.5,"check":"50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV today if sold at year 3","value":23482.34,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(22000-0.3*(22000-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2))/1.1**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV today if sold at year 4","value":19852.3,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(0.3*0.3*50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2+17500-0.3*(17500-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**3))/1.1**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Advantage of selling at year 3","value":3630.05,"check":"0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(22000-0.3*(22000-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2))/1.1**3-(0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(0.3*0.3*50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2+17500-0.3*(17500-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**3))/1.1**4)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CCA: 7,500.00, 12,750.00, 8,925.00, 6,247.50; UCC3 = 20,825.00, UCC4 = 14,577.50","Sale at year 3: recapture 1,175.00, CF_AT = 21,647.50. Sale at year 4: recapture 2,922.50, CF_AT = 16,623.25","PV (year 3 sale) = shields years 1-3 + CF_AT/1.1^3 = 23,482.34","PV (year 4 sale) = shields years 1-3 + (year-4 shield 1,874.25 + CF_AT 16,623.25)/1.1^4 = 19,852.30","Difference = $3,630.05"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = 0, C01 = 2250, C02 = 3825, C03 = 2677.5 + 21647.5, NPV I = 10 for the year-3 plan; extend to C04 for the year-4 plan"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Compared only the after-tax sale proceeds discounted","value":4910.18,"check":"(22000-0.3*(22000-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2))/1.1**3-(17500-0.3*(17500-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**3))/1.1**4","explain":"Waiting a year also earns one more year of CCA shield; both plans must count all shields."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the year-4 flows by four years","value":2366.64,"check":"(0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(22000-0.3*(22000-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2))/1.1**3)-(0.3*0.5*0.3*50000/1.1+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3))/1.1**2+0.3*0.3*(50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3))/1.1**3+(0.3*0.3*50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**2+17500-0.3*(17500-50000*(1-0.5*0.3)*(1-0.3)**3))/1.1**3)","explain":"Year-4 flows are discounted four years."}],"explanation":"Compare the PV of the two complete plans: shields plus after-tax sale in each.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s26, s23-25"},{"id":"fin201-q10-554","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Okotoks Industrial owns land worth $2,500,000 after tax today, rented for $260,000 a year (taxed at 25%) for the next 5 years. It could instead sell the land now and buy equivalent land back in 5 years for $2,500,000 (the market is flat). The discount rate is 12%. What is the NPV of renting minus selling and buying back? A negative answer means selling is better.","answer":-378501.5,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-2500000+260000*(1-0.25)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+2500000/1.12**5","steps":[{"label":"After-tax rent per year","value":195000.0,"check":"260000*(1-0.25)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of after-tax rent","value":702931.36,"check":"260000*(1-0.25)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the land at year 5","value":1418567.14,"check":"2500000/1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of renting vs selling and rebuying","value":-378501.5,"check":"-2500000+260000*(1-0.25)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+2500000/1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Renting means giving up the sale proceeds now (-2,500,000) and keeping the land worth 2,500,000 in year 5","PV of rent = 195,000 x 3.6048 = 702,931.36; PV of land at year 5 = 1,418,567.14","NPV = -$378,501.50; negative, so selling and buying back is better"],"keystrokes":["N = 5, I/Y = 12, PMT = 195000, CPT PV; FV = 2500000, CPT PV; subtract 2500000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-times-t","label":"Used the rent before tax","value":-144191.05,"check":"-2500000+260000*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+2500000/1.12**5","explain":"Rent is income and is taxed at 25%."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the land at year 5","value":702931.36,"check":"-2500000+260000*(1-0.25)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12+2500000","explain":"The land is bought back in 5 years; discount it."},{"id":"sign-cf0","label":"Reversed the sign (selling minus renting)","value":378501.5,"check":"2500000-260000*(1-0.25)*(1-1/1.12**5)/0.12-2500000/1.12**5","explain":"The question asks for rent minus sell; the sign tells which is better."}],"explanation":"The lecture's first comparison: renting out versus selling and buying back the land.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s28-29"},{"id":"fin201-q10-555","concept":"fin201-c10-timing-of-sale","also":["fin201-c10-pool-open-closed"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow Valley Dairy can sell a machine from an open pool (other assets remain) for $35,000 at the end of year 3. The sale has no immediate tax effect, but it removes the proceeds from the pool's UCC. CCA rate 20%, T = 35%, k = 10%. What is the NPV at year 3 of selling instead of holding, counting the shield lost on the amount sold?","answer":26833.33,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"35000-35000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)","steps":[{"label":"PV at year 3 of the shield lost","value":8166.67,"check":"35000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"NPV of selling vs holding","value":26833.33,"check":"35000-35000*0.2*0.35/(0.1+0.2)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Shield lost on 35,000 = 35,000 x 0.20 x 0.35/(0.10 + 0.20) = 8,166.67","NPV = 35,000 - 8,166.67 = $26,833.33"],"keystrokes":["35000 x 0.2 x 0.35 / 0.3 = 8166.67","35000 - 8166.67 = 26833.33"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Ignored the lost shield","value":35000.0,"check":"35000","explain":"Selling shrinks the pool, so the CCA on that amount is given up."},{"id":"k-minus-d","label":"Used k - d","value":10500.0,"check":"35000-35000*0.2*0.35/(0.2-0.1)","explain":"The denominator is k + d."}],"explanation":"An open-pool sale is received untaxed but costs the shield on the amount sold.","source":"generated","ref":"L05B s22, s26"},{"id":"fin201-q10-561","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fraser Rentals must choose between two vans, one lasting 3 years and one lasting 5 years. Each will be replaced by an identical van when it wears out, and both provide the same service. Which method compares them properly?","options":["Compare the NPVs of costs directly","Equivalent annual cost over each van's own life","Compare the IRRs of the two vans","Compare the payback periods of the two vans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The vans have unequal lives and a continuing need, so each needs to be turned into a cost per year. EAC = PV of costs divided by the annuity factor for the van's own life.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6, S6 s11-14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-562","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The equivalent annual cost of an asset is its present value of costs divided by the _____ factor for its life.","options":["perpetuity","discount","growth","annuity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The EAC is the level annual payment with the same PV as the costs over the asset's life, so it is PV/[(1 - 1/(1 + k)^N)/k].","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-563","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can comparing the raw present values of costs of a 3-year machine and a 5-year machine give the wrong answer?","options":["The raw PV ignores the CCA tax shield","The shorter-lived machine's smaller PV buys fewer years of service","Raw PVs are stated in different currencies","The longer-lived machine always has the lower PV of costs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Raw PVs are totals over different time spans. A 3-year machine will look cheaper even if its annual cost is higher. EAC puts them on a per-year footing.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6, SM 10-27"},{"id":"fin201-q10-564","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT an assumption behind using EAC to compare machines?","options":["Each machine will be replaced by an identical one when it wears out","Costs and the discount rate stay the same in future replacement cycles","Each machine will be used once and never replaced","The need for the service continues indefinitely"],"answer":2,"explanation":"EAC works because each option is assumed to repeat forever, so annual costs are the fair comparison. If a machine is used only once, simply compare PVs over the same service period.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q10-565","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the PV of costs used in an EAC calculation subtract the PV of the CCA tax shield?","options":["CCA cuts taxes, which lowers the cost of owning the machine","CCA is a cash outflow that has to be reversed","CCA is already included in the operating cost","CCA raises the discount rate applied to the costs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a cost project, cash outflows are positive costs. The shield saves tax, so it is subtracted. The same is true of the discounted salvage value.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s11-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-566","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rental van X has an equivalent annual cost of $7,240 and van Y has $7,905. What does this mean?","options":["Van X costs $7,240 in total over its life","Van X is cheaper by $665 for each year of service, so it should be bought","The two are equal because EAC removes all cost differences","Van Y is cheaper because its life is shorter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"EAC is a cost per year of service. The lower the EAC, the cheaper the machine to provide the same service. The EACs, not the total PVs, are compared.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-567","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":["fin201-c10-pvcca"],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In PV of costs for an EAC with taxes, which pair of items reduces the cost?","options":["The after-tax operating costs and the salvage value","The purchase price and the PV of the CCA tax shield","The CCA tax shield and the after-tax operating costs","The PV of the CCA tax shield and the PV of the salvage value"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PV of costs = purchase price + PV of after-tax operating costs - PV of salvage - PV of the CCA tax shield. Both salvage and the shield bring cash or tax savings back.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s12-13"},{"id":"fin201-q10-568","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Computing EAC for two machines with unequal lives gives the same ranking as chaining each machine's cash flows over their lowest common multiple of years.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The EAC method implicitly assumes the replacement chain repeats forever, so it picks the same machine as a replacement chain over the lowest common multiple of the lives.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-44"},{"id":"fin201-q10-569","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 3-year machine has an EAC of $30,088 and a 5-year machine has an EAC of $29,138. Both will be replaced forever. Which should the firm buy?","options":["The 5-year machine: it has the lower annual cost","The 3-year machine: its life is shorter and its PV of costs is lower","Either one: EAC is only for ranking, not for choosing","Neither: a cost can never be preferred"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The firm minimizes cost per year of service. The 5-year machine saves $950 a year. The shorter life is already accounted for in its higher annual figure.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-570","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Saskatoon Seed is choosing between press C and press D, both replaced forever. Press C costs $64,000, has pre-tax operating costs of $9,000 a year, lasts 4 years and sells for $14,000 (pool open). CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, T = 35%, k = 10%. What is press C's equivalent annual cost?","options":["$13,996.44","$15,239.54","$19,230.52","$22,380.52","$23,023.54"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PV of costs = 64,000 + 9,000 x 0.65 x 3.1699 - 14,000/1.1^4 - PV CCA (12,023.37) = 60,958.16. EAC = 60,958.16/3.1699 = $19,230.52. Dividing by N, using the other press's annuity factor, ignoring the shield or leaving the operating costs untaxed each produce a different number.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s12-14, SM 10-30"},{"id":"fin201-q10-571","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Press D costs $98,000, has operating costs of $6,500 a year before tax, lasts 6 years and sells for $16,000 (pool open); press C (4 years) has an EAC of $19,230.52. Same CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, T = 35%, k = 10%. By how much per year is the cheaper press cheaper than the other?","options":["$631.29","$894.45","$1,629.27","$8,420.34","$26,691.33"],"answer":1,"explanation":"EAC of D = 87,649.49/4.3553 = 20,124.97. Press C is cheaper by 20,124.97 - 19,230.52 = $894.45 a year. Differences of raw PVs ($26,691.33) mix different lives; $8,420.34 uses press C's 4-year annuity factor for D; $631.29 divides each PV by N; $1,629.27 leaves out both CCA shields.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s14, SM 10-30"},{"id":"fin201-q10-572","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Haulage needs a loader and is comparing two models, each replaced by an identical model when it wears out. Model A costs $85,000, has operating costs of $12,000 a year before tax, lasts 3 years and sells for $25,000. CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, pool open, T = 30%, k = 11%. What is model A's equivalent annual cost, as a positive number?","answer":30087.82,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(85000+0+12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)-(25000+0)/(1+0.11)**3-(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)","steps":[{"label":"After-tax operating cost per year","value":8400.0,"check":"12000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of after-tax operating costs","value":20527.2,"check":"12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/1.11**3)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of salvage","value":18279.78,"check":"25000/1.11**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":13721.38,"check":"(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of costs","value":73526.04,"check":"(85000+0+12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)-(25000+0)/(1+0.11)**3-(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Annuity factor (11%, 3)","value":2.4437,"check":"((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"EAC","value":30087.82,"check":"(85000+0+12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)-(25000+0)/(1+0.11)**3-(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of operating costs = 8,400 x 2.4437 = 20,527.20; PV of salvage = 18,279.78","PV CCA = 85,000 x 0.3 x 0.3/0.41 x 1.055/1.11 - 25,000 x 0.3 x 0.3/0.41 / 1.11^3 = 13,721.38","PV of costs = 85,000 + 20,527.20 - 18,279.78 - 13,721.38 = 73,526.04","EAC = 73,526.04 / 2.4437 = $30,087.82"],"keystrokes":["PV of costs first (see steps), then N = 3, I/Y = 11, PV = -PV of costs, CPT PMT"],"errors":[{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided the PV of costs by N","value":24508.68,"check":"(85000+0+12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)-(25000+0)/(1+0.11)**3-(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))/3","explain":"A level annual cost with the same PV must be found with the annuity factor."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":35702.78,"check":"((85000+0+12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)-(25000+0)/(1+0.11)**3-(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))+(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))/((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)","explain":"The CCA shield lowers the cost of owning the machine."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used the other model's 5-year annuity factor","value":19893.96,"check":"(85000+0+12000*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**3)/0.11)-(25000+0)/(1+0.11)**3-(85000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-25000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**3))/((1-1/1.11**5)/0.11)","explain":"The annuity factor uses this model's own life, N = 3."}],"explanation":"EAC = PV of costs (price + after-tax operating costs - salvage - CCA shield) divided by the annuity factor for its own life.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s11-14, SM 10-28"},{"id":"fin201-q10-573","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Haulage's other loader, model B, costs $120,000, has operating costs of $8,500 a year before tax, lasts 5 years and sells for $20,000. CCA rate 30%, half-year rule, pool open, T = 30%, k = 11%. Model A has an EAC of $30,087.82. How much less per year does the cheaper model cost than the other? Answer as a positive number.","answer":949.91,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"30087.82-(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)","steps":[{"label":"PV of costs for B","value":107690.7,"check":"(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Annuity factor (11%, 5)","value":3.6959,"check":"((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"EAC of B","value":29137.91,"check":"(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EAC of A minus EAC of B","value":949.91,"check":"30087.82-(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of operating costs = 5,950 x 3.6959 = 21,990.59; PV of salvage = 11,869.03; PV CCA = 22,430.86","PV of costs = 120,000 + 21,990.59 - 11,869.03 - 22,430.86 = 107,690.70","EAC of B = 107,690.70 / 3.6959 = 29,137.91","Model B is cheaper by 30,087.82 - 29,137.91 = $949.91 a year. Raw PV of costs would wrongly favour A ($73,526.04 versus $107,690.70)"],"keystrokes":["PV of costs for B, then N = 5, I/Y = 11, PV = -PV, CPT PMT; subtract from A's EAC"],"errors":[{"id":"raw-npv-unequal-lives","label":"Compared the raw PVs of costs","value":34164.66,"check":"(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))-73526.04","explain":"Raw PVs hide the fact that B serves two more years."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided each PV by its life","value":2970.54,"check":"73526.04/3-(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))/5","explain":"EAC uses the annuity factor, not N."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 3-year annuity factor for B","value":13980.63,"check":"abs(30087.82-(120000+0+8500*(1-0.3)*((1-1/(1+0.11)**5)/0.11)-(20000+0)/(1+0.11)**5-(120000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.11)/(1+0.11)-20000*0.3*0.3/(0.11+0.3)/(1+0.11)**5))/((1-1/1.11**3)/0.11))","explain":"B lasts 5 years."}],"explanation":"The longer-lived model has the lower annual cost even though its total PV of costs is higher.","source":"generated","ref":"S6 s11-14, SM 10-28"},{"id":"fin201-q10-574","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Bow River Cleaners buys a dryer for $14,000. It costs $1,100 a year to run, lasts 4 years and sells for $2,000. There are no taxes and k = 9%. What is the equivalent annual cost, as a positive number?","answer":4984.02,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(14000+1100*((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)-2000/1.09**4)/((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)","steps":[{"label":"PV of costs","value":16146.84,"check":"14000+1100*((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)-2000/1.09**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Annuity factor (9%, 4)","value":3.2397,"check":"(1-1/1.09**4)/0.09","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"EAC","value":4984.02,"check":"(14000+1100*((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)-2000/1.09**4)/((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of costs = 14,000 + 1,100 x 3.2397 - 2,000/1.09^4 = 16,146.84","EAC = 16,146.84 / 3.2397 = $4,984.02"],"keystrokes":["N = 4, I/Y = 9, PMT = 1100, CPT PV; add 14000; subtract PV of 2000; then CPT PMT"],"errors":[{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided by N","value":4036.71,"check":"(14000+1100*((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)-2000/1.09**4)/4","explain":"Use the annuity factor, not N."},{"id":"salvage-not-discounted","label":"Did not discount the salvage","value":4804.02,"check":"(14000+1100*((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)-2000)/((1-1/1.09**4)/0.09)","explain":"The $2,000 arrives in 4 years."}],"explanation":"No taxes: PV of costs over the annuity factor, with salvage reducing the cost.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 10.6"},{"id":"fin201-q10-575","concept":"fin201-c10-eac-taxes","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kootenay Foods compares a packaging system that costs $210,000, needs $15,000 of working capital (recovered at the end), has operating costs of $31,000 a year before tax, lasts 5 years and sells for $18,000. CCA rate 20%, half-year rule, pool open, T = 40%, k = 12%, replaced forever. What is its EAC, as a positive number?","answer":62747.22,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(210000+15000+31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)-(18000+15000)/(1+0.12)**5-(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","steps":[{"label":"PV of after-tax operating costs","value":67048.84,"check":"31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/1.12**5)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of salvage + NWC recovery","value":18725.09,"check":"(18000+15000)/1.12**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of CCA tax shield","value":47134.08,"check":"(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of costs","value":226189.67,"check":"(210000+15000+31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)-(18000+15000)/(1+0.12)**5-(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"EAC","value":62747.22,"check":"(210000+15000+31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)-(18000+15000)/(1+0.12)**5-(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5))/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["PV of operating costs = 18,600 x 3.6048 = 67,048.84; PV of salvage and NWC = 33,000/1.12^5 = 18,725.09","PV CCA = 47,134.08","PV of costs = 210,000 + 15,000 + 67,048.84 - 18,725.09 - 47,134.08 = 226,189.67","EAC = 226,189.67 / 3.6048 = $62,747.22"],"keystrokes":["PV of costs (steps above), then N = 5, I/Y = 12, PV = -PV of costs, CPT PMT"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-nwc-recovery","label":"Did not recover the NWC","value":65108.36,"check":"((210000+15000+31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)-(18000+15000)/(1+0.12)**5-(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5))+15000/1.12**5)/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","explain":"Working capital is released at the end."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Dropped the salvage term in PV_CCA","value":63455.56,"check":"((210000+15000+31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)-(18000+15000)/(1+0.12)**5-(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5))+18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/1.12**5)/((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)","explain":"The machine is sold, so the shield on the salvage is lost."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Divided by N","value":45237.93,"check":"(210000+15000+31000*(1-0.4)*((1-1/(1+0.12)**5)/0.12)-(18000+15000)/(1+0.12)**5-(210000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)*(1+0.5*0.12)/(1+0.12)-18000*0.2*0.4/(0.12+0.2)/(1+0.12)**5))/5","explain":"Use the annuity factor."}],"explanation":"NWC goes in at time 0 and comes back at the end; everything else follows the same pattern.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-29, S6 s11-14"},{"id":"fin201-q10-581","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Athabasca Pipe Works forecasts its project's cash flows in constant year-0 dollars, with no allowance for price increases. Which discount rate is consistent with those flows?","options":["The nominal rate","The inflation rate","The real rate","The effective annual rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Flows in constant purchasing power are real flows and must be discounted at a real rate. Nominal flows, which include the expected price increases, use the nominal rate. Both give the same PV.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s10, FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-582","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The relationship between the three rates is (1 + R_nominal) = (1 + R_inflation) x (1 + _____).","options":["R_effective","R_nominal","R_real","R_after-tax"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Fisher relationship compounds inflation and the real return. It is not the same as R_nominal = R_inflation + R_real, which ignores the cross term R_inflation x R_real.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1, L02C s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-583","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a real cash flow differ from a nominal cash flow?","options":["A real cash flow is stated in today's purchasing power; a nominal cash flow includes expected inflation","A real cash flow is after tax; a nominal cash flow is before tax","A real cash flow is certain; a nominal cash flow is only an expected value","A real cash flow is discounted at the nominal rate; a nominal cash flow at the real rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nominal flows are in the dollars of the year they arrive, so they include inflation; real flows are in constant (today's) dollars: CF_real,i = CF_nominal,i/(1 + R_inf)^i. Each is paired with its own rate (nominal with nominal, real with real); tax and risk are separate issues.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1; SM 10 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-584","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following combinations is NOT consistent?","options":["Nominal cash flows discounted at the nominal rate","Real cash flows discounted at the real rate","Nominal cash flows converted to real by dividing by (1 + R_inf)^i, then discounted at the real rate","Real cash flows discounted at the nominal rate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A real flow has no inflation in it, so a discount rate that includes inflation over-discounts it and understates the PV. The three consistent pairings all give the same PV.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s10, SM 10-40"},{"id":"fin201-q10-585","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the real rate not simply the nominal rate minus the inflation rate?","options":["Inflation is paid out of the real rate, not the nominal rate","Purchasing power is a ratio: (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf) − 1 is a bit below R_nom − R_inf","The real rate is always zero when a rate is stated in nominal terms","Taxes are paid on the nominal amount only, not on the real amount"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At 10% nominal and 3% inflation, $1,000 grows to $1,100 while goods rise to $1,030: purchasing power grows 1,100/1,030 = 6.7961%, not 7%. The subtraction approximation drifts further for larger rates.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-586","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A firm computes a project's EAC with real cash flows at the real rate and gets a lower annual cost figure than with nominal cash flows at the nominal rate, although both PVs of costs are identical. Why?","options":["One of the two calculations must contain a mistake","Real cash flows are taxed at a lower rate than nominal ones","The nominal rate is risk free and the real rate is not","The real rate is lower, so its annuity factor is larger and the annual figure smaller"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PVs agree because both methods are consistent, but EACs are expressed in different dollars. A real EAC is in constant dollars; a nominal EAC is a level nominal payment. Because the real rate is lower, the real annuity factor is larger, so the real EAC is smaller.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-587","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Ridge Mills forecasts a project's cash flows in nominal dollars and has a 12% required return that already includes expected inflation. What should the analyst do?","options":["Subtract inflation from the cash flows and discount at 12%","Discount the nominal cash flows at 12%","Discount the nominal cash flows at the real rate only","Add inflation to 12% before discounting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 12% includes inflation, so it matches nominal cash flows. Adjusting it again would double count the inflation effect.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s10"},{"id":"fin201-q10-588","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A nominal cash flow received in year i is converted to a real cash flow by dividing it by (1 + R_inflation)^i.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dividing by cumulative inflation expresses the future dollars in today's purchasing power: CF_real,i = CF_nominal,i/(1 + R_inf)^i.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-589","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Discounting nominal cash flows at the nominal rate and real cash flows at the real rate gives the same present value.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both methods remove inflation from the comparison consistently. The nominal rate contains the inflation premium that the nominal cash flows also contain, and the real versions contain none.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s10, SM 10-40"},{"id":"fin201-q10-590","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Foothills Gas expects 2.4% annual inflation and its required nominal return is 9.5% per annum. What is the real required return?","options":["6.93% per annum","7.10% per annum","11.90% per annum","12.13% per annum"],"answer":0,"explanation":"(1 + R_real) = (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf) = 1.095/1.024 = 1.069336, so R_real = 6.9336% per annum. Subtracting 9.5% - 2.4% = 7.10% ignores the cross term; multiplying the factors gives a nominal rate, not a real one.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1, SM 10 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-591","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bow Valley Ltd. must pay $12,000 a year in today's dollars (prices rise with 2% inflation, so the nominal payments grow at 2%) for 5 years, the first payment in one year. The nominal discount rate is 8% per annum. What is the PV of the payments?","options":["$47,912.52","$50,548.37","$50,710.56","$53,713.92","$60,000.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Real rate = 1.08/1.02 - 1 = 5.8824%; PV = 12,000 x [1 - 1.058824^-5]/0.058824 = $50,710.56, the same as discounting the nominal flows 12,240, 12,484.80, ... at 8%. $47,912.52 discounts the real $12,000 at the nominal 8%; $50,548.37 uses 8% - 2% = 6% as the real rate; $53,713.92 discounts the nominal flows at the real rate; $60,000 ignores discounting.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s10, SM 10-40"},{"id":"fin201-q10-592","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Nechako Timber's required return is 8.2% per annum nominal and inflation is 2.6% per annum. What is the real required return, in % per annum?","answer":5.46,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(1.082/1.026-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"1 + R_real = (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf)","value":1.0546,"check":"1.082/1.026","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"R_real in %","value":5.4581,"check":"(1.082/1.026-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real)","1 + R_real = 1.082/1.026 = 1.054581","R_real = 5.4581% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.082 / 1.026 = 1.054581","- 1 = 0.054581 -> 5.46%"],"errors":[{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Subtracted inflation from the nominal rate","value":5.6,"check":"(0.082-0.026)*100","explain":"Real = (1 + nominal)/(1 + inflation) - 1, not nominal minus inflation."},{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Multiplied instead of dividing","value":11.01,"check":"(1.082*1.026-1)*100","explain":"Multiplying the factors gives a nominal rate from a real and inflation, not the reverse."}],"explanation":"Divide the nominal growth factor by the inflation factor.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10 App A1, FS p1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-593","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Foods wants a real return of 4.5% per annum on a project while inflation is expected to be 3.2% per annum. What nominal discount rate should it use, in % per annum?","answer":7.84,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(1.045*1.032-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"1 + R_nom = (1 + R_real)(1 + R_inf)","value":1.0784,"check":"1.045*1.032","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"R_nom in %","value":7.844,"check":"(1.045*1.032-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real) = 1.032 x 1.045 = 1.07844","R_nom = 7.8440% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.045 x 1.032 = 1.07844 - 1 = 7.844%"],"errors":[{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Added the two rates","value":7.7,"check":"(0.045+0.032)*100","explain":"The cross term R_real x R_inf = 0.144% is missing."},{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Divided instead of multiplying","value":1.26,"check":"(1.045/1.032-1)*100","explain":"Nominal = real compounded with inflation: multiply the factors."}],"explanation":"Fisher relationship, solved for the nominal rate.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1, L02C s8"},{"id":"fin201-q10-594","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 4-year service contract costs the firm $10,000 a year in today's dollars; prices rise with 3% inflation, so the nominal payments rise 3% a year from $10,300. The nominal discount rate is 9% per annum. Find the present value of the payments using real cash flows and the real rate.","answer":34790.17,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"10000*(1-1/(1.09/1.03)**4)/(1.09/1.03-1)","steps":[{"label":"Real discount rate in %","value":5.8252,"check":"(1.09/1.03-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Real annuity factor, 4 years","value":3.479,"check":"(1-1/(1.09/1.03)**4)/(1.09/1.03-1)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"PV of real cash flows","value":34790.17,"check":"10000*(1-1/(1.09/1.03)**4)/(1.09/1.03-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Check: PV of nominal cash flows at 9%","value":34790.17,"check":"10000*1.03/1.09+10000*1.03**2/1.09**2+10000*1.03**3/1.09**3+10000*1.03**4/1.09**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["R_real = 1.09/1.03 - 1 = 5.8252%","Real annuity factor = (1 - 1/1.058252^4)/0.058252 = 3.4790","PV = 10,000 x 3.4790 = $34,790.17","Check: nominal flows 10,300; 10,609; 10,927.27; 11,255.09 discounted at 9% = $34,790.17"],"keystrokes":["Real rate 5.8252%: N = 4, I/Y = 5.8252, PMT = 10000, CPT PV","Check with CF0 = 0, C01 = 10300, C02 = 10609, C03 = 10927.27, C04 = 11255.09, I = 9, NPV"],"errors":[{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Discounted the real flows at the nominal 9%","value":32397.2,"check":"10000*(1-1/1.09**4)/0.09","explain":"Real flows must be discounted at the real rate."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Used 9% - 3% = 6% as the real rate","value":34651.06,"check":"10000*(1-1/1.06**4)/0.06","explain":"The real rate is 1.09/1.03 - 1 = 5.8252%, slightly lower than 6%."}],"explanation":"Two consistent routes: real flows at the real rate, or nominal flows at the nominal rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s10-11, SM 10-40"},{"id":"fin201-q10-595","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A supply contract pays $250,000 in nominal dollars at the end of next year, then rises with inflation of 2.5% per annum forever. The nominal discount rate is 9.5% per annum. Find its present value by converting to real cash flows and discounting at the real rate.","answer":3571428.57,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(250000/1.025)/(1.095/1.025-1)","steps":[{"label":"Real cash flow each year (constant)","value":243902.44,"check":"250000/1.025","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Real discount rate in %","value":6.8293,"check":"(1.095/1.025-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"PV of the real perpetuity","value":3571428.57,"check":"(250000/1.025)/(1.095/1.025-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Check: nominal growing perpetuity D1/(R - g)","value":3571428.57,"check":"250000/(0.095-0.025)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Year-1 nominal 250,000 = 250,000/1.025 = 243,902.44 in today's dollars; real flows stay constant","R_real = 1.095/1.025 - 1 = 6.8293%","PV = 243,902.44 / 0.068293 = $3,571,428.57","Check: 250,000/(0.095 - 0.025) = $3,571,428.57"],"keystrokes":["243902.44 / 0.068293 = 3571428.57"],"errors":[{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Discounted the real perpetuity at the nominal rate","value":2567394.09,"check":"(250000/1.025)/0.095","explain":"Real flows need the real rate."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Used 9.5% - 2.5% = 7% as the real rate","value":3484320.56,"check":"(250000/1.025)/(0.095-0.025)","explain":"The exact real rate is 6.8293%, not 7%."}],"explanation":"A growing perpetuity in nominal terms is a constant perpetuity in real terms.","source":"generated","ref":"L02C s11, SM 10-40"},{"id":"fin201-q10-596","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 3-year machine has a PV of costs of $9,250, found with nominal cash flows at a nominal rate of 12% per annum. Inflation is 2.5% per annum. What is its equivalent annual cost in real (today's) dollars, using the real discount rate?","answer":3671.74,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"9250/((1-1/(1.12/1.025)**3)/(1.12/1.025-1))","steps":[{"label":"Real discount rate in %","value":9.2683,"check":"(1.12/1.025-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Real annuity factor, 3 years","value":2.5192,"check":"(1-1/(1.12/1.025)**3)/(1.12/1.025-1)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Real EAC","value":3671.74,"check":"9250/((1-1/(1.12/1.025)**3)/(1.12/1.025-1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["R_real = 1.12/1.025 - 1 = 9.2683%","Real annuity factor = 2.5192","Real EAC = 9,250 / 2.5192 = $3,671.74","For comparison the nominal EAC is 9,250/2.4018 = $3,851.23"],"keystrokes":["N = 3, I/Y = 9.2683, PV = -9250, CPT PMT"],"errors":[{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Used the nominal annuity factor","value":3851.23,"check":"9250/((1-1/1.12**3)/0.12)","explain":"That is the nominal EAC. A real EAC uses the real rate in the annuity factor."},{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Used 12% - 2.5% = 9.5% as the real rate","value":3686.86,"check":"9250/((1-1/1.095**3)/0.095)","explain":"Use (1.12/1.025) - 1."}],"explanation":"The PV is the same either way, but dividing by the real annuity factor gives the cost in constant dollars.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q10-597","concept":"fin201-c10-nominal-real","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A machine will be sold for $50,000 in nominal dollars at the end of year 5. Inflation is 2.5% per annum. What is the sale price in today's dollars (real terms)?","answer":44192.71,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"50000/1.025**5","steps":[{"label":"Inflation factor over 5 years","value":1.1314,"check":"1.025**5","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Real value","value":44192.71,"check":"50000/1.025**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["CF_real = CF_nominal/(1 + R_inf)^5 = 50,000/1.025^5","= 50,000/1.131408 = $44,192.71"],"keystrokes":["1.025 y^x 5 = 1.131408","50000 / 1.131408 = 44192.71"],"errors":[{"id":"arithmetic","label":"Multiplied by the inflation factor","value":56570.41,"check":"50000*1.025**5","explain":"Today's dollars are worth more per dollar: divide, don't multiply."},{"id":"annual-periodic","label":"Used simple inflation of 12.5%","value":44444.44,"check":"50000/(1+5*0.025)","explain":"Inflation compounds: use (1 + 0.025)^5."}],"explanation":"Deflate a future nominal amount by the cumulative inflation.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1, L02C s10"}],"color":"#B5623A","short":"Cash flows","tag":"What counts, CCA and salvage","province":"Dacia","numeral":"X"},{"chapter":11,"title":"Project analysis and real options","topics":[{"id":"fin201-t11-1","order":1,"title":"Forecasting risk and what-if analysis","summary":"Every NPV rests on forecasts, and forecasts can be wrong. This topic gives you four tools for seeing how much a forecasting error could hurt: sensitivity analysis (one input at a time), scenario analysis (several inputs together), simulation (random inputs, look at the whole output distribution) and NPV break-even (how far an input can move before NPV hits zero).","concepts":["fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","fin201-c11-sensitivity","fin201-c11-scenario","fin201-c11-simulation","fin201-c11-npv-breakeven"]},{"id":"fin201-t11-2","order":2,"title":"Break-even points and operating leverage","summary":"Break-even asks how many units we must sell. Cash break-even covers cash costs, accounting break-even also covers depreciation, and financial break-even also earns the required return (NPV = 0), so they always come in that order. Operating leverage then shows how strongly operating cash flow reacts to a change in sales volume.","concepts":["fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","fin201-c11-dol"]},{"id":"fin201-t11-3","order":3,"title":"Real options","summary":"A real option is the right, but not the obligation, to expand, abandon, wait or change production later, once we know more. Flexibility has value, so ignoring it understates NPV. Decision trees, working from right to left with probabilities and NPVs that are already discounted, put a number on that value.","concepts":["fin201-c11-real-options","fin201-c11-decision-trees","fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","topic":"fin201-t11-1","term":"Forecasting risk (estimation risk)","definition":"The risk that we make a bad investment decision because the projected cash flows are wrong, for example accepting a project that only looks like positive NPV because the forecasts were too optimistic.","explain":"Every NPV rests on forecasts of sales, price, costs and project life, and each forecast can be wrong. The danger is greatest for a new product with no history. The first line of defence is to ask what specific source of value makes the NPV positive; the what-if tools of this chapter then show how much damage an error would do.","example":"Prairie Paws Pet Foods computes NPV = +$420,000 for a new dog biscuit from a survey-based sales forecast. If real sales come in far below the survey the true NPV is negative, even though the arithmetic was perfect: the forecast, not the formula, was wrong.","contrast":"Forecasting risk is the danger (inputs that may be wrong); sensitivity, scenario, simulation and NPV break-even are the tools that measure it. It is not the market risk (σ, β) of Chapters 12-13 and not a spreadsheet typo.","trap":"A positive NPV does not prove value creation. Positive-NPV projects are rare in competitive markets, so a large NPV with no stated source of value is a warning sign. Cue words: new product, optimistic forecast, rivals will respond.","mnemonic":"Garbage in, garbage out: NPV is only as good as the forecast.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","topic":"fin201-t11-1","term":"Sensitivity analysis","definition":"Change one input at a time, hold every other input at its base-case value, recompute the NPV and measure ΔNPV/Δinput. With N inputs this takes 2N + 1 full DCF runs: one base case plus one up-run and one down-run per input.","explain":"It shows which single forecast NPV is most sensitive to, so we know where forecasting errors do the most damage. A steep ΔNPV/Δinput means a small error moves NPV a lot. NPV is a straight line in price, quantity and unit costs, so the slope is the same whichever second value you test. It points to the problem but gives no accept or reject rule.","example":"Boreal Coffee Roasters: line costs $360,000, 6 years, P = $14.00, v = $8.50, Q = 52,000, FC = $140,000, D = $60,000, T = 30%, R = 11%. OCF = [(14 − 8.5)(52,000) − 140,000](0.7) + 0.3(60,000) = $120,200.00 and NPV = $148,510.65. At P = $15 OCF = $156,600.00 and NPV = $302,502.23, so ΔNPV/ΔP = $153,991.58 per $1.","contrast":"Sensitivity flexes ONE input over a broad range (2N + 1 runs); scenario flexes SEVERAL inputs together (3 runs for base, best, worst); simulation draws all inputs at random; NPV break-even solves for the input value where NPV = 0.","trap":"ΔNPV needs the annuity factor (multiply the ΔOCF by it); price, quantity and cost changes are taxed, so use (1 − T), but the CCA shield T·D does not change. Compare inputs on the same percentage change, because a per-unit slope depends on the units.","mnemonic":"One at a time: 2N + 1.","formula":"ΔNPV/Δx = (NPV_new − NPV_base)/(x_new − x_base); number of runs = 2N + 1","given":false,"archetypes":["A20"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"The four what-if tools side by side","head":["Tool","What changes","Runs","What you learn","Limit"],"rows":[["Sensitivity","one input at a time, others at base","2N + 1","which input NPV reacts to most (ΔNPV/Δinput)","no decision rule; inputs do not really move alone"],["Scenario","several inputs together (base, best, worst)","3, one per case","NPV in sensible cases and the size of the downside; not linear","few points; no probabilities unless added"],["Simulation","all inputs at random from distributions","thousands","the whole NPV distribution (spread, skew, chance of NPV < 0)","needs distributions; no simple decision rule"],["NPV break-even","one input, solved for NPV = 0","one equation","how far the input can move before the decision flips","one input only; says nothing about likelihood"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c11-scenario","topic":"fin201-t11-1","term":"Scenario analysis","definition":"Change several inputs together, in a sensible and consistent way, to build a few cases (typically base, best and worst) and compute the NPV of each. The results are not linear in the input changes because fixed costs do not scale with volume.","explain":"Best case: price and quantity up, variable and fixed costs down. Worst case: the reverse. It shows what can happen and how bad the downside is, but not how likely each case is: to get an expected NPV you must add probabilities (E[NPV] = Σ p × NPV). More and more scenarios only add possibilities, not a decision rule.","example":"Okotoks Trailer Works (outlay $900,000, 4 years, D = $225,000, T = 30%, R = 12%): base P = $2,400, v = $1,350, Q = 1,200, FC = $620,000 gives NPV = $665,753.59. Worst case (P and Q −10%, v and FC +10%): OCF = $100,400.00, NPV = −$595,050.13. Best case (P and Q +10%, v and FC −10%): NPV = $2,117,910.31.","contrast":"Scenario = a few hand-picked consistent combinations; sensitivity = one input at a time; simulation = thousands of random combinations. Scenarios give a handful of NPVs, simulation gives a distribution.","trap":"Best case means EVERY input moves the favourable way, so costs go DOWN; moving a cost the wrong way is the classic slip. A 10% favourable move in every input does not change NPV by 10%. Probabilities must sum to 1 before you weight the NPVs.","mnemonic":"Best = revenues up, costs down. Worst = the reverse.","formula":"E[NPV] = Σ p_s × NPV_s (Σ p_s = 1); OCF = [(P − v)Q − FC](1 − T) + T·D","given":false,"archetypes":["A20"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-simulation","topic":"fin201-t11-1","term":"Simulation analysis","definition":"Let a computer draw every uncertain input at random from a chosen probability distribution, compute the NPV for each draw, repeat thousands of times and study the distribution of NPV (mean, spread, chance of NPV < 0), not only its expected value.","explain":"It combines scenario analysis (all inputs change) with sensitivity analysis (each input takes many values). The shape of the output matters: it will probably not be symmetrical and the baseline or expected value may not sit at the peak. It needs a distribution for every input and a view on how inputs move together, and it gives no simple decision rule.","example":"A rail terminal has eight uncertain inputs. The analyst assigns a distribution to each, runs 10,000 trials and finds a mean NPV of +$310,000 but a negative NPV in 35% of the trials. The mean alone would have hidden that chance of loss.","contrast":"Simulation is not scenario analysis (a few hand-picked cases) and not sensitivity analysis (one input at a time). Unlike NPV break-even it does not solve for a threshold: it describes the whole range of outcomes.","trap":"Do not answer \"the average NPV\" when the question asks about risk: the answer is the distribution. The simulated mean need not equal the base-case NPV. Independent draws can create unrealistic combinations if the inputs are related.","mnemonic":"Random in, whole distribution out.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","topic":"fin201-t11-1","term":"NPV break-even analysis","definition":"The level of one input (price, quantity, EBITDA, variable cost) at which NPV = 0, all other inputs at base: how far the input can change before the decision to accept would change. It is the finance break-even, not the accounting (profit = 0) break-even.","explain":"Write NPV = 0, replace the unknown input by a symbol and solve. Use an annuity factor for finite flows, a perpetuity for flows that never end, and include the CCA tax shield. Because EBITDA is pre-tax, the after-tax amount needed must be divided by (1 − T). At the break-even level IRR = R.","example":"Lecture plant: cost $1,000,000, CCA straight-line over 8 years (simplified), income forever, R = 10%, T = 30%. DTS = 0.3 × 1,000,000 ÷ 8 = $37,500 a year, PV = 37,500 × 5.334926 = $200,059.73. NPV = −1,000,000 + 200,059.73 + (0.7 ÷ 0.10) × EBITDA = 0, so EBITDA = $114,277.18.","contrast":"Sensitivity, scenario and simulation show how NPV moves; break-even asks where it hits zero. NPV (financial) break-even is higher than accounting break-even, because accounting profit ignores the required return.","trap":"Gross the answer up for tax (÷(1 − T)), divide by an annuity factor and not by N, keep the CCA shield in the equation, and use a perpetuity when the income is perpetual. Giving the accounting (profit) break-even is wrong.","mnemonic":"Set NPV = 0, solve for the one thing.","formula":"NPV = 0 ⇒ EBITDA* = [I0 − PV(DTS)] ÷ [(1 − T) × annuity factor (or 1/R for a perpetuity)]","given":false,"archetypes":["A20"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","topic":"fin201-t11-2","term":"Cash break-even","definition":"The sales quantity at which operating cash flow is zero: Q_C = FC/(P − v). Depreciation is non-cash, so it is not part of the cash break-even.","explain":"Each unit contributes P − v toward fixed cash costs. Below Q_C the project does not even cover its cash costs. At Q_C, OCF = 0, so the investment is never recovered: NPV = −I0, IRR = −100% and there is no payback. It is the lowest of the three break-evens.","example":"P = $24, v = $15, FC = $90,000: contribution = $9 per unit, so Q_C = 90,000 ÷ 9 = 10,000 units.","contrast":"Cash break-even leaves out depreciation; accounting break-even adds it (Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)); financial break-even adds the required return. Order: Q_C < Q_A < Q_F.","trap":"Do not add D to the cash break-even. The simple formula ignores tax; with taxes, setting OCF = 0 in Q = {FC + (OCF − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v) can give a slightly lower figure because the CCA shield helps when income is negative.","mnemonic":"Cash first: cover the cash costs.","formula":"Q_C = FC/(P − v)","given":false,"archetypes":["A21"],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","topic":"fin201-t11-2","term":"Accounting break-even","definition":"The sales quantity at which net income is zero: Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v). At Q_A, OCF = D and no tax is paid, so the tax rate drops out of the formula.","explain":"Each unit must cover variable cost, fixed cost and depreciation. Because profit is zero there, tax is zero, and Q_A is the same with or without taxes. At Q_A the project just recovers its investment over its life: IRR = 0%, payback = N years and NPV = I0[(1/N) × annuity factor − 1], which is negative.","example":"Same project, with a $225,000 outlay depreciated over 5 years (D = $45,000): Q_A = (90,000 + 45,000) ÷ 9 = 15,000 units. At R = 10% the NPV at Q_A is 225,000 × (3.790787 ÷ 5 − 1) = −$54,414.60.","contrast":"Accounting break-even makes profit zero (NI = 0); NPV or financial break-even makes NPV zero. Accounting is higher than cash (it adds D) and lower than financial (it ignores the required return).","trap":"Forgetting D in the numerator, grossing up for tax (not needed at Q_A), or treating Q_A as the point where the project creates value. At Q_A, OCF = D, so (FC + OCF)/(P − v) gives the same number.","mnemonic":"Accounting = cash + depreciation.","formula":"Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)","given":false,"archetypes":["A21"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","topic":"fin201-t11-2","term":"Financial break-even","definition":"The sales quantity at which NPV = 0: Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v), where OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor(R, N) is the constant OCF that just repays the investment at the required return. With taxes, Q_F = {FC + (OCF* − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v). At Q_F, IRR = R.","explain":"It is the break-even that matters to shareholders: a project can clear the cash and accounting break-evens and still sit below Q_F and lose value. First find OCF* from the annuity factor, then convert it to the quantity that produces it.","example":"Same project (I0 = $225,000, 5 years, R = 10%, FC = $90,000, P − v = $9): OCF* = 225,000 ÷ 3.790787 = $59,354.43. Ignoring tax Q_F = (90,000 + 59,354.43) ÷ 9 = 16,594.94 units. With T = 30% and D = $45,000: Q_F = {90,000 + (59,354.43 − 13,500) ÷ 0.7} ÷ 9 = 17,278.48 units.","contrast":"Q_C < Q_A < Q_F. OCF* uses the annuity factor (time value of money), whereas I0/N is the depreciation and leads back to the accounting break-even.","trap":"Using I0/N instead of the annuity factor; forgetting the T·D and ÷(1 − T) steps when taxes are present; assuming that a project above the accounting break-even must have positive NPV.","mnemonic":"Cash < Accounting < Financial.","formula":"OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor(R, N); Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v); with taxes Q = {FC + (OCF* − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v)","given":false,"archetypes":["A21"],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Cash vs accounting vs financial break-even","head":["Break-even","Formula","OCF at that level","NPV","IRR","Payback"],"rows":[["Cash","Q_C = FC/(P − v)","0","negative: −I0 (the whole outlay is lost)","−100%","never (cash flow is zero)"],["Accounting","Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)","D (net income = 0)","negative: I0[(1/N) × annuity factor − 1]","0%","N years (the project's life)"],["Financial","Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v), OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor","OCF* (the level that repays I0 at R)","zero","R (the required return)","less than N years"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c11-dol","topic":"fin201-t11-2","term":"Degree of operating leverage (DOL)","definition":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF = %ΔOCF ÷ %ΔQ: the percentage change in operating cash flow for a 1% change in quantity sold. At the accounting break-even, OCF = D, so DOL = 1 + FC/D.","explain":"The more of the cost base is fixed, the more OCF swings when sales move, so high fixed costs mean high operating leverage and high forecasting risk. DOL is not constant: it falls as quantity rises (OCF grows) and rises as quantity falls.","example":"Ignoring tax with P − v = $9 and FC = $90,000: at Q = 20,000 OCF = 90,000, so DOL = 1 + 90,000 ÷ 90,000 = 2.0, and a 10% rise in Q (to 22,000) raises OCF by 20% (to 108,000). At Q_A = 15,000, OCF = D = 45,000 and DOL = 1 + 90,000 ÷ 45,000 = 3.0.","contrast":"DOL is a ratio of percentage changes, evaluated at one quantity; do not mix it up with the break-even quantities. Fixed costs are FC, without D, in the formula.","trap":"Putting FC + D in the numerator, using the wrong OCF (it must be the OCF at the current quantity), or applying the old DOL after quantity has changed. The solutions manual derives a tax-adjusted form, but the course formula is the simple one.","mnemonic":"More fixed cost per dollar of OCF, more leverage.","formula":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF; %ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ; DOL at Q_A = 1 + FC/D","given":false,"archetypes":["A21"],"weight":1,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-real-options","topic":"fin201-t11-3","term":"Real option","definition":"An option is the right, but not the obligation, to do something in the future, decided later with information not available today. A financial option is a right to buy or sell a financial asset; a real option is a right to undertake a business initiative (expand, abandon, wait, change production). We usually pay a premium up front for it.","explain":"We assume that when the decision time arrives we will make the best financial choice with the information then available. Because the right can be dropped when it is not worth using, an option never has negative value, so an NPV that ignores flexibility understates a project. Option pricing formulas are not examined here.","example":"Building the freeway bridges longer than needed costs extra now (the premium) but keeps the right to widen the road cheaply later if traffic is heavy. Car insurance is the lecture's everyday example of a put option.","contrast":"Option (right, free to walk away) vs obligation or forward (must act). Real option (business activity) vs financial option (security). Expansion, abandonment, timing and production flexibility are different kinds of real option.","trap":"Don't treat the project as a now-or-never choice when the firm can adapt later. The premium is paid up front and must be compared with the value of the option. Match the example to the type: bigger machine or extra space = expansion; early exit = abandonment.","mnemonic":"EATP: Expansion, Abandonment, Timing, Production flexibility.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Main types of real option","head":["Type","Idea","Example"],"rows":[["Expansion","pay now for the right to grow later","buy a larger machine or extra space; build longer bridges so the freeway can be widened"],["Abandonment","exit early if the project performs badly","shut down and sell the assets"],["Timing","choose the best moment to act","pick the best time to replace a machine"],["Production flexibility","change the rate or type of production","switch output as the market changes"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","topic":"fin201-t11-3","term":"Decision trees","definition":"A diagram of today's choices, uncertain events with their probabilities and later decisions. Roll it back from right to left: at a decision node keep the best branch, at a chance node take the probability-weighted average E = Σ p × NPV, and at today's node compare the expected values including up-front costs.","explain":"If the amounts on the tree are already NPVs (present values), no further discounting is needed. Choose at each later decision node before averaging at the earlier chance node, because we will pick the best action when we get there.","example":"Freeway bridges (lecture numbers in $M, all already NPVs; busy road 80%, quiet 20%). Short bridges cost 120. If the road is busy, widening means knocking them down (20), building long bridges (160) and widening the surface (40) = 220, worse than the 150 cost to society of not widening, so we do not widen: 120 + 150 = 270; if quiet, 120. Expected cost = 0.8 × 270 + 0.2 × 120 = 240. Long bridges cost 160: if busy, widen for 40 (better than 150), total 200; if quiet, 160. Expected cost = 0.8 × 200 + 0.2 × 160 = 192. Build long bridges (192 < 240): the 40 premium saves 150 − 40 = 110 in the busy state, worth 0.8 × 110 = 88.","contrast":"Decision node (we choose, take the best branch) vs chance node (nature chooses, take the weighted average). A tree with NPVs on it is not discounted again.","trap":"Averaging before deciding at a later node, forgetting to include the extra up-front cost of the flexible choice, discounting amounts that are already present values, or mixing costs (lower is better) with benefits (higher is better).","mnemonic":"Roll back: decide at squares, average at circles.","formula":"E = Σ p_i × NPV_i (chance node); value of a decision node = best branch","given":false,"archetypes":["A22"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","topic":"fin201-t11-3","term":"Abandonment and expansion options","definition":"The abandonment option is the right to shut down or sell a project's assets if it does badly: abandon when the abandonment (salvage or resale) value exceeds the PV of continuing. The expansion option is the right to scale up if it does well; its value is the probability of that outcome times the PV of the extra gain, discounted to today.","explain":"Abandonment limits the losses, so ignoring it underestimates NPV. The abandonment value is also the opportunity cost of continuing. Expansion adds value only in the good state, so weight it by the probability of that state and discount it back.","example":"A project's year-1 value is $9.0M if demand is high (50%) and only $1.5M if low (50%), but the assets can be sold for $2.4M. With the option: 0.5 × 9.0 + 0.5 × 2.4 = $5.70M; without: 0.5 × 9.0 + 0.5 × 1.5 = $5.25M. The option is worth $0.45M at year 1, or 0.45 ÷ 1.12 = $0.40M today at R = 12%.","contrast":"Abandonment helps in the bad state (floor on value); expansion helps in the good state (upside). Both are real options, and both raise NPV above the static value.","trap":"Compare the abandonment value with the PV of continuing at that date; do not average before deciding. Subtract the extra up-front cost of buying the flexibility, and discount an option value that arises in a later year.","mnemonic":"Abandon when continuing is worth less than selling.","formula":"Abandon if salvage > PV(continuing); option value = value with option − value without option; expansion value = p × gain ÷ (1 + R)","given":false,"archetypes":["A22"],"weight":2,"figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"fin201-a11-01","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","title":"Order the steps of a sensitivity analysis","prompt":"Put the steps of a sensitivity analysis in the correct order.","items":["Compute the base-case NPV with every input at its expected value","Choose one input and change it, holding all other inputs at base values","Recompute the full DCF (OCF, then NPV) with the new input value","Form ΔNPV/Δinput = (NPV_new − NPV_base)/(input_new − input_base)","Repeat for every input and compare which slope is steepest"],"explanation":"Sensitivity analysis flexes one input at a time around the base case. The slope ΔNPV/Δinput is computed for each input in turn, and the steepest slope marks the forecast whose error does the most damage. With N inputs this is 2N + 1 runs."},{"id":"fin201-a11-02","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","title":"Sort the what-if techniques","prompt":"Drag each description to the technique it describes.","buckets":["Sensitivity analysis","Scenario analysis","Simulation analysis","NPV break-even"],"items":[{"text":"Changes only the unit price and recomputes NPV","bucket":0},{"text":"Needs 2N + 1 full DCF runs for N inputs","bucket":0},{"text":"Gives ΔNPV/Δinput for each input in turn","bucket":0},{"text":"Best, base and worst cases","bucket":1},{"text":"Sales up 10% and costs up 8% together as one sensible story","bucket":1},{"text":"Results are not linear because fixed costs do not scale with volume","bucket":1},{"text":"Draws every input at random from a chosen distribution","bucket":2},{"text":"Repeats thousands of times and studies the shape of the NPV output","bucket":2},{"text":"The expected value may not sit at the peak of the output distribution","bucket":2},{"text":"Solves NPV = 0 for one input","bucket":3},{"text":"Answers how far the price can fall before we would reject the project","bucket":3},{"text":"The finance counterpart of the accounting (profit) break-even","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Sensitivity = one input at a time. Scenario = several inputs together in a consistent story. Simulation = random draws and the whole output distribution. NPV break-even = solve for the input that makes NPV zero."},{"id":"fin201-a11-03","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","title":"Match the real option to its example","prompt":"Match each kind of real option with the example that illustrates it.","pairs":[{"left":"Expansion option","right":"Building longer bridges now so the freeway can be widened cheaply later"},{"left":"Abandonment option","right":"Shutting down a poor project early and selling its assets"},{"left":"Timing option","right":"Choosing the best moment to replace a machine"},{"left":"Production flexibility","right":"Changing the rate or type of output as the market changes"},{"left":"Option premium","right":"The amount paid up front for the right, like the premium on a car insurance policy"},{"left":"Real option","right":"A right to undertake a business initiative in the future"}],"explanation":"The lecture's four main types are expansion, abandonment, timing and production flexibility. The premium is paid up front for the right, and a real option concerns a business activity rather than a security."},{"id":"fin201-a11-04","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","title":"Match the break-even to its formula","prompt":"Match each term with its formula or meaning.","pairs":[{"left":"Cash break-even (OCF = 0)","right":"Q_C = FC/(P − v)"},{"left":"Accounting break-even (net income = 0)","right":"Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)"},{"left":"Financial break-even (NPV = 0)","right":"Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v)"},{"left":"OCF*","right":"I0 ÷ annuity factor(R, N)"},{"left":"Degree of operating leverage","right":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF"}],"explanation":"Each break-even adds one layer: cash costs, then depreciation, then the required return. OCF* is the level of operating cash flow that repays the outlay at the required return, and DOL measures how strongly OCF reacts to a change in quantity."},{"id":"fin201-a11-05","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","title":"Roll back a decision tree","prompt":"Put the steps for solving a decision tree in the correct order.","items":["Lay out today's choices, the uncertain events with their probabilities and the later decisions","Write the NPV (already discounted) at the end of every branch","Start at the far right and, at each later decision node, keep the better branch","At each chance node take the probability-weighted average of the values to its right","At today's decision node compare the expected values, including the up-front costs, and choose"],"explanation":"A tree is solved from right to left. We decide at the later nodes first (we will pick the best action when we get there) and only then average over the uncertain events. Averaging before deciding is the classic error."}],"formulas":[{"id":"fin201-f11-ocf","name":"Operating cash flow (tax shield form)","formula":"OCF = [(P − v)Q − FC](1 − T) + T·D","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-sensitivity","fin201-c11-scenario","fin201-c11-npv-breakeven"],"when":"Recompute the yearly cash flow each time an input in a what-if analysis changes.","trap":"The CCA shield T·D does not change when price, quantity or costs change; interest never appears."},{"id":"fin201-f11-sens-slope","name":"Sensitivity of NPV to an input","formula":"ΔNPV/Δx = (NPV_new − NPV_base)/(x_new − x_base)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-sensitivity"],"when":"Measure how much NPV moves per unit change in one input, all others at base.","trap":"Use NPV, not OCF (multiply a ΔOCF by the annuity factor), and compare inputs on equal percentage changes."},{"id":"fin201-f11-sens-runs","name":"Runs in a sensitivity analysis","formula":"number of DCF runs = 2N + 1","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-sensitivity","fin201-c11-scenario"],"when":"Count the work for N uncertain inputs (base + up + down for each).","trap":"A scenario analysis needs only one run per case (3 for base, best, worst), however many inputs there are."},{"id":"fin201-f11-expected-npv","name":"Expected NPV across scenarios","formula":"E[NPV] = Σ p_s × NPV_s","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-scenario","fin201-c11-decision-trees"],"when":"Weight scenario or branch NPVs by their probabilities.","trap":"Probabilities must sum to 1; a simple average is only right for equally likely cases."},{"id":"fin201-f11-annuity","name":"Annuity factor","formula":"AF(R, N) = [1 − (1 + R)^−N] ÷ R","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","fin201-c11-financial-breakeven"],"when":"Value a level cash flow for N years, e.g. the DTS or the required OCF*.","trap":"Use the project's N (not N − 1) and the same period for R."},{"id":"fin201-f11-perpetuity","name":"Perpetuity","formula":"PV = C ÷ R","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c11-npv-breakeven"],"when":"Value income that continues forever.","trap":"After-tax income is (1 − T) × EBITDA; the perpetuity is not a growing one unless told."},{"id":"fin201-f11-npv-be","name":"NPV break-even (plant with DTS annuity and perpetual income)","formula":"−I0 + DTS × AF(R, N) + (1 − T) × EBITDA ÷ R = 0, so EBITDA* = [I0 − DTS × AF(R, N)] × R ÷ (1 − T)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-npv-breakeven"],"when":"Find the pre-tax income that makes NPV exactly zero.","trap":"Divide by (1 − T) to get pre-tax, and use the annuity factor for the shield, not 1/R."},{"id":"fin201-f11-pvcca","name":"PV of the CCA tax shield","formula":"PV_CCA = (I0 d T_C)/(k + d) × {1, (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k), (1.5k + 1)/(1 + k)} − (S_N d T_C)/(k + d) × 1/(1 + k)^N","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c11-npv-breakeven"],"when":"Put the whole tax shield into one PV inside a break-even equation.","trap":"Choose the right first-year factor (half-year rule: (1 + 0.5k)/(1 + k)) and discount the salvage term N years."},{"id":"fin201-f11-qc","name":"Cash break-even quantity","formula":"Q_C = FC/(P − v)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-cash-breakeven"],"when":"Sales level at which operating cash flow is zero.","trap":"Depreciation is not included."},{"id":"fin201-f11-qa","name":"Accounting break-even quantity","formula":"Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven"],"when":"Sales level at which net income is zero.","trap":"Tax drops out because profit is zero; at Q_A, OCF = D."},{"id":"fin201-f11-ocfstar","name":"Financial break-even OCF","formula":"OCF* = I0 ÷ AF(R, N)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-financial-breakeven"],"when":"Constant OCF that gives NPV = 0.","trap":"I0/N is just the depreciation and leads back to the accounting break-even."},{"id":"fin201-f11-qf","name":"Financial break-even quantity","formula":"Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-financial-breakeven"],"when":"Sales level at which NPV = 0 (no taxes).","trap":"Order: Q_C < Q_A < Q_F."},{"id":"fin201-f11-qf-tax","name":"Break-even quantity with taxes","formula":"Q = {FC + (OCF − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","fin201-c11-cash-breakeven"],"when":"Quantity that produces a given OCF when the firm pays tax (OCF = OCF* for financial break-even).","trap":"Convert the OCF target to a pre-tax amount: subtract T·D first, then divide by (1 − T)."},{"id":"fin201-f11-npv-qa","name":"NPV at the accounting break-even","formula":"NPV = I0 × [(1/N) × AF(R, N) − 1]","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","fin201-c11-financial-breakeven"],"when":"Show that the accounting break-even loses value (IRR 0%, payback N years).","trap":"NPV is negative whenever R > 0."},{"id":"fin201-f11-dol","name":"Degree of operating leverage","formula":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-dol"],"when":"Measure how much OCF moves per 1% change in quantity.","trap":"Use FC without D; DOL changes with Q."},{"id":"fin201-f11-dol-pct","name":"OCF response to a change in quantity","formula":"%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-dol"],"when":"Forecast the new OCF from a percentage change in sales.","trap":"Compute the new DOL from the new OCF, not the old one."},{"id":"fin201-f11-dol-qa","name":"DOL at the accounting break-even","formula":"DOL = 1 + FC/D","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-dol","fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven"],"when":"Leverage at the point where net income is zero (OCF = D).","trap":"Only valid at Q_A."},{"id":"fin201-f11-abandon","name":"Abandonment rule and option value","formula":"Abandon if salvage > PV(continuing); option value = value with option − value without option","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","fin201-c11-real-options"],"when":"Decide whether to shut a project down and value the flexibility.","trap":"Compare at the decision date and discount the option value back to today."},{"id":"fin201-f11-expansion","name":"Value of an expansion option","formula":"value = p(success) × gain ÷ (1 + R)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion"],"when":"Value the right to expand when the good state has probability p.","trap":"The gain arises only in the good state and lies in a later year, so discount it."}],"questions":[{"id":"fin201-q11-001","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In project analysis, forecasting risk (also called estimation risk) is best described as:","options":["the possibility of a bad decision because the projected cash flows contain errors","the variability of the project's returns relative to the market portfolio","the chance that the firm cannot raise enough capital to build the project","the risk that the arithmetic inside the NPV spreadsheet contains a typing mistake"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forecasting risk is the danger that errors in the projected cash flows lead to the wrong accept or reject decision. It is about wrong inputs, not about market-wide return variability (that is the σ and β of Chapters 12-13) and not about spreadsheet typos.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-002","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Paws Pet Foods reports an NPV of +$420,000 for a new organic dog biscuit. The forecast rests on one customer survey in which shoppers said they would \"definitely try\" the product, and the main worry is that real sales will fall far short of the survey. This worry is best called:","options":["liquidity risk","forecasting risk","interest-rate risk","the risk of an unsystematic event"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The worry is that projected cash flows (sales) are wrong and a poor decision follows: forecasting risk. Liquidity and interest-rate risk are about the firm's funding and rate exposure, not about the accuracy of the sales forecast.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1; SM 11 CTQ 1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-003","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"Why is forecasting risk usually greatest for a brand-new product compared with replacing a worn-out machine?","options":["new products are always taxed at a higher rate, which distorts the cash flows","replacement decisions have no cash flows to forecast, so the NPV rule cannot be applied","the NPV formula is only valid for products that already exist","there is no sales history, so unit sales and prices are much harder to predict"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A new product has no track record to anchor the sales and price forecasts, so the projected cash flows are likelier to be far off. A replacement still has cash flows, and the NPV formula applies to both.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-004","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a sensible response to forecasting risk?","options":["ask what specific source of value makes the NPV positive","run sensitivity and scenario analyses to see which forecasts matter most","examine how well supported the sales forecast is before relying on it","assume that competitors will not react if the project turns out to be profitable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Success attracts imitators, so assuming rivals will sit still builds optimism into the forecast and increases forecasting risk. The other three responses are exactly the defences the course recommends: find the source of value, run what-if analyses, and test the forecast.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-005","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement correctly separates forecasting risk from the what-if techniques of this chapter?","options":["forecasting risk is the danger of wrong inputs; sensitivity, scenario, simulation and NPV break-even measure how much damage such errors could do","forecasting risk is a technique that tells us which input to flex, and sensitivity analysis is the danger of wrong inputs","forecasting risk and scenario analysis are two names for the same thing","forecasting risk concerns cost estimates only, and the what-if techniques concern revenue estimates only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Forecasting risk is the problem (inputs that may be wrong). The what-if techniques are the tools for seeing how sensitive NPV is to those inputs. They are not synonyms and they cover revenues and costs alike.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1-11.2; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-006","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A project can show a positive NPV even though its true NPV is negative if the cash-flow forecasts were too optimistic.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. NPV is only as good as the cash flows fed into it. Over-optimistic forecasts make a project look better than it is, which is exactly what forecasting risk means.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-007","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Once the NPV is calculated with the correct formula, forecasting risk has been eliminated.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. A correct formula applied to wrong inputs still gives a wrong answer. Forecasting risk lives in the inputs, so it is managed with source-of-value questions and what-if analysis, not removed by arithmetic.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-008","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The possibility that errors in projected cash flows lead to a wrong accept or reject decision is called _____ risk.","options":["systematic","liquidity","forecasting","default"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forecasting risk (estimation risk) is the term for the danger that errors in projected cash flows cause a bad decision. Systematic risk is the market-wide part of a stock's risk (Chapter 13).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1; REV p1-2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-009","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Great Plains Coffee Kiosks calculates NPV = +$150,000 for a kiosk in a mall that already has three established rivals. The analyst cannot name any cost, product or location advantage. What is the best response?","options":["accept, because any NPV above zero creates value no matter how the forecast was reached","reject, because competition always drives the NPV of every project below zero","be suspicious: positive-NPV projects are rare when competition is stiff, so find the source of value first","raise the discount rate until the NPV falls to zero and treat the project as break-even"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Where competition is stiff, a positive NPV with no articulated edge should be viewed with suspicion. The course answer is to look for the source of value, not to accept blindly or to fudge the discount rate.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-010","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A what-if analysis shows that a new product's NPV stays positive even in the worst case the team defined. Which conclusion is the most reasonable?","options":["forecasting risk looks lower for the ranges tested, but actual outcomes can fall outside the worst case","the project is risk-free, because its worst-case NPV is positive in the analysis","the base-case NPV must have been calculated incorrectly by the team","competitors can no longer affect the project's cash flows after launch"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive worst case is reassuring only for the ranges examined. The text warns that even worst-case scenarios may not be low enough, so forecasting risk is lower, not zero.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-011","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can success itself increase forecasting risk in a competitive market?","options":["success lowers the required return, so earlier forecasts become invalid","success raises depreciation, which changes the cash flows","success raises the tax rate on the project's profits","success attracts imitators, who push volume and margins below the forecast"],"answer":3,"explanation":"If the project works, rivals will copy it and drive down volume and profit. A forecast that ignores that reaction is too optimistic, which is forecasting risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-012","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A retailer's expansion plan assumed each new store would serve 4,000 customers a week, but the stores actually serve 2,600 and two of them close. This episode illustrates:","options":["capital rationing","forecasting risk","the multiple-IRR problem","reinvestment risk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The plan failed because projected cash flows (traffic) were much higher than reality. That is forecasting risk. Rationing is about a budget limit and multiple IRRs are about sign changes, neither of which is described.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-013","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Projects X and Y both have a base-case NPV of $200,000. X's NPV turns negative if sales are 4% below forecast. Y's NPV stays positive until sales are 35% below forecast. Which statement is correct?","options":["Y has the higher forecasting risk, because it can absorb a much larger shortfall in sales","X has the higher forecasting risk for sales; sensitivity or NPV break-even analysis exposes the difference","the two have the same forecasting risk, because their base-case NPVs are both $200,000","neither project has any forecasting risk, because both base-case NPVs are positive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A small forecasting error flips X's decision, so X is far more exposed. Equal base NPVs hide this, which is why we flex the inputs instead of reading only the base case.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1-11.2; L05C s4-7"},{"id":"fin201-q11-014","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"According to the text, the first line of defence against forecasting risk is to ask:","options":["what IRR the project earns to one decimal place","how many decimal places the sales forecast should carry","what specific source of value makes this investment have a positive NPV","whether the project can be financed entirely with debt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Before trusting a positive NPV, we should be able to point to something specific (a cost edge, a better product, a niche) that creates the value. Precision of the arithmetic or the financing mix does not address forecasting risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-015","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A team reports base-case NPV = +$55,000, worst-case NPV = −$310,000 and best-case NPV = +$190,000. Which reading is fairest?","options":["accept, because a positive base-case NPV is all that matters for the decision","forecasting risk is significant: the downside exceeds the base-case gain, and the likelihood of each case matters","reject, because any negative scenario NPV means the project must fail and cannot be saved","average the three NPVs equally to −$21,666.67 and reject the project"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scenario results show what can happen, not how likely it is. A base-case gain of $55,000 against a worst-case loss of $310,000 signals high forecasting risk. An equal-weight average is wrong unless the three cases are equally likely.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2; SM 11-19"},{"id":"fin201-q11-016","concept":"fin201-c11-forecasting-risk","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a highly competitive market positive-NPV projects are common, so a large positive NPV needs no further explanation.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Positive-NPV investments are probably not common, especially under stiff competition. If we cannot name a sound economic reason for expecting an edge, a large positive NPV should be viewed with suspicion because the forecast may be too optimistic.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-017","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sensitivity analysis in project evaluation means:","options":["changing several inputs together in a consistent best-case or worst-case story","changing one input at a time, holding the others at their base values, and seeing how NPV responds","letting a computer draw every input at random from a distribution","finding the input value at which accounting profit is exactly zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sensitivity analysis flexes ONE input while every other input stays at its base-case value. Several inputs together is scenario analysis, random draws are simulation, and a zero crossing is a break-even analysis.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4; SM 11-4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-018","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A sensitivity analysis on N inputs needs 2N + 1 full DCF calculations. The \"+1\" is:","options":["the base case","the discount rate","the worst-case scenario","the summary table of results"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For each of the N inputs we run one version with the input up and one with it down (2N runs), plus one baseline run with every input at its base value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-019","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Alder Creek Hotels recomputes the NPV of a renovation four times: occupancy 5% lower, occupancy 5% higher, nightly rate 5% lower and nightly rate 5% higher. Each time every other input stays at its base value. This technique is:","options":["scenario analysis","simulation analysis","NPV break-even analysis","sensitivity analysis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"One input at a time with the rest held at base values is sensitivity analysis. Scenario analysis would move occupancy and rate together, and simulation would draw them randomly.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-020","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts sensitivity analysis with scenario analysis?","options":["sensitivity analysis flexes several inputs together, while scenario analysis flexes one input at a time","sensitivity analysis flexes one input at a time (2N + 1 runs); scenario analysis flexes several together (often just three cases)","sensitivity analysis uses random draws, while scenario analysis does not","sensitivity analysis solves for the input that sets NPV to zero, while scenario analysis does not"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sensitivity = one variable over a broad range, others frozen; scenario = all variables over a limited range, moved together in a sensible way. The distractors swap the definitions or borrow from simulation and break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4-5; SM 11 CTQ 2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-021","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does a steep ΔNPV/Δinput slope signal high forecasting risk for that input?","options":["a steep slope means the input is known with certainty, so errors cannot occur","a steep slope forces the project's IRR below zero in every scenario","a steep slope means the input affects only depreciation, not cash flow","a small error in that input produces a large change in NPV and could flip the decision"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Slope measures how much NPV moves per unit of input error. The steeper it is, the more damage a given forecasting mistake does, so that input deserves the most attention.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-022","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"For the same 10% change, a project's NPV falls by $52,000 when unit sales are lower, by $18,000 when fixed costs are higher and by $96,000 when price is lower. Which conclusion follows?","options":["NPV is most sensitive to price, so the price forecast deserves the most attention","fixed costs matter most because their NPV effect is the smallest","unit sales matter most because they are the first input listed","none of the forecasts matters because the NPV effects are all different"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With equal percentage changes, the largest NPV change marks the most sensitive input: price (−$96,000). The smallest effect belongs to fixed costs, which carry the least forecasting risk here.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-023","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about sensitivity analysis is NOT true?","options":["it holds all other inputs at their base-case values","it shows where forecasting errors do the most damage","it tells management whether to accept the project","for an input that enters OCF linearly, the NPV-versus-input graph is a straight line"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sensitivity analysis points to the inputs that matter but gives no accept or reject rule. The other statements are true; in particular, NPV is linear in price, quantity and unit costs.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-024","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A sensitivity analysis of a project with 5 uncertain inputs requires 11 full DCF calculations.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. 2N + 1 = 2(5) + 1 = 11: one base case plus one up-run and one down-run for each of the 5 inputs.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-025","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Because NPV is a straight-line function of unit sales, ΔNPV/ΔQ comes out the same whichever second sales level is tested.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. NPV changes by a constant amount per unit, so any second sales level gives the same slope. The solutions manual notes that the choice of 70,000 units does not matter.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-5, 11-17"},{"id":"fin201-q11-026","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"An analyst finds that NPV is far more sensitive to unit sales than to fixed costs. What is the most useful next step?","options":["ignore unit sales, because they are too uncertain to forecast","drop fixed costs from the model and recompute the NPV","spend effort, such as market research, to firm up the unit-sales forecast","replace the NPV rule with the payback rule for this project"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sensitivity analysis tells us where errors do the most damage; the sensible response is to improve the forecast of the sensitive input.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-027","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a sensitivity analysis, what comes immediately after computing the base-case NPV?","options":["change every input to its worst value together at once","draw all inputs at random thousands of times from distributions","change one input, hold the rest at base values, and recompute the NPV","solve for the input at which NPV equals zero exactly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The sequence is: base-case NPV, flex one input and recompute, form ΔNPV/Δinput, repeat for each input, compare. Moving everything together is a scenario, and random draws are simulation.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-028","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Boreal Coffee Roasters is evaluating a $360,000 roasting line for a 6-year project with no salvage. Base case: price $14.00 per bag, variable cost $8.50 per bag, 52,000 bags per year, fixed costs $140,000 per year. The CCA tax shield is treated as straight-line, so D = $60,000 per year. T = 30% and the required return is 11% per annum. Using a second price of $15.00, find the sensitivity of NPV to price, ΔNPV/ΔP, in dollars of NPV per $1 change in price.","answer":153991.58,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((-360000+(((15-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11))-(-360000+(((14-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)))/(15-14)","steps":[{"label":"Base-case OCF at $14.00","value":120200.0,"check":"(((14-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Base-case NPV","value":148510.65,"check":"(-360000+(((14-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"OCF at $15.00","value":156600.0,"check":"(((15-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV at $15.00","value":302502.23,"check":"(-360000+(((15-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"ΔNPV/ΔP","value":153991.58,"check":"((-360000+(((15-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11))-(-360000+(((14-8.5)*52000-140000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*60000)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)))/(15-14)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["OCF = [(P − v)Q − FC](1 − T) + T·D; at $14: [(14 − 8.5)(52,000) − 140,000](0.7) + 0.3(60,000) = 120,200.00","Annuity factor (11%, 6) = 4.230538; NPV = −360,000 + 120,200.00 × 4.230538 = $148,510.65","At $15: OCF = [(15 − 8.5)(52,000) − 140,000](0.7) + 18,000 = 156,600.00; NPV = $302,502.23","ΔNPV/ΔP = ($302,502.23 − $148,510.65) ÷ (15 − 14) = $153,991.58 per $1"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 6, I/Y = 11, PMT = −1, CPT PV = 4.230538","CF worksheet: CF0 = −360000, C01 = 120,200, F01 = 6, I = 11, NPV CPT → 148,510.65","Change C01 = 156,600 → NPV = 302,502.23","(302,502.23 − 148,510.65) ÷ 1 = 153,991.58"],"errors":[{"id":"pretax-sensitivity","label":"Ignored the (1 − T) factor","value":219987.97,"check":"52000*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","explain":"A dollar of extra price is taxed: only (1 − T) of it reaches OCF. The slope is Q(1 − T) × annuity factor, not Q × annuity factor."},{"id":"ocf-not-npv","label":"Reported ΔOCF/ΔP and never applied the annuity factor","value":36400.0,"check":"52000*(1-0.3)","explain":"$36,400 is the change in each year's OCF. NPV moves by that amount in every one of the 6 years, so multiply by the annuity factor."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 5-year annuity factor","value":134530.65,"check":"52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-5)/0.11)","explain":"The project lasts 6 years, so the annuity factor must use N = 6."}],"explanation":"Raising price by $1 adds Q(1 − T) = 52,000 × 0.7 = $36,400 to OCF in each of the 6 years. Discounted at 11%, that is $36,400 × 4.230538 ≈ $153,991 of NPV per $1 of price. Rerunning the full DCF at two prices and dividing gives the same slope.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4; SM 11-4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-029","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the Boreal Coffee Roasters project the price is $14.00 and the variable cost is $8.50 per bag, fixed costs are $140,000 per year, the life is 6 years, T = 30% and the required return is 11% per annum. By how much does NPV fall if unit sales come in 6,000 bags below the 52,000-bag forecast?","answer":97725.42,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"6000*(14-8.5)*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","steps":[{"label":"ΔOCF per bag sold = (P − v)(1 − T)","value":3.85,"check":"(14-8.5)*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"ΔNPV per bag = ΔOCF per bag × annuity factor","value":16.29,"check":"(14-8.5)*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV drop for 6,000 fewer bags","value":97725.42,"check":"6000*(14-8.5)*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Each bag adds (P − v)(1 − T) = 5.50 × 0.70 = $3.85 of OCF per year","Over 6 years at 11%: $3.85 × 4.230538 = 16.2876 of NPV per bag","6,000 × 16.2876 = $97,725.42"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 6, I/Y = 11, PMT = −1, CPT PV = 4.230538","5.5 × 0.7 × 4.230538 × 6000 = 97,725.42"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Ignored the tax factor (1 − T)","value":139607.75,"check":"6000*(14-8.5)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","explain":"Extra contribution is taxed, so only (1 − T) of it reaches OCF."},{"id":"ocf-not-npv","label":"Reported the one-year OCF change","value":23100.0,"check":"6000*(14-8.5)*(1-0.3)","explain":"$23,100 is only one year's OCF loss; the shortfall repeats each year for 6 years, so discount with the annuity factor."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 5-year annuity factor","value":85375.22,"check":"6000*(14-8.5)*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-5)/0.11)","explain":"Use N = 6 for a 6-year project."}],"explanation":"Fixed costs do not depend on volume, so each lost bag costs only the contribution margin after tax: (14 − 8.5)(1 − 0.30) = $3.85 per year. Over 6 years at 11% that is 3.85 × 4.230538 = $16.29 of NPV per bag, so 6,000 bags cost about $97,725.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-5, 11-27"},{"id":"fin201-q11-030","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Boreal Coffee Roasters is evaluating a $360,000 roasting line for a 6-year project with no salvage. Base case: price $14.00 per bag, variable cost $8.50 per bag, 52,000 bags per year, fixed costs $140,000 per year. The CCA tax shield is treated as straight-line, so D = $60,000 per year. T = 30% and the required return is 11% per annum. Compare a selling price 5% below base with a variable cost 5% above base: by how much more NPV is lost under the price shock?","answer":42347.68,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(14*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)-(8.5*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","steps":[{"label":"NPV lost from a 5% price drop ($0.70 per bag)","value":107794.1,"check":"(14*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"NPV lost from a 5% variable-cost rise ($0.425 per bag)","value":65446.42,"check":"(8.5*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Difference in NPV lost","value":42347.68,"check":"(14*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)-(8.5*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Price shock: 0.05 × 14 = $0.70 per bag; ΔOCF = 0.70 × 52,000 × 0.7 = $25,480; ΔNPV = 25,480 × 4.230538 = $107,794.10","Cost shock: 0.05 × 8.5 = $0.425 per bag; ΔOCF = 0.425 × 52,000 × 0.7 = $15,470; ΔNPV = $65,446.42","Difference = $107,794.10 − $65,446.42 = $42,347.68"],"keystrokes":["N = 6, I/Y = 11, PMT = −1, CPT PV = 4.230538","0.70 × 52,000 × 0.7 × 4.230538 = 107,794.10","0.425 × 52,000 × 0.7 × 4.230538 = 65,446.42","107,794.10 − 65,446.42 = 42,347.68"],"errors":[{"id":"equal-dollar-slopes","label":"Assumed price and cost have the same NPV effect, so no difference","value":0.0,"check":"0","explain":"A $1 change in price and a $1 change in variable cost do have equal-size effects (Q(1 − T) × annuity factor), but a 5% change is $0.70 for price and only $0.425 for cost. Compare equal percentage changes."},{"id":"pretax-sensitivity","label":"Ignored the tax factor (1 − T)","value":60496.69,"check":"((14*0.05)-(8.5*0.05))*52000*((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","explain":"Both effects are after-tax; leaving out (1 − T) overstates the difference."},{"id":"ocf-not-npv","label":"Used the one-year OCF difference","value":10010.0,"check":"(14*0.05-8.5*0.05)*52000*(1-0.3)","explain":"$10,010 is the annual OCF difference; NPV counts it for all 6 years, so multiply by the annuity factor."}],"explanation":"Equal percentage changes make the comparison fair. The price shock removes $0.70 × 52,000 × 0.7 = $25,480 of OCF a year, the cost shock only $15,470, because price is the larger base number. Discounting the $10,010 annual gap at 11% for 6 years gives about $42,348.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2; SM 11-4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-031","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A project team will run a sensitivity analysis on 8 uncertain inputs: one baseline run plus one \"up\" and one \"down\" run for every input. How many full DCF calculations are needed?","answer":17.0,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"2*8+1","steps":[{"label":"Number of runs = 2N + 1","value":17.0,"check":"2*8+1","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Runs = 2N + 1 with N = 8","= 2(8) + 1 = 17"],"keystrokes":["2 × 8 + 1 = 17"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-base-case","label":"Left out the baseline run","value":16.0,"check":"2*8","explain":"Eight up-runs and eight down-runs give 16, but the base case is also a full DCF calculation."},{"id":"one-direction-only","label":"Ran only one direction per input","value":9.0,"check":"8+1","explain":"Each input is flexed both up and down, so there are 2N flexed runs, not N."},{"id":"runs-equal-n","label":"Counted one run per input","value":8.0,"check":"8","explain":"N counts the inputs, not the runs."}],"explanation":"Each of the 8 inputs gets an up-run and a down-run (16 runs), and there is one baseline: 2N + 1 = 17.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-032","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the Boreal Coffee Roasters project (52,000 bags per year, T = 30%) the variable cost per bag rises by $1.00 and nothing else changes. By how much does each year's operating cash flow change? Enter a negative number for a decrease.","answer":-36400.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-1*52000*(1-0.3)","steps":[{"label":"ΔOCF = −Δv × Q × (1 − T)","value":-36400.0,"check":"-1*52000*(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Extra variable cost = $1.00 × 52,000 = $52,000 per year (pre-tax)","After tax: −52,000 × (1 − 0.30) = −$36,400 per year"],"keystrokes":["−1 × 52000 × (1 − 0.3) = −36,400"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Ignored the tax saving on the extra cost","value":-52000.0,"check":"-1*52000","explain":"The extra cost is deductible, so only (1 − T) of it reduces cash flow."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Reported an increase","value":36400.0,"check":"1*52000*(1-0.3)","explain":"A higher variable cost lowers OCF, so the change is negative."}],"explanation":"A $1 rise in variable cost lowers pre-tax profit by $52,000; the tax shield recovers 30%, so OCF falls by $36,400 each year. Multiply by the annuity factor (4.230538) if you want the effect on NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-5c"},{"id":"fin201-q11-033","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Boreal Coffee Roasters' base-case NPV is $148,510.65 (annual OCF $120,200; 6 years; R = 11%; T = 30%). Fixed costs are re-forecast to be $15,000 per year higher, with everything else unchanged. What is the new NPV?","options":["$85,052.58","$138,010.65","$104,090.00","$192,931.30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After-tax, the extra $15,000 costs 15,000 × 0.7 = $10,500 a year. Over 6 years at 11% that is 10,500 × 4.230538 = $44,420.65, so NPV = $148,510.65 − $44,420.65 = $104,090.00. Skipping (1 − T) understates the new NPV, skipping the annuity factor treats one year's loss as the total, and a plus sign treats a cost as a gain.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-19b"},{"id":"fin201-q11-034","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A model of Calgary Aero Parts gives NPV = $210,000 when 10,000 units are sold and NPV = $273,000 when 11,500 units are sold, with every other input at its base value. What is the sensitivity ΔNPV/ΔQ?","options":["$42.00 per unit","$0.02 per unit","$5.48 per unit","$23.74 per unit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Slope = (273,000 − 210,000) ÷ (11,500 − 10,000) = 63,000 ÷ 1,500 = $42 per unit. Inverting the ratio gives $0.02, dividing the NPV change by the new quantity gives $5.48, and dividing the new NPV by the new quantity gives $23.74; none of these is a change in NPV per change in quantity.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-035","concept":"fin201-c11-sensitivity","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A sensitivity analysis will flex 9 uncertain inputs up and down around a base case. How many full DCF calculations does it need?","options":["18","10","9","19"],"answer":3,"explanation":"2N + 1 = 2(9) + 1 = 19. Eighteen forgets the base-case run, 10 counts only one direction per input, and 9 counts inputs instead of runs.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-036","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Scenario analysis in project evaluation means:","options":["changing one input at a time while all the others stay at their base values","drawing every input at random thousands of times from a distribution","solving for the single input value at which NPV equals zero","changing several inputs together in a sensible, consistent way to build base, best and worst cases"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Scenario analysis moves several inputs at once to create sensible cases (for example sales up 10% and costs up 8%), usually a base, a best and a worst case. One at a time is sensitivity, random draws are simulation, and a zero crossing is NPV break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5; SM 11 CTQ 2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-037","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Lights Cinemas builds three cases for a new multiplex. In the best case attendance and ticket price are 12% above forecast while staffing and concession costs are 8% below. The worst case is the mirror image, and the third case is the forecast itself. This technique is:","options":["scenario analysis","sensitivity analysis","simulation analysis","NPV break-even analysis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Several inputs move together in a consistent story, giving best, base and worst cases: scenario analysis. Sensitivity would move attendance alone, then price alone.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-038","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts scenario analysis with simulation?","options":["scenario analysis draws its inputs at random, while simulation uses three hand-picked cases","scenario analysis changes only one input, while simulation changes all of them at once","scenario analysis gives a distribution of NPV, while simulation gives only three NPV values","scenario analysis uses a few hand-picked consistent cases; simulation draws all inputs at random thousands of times"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A scenario analysis has a handful of named cases (typically base, best and worst), so it produces only a handful of NPVs. Simulation randomizes all inputs from distributions and produces an entire distribution.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5-6; TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-039","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a scenario analysis, why is the change in OCF not proportional to the change in sales?","options":["the discount rate rises whenever sales rise, which lowers the NPV","fixed costs do not move with volume, so cost per unit falls as units rise","depreciation is zero whenever the level of sales changes","variable costs are ignored in scenario analysis calculations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The lecture's reason: we have fixed and variable costs, and the fixed costs are spread over the number of units produced. So a 10% change in sales changes OCF by more than 10% when fixed costs are significant.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-040","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following would NOT belong in the worst-case scenario of a typical project?","options":["fixed costs lower than the base case","unit sales lower than the base case","selling price lower than the base case","variable cost per unit higher than the base case"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the worst case sales and price fall while costs (variable and fixed) rise. Lower fixed costs would be favourable, so they belong in the best case.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-041","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A scenario analysis gives NPV = +$1.8M in the best case, +$0.4M in the base case and −$1.2M in the worst case. What can we conclude?","options":["accept, because the best-case gain is bigger than the worst-case loss in dollars","the project could gain or lose a lot; scenarios show what can happen, not how likely each case is","reject, because the worst-case NPV is negative and so the project fails","the expected NPV is the simple average of the three, about +$0.33M, so accept"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scenario analysis tells us what can happen and helps gauge the potential for disaster, but it does not by itself tell us whether to take the project. To go further we need probabilities for the cases; an equal-weight average assumes they are equally likely.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2; SM 11-26"},{"id":"fin201-q11-042","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why not keep adding scenarios until every possibility is covered?","options":["each extra scenario changes the discount rate used for the others","accounting rules limit scenario analysis to three cases per project","more cases only show more possibilities and give no decision rule (paralysis of analysis)","extra scenarios always lower the base-case NPV when added"],"answer":2,"explanation":"No matter how many scenarios we run, all we learn are possibilities, some good and some bad. Beyond a handful of sensible cases the extra work adds little guidance.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-043","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a best-case scenario, price and quantity are raised while variable costs and fixed costs are lowered.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. A best case moves every input in its favourable direction: revenues up, costs down. The worst case is the reverse.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-3, 11-6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-044","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"If every input in a scenario moves 10% in the favourable direction, NPV rises by exactly 10%.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Scenario results are not linear. Fixed costs do not scale with volume and the investment outlay does not change, so a 10% favourable move in each input changes OCF and NPV by very different percentages (in the textbook example the best-case NPV is several times the base case).","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5; SM 11-6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-045","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"The base-case NPV of a project is positive and its worst-case NPV is negative. What extra information lets management compute an expected NPV across the three scenarios?","options":["the probability of each scenario","the IRR of each scenario","the payback period of the base case","the sensitivity of NPV to each input"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Expected NPV = Σ p × NPV, so each case needs a probability, and the probabilities must sum to 1. IRRs, payback and sensitivities do not weight the cases.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-26"},{"id":"fin201-q11-046","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"With probabilities p_best, p_base and p_worst (summing to 1), the expected NPV across scenarios is:","options":["(NPV_best + NPV_base + NPV_worst) ÷ 3 for any probabilities","p_best × NPV_best + p_base × NPV_base + p_worst × NPV_worst","NPV_base × (1 + the average percentage change of the inputs)","NPV_best − NPV_worst"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expected value is the probability-weighted sum of the scenario NPVs. A simple average is correct only if the three cases are equally likely, and the other two options are not expected values at all.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-26"},{"id":"fin201-q11-047","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okotoks Trailer Works plans a new trailer line costing $900,000 over 4 years, with no salvage, and treats CCA as straight-line (D = $225,000 per year). T = 30% and the required return is 12% per annum. Base case: price $2,400 and variable cost $1,350 per trailer, 1,200 trailers per year, fixed costs $620,000 per year. In the worst-case scenario, price and quantity are each 10% below base, and variable and fixed costs are each 10% above base. What is the worst-case NPV?","answer":-595050.13,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-900000+(((2160-1485)*1080-682000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","steps":[{"label":"Worst-case contribution per trailer (P − v)","value":675.0,"check":"2400*0.9-1350*1.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Worst-case EBITDA = (P − v)Q − FC","value":47000.0,"check":"(2400*0.9-1350*1.1)*1200*0.9-620000*1.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Worst-case OCF","value":100400.0,"check":"(((2160-1485)*1080-682000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Worst-case NPV","value":-595050.13,"check":"-900000+(((2160-1485)*1080-682000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Worst inputs: P = 2,400 × 0.9 = 2,160; v = 1,350 × 1.1 = 1,485; Q = 1,200 × 0.9 = 1,080; FC = 620,000 × 1.1 = 682,000","Contribution = 2,160 − 1,485 = $675; EBITDA = 675 × 1,080 − 682,000 = $47,000","OCF = 47,000 × 0.7 + 0.3 × 225,000 = 100,400.00","NPV = −900,000 + 100,400.00 × 3.037349 = −$595,050.13"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 4, I/Y = 12, PMT = −1, CPT PV = 3.037349","CF worksheet: CF0 = −900000, C01 = 100,400, F01 = 4, I = 12, NPV CPT → -595,050.13"],"errors":[{"id":"scenario-direction","label":"Lowered variable cost in the worst case","value":24933.62,"check":"-900000+(((2160-1215)*1080-682000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","explain":"In the worst case every cost moves UP. Cutting variable cost by 10% is a favourable move."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield T·D","value":-800071.21,"check":"-900000+((2160-1485)*1080-682000)*(1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","explain":"OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T·D. Dropping T·D = $67,500 a year overstates the loss."},{"id":"assumed-linear","label":"Scaled the base OCF down by 10%","value":509178.23,"check":"-900000+515500*0.9*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","explain":"The scenario changes four inputs and fixed costs do not scale, so OCF does not simply fall 10%; it collapses to $100,400."}],"explanation":"The worst case changes all four inputs the unfavourable way. Because fixed costs rise while contribution shrinks, EBITDA drops from $640,000 to $47,000 and OCF to $100,400, producing a large negative NPV of about −$595,050. The change is far from a 10% move.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-6, 11-19a"},{"id":"fin201-q11-048","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Elbow River Brewing is considering a $420,000 canning line for a 5-year project with no salvage. CCA is treated as straight-line (D = $84,000 per year), T = 25% and the required return is 9% per annum. Base case: price $31.00 and variable cost $19.00 per case, 60,000 cases per year, fixed costs $310,000. In the best-case scenario quantity is 20% above base, price 5% above, variable cost 5% below and fixed costs 10% below. What is the best-case NPV?","answer":1893370.09,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-420000+(((32.55-18.05)*72000-279000)*(1-0.25)+0.25*84000)*((1-(1+0.09)**-5)/0.09)","steps":[{"label":"Best-case price","value":32.55,"check":"31*1.05","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Best-case variable cost","value":18.05,"check":"19*0.95","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Best-case EBITDA = (P − v)Q − FC","value":765000.0,"check":"(31*1.05-19*0.95)*60000*1.2-310000*0.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Best-case OCF","value":594750.0,"check":"(((32.55-18.05)*72000-279000)*(1-0.25)+0.25*84000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Best-case NPV","value":1893370.09,"check":"-420000+(((32.55-18.05)*72000-279000)*(1-0.25)+0.25*84000)*((1-(1+0.09)**-5)/0.09)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Best inputs: P = 32.55; v = 18.05; Q = 72,000; FC = 279,000","EBITDA = (32.55 − 18.05)(72,000) − 279,000 = 1,044,000 − 279,000 = $765,000","OCF = 765,000 × 0.75 + 0.25 × 84,000 = 594,750.00","Annuity factor (9%, 5) = 3.889651; NPV = −420,000 + 594,750.00 × 3.889651 = $1,893,370.09"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 5, I/Y = 9, PMT = −1, CPT PV = 3.889651","CF worksheet: CF0 = −420000, C01 = 594,750, F01 = 5, I = 9, NPV CPT → 1,893,370.09"],"errors":[{"id":"scenario-direction","label":"Raised variable cost 5% in the best case","value":1494291.87,"check":"-420000+(((32.55-19.95)*72000-279000)*(1-0.25)+0.25*84000)*((1-(1+0.09)**-5)/0.09)","explain":"In the best case costs move DOWN. Variable cost 5% above base is an unfavourable move."},{"id":"scenario-direction","label":"Raised fixed costs 10% in the best case","value":1712501.31,"check":"-420000+(((32.55-18.05)*72000-341000)*(1-0.25)+0.25*84000)*((1-(1+0.09)**-5)/0.09)","explain":"In the best case fixed costs fall by 10%, to $279,000, not rise to $341,000."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":1811687.41,"check":"-420000+(((32.55-18.05)*72000-279000)*(1-0.25))*((1-(1+0.09)**-5)/0.09)","explain":"OCF includes T·D = 0.25 × 84,000 = $21,000 a year from the tax shield."}],"explanation":"Best case means revenues up (price and quantity) and costs down (variable and fixed). EBITDA climbs from $410,000 in the base case to $765,000, OCF to $594,750, and NPV to about $1.89 million, much more than a 10% to 20% change.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-6, 11-19a"},{"id":"fin201-q11-049","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lac Ste. Anne Outfitters values a project in three scenarios: best case NPV = $1,350,000 (probability 20%), base case NPV = $410,000 (probability 50%) and worst case NPV = −$690,000 (probability 30%). What is the project's expected NPV?","answer":268000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.2*1350000+0.5*410000+0.3*(-690000)","steps":[{"label":"Best-case contribution = 0.20 × 1,350,000","value":270000.0,"check":"0.2*1350000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Base-case contribution = 0.50 × 410,000","value":205000.0,"check":"0.5*410000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Worst-case contribution = 0.30 × (−690,000)","value":-207000.0,"check":"0.3*(-690000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Expected NPV","value":268000.0,"check":"0.2*1350000+0.5*410000+0.3*(-690000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["E[NPV] = Σ p × NPV","= 0.20(1,350,000) + 0.50(410,000) + 0.30(−690,000)","= 270,000 + 205,000 − 207,000 = $268,000.00"],"keystrokes":["0.2 × 1350000 = 270000; + 0.5 × 410000 = 475000; + 0.3 × (−690000) → 268000"],"errors":[{"id":"averaged-equal-weights","label":"Used a simple average of the three NPVs","value":356666.67,"check":"(1350000+410000-690000)/3","explain":"A simple average treats the three cases as equally likely; the probabilities here are 20%, 50% and 30%."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Added the worst-case loss instead of subtracting it","value":682000.0,"check":"0.2*1350000+0.5*410000+0.3*690000","explain":"The worst-case NPV is negative, so its weighted contribution is −$207,000."},{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Reported the base-case NPV only","value":410000.0,"check":"410000","explain":"Expected NPV weights all three scenarios by their probabilities."}],"explanation":"Expected NPV is the probability-weighted sum of the scenario NPVs: 0.2(1,350,000) + 0.5(410,000) + 0.3(−690,000) = $268,000. The positive expected value does not remove the 30% chance of a $690,000 loss.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-26"},{"id":"fin201-q11-050","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okotoks Trailer Works plans a new trailer line costing $900,000 over 4 years, with no salvage, and treats CCA as straight-line (D = $225,000 per year). T = 30% and the required return is 12% per annum. Base case: price $2,400 and variable cost $1,350 per trailer, 1,200 trailers per year, fixed costs $620,000 per year. Optimistic scenario: sales 20% above forecast and, with extra supervisors, fixed costs 5% higher; price and variable cost per trailer unchanged. What is the scenario NPV?","answer":1135631.53,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"-900000+(((2400-1350)*1440-651000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","steps":[{"label":"Scenario EBITDA = (P − v)Q − FC","value":861000.0,"check":"(2400-1350)*1200*1.2-620000*1.05","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Scenario OCF","value":670200.0,"check":"(((2400-1350)*1440-651000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Scenario NPV","value":1135631.53,"check":"-900000+(((2400-1350)*1440-651000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Q = 1,200 × 1.2 = 1,440; FC = 620,000 × 1.05 = 651,000","EBITDA = (2,400 − 1,350)(1,440) − 651,000 = 1,512,000 − 651,000 = $861,000","OCF = 861,000 × 0.7 + 67,500 = 670,200.00","NPV = −900,000 + 670,200.00 × 3.037349 = $1,135,631.53","OCF rises 30.01% and NPV rises 70.6% for a 20% rise in sales: not linear."],"keystrokes":["CF worksheet: CF0 = −900000, C01 = 670200, F01 = 4, I = 12, NPV CPT → 1,135,631.53"],"errors":[{"id":"assumed-linear","label":"Scaled the base NPV up by 20%","value":798904.31,"check":"665753.5881859129*1.2","explain":"Sales up 20% does not mean NPV up 20%. Fixed costs are spread over more units and the outlay is unchanged, so NPV rises far more (about 70.6%)."},{"id":"ignored-fixed-cost-change","label":"Forgot the 5% rise in fixed costs","value":1201542.01,"check":"-900000+(((2400-1350)*1440-620000)*(1-0.3)+0.3*225000)*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","explain":"The scenario changes two inputs together: volume and fixed costs."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":930610.45,"check":"-900000+(((2400-1350)*1440-651000)*(1-0.3))*((1-(1+0.12)**-4)/0.12)","explain":"OCF = (S − C)(1 − T) + T·D; the shield is $67,500 a year."}],"explanation":"A scenario changes several inputs together. Here volume +20% and fixed costs +5% lift EBITDA from $640,000 to $861,000 (+34.5%), OCF by about 30%, and NPV by about 70.6% because the $900,000 outlay does not change. This is why scenario results are not linear.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5; TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-051","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's base case is: price $50, variable cost $30 per unit, 10,000 units, fixed costs $100,000, CCA $40,000 a year and T = 40%. In the best-case scenario price and quantity are each 10% higher and variable and fixed costs are each 10% lower. What is the best-case annual OCF?","options":["$107,200.00","$146,800.00","$134,800.00","$130,800.00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Best case inputs: P = 55, v = 27, Q = 11,000, FC = 90,000. EBITDA = (55 − 27)(11,000) − 90,000 = 218,000; OCF = 218,000 × 0.6 + 0.4 × 40,000 = $146,800. Raising variable cost (107,200) or fixed cost (134,800) in the best case moves a cost the wrong way, and dropping the tax shield gives $130,800.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-052","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's NPV is estimated at $820,000 in the best case (probability 25%), $300,000 in the base case (45%) and −$560,000 in the worst case (30%). What is the expected NPV?","options":["$186,666.67","$300,000.00","$172,000.00","$241,000.00"],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[NPV] = 0.25(820,000) + 0.45(300,000) + 0.30(−560,000) = 205,000 + 135,000 − 168,000 = $172,000. The equal-weight average is $186,666.67, the base case alone is $300,000, and attaching the probabilities to the wrong scenarios gives $241,000.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-26"},{"id":"fin201-q11-053","concept":"fin201-c11-scenario","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A project has 6 uncertain inputs. How many full DCF calculations are needed for a sensitivity analysis, and how many for a scenario analysis with a base, a best and a worst case?","options":["13 for sensitivity and 3 for scenario","12 for sensitivity and 2 for scenario","7 for sensitivity and 3 for scenario","13 for sensitivity and 6 for scenario"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sensitivity needs 2N + 1 = 13 runs; a scenario analysis needs one run per case, so 3 no matter how many inputs there are. Twelve forgets the base case, 7 is N + 1, and 6 confuses the number of inputs with the number of cases.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s4-5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-054","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Simulation analysis in capital budgeting is best described as:","options":["changing one input at a time and recomputing NPV, for 2N + 1 runs in total","building a best, a base and a worst case by moving several inputs together","a computer drawing all uncertain inputs at random many times and studying the distribution of NPV","solving for the single input value that sets NPV equal to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Simulation randomizes all inputs according to chosen distributions, repeats thousands of times, and then looks at the distribution of the output. The three distractors describe sensitivity analysis, scenario analysis and NPV break-even analysis.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6; TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-055","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Cedar Mills fits a triangular distribution to each of six uncertain inputs, has a spreadsheet draw a value for all six at once, computes the NPV, and repeats this 10,000 times. The analyst then plots a histogram of the 10,000 NPVs. This technique is:","options":["sensitivity analysis","scenario analysis","NPV break-even analysis","simulation analysis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Random draws from distributions for all inputs, repeated thousands of times, with a histogram of the NPV output, is simulation. Sensitivity moves one input at a time and scenario uses a few hand-picked consistent cases.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-056","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does simulation analysis differ from scenario analysis?","options":["scenario analysis changes one input at a time, while simulation changes several together","scenario analysis tries a few hand-picked combinations; simulation draws all inputs at random and gives a distribution of NPV","simulation produces a single accept or reject threshold, while scenario analysis produces a distribution","the two are identical and differ only in the software used"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A scenario analysis uses a handful of sensible, consistent cases (base, best, worst). Simulation randomizes every input from a distribution many times and yields the whole output distribution.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5-6; TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-057","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we study the whole distribution of simulated NPVs rather than only the average?","options":["the average of simulated NPVs is always wrong and must be replaced","only the single highest NPV matters for the accept decision","the distribution is always a normal curve centred on the base-case NPV","the shape shows how likely losses are and how skewed the outcomes are, which the average alone hides"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The professor stresses that the shape is important, not just the baseline: two projects with the same mean can have very different chances of NPV below zero. The output is usually not symmetrical and its peak need not sit at the base case.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-058","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A simulation of 8,000 trials gives a mean NPV of +$310,000, but 35% of the trials have a negative NPV. Which reading is best?","options":["the mean is positive, but over a third of outcomes destroy value, so the downside matters, not only the mean","any negative trial proves the project must be rejected, whatever the mean is","the true NPV of the project is -35%, because 35% of trials are negative","the simulation shows that the base-case forecast was wrong and must be redone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Simulation describes the range and likelihood of outcomes. A positive mean with a 35% chance of NPV below zero means substantial downside risk; it does not by itself prove rejection, and 35% is a probability of loss, not an NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6; TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-059","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the output distribution of a simulation is NOT necessarily true?","options":["it is symmetrical, with its peak at the base-case NPV","its shape matters, not just its mean","it may be skewed to one side","it is built from many random draws of the inputs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lecture warns that the output distribution will probably not be symmetrical and that the baseline or expected value may not be at the peak. The other three statements are true.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-060","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Simulation analysis gives a simple decision rule that tells us whether to accept the project.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Simulation describes the range and likelihood of NPV outcomes, but no simple rule tells us what to do with them. Judgement about the downside is still needed.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-061","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"Before a simulation can be run, the analyst must choose a probability distribution for each uncertain input.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The computer draws each input from the distribution the analyst chooses, so those distributions (and any links between inputs) are the key assumptions of the whole method.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-062","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pacific Rail is evaluating a terminal with eight uncertain inputs (volume, price, fuel, wages and others). The board wants the probability that the project's NPV is negative. Which technique can supply it?","options":["sensitivity analysis","scenario analysis with best, base and worst cases","simulation analysis","NPV break-even analysis on unit sales"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only simulation produces a whole distribution of NPV, from which the share of negative outcomes can be read. Sensitivity flexes one input, scenarios give three points, and break-even gives one threshold.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6; REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-063","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why should a realistic simulation model the relationships between inputs, such as price and quantity sold?","options":["NPV cannot be calculated at all unless the inputs are correlated with each other","correlation between inputs makes every simulated NPV come out positive","inputs are not independent, and separate draws can produce combinations that never occur together","taxes are computed from the correlation between price and quantity sold"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If price and quantity tend to move together (or against each other), independent draws will generate unrealistic combinations and distort the NPV distribution. This is one reason simulation is hard to do well.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-064","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"The textbook describes simulation analysis as an extension that combines:","options":["payback analysis and discounted payback analysis, applied together","cash break-even analysis and accounting break-even analysis together","the capital asset pricing model and the capital market line","scenario analysis (many inputs change) and sensitivity analysis (many values)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Scenario analysis lets all inputs change but only to a few values; sensitivity lets one input take many values. Letting all inputs take many values at once is a crude form of simulation.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-065","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"In simulation analysis the analyst studies the _____ of the output NPV, not only its expected value.","options":["payback","distribution","break-even","tax rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The point of simulation is the shape of the output: spread, skew and the chance of negative NPV. The baseline or expected value alone does not tell us that.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-066","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A simulation of a new project produces 5,000 NPV results, and 1,100 of them are negative. What percentage of the simulated outcomes have a negative NPV?","answer":22.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"1100/5000*100","steps":[{"label":"Share of trials with NPV < 0","value":22.0,"check":"1100/5000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["P(NPV < 0) = negative trials ÷ total trials","= 1,100 ÷ 5,000 = 0.22 = 22%"],"keystrokes":["1100 ÷ 5000 = 0.22 → 22%"],"errors":[{"id":"complement-probability","label":"Reported the share of positive trials","value":78.0,"check":"(5000-1100)/5000*100","explain":"3,900 of the 5,000 trials are positive (78%). The question asks for the negative ones."},{"id":"wrong-denominator","label":"Divided by the positive trials only","value":28.21,"check":"1100/(5000-1100)*100","explain":"The share of outcomes is out of all 5,000 trials, not out of the positive ones."}],"explanation":"The probability of a negative NPV is estimated by the share of simulated trials with NPV below zero: 1,100 out of 5,000 is 22%. This is the kind of statement simulation can make and the other what-if tools cannot.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-067","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A simulation's average NPV is $212,000, while the base-case NPV (all inputs at their expected values) is $240,000. Which explanation is possible?","options":["the output distribution can be skewed, so its average need not equal the NPV computed at the average inputs","the simulation must contain a programming error, because the two numbers must always be equal","the base case ignored taxes, which is the only possible source of a difference","the simulation automatically used a higher discount rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The baseline or expected value may not be at the peak, and the output is probably not symmetrical, so the simulated mean can differ from the base-case NPV. A difference is not evidence of a mistake.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-068","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In one run of a simulation, what happens immediately after the computer draws a random value for every uncertain input?","options":["the probability distribution for each input is chosen","the histogram of all the NPVs is summarized","the input is solved for the value that gives NPV = 0","the NPV for that set of inputs is computed and recorded"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The sequence is: choose distributions, draw all inputs, compute and record the NPV, repeat thousands of times, then summarize the distribution. Distributions are chosen before the first draw, and the summary comes after all runs.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-069","concept":"fin201-c11-simulation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which is a typical weakness of simulation analysis?","options":["it can only be used once the project's NPV is already known to be positive","it needs a distribution for every input and their relationships, which are hard to estimate","it can vary only one input at a time, so it misses combinations of inputs","it ignores the time value of money when computing each trial's NPV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Simulation is limited by the quality of the assumed distributions and correlations, and by the lack of a simple decision rule. It still discounts cash flows in every run and varies all inputs at once.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.2"},{"id":"fin201-q11-070","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"NPV break-even analysis asks:","options":["how far can an input change before NPV crosses zero and the accept decision flips","at what sales level accounting net income is exactly zero","what the NPV is when every input takes its best value","what the distribution of NPV looks like after thousands of random draws"],"answer":0,"explanation":"NPV break-even finds the input level at which NPV = 0, i.e. how far an input can move before the decision to accept would change. The distractors describe accounting break-even, best-case scenario and simulation.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7"},{"id":"fin201-q11-071","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"NPV break-even (the finance version) differs from accounting break-even in that:","options":["it ignores depreciation, while accounting break-even includes it","it sets NPV, not accounting profit, to zero, so it reflects the required return","its sales level is always lower than the accounting break-even sales level","it looks only at variable costs and ignores fixed costs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accounting break-even makes net income zero and ignores the required return. NPV break-even makes NPV zero, so it needs a higher level of sales for a normal project. Leaving out depreciation describes cash break-even, not NPV break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7; SM 11 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-072","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's sales are above its accounting break-even level, so it reports a profit. Why can its NPV still be negative?","options":["accounting profit already includes the cost of capital, so NPV counts it twice","accounting break-even uses fixed costs only, so it is always too low","accounting profit ignores the time value of money and the return investors require on the investment","NPV ignores depreciation entirely, which makes it lower"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A profit only says that revenues cover costs and depreciation. NPV also asks whether the cash flows earn the required return after discounting. A project can be profitable on paper and still destroy shareholder value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7; SM 11 CTQ 9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-073","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"At a planning meeting the CFO of Sunrise Bakeries asks: \"By how much could our selling price fall before this project stops being worth doing?\" Answering that question is:","options":["NPV break-even analysis","scenario analysis","simulation analysis","accounting break-even analysis on sales volume"],"answer":0,"explanation":"She asks how far one input can move until the accept decision changes, i.e. until NPV reaches zero. That is NPV break-even. Scenario and simulation describe ranges of outcomes, and accounting break-even is about zero profit, not zero NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7"},{"id":"fin201-q11-074","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's expected price is $9.40 and its NPV break-even price is $8.75, all else equal. Which interpretation is correct?","options":["price can fall about 7.4% below the forecast before NPV reaches zero, which is a comfortable cushion","the project should be rejected because the break-even price is a positive number","accounting profit is zero when the price is $8.75, so the project breaks even on paper","price can fall about 6.9% below the forecast before NPV reaches zero; a thin cushion means high forecasting risk"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The cushion is (9.40 − 8.75) ÷ 9.40 = 0.65 ÷ 9.40 = 6.9% of the forecast price. Dividing by the break-even price gives 7.4%, the wrong base. NPV is still positive at $9.40, and NPV = 0 at $8.75 does not mean accounting profit is zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7; TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-075","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement is NOT true at the NPV break-even level of an input (all other inputs at their base values)?","options":["the NPV equals zero","for conventional cash flows the IRR equals the required return","accounting net income equals zero","the accept or reject decision is exactly on the margin"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At NPV break-even, NPV = 0, IRR = R and the decision is on the margin. Net income is zero at the accounting break-even, a different and lower sales level.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7; SM 11-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-076","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"At an input's NPV break-even level, the project's IRR equals the required return (for conventional cash flows).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. NPV = 0 at a discount rate R means that R is the IRR, so the IRR equals the required return at the NPV break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-16c"},{"id":"fin201-q11-077","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"NPV break-even analysis changes several inputs together to find the combination that makes NPV zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. NPV break-even solves for ONE input, holding the others at their base values, so that NPV = 0. Changing several inputs together is scenario analysis.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7-9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-078","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A plant costs I0. Its CCA tax shield is a level annuity of DTS for N years and its pre-tax operating income (EBITDA) is a level perpetuity. Which equation gives the break-even EBITDA?","options":["−I0 + DTS × annuity factor(R, N) + EBITDA ÷ R = 0","−I0 + (1 − T) × EBITDA ÷ R = 0","−I0 + DTS × annuity factor(R, N) + (1 − T) × EBITDA × N = 0","−I0 + DTS × annuity factor(R, N) + (1 − T) × EBITDA ÷ R = 0"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The three components of NPV are the outlay, the PV of the DTS annuity and the PV of the after-tax perpetuity (1 − T)EBITDA ÷ R. Leaving out (1 − T) ignores tax, leaving out the DTS ignores the shield, and multiplying by N treats a perpetuity as N payments.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s8-9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-079","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"When solving for the break-even EBITDA, why do we divide the required after-tax cash flow by (1 − T)?","options":["because the tax rate must be added to the discount rate in the perpetuity","EBITDA is pre-tax, and only (1 − T) of each pre-tax dollar remains after tax","because the CCA tax shield has to be taxed a second time as income","because EBITDA is measured after interest and depreciation have been deducted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The NPV equation needs after-tax cash flow, which is (1 − T) × EBITDA. To find the pre-tax EBITDA that delivers a required after-tax amount we divide by (1 − T). Forgetting this step gives an answer that is too low.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s9; SM 10-23"},{"id":"fin201-q11-080","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a sensitivity graph of NPV against selling price, the NPV line crosses zero at a price of $8.75. That price is called the project's:","options":["accounting break-even price","cash break-even price","internal rate of return","NPV break-even price"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The price at which NPV crosses zero is the NPV (financial) break-even price. Accounting break-even sets net income to zero and cash break-even sets operating cash flow to zero; neither is where NPV is zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7; TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-081","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Base-case EBITDA for a plant is forecast at $312,000 a year and the NPV break-even EBITDA is $274,500. What does the analysis support?","options":["accept, since EBITDA can fall about 12.0% before NPV reaches zero","accept, since EBITDA can fall about 13.7% before NPV reaches zero","reject, because the break-even EBITDA is above zero","no decision is possible without knowing the accounting break-even"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The forecast is above the break-even, so NPV is positive at the base case. The cushion is (312,000 − 274,500) ÷ 312,000 = 12.0% of the forecast. Dividing by the break-even gives 13.7%, the wrong base.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s7-9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-082","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Glenmore Stamping will build a plant for $1,600,000. For simplicity CCA is treated as straight-line over 10 years, and the plant will then earn the same pre-tax operating income (EBITDA) every year forever. T = 30% and the discount rate is 8% per annum. What is the minimum EBITDA that gives NPV = 0?","answer":146047.55,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1600000-0.3*1600000/10*((1-(1+0.08)**-10)/0.08))/((1-0.3)/0.08)","steps":[{"label":"Annual DTS = T × I0 ÷ 10","value":48000.0,"check":"0.3*1600000/10","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of the 10-year DTS annuity","value":322083.91,"check":"0.3*1600000/10*((1-(1+0.08)**-10)/0.08)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV the income must supply = I0 − PV(DTS)","value":1277916.09,"check":"1600000-0.3*1600000/10*((1-(1+0.08)**-10)/0.08)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Break-even EBITDA","value":146047.55,"check":"(1600000-0.3*1600000/10*((1-(1+0.08)**-10)/0.08))/((1-0.3)/0.08)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["DTS = 0.30 × 1,600,000 ÷ 10 = $48,000 per year for 10 years","PV(DTS) = 48,000 × annuity factor(8%, 10) = 48,000 × 6.710081 = $322,083.91","NPV = −1,600,000 + $322,083.91 + (1 − 0.30) × EBITDA ÷ 0.08 = 0, so 8.75 × EBITDA = $1,277,916.09","EBITDA = $1,277,916.09 ÷ 8.75 = $146,047.55"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 10, I/Y = 8, PMT = −48000, CPT PV = 322,083.91","1,600,000 − 322,083.91 = 1,277,916.09","÷ (0.7 ÷ 0.08) = ÷ 8.75 → 146,047.55"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Treated the required amount as pre-tax","value":102233.29,"check":"(1600000-0.3*1600000/10*((1-(1+0.08)**-10)/0.08))*0.08","explain":"EBITDA is pre-tax. The perpetuity pays (1 − T) × EBITDA after tax, so divide by (1 − T)/R, not just by 1/R."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":182857.14,"check":"1600000/((1-0.3)/0.08)","explain":"The shield is worth $322,084 of the outlay, so the income only has to supply the rest."},{"id":"dts-as-perpetuity","label":"Valued the 10-year shield as a perpetuity","value":114285.71,"check":"(1600000-0.3*1600000/10/0.08)/((1-0.3)/0.08)","explain":"The shield lasts only 10 years; use the annuity factor, not 1/R."}],"explanation":"Set NPV = 0: the outlay of $1,600,000 must be repaid by the CCA tax shield (a 10-year annuity worth about $322,084) and by the after-tax perpetuity (1 − T)EBITDA ÷ R = 8.75 × EBITDA. That leaves 8.75 × EBITDA = $1,277,916, so EBITDA is at least $146,047.55.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s8-9; SM 11-27"},{"id":"fin201-q11-083","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Pembina Packaging will buy a sealing line for $350,000 for a 5-year project with no salvage. CCA is treated as straight-line (D = $70,000 per year), T = 30% and R = 12% per annum. Volume is 25,000 units per year, variable cost is $18.00 per unit and fixed costs are $120,000 per year. What is the minimum unit price that gives NPV = 0?","answer":27.15,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"((350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)+120000)/25000+18","steps":[{"label":"Required OCF for NPV = 0 (I0 ÷ annuity factor)","value":97093.41,"check":"350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Required pre-tax EBITDA = (OCF − T·D) ÷ (1 − T)","value":108704.87,"check":"(350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Required contribution per unit = (EBITDA + FC) ÷ Q","value":9.15,"check":"((350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)+120000)/25000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Break-even price = v + contribution","value":27.15,"check":"((350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)+120000)/25000+18","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Annuity factor (12%, 5) = 3.604776; OCF* = 350,000 ÷ 3.604776 = $97,093.41","OCF = EBITDA(1 − T) + T·D, so EBITDA = ($97,093.41 − 0.3 × 70,000) ÷ 0.7 = $108,704.87","EBITDA = (P − v)Q − FC, so P − v = ($108,704.87 + 120,000) ÷ 25,000 = $9.1482","P = 18.00 + 9.1482 = $27.15"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 5, I/Y = 12, PV = −350000, CPT PMT = 97,093.41","(97,093.41 − 21,000) ÷ 0.7 = 108,704.87","(108,704.87 + 120,000) ÷ 25,000 + 18 = 27.15"],"errors":[{"id":"profit-not-npv-breakeven","label":"Found the accounting break-even price (net income = 0)","value":25.6,"check":"(120000+70000)/25000+18","explain":"At that price OCF equals D = $70,000, which only repays the outlay with no return. Accounting break-even ignores the 12% required return."},{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Treated OCF − T·D as pre-tax EBITDA","value":25.84,"check":"(350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)-0.3*70000+120000)/25000+18","explain":"OCF − T·D is an after-tax amount; divide it by (1 − T) to get the pre-tax EBITDA."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":28.35,"check":"(350000/((1-(1+0.12)**-5)/0.12)/(1-0.3)+120000)/25000+18","explain":"OCF includes T·D = $21,000 from the shield, so the operations need to supply that much less."}],"explanation":"NPV = 0 requires a constant OCF of 350,000 ÷ 3.604776 = $97,093.41. Subtract the CCA shield (0.3 × 70,000 = 21,000) and gross up by 1 ÷ (1 − 0.3) to get EBITDA of $108,704.87. Add fixed costs, divide by 25,000 units and add the $18.00 variable cost to reach a price of about $27.15, well above the $25.60 accounting break-even price.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3; SM 11-27"},{"id":"fin201-q11-084","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Wolf Willow Vineyards will plant a new block for $750,000. The investment earns no CCA tax shield, and it should earn the same pre-tax net cash revenue every year forever. T = 28% and the discount rate is 9% per annum. What is the minimum annual pre-tax net cash revenue that makes NPV = 0?","answer":93750.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"750000*0.09/(1-0.28)","steps":[{"label":"Required after-tax cash flow = R × I0","value":67500.0,"check":"750000*0.09","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Required pre-tax amount = after-tax ÷ (1 − T)","value":93750.0,"check":"750000*0.09/(1-0.28)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["A perpetuity is worth C ÷ R, so NPV = −750,000 + (1 − T)·X ÷ R = 0","After-tax cash flow needed: C = R × I0 = 0.09 × 750,000 = $67,500","Pre-tax revenue X = 67,500 ÷ (1 − 0.28) = 67,500 ÷ 0.72 = $93,750.00"],"keystrokes":["0.09 × 750000 = 67500","÷ (1 − 0.28) = 93,750.00"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Stopped at the after-tax amount","value":67500.0,"check":"750000*0.09","explain":"$67,500 is what must arrive after tax. The pre-tax figure must be larger so that 72% of it equals $67,500."},{"id":"multiplied-not-divided","label":"Multiplied by (1 − T) instead of dividing","value":48600.0,"check":"750000*0.09*(1-0.28)","explain":"Tax takes a share of pre-tax revenue, so the pre-tax figure is the after-tax figure divided by (1 − T)."}],"explanation":"With no tax shield and a perpetual flow, NPV = 0 needs (1 − T)X ÷ R = I0, so X = R × I0 ÷ (1 − T) = 0.09 × 750,000 ÷ 0.72 = $93,750.00.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s9; SM 11-23b"},{"id":"fin201-q11-085","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mackenzie Foods computes its project's NPV at two sales levels, with every other input at its base value: NPV = $412,000 at 80,000 units and NPV = $603,000 at 90,000 units. NPV is a straight-line function of units sold. At how many units sold does NPV equal zero?","answer":58429.32,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"80000-412000/((603000-412000)/(90000-80000))","steps":[{"label":"Slope ΔNPV/ΔQ in dollars per unit","value":19.1,"check":"(603000-412000)/(90000-80000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Break-even units sold","value":58429.32,"check":"80000-412000/((603000-412000)/(90000-80000))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Slope = (603,000 − 412,000) ÷ (90,000 − 80,000) = 191,000 ÷ 10,000 = $19.10 per unit","From 80,000 units, NPV must fall by $412,000: 412,000 ÷ 19.10 = 21,570.68 units","Break-even = 80,000 − 21,570.68 = 58,429.32 units"],"keystrokes":["(603000 − 412000) ÷ 10000 = 19.1","412000 ÷ 19.1 = 21570.68","80000 − 21570.68 = 58429.32"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-direction","label":"Added the distance instead of subtracting","value":101570.68,"check":"80000+412000/((603000-412000)/(90000-80000))","explain":"NPV is positive at 80,000 units and rises with volume, so the zero crossing lies below 80,000 units."},{"id":"slope-not-per-unit","label":"Used the NPV change for 10,000 units as the slope per unit","value":79997.84,"check":"80000-412000/(603000-412000)","explain":"$191,000 is the change over 10,000 units. Divide by 10,000 to get $19.10 per unit before solving."}],"explanation":"NPV is linear in units, so the slope is constant: $19.10 per unit. NPV is $412,000 at 80,000 units, so it reaches zero 412,000 ÷ 19.10 = 21,570.68 units lower, at about 58,429.32 units.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-27"},{"id":"fin201-q11-086","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kananaskis Snow Systems buys snow-making equipment for $900,000 for a 5-year project. The CCA rate is 30% with the half-year rule, T = 30% and the discount rate is k = 10% per annum. The equipment is sold for $120,000 at the end of year 5. Pre-tax operating income (EBITDA) is the same each year and there is no net working capital. Using the formula-sheet PV of the CCA tax shield with its salvage term, find the minimum annual EBITDA that gives NPV = 0.","answer":244562.45,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(900000-(900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-120000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5)-120000/(1+0.1)**5)/((1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.1)**-5)/0.1))","steps":[{"label":"PV of the CCA tax shield (with salvage term)","value":176530.58,"check":"900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-120000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV of the salvage proceeds","value":74510.56,"check":"120000/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"PV the operations must supply","value":648958.86,"check":"900000-(900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-120000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5)-120000/(1+0.1)**5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Break-even EBITDA","value":244562.45,"check":"(900000-(900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-120000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5)-120000/(1+0.1)**5)/((1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.1)**-5)/0.1))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["PVCCA = [I0·d·T ÷ (k + d)] × (1 + 0.5k) ÷ (1 + k) − [S·d·T ÷ (k + d)] × 1 ÷ (1 + k)^5","= 202,500 × 0.954545 − 27,000 × 0.620921 = 193,295.45 − 16,764.87 = $176,530.58","PV(salvage) = 120,000 ÷ 1.1^5 = $74,510.56","NPV = −900,000 + $176,530.58 + $74,510.56 + (1 − 0.30) × EBITDA × 3.790787 = 0","EBITDA = $648,958.86 ÷ (0.7 × 3.790787) = $244,562.45"],"keystrokes":["PVCCA: 900000 × 0.3 × 0.3 ÷ 0.4 = 202500; × 1.05 ÷ 1.1 = 193295.45","120000 × 0.3 × 0.3 ÷ 0.4 ÷ 1.1^5 = 16764.87","TVM: N = 5, I/Y = 10, PMT = −1, CPT PV = 3.790787","648,958.86 ÷ (0.7 × 3.790787) = 244,562.45"],"errors":[{"id":"missing-half-year","label":"Used a first-year factor of 1 instead of (1 + 0.5k) ÷ (1 + k)","value":241093.68,"check":"(900000-(900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)-120000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5)-120000/(1+0.1)**5)/((1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.1)**-5)/0.1))","explain":"Under the half-year rule the first-year factor is (1 + 0.5k) ÷ (1 + k) = 0.9545, which lowers the PV of the shield."},{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Divided by the annuity factor but not by (1 − T)","value":171193.71,"check":"(900000-(900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1)-120000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)/(1+0.1)**5)-120000/(1+0.1)**5)/((1-(1+0.1)**-5)/0.1)","explain":"The operations supply (1 − T) × EBITDA after tax, so the annuity factor must be multiplied by (1 − T)."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Dropped the salvage term from the PVCCA formula but kept the sale proceeds","value":238244.54,"check":"(900000-(900000*0.3*0.3/(0.1+0.3)*(1+0.5*0.1)/(1+0.1))-120000/(1+0.1)**5)/((1-0.3)*((1-(1+0.1)**-5)/0.1))","explain":"When the asset is sold the pool loses S, so the later shield falls: subtract S·d·T ÷ (k + d) × 1 ÷ (1 + k)^N."}],"explanation":"NPV = 0 requires the operations to cover what the shield and the salvage do not: 900,000 − 176,530.58 − 74,510.56 = $648,958.86. That is the PV of a 5-year annuity of (1 − T) × EBITDA at 10%, so EBITDA = 648,958.86 ÷ (0.7 × 3.790787) = $244,562.45.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s8-9; SM 10-23"},{"id":"fin201-q11-087","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cypress Hills Cabinets will buy a $420,000 machine for a 6-year project with no salvage. CCA is treated as straight-line (D = $70,000 per year), T = 30% and R = 11% per annum. Price is $40.00 per unit, volume is 30,000 units per year and fixed costs are $200,000 per year. What is the highest variable cost per unit at which NPV is still zero?","answer":29.61,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"40-((420000/((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)+200000)/30000","steps":[{"label":"Required OCF for NPV = 0","value":99278.16,"check":"420000/((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Required EBITDA","value":111825.94,"check":"(420000/((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Highest variable cost per unit","value":29.61,"check":"40-((420000/((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)-0.3*70000)/(1-0.3)+200000)/30000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["OCF* = 420,000 ÷ 4.230538 = $99,278.16","EBITDA = ($99,278.16 − 0.3 × 70,000) ÷ 0.7 = $111,825.94","Contribution needed = ($111,825.94 + 200,000) ÷ 30,000 = $10.3942 per unit","v = 40.00 − 10.3942 = $29.61"],"keystrokes":["TVM: N = 6, I/Y = 11, PV = −420000, CPT PMT = 99,278.16","(99,278.16 − 21,000) ÷ 0.7 = 111,825.94","(111,825.94 + 200,000) ÷ 30,000 = 10.3942","40 − 10.3942 = 29.61"],"errors":[{"id":"profit-not-npv-breakeven","label":"Found the variable cost at which accounting profit is zero","value":31.0,"check":"40-(200000+70000)/30000","explain":"At $31.00 net income is zero, but OCF then only equals D and earns no return. The NPV break-even variable cost is lower."},{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Treated OCF − T·D as pre-tax EBITDA","value":30.72,"check":"40-(420000/((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)-0.3*70000+200000)/30000","explain":"Divide the after-tax amount by (1 − T) to get pre-tax EBITDA."},{"id":"missing-cca-shield","label":"Left out the CCA tax shield","value":28.61,"check":"40-(420000/((1-(1+0.11)**-6)/0.11)/(1-0.3)+200000)/30000","explain":"OCF includes T·D = $21,000 from the shield; ignoring it demands too much from operations."}],"explanation":"A 6-year annuity at 11% must repay $420,000, so OCF must be at least $99,278.16. Strip out the shield (0.3 × 70,000) and gross up for tax to get EBITDA of $111,825.94. Adding fixed costs and dividing by 30,000 units gives a required contribution of $10.39 per unit, so variable cost can be at most $29.61.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3; SM 11-27"},{"id":"fin201-q11-088","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Fort Saskatchewan Plastics buys a press for $500,000 and treats CCA as straight-line over 5 years. Pre-tax operating income (EBITDA) will be the same every year forever. T = 40% and R = 10% per annum. What is the minimum EBITDA for NPV = 0?","options":["$34,836.85","$83,333.33","$16,666.67","$58,061.42"],"answer":3,"explanation":"DTS = 0.4 × 500,000 ÷ 5 = 40,000 a year; PV(DTS) = 40,000 × 3.790787 = 151,631.47. Then (1 − T) ÷ R = 6, so EBITDA = (500,000 − 151,631.47) ÷ 6 = $58,061.42. Stopping before dividing by (1 − T) gives $34,836.85, ignoring the shield gives $83,333.33, and valuing the shield as a perpetuity gives $16,666.67.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s8-9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-089","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A $600,000 production line lasts 5 years with no salvage. CCA is treated as straight-line (D = $120,000 per year), T = 30% and R = 10% per annum. Each unit earns a contribution (price minus variable cost) of $20.00 and fixed costs are $150,000 per year. What annual sales volume gives NPV = 0?","options":["16,234 units","13,500 units","13,614 units","7,500 units"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OCF* = 600,000 ÷ 3.790787 = 158,278.49. EBITDA = (158,278.49 − 0.3 × 120,000) ÷ 0.7 = 174,683.55. Q = (150,000 + 174,683.55) ÷ 20 = 16,234 units. The accounting break-even is 13,500, forgetting ÷(1 − T) gives 13,614, and the cash break-even FC ÷ contribution is 7,500.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-24b, 11-28"},{"id":"fin201-q11-090","concept":"fin201-c11-npv-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"An investment of $480,000 will produce the same after-tax operating cash flow in each of 4 years. The required return is 14% per annum. What is the smallest annual OCF that gives NPV = 0?","options":["$120,000.00","$164,738.30","$67,200.00","$139,816.10"],"answer":1,"explanation":"OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor(14%, 4) = 480,000 ÷ 2.913712 = $164,738.30. Dividing by N gives $120,000 (no return), treating the flow as a perpetuity gives $67,200, and using a 5-year factor gives $139,816.10.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-401","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"At the cash break-even sales level, a project's _____ is exactly zero.","options":["net income","NPV","payback period","operating cash flow"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cash break-even sets OCF = 0, so Q_C = FC/(P − v). Net income = 0 is the accounting break-even (depreciation joins the numerator) and NPV = 0 is the financial break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-402","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Kootenay Outfitters launches a new product line. At 9,000 units a year the line just covers its fixed operating costs in cash, but nothing is left over to repay the initial outlay. Which break-even point is this?","options":["Cash break-even","Accounting break-even","Financial break-even","Payback break-even"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Covering the fixed cash costs and nothing more means OCF = 0, the cash break-even Q_C = FC/(P − v). Nothing is left to recover the outlay, so the IRR is −100%. Accounting break-even would at least return the depreciation (OCF = D), and financial break-even would also earn the required return.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4; SM 11 CTQ 10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-403","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Fraser Valley Kayaks plans a paddleboard line. The price is $64.50 per board, variable cost is $38.20 per board, fixed costs are $412,000 a year and depreciation is $165,000 a year. Ignoring taxes, what is the cash break-even quantity (boards per year)?","answer":15665.4,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"412000/(64.5-38.2)","steps":[{"label":"Contribution margin P − v","value":26.3,"check":"64.5-38.2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Cash break-even Q_C = FC/(P − v)","value":15665.4,"check":"412000/(64.5-38.2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Contribution margin P − v = 64.50 − 38.20 = $26.30 per board","Cash break-even: OCF = 0, so Q_C = FC/(P − v) = 412,000/26.30 = 15,665.40 boards per year"],"keystrokes":["64.50 − 38.20 = 26.30","412000 ÷ 26.30 = 15,665.40"],"errors":[{"id":"d-in-cash-breakeven","label":"Added depreciation to the numerator","value":21939.16,"check":"(412000+165000)/(64.5-38.2)","explain":"That is the accounting break-even. Depreciation is not a cash cost, so it stays out of the cash break-even."},{"id":"fc-over-price","label":"Divided fixed costs by the price","value":6387.6,"check":"412000/64.5","explain":"Each board contributes P − v = $26.30 toward fixed costs, not the whole price."},{"id":"fc-over-v","label":"Divided fixed costs by the variable cost","value":10785.34,"check":"412000/38.2","explain":"The denominator is the contribution margin P − v, not the variable cost per unit."}],"explanation":"Cash break-even is the sales level where OCF = 0: Q_C = FC/(P − v). Depreciation is not a cash cost, so it plays no part (that is why the $165,000 is a distractor).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-1; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-404","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peace River Pottery sells mugs for $46.80. Each mug costs $11.35 in labour, $7.90 in clay and glaze and $1.25 in packaging. Fixed costs are $385,000 a year and depreciation is $140,000 a year. Ignoring taxes, what is the cash break-even quantity?","options":["19,962 mugs","13,975 mugs","14,639 mugs","8,226 mugs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total variable cost is v = 11.35 + 7.90 + 1.25 = $20.50, so P − v = $26.30 and Q_C = 385,000/26.30 = 14,639 mugs. Adding the $140,000 of depreciation gives 19,962 (the accounting break-even), and dropping packaging from v overstates the margin and understates the break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-1","check":"385000/(46.8-(11.35+7.9+1.25))","option_checks":["(385000+140000)/(46.8-(11.35+7.9+1.25))","385000/(46.8-(11.35+7.9))","385000/(46.8-(11.35+7.9+1.25))","385000/46.8"],"option_errors":["d-in-cash-breakeven","partial-variable-cost",null,"fc-over-price"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-405","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Algoma Canoe sells a model for $1,240 with a variable cost of $815 per canoe. The project's cash break-even is 538 canoes a year. What annual fixed costs does that imply?","answer":228650.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"538*(1240-815)","steps":[{"label":"Contribution margin P − v","value":425.0,"check":"1240-815","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Fixed costs FC = Q_C × (P − v)","value":228650.0,"check":"538*(1240-815)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Q_C = FC/(P − v) rearranges to FC = Q_C × (P − v)","P − v = 1,240 − 815 = $425.00","FC = 538 × 425.00 = $228,650.00"],"keystrokes":["1240 − 815 = 425","× 538 = 228,650.00"],"errors":[{"id":"fc-from-price","label":"Multiplied Q_C by the price","value":667120.0,"check":"538*1240","explain":"Only the contribution margin P − v covers fixed costs, so FC = Q_C × (P − v), not Q_C × P."},{"id":"fc-from-v","label":"Multiplied Q_C by the variable cost","value":438470.0,"check":"538*815","explain":"That is total variable cost at the break-even volume, not the fixed costs."},{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Divided Q_C by the margin","value":1.27,"check":"538/(1240-815)","explain":"The formula is FC/(P − v) = Q_C, so FC is the product Q_C × (P − v), not the quotient."}],"explanation":"Rearrange Q_C = FC/(P − v) to FC = Q_C × (P − v). At the break-even volume the total contribution exactly equals fixed costs.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-10; SM 11-8"},{"id":"fin201-q11-406","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":["fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Cider Works prices a new line at $58.00 a case with a variable cost of $33.50. Fixed costs are $310,000 a year and depreciation is $120,000 a year. The tax rate is 30%, and any tax loss on this project can be used against the firm's other income. At what quantity is OCF exactly zero?","answer":10553.94,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(310000-0.3*120000/(1-0.3))/(58-33.5)","steps":[{"label":"Contribution margin P − v","value":24.5,"check":"58-33.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Grossed-up shield T·D/(1 − T)","value":51428.57,"check":"0.3*120000/(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Numerator FC + (0 − T·D)/(1 − T)","value":258571.43,"check":"310000-0.3*120000/(1-0.3)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Cash break-even Q_C with taxes","value":10553.94,"check":"(310000-0.3*120000/(1-0.3))/(58-33.5)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["OCF = [(P − v)Q − FC](1 − T) + T·D; set OCF = 0","Q_C = {FC + (0 − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v)","T·D = 0.30 × 120,000 = 36,000; 36,000/0.70 = 51,428.57","Numerator = 310,000 − 51,428.57 = 258,571.43; P − v = 24.50","Q_C = 258,571.43/24.50 = 10,553.94 cases","Check: [(24.50 × 10,553.94) − 310,000](0.70) + 36,000 = −36,000 + 36,000 = 0"],"keystrokes":["0.30 × 120000 ÷ 0.70 = 51,428.57","310000 − 51428.57 = 258,571.43","÷ 24.50 = 10,553.94"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Subtracted T·D but did not divide by (1 − T)","value":11183.67,"check":"(310000-0.3*120000)/(58-33.5)","explain":"The shield sits after tax while the numerator is pre-tax, so it must be grossed up by 1/(1 − T)."},{"id":"taxes-ignored","label":"Ignored taxes: FC/(P − v)","value":12653.06,"check":"310000/(58-33.5)","explain":"With taxes the depreciation shield still produces cash when the pre-tax result is negative, which lowers the cash break-even."},{"id":"shield-sign","label":"Added the grossed-up shield instead of subtracting it","value":14752.19,"check":"(310000+0.3*120000/(1-0.3))/(58-33.5)","explain":"At OCF = 0 the shield replaces some of the fixed cost burden, so it is subtracted from FC."}],"explanation":"With taxes, OCF = [(P − v)Q − FC](1 − T) + T·D. Setting it to zero gives Q_C = {FC − T·D/(1 − T)}/(P − v) = 10,553.94, below FC/(P − v) = 12,653.06 because the depreciation tax shield is still earned when pre-tax profit is negative.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-24, SM 11-28; SM 11 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-407","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts cash break-even with accounting break-even?","options":["Cash break-even includes the required return on the investment; accounting break-even does not","Cash break-even leaves depreciation out of the numerator; accounting break-even includes it","Cash break-even is the higher of the two quantities for a project with positive depreciation","Cash break-even sets net income to zero; accounting break-even sets operating cash flow to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q_C = FC/(P − v) covers only cash fixed costs; Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) also covers depreciation, so Q_A > Q_C. The required return belongs to financial break-even, and the claim that cash break-even sets net income to zero swaps the roles of net income and OCF.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4; SM 11 CTQ 10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-408","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about a project operating exactly at its cash break-even point is NOT true?","options":["Operating cash flow is zero in every year","The IRR is −100%","The payback period equals the project's life","The NPV equals minus the initial outlay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At cash break-even no cash comes back, so the project never pays back, the IRR is −100% and NPV = −I0. Payback exactly equal to the life describes the accounting break-even, where OCF = D.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 Table 11.1; SM 11-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-409","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the cash break-even quantity always below the accounting break-even quantity when a project has positive depreciation?","options":["Cash break-even uses the after-tax contribution margin, which is larger","Cash break-even discounts the fixed costs, which makes the numerator smaller","Cash break-even leaves out variable costs, which makes the denominator larger","Depreciation is not a cash cost, so cash break-even only has to cover the cash fixed costs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Q_A adds D to the numerator, and D is an accounting charge rather than a cash outflow. Discounting enters only the financial break-even (and raises it), and variable costs sit in the denominator of all three.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 10; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-410","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tri-City Print's new line has a cash break-even of 8,000 units a year, and management forecasts sales of 7,400 units. What does this tell management?","options":["Operating cash flow is negative, so the line needs cash just to keep running","Net income is zero, so the line earns its depreciation in cash","NPV is zero, so the line earns exactly its required return","The line pays back its outlay in exactly its useful life"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sales below Q_C mean (P − v)Q < FC, so OCF < 0. That is worse than the accounting break-even case, where OCF at least equals depreciation. Zero NPV and payback equal to life describe other break-even points.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4; SM 11-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-411","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeland Foods has spare capacity. A one-time order for 5,000 jars is offered at $2.60 a jar. Variable cost is $2.05 a jar, the $480,000 of fixed costs is paid whether or not the order is accepted, and the average cost is $3.10 a jar. What should it do?","options":["Reject: $2.60 is below the average cost of $3.10 a jar","Accept: the $0.55 contribution per jar adds $2,750 to cash flow and fixed costs are paid anyway","Reject: the order must also cover its share of the $480,000 of fixed costs","Accept only if the price exceeds $3.10, so the order shows an accounting profit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixed costs are effectively sunk for this choice, so only the marginal cost (v = $2.05) matters: 5,000 × (2.60 − 2.05) = $2,750 of extra cash. Average cost includes fixed costs that do not change, which is why it misleads.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-2; TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-412","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"In Q_C = FC/(P − v), fixed costs rise by 20% and the contribution margin P − v also rises by 20%. What happens to the cash break-even quantity?","options":["It rises by 20%","It rises by 44%","It is unchanged","It falls by 20%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both the numerator and the denominator are multiplied by 1.20, so Q_C = 1.20 FC/(1.20 (P − v)) is unchanged. A 20% rise in only FC would lift Q_C by 20%, and a 44% rise has no basis in the formula.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-413","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"When taxes are ignored, a project's cash break-even quantity does not depend on its depreciation.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Q_C = FC/(P − v) has no D in it. Depreciation affects the accounting break-even Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v), not the cash break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-414","concept":"fin201-c11-cash-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Mapleview Bikes' project has fixed costs of $1,020,000 a year and a variable cost of $310 per bike. At a price of $480 its cash break-even is 6,000 bikes. If the price is raised to $520 with costs unchanged, what is the new cash break-even?","options":["4,857 bikes","1,962 bikes","4,588 bikes","5,538 bikes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The contribution margin rises from $170 to $210, so Q_C = 1,020,000/210 = 4,857 bikes. Dividing by the whole price (1,962) ignores variable cost, cutting 6,000 by the 23.5% rise in the margin (4,588) treats Q_C as linear in the margin, and scaling 6,000 by the price ratio (5,538) treats Q_C as proportional to 1/P. Q_C = FC/(P − v) must be recomputed with the new margin.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-1; SM 11-7","check":"1020000/(520-310)","option_checks":["1020000/(520-310)","1020000/520","6000*(1-(520-480)/(480-310))","6000*480/520"],"option_errors":[null,"fc-over-price","linear-percent-change","scaled-by-price"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-420","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"At the accounting break-even sales level, a project's _____ is exactly zero.","options":["operating cash flow","NPV","net income","payback period"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Accounting break-even sets net income (and so EBIT and taxes) to zero: Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v). Operating cash flow equals depreciation at that point, not zero.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-421","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"At 4,600 units a year the income statement of a new line shows EBIT of zero, no taxes and net income of zero, while operating cash flow equals the annual depreciation. Which break-even point is this?","options":["Cash break-even","Financial break-even","Payback break-even","Accounting break-even"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net income = 0 defines accounting break-even. Since OCF = NI + D, OCF = D there, which is positive; at cash break-even OCF would be zero and at financial break-even NPV would be zero.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3, 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-422","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Cariboo Trailers will invest $2,640,000 in equipment, depreciated straight-line to zero over 8 years. Fixed costs are $845,000 a year, the price is $31.40 per unit and variable cost is $18.65 per unit. Ignoring taxes, what is the accounting break-even quantity (units per year)?","answer":92156.86,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(845000+2640000/8)/(31.4-18.65)","steps":[{"label":"Annual depreciation D = I0/N","value":330000.0,"check":"2640000/8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Contribution margin P − v","value":12.75,"check":"31.4-18.65","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Accounting break-even Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)","value":92156.86,"check":"(845000+2640000/8)/(31.4-18.65)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["D = 2,640,000/8 = $330,000.00 a year","P − v = 31.40 − 18.65 = $12.75","Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) = (845,000 + 330,000)/12.75 = 92,156.86 units a year"],"keystrokes":["2640000 ÷ 8 = 330,000","845000 + 330000 = 1,175,000","÷ 12.75 = 92,156.86"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-d-in-accounting","label":"Left depreciation out (used FC only)","value":66274.51,"check":"845000/(31.4-18.65)","explain":"That is the cash break-even. Accounting break-even must cover depreciation too."},{"id":"i0-for-d","label":"Added the whole investment instead of one year's depreciation","value":273333.33,"check":"(845000+2640000)/(31.4-18.65)","explain":"Only the annual charge D = I0/N belongs in the numerator."},{"id":"fc-over-price","label":"Divided by the price instead of P − v","value":37420.38,"check":"(845000+330000)/31.4","explain":"The denominator is the contribution margin P − v, not the price."}],"explanation":"Accounting break-even is where net income is zero: Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) with D = I0/N for straight-line to zero. Taxes do not matter because taxable income is zero there.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-1, SM 11-5; TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-423","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":["fin201-c11-financial-breakeven"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Yellowhead Signage plans a project with price $27.50, variable cost $16.20 and fixed costs of $520,000 a year. It needs a $1,350,000 investment, depreciated straight-line to zero over 6 years. The required return is 10%; ignore taxes. If sales sit exactly at the accounting break-even each year, what is the NPV?","answer":-370066.34,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"-1350000+(1350000/6)*((1-(1+0.1)**-6)/0.1)","steps":[{"label":"Annual depreciation D","value":225000.0,"check":"1350000/6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Accounting break-even Q_A","value":65929.2,"check":"(520000+1350000/6)/(27.5-16.2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"OCF at Q_A = (P − v)Q − FC","value":225000.0,"check":"(27.5-16.2)*((520000+1350000/6)/(27.5-16.2))-520000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"NPV = −I0 + OCF × annuity factor","value":-370066.34,"check":"-1350000+(1350000/6)*((1-(1+0.1)**-6)/0.1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["D = 1,350,000/6 = $225,000.00","Q_A = (520,000 + 225,000)/11.30 = 65,929.20 units","OCF = NI + D = 0 + 225,000 = $225,000 every year (check: 11.30 × Q_A − 520,000 = 225,000)","Annuity factor (10%, 6 years) = 4.355261","NPV = −1,350,000 + 225,000 × 4.355261 = −$370,066.34"],"keystrokes":["N = 6, I/Y = 10, PMT = −225000, FV = 0, CPT PV = 979,933.66","979,933.66 − 1,350,000 = −370,066.34"],"errors":[{"id":"profit-not-npv-breakeven","label":"Treated zero profit as zero NPV","value":0.0,"check":"0","explain":"Accounting break-even makes net income zero, but the investment is only recovered with no return, so NPV is negative."},{"id":"cash-for-accounting","label":"Set OCF to zero (cash break-even)","value":-1350000.0,"check":"0-1350000","explain":"At accounting break-even OCF equals depreciation, not zero; the flows of $225,000 are still worth something."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 5-year annuity factor","value":-497072.98,"check":"-1350000+(1350000/6)*((1-(1+0.1)**-5)/0.1)","explain":"The project lasts 6 years, so the annuity factor must use N = 6."}],"explanation":"At accounting break-even OCF = D = I0/N each year, so NPV = I0[(1/N) × annuity factor − 1]. With any positive required return that is negative: the project returns the money invested but never the time value (IRR = 0%).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-16a; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-424","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Yukon Snowmobile Parts has a price of $212 and a variable cost of $139 per unit. Fixed costs are $1,460,000 a year. It invests $5,600,000 in equipment, depreciated straight-line to zero over 7 years, and expects operating cash flow of $1,200,000 a year. Ignoring taxes, what is the accounting break-even quantity?","options":["36,438 units","20,000 units","30,959 units","96,712 units"],"answer":2,"explanation":"D = 5,600,000/7 = $800,000, so Q_A = (1,460,000 + 800,000)/73 = 30,959 units. The $1,200,000 of operating cash flow is a lure: at accounting break-even OCF equals D, not the base-case OCF. Using FC alone gives the cash break-even and using the whole investment overstates the charge.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-5; SM 11-1","check":"(1460000+5600000/7)/(212-139)","option_checks":["(1460000+1200000)/(212-139)","1460000/(212-139)","(1460000+5600000/7)/(212-139)","(1460000+5600000)/(212-139)"],"option_errors":["ocf-in-accounting-breakeven","forgot-d-in-accounting",null,"i0-for-d"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-425","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project must reach accounting break-even at 12,500 units a year. Fixed costs are $288,000, depreciation is $96,000 and variable cost is $14.80 per unit. What selling price achieves that?","options":["$30.72","$37.84","$15.92","$45.52"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Solve Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) for P: P = (FC + D)/Q_A + v = 384,000/12,500 + 14.80 = $45.52. Leaving out v gives only the contribution margin of $30.72, omitting D lowers the price to $37.84, and subtracting v moves the wrong way.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-8","check":"(288000+96000)/12500+14.8","option_checks":["(288000+96000)/12500","288000/12500+14.8","(288000+96000)/12500-14.8","(288000+96000)/12500+14.8"],"option_errors":["forgot-v","forgot-d-in-accounting","sign-on-v",null]},{"id":"fin201-q11-426","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":["fin201-c11-cash-breakeven"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Window Systems has a price of $410, variable cost of $268, fixed costs of $390,000 and depreciation of $150,000 a year; the tax rate is 32%. Use OCF = NI + D at the break-even, then Q = {FC + (OCF − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v), to find the accounting break-even quantity.","answer":3802.82,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(390000+(150000-0.32*150000)/(1-0.32))/(410-268)","steps":[{"label":"OCF at accounting break-even (NI = 0)","value":150000.0,"check":"0+150000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"(OCF − T·D)/(1 − T)","value":150000.0,"check":"(150000-0.32*150000)/(1-0.32)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Numerator FC + (OCF − T·D)/(1 − T)","value":540000.0,"check":"390000+(150000-0.32*150000)/(1-0.32)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Accounting break-even Q_A","value":3802.82,"check":"(390000+(150000-0.32*150000)/(1-0.32))/(410-268)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["NI = 0 at accounting break-even, so OCF = NI + D = $150,000","(OCF − T·D)/(1 − T) = (150,000 − 48,000)/0.68 = 150,000 (the tax rate cancels)","Numerator = 390,000 + 150,000 = 540,000; P − v = 142","Q_A = 540,000/142 = 3,802.82 units, identical to (FC + D)/(P − v)"],"keystrokes":["0.32 × 150000 = 48,000","(150000 − 48000) ÷ 0.68 = 150,000","390000 + 150000 = 540,000","÷ 142 = 3,802.82"],"errors":[{"id":"tax-grossed-up","label":"Divided the margin by (1 − T) as well","value":5592.38,"check":"540000/(142*(1-0.32))","explain":"No tax is paid at accounting break-even, so there is nothing to gross up in the denominator."},{"id":"d-after-tax","label":"Used after-tax depreciation D(1 − T)","value":3464.79,"check":"(390000+150000*(1-0.32))/142","explain":"Depreciation is a pre-tax charge in the break-even condition; the shield was already handled by the general formula."},{"id":"numerator-after-tax","label":"Multiplied the whole numerator by (1 − T)","value":2585.92,"check":"(390000+150000)*(1-0.32)/142","explain":"At zero profit the tax rate cancels completely, so the numerator is just FC + D."}],"explanation":"Because OCF = D at accounting break-even, (OCF − T·D)/(1 − T) = D(1 − T)/(1 − T) = D, so Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) whatever the tax rate. The tax rate is a distractor.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-24c"},{"id":"fin201-q11-427","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts accounting break-even with financial break-even?","options":["Accounting break-even charges the opportunity cost of capital; financial break-even does not","Accounting break-even sets net income to zero and ignores the time value of money; financial sets NPV to zero","Accounting break-even is always higher than financial break-even, because depreciation is included","The two are equal whenever the project has no salvage value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) recovers the investment with no return, while Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v) uses the OCF* that earns the required return, so for a normal project Q_F > Q_A.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 Table 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-428","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement about a project operating exactly at its accounting break-even point is NOT true?","options":["Net income is zero","Taxes paid are zero","The project's NPV is zero","Operating cash flow equals depreciation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At accounting break-even EBIT, taxes and net income are zero and OCF = NI + D = D. The investment comes back with no return (IRR = 0%), so NPV is negative at any positive required return.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3, 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-429","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is clearing the accounting break-even not enough to justify accepting a project?","options":["It counts depreciation as a cash outflow, which makes NPV look worse than it is","It includes interest expense, which does not belong in project cash flows","It ignores variable costs, so the quantity it gives is too low","At accounting break-even the IRR is zero, so NPV is negative at any positive required return"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Accounting break-even earns only the depreciation back (OCF = D, payback equal to the life). Without a return on the money tied up, NPV = I0[(1/N) × annuity factor − 1] < 0. That is why we also need the financial break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3, 11.4; SM 11-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-430","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Muskoka Skiff Co. finds an accounting break-even of 44 skiffs a year against a sales forecast of 58. A director says, \"We clear break-even by 14 skiffs, so the project is safe.\" What is the best response?","options":["Clearing accounting break-even says nothing about NPV, so compute the financial break-even","Agree: any project forecast above accounting break-even has a positive NPV","Agree: above accounting break-even the IRR must exceed the required return","Disagree: the project must still reach its cash break-even before it is safe"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sales above Q_A keep net income positive, but the IRR only exceeds zero, not necessarily the required return. Since Q_F > Q_A, a forecast of 58 skiffs could still fall short of Q_F. Cash break-even is lower than Q_A, so clearing Q_A already clears it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 (Wettway); SM 11 CTQ 9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-431","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which change would lower a project's accounting break-even quantity, all else equal?","options":["Buying more equipment that raises straight-line depreciation","A cut in annual fixed costs","A lower selling price","A higher variable cost per unit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v): smaller FC shrinks the numerator, while more depreciation raises it and a lower P or higher v shrinks the contribution margin in the denominator.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-432","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A pricier machine adds $50,000 to annual straight-line depreciation. Fixed cash costs and the contribution margin of $12.50 per unit are unchanged. What happens to the break-even quantities?","options":["Q_A rises by 4,000 units and Q_C rises by 4,000 units","Q_A falls by 4,000 units and Q_C is unchanged","Q_A rises by 4,000 units and Q_C is unchanged","Both are unchanged, because depreciation is not a cash cost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ΔQ_A = ΔD/(P − v) = 50,000/12.50 = 4,000 units, and it is a rise because D sits in the numerator. Q_C = FC/(P − v) contains no depreciation, so it is unchanged. Depreciation does matter to Q_A even though it is not a cash cost.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3, 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-433","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a project depreciated straight-line to zero, operating at its accounting break-even every year gives a payback period equal to the project's life and an IRR of zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At accounting break-even OCF = D = I0/N each year, so the cash flows add up to exactly I0 over N years: payback = N and IRR = 0%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4; SM 11-16a"},{"id":"fin201-q11-434","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Algonquin Candles sells candles at $18.50 with a variable cost of $7.25 each. Fixed costs are $196,000 a year and depreciation is $59,000 a year. Ignoring taxes, what annual sales revenue is needed to reach the accounting break-even?","answer":419333.33,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(196000+59000)/(18.5-7.25)*18.5","steps":[{"label":"Contribution margin P − v","value":11.25,"check":"18.5-7.25","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Accounting break-even Q_A","value":22666.67,"check":"(196000+59000)/(18.5-7.25)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Break-even revenue P × Q_A","value":419333.33,"check":"(196000+59000)/(18.5-7.25)*18.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["P − v = 18.50 − 7.25 = $11.25","Q_A = (196,000 + 59,000)/11.25 = 22,666.67 candles","Revenue = P × Q_A = 18.50 × 22,666.67 = $419,333.33"],"keystrokes":["196000 + 59000 = 255,000","÷ 11.25 = 22,666.67","× 18.50 = 419,333.33"],"errors":[{"id":"cash-for-accounting","label":"Used the cash break-even quantity","value":322311.11,"check":"196000/(18.5-7.25)*18.5","explain":"That leaves depreciation out; the accounting break-even must cover FC + D."},{"id":"margin-for-revenue","label":"Reported total contribution (FC + D) as revenue","value":255000.0,"check":"196000+59000","explain":"FC + D is the contribution needed, not the sales revenue; revenue is P × Q_A."},{"id":"volume-times-v","label":"Multiplied the quantity by the variable cost","value":164333.33,"check":"(196000+59000)/(18.5-7.25)*7.25","explain":"Revenue is quantity × price, not quantity × variable cost."}],"explanation":"Find Q_A first, then convert to dollars with the price. At revenue of $419,333.33, variable costs are $164,333.33, fixed costs $196,000 and depreciation $59,000: EBIT = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-435","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does the tax rate not appear in Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v)?","options":["Taxes are paid only on cash flows, and operating cash flow is zero at that point","Net income is zero there, so no tax is paid and the rate drops out","Taxes are already included in the fixed costs FC","The depreciation tax shield exactly cancels tax on revenue at every sales level"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Net income = (S − VC − FC − D)(1 − T) = 0 requires S − VC − FC − D = 0 whatever T is, so dividing by (1 − T) removes it. At that point OCF = D, not zero.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.3; SM 11 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-436","concept":"fin201-c11-accounting-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project has an accounting break-even of 4,400 units a year, a price of $85, a variable cost of $52 and fixed costs of $90,000 a year. Its equipment is depreciated straight-line to zero over 5 years. What initial investment does this imply?","answer":276000.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"5*(4400*(85-52)-90000)","steps":[{"label":"Contribution margin P − v","value":33.0,"check":"85-52","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Annual depreciation D = Q_A(P − v) − FC","value":55200.0,"check":"4400*(85-52)-90000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Initial investment I0 = N × D","value":276000.0,"check":"5*(4400*(85-52)-90000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) rearranges to D = Q_A(P − v) − FC","D = 4,400 × 33 − 90,000 = 145,200 − 90,000 = $55,200.00","Straight-line over 5 years: I0 = 5 × 55,200.00 = $276,000.00"],"keystrokes":["4400 × 33 = 145,200","145200 − 90000 = 55,200","× 5 = 276,000"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-fc-in-solve","label":"Did not subtract fixed costs when solving for D","value":726000.0,"check":"5*(4400*(85-52))","explain":"The break-even quantity covers FC + D, so D is what is left after fixed costs."},{"id":"forgot-times-n","label":"Reported one year's depreciation as the investment","value":55200.0,"check":"4400*(85-52)-90000","explain":"Straight-line depreciation is I0/N, so the investment is N times the annual charge."}],"explanation":"Work backwards: D = Q_A(P − v) − FC = $55,200, and with straight-line depreciation to zero over 5 years the investment is I0 = 5D = $276,000.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-10; SM 11-8"},{"id":"fin201-q11-440","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"At the financial break-even sales level, a project's _____ is exactly zero.","options":["net income","operating cash flow","NPV","payback period"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Financial break-even is the sales level that makes NPV = 0, so the project earns exactly its required return. Net income = 0 is the accounting break-even and OCF = 0 is the cash break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-441","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Sault Boatworks finds that at 3,200 boats a year its project's IRR equals the 12% required return and its discounted payback equals the six-year life. Which break-even point has it found?","options":["Accounting break-even","Cash break-even","Payback break-even","Financial break-even"],"answer":3,"explanation":"IRR = R, NPV = 0 and discounted payback equal to the life all describe the financial break-even. Accounting break-even would give IRR = 0% and payback equal to the life, and cash break-even gives IRR = −100%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 Table 11.1; SM 11-16c"},{"id":"fin201-q11-442","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yoho Gear is evaluating a $412,000 project with a 5-year life. Fixed costs are $96,000 a year, the price is $37.80 and variable cost is $24.30 per unit. The required return is 11% and depreciation is straight-line to zero. Ignoring taxes, what is the financial break-even quantity (units per year)?","answer":15368.52,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(96000+412000/((1-(1+0.11)**-5)/0.11))/(37.8-24.3)","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (11%, 5 years)","value":3.6959,"check":"((1-(1+0.11)**-5)/0.11)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Required OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor","value":111474.97,"check":"412000/((1-(1+0.11)**-5)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Financial break-even Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v)","value":15368.52,"check":"(96000+412000/((1-(1+0.11)**-5)/0.11))/(37.8-24.3)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["NPV = 0 means I0 = OCF* × annuity factor, so OCF* = I0 ÷ [(1 − 1.11^−5)/0.11]","Annuity factor = 3.695897; OCF* = 412,000/3.695897 = $111,474.97","P − v = 37.80 − 24.30 = 13.50","Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v) = (96,000 + 111,474.97)/13.50 = 15,368.52 units"],"keystrokes":["N = 5, I/Y = 11, PV = −412000, FV = 0, CPT PMT = 111,474.97 (this is OCF*)","(96000 + 111,474.97) ÷ 13.50 = 15,368.52"],"errors":[{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Used I0/N as the required OCF","value":13214.81,"check":"(96000+412000/5)/(37.8-24.3)","explain":"I0/N is the depreciation, so that is the accounting break-even. OCF* must also earn the required return: divide by the annuity factor."},{"id":"pv-as-ocf","label":"Used the whole investment as one year's OCF","value":37629.63,"check":"(96000+412000)/(37.8-24.3)","explain":"The investment is the PV of the OCF annuity, not one year's OCF; divide by the annuity factor."},{"id":"cash-for-financial","label":"Left OCF* out (cash break-even)","value":7111.11,"check":"96000/(37.8-24.3)","explain":"That sets OCF = 0. Financial break-even needs OCF* large enough to give NPV = 0."}],"explanation":"Financial break-even: find OCF* that makes the PV of the annuity equal I0, then Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v). The order cash (7,111) < accounting (13,215) < financial (15,369) holds.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-9; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-443","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":["fin201-c11-cash-breakeven"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cranbrook Cabinets has a price of $240 and a variable cost of $162 per unit, with fixed costs of $410,000 a year. A $1,500,000 investment is depreciated straight-line to zero over its 5-year life. The tax rate is 35% and the required return is 14%. What is the financial break-even quantity (units per year)?","answer":11803.26,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(410000+(1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)-0.35*300000)/(1-0.35))/(240-162)","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (14%, 5 years)","value":3.4331,"check":"((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Required OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor","value":436925.32,"check":"1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"(OCF* − T·D)/(1 − T)","value":510654.34,"check":"(1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)-0.35*300000)/(1-0.35)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Financial break-even Q_F with taxes","value":11803.26,"check":"(410000+(1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)-0.35*300000)/(1-0.35))/(240-162)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["D = 1,500,000/5 = 300,000; T·D = 0.35 × 300,000 = 105,000","OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor = 1,500,000/3.433081 = $436,925.32","(OCF* − T·D)/(1 − T) = (436,925.32 − 105,000)/0.65 = 510,654.34","Q_F = {FC + (OCF* − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v) = (920,654.34)/78 = 11,803.26 units","Check: [(78 × 11,803.26) − 410,000](0.65) + 105,000 = 436,925.32 = OCF*"],"keystrokes":["N = 5, I/Y = 14, PV = −1500000, FV = 0, CPT PMT = 436,925.32","(436,925.32 − 105000) ÷ 0.65 + 410000 = 920,654.34","÷ 78 = 11,803.26"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-untax","label":"Did not divide (OCF* − T·D) by (1 − T)","value":9511.86,"check":"(410000+1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)-0.35*300000)/(240-162)","explain":"OCF* is after tax but FC and the margin are pre-tax, so the after-tax gap OCF* − T·D must be grossed up by 1/(1 − T)."},{"id":"taxes-ignored","label":"Ignored taxes: (FC + OCF*)/(P − v)","value":10858.02,"check":"(410000+1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14))/(240-162)","explain":"With a 35% tax rate the firm must earn extra pre-tax income to pay tax, so the no-tax formula understates the break-even."},{"id":"shield-sign","label":"Added T·D instead of subtracting it","value":15945.27,"check":"(410000+(1500000/((1-(1+0.14)**-5)/0.14)+0.35*300000)/(1-0.35))/(240-162)","explain":"The depreciation tax shield supplies part of OCF*, so it reduces what the operations must produce."}],"explanation":"With taxes the OCF identity is OCF = [(P − v)Q − FC](1 − T) + T·D. Solving for Q at OCF = OCF* gives Q_F = {FC + (OCF* − T·D)/(1 − T)}/(P − v). Forgetting the ÷(1 − T) understates Q_F by about 2,300 units.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-24; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-444","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project has fixed costs of $240,000 a year, a variable cost of $31 per unit and a 4-year life with straight-line depreciation to zero. Its cash break-even is 9,600 units and its accounting break-even is 13,000 units. The required return is 13%; ignore taxes. What is the financial break-even quantity (units)?","answer":14172.24,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"(240000+(4*(13000*(240000/9600)-240000))/((1-(1+0.13)**-4)/0.13))/(240000/9600)","steps":[{"label":"Contribution margin P − v = FC/Q_C","value":25.0,"check":"240000/9600","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Annual depreciation D = Q_A(P − v) − FC","value":85000.0,"check":"13000*(240000/9600)-240000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Investment I0 = 4 × D","value":340000.0,"check":"4*(13000*(240000/9600)-240000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Required OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor (13%, 4 years)","value":114306.03,"check":"(4*(13000*(240000/9600)-240000))/((1-(1+0.13)**-4)/0.13)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Financial break-even Q_F","value":14172.24,"check":"(240000+(4*(13000*(240000/9600)-240000))/((1-(1+0.13)**-4)/0.13))/(240000/9600)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Q_C = FC/(P − v) gives P − v = 240,000/9,600 = $25 (so P = $56)","Q_A = (FC + D)/(P − v) gives D = 13,000 × 25 − 240,000 = $85,000","Straight-line over 4 years: I0 = 4 × 85,000 = $340,000","OCF* = 340,000/2.974471 = $114,306.03","Q_F = (240,000 + 114,306.03)/25 = 14,172.24 units"],"keystrokes":["240000 ÷ 9600 = 25","13000 × 25 − 240000 = 85,000","× 4 = 340,000","N = 4, I/Y = 13, PV = −340000, FV = 0, CPT PMT = 114,306.03","(240000 + 114,306.03) ÷ 25 = 14,172.24"],"errors":[{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Used I0/N as the required OCF","value":13000.0,"check":"(240000+340000/4)/25","explain":"I0/N = D, which just reproduces the accounting break-even (13,000). OCF* needs the annuity factor."},{"id":"forgot-times-n","label":"Took one year's D as the investment","value":10743.06,"check":"(240000+85000/((1-(1+0.13)**-4)/0.13))/25","explain":"Straight-line depreciation is I0/N, so I0 = 4D = $340,000."},{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used a 5-year annuity factor","value":13466.68,"check":"(240000+340000/((1-(1+0.13)**-5)/0.13))/25","explain":"The project lasts 4 years, so the annuity factor must use N = 4."}],"explanation":"Work backwards through Q_C and Q_A to P − v, D and I0, then find OCF* from the annuity factor. The three answers 9,600 < 13,000 < 14,172 confirm the order cash < accounting < financial.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-10; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-445","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Thompson Valley Distillery has a price of $92 and a variable cost of $57 per case, with fixed costs of $310,000 a year. A $1,900,000 investment is depreciated straight-line to zero over 8 years. The required return is 9% and taxes are ignored. What is the financial break-even quantity?","options":["15,643 cases","8,857 cases","19,643 cases","18,665 cases"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The annuity factor (9%, 8 years) is 5.5348, so OCF* = 1,900,000/5.534819 = $343,281 and Q_F = (310,000 + 343,282)/35 = 18,665 cases. Using I0/N = $237,500 gives the accounting break-even (15,643), and using no OCF* gives the cash break-even (8,857).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-9; TB 11.4","check":"(310000+1900000/((1-(1+0.09)**-8)/0.09))/(92-57)","option_checks":["(310000+1900000/8)/(92-57)","310000/(92-57)","(310000+1900000/((1-(1+0.09)**-7)/0.09))/(92-57)","(310000+1900000/((1-(1+0.09)**-8)/0.09))/(92-57)"],"option_errors":["divided-by-n","cash-for-financial","wrong-n-annuity",null]},{"id":"fin201-q11-446","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bruce Peninsula Canoes earns a contribution margin of $11.50 per canoe and has fixed costs of $150,000 a year. A $780,000 investment is depreciated straight-line to zero over 6 years, the required return is 15% and taxes are ignored. What is the financial break-even quantity?","options":["269,730 canoes","24,348 canoes","13,043 canoes","None of the above"],"answer":3,"explanation":"OCF* = 780,000/3.784483 = $206,105 and Q_F = (150,000 + 206,105)/11.50 = 30,966 canoes, which none of the listed figures equals. Multiplying by the annuity factor, using I0/N (the accounting figure, 24,348) and ignoring OCF* (the cash figure, 13,043) are the classic slips.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-9; TB 11.4","check":"(150000+780000/((1-(1+0.15)**-6)/0.15))/11.5","option_checks":["(150000+780000*((1-(1+0.15)**-6)/0.15))/11.5","(150000+780000/6)/11.5","150000/11.5",null],"option_errors":["multiplied-by-af","divided-by-n","cash-for-financial",null]},{"id":"fin201-q11-447","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the financial break-even quantity compare with the cash break-even quantity?","options":["Financial break-even is lower, because discounting shrinks the cash flows","Financial break-even is higher, because OCF must also repay I0 at the required return","The two are equal, because both set operating cash flow to its break-even level","Financial break-even is lower, because it ignores fixed costs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q_C = FC/(P − v) needs OCF = 0, while Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v) needs OCF* > 0 to give NPV = 0. Discounting raises the OCF required; it does not shrink it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4; SM 11 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q11-448","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about a project operating exactly at its financial break-even point is NOT true?","options":["Its NPV is zero","Its IRR equals the required return","Its ordinary payback exceeds its life","Its discounted payback equals its life"],"answer":2,"explanation":"At financial break-even the PV of the OCFs just equals I0, so NPV = 0, IRR = R and discounted payback = life. Undiscounted flows add up to more than I0, so ordinary payback is shorter than the life, not longer.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 Table 11.1; SM 11-16c"},{"id":"fin201-q11-449","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the financial break-even the most important of the three for shareholders?","options":["It is the lowest of the three, so a project is most likely to pass it","It ignores the time value of money, which keeps the analysis simple","It is unaffected by forecasting risk in the sales volume","A project can clear the other two break-evens yet still have a negative NPV"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Only Q_F = (FC + OCF*)/(P − v) ties the sales level to NPV = 0. Forecast sales above Q_C and Q_A but below Q_F still destroy value. Q_F is the highest of the three, not the lowest.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 9"},{"id":"fin201-q11-450","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Selkirk Vineyards has a financial break-even of 3,010 bottles a year against a sales forecast of 3,100. What does this tell management?","options":["The NPV is positive only if sales hold within about 3% of the forecast, so forecasting risk is high","The project is safe, because the forecast is above the break-even level","The NPV is exactly zero, because the break-even is so close to the forecast","The project pays back in exactly its life, so the IRR is zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Margin of safety = (3,100 − 3,010)/3,100 ≈ 2.9%. NPV is positive at the forecast, but a small miss in volume wipes it out. This is the point the text makes about the Wettway sailboat project.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 (Wettway)"},{"id":"fin201-q11-451","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a project with an initial outlay, positive depreciation and a positive required return, which ordering of the three break-even quantities is always correct?","options":["Cash < financial < accounting","Cash < accounting < financial","Accounting < cash < financial","Financial < accounting < cash"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q_C = FC/(P − v), Q_A adds D, and Q_F adds OCF*, which is larger than D because OCF* must repay I0 with interest. So Q_C < Q_A < Q_F.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-452","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's price, costs, depreciation and investment stay the same but its required return rises. What happens to the three break-even quantities?","options":["All three rise","Q_C, Q_A and Q_F are all unchanged","Q_C and Q_A are unchanged and Q_F rises","Q_F falls, because the annuity factor falls"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The required return enters only through OCF* = I0 ÷ annuity factor. A higher rate lowers the annuity factor, so OCF* and Q_F rise. The annuity factor falling does not lower Q_F, since it is in the denominator of OCF*.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-453","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Pacific Rim Tiles' project has Q_C = 7,100, Q_A = 13,200 and Q_F = 15,400 units a year, and sales are forecast at 14,500 units. What follows?","options":["Accept: forecast sales exceed the accounting break-even, so the project earns a profit and a positive NPV","Accept: operating cash flow is positive, which is all that matters","Reject: the forecast is below the accounting break-even","It clears Q_C and Q_A but not Q_F, so NPV is negative: reject unless other value is found"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Only the financial break-even is tied to NPV. Forecast 14,500 < Q_F = 15,400 means OCF is below OCF*, so NPV < 0 even though accounting profit and OCF are positive.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 9; TB 11.4"},{"id":"fin201-q11-454","concept":"fin201-c11-financial-breakeven","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"At the financial break-even quantity, a project's IRR equals its required return.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Financial break-even is NPV = 0, and the IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV zero, so IRR = R at that sales level.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.4 Table 11.1"},{"id":"fin201-q11-460","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The degree of operating leverage is DOL = 1 + _____ ÷ OCF.","options":["fixed costs plus depreciation","variable costs","fixed costs","depreciation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF. A common slip is to put depreciation into the numerator as well; D is already inside OCF and is not part of the leverage ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-461","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A project's operating cash flow rises 14% when the quantity sold rises 5%. What does the ratio 14%/5% = 2.8 measure?","options":["The profitability index","The internal rate of return","The contribution margin","The degree of operating leverage"],"answer":3,"explanation":"DOL is defined by %ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ, so the ratio %ΔOCF/%ΔQ is the project's degree of operating leverage (2.8 here).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-462","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Bow Valley Signs expects fixed costs of $215,000 and operating cash flow of $96,400 at its forecast volume. Depreciation is $48,000 a year. Ignoring taxes, what is the project's degree of operating leverage?","answer":3.23,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"1+215000/96400","steps":[{"label":"Fixed costs ÷ OCF","value":2.2303,"check":"215000/96400","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF","value":3.2303,"check":"1+215000/96400","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["FC/OCF = 215,000/96,400 = 2.2303","DOL = 1 + 2.2303 = 3.2303, about 3.23"],"keystrokes":["215000 ÷ 96400 = 2.2303","+ 1 = 3.2303"],"errors":[{"id":"fc-plus-d-dol","label":"Added depreciation to fixed costs","value":3.73,"check":"1+(215000+48000)/96400","explain":"Depreciation is not part of the leverage ratio; the numerator is FC alone."},{"id":"forgot-one-in-dol","label":"Dropped the 1","value":2.23,"check":"215000/96400","explain":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF. With zero fixed costs DOL is 1, not 0."},{"id":"inverted-dol","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":1.45,"check":"1+96400/215000","explain":"The ratio is fixed costs divided by OCF, not OCF divided by fixed costs."}],"explanation":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF = 1 + 215,000/96,400 = 3.23: each 1% change in quantity moves OCF by 3.23%. The $48,000 of depreciation is a distractor.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-13; TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-463","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project has a degree of operating leverage of 2.65 at 18,000 units a year. Volume rises to 19,500 units. By what percentage does operating cash flow rise?","answer":22.08,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"2.65*(19500-18000)/18000*100","steps":[{"label":"Percentage change in quantity","value":8.3333,"check":"(19500-18000)/18000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ","value":22.0833,"check":"2.65*(19500-18000)/18000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["%ΔQ = (19,500 − 18,000)/18,000 = 8.3333%","%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ = 2.65 × 8.3333% = 22.08%"],"keystrokes":["19500 − 18000 = 1500","÷ 18000 = 0.083333","× 2.65 = 0.2208 → 22.08%"],"errors":[{"id":"dol-minus-one","label":"Used DOL − 1 as the multiplier","value":13.75,"check":"1.65*(19500-18000)/18000*100","explain":"The multiplier is DOL itself: %ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ."},{"id":"divided-instead-of-times","label":"Divided %ΔQ by DOL","value":3.14,"check":"(19500-18000)/18000*100/2.65","explain":"Leverage magnifies: OCF changes by DOL times the percentage change in quantity."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Gave the answer as a decimal instead of a percent","value":0.22,"check":"2.65*(19500-18000)/18000","explain":"The answer box is in %, so 0.2208 must be written as 22.08."}],"explanation":"%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ = 2.65 × 8.33% = 22.08%. Because OCF is linear in Q, this relationship is exact for any size of change, though DOL itself then falls.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-11; TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-464","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prince George Tire has a degree of operating leverage of 3.40 at 48,000 tires a year, and fixed costs of $210,000. Ignoring taxes, volume falls to 43,000 tires. What is the new degree of operating leverage?","answer":4.72,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"1+210000/(210000/(3.4-1)*(1+3.4*(43000-48000)/48000))","steps":[{"label":"Current OCF = FC/(DOL − 1)","value":87500.0,"check":"210000/(3.4-1)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"%ΔQ","value":-10.4167,"check":"(43000-48000)/48000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ","value":-35.4167,"check":"3.4*(43000-48000)/48000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"New OCF","value":56510.42,"check":"210000/(3.4-1)*(1+3.4*(43000-48000)/48000)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"New DOL = 1 + FC/new OCF","value":4.7161,"check":"1+210000/(210000/(3.4-1)*(1+3.4*(43000-48000)/48000))","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["DOL = 1 + FC/OCF, so OCF = FC/(DOL − 1) = 210,000/2.40 = $87,500","%ΔQ = (43,000 − 48,000)/48,000 = −10.4167%","%ΔOCF = 3.40 × (−10.4167%) = −35.4167%","New OCF = 87,500 × (1 − 0.3542) = $56,510.42","New DOL = 1 + 210,000/56,510.42 = 4.7161, about 4.72"],"keystrokes":["210000 ÷ (3.40 − 1) = 87,500","(43000 − 48000) ÷ 48000 = −0.104167","× 3.40 = −0.354167","87500 × (1 − 0.354167) = 56,510.42","210000 ÷ 56,510.42 + 1 = 4.7161"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-dol-changes","label":"Kept the old DOL of 3.40","value":3.4,"check":"3.4","explain":"DOL depends on the current OCF; when volume falls, OCF falls and FC/OCF rises, so DOL rises."},{"id":"ocf-proportional","label":"Cut OCF in proportion to volume","value":3.68,"check":"1+210000/(210000/(3.4-1)*43000/48000)","explain":"OCF falls by DOL × %ΔQ (35.4%), not by %ΔQ (10.4%), because fixed costs do not fall."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Raised OCF instead of cutting it","value":2.77,"check":"1+210000/(210000/(3.4-1)*(1-3.4*(43000-48000)/48000))","explain":"Volume fell, so %ΔQ is negative and OCF falls."}],"explanation":"Back out OCF from the DOL, apply %ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ, then recompute DOL on the lower OCF. Operating leverage rises as volume falls, so the project becomes more fragile.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-12; TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-465","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Thunder Bay Cabinets sells 12,000 units a year at $150 with a variable cost of $90. Fixed costs are $480,000 and depreciation is $75,000 a year. Ignoring taxes, what is its degree of operating leverage?","options":["3.31","3.91","2.00","3.00"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Without taxes OCF = (P − v)Q − FC = 60 × 12,000 − 480,000 = $240,000, so DOL = 1 + 480,000/240,000 = 3.00. Adding D to FC gives 3.31, using EBIT (after the $75,000 depreciation) as if it were OCF gives 3.91, and dropping the 1 gives 2.00.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-13, SM 11-18","check":"1+480000/((150-90)*12000-480000)","option_checks":["1+(480000+75000)/((150-90)*12000-480000)","1+480000/((150-90)*12000-480000-75000)","480000/((150-90)*12000-480000)","1+480000/((150-90)*12000-480000)"],"option_errors":["fc-plus-d-dol","ebit-for-ocf","forgot-one-in-dol",null]},{"id":"fin201-q11-466","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Muskoka Spa Covers earns operating cash flow of $150,000 at 20,000 units a year and has fixed costs of $330,000. If volume falls 10% (ignoring taxes), what is the new operating cash flow?","options":["$102,000","$135,000","$117,000","$198,000"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DOL = 1 + 330,000/150,000 = 3.2, so %ΔOCF = 3.2 × (−10%) = −32% and new OCF = 150,000 × 0.68 = $102,000. Cutting OCF only 10% ignores the fixed costs, which do not fall with volume.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-11, SM 11-14","check":"150000*(1-(1+330000/150000)*0.10)","option_checks":["150000*(1-(1+330000/150000)*0.10)","150000*(1-0.10)","150000*(1-(330000/150000)*0.10)","150000*(1+(1+330000/150000)*0.10)"],"option_errors":[null,"ocf-moves-with-q","forgot-one-in-dol","sign-error"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-467","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kelowna Kitchenware has fixed costs of $648,000 a year and a $1,120,000 investment depreciated straight-line to zero over 7 years. Ignoring taxes, what is the degree of operating leverage when it sells exactly the accounting break-even quantity?","answer":5.05,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"1+648000/(1120000/7)","steps":[{"label":"Annual depreciation D = I0/N","value":160000.0,"check":"1120000/7","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"OCF at accounting break-even = D","value":160000.0,"check":"1120000/7","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"DOL = 1 + FC/D","value":5.05,"check":"1+648000/(1120000/7)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["D = 1,120,000/7 = $160,000.00","At accounting break-even NI = 0, so OCF = NI + D = D = $160,000","DOL = 1 + FC/OCF = 1 + 648,000/160,000 = 5.05"],"keystrokes":["1120000 ÷ 7 = 160,000","648000 ÷ 160000 = 4.05","+ 1 = 5.05"],"errors":[{"id":"fc-plus-d-dol","label":"Added depreciation to fixed costs","value":6.05,"check":"1+(648000+160000)/(1120000/7)","explain":"The numerator is FC alone; D appears only as the OCF in the denominator at this special point."},{"id":"forgot-one-in-dol","label":"Dropped the 1","value":4.05,"check":"648000/(1120000/7)","explain":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF."},{"id":"i0-for-d","label":"Used the whole investment as OCF","value":1.58,"check":"1+648000/1120000","explain":"OCF equals one year's depreciation (I0/N), not the whole investment."}],"explanation":"At accounting break-even OCF = D, so DOL = 1 + FC/D = 5.05. A 1% rise in volume above break-even lifts OCF by 5.05%: leverage is highest near break-even.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-5a; TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-468","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two plans for the same product both earn operating cash flow of $400,000 at forecast volume. Plan X (in-house production) has fixed costs of $600,000 a year; Plan Y (subcontracting) has fixed costs of $120,000. Which statement is correct?","options":["Plan Y has the higher DOL, because its fixed costs are lower","The DOLs are equal, because both plans earn the same OCF","Plan X has DOL 2.5 against 1.3 for Plan Y, so its OCF is more exposed","Plan X has the lower DOL, because it earns more per unit sold"],"answer":2,"explanation":"DOL_X = 1 + 600/400 = 2.5 and DOL_Y = 1 + 120/400 = 1.3. Equal OCF does not mean equal leverage: DOL depends on FC/OCF, so the plan with the heavier fixed costs has more forecasting risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5 (Wettway subcontracting)"},{"id":"fin201-q11-469","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a consequence of a high degree of operating leverage?","options":["Small percentage changes in volume cause large percentage changes in operating cash flow","Errors in the sales forecast are magnified in the cash flow forecast","Break-even quantities tend to be higher","Operating cash flow becomes more stable when sales volume fluctuates"],"answer":3,"explanation":"High fixed costs magnify volume swings in OCF (%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ), raise forecasting risk and push the break-even points up. Stability of OCF is the opposite of what leverage does.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5; SM 11 CTQ 7"},{"id":"fin201-q11-470","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does a project's degree of operating leverage fall as the quantity sold rises?","options":["OCF grows with volume while fixed costs stay put, so FC/OCF shrinks","Variable costs per unit fall as volume grows","The contribution margin per unit shrinks as volume grows","Depreciation per unit falls, which lowers the numerator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF. OCF = (P − v)Q − FC rises with Q, while FC is constant, so FC/OCF and DOL decline (the text's sailboat: DOL 2.0 at 50 boats, 1.5 at 75).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-471","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's degree of operating leverage is 4.5 at its forecast volume. What does this mean?","options":["Each 1% change in units sold changes operating cash flow by 0.45%","Each 1% change in units sold changes operating cash flow by 4.5%","Operating cash flow is 4.5 times the fixed costs","The project breaks even at 4.5 times the forecast volume"],"answer":1,"explanation":"%ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ = 4.5 × 10% = 45%. Since DOL = 1 + FC/OCF, FC/OCF = 3.5, so fixed costs are 3.5 times OCF, not the other way round.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-472","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Muskoka Cycles can make frames in-house (DOL 1.85 at forecast volume) or subcontract them (DOL 1.25). The two plans have similar NPVs, and management fears its sales forecast is too optimistic. Which plan is better?","options":["In-house: the higher DOL protects OCF if volume falls short","Subcontract: a lower DOL guarantees a higher NPV","Subcontract: the lower DOL means a volume forecasting error is magnified less in OCF","Either: once NPVs are equal, operating leverage does not affect forecasting risk"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With NPVs level, the plan with lower operating leverage is less exposed to forecasting error: a 10% volume miss cuts OCF by about 12.5% rather than 18.5%. Low DOL lowers risk; it does not raise NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-473","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the degree of operating leverage of a project with zero fixed costs?","options":["0.00","2.00","It is undefined","1.00"],"answer":3,"explanation":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF = 1 + 0 = 1: percentage changes in quantity pass one for one into OCF, so there is no magnification.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-474","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's degree of operating leverage stays the same whatever quantity is sold.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF depends on the current OCF. As Q rises, OCF rises and DOL falls (the text's sailboat: 2.0 at 50 boats, 1.5 at 75). The relationship %ΔOCF = DOL × %ΔQ holds from the starting point.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-475","concept":"fin201-c11-dol","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Selkirk Boats sells 5,000 boats a year at $1,450 with a variable cost of $892 per boat. Fixed costs are $2,050,000 and depreciation is $310,000 a year. Ignore taxes. Using the DOL, what is operating cash flow if volume rises 8%?","answer":963200.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"((1450-892)*5000-2050000)*(1+(1+2050000/((1450-892)*5000-2050000))*0.08)","steps":[{"label":"Current OCF = (P − v)Q − FC","value":740000.0,"check":"(1450-892)*5000-2050000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF","value":3.7703,"check":"1+2050000/((1450-892)*5000-2050000)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"%ΔOCF = DOL × 8%","value":30.1622,"check":"(1+2050000/((1450-892)*5000-2050000))*8","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"New OCF","value":963200.0,"check":"((1450-892)*5000-2050000)*(1+(1+2050000/((1450-892)*5000-2050000))*0.08)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["OCF = (1,450 − 892) × 5,000 − 2,050,000 = 2,790,000 − 2,050,000 = $740,000","DOL = 1 + 2,050,000/740,000 = 3.7703","%ΔOCF = 3.7703 × 8% = 30.1622%","New OCF = 740,000 × (1 + 0.30162) = $963,200.00","Check: (558 × 5,400) − 2,050,000 = 963,200"],"keystrokes":["558 × 5000 − 2050000 = 740,000","1 + 2050000 ÷ 740000 = 3.7703","× 8 = 30.16%","740000 × 1.3016 = 963,200.00"],"errors":[{"id":"ocf-moves-with-q","label":"Raised OCF by only 8%","value":799200.0,"check":"((1450-892)*5000-2050000)*1.08","explain":"Fixed costs do not rise with volume, so OCF rises by DOL × 8%, not 8%."},{"id":"forgot-one-in-dol","label":"Dropped the 1 from the DOL","value":904000.0,"check":"((1450-892)*5000-2050000)*(1+(2050000/((1450-892)*5000-2050000))*0.08)","explain":"DOL = 1 + FC/OCF; the 1 is the part of the swing that is not leverage."},{"id":"fc-plus-d-dol","label":"Added depreciation to fixed costs in the DOL","value":988000.0,"check":"((1450-892)*5000-2050000)*(1+(1+(2050000+310000)/((1450-892)*5000-2050000))*0.08)","explain":"Depreciation is not part of FC in the leverage ratio."}],"explanation":"OCF = (P − v)Q − FC = $740,000, DOL = 3.77, so +8% volume raises OCF by about 30.2% to $963,200. The direct check (558 × 5,400 − 2,050,000) gives the same result, confirming DOL is exact for OCF.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-13, SM 11-14; TB 11.5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-480","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A real option is the right, but not the _____, to undertake a business initiative in the future.","options":["premium","probability","obligation","discount"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An option is a right, not an obligation: the holder decides in the future, using information not yet available, whether to act. If action were compulsory it would be a commitment, not an option.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-481","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement defines an option?","options":["A binding contract to carry out a business activity on a fixed future date","A forecast of project cash flows under best, base and worst cases","A probability-weighted average of the NPVs of several projects","A right, but not an obligation, to do something in the future"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The lecture defines an option as a right (but not an obligation) to act later, once more is known. A binding contract is a commitment, a three-case forecast is scenario analysis and a probability-weighted average is an expected value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-482","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Atlantic Bakery builds its new plant with extra foundations and utility connections so that it can add a second production line later if demand proves strong. Which type of real option is this?","options":["Expansion","Abandonment","Timing","Production flexibility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Paying a little more now so that output can be increased later is the expansion option (the lecture's bigger machine or extra space). Abandonment is the right to exit early, and timing is the right to choose when to act.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-483","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A start-up trucking firm negotiates a clause that lets it resell its rigs at an agreed price after year 2 if its routes prove unprofitable. Which type of real option does the clause create?","options":["Expansion","Abandonment","Timing","Production flexibility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The right to exit a project early if it is not performing is the abandonment option, and the agreed resale price puts a floor under its value. Nothing in the clause adds capacity (expansion) or changes when the firm acts (timing).","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-484","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A brewery can launch a canned seltzer this spring, or wait a year to see whether a rival's launch succeeds and then decide. What type of real option is the right to wait?","options":["Expansion","Abandonment","Timing","Production flexibility"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Choosing the best time to act, after more information arrives, is the timing option (the lecture's example is the best time to replace a machine). Waiting does not add capacity or end an existing project.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-485","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A Saskatchewan fertilizer plant is built so that it can switch its output between two product grades as relative prices change. What type of real option is this?","options":["Expansion","Abandonment","Timing","Production flexibility"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Being able to change the rate or type of production according to the market is production flexibility. The plant stays in operation, so this is neither abandonment nor expansion.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-486","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts a financial option with a real option?","options":["A financial option is a right to buy or sell an asset; a real option is a right to undertake a business activity","A financial option carries an obligation to exercise; a real option does not","A financial option has a premium; a real option is always free","A real option must be exercised on a fixed date; a financial option may lapse"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Both are rights without obligations and both usually cost something up front (the premium). They differ in what the right is about: a financial asset, or a business initiative.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-487","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the option to expand and the option to abandon differ in the way they add value?","options":["Expansion limits the loss when things go badly; abandonment captures more upside","Expansion captures more upside when things go well; abandonment limits the loss when they go badly","Both lower the project's risk by cutting off its upside","Expansion is a financial option while abandonment is a real option"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expansion is a call-like right to do more if the news is good; abandonment is a put-like right to sell the assets if the news is bad. Both are real options and neither cuts off the upside.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12; SM 11 CTQ 5, 6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-488","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a real option?","options":["The right to enlarge a plant later if demand turns out strong","The right to sell a project's assets early if it performs badly","A best, base and worst-case table of NPVs for different sales forecasts","The right to postpone a launch until more is known"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A scenario table changes inputs together to show the range of NPVs; it gives the firm no right to do anything. The other three are the expansion, abandonment and timing options.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5, s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-489","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does ignoring real options tend to understate a project's NPV rather than overstate it?","options":["Options raise the discount rate, which lowers the NPV of a fixed plan","Option premiums are tax deductible, so ignoring them lowers cash flows","A fixed-plan DCF gives too much weight to the best case","An option is used only when it pays, so it can only add value, and a fixed-plan DCF leaves it out"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Because the firm uses an option only when it pays, it cannot make the project worse and often makes it better. A static DCF assumes the plan is fixed and values none of that flexibility (SM: ignoring the option to abandon underestimates NPV).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 5; L05C s11"},{"id":"fin201-q11-490","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we usually pay a premium up front to hold an option?","options":["The right without the obligation is valuable, because the holder uses it only when it pays","The premium is the NPV the project is expected to earn","The premium compensates the seller for the holder's obligation to exercise","The premium is the resale value of the assets if the option is exercised"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Car insurance is the lecture's example. The point is that the right is worth money, so the person who grants it must be compensated. The challenge is to value the option, that is, to find a fair premium for the risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11"},{"id":"fin201-q11-491","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project's static NPV is −$0.8 million. Analysts value its option to expand at $1.3 million. How should the result be interpreted?","options":["The project is worth −$0.8 million and should be rejected, because the option cannot be counted","The project including its option is worth $0.5 million, so it is worth undertaking","The project is worth $2.1 million, found by subtracting the static NPV from the option value","The project is worth $1.3 million, because the option replaces the static NPV"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The option adds to the static value: −0.8 + 1.3 = +$0.5 million. Dropping the option leaves the NPV too low, and the option does not replace the static NPV or enter with the wrong sign.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11; SM 11 CTQ 5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-492","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A highway authority can build bridges sized for two lanes, or pay more now for longer bridges that make a later widening to three lanes cheap. How should the extra up-front cost of the longer bridges be viewed?","options":["As a sunk cost that should be ignored","As a financing cost that should be excluded from the project","As the premium paid for a real option to expand the road later","As a side effect on the existing bridges"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Paying more now to keep the right to expand cheaply later is exactly the purchase of an expansion option (the lecture's freeway bridges). It is a new, avoidable cost, not a sunk or financing item.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s13-14"},{"id":"fin201-q11-493","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"When we value a real option, what do we assume about the future decision date?","options":["That the firm will follow the original plan whatever it learns","That the firm will exercise the option in every state of the world","That the firm will choose the branch with the highest probability","That the firm will make the best financial decision given the information available then"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An option is valued by assuming the firm uses the right only when it is worth using. That is what lets us decide at each later node first and then work backwards (decision trees).","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11"},{"id":"fin201-q11-494","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"How is the value of a real option measured?","options":["Project value with the flexibility minus value without it","The premium the firm pays up front to obtain it","The best-case NPV minus the worst-case NPV","The probability of exercise times the base-case NPV"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A real option's value is what the flexibility adds: NPV with the option minus NPV without. The premium is its price, which should not exceed that value. The best-minus-worst gap is a range, not an option value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11, s16; SM 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-495","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the freeway bridges example, in which order do the events occur?","options":["Learn whether the road is busy or quiet, then choose the bridges, then decide whether to widen","Choose short or long bridges, then learn whether the road is busy or quiet, then decide whether to widen","Decide whether to widen, then choose the bridges, then learn whether the road is busy or quiet","Choose the bridges, then decide whether to widen, then learn whether the road is busy or quiet"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Today's decision is the bridges, the unknown arrives next (busy or quiet, with probabilities), and the future decision (widen or not) comes last, once the information is known. That timeline is why we solve the tree from the last decision backwards.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s14"},{"id":"fin201-q11-496","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A real option obliges the firm to carry out the business activity if conditions turn favourable.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An option is a right, not an obligation. The firm decides later, using the then-available information, whether to exercise.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-497","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":2,"stem":"Standard NPV analysis based on expected cash flows tends to ignore the value of managerial options.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A fixed-plan DCF values the expected flows of one plan and puts no value on the right to expand, abandon, wait or switch later. That is why options are analysed separately, for example with decision trees.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.7; SM 11 CTQ 5"},{"id":"fin201-q11-498","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Muskoka Cycles can subcontract its frame production now or build an in-house plant. Subcontracting has a slightly lower NPV, but if sales boom the firm can start producing in-house later. Why might it still choose to subcontract?","options":["Lower operating leverage plus an option to move production in-house later, which NPV omits","Real options never add value, so the lower NPV is a mistake","Subcontracting has no fixed costs, so it can never lose money","Choosing the plan with the lower NPV maximizes shareholder wealth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The text notes that it is much easier to increase operating leverage later (buy equipment) than to reduce it. The lower DOL and the option to expand are worth something that the static NPV omits, though the choice should rest on a value comparison.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 11.5; L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-499","concept":"fin201-c11-real-options","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The lecture describes a car insurance policy as a put option. In that example, what is the option's premium?","options":["The deductible","The price paid for the policy","The payout after a claim","The value of the car"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The premium is what we pay up front for the right. Here it is the cost of the policy; the payout is what the right is worth if it is used.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11"},{"id":"fin201-q11-500","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"To solve a decision tree we start at the _____ decision node and work back to today.","options":["earliest","most likely","latest","cheapest"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The later decision determines the value that flows back to the earlier node, so we decide at the latest node first and then probability-weight on the way back.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-501","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"In a decision-tree diagram, \"the road will be busy with probability 80% and quiet with probability 20%\" is shown at which kind of node?","options":["A decision node, valued by taking the best branch","A terminal node, valued by discounting its payoff","A decision node, valued by averaging its branches","A chance node, valued by probability-weighting its branches"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Nature, not the firm, chooses between busy and quiet, so the node carries probabilities and is valued as Σp × V. A decision node is where the firm chooses and takes the best branch, never an average.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s14, s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-502","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Prairie Cold Storage must choose between a compact warehouse ($9.5M) and an expandable one ($12.8M). Demand will be high (probability 0.65) or low (0.35). If high, the compact building needs a $7.2M add-on and the expandable one only $2.6M; leaving high demand unserved costs $10.8M in lost margin. If low, nothing more is spent. All amounts are present values. What is the expected total cost today of the cheaper design, in $ millions?","answer":14.18,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"9.5+0.65*7.2","steps":[{"label":"Compact, high-demand node: cheaper of add-on $7.2M or lost margin $10.8M","value":7.2,"check":"7.2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expandable, high-demand node: cheaper of add-on $2.6M or lost margin $10.8M","value":2.6,"check":"2.6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected total cost, compact = 9.5 + 0.65 × 7.2","value":14.18,"check":"9.5+0.65*7.2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected total cost, expandable = 12.8 + 0.65 × 2.6","value":14.49,"check":"12.8+0.65*2.6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Decide at the latest node first: if demand is high, add on (7.2 < 10.8 for compact; 2.6 < 10.8 for expandable); if low, spend nothing","Compact: 9.5 + 0.65 × 7.2 + 0.35 × 0 = $14.18M","Expandable: 12.8 + 0.65 × 2.6 + 0.35 × 0 = $14.49M","The lower expected cost is the compact design: $14.18 million (the amounts are already present values, so no further discounting)"],"keystrokes":["0.65 × 7.2 = 4.68","+ 9.5 = 14.18","0.65 × 2.6 = 1.69","+ 12.8 = 14.49"],"errors":[{"id":"averaged-before-deciding","label":"Averaged add-on and lost-margin costs before choosing","value":15.35,"check":"9.5+0.65*((7.2+10.8)/2)","explain":"At the high-demand node the firm picks the cheaper branch; averaging $7.2M and $10.8M pretends it might leave demand unserved."},{"id":"prob-swapped","label":"Used 0.35 as the chance of the add-on","value":12.02,"check":"9.5+0.35*7.2","explain":"The add-on happens when demand is high, which has probability 0.65."},{"id":"picked-dearer-design","label":"Reported the expandable design's cost","value":14.49,"check":"12.8+0.65*2.6","explain":"The question asks for the cheaper design; the compact building costs less in expectation."}],"explanation":"Solve backwards: at each high-demand node choose the cheaper branch, then probability-weight. Compact costs $14.18M against $14.49M for expandable, so compact is cheaper by $0.31M in expected terms.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s13-16 (freeway bridges)"},{"id":"fin201-q11-503","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bayview Brewing can run a $1.8M pilot of a new line. If demand proves strong (probability 0.35), a national rollout has an NPV of +$9.6M; if weak (0.65), rollout would have an NPV of −$4.2M, so the firm would decline it. Rollout NPVs are present values before the pilot cost and already reflect Bayview's 10% required return. What is the expected NPV of running the pilot, in $ millions?","answer":1.56,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"0.35*9.6+0.65*0-1.8","steps":[{"label":"Weak-demand decision node: decline rollout","value":0.0,"check":"0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected value of the rollout opportunity","value":3.36,"check":"0.35*9.6+0.65*0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Value of the pilot today","value":1.56,"check":"0.35*9.6+0.65*0-1.8","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Decide first at the weak-demand node: declining (0) beats rolling out (−4.2)","Chance node: 0.35 × 9.6 + 0.65 × 0 = 3.36","Pilot value = 3.36 − 1.8 = $1.56 million; the NPVs are already present values, so no more discounting"],"keystrokes":["0.35 × 9.6 = 3.36","+ 0.65 × 0 = 3.36","− 1.8 = 1.56"],"errors":[{"id":"averaged-before-deciding","label":"Rolled out in the weak state too","value":-1.17,"check":"0.35*9.6+0.65*(-4.2)-1.8","explain":"The firm would decline the rollout when demand is weak, so that branch is worth 0, not −4.2."},{"id":"discounted-twice","label":"Discounted the NPVs again at 10%","value":1.25,"check":"(0.35*9.6)/1.1-1.8","explain":"The branch values are already present values; discounting again counts the time value twice."},{"id":"forgot-extra-cost","label":"Left out the $1.8M pilot cost","value":3.36,"check":"0.35*9.6","explain":"The pilot is the up-front price of the rollout option and must be subtracted."}],"explanation":"Choose at the latest node first (decline when weak), then probability-weight the branches and subtract the pilot cost. The positive $1.56M shows the pilot's option to roll out is worth more than its cost.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16; SM 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-504","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cedar Foods can test-market a new line for $1.0M. With probability 0.5 the test passes; the firm then chooses between a regional launch (NPV $3.2M for sure) and a national launch (probability 0.6 of $9.0M, probability 0.4 of −$2.0M). If the test fails, it stops. All NPVs are present values before the test cost. What is the test-market worth today, in $ millions?","answer":1.3,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"-1.0+0.5*(0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0))+0.5*0","steps":[{"label":"Expected value of the national launch","value":4.6,"check":"0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Decision node after a pass: larger of regional 3.2 and national","value":4.6,"check":"0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected value of the test outcomes","value":2.3,"check":"0.5*(0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0))+0.5*0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Value of the test-market today","value":1.3,"check":"-1.0+0.5*(0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0))+0.5*0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["National launch: 0.6 × 9.0 + 0.4 × (−2.0) = 4.6","Decision after a pass: national (4.6) beats regional (3.2), so carry back 4.6","Chance node at the test: 0.5 × 4.6 + 0.5 × 0 = 2.3","Value today = −1.0 + 2.3 = $1.30 million"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 9.0 + 0.4 × (−2.0) = 4.6","0.5 × 4.6 = 2.3","2.3 − 1.0 = 1.30"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-safe-branch","label":"Took the sure regional launch","value":0.6,"check":"-1.0+0.5*3.2","explain":"The national launch has the higher expected value (4.6 versus 3.2); at a decision node the firm takes the larger value."},{"id":"averaged-before-deciding","label":"Averaged the two launch options","value":0.95,"check":"-1.0+0.5*((3.2+(0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0)))/2)","explain":"A decision node takes the best branch; averaging 3.2 and 4.6 treats the choice as random."},{"id":"forgot-extra-cost","label":"Ignored the $1.0M test cost","value":2.3,"check":"0.5*(0.6*9.0+0.4*(-2.0))","explain":"The test cost is paid today whatever the outcome."}],"explanation":"Work from the right: the national chance node (4.6) feeds the decision node, which picks the better of 4.6 and 3.2; only then probability-weight the test outcome and subtract the cost.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16; SM 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-505","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":["fin201-c11-real-options"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Gulf Islands Resorts can invest now (NPV +$3.0M) or wait a year for demand to become clear. If demand is good (probability 0.6), investing then has a present-value NPV of $6.5M; if poor (0.4), it would be −$2.5M, so the firm would not invest. These are present values at a 10% required return. How much more is waiting worth than investing now, in $ millions?","answer":0.9,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"0.6*6.5+0.4*0-3.0","steps":[{"label":"Poor-demand decision node: do not invest","value":0.0,"check":"0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Value of waiting = 0.6 × 6.5 + 0.4 × 0","value":3.9,"check":"0.6*6.5+0.4*0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Waiting minus investing now","value":0.9,"check":"0.6*6.5+0.4*0-3.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Decide at the poor-demand node first: not investing (0) beats −2.5","Value of waiting = 0.6 × 6.5 + 0.4 × 0 = 3.9","Extra value of waiting = 3.9 − 3.0 = $0.90 million (no discounting: the values are present values)"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 6.5 = 3.9","3.9 − 3.0 = 0.90"],"errors":[{"id":"averaged-before-deciding","label":"Invested in the poor state too","value":-0.1,"check":"0.6*6.5+0.4*(-2.5)-3.0","explain":"If demand is poor the firm will not invest, so that branch is 0; including −2.5 makes waiting look worse than investing now."},{"id":"discounted-twice","label":"Discounted the waiting value again","value":0.55,"check":"(0.6*6.5)/1.1-3.0","explain":"The NPVs are already present values, so no further discounting is needed."},{"id":"forgot-alternative","label":"Reported the value of waiting without subtracting NPV now","value":3.9,"check":"0.6*6.5","explain":"The question asks how much more waiting is worth than investing now: subtract the $3.0M."}],"explanation":"Waiting lets the firm avoid the poor-demand loss, so it is worth $3.9M against $3.0M now. The $0.90M difference is the value of the timing option.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12, s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-506","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Wolf Creek's overpass can be built narrow ($85M) or wide ($110M). Traffic will be heavy (probability 0.7) or light (0.3). If heavy, the narrow overpass needs a $30M rebuild and the wide one a $12M widening; doing nothing would cost society $90M. If light, nothing more is spent. All amounts are present values. What is the expected total cost of the cheaper design?","options":["$106.0 million","$118.4 million","$127.0 million","$115.0 million"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At the heavy-traffic node the cheaper action is the rebuild ($30M, not $90M). Narrow: 85 + 0.7 × 30 = $106.0M; wide: 110 + 0.7 × 12 = $118.4M. Averaging $30M with $90M, or adding the rebuild with certainty, gives wrong totals.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s13-16","check":"85+0.7*30","option_checks":["85+0.7*30","110+0.7*12","85+0.7*((30+90)/2)","85+30"],"option_errors":[null,"picked-dearer-design","averaged-before-deciding","forgot-probability"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-507","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kodiak Energy pays $5.0M for a drilling lease. With probability 0.4 it finds oil worth $14.0M; with probability 0.6 the well is dry, and the firm then either sells the lease for $1.5M or spends $3.0M on a deeper well (probability 0.2 of $9.0M, 0.8 of nothing). All amounts are present values. What is the lease's expected NPV today?","options":["$0.69 million","$1.50 million","−$0.12 million","$0.60 million"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At the dry-hole node the deeper well is worth 0.2 × 9.0 − 3.0 = −$1.2M, so the firm sells the lease for $1.5M. Then NPV = −5.0 + 0.4 × 14.0 + 0.6 × 1.5 = $1.50M. Averaging the two choices, or drilling deeper, gives wrong answers; ignoring the dry branch drops the lease sale.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16; SM 11-21","check":"-5.0+0.4*14.0+0.6*1.5","option_checks":["-5.0+0.4*14.0+0.6*((1.5+(0.2*9.0-3.0))/2)","-5.0+0.4*14.0+0.6*1.5","-5.0+0.4*14.0+0.6*(0.2*9.0-3.0)","-5.0+0.4*14.0"],"option_errors":["averaged-before-deciding",null,"picked-wrong-branch","omitted-branch"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-508","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A compact warehouse costs $9.5M and an expandable one $12.8M. If demand turns out high (probability p), the compact building needs a $7.2M add-on and the expandable one $2.6M; if demand is low, neither needs one. Amounts are present values. At what probability of high demand are the two designs equally costly? Answer in %.","answer":71.74,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(12.8-9.5)/(7.2-2.6)*100","steps":[{"label":"Extra up-front cost of the expandable design","value":3.3,"check":"12.8-9.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Add-on saving if demand is high","value":4.6,"check":"7.2-2.6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Break-even probability p* = premium ÷ saving","value":71.74,"check":"(12.8-9.5)/(7.2-2.6)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Compact: 9.5 + 7.2p; expandable: 12.8 + 2.6p; set them equal","12.8 − 9.5 = (7.2 − 2.6)p, so p = 3.3/4.6","p* = 71.74%: above it the expandable design is cheaper, below it the compact one"],"keystrokes":["12.8 − 9.5 = 3.3","7.2 − 2.6 = 4.6","3.3 ÷ 4.6 × 100 = 71.74"],"errors":[{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Divided the saving by the premium","value":139.39,"check":"(7.2-2.6)/(12.8-9.5)*100","explain":"The premium is paid for sure and the saving arrives with probability p, so p = premium/saving. This answer exceeds 100%."},{"id":"forgot-expandable-addon","label":"Ignored the $2.6M add-on on the expandable design","value":45.83,"check":"(12.8-9.5)/7.2*100","explain":"The saving is the difference between the two add-ons, 7.2 − 2.6 = 4.6."},{"id":"complement-probability","label":"Reported the probability of low demand","value":28.26,"check":"100-(12.8-9.5)/(7.2-2.6)*100","explain":"p is the probability of high demand, which is when the add-on is needed."}],"explanation":"The extra $3.3M is a premium paid for sure; the saving of $4.6M arrives only with probability p. The designs tie where p × 4.6 = 3.3, so the expandable (option) design pays only if high demand is more than about 72% likely.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-509","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does a decision tree differ from scenario analysis?","options":["A decision tree changes several inputs together; scenario analysis models the firm's later choices","A decision tree uses random draws from distributions; scenario analysis uses probabilities","A decision tree ignores probabilities; scenario analysis requires them","A decision tree models the firm's later choices after uncertainty resolves; scenario analysis just changes inputs together"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Scenario analysis shows the NPV under best, base and worst combinations but assumes the firm sticks to its plan. A decision tree assigns probabilities and lets the firm respond at later decision nodes, which is where option value comes from.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s5, s14-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-510","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT part of solving a decision tree?","options":["Averaging the outcomes of a later decision node before choosing between its branches","Choosing the best branch at the latest decision node first","Probability-weighting the branch values at each chance node","Comparing the values of today's alternatives and picking the best"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At a decision node the firm picks the best branch; only chance nodes are averaged. Averaging first would assume the firm chooses at random, the classic averaging-before-deciding error.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11, s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-511","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we start at the latest decision node and work back toward today?","options":["Later cash flows are larger, so they should be valued first","The best later choice sets the value carried back to the earlier node","Probabilities are only known at the end of the tree","Discounting must always start from the final year"],"answer":1,"explanation":"What we do today depends on what we expect to do after the uncertainty resolves. So we value the last decisions first, using the best action at each, and carry those values back through the chance nodes.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11, s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-512","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the professor's decision-tree examples, why do we not discount the branch values again?","options":["Decision trees ignore the time value of money entirely","The branch probabilities already include the discount rate","They are already present values, so discounting again would double-count","Only cash flows beyond year 5 are ever discounted"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The slide states that the amounts are all NPVs, already discounted, which makes the tree easier. If the values were undiscounted future cash flows we would need DCF first, but we never discount a present value twice.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s15-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-513","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":["fin201-c11-real-options"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"In a warehouse tree (compact $9.5M, expandable $12.8M; high demand with probability 0.65; add-on $7.2M versus $2.6M) the expected costs are $14.18M and $14.49M. What does this say about the expandable design's extra $3.3M?","options":["The premium is worth paying because the add-on is cheaper if demand is high","The premium is a sunk cost and should be ignored","The expandable design wins because its worst-case cost is lower","It buys expected savings of only 0.65 × $4.6M = $2.99M, so it is not worth paying"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The comparison is on expected cost. The expandable design has the lower cost when demand is high (15.4 versus 16.7), but that happens only 65% of the time and saves $4.6M, so the expected saving of $2.99M falls short of the $3.3M premium.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-514","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the post-pass decision node of a tree, a regional launch is worth $3.2M and a national launch has an expected value of $4.6M. What value is carried back to the node before it?","options":["$4.6M, the larger of the two","$3.9M, the average of the two","$3.2M, the safer choice","$7.8M, the sum of the two"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A decision node takes the best branch, so the value carried back is the maximum, $4.6M. Averaging treats the choice as random, the safer option ignores the higher expected value, and the sum has no meaning.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-515","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence lists the steps for solving a decision tree in the right order?","options":["Write end-of-branch values, probability-weight every chance node, take the best branch at the latest decision node, compare today's alternatives","Write end-of-branch values, take the best branch at the latest decision node, probability-weight the chance nodes, compare today's alternatives","Take the best branch at the earliest decision node, write end-of-branch values, probability-weight the chance nodes, compare today's alternatives","Compare today's alternatives, write end-of-branch values, take the best branch at the latest decision node, probability-weight the chance nodes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Start with the values at the ends, resolve the latest decision by taking the best branch, carry that value back through the chance nodes, and only then compare today's alternatives. Averaging before deciding is the standard error.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-516","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A planner lists today's choice (short or long bridges), the unknown future (a busy or quiet road, with probabilities) and a later decision (whether to widen). Which analysis tool is this?","options":["Sensitivity analysis","Simulation analysis","Decision tree","NPV break-even analysis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A decision tree combines a choice today, an uncertain future with probabilities and a future decision. Sensitivity changes one input at a time, simulation draws random inputs, and NPV break-even solves for the input that makes NPV zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s14"},{"id":"fin201-q11-517","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A chance node has three branches worth $8.0M (probability 0.2), $3.0M (probability 0.5) and −$2.0M (probability 0.3). What is the expected value at the node?","options":["$3.0 million","$3.1 million","$9.0 million","$2.5 million"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Expected value = Σ p × V = 1.6 + 1.5 − 0.6 = $2.5M. The simple average (3.0) ignores the probabilities, dropping the loss branch gives 3.1, and summing the values gives 9.0.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16","check":"0.2*8+0.5*3+0.3*(-2)","option_checks":["(8+3-2)/3","0.2*8+0.5*3","8+3-2","0.2*8+0.5*3+0.3*(-2)"],"option_errors":["forgot-probability","omitted-branch","summed-branches",null]},{"id":"fin201-q11-518","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The probabilities on the branches leaving a chance node must add to 1.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A chance node covers every possible outcome, so its probabilities sum to 1 (for example 80% busy and 20% quiet). A decision node has no probabilities.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-519","concept":"fin201-c11-decision-trees","also":["fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Timber Ridge Homes buys land for $2.0M. Rezoning is approved (probability 0.5), partly approved (0.3) or denied (0.2). Approved: build (PV $6.0M) or resell (PV $3.0M). Partly approved: build $2.2M or resell $2.6M. Denied: build −$1.1M or resell $0.4M. Values are present values before the land cost. What is the expected NPV of buying the land, in $ millions?","answer":1.86,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"-2.0+0.5*6.0+0.3*2.6+0.2*0.4","steps":[{"label":"Approved node: best of build 6.0 and resell 3.0","value":6.0,"check":"6.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Partly approved node: best of build 2.2 and resell 2.6","value":2.6,"check":"2.6","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Denied node: best of build −1.1 and resell 0.4","value":0.4,"check":"0.4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected value before the land cost","value":3.86,"check":"0.5*6.0+0.3*2.6+0.2*0.4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected NPV of buying the land","value":1.86,"check":"-2.0+0.5*6.0+0.3*2.6+0.2*0.4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Decide at each later node first: build (6.0) when approved, resell (2.6) when partly approved, resell (0.4) when denied","Chance node: 0.5 × 6.0 + 0.3 × 2.6 + 0.2 × 0.4 = 3.0 + 0.78 + 0.08 = 3.86","NPV today = 3.86 − 2.0 = $1.86 million"],"keystrokes":["0.5 × 6.0 = 3.0","0.3 × 2.6 = 0.78","0.2 × 0.4 = 0.08","3.86 − 2.0 = 1.86"],"errors":[{"id":"always-build","label":"Built in every outcome","value":1.44,"check":"-2.0+0.5*6.0+0.3*2.2+0.2*(-1.1)","explain":"At each later node the firm picks the better action; building when partly approved or denied is worse than reselling."},{"id":"always-resell","label":"Resold in every outcome","value":0.36,"check":"-2.0+0.5*3.0+0.3*2.6+0.2*0.4","explain":"When approved, building (6.0) beats reselling (3.0)."},{"id":"forgot-extra-cost","label":"Left out the $2.0M land price","value":3.86,"check":"0.5*6.0+0.3*2.6+0.2*0.4","explain":"The land price is paid today and must be subtracted."}],"explanation":"Choose the best action at each rezoning outcome (build, resell, resell), probability-weight those values to 3.86 and subtract the land price. The flexibility to resell is what lifts the value above the always-build figure of $1.44M.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-520","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The option to _____ lets a firm commit more capital later, if the project is going well, to capture extra upside.","options":["abandon","delay","expand","hedge"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The expansion option is the right to do more (bigger plant, extra space) once good news arrives. Abandonment limits losses and timing chooses when to act.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12; SM 11 CTQ 6"},{"id":"fin201-q11-521","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maritime Wind Energy installs the grid connection and land lease for 12 more turbines at little extra cost, and will build them only if the first phase earns well. Which real option is this?","options":["Option to abandon","Option to delay (timing)","Production flexibility","Option to expand"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Preparing now so that capacity can be added later if results are good is the expansion option. It is exercised only in the good outcomes, which is why it is a right and not an obligation.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-522","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Rocky Mountain Drilling Tools earns $128 after tax per unit sold each year. At the end of year 1 the project can be sold for $1,900,000; if kept, it runs 12 more years (years 2 to 13) at annual volume Q. The discount rate is 11%. Below what annual quantity Q is abandoning at the end of year 1 the better choice?","answer":2286.34,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05,"rel":0.0002},"check":"1900000/(128*((1-(1+0.11)**-12)/0.11))","steps":[{"label":"Annuity factor (11%, 12 years)","value":6.4924,"check":"((1-(1+0.11)**-12)/0.11)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"PV at the end of year 1 per unit of Q: 128 × annuity factor","value":831.02,"check":"128*((1-(1+0.11)**-12)/0.11)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Abandonment threshold Q* = 1,900,000 ÷ PV per unit","value":2286.34,"check":"1900000/(128*((1-(1+0.11)**-12)/0.11))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Abandon when the PV of continuing is below the resale value: 128 × Q × annuity factor(11%, 12) < 1,900,000","Annuity factor (11%, 12 years) = 6.492356; 128 × 6.492356 = 831.02 per unit of Q","Q* = 1,900,000/831.02 = 2,286.34 units; abandon if Q < 2,286"],"keystrokes":["N = 12, I/Y = 11, PMT = −128, FV = 0, CPT PV = 831.02","1900000 ÷ 831.02 = 2,286.34"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-n-annuity","label":"Used 13 years in the annuity factor","value":2199.12,"check":"1900000/(128*((1-(1+0.11)**-13)/0.11))","explain":"The later flows run in years 2 to 13, which is 12 payments valued at the end of year 1."},{"id":"divided-by-n","label":"Spread the resale value evenly instead of using an annuity factor","value":1236.98,"check":"1900000/(128*12)","explain":"That ignores the time value of money; the flows must be valued with the annuity factor."},{"id":"no-annuity-factor","label":"Divided the resale value by one year's flow per unit","value":14843.75,"check":"1900000/128","explain":"One year of flow per unit is not the value of 12 years of flows; multiply by the annuity factor."}],"explanation":"Abandon when the continuation value falls below the resale value. Setting 128 × Q × AF = 1,900,000 gives the break-even volume Q* = 2,286.34; below it the project is worth more sold. Both sides are measured at the end of year 1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20"},{"id":"fin201-q11-523","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar Mill Timber's project has a 0.4 probability of success (NPV +$8.5M) and a 0.6 probability of failure (NPV −$3.8M if it keeps running). If it fails, the firm can abandon and limit the NPV to −$1.6M. NPVs are present values today. What is the value of the abandonment option, in $ millions?","answer":1.32,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"0.4*8.5+0.6*(-1.6)-(0.4*8.5+0.6*(-3.8))","steps":[{"label":"Expected NPV without the option","value":1.12,"check":"0.4*8.5+0.6*(-3.8)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected NPV with the option","value":2.44,"check":"0.4*8.5+0.6*(-1.6)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Option value = with − without","value":1.32,"check":"0.4*8.5+0.6*(-1.6)-(0.4*8.5+0.6*(-3.8))","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Without the option: 0.4 × 8.5 + 0.6 × (−3.8) = 1.12","With the option: 0.4 × 8.5 + 0.6 × (−1.6) = 2.44","Option value = 2.44 − (1.12) = $1.32 million, which is 0.6 × (3.8 − 1.6)"],"keystrokes":["0.4 × 8.5 − 0.6 × 3.8 = 1.12","0.4 × 8.5 − 0.6 × 1.6 = 2.44","2.44 − 1.12 = 1.32"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-probability","label":"Used the gain in the failure state without weighting it","value":2.2,"check":"-1.6-(-3.8)","explain":"The gain of $2.2M arises only with the failure probability of 0.6."},{"id":"prob-swapped","label":"Weighted the gain by the success probability","value":0.88,"check":"0.4*(-1.6-(-3.8))","explain":"Abandonment helps in the failure state, whose probability is 0.6."},{"id":"npv-for-option-value","label":"Reported the NPV with the option","value":2.44,"check":"0.4*8.5+0.6*(-1.6)","explain":"The option's value is the increase in NPV it creates (with minus without), not the total."}],"explanation":"The abandonment option floors the failure outcome at −$1.6M instead of −$3.8M. Its value is the probability of failure times the improvement: 0.6 × 2.2 = $1.32M. Ignoring it understates the project's NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 5; SM 11-20, 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-524","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Northern Grain Terminals invests $4.2M today and receives $1.1M at the end of year 1. At that date (t = 1) the market turns out strong (probability 0.5), where continuing is worth $7.6M, or weak (probability 0.5), where continuing is worth $1.8M but the assets can be sold for $2.9M. These three values are measured at t = 1, not today, so discount them (with the $1.1M) once. The required return is 12%. What is the project's NPV today with the option to abandon, in $ millions?","answer":1.47,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9+1.1)/1.12-4.2","steps":[{"label":"Weak state: best of continue 1.8 and sell 2.9","value":2.9,"check":"2.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected value at year 1 of the best choices","value":5.25,"check":"0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Add the year-1 cash flow of 1.1","value":6.35,"check":"0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9+1.1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Present value today of the year-1 total","value":5.6696,"check":"(0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9+1.1)/1.12","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"NPV today","value":1.4696,"check":"(0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9+1.1)/1.12-4.2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Decide at the year-1 nodes first: strong, continue (7.6 > 2.9); weak, sell (2.9 > 1.8)","Expected value at the end of year 1 = 0.5 × 7.6 + 0.5 × 2.9 = 5.25","Add the year-1 cash flow: 5.25 + 1.10 = 6.35","Discount one year (these amounts are at t = 1): 6.35/1.12 = 5.6696","NPV = 5.6696 − 4.2 = $1.47 million"],"keystrokes":["0.5 × 7.6 + 0.5 × 2.9 = 5.25","5.25 + 1.1 = 6.35","6.35 ÷ 1.12 = 5.6696","5.6696 − 4.2 = 1.47"],"errors":[{"id":"no-abandonment","label":"Continued in the weak state as well","value":0.98,"check":"(0.5*7.6+0.5*1.8+1.1)/1.12-4.2","explain":"The firm would sell for 2.9 rather than continue worth 1.8, so the weak state is worth 2.9."},{"id":"no-discount-t1","label":"Forgot to discount the year-1 values","value":2.15,"check":"0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9+1.1-4.2","explain":"The 7.6, 2.9 and 1.1 are all received at t = 1, so they must be discounted one year."},{"id":"forgot-cf1","label":"Left out the $1.1M year-1 cash flow","value":0.49,"check":"(0.5*7.6+0.5*2.9)/1.12-4.2","explain":"The year-1 cash flow is received whichever state follows and belongs in the PV."}],"explanation":"Take the better of continuing and selling at each year-1 node, probability-weight, add the year-1 cash flow and discount once. Here the values sit at t = 1, so unlike the pure present-value trees they must be discounted. Without abandonment NPV is $0.98M; the option adds about $0.49M.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-21; SM 10-46"},{"id":"fin201-q11-525","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sunridge Solar has a pilot array. At the end of year 1 (t = 1), success (probability 0.35) lets it expand: the expansion costs $3.0M at t = 1 and adds cash flows worth $9.4M measured at t = 1. In failure (probability 0.65) expansion would add only $1.1M for the same cost, so the firm would not expand. These are t = 1 values, so discount them once to today. The required return is 13%. What is the option to expand worth today, in $ millions?","answer":1.98,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.35*(9.4-3.0)/1.13","steps":[{"label":"Net gain from expanding in success","value":6.4,"check":"9.4-3.0","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected gain at year 1 (failure: do not expand, 0)","value":2.24,"check":"0.35*(9.4-3.0)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Value of the option today","value":1.9823,"check":"0.35*(9.4-3.0)/1.13","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["Success: expand, net gain = 9.4 − 3.0 = 6.4 (at t = 1). Failure: 1.1 < 3.0, so do not expand and gain 0","Expected gain at year 1 = 0.35 × 6.4 + 0.65 × 0 = 2.24","Discount one year: 2.24/1.13 = $1.98 million"],"keystrokes":["9.4 − 3.0 = 6.4","0.35 × 6.4 = 2.24","2.24 ÷ 1.13 = 1.9823"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-extra-cost","label":"Ignored the $3.0M cost of expanding","value":2.91,"check":"0.35*9.4/1.13","explain":"Expansion costs $3.0M, so the gain is 9.4 − 3.0, not 9.4."},{"id":"no-discount-t1","label":"Forgot to discount the year-1 gain","value":2.24,"check":"0.35*(9.4-3.0)","explain":"The gain arises at the end of year 1; it must be discounted one year to give today's value."},{"id":"ignored-right-not-obligation","label":"Forced expansion in the failure state too","value":0.89,"check":"(0.35*(9.4-3.0)+0.65*(1.1-3.0))/1.13","explain":"An option is a right, not an obligation: in failure the firm simply does not expand."}],"explanation":"The option to expand is exercised only where it adds value: value = p × (added PV − cost)/(1 + R) = 0.35 × 6.4/1.13 = $1.98M. Forcing expansion in the failure state would wrongly subtract $1.9M × 0.65.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-526","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":["fin201-c11-real-options"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cobalt Bay Mining's mill project has a static NPV of −$1.3M. Demand proves strong with probability 0.30, and then expansion adds a net present value of $6.9M (after its own cost, in today's dollars); otherwise the firm does not expand. What is the project's value including the option to expand, in $ millions?","answer":0.77,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"-1.3+0.30*6.9","steps":[{"label":"Expected value of the expansion option","value":2.07,"check":"0.30*6.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"Project value including the option","value":0.77,"check":"-1.3+0.30*6.9","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["The expansion is exercised only when demand is strong: option value = 0.30 × 6.9 + 0.70 × 0 = 2.07","Total = static NPV + option value = −1.30 + 2.07 = $0.77 million (already present values, so no discounting)","The project is worth taking once its option is counted"],"keystrokes":["0.30 × 6.9 = 2.07","−1.30 + 2.07 = 0.77"],"errors":[{"id":"dropped-option","label":"Ignored the option","value":-1.3,"check":"-1.3","explain":"The static NPV leaves out the right to expand, so it understates the project's value."},{"id":"forgot-probability","label":"Added the full expansion NPV","value":5.6,"check":"-1.3+6.9","explain":"Expansion happens only if demand is strong, which has probability 0.30."},{"id":"prob-swapped","label":"Weighted by the probability of weak demand","value":3.53,"check":"-1.3+0.70*6.9","explain":"The expansion value arises in the strong state, whose probability is 0.30."}],"explanation":"A negative static NPV can hide a valuable option: −1.30 + 0.30 × 6.9 = +$0.77M. The decision flips from reject to accept once the option to expand is valued.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s11; SM 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-527","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hudson Bay Marine invests $6.0M in a vessel that earns $1.5M a year for 4 years. Its resale value is $4.0M after year 1, $4.5M after year 2, $3.0M after year 3 and $1.0M after year 4 (the end of its life). Ignore taxes; the required return is 12%. What is the highest NPV across the four possible exit dates, in $ millions?","answer":0.12,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+1.5/1.12**2+4.5/1.12**2","steps":[{"label":"NPV if exited at the end of year 1","value":-1.0893,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+4.0/1.12**1","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"NPV if exited at the end of year 2","value":0.1224,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+1.5/1.12**2+4.5/1.12**2","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"NPV if exited at the end of year 3","value":-0.2619,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+1.5/1.12**2+1.5/1.12**3+3.0/1.12**3","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}},{"label":"NPV if exited at the end of year 4","value":-0.8085,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+1.5/1.12**2+1.5/1.12**3+1.5/1.12**4+1.0/1.12**4","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01,"rel":0.0005}}],"solution":["NPV(exit at year n) = −6.0 + Σ 1.5/1.12^t (t = 1..n) + resale_n/1.12^n","Exit year 1: −1.0893; year 2: 0.1224; year 3: −0.2619; year 4 (full life): −0.8085","The best exit date is the end of year 2, with NPV = $0.12 million; running the full life gives −0.81"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = −6.0, C01 = 1.5 (F01 = 1), C02 = 6.0 (1.5 + 4.5), then NPV: I = 12, CPT","NPV = 0.1224 (exit at year 2)","Repeat with the other exit dates and compare"],"errors":[{"id":"end-of-life-only","label":"Ran the vessel to the end of its life","value":-0.81,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+1.5/1.12**2+1.5/1.12**3+1.5/1.12**4+1.0/1.12**4","explain":"Abandonment lets the firm leave when the resale value is high relative to the remaining flows; the full life is not the best date here."},{"id":"year-1-exit","label":"Exited after year 1","value":-1.09,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12**1+4.0/1.12**1","explain":"Exiting after year 1 gives up a good year-2 flow and resale value; compare every exit date."},{"id":"dropped-salvage-term","label":"Left out the year-2 resale value","value":-3.46,"check":"-6.0+1.5/1.12+1.5/1.12**2","explain":"Abandonment brings the resale value in the exit year; omitting it ignores the whole option."}],"explanation":"Compute the NPV for each possible exit date and take the largest. Running to the end of the life gives −$0.81M, a reject, but planning to sell after year 2 gives +$0.12M: the abandonment option turns a rejected project into an acceptable one.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-46; L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-528","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pinecrest Rentals earns $96 after tax per rental unit each year. At the end of year 1 it could sell the business for $2.4M; if kept, it runs 9 more years (years 2 to 10) at annual volume Q. The discount rate is 10%. Below what annual volume should it abandon at the end of year 1?","options":["4,069 units","2,778 units","4,341 units","25,000 units"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Later flows are 9 payments, valued at the end of year 1: 96 × 5.759024 = $552.87 per unit of Q, so Q* = 2,400,000/552.87 = 4,341 units. Using 10 payments gives 4,069, dividing by 9 ignores discounting, and dividing by 96 ignores the later years altogether.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20","check":"2400000/(96*((1-(1+0.1)**-9)/0.1))","option_checks":["2400000/(96*((1-(1+0.1)**-10)/0.1))","2400000/(96*9)","2400000/(96*((1-(1+0.1)**-9)/0.1))","2400000/96"],"option_errors":["wrong-n-annuity","divided-by-n",null,"no-annuity-factor"]},{"id":"fin201-q11-529","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"After year 1, Northern Lights Cinemas faces a decision. If it continues, the later flows are worth $7.0M (probability 0.6, strong) or $1.0M (probability 0.4, weak) at that date. If it abandons, it sells the assets for $3.0M in either state. What is the value at that date of its best policy, in $ millions?","options":["$4.6 million","$3.0 million","$7.0 million","$5.4 million"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Decide state by state: continue when strong (7.0 > 3.0) and abandon when weak (3.0 > 1.0). Value = 0.6 × 7.0 + 0.4 × 3.0 = $5.4M. Averaging the continuation values first gives 4.6 and then choosing continue; always abandoning gives only 3.0, and 7.0 is just the best case.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-21; L05C s16","check":"0.6*7.0+0.4*3.0","option_checks":["0.6*7.0+0.4*1.0","3.0","7.0","0.6*7.0+0.4*3.0"],"option_errors":["averaged-before-deciding","always-abandon","best-case-only",null]},{"id":"fin201-q11-530","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"When deciding whether to abandon a project, which figure is set against the value of continuing, and how does it differ from book value?","options":["What the assets could be sold for now, an opportunity cost that can differ from book value","The original cost less depreciation to date, because book value is what the firm paid","The initial investment, because it is the amount at risk","The latest year's accounting profit, because losses signal exit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The abandonment value is what the project could be sold for now (the $2.8M market value in the solutions manual's example). Continuing means giving that up, so it is the opportunity cost. Book value and the initial investment are history.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20c; L05C s12"},{"id":"fin201-q11-531","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the option to abandon is NOT true?","options":["It raises the project's value by limiting the loss in bad outcomes","It should be exercised whenever the project reports an accounting loss","Ignoring it tends to understate the project's NPV","It is worth exercising when continuing is worth less than the resale value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The rule is economic: abandon when the value of continuing is below the abandonment value. An accounting loss may still come with continuation value above resale value, and exercising is optional.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11 CTQ 5; SM 11-20"},{"id":"fin201-q11-532","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the abandonment value act as a floor under the project's value?","options":["The resale value is guaranteed to exceed the book value","Abandoning always increases accounting profit","The firm can always take the larger of continuing and the resale value","Option premiums are refunded if the project is abandoned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because abandonment is a right, the firm compares the two values and takes the better one. That is why the value at the node is max(continue, abandon), which can only add value relative to a fixed plan.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20, 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-533","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's NPV if abandoned at the end of year 1, 2, 3 and 4 is −$0.95M, −$0.30M, −$0.52M and −$0.88M. What does this show?","options":["The firm should abandon after year 4, because the full life is always best","The project is acceptable because abandoning at year 2 beats running to year 4","The firm should abandon after year 1, because that is the earliest exit","The best exit date is the end of year 2, but even that NPV is negative, so the project should not be undertaken"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pick the exit date with the highest NPV (year 2 at −0.30), then apply the NPV rule to it: negative means reject. Being better than the full-life NPV (−0.88) does not make it positive.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 10-46"},{"id":"fin201-q11-534","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the end of year 1 the value of continuing a project is $3.4M and the assets could be sold for $4.1M. What should the firm do, and what value is used at that node?","options":["Abandon, and use $4.1M","Continue, and use $3.4M","Abandon, and use $0.7M","Continue, and use $3.75M, the average of the two"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At a decision node take the larger value: 4.1 > 3.4, so sell and carry $4.1M back. The gain from abandoning (0.7) is not the node value, and averaging treats the choice as random.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20; L05C s16"},{"id":"fin201-q11-535","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"At the break-even sales volume Q* for abandonment, which equality holds?","options":["Accounting profit equals zero","PV of the continuing cash flows equals the resale value","The initial investment equals the resale value","The NPV at time 0 of the whole project equals zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Q* is where the firm is indifferent between continuing and selling: PV(continuing flows at Q*) = resale value. For example, with $128 a unit, 12 remaining years at 11% and a $1.9M resale value, solve 128 × Q × annuity factor = 1,900,000 for Q*.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20"},{"id":"fin201-q11-536","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The option to expand is exercised only in the outcomes where expanding adds value.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An option is a right, not an obligation, so the firm expands only where the added value exceeds the cost; in the other states it does nothing and the option contributes zero.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-21; L05C s10"},{"id":"fin201-q11-537","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which expression gives the value today of an option to expand that is exercised only after success at the end of year 1?","options":["Probability of success × added PV ÷ (1 + R), ignoring the expansion cost","Expansion cost × probability of success","(Added PV − expansion cost) in the failure state ÷ (1 + R)","Probability of success × (added PV − expansion cost) ÷ (1 + R)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Value = p × (gain net of cost)/(1 + R): weight the net gain by the chance it happens and discount the year-1 amount back to today. Leaving out the cost overstates the option.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-21"},{"id":"fin201-q11-538","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A project's abandonment threshold is Q* = 2,286 units a year. Sales are forecast at 3,100 units with a plausible range of 2,480 to 3,720. How should this be interpreted?","options":["Even the low forecast is above Q*, so abandonment is unlikely and the option adds little","The project must be abandoned, because the forecast is above the threshold","The option is very valuable, because the threshold is below the forecast","The option is worthless, because options never add value"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Abandonment pays only when volume falls below Q*. If the plausible range stays above it, the option is rarely exercised and worth little, although it remains a free floor. A forecast above Q* means continuing.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 11-20"},{"id":"fin201-q11-539","concept":"fin201-c11-abandonment-expansion","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A pilot packaging line is financed through a lease that lets the firm return the equipment after year 1 if demand disappoints. Which real option is this?","options":["Option to expand","Option to abandon","Option to delay (timing)","Production flexibility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The right to exit early and give the equipment back if the project is not performing is the abandonment option; its lease terms set the exit value.","source":"generated","ref":"L05C s12"}],"color":"#4F6A3F","short":"Project analysis","tag":"What-ifs and real options","province":"Thracia","numeral":"XI"},{"chapter":12,"title":"Risk, return and diversification","topics":[{"id":"fin201-t12-1","order":1,"title":"Risk and reward","summary":"Investors who lend or buy shares want to be paid for waiting and for taking risk, and that payment is exactly what a firm must pay to raise capital. This topic links the investor's required return to the firm's cost of capital, explains risk aversion and the risk premium, and summarises what a century of market history says about risk and reward.","concepts":["fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","fin201-c12-history-lessons"]},{"id":"fin201-t12-2","order":2,"title":"Measuring return and risk","summary":"Return is measured by the expected (mean) return and risk by the standard deviation. You compute both from states of the economy with probabilities, or from a history of returns using the population or sample formula, then use the normal distribution to turn them into ranges and probabilities and plot each stock on a risk-return diagram.","concepts":["fin201-c12-expected-return-states","fin201-c12-variance-states","fin201-c12-historical-sd","fin201-c12-normal-distribution","fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram"]},{"id":"fin201-t12-3","order":3,"title":"Two-stock portfolios","summary":"A portfolio's expected return is a simple weighted average, but its risk is not: it depends on how the two stocks move together, measured by correlation. Whenever the correlation is below 1 the portfolio is less risky than the weighted average of the two risks, and adding more and more stocks reduces risk toward the limit set by the market itself.","concepts":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","fin201-c12-diversification-limit"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","topic":"fin201-t12-1","term":"Required return and cost of capital","definition":"The expected return E[R] that investors need for bearing a given amount of risk. From the investor's (lender's) side it is the required rate of return; from the firm's (borrower's) side the same number is the cost of capital.","explain":"Money flows from investors to firms, and the interest rate or return in between is both the reward of one side and the cost of the other. Investments of similar risk should offer similar returns, so a project is judged against the return investors require for projects of its own risk. This is why the whole chapter is about measuring risk and the reward it earns.","example":"Investors in comparable pipeline bonds require 6.1% per annum. Bow River Pipelines must offer about 6.1% to raise money for a line of the same risk, so 6.1% is its cost of capital: a line expected to earn only 5.4% should be rejected. For shares, R = D1/P0 + g: D1 = $1.976, P0 = $38.00, g = 4% gives R = 5.2% + 4% = 9.2%.","contrast":"Required return is forward-looking and tied to risk; a realized return is what actually happened and can be far above or below it. Cost of capital is what investors demand, not the accounting interest a firm paid last year or a project's own IRR (which is compared with it).","trap":"Do not treat the risk-free rate (or a project's own return) as the discount rate: the benchmark is the return required for the project's risk. Watch for 'similar risk', 'cost of capital' and 'required return' as cue words: they all point to the same number seen from two sides.","mnemonic":"One rate, two views: the lender's reward is the borrower's cost.","formula":"E[R] = R_F + risk premium; R = D1/P0 + g","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","topic":"fin201-t12-1","term":"Risk aversion and the risk premium","definition":"People are risk averse when they want compensation for taking on higher risk; the risk premium (excess return) is that compensation, E[R] − R_F, the expected return above the risk-free rate.","explain":"Risk aversion does not mean people refuse risk; it means they take risk only when paid for it. The premium is measured against the risk-free asset (T-bills): for a stock the equity risk premium is the expected stock return minus the T-bill rate. Historically it is estimated as the average stock return minus the average T-bill return over the same years.","example":"T-bills pay 2% and the stock market is expected to return 8%, so the equity risk premium is 8% − 2% = 6%. Historically, a stock that averaged 11.0% while T-bills averaged 3.9% earned a premium of 11.0% − 3.9% = 7.1% per annum.","contrast":"Risk averse: needs compensation for higher risk. Neutral toward risk: needs none, only E[R] matters. Risk seeking: will accept less expected return to take risk. 'Risk averse' is not 'risk avoiding'. Risk premium is not the stock's total return.","trap":"Subtract the risk-free rate from the expected return (not the other way round, not the latest T-bill rate if the question gives a series: use the average over the same years). Ex ante premiums are positive; realized premiums over a short sample can be negative.","mnemonic":"Premium = pay for risk = E[R] − R_F.","formula":"risk premium = excess return = E[R] − R_F","given":false,"archetypes":["A24"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Attitudes to risk (L06 s8): averse, neutral, seeking","head":["Investor type","What they need to take on more risk"],"rows":[["Risk averse","Compensation (a risk premium) for higher risk"],["Neutral toward risk","No compensation: only the expected return matters"],["Risk seeking","Would accept a lower expected return to take on risk (the lecture: wants compensation for lower risk)"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","topic":"fin201-t12-1","term":"Lessons from capital market history","definition":"Over 1926-2016 (U.S. data in the text) small-company stocks beat large-company stocks, which beat long-term bonds, which beat T-bills. Lesson 1: risky assets on average earn a risk premium. Lesson 2: the greater the potential reward, the greater the risk (standard deviation). Real returns come from the Fisher relationship.","explain":"Higher average returns went hand in hand with higher volatility: small stocks paid most and swung most. The averages hide big year-to-year swings, so a risk premium is not a guarantee in any single year. Nominal returns must be converted to real returns with (1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real), not by simple subtraction.","example":"Large-company stocks averaged 12.0% (σ 19.9%) and T-bills 3.4% (σ 3.1%), a premium of 8.6%. With inflation averaging 3.0%, the real T-bill return was 1.034/1.030 − 1 ≈ 0.4% per year, and the real return on small stocks was 1.166/1.03 − 1 = 13.2%.","contrast":"Lesson 1 (a positive average premium for risk) versus Lesson 2 (more reward comes with more risk). Nominal versus real return: the nominal return includes inflation. T-bills are nearly free of default risk but not of inflation risk.","trap":"Subtracting inflation from the nominal rate is only an approximation; the exam rewards the Fisher division (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf) − 1. One bad year does not contradict the premium: premiums are long-run averages.","mnemonic":"Stocks > bonds > bills; small > large; more reward, more risk.","formula":"(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real)","given":true,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"U.S. history 1926-2016 (Ross 12e, Tables 12.2-12.3, Figure 12.10)","head":["Investment","Average return","Risk premium","Standard deviation"],"rows":[["Small-company stocks","16.6%","13.2%","31.9%"],["Large-company stocks","12.0%","8.6%","19.9%"],["Long-term corporate bonds","6.3%","2.9%","8.4%"],["Long-term government bonds","6.0%","2.6%","9.9%"],["Treasury bills","3.4%","0.0%","3.1%"],["Inflation","3.0%","-","4.1%"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","topic":"fin201-t12-2","term":"Expected return from states of the economy","definition":"E[R] = Σ p_i R_i: the probability-weighted average of an asset's returns R_i across the states i, where the probabilities p_i add to 1. For a portfolio, find the portfolio return in each state first, or use E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k].","explain":"When we forecast returns for scenarios such as boom, normal and recession, we weight each scenario by how likely it is. The result is an average, not a promise: it may not equal any single state's return. Equal probabilities reduce it to the simple average, which is also what a historical mean does.","example":"Boom (0.30): 22%; normal (0.50): 9%; recession (0.20): −12%. E[R] = 0.30 × 22 + 0.50 × 9 + 0.20 × (−12) = 6.6 + 4.5 − 2.4 = 8.7%.","contrast":"Forecast states use probabilities p_i; a historical mean uses weights 1/N. E[R] (reward) is not σ (risk). The expected return is not the most likely return.","trap":"Probabilities must add to 1 (find a missing one as 1 minus the others). Keep the sign of negative returns. Do not use the simple average when the probabilities differ, and do not divide by N − 1 (no N − 1 with probabilities).","mnemonic":"Weight by likelihood, then add.","formula":"E[R] = Σ p_i R_i","given":false,"archetypes":["A23"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-variance-states","topic":"fin201-t12-2","term":"Variance and standard deviation from states","definition":"σ² = Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])², the probability-weighted average of squared deviations from the expected return; σ = √σ² is the standard deviation, the measure of total (volatility) risk, in the same units as returns.","explain":"Standard deviation tells us how widely outcomes are spread around the expected return. Compute E[R] first, then the deviation in each state, square it, weight by the probability, add, and take the square root. The risk-free asset has σ = 0 because its return is the same in every state.","example":"Using 0.30/0.50/0.20 with returns 22%, 9%, −12% and E[R] = 8.7%: deviations 13.3, 0.3, −20.7. σ² = 0.30 × 13.3² + 0.50 × 0.3² + 0.20 × (−20.7)² = 53.067 + 0.045 + 85.698 = 138.81 (%²), so σ = √138.81 = 11.78%.","contrast":"Variance σ² is in squared units (0.0139); standard deviation σ is its square root (11.78%) and is what we plot. With forecast probabilities there is no N − 1; N − 1 belongs to the sample variance of historical returns.","trap":"Do not stop at the variance, do not divide by N − 1, do not square the returns instead of the deviations, and keep units consistent (decimals with decimals, or percents throughout). For a portfolio compute the portfolio's return in each state first; never average the stocks' σ's.","mnemonic":"Deviate, square, weight, add, root.","formula":"σ² = Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])²; σ = √σ²","given":false,"archetypes":["A23"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","topic":"fin201-t12-2","term":"Historical mean and standard deviation (population vs sample)","definition":"For N past returns, E[R] = μ = (R_1 + … + R_N)/N. The population standard deviation divides the sum of squared deviations by N; the sample standard deviation divides by N − 1. Use population when the data are the entire group (all stocks in your portfolio), sample when they are a sample (five years of your favourite stock).","explain":"With history every observation counts equally, so no probabilities appear. The sample formula divides by N − 1 because the deviations are measured from the sample's own mean, which would otherwise understate the spread. The sum of the deviations from the mean is always zero, a handy check.","example":"A stock returned 12%, −4%, 18%, 7%, 9% over five years (a sample). Mean = 8.4%; squared deviations sum to 261.2; variance = 261.2/4 = 65.3; σ = 8.08%. Treated as a population, 261.2/5 = 52.24 and σ = 7.23%.","contrast":"Population (÷N) versus sample (÷(N − 1)): the sample σ is slightly larger. Historical (equal weights, ÷N or N − 1) versus states with probabilities (weights p_i, no N − 1). Standard deviation (percent) versus variance (percent squared).","trap":"The question says which one applies ('a sample of five years', 'all nine stocks'): follow it. Using N for a sample or N − 1 for a population are the two classic errors. Report σ in percent, not the variance, and use the average of the T-bill series (not the last year) for historical premiums.","mnemonic":"Sample is N minus one; the whole group is N.","formula":"μ = (1/N)ΣR_i; σ_pop = √[(1/N)Σ(R_i − μ)²]; σ_sample = √[(1/(N−1))Σ(R_i − μ)²]","given":true,"archetypes":["A24"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","topic":"fin201-t12-2","term":"Normal distribution and the 68/95/99 rule","definition":"A symmetric bell-shaped distribution completely described by its mean μ and standard deviation σ. About 68% of outcomes fall within μ ± 1σ, about 95% within μ ± 2σ and about 99% within μ ± 3σ. Probabilities for other cut-offs come from z = (X − μ)/σ and the cumulative normal table N(z), with N(−z) = 1 − N(z).","explain":"Annual returns are roughly mound-shaped and symmetric, so we use the normal as an approximation. Two numbers (μ and σ) are enough to give ranges ('two years in three') and the probability of a loss. The table sheet gives N(z) for positive z only; for negative z subtract from 1.","example":"U.S. large-company stocks (1926-2016): μ = 12.0%, σ = 19.9%, so about 68% of years are between −7.9% and 31.9%. For μ = 9.0%, σ = 15.0%, P(loss) = N(−0.60) = 1 − 0.7257 = 27.43%.","contrast":"Within ±1σ (68%) is not the same as below μ − σ (about 16%, half of the 32% outside). The normal is an approximation to actual returns, not an exact description.","trap":"Pick the right multiple (1, 2 or 3 σ) for 68/95/99; centre the range on the mean; for lower-tail probabilities use 1 − N(|z|); for 'between' use N(z_upper) − N(z_lower). Divide by σ, not σ².","mnemonic":"68-95-99 for 1-2-3 sigmas.","formula":"z = (X − μ)/σ; P(R < X) = N(z); N(−z) = 1 − N(z)","given":false,"archetypes":["A24"],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Normal ranges (example: U.S. large-company stocks 1926-2016, μ = 12.0%, σ = 19.9%)","head":["Range","Share of outcomes inside","Example"],"rows":[["μ ± 1σ","about 68% (roughly 2/3)","−7.9% to 31.9%"],["μ ± 2σ","about 95%","−27.8% to 51.8%"],["μ ± 3σ","about 99%","−47.7% to 71.7%"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","topic":"fin201-t12-2","term":"Risk-return diagram, domination and the efficient frontier","definition":"A graph with σ (risk, volatility) on the horizontal axis and E[R] (return) on the vertical axis, where each dot is one stock. A stock dominates another if it has a higher E[R] for the same σ, a lower σ for the same E[R], or both. The efficient frontier is the set of points that nothing dominates, along the upper-left boundary.","explain":"To answer 'how do we measure risk and return of a stock', compute E[R] and σ from past returns (for example the last five years) and plot the dot. Up and to the left is better. Stocks off the frontier can be beaten, so only the frontier matters; which frontier stock an investor chooses depends on risk preference. The risk-free asset sits on the vertical axis at R_F.","example":"Tamarack (σ 14%, E 8%), Birchmount (14%, 11%), Quill (10%, 8%): Birchmount and Quill each dominate Tamarack; neither dominates the other because Birchmount offers more return but more risk.","contrast":"Domination compares two points; the efficient frontier is the set of undominated points. The risk-return diagram has σ on the x-axis; the security market line (next chapter) has β on the x-axis.","trap":"Do not confuse the axes; higher E[R] with higher σ is a trade-off, not domination. Equal E[R] with lower σ is domination. Past performance does not guarantee future returns.","mnemonic":"Up and left wins.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","topic":"fin201-t12-3","term":"Portfolio weights and expected portfolio return","definition":"The weight of asset k is w_k = dollars in k ÷ total dollars invested, so Σ w_k = 1 (a borrowed amount makes a weight negative). The expected return of the portfolio is the weighted average E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k], which is linear.","explain":"Weights come from dollar values, and the portfolio return is just the weighted average of the assets' expected returns, so it always lies between the lowest and highest asset returns. A risk-free asset is part of the portfolio with its own weight and return R_F.","example":"$30,000 in a 3% term deposit and $70,000 in an equity fund expected to return 9%: w = 0.30 and 0.70, E[R_P] = 0.30 × 3 + 0.70 × 9 = 0.9 + 6.3 = 7.2%.","contrast":"Expected return is linear in the weights; the portfolio standard deviation is not. Weights are proportions of the whole portfolio, not dollar amounts or returns.","trap":"Weights must add to 1 and use market (dollar) values; include the risk-free asset; do not average the returns equally unless the weights are equal. Check that the answer lies between the asset returns.","mnemonic":"Return is a weighted average; weights sum to one.","formula":"w_k = $k/Σ$; Σ w_k = 1; E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k]","given":true,"archetypes":["A25"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","topic":"fin201-t12-3","term":"Standard deviation of a two-stock portfolio","definition":"σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B), where ρ_AB is the correlation of the two stocks' returns. With a risk-free asset (σ = 0, ρ = 0) it reduces to σ = w_P σ_P.","explain":"Portfolio risk is not a weighted average of the two risks: it includes a cross term that depends on how the stocks move together. Compute the two stand-alone terms, add the cross term, and take the square root. The correlation matrix view shows the same three terms.","example":"60% in A (σ = 20%) and 40% in B (σ = 10%), ρ = 0.3: terms 12² = 144 and 4² = 16, cross term 2 × 0.3 × 12 × 4 = 28.8; σ_P² = 188.8 and σ_P = 13.74%, below the weighted average 0.6 × 20 + 0.4 × 10 = 16%.","contrast":"σ_P versus the weighted average of the σ's (equal only if ρ = 1). Covariance (cov = ρσ_Aσ_B) versus correlation ρ: use the version of the formula that matches what is given.","trap":"Do not forget the square root, do not drop the cross term, do not use cov where ρ is required (or vice versa), and keep σ in decimals or percents consistently. If ρ = 1 you may use the weighted average; for ρ < 1 you may not.","mnemonic":"Two squares plus a cross term, then root.","formula":"σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B)","given":true,"archetypes":["A25"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","topic":"fin201-t12-3","term":"Covariance and correlation","definition":"cov(A,B) = Σ p_i (R_A,i − E[R_A])(R_B,i − E[R_B]) measures how returns move together; the correlation ρ_AB = cov(A,B)/(σ_A σ_B) is the scaled version, always between −1 and +1. (For historical data the covariance divides by N, or N − 1 for a sample.)","explain":"ρ = +1 means the stocks move in perfect lockstep, 0 means no linear relationship (uncorrelated), −1 means perfect opposite movement. Stocks in the same industry or country tend to have high positive ρ; different industries and countries have lower ρ. The correlation is a property of the pair, like E[R] and σ are of each stock.","example":"Three states (0.25, 0.50, 0.25): A returns 18%, 8%, −2% (E = 8, σ = 7.07); B returns 14%, 7%, 6% (E = 8.5, σ = 3.20). cov = 0.25 × 10 × 5.5 + 0.5 × 0 × (−1.5) + 0.25 × (−10) × (−2.5) = 20 (%²) = 0.0020; ρ = 20/(7.07 × 3.20) = 0.88.","contrast":"Covariance has units (%²) and no fixed bounds; correlation is unit-free and bounded by ±1. Correlation is not beta (beta divides by the market variance and is not bounded).","trap":"A correlation outside −1 to +1 signals a units mix or an arithmetic slip. Divide cov by σ_Aσ_B, not by one σ or by a variance. In the portfolio formula use ρ with the σ's, or cov alone, but never cov in the ρ slot.","mnemonic":"Correlation is covariance with the units taken out.","formula":"cov(A,B) = Σ p_i (R_A,i − E[R_A])(R_B,i − E[R_B]); ρ_AB = cov(A,B)/(σ_A σ_B)","given":false,"archetypes":["A26"],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","topic":"fin201-t12-3","term":"Diversification benefit","definition":"Whenever ρ_AB < 1, a portfolio of A and B has σ_P below the weighted average of σ_A and σ_B (w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B): the diversification benefit. The lower the correlation, the larger the benefit.","explain":"Because stocks do not move in perfect lockstep, their ups and downs partly cancel, so risk falls while expected return stays the weighted average. As the weight moves from all B to all A, the portfolio traces a curve whose bend depends on ρ. Some weights are inefficient (holding only B when adding some A lowers risk). With many stocks the efficient frontier lies to the left of all individual stocks.","example":"Two stocks with σ = 24% each, 50/50, ρ = 0.25: σ_P² = 2 × (0.5 × 24)² + 2 × 0.25 × 12 × 12 = 288 + 72 = 360, so σ_P = 18.97%, well below the weighted average 24%.","contrast":"Diversification benefit (risk falls for the same weighted return) versus expected return (linear, no benefit). ρ = 1 gives no benefit; ρ = −1 can remove all risk.","trap":"The weighted-average shortcut σ_P = w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B is valid only for ρ = 1. 'Do not put all your eggs in one basket' needs ρ < 1: adding a stock perfectly correlated with the rest does not help.","mnemonic":"Benefit whenever ρ < 1.","formula":"σ_P < w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B whenever ρ_AB < 1 (equal when ρ_AB = 1)","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"list","title":"Shape of the feasible set (E[R_P] against σ_P) for ρ = 1, 0, −1","items":["ρ = +1: a straight line from A to B; no diversification benefit, σ_P = w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B.","ρ = 0: a curve bowed to the left of the straight line; some mixes have σ_P below both stocks' σ's.","ρ = −1: two straight segments that touch the vertical axis (σ_P = 0) at w_A = σ_B/(σ_A + σ_B); σ_P = |w_Aσ_A − w_Bσ_B|.","Between the lowest-risk mix and the lower-σ stock the curve is inefficient: the same σ is available with a higher E[R]."]}},{"id":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","topic":"fin201-t12-3","term":"Limit of diversification: systematic risk","definition":"As more stocks are added, portfolio σ falls at a decreasing rate and levels off at σ_M, the volatility of the market. Company-specific (diversifiable, non-systematic) risk can be diversified away; market (non-diversifiable, systematic) risk cannot.","explain":"The first few stocks remove most of the specific risk; later ones help less and less, and a very large portfolio ends up behaving like the market. This is why a well-diversified investor is rewarded only for market risk, which leads to beta and the CAPM in the next chapter.","example":"Illustrative numbers: a one-stock portfolio with σ = 45% falls to about 30% with five stocks and about 25% with fifteen, levelling off near the market's σ_M of 20%: the first stocks cut risk most, and the remaining 20% (σ_M) cannot be removed.","contrast":"Diversifiable (company-specific, non-systematic) versus non-diversifiable (market, systematic) risk. Volatility σ measures total risk; β measures only the systematic part.","trap":"The answer to 'what happens to σ as N grows' is: it decreases, but at a decreasing rate, and never below σ_M. 'Decreases at an increasing rate' and 'eliminates all risk' are wrong.","mnemonic":"Falls, but flattens out at the market.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":3,"figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"fin201-a12-01","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","title":"Risk averse, neutral or seeking?","prompt":"Sort each statement into the investor type it describes.","buckets":["Risk averse","Neutral toward risk","Risk seeking"],"items":[{"text":"Wants a higher expected return before taking on a more volatile investment","bucket":0},{"text":"Demands an extra 4 points of expected return to hold a much more volatile bond","bucket":0},{"text":"Holds a risky fund only if it is expected to pay a premium over T-bills","bucket":0},{"text":"Would pay $50 for a coin flip that pays $0 or $100 with equal chance","bucket":1},{"text":"Needs no premium over T-bills to hold a volatile stock","bucket":1},{"text":"Cares only about the expected return, not the standard deviation","bucket":1},{"text":"Accepts a lower expected return than T-bills because she enjoys the gamble","bucket":2},{"text":"Pays $55 for a coin flip that pays $0 or $100 with equal chance","bucket":2},{"text":"Buys a lottery ticket whose price is above its expected payoff","bucket":2}],"explanation":"Risk averse: wants compensation for higher risk. Neutral toward risk: needs no compensation, so only E[R] matters. Risk seeking: will accept less expected return to take on risk. Risk aversion does not mean refusing risk; it means requiring a premium."},{"id":"fin201-a12-02","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","title":"Order the steps: σ from states","prompt":"Put the steps for finding the standard deviation of a return from states of the economy in the correct order.","items":["List each state's probability and return and check the probabilities add to 1","Compute the expected return E[R] = Σ p_i R_i","Subtract E[R] from each state's return to get the deviations","Square each deviation and multiply by the state's probability","Add the weighted squared deviations to get the variance σ²","Take the square root to get σ in percent"],"explanation":"E[R] must come first because every deviation is measured from it. The weighted squared deviations add up to the variance (no N − 1 with probabilities), and the square root turns it into the standard deviation in the units of returns."},{"id":"fin201-a12-03","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","title":"Population or sample?","prompt":"Decide which standard deviation formula each situation calls for.","buckets":["Population σ (divide by N)","Sample σ (divide by N − 1)"],"items":[{"text":"All 12 stocks you hold today, treated as the whole group","bucket":0},{"text":"The four stocks in a trust, measuring the spread among them","bucket":0},{"text":"Last month's return on each of the nine holdings in your portfolio","bucket":0},{"text":"A question that says 'treat the five returns as the entire population'","bucket":0},{"text":"Five years of returns on your favourite stock","bucket":1},{"text":"Ten years of a mutual fund's returns used to judge its volatility","bucket":1},{"text":"A random sample of 30 TSX stocks used to estimate the whole market","bucket":1},{"text":"A question that says 'a sample of six years of returns'","bucket":1}],"explanation":"Use N when the data are the entire group of interest; use N − 1 when the data are only a sample used to estimate something larger. The sample σ is slightly larger for the same numbers."},{"id":"fin201-a12-04","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","title":"Symbol and meaning","prompt":"Match each symbol with its meaning.","pairs":[{"left":"E[R]","right":"Expected (mean) return"},{"left":"R_F","right":"Risk-free rate, the return on T-bills"},{"left":"E[R] − R_F","right":"Risk premium, also called the excess return"},{"left":"σ","right":"Standard deviation of returns: volatility risk"},{"left":"σ²","right":"Variance of returns, in squared units"},{"left":"p_i","right":"Probability of state i; all the p_i add to 1"},{"left":"w_k","right":"Portfolio weight: dollars in asset k ÷ total dollars"},{"left":"ρ_AB","right":"Correlation of A's and B's returns, between −1 and +1"}],"explanation":"Know every symbol on the formula sheet and in the lecture: E[R] and R_F give the premium, σ and σ² measure risk, p_i weights states, w_k weights assets and ρ_AB measures how two stocks move together."},{"id":"fin201-a12-05","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","title":"Order the steps: two-stock σ_P","prompt":"Put the steps for the standard deviation of a two-stock portfolio in the correct order.","items":["Convert the dollar amounts into weights w_A and w_B (they add to 1)","Compute the stand-alone terms w_A²σ_A² and w_B²σ_B²","Compute the cross term 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B","Add the three terms to get the portfolio variance σ_P²","Take the square root to get σ_P","Compare σ_P with w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B to see the diversification benefit"],"explanation":"Weights come first, then two squares and a cross term; their sum is the variance, whose square root is σ_P. If ρ < 1, σ_P is less than the weighted average of the σ's; that gap is the diversification benefit."}],"formulas":[{"id":"fin201-f12-required-return","name":"Required return build-up","formula":"E[R] = R_F + risk premium (investor's required return = firm's cost of capital)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium"],"when":"Building the return investors need for a given risk, or reading a cost of capital as the investor's E[R].","trap":"The premium is the extra over R_F, so R_F must be added back."},{"id":"fin201-f12-risk-premium","name":"Risk premium (excess return)","formula":"risk premium = E[R] − R_F","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium"],"when":"Any 'risk premium', 'excess return' or 'equity risk premium' question.","trap":"Subtract the average T-bill return over the same years, not the latest rate."},{"id":"fin201-f12-fisher","name":"Fisher relationship (real vs nominal)","formula":"(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c12-history-lessons"],"when":"Converting nominal historical returns to real ones, or the reverse.","trap":"R_nom − R_inf is only an approximation; divide the growth factors."},{"id":"fin201-f12-expected-states","name":"Expected return from states","formula":"E[R] = Σ p_i R_i","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-expected-return-states"],"when":"Returns are forecast for states of the economy with probabilities.","trap":"Probabilities must add to 1; do not use the equal-weight average unless the states are equally likely."},{"id":"fin201-f12-variance-states","name":"Variance and standard deviation from states","formula":"σ² = Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])²; σ = √σ²","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-variance-states"],"when":"States with probabilities; for a portfolio use the portfolio's return in each state.","trap":"No N − 1 with probabilities; finish with the square root."},{"id":"fin201-f12-historical","name":"Historical mean and standard deviation","formula":"μ = (1/N)ΣR_i; σ_pop = √[(1/N)Σ(R_i − μ)²]; σ_sample = √[(1/(N−1))Σ(R_i − μ)²]","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c12-historical-sd","fin201-c12-expected-return-states"],"when":"A list of past returns: population when the data are the whole group, sample when they are a sample.","trap":"N versus N − 1 depends on what the question says the data are."},{"id":"fin201-f12-normal-range","name":"Normal ranges 68/95/99","formula":"μ ± 1σ ≈ 68%; μ ± 2σ ≈ 95%; μ ± 3σ ≈ 99%","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-normal-distribution"],"when":"Ranges of returns for roughly normal returns.","trap":"Centre on the mean and use the right multiple of σ."},{"id":"fin201-f12-z-score","name":"z-score and cumulative normal","formula":"z = (X − μ)/σ; P(R < X) = N(z); N(−z) = 1 − N(z)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-normal-distribution"],"when":"Probability of a loss or of exceeding a target, using the cumulative normal table on the table sheet.","trap":"Divide by σ (not σ²); for negative z use 1 − N(|z|); for 'between' subtract the two N's."},{"id":"fin201-f12-weights","name":"Portfolio weights","formula":"w_k = $k ÷ Σ$; Σ w_k = 1","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return"],"when":"Turning dollar amounts into weights (including the risk-free asset).","trap":"Weights add to 1 and can be negative when borrowing."},{"id":"fin201-f12-portfolio-return","name":"Expected portfolio return","formula":"E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k]","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return"],"when":"Any portfolio of any number of assets.","trap":"It is linear in the weights; the result lies between the asset returns."},{"id":"fin201-f12-portfolio-sd","name":"Two-stock portfolio standard deviation","formula":"σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"when":"Risk of a portfolio of two risky assets; with a risk-free asset σ_P = w_P σ_P.","trap":"Do not forget the square root or the cross term, and do not put cov in the ρ slot."},{"id":"fin201-f12-cov-corr","name":"Covariance and correlation","formula":"cov(A,B) = Σ p_i (R_A,i − E[R_A])(R_B,i − E[R_B]); ρ_AB = cov(A,B)/(σ_A σ_B)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-covariance-correlation"],"when":"Measuring how two assets move together from states or data.","trap":"ρ must lie between −1 and +1; divide by σ_Aσ_B, not by σ_M²."},{"id":"fin201-f12-diversification","name":"Diversification benefit condition","formula":"σ_P < w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B whenever ρ_AB < 1; equal when ρ_AB = 1","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","fin201-c12-diversification-limit"],"when":"Deciding whether combining two stocks lowers risk.","trap":"The weighted-average shortcut is valid only for ρ = 1."}],"questions":[{"id":"fin201-q12-001","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"From an investor's point of view the expected return E[R] on an investment is the _____; from the borrowing firm's point of view the very same number is its cost of capital.","options":["realized holding period return","coupon rate printed on the bond","required rate of return","accounting return on book equity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lenders and investors supply the capital and need E[R] as compensation for the risk they take; the borrower must pay that same rate. One rate, two views: it is the investor's required return and the firm's cost of capital. Realized returns and coupon rates are different things.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-002","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Investors in listed pipeline companies of similar risk currently require 6.1% per annum. Bow River Pipelines wants to raise money for a new line that has the same risk as those companies. For this financing decision, the 6.1% is Bow River's:","options":["internal rate of return on the new line","accounting return on book equity","cost of capital for projects of that risk","dividend payout ratio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The rate investors demand for a given level of risk is what the borrower must pay to attract capital, so it is the cost of capital for projects of that risk. An IRR is what a specific project earns; it would be compared with the 6.1%, not equal to it.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s3, s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-003","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly separates a required return from a realized return?","options":["A required return is what was actually earned last year; a realized return is what investors hope to earn next year.","A required return is always just the risk-free rate; a realized return is the risk-free rate plus a risk premium.","They are the same number measured at different dates, so over any period the two must be equal to each other.","A required return is the forward-looking reward investors need for the risk; a realized return is what was actually earned."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Required returns look forward and are tied to risk; realized returns are outcomes. A stock can realize 30% or −10% in a year while investors' required return stays near 9%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45, TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-004","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why should two projects of similar risk be discounted at similar rates?","options":["Because projects of similar risk must always have identical cash flows in every future year.","Because investors can choose either, so each must offer a similar expected return for its risk.","Because the discount rate is fixed by contract with the firm's lenders for all projects.","Because the NPV of a project does not depend on which discount rate is used."],"answer":1,"explanation":"All investments of similar risk should offer similar returns (L06 s45). If one offered more for the same risk, investors would bid its price up until the returns converged.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-005","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Aspen Foods considers a new snack line. Shareholders of listed snack-food companies with the same risk require 9.5% per annum. Aspen's best estimate of the new line's expected return is 8.2% per annum. What does this tell Aspen?","options":["The line is attractive because 8.2% is well above the risk-free rate, so it earns a premium.","8.2% is below the 9.5% required for this risk, so investors could do better elsewhere; reject the line.","The line is attractive because any positive expected return adds value for shareholders.","The 8.2% should replace 9.5% as the firm's cost of capital, since it is the project's own figure."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A project must earn at least the return investors require for its risk. Beating the risk-free rate is not enough; the benchmark is the 9.5% cost of capital for similar-risk businesses.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-006","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement is NOT part of the link between investing and financing?","options":["The lender's required return is the borrower's cost of financing.","Investments of similar risk should offer similar returns.","A firm may pick any discount rate it likes for a project, whatever its risk.","E[R] is the required rate of return to the investor and the cost of capital to the firm."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cost of capital is set by what investors can earn on other investments of the same risk, not by management's preference. Choosing it freely would let a firm accept bad projects by picking a low rate.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s3, s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-007","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Gate Rail has just paid an annual dividend of $1.90 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 4% per year indefinitely and the shares sell for $38.00. Investors' required return, which is also the firm's cost of equity, is what percent per annum?","answer":9.2,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"1.90*1.04/38.00*100+4","steps":[{"label":"Next dividend D1","value":1.976,"check":"1.90*1.04","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Dividend yield D1/P0","value":5.2,"check":"1.90*1.04/38.00*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required return R = D1/P0 + g","value":9.2,"check":"1.90*1.04/38.00*100+4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["D1 = D0(1 + g) = 1.90 × 1.04 = 1.976","Dividend yield = D1/P0 = 1.976/38.00 = 5.20%","R = D1/P0 + g = 5.20% + 4% = 9.20% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.90 × 1.04 = 1.976","÷ 38 = 0.052","+ 0.04 = 0.092 (9.20%)"],"errors":[{"id":"d0-for-d1","label":"Used D0 instead of D1","value":9.0,"check":"1.90/38.00*100+4","explain":"The dividend was just paid, so next year's dividend is D1 = D0(1 + g) = $1.976."},{"id":"omitted-growth","label":"Left out the growth rate","value":5.2,"check":"1.90*1.04/38.00*100","explain":"Required return = dividend yield + capital gain yield; the dividend yield alone is only part of it."},{"id":"yield-minus-g","label":"Subtracted g from the yield","value":1.2,"check":"1.90*1.04/38.00*100-4","explain":"R = D1/P0 + g. The capital gain yield (g) is added to the dividend yield, not subtracted."}],"explanation":"R = D1/P0 + g: the 9.20% that shareholders require is what Prairie Gate Rail must earn on equity-financed projects of this risk, i.e. its cost of equity.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45, SM 8-5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-008","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Brewing's shares carry about average stock-market risk. The T-bill rate is 2.8% per annum and investors require an equity risk premium of 5.9% per annum for average market risk. What return do investors require on Foothills' shares, in % per annum?","answer":8.7,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"2.8+5.9","steps":[{"label":"Required return = R_F + risk premium","value":8.7,"check":"2.8+5.9","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Required return = R_F + risk premium","= 2.8% + 5.9% = 8.70% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2.8 + 5.9 = 8.7"],"errors":[{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Used the premium as the whole required return","value":5.9,"check":"5.9","explain":"The premium is only the extra reward for risk; the investor also needs the risk-free return as the base."},{"id":"forgot-premium","label":"Used only the risk-free rate","value":2.8,"check":"2.8","explain":"T-bills are risk free. A risky stock must offer the risk-free rate plus a premium."}],"explanation":"Required return = risk-free rate + risk premium. This build-up is the idea behind the cost of capital: base reward for waiting plus extra reward for bearing risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9, s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-009","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"The cost of capital is the return a company actually earned on its investments last year.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The cost of capital is the forward-looking return investors require for the risk of the funds they provide. A firm's past earned return is a realized outcome and may be above or below it.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-010","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which expression builds the required return on a risky asset?","options":["E[R] = R_F − risk premium","E[R] = risk premium ÷ R_F","E[R] = σ × R_F","E[R] = R_F + risk premium"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The required return is the risk-free return plus compensation for risk: E[R] = R_F + risk premium, so the premium is E[R] − R_F.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-011","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does a riskier borrower typically have to pay a higher interest rate than a safer one?","options":["Because the risk-free rate is higher for risky borrowers.","Because riskier borrowers always earn higher profits, which they share with lenders.","Because risk-averse lenders need compensation for the extra risk the riskier borrower carries.","Because lenders in Canada are required to charge more to firms with a higher standard deviation."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Investors and lenders are risk averse, so they want compensation for higher risk. That compensation is the extra interest (risk premium) the borrower pays; the risk-free rate is the same for everyone.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s3, s8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-012","concept":"fin201-c12-required-return-cost-of-capital","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An investor says, \"I will not buy this stock unless it offers me at least 10% per annum for the risk I am taking.\" That 10% is the investor's:","options":["required rate of return","realized holding period return","standard deviation of returns","dividend payout ratio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The minimum expected return an investor accepts for a given risk is the required rate of return. It is the same number the firm sees as its cost of capital.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45"},{"id":"fin201-q12-013","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The risk premium on an asset is:","options":["the extra expected return over R_F that compensates for risk, E[R] − R_F","the total expected return of the risky asset, including the risk-free part","the standard deviation of the asset's returns, which measures its risk","the expected return on the whole stock market portfolio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Risk premium = E[R] − R_F, also called the excess return. It measures only the reward above the risk-free rate, not the whole return and not the risk itself.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-014","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Chris will move money from a 3% GIC into a stock fund only if the fund's expected return beats 3% by enough to pay him for the extra risk. Chris is:","options":["neutral toward risk","risk seeking","risk free","risk averse"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A risk-averse investor wants compensation (a premium) for taking more risk. An investor who is neutral toward risk would need no extra return; a risk-seeking investor would even accept less.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-015","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pat holds a speculative stock whose expected return is below the T-bill rate because she enjoys the gamble. In the professor's classification Pat is:","options":["risk averse","neutral toward risk","risk seeking","diversified"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A risk-seeking investor is willing to give up expected return to take on risk, so a lower expected return is acceptable. A risk-averse investor would never accept less than the risk-free rate for more risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-016","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement best separates risk aversion from risk avoidance?","options":["Risk-averse investors refuse to hold any risky asset.","Risk-averse investors require the same return for every level of risk.","Risk-averse investors prefer the asset with the higher standard deviation when expected returns are equal.","Risk-averse investors do take risk, but only when they are compensated for it."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The professor's point: risk aversion does NOT mean people do not take on risk. It means they want compensation, so riskier assets must offer a risk premium.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-017","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Government T-bills currently pay 2.6% per annum and the stock market is expected to return 9.4% per annum. What is the equity risk premium, in % per annum?","answer":6.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"9.4-2.6","steps":[{"label":"Risk premium = E[R] − R_F","value":6.8,"check":"9.4-2.6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Risk premium = E[R] − R_F","= 9.4% − 2.6% = 6.80% per annum"],"keystrokes":["9.4 − 2.6 = 6.8"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Reported the market return, not the excess","value":9.4,"check":"9.4","explain":"The premium is the return above the risk-free rate; subtract R_F."},{"id":"wrong-order","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":-6.8,"check":"2.6-9.4","explain":"A premium for bearing risk is E[R] − R_F, so it is positive when the risky asset is expected to do better."},{"id":"added-rf","label":"Added the risk-free rate","value":12.0,"check":"9.4+2.6","explain":"The premium is a difference, not a sum."}],"explanation":"Equity risk premium = 9.4% − 2.6% = 6.8% per annum: the extra reward investors expect for holding stocks instead of T-bills.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-018","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over five years Okanagan Orchards' shares returned 18%, −7%, 24%, 11% and 9%. Treasury bills averaged 3.9% per annum over the same five years. What was the stock's average historical risk premium, in % per annum?","answer":7.1,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(18-7+24+11+9)/5-3.9","steps":[{"label":"Average stock return","value":11.0,"check":"(18-7+24+11+9)/5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Average risk premium = average return − average T-bill return","value":7.1,"check":"(18-7+24+11+9)/5-3.9","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Average return = (18 − 7 + 24 + 11 + 9)/5 = 55/5 = 11.00%","Average risk premium = 11.00% − 3.9% = 7.10% per annum"],"keystrokes":["18 + (−7) + 24 + 11 + 9 = 55","÷ 5 = 11","− 3.9 = 7.1"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Did not subtract the T-bill average","value":11.0,"check":"(18-7+24+11+9)/5","explain":"That is the stock's average return, not its premium over the risk-free rate."},{"id":"n-minus-1-in-mean","label":"Divided the sum by N − 1 when averaging","value":9.85,"check":"(18-7+24+11+9)/4-3.9","explain":"The mean divides by N. The N − 1 divisor belongs only in the sample variance."}],"explanation":"The historical premium is the average stock return minus the average T-bill return: 11.0% − 3.9% = 7.1% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-9, TB 12.3"},{"id":"fin201-q12-019","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Canadian equity fund returned 14%, −9% and 22% over three consecutive years. The one-year T-bill returned 3.0%, 3.4% and 4.1% in those same years. What was the fund's average risk premium over the three years, in % per annum?","answer":5.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(14-9+22)/3-(3.0+3.4+4.1)/3","steps":[{"label":"Average fund return","value":9.0,"check":"(14-9+22)/3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Average T-bill return","value":3.5,"check":"(3.0+3.4+4.1)/3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Average risk premium","value":5.5,"check":"(14-9+22)/3-(3.0+3.4+4.1)/3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Average fund return = (14 − 9 + 22)/3 = 27/3 = 9.00%","Average T-bill return = (3.0 + 3.4 + 4.1)/3 = 10.5/3 = 3.50%","Average risk premium = 9.00% − 3.50% = 5.50% per annum (same as averaging the three yearly premiums 11.0, −12.4, 17.9)"],"keystrokes":["14 + (−9) + 22 = 27; ÷ 3 = 9","3.0 + 3.4 + 4.1 = 10.5; ÷ 3 = 3.5","9 − 3.5 = 5.5"],"errors":[{"id":"latest-tbill","label":"Subtracted only the latest T-bill return","value":4.9,"check":"(14-9+22)/3-4.1","explain":"The risk-free benchmark must be the average over the same three years, not the last year's rate."},{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Reported the fund's average return","value":9.0,"check":"(14-9+22)/3","explain":"That ignores the risk-free return the investor could have earned."}],"explanation":"Average the fund returns, average the T-bill returns over the same years, then subtract: 9.00% − 3.50% = 5.50% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-7, 12-9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-020","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Analysts expect the stock market to return 10.2% per annum and estimate the equity risk premium at 7.1% per annum. What risk-free rate is implied, in % per annum?","answer":3.1,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"10.2-7.1","steps":[{"label":"R_F = E[R] − risk premium","value":3.1,"check":"10.2-7.1","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Risk premium = E[R] − R_F, so R_F = E[R] − risk premium","R_F = 10.2% − 7.1% = 3.10% per annum"],"keystrokes":["10.2 − 7.1 = 3.1"],"errors":[{"id":"r-plus-premium","label":"Added the premium to the market return","value":17.3,"check":"10.2+7.1","explain":"Rearranging premium = E[R] − R_F gives R_F = E[R] − premium; adding goes the wrong way."},{"id":"premium-as-rf","label":"Reported the premium as the risk-free rate","value":7.1,"check":"7.1","explain":"The premium is the excess over R_F; it is not R_F itself."}],"explanation":"Rearranging risk premium = E[R] − R_F: R_F = 10.2% − 7.1% = 3.1% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-021","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock Q is expected to return 12.35% per annum and the risk-free rate is 3.15% per annum. What is Stock Q's risk premium?","options":["−9.20%","9.20%","12.32%","15.50%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Risk premium = E[R] − R_F = 12.35% − 3.15% = 9.20%. Adding the two gives 15.50%; reversing the order gives −9.20%; subtracting 0.0315 from 12.35 (a percent-decimal mix) gives 12.32%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-022","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over 1926–2016 (U.S. data in the textbook) small-company stocks averaged 16.6% per year, large-company stocks 12.0% and Treasury bills 3.4%. What was the average risk premium on small-company stocks?","options":["4.60%","8.60%","13.20%","16.60%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Small-company premium = 16.6% − 3.4% = 13.2%. The 16.6% is the whole return; 8.6% is the large-company premium (12.0 − 3.4); 4.6% is the difference between the two stock classes (16.6 − 12.0), not a premium over T-bills.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.3"},{"id":"fin201-q12-023","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do risk-averse investors demand a positive risk premium on stocks?","options":["Because they could hold the risk-free asset instead, so a risky asset must be expected to pay more.","Because stocks are guaranteed to beat T-bills in every year, so the premium is certain.","Because the government requires issuers to pay a premium over T-bills.","Because the standard deviation of stocks is always lower than that of T-bills."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The risk premium is compensation: people want to be paid for taking on risk. It is an expected (average) reward, not a guarantee in any single year.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s8-9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-024","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Using six years of data, the average observed risk premium on large-company stocks was −1.51% per annum. Does this show that investors are not risk averse?","options":["Yes. A negative average premium proves that investors accept less than the risk-free rate for bearing risk.","Yes. A negative premium means the stock has become risk free relative to Treasury bills.","No. A premium can never be negative by definition, so the six-year data must contain an error.","No. Expected premiums are positive, but realized premiums can be negative when stock returns come in low or T-bill returns come in high."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Risk aversion is about what investors require ex ante. Ex post, a short sample can easily produce a negative realized premium; this does not change the required premium going forward.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-7d"},{"id":"fin201-q12-025","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT another name for the risk premium?","options":["the risk-free rate","the excess return","the equity risk premium (when the risky asset is the stock market)","the reward for bearing risk"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The risk-free rate R_F is the base that is subtracted. The risk premium E[R] − R_F is also called the excess return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-026","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"An investor who is neutral toward risk needs no compensation for it: she would accept the same expected return on a risky asset as on T-bills.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The lecture's three attitudes are averse, neutral and seeking. Being neutral toward risk means no compensation is needed for risk, so only the expected return matters, not the standard deviation.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-027","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The risk premium is also called the _____ return.","options":["nominal","real","excess","geometric"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The professor uses 'risk premium' and 'excess return' interchangeably: the return above the risk-free rate, E[R] − R_F.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-028","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which ordering of long-run average annual returns (highest first) matches the U.S. record from 1926 to 2016?","options":["large-company stocks, small-company stocks, Treasury bills, long-term bonds","long-term bonds, large-company stocks, small-company stocks, Treasury bills","small-company stocks, large-company stocks, long-term bonds, Treasury bills","Treasury bills, long-term bonds, large-company stocks, small-company stocks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Averages were roughly 16.6% for small stocks, 12.0% for large stocks, 6.0-6.3% for long-term bonds and 3.4% for T-bills: stocks beat bonds beat bills, and small beat large.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-029","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which observation best illustrates the first lesson of capital market history, that risky assets on average earn a risk premium?","options":["Large-company stocks averaged about 8.6 percentage points more per year than Treasury bills.","Treasury bills had the highest standard deviation of all asset classes.","Inflation averaged about 3.0% per year over the same 1926-2016 period.","Long-term corporate bonds outperformed small-company stocks."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A positive average gap between a risky asset (large stocks, 12.0%) and the risk-free asset (T-bills, 3.4%) is the reward for bearing risk. The other options are false or unrelated to the premium.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.3"},{"id":"fin201-q12-030","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the first and second lessons of capital market history differ?","options":["First: the greater the reward, the greater the risk. Second: risky assets earn a premium on average.","First: bonds beat stocks on average. Second: stocks then beat bills in the long run.","First: returns are normally distributed. Second: returns are independent of risk.","First: risky assets earn a premium on average. Second: greater potential reward comes with greater risk."],"answer":3,"explanation":"The first lesson is about the average reward for bearing risk; the second links the size of that reward to the size of the risk, measured by standard deviation: small stocks (σ about 31.9%) paid most and were most volatile.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.3-12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-031","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over 1926-2016 (U.S. data in the textbook) small-company stocks averaged 16.6% per year against 12.0% for large-company stocks. Why doesn't everyone simply buy small-company stocks?","options":["Because small-company stocks have a negative risk premium over T-bills.","Because their returns are far more volatile: σ about 31.9% versus 19.9%.","Because small-company stocks have a lower standard deviation than T-bills.","Because large-company stocks have always outperformed them in every year."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Higher average return comes with higher volatility, the second lesson of history. The extra return is the compensation for that risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.2-12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-032","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a year when Treasury bills returned 7.7% and inflation was 6.2%, what was the real return on Treasury bills, in % per annum? Use the Fisher relationship.","answer":1.41,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(1.077/1.062-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"1 + R_real = (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf)","value":1.0141,"check":"1.077/1.062","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"Real return","value":1.41,"check":"(1.077/1.062-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real)","1 + R_real = 1.077/1.062 = 1.01412","R_real = 1.412% ≈ 1.41% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.077 ÷ 1.062 = 1.01412","− 1 = 0.01412 (1.41%)"],"errors":[{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Subtracted inflation from the nominal rate","value":1.5,"check":"7.7-6.2","explain":"That shortcut ignores that inflation also erodes the return itself; the Fisher relationship divides the growth factors."},{"id":"fisher-inverted","label":"Divided the factors upside down","value":-1.39,"check":"(1.062/1.077-1)*100","explain":"Real purchasing-power growth is (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf), nominal over inflation."}],"explanation":"Fisher: (1 + R_real) = (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf) = 1.077/1.062 = 1.0141, so the real return is about 1.41%, a little less than the simple 1.5% difference.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-4c, SM 12-5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-033","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A pension fund wants a real return of 4.0% per annum when inflation is expected to be 2.5% per annum. What nominal return does it need, in % per annum?","answer":6.6,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(1.04*1.025-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Growth factor (1 + R_real)(1 + R_inf)","value":1.066,"check":"1.04*1.025","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"Nominal return","value":6.6,"check":"(1.04*1.025-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real) = 1.025 × 1.04 = 1.066","R_nom = 6.60% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.025 × 1.04 = 1.066","− 1 = 0.066 (6.60%)"],"errors":[{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Added real return and inflation","value":6.5,"check":"4.0+2.5","explain":"Adding ignores the cross term: the extra purchasing power must itself be inflated (0.04 × 0.025 = 0.1%)."},{"id":"fisher-inverted","label":"Divided instead of multiplying","value":1.46,"check":"(1.04/1.025-1)*100","explain":"To go from real to nominal you multiply the growth factors; dividing goes from nominal to real."}],"explanation":"Fisher: (1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real) = 1.025 × 1.04 = 1.066, so the fund needs 6.60% nominal.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1 (Fisher), SM 12-4c"},{"id":"fin201-q12-034","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"From 1926 to 2016 (U.S. data in the textbook) small-company stocks averaged a nominal 16.6% per year while inflation averaged 3.0% per year. What was the average real return on small-company stocks, in % per annum?","answer":13.2,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(1.166/1.03-1)*100","steps":[{"label":"Real return = (1 + R_nom)/(1 + R_inf) − 1","value":13.2,"check":"(1.166/1.03-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["1 + R_real = 1.166/1.030 = 1.13204","R_real = 13.20% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.166 ÷ 1.03 = 1.13204","− 1 = 0.13204 (13.20%)"],"errors":[{"id":"nominal-real-mix","label":"Subtracted inflation from the nominal return","value":13.6,"check":"16.6-3.0","explain":"The back-of-envelope 13.6% overstates the real return; the Fisher relationship gives 13.20%."},{"id":"multiplied-not-divided","label":"Multiplied by the inflation factor","value":20.1,"check":"(1.166*1.03-1)*100","explain":"Real returns come from dividing by the inflation factor, not multiplying."}],"explanation":"Real return = 1.166/1.03 − 1 = 13.20%; the textbook's quick estimate 16.6% − 3.0% = 13.6% is only an approximation.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-035","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Canadian equity fund earned a nominal 9.0% in a year when inflation was 2.4%. What was its real return?","options":["−6.60%","6.45%","6.60%","11.62%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Real return = 1.090/1.024 − 1 = 6.45%. Simple subtraction gives 6.60% (ignores compounding of inflation), multiplying the factors gives 11.62% (that is how you would go from real to nominal), and 2.4 − 9.0 = −6.60% is the wrong order.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-9a"},{"id":"fin201-q12-036","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Because Treasury bills averaged 3.4% and inflation 3.0% over 1926-2016 (U.S. data in the textbook), the average real return on T-bills was only about 0.4% per year.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. T-bills barely kept pace with inflation: 1.034/1.030 − 1 ≈ 0.39%. They had little default risk but still carried inflation risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-037","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"In one year large-company stocks lost 12% while T-bills gained 3%. Does this contradict the first lesson of capital market history?","options":["No. The first lesson is about long-run average premiums; in any single year a large loss is still possible.","Yes. If stocks carry a premium they must beat T-bills in every single year, without exception.","Yes. A single losing year is enough to show that stocks carry no premium at all.","No. Stocks that lose in one year are guaranteed to recover the whole loss within the next year."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Risk premiums are averages. Individual years can be far below T-bills, which is precisely what volatility (standard deviation) measures.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-038","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement is NOT supported by the historical record for 1926-2016 in the textbook?","options":["Treasury bills were more volatile than small-company stocks.","Small-company stocks had the highest standard deviation of the asset classes shown.","Treasury bills earned only slightly more than inflation on average.","Large-company stocks earned a positive average premium over Treasury bills."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard deviations: T-bills about 3.1% versus small-company stocks about 31.9%. Higher average returns came with higher volatility, not the reverse.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-039","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Treasury bills are usually called risk free. In what sense can they still be risky for an investor?","options":["Inflation can exceed their return, so purchasing power can fall even though the nominal return is positive.","They have a high probability of default because governments often fail to repay.","Their prices swing more than stock prices do because of their very long maturity.","They carry the same standard deviation as small-company stocks, about 31.9% per year."],"answer":0,"explanation":"T-bills have very little default risk and, being short term, little interest rate risk. The remaining risk is inflation risk: a nominal return of 7% is a loss in purchasing power when inflation is 9%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-14d"},{"id":"fin201-q12-040","concept":"fin201-c12-history-lessons","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which equation links the nominal return, the inflation rate and the real return?","options":["R_real = R_nom − R_inf exactly","(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf) + (1 + R_real)","R_nom = R_real ÷ R_inf","(1 + R_nom) = (1 + R_inf)(1 + R_real)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Fisher relationship multiplies growth factors. R_nom − R_inf is only a close approximation when rates are small.","source":"generated","ref":"FS p1 (Fisher)"},{"id":"fin201-q12-041","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the professor's notation, the expected return of an asset over states of the economy is calculated as:","options":["E[R] = (R_1 + … + R_N)/N, the simple average of the state returns","E[R] = Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])², the weighted squared deviations","E[R] = Σ R_i, the sum of the state returns","E[R] = Σ p_i R_i, the probability-weighted average of the state returns"],"answer":3,"explanation":"With forecast states we multiply each state's return by its probability and add. The simple average (1/N) is the historical version, where every past year counts equally.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s11, S7 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-042","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An analyst assigns a 30% chance to a boom (stock returns 20%), 50% to normal times (8%) and 20% to a recession (−10%). She multiplies each return by its probability and adds the results. Which measure has she computed?","options":["the historical arithmetic average return","the expected return E[R]","the geometric average return","the standard deviation of returns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Probability-weighted state returns give the expected return: 0.30 × 20 + 0.50 × 8 + 0.20 × (−10) = 8.0%. A historical average would use realized past returns, each with weight 1/N.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-043","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does an expected return computed from states differ from a historical average return?","options":["Expected return uses realized past returns; a historical average uses forecast probabilities.","Expected return divides by N − 1 while a historical average divides by N, so the first is larger.","They are the same calculation, and the names are used interchangeably in this course.","Expected return weights projected outcomes by forecast probabilities; a historical average weights each year 1/N."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Projected future returns come with probabilities, so we weight by p_i. Observed past returns are all equally weighted, 1/N each. The N − 1 divisor appears only in a sample variance, never in a mean.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.1, L06 s11-13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-044","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about an expected return computed from states is NOT true?","options":["It always equals the return in the most probable state.","The state probabilities must add to 1.","It can be a number that no single state actually produces.","A state with a higher probability has more influence on it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"E[R] is an average over the states, so it generally lies between the lowest and highest state returns and need not match any one of them.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5, TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-045","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we weight each state's return by its probability rather than average the state returns equally?","options":["Because states differ in likelihood, a likelier state should count for more in the average.","Because equal weights would always overstate the risk-free rate in the portfolio.","Because using probabilities makes the variance come out smaller than it otherwise would.","Because the formula sheet only allows weights that add up to 100 rather than 1."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Probability weighting reflects how likely each outcome is. A 10% recession should not move the average as much as a 50% normal year.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-046","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"From forecast states, Alder Tech's expected return is 8.7%. The possible state returns are 22%, 9% and −12%. What does the 8.7% mean?","options":["It is the return the stock is guaranteed to earn each year if the forecast holds.","It is the return in the single most likely of the three states, the normal case.","It is the lowest return the stock can earn in a bad year, a worst-case figure.","It is the probability-weighted average outcome, not a return the stock will actually earn in any state."],"answer":3,"explanation":"E[R] summarises the centre of the distribution of outcomes. It is the benchmark against which the spread (σ) is measured; it is not a promise.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-047","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock's three possible states are given probabilities 0.2, 0.5 and 0.2. Multiplying each state's return by its probability and adding the three products gives a valid expected return.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The probabilities add to 0.9, not 1, so they are incomplete or wrong. The probabilities across all states must sum to 1 before E[R] = Σ p_i R_i is valid.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-048","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Northgate Mining shares will return 24% if a commodity boom occurs (probability 35%) and −4% if a bust occurs (probability 65%). What is the expected return, in % per annum?","answer":5.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.35*24+0.65*(-4)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return E[R] = Σ p_i R_i","value":5.8,"check":"0.35*24+0.65*(-4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.35 × 24 + 0.65 × (−4)","= 8.40 − 2.60 = 5.80% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.35 × 24 = 8.4","+ 0.65 × (−4) = −2.6","= 5.8"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Took the simple average of the two returns","value":10.0,"check":"(24-4)/2","explain":"Probabilities are not 50/50, so the equal-weight average is wrong."},{"id":"probabilities-swapped","label":"Attached each probability to the wrong state","value":14.2,"check":"0.65*24+0.35*(-4)","explain":"The 35% belongs with the boom return 24% and the 65% with the bust return −4%."}],"explanation":"E[R] = 0.35 × 24% + 0.65 × (−4%) = 8.4% − 2.6% = 5.8%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-049","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cascade Foods has three forecast states: boom (probability 25%, return 26%), normal (55%, 11%) and recession (20%, −14%). What is its expected return, in % per annum?","answer":9.75,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.25*26+0.55*11+0.2*(-14)","steps":[{"label":"Boom contribution 0.25 × 26","value":6.5,"check":"0.25*26","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Normal contribution 0.55 × 11","value":6.05,"check":"0.55*11","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Recession contribution 0.20 × (−14)","value":-2.8,"check":"0.20*(-14)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return","value":9.75,"check":"0.25*26+0.55*11+0.2*(-14)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.25 × 26 + 0.55 × 11 + 0.20 × (−14)","= 6.50 + 6.05 − 2.80 = 9.75% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.25 × 26 = 6.5","0.55 × 11 = 6.05","0.20 × (−14) = −2.8","6.5 + 6.05 − 2.8 = 9.75"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Took the simple average of the three returns","value":7.67,"check":"(26+11-14)/3","explain":"The states are not equally likely; weight by the probabilities 0.25, 0.55 and 0.20."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Multiplied percent probabilities by percent returns","value":975.0,"check":"25*26+55*11+20*(-14)","explain":"Mixing 25 with 26 instead of 0.25 with 26 inflates the answer by a factor of 100."},{"id":"dropped-negative-sign","label":"Treated the recession return as positive","value":15.35,"check":"0.25*26+0.55*11+0.20*14","explain":"The recession return is −14%; keep the sign when multiplying."}],"explanation":"Weighted sum: 6.50 + 6.05 − 2.80 = 9.75% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-6, S7 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-050","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Three states of the economy are equally likely. A stock would return 17% in a strong economy, 6% in a moderate one and −8% in a weak one. What is its expected return, in % per annum?","answer":5.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(1/3)*17+(1/3)*6+(1/3)*(-8)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return","value":5.0,"check":"(1/3)*17+(1/3)*6+(1/3)*(-8)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Each probability is 1/3","E[R] = (1/3)(17) + (1/3)(6) + (1/3)(−8) = 15/3 = 5.00% per annum"],"keystrokes":["17 + 6 − 8 = 15","÷ 3 = 5"],"errors":[{"id":"wrong-divisor","label":"Divided the sum by 2","value":7.5,"check":"(17+6-8)/2","explain":"With three equally likely states each carries weight 1/3, so divide by 3."},{"id":"omitted-state","label":"Left out the weak state","value":7.67,"check":"(17+6)/3","explain":"Every state, including the loss state, must be in the sum."}],"explanation":"Equal probabilities of 1/3 make this the simple average of the three state returns: (17 + 6 − 8)/3 = 5.0%.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-051","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peace Arch Software faces four states: boom (probability 10%, return 36%), good (40%, 15%), poor (35%, 2%) and bust (15%, −24%). What is its expected return, in % per annum?","answer":6.7,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.10*36+0.40*15+0.35*2+0.15*(-24)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return = Σ p_i R_i","value":6.7,"check":"0.10*36+0.40*15+0.35*2+0.15*(-24)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.10 × 36 + 0.40 × 15 + 0.35 × 2 + 0.15 × (−24)","= 3.60 + 6.00 + 0.70 − 3.60 = 6.70% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.10 × 36 = 3.6","0.40 × 15 = 6","0.35 × 2 = 0.7","0.15 × (−24) = −3.6","sum = 6.7"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Took the simple average of the four returns","value":7.25,"check":"(36+15+2-24)/4","explain":"The four states have different probabilities, so they cannot be weighted equally."},{"id":"dropped-negative-sign","label":"Treated the bust return as positive","value":13.9,"check":"0.10*36+0.40*15+0.35*2+0.15*24","explain":"The bust return is −24%; its contribution is −3.60."}],"explanation":"Four weighted products add to 6.70% per annum. Check the weights first: 0.10 + 0.40 + 0.35 + 0.15 = 1.00.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-10"},{"id":"fin201-q12-052","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Lakeshore Hotels has three states. Boom: probability 0.25, return 30%. Normal: probability not given, return 10%. Recession: probability 0.35, return −15%. What is the expected return, in % per annum?","answer":6.25,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.25*30+(1-0.25-0.35)*10+0.35*(-15)","steps":[{"label":"Probability of normal = 1 − 0.25 − 0.35","value":0.4,"check":"1-0.25-0.35","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Expected return","value":6.25,"check":"0.25*30+(1-0.25-0.35)*10+0.35*(-15)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["The probabilities must sum to 1, so p(normal) = 1 − 0.25 − 0.35 = 0.40","E[R] = 0.25 × 30 + 0.40 × 10 + 0.35 × (−15) = 7.50 + 4.00 − 5.25 = 6.25% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1 − 0.25 − 0.35 = 0.40","0.25 × 30 + 0.40 × 10 + 0.35 × (−15) = 6.25"],"errors":[{"id":"omitted-state","label":"Left out the normal state","value":2.25,"check":"0.25*30+0.35*(-15)","explain":"The normal state has probability 0.40 and must be included."},{"id":"missing-probability-wrong","label":"Used 1 − 0.25 = 0.75 for the normal state","value":9.75,"check":"0.25*30+(1-0.25)*10+0.35*(-15)","explain":"The remaining probability is 1 minus both other probabilities, 0.40."}],"explanation":"Fill in the missing probability first (1 − 0.25 − 0.35 = 0.40), then take the weighted sum: 6.25%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5, SM 13-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-053","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You hold 60% of your money in Stock A and 40% in Stock B. The economy has two states: boom (probability 0.40), where A returns 18% and B returns 6%, and bust (probability 0.60), where A returns −4% and B returns 12%. What is the portfolio's expected return, in % per annum?","answer":6.72,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.4*(0.6*18+0.4*6)+0.6*(0.6*(-4)+0.4*12)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio return in the boom","value":13.2,"check":"0.6*18+0.4*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio return in the bust","value":2.4,"check":"0.6*(-4)+0.4*12","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected portfolio return","value":6.72,"check":"0.4*(0.6*18+0.4*6)+0.6*(0.6*(-4)+0.4*12)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Boom: 0.6 × 18 + 0.4 × 6 = 13.2%","Bust: 0.6 × (−4) + 0.4 × 12 = 2.4%","E[R_P] = 0.40 × 13.2 + 0.60 × 2.4 = 5.28 + 1.44 = 6.72% per annum","Check: E[R_A] = 4.8%, E[R_B] = 9.6%, so 0.6 × 4.8 + 0.4 × 9.6 = 6.72%"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 18 + 0.4 × 6 = 13.2","0.6 × (−4) + 0.4 × 12 = 2.4","0.4 × 13.2 + 0.6 × 2.4 = 6.72"],"errors":[{"id":"equal-weights","label":"Gave the two stocks equal weights","value":7.2,"check":"0.4*(0.5*18+0.5*6)+0.6*(0.5*(-4)+0.5*12)","explain":"The money is split 60/40, not 50/50."},{"id":"weights-swapped","label":"Put 40% in A and 60% in B","value":7.68,"check":"0.4*(0.4*18+0.6*6)+0.6*(0.4*(-4)+0.6*12)","explain":"Stock A has weight 0.6 and Stock B has weight 0.4."},{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Averaged the two state portfolio returns equally","value":7.8,"check":"(13.2+2.4)/2","explain":"The boom has probability 0.40 and the bust 0.60; weight accordingly."}],"explanation":"Compute the portfolio return in each state, then weight by the state probabilities; this equals the weighted average of the stocks' expected returns because E[R] is linear.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-9, SM 13-10, L06 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-054","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":["fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maple Ridge Utilities shares return 15% with probability 40% and 5% with probability 60%. T-bills yield 3.2% per annum. What is the expected risk premium on the shares, in % per annum?","answer":5.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.4*15+0.6*5-3.2","steps":[{"label":"Expected return","value":9.0,"check":"0.4*15+0.6*5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Risk premium = E[R] − R_F","value":5.8,"check":"0.4*15+0.6*5-3.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.40 × 15 + 0.60 × 5 = 6.0 + 3.0 = 9.0%","Risk premium = E[R] − R_F = 9.0% − 3.2% = 5.80% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.4 × 15 + 0.6 × 5 = 9","− 3.2 = 5.8"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Reported E[R] as the premium","value":9.0,"check":"0.4*15+0.6*5","explain":"The premium is measured above the risk-free rate: subtract R_F."},{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Used the equal-weight average return","value":6.8,"check":"(15+5)/2-3.2","explain":"The states are 40/60, not 50/50."}],"explanation":"First the expected return (9.0%), then subtract the risk-free rate: 5.8% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5, L06 s9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-055","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Tamarack Energy's expected return is 7.1% per annum. Its three states are boom (probability 0.30, return 25%), normal (0.50, return 5%) and recession (0.20, return unknown). What return does the recession state imply, in % per annum?","answer":-14.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(7.1-0.30*25-0.50*5)/0.20","steps":[{"label":"Recession contribution 0.20 × R","value":-2.9,"check":"7.1-0.30*25-0.50*5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Recession return R","value":-14.5,"check":"(7.1-0.30*25-0.50*5)/0.20","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["7.1 = 0.30 × 25 + 0.50 × 5 + 0.20 × R","7.1 = 7.5 + 2.5 + 0.20 R, so 0.20 R = −2.9","R = −2.9/0.20 = −14.50%"],"keystrokes":["7.1 − 7.5 − 2.5 = −2.9","÷ 0.20 = −14.5"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-probability","label":"Did not divide by the state's probability","value":-2.9,"check":"7.1-0.30*25-0.50*5","explain":"−2.9 is the contribution 0.20 × R; divide by 0.20 to get R."},{"id":"sign-error","label":"Lost the sign","value":14.5,"check":"-(7.1-0.30*25-0.50*5)/0.20","explain":"The recession return must be negative to pull a 10.0 weighted sum down to 7.1."}],"explanation":"Work backwards: the other two states contribute 10.0 percentage points, so the recession state must contribute −2.9 and, with weight 0.20, equals −14.5%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-11 (backward solve), SM 13-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-056","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Hurley Energy's shares have three states: strong (probability 0.30, return 18%), average (0.50, 8%) and weak (0.20, −10%). What is the expected return?","options":["4.60%","5.33%","7.40%","9.40%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R] = 0.30 × 18 + 0.50 × 8 + 0.20 × (−10) = 5.4 + 4.0 − 2.0 = 7.40%. The simple average gives 5.33%; swapping the strong and weak probabilities gives 4.60%; leaving out the weak state gives 9.40%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-057","concept":"fin201-c12-expected-return-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock returns 14% with probability 60% and −9% with probability 40%. What is its expected return?","options":["0.20%","2.50%","4.80%","12.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R] = 0.60 × 14 + 0.40 × (−9) = 8.4 − 3.6 = 4.80%. Swapping the probabilities gives 0.20%; the simple average gives 2.50%; dropping the negative sign gives 12.00%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-058","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"For returns R_i in states with probabilities p_i, the variance of an asset's return is:","options":["σ² = Σ p_i R_i, the probability-weighted average of the returns","σ² = √(Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])²), the square root of the weighted squared deviations","σ² = Σ (R_i − E[R])² ÷ (N − 1), the sample formula for historical returns","σ² = Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])², the weighted average of squared deviations from E[R]"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Variance is the weighted average of squared deviations from the expected return. Its square root is the standard deviation. With given probabilities we do not divide by N − 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s12, S7 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-059","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statistic does the lecture use to measure the volatility risk of a stock's returns?","options":["the standard deviation of returns","the expected return","the risk premium","the correlation coefficient"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Risk (volatility) is measured by σ, the standard deviation of returns, and it goes on the horizontal axis of the risk-return diagram. E[R] measures reward, and the correlation relates two assets.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s12, s14"},{"id":"fin201-q12-060","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does the variance of returns differ from the standard deviation?","options":["The variance is the square root of the standard deviation, so it is always the smaller number.","The standard deviation is the square root of the variance, so it is in percent while the variance is in squared units.","The variance includes the expected return; the standard deviation does not.","The variance is used on the risk-return diagram; the standard deviation is not."],"answer":1,"explanation":"σ = √σ². We report and plot σ because it is an ordinary percentage. A variance of 0.0225 corresponds to σ = 0.15, or 15%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4, TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-061","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a step in computing the standard deviation of returns from states of the economy?","options":["Compute the expected return E[R] = Σ p_i R_i.","Square each state's deviation R_i − E[R].","Divide the weighted sum of squared deviations by (N − 1).","Take the square root of the variance."],"answer":2,"explanation":"With forecast probabilities the weighted sum of squared deviations already is the variance; there is no N − 1 divisor. N − 1 belongs to the sample variance of historical returns.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-7, TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-062","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why do we square the deviations (R_i − E[R]) before weighting them?","options":["Weighted deviations from E[R] add to zero because they cancel; squaring makes every deviation count as spread.","Because squaring converts percentage deviations into dollar amounts that can be priced.","Because the formula sheet requires every number in a sum to be positive before adding.","Because squaring removes the effect of the probabilities so that all states count equally."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without squaring, Σ p_i (R_i − E[R]) = 0 always, so it could not measure risk. Squaring avoids the cancellation and penalises large deviations more.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-063","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock X and Stock Y both have an expected return of 9%. Stock X has σ = 8% and Stock Y has σ = 24%. What does this tell you?","options":["Y is the better stock because a larger σ means a larger return, as the premium is always paid.","X and Y are equally risky because their expected returns are equal, so σ must be equal as well.","X must be a risk-free asset because its σ is lower than Y's, even though σ is not zero.","Y is riskier: its returns spread much more widely around the same expected return, so X is preferred."],"answer":3,"explanation":"At equal expected returns, the stock with the smaller standard deviation dominates. A larger σ means more uncertainty, not a higher expected return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s12, s16"},{"id":"fin201-q12-064","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"You can play one of three fair-coin games, each with a payoff of $1.00 on average: (a) win $1 on heads and $1 on tails; (b) win $0 on heads and $2 on tails; (c) lose $100 on heads and win $102 on tails. Which statement is correct?","options":["All three are worth the same to a risk-averse player because the expected payoffs are equal.","Game (c) is worth the most to a risk-averse player because it has the largest possible payoff.","(a) is worth most to a risk-averse player: same $1 mean, but σ of $0, $1 and $101 for (a), (b) and (c).","Game (b) is worth the most to a risk-averse player because it has the lowest standard deviation."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Expected payoff is $1 in all three, so the difference is risk. For (c) the deviations are ±$101, so σ = $101. A risk-averse player wants compensation for σ, so the riskless game is worth the most.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s12"},{"id":"fin201-q12-065","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"When state probabilities are given, the variance is the weighted sum of squared deviations divided by N − 1.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. With given probabilities σ² = Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])² and nothing else is divided. The N − 1 divisor appears only for the sample variance of historical returns.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-066","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Peace River Air shares have a 20% chance of returning 17% and an 80% chance of returning −2%. What is the standard deviation of the return, in % per annum?","answer":7.6,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.2*(17-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2+0.8*((-2)-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return","value":1.8,"check":"0.2*17+0.8*(-2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":57.76,"check":"0.2*(17-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2+0.8*((-2)-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":7.6,"check":"sqrt(0.2*(17-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2+0.8*((-2)-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.2 × 17 + 0.8 × (−2) = 1.80%","σ² = 0.2 × (17 − 1.8)² + 0.8 × (−2 − 1.8)² = 0.2 × 231.04 + 0.8 × 14.44 = 46.208 + 11.552 = 57.76","σ = √57.76 = 7.60%"],"keystrokes":["0.2 × 17 + 0.8 × (−2) = 1.8","0.2 × 15.2² + 0.8 × (−3.8)² = 57.76","√ 57.76 = 7.6"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance as the standard deviation","value":57.76,"check":"0.2*(17-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2+0.8*((-2)-(0.2*17+0.8*(-2)))**2","explain":"57.76 is σ² (in %²); take the square root to get σ in percent."},{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Gave both states equal weight in the variance","value":11.08,"check":"sqrt(((17-1.8)**2+(-2-1.8)**2)/2)","explain":"The squared deviations must be weighted by 0.2 and 0.8, not 0.5 and 0.5."}],"explanation":"Expected return 1.8%, variance 57.76 (%²), so σ = √57.76 = 7.60%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5, SM 13-7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-067","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Summit Rail has three forecast states: recession (probability 0.20, return −8%), normal (0.50, return 7%) and expansion (0.30, return 20%). What is the standard deviation of its return, in % per annum?","answer":9.74,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.2*((-8)-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.5*(7-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.3*(20-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return","value":7.9,"check":"0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":94.89,"check":"0.2*((-8)-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.5*(7-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.3*(20-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":9.74,"check":"sqrt(0.2*((-8)-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.5*(7-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.3*(20-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.2 × (−8) + 0.5 × 7 + 0.3 × 20 = −1.6 + 3.5 + 6.0 = 7.9%","σ² = 0.2 × (−8 − 7.9)² + 0.5 × (7 − 7.9)² + 0.3 × (20 − 7.9)² = 50.562 + 0.405 + 43.923 = 94.89","σ = √94.89 = 9.74%"],"keystrokes":["Expected return: −1.6 + 3.5 + 6 = 7.9","(−15.9)² = 252.81 × 0.2 = 50.562; (−0.9)² × 0.5 = 0.405; 12.1² = 146.41 × 0.3 = 43.923","sum = 94.89; √ = 9.74"],"errors":[{"id":"n-minus-1-with-probs","label":"Divided the weighted sum by N − 1 = 2","value":6.89,"check":"sqrt((0.2*((-8)-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.5*(7-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.3*(20-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2)/2)","explain":"Probabilities already do the averaging; there is no N − 1 divisor with states."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the variance","value":94.89,"check":"0.2*((-8)-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.5*(7-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+0.3*(20-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2","explain":"The variance 94.89 is in %²; σ is its square root."},{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Averaged the squared deviations equally","value":11.55,"check":"sqrt(((-8-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+(7-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2+(20-(0.2*(-8)+0.5*7+0.3*20))**2)/3)","explain":"The states have different probabilities; each squared deviation is weighted by its own p."}],"explanation":"σ² = 94.89 (%²) and σ = 9.74% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-7, S7 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-068","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Kootenay Lumber's returns are 12%, 3% and −9% in three equally likely states. What is the variance of its return, as a decimal rounded to four places (for example 0.0123)?","answer":0.0074,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001},"check":"(1/3)*(0.12-0.02)**2+(1/3)*(0.03-0.02)**2+(1/3)*(-0.09-0.02)**2","steps":[{"label":"Expected return (in %)","value":2.0,"check":"(12+3-9)/3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Variance","value":0.0074,"check":"(1/3)*(0.12-0.02)**2+(1/3)*(0.03-0.02)**2+(1/3)*(-0.09-0.02)**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}}],"solution":["E[R] = (12 + 3 − 9)/3 = 2% = 0.02","σ² = (1/3)(0.12 − 0.02)² + (1/3)(0.03 − 0.02)² + (1/3)(−0.09 − 0.02)²","= (1/3)(0.0100 + 0.0001 + 0.0121) = 0.0222/3 = 0.0074"],"keystrokes":["Mean 2% = 0.02","0.10² = 0.01; 0.01² = 0.0001; (−0.11)² = 0.0121","(0.01 + 0.0001 + 0.0121) ÷ 3 = 0.0074"],"errors":[{"id":"n-minus-1-with-probs","label":"Divided the sum of squares by N − 1 = 2","value":0.0111,"check":"(0.0100+0.0001+0.0121)/2","explain":"States with probabilities use weights 1/3 each (divide by 3), not N − 1."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Worked in percent and left it in %²","value":74.0,"check":"(100+1+121)/3","explain":"Squared percentages (74) are not the decimal variance 0.0074; convert to decimals first."},{"id":"sigma-for-variance","label":"Reported σ in decimals instead of σ²","value":0.086,"check":"sqrt(0.0074)","explain":"0.0860 is the standard deviation (8.60%); the question asks for the variance."}],"explanation":"Variance 0.0074 (σ = 8.60%).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-7, S7 s4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-069","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Athabasca Tech has four forecast states: boom (probability 10%, return 38%), good (45%, 14%), poor (35%, 3%) and bust (10%, −27%). What is the standard deviation of its return, in % per annum?","answer":15.4,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.1*(38-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.45*(14-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.35*(3-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.1*((-27)-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return","value":8.45,"check":"0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":237.25,"check":"0.1*(38-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.45*(14-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.35*(3-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.1*((-27)-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":15.4,"check":"sqrt(0.1*(38-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.45*(14-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.35*(3-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.1*((-27)-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.10 × 38 + 0.45 × 14 + 0.35 × 3 + 0.10 × (−27) = 8.45%","Deviations: 29.55, 5.55, −5.45, −35.45","σ² = 0.10 × 29.55² + 0.45 × 5.55² + 0.35 × 5.45² + 0.10 × 35.45² = 87.320 + 13.861 + 10.396 + 125.670 = 237.25","σ = √237.25 = 15.40%"],"keystrokes":["Store E[R] = 8.45","0.1 × 29.55² = 87.32; 0.45 × 5.55² = 13.86; 0.35 × (−5.45)² = 10.40; 0.1 × (−35.45)² = 125.67","Sum = 237.25; √ = 15.40"],"errors":[{"id":"n-minus-1-with-probs","label":"Divided the weighted sum by N − 1 = 3","value":8.89,"check":"sqrt((0.1*(38-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.45*(14-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.35*(3-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.1*((-27)-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2)/3)","explain":"With probabilities, the weights already sum to 1; no N − 1."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance","value":237.25,"check":"0.1*(38-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.45*(14-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.35*(3-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2+0.1*((-27)-(0.1*38+0.45*14+0.35*3+0.1*(-27)))**2","explain":"237.25 is the variance in %²; σ is √237.25."},{"id":"raw-second-moment","label":"Weighted the squared returns instead of the squared deviations","value":17.57,"check":"sqrt(0.10*38**2+0.45*14**2+0.35*3**2+0.10*(-27)**2)","explain":"You must subtract E[R] before squaring; Σ p R² is not the variance."}],"explanation":"σ² = 237.25 (%²), so σ = 15.40% per annum.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-10b"},{"id":"fin201-q12-070","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You invest 70% in Stock X and 30% in Stock Y. In an expansion (probability 0.55) X returns 16% and Y returns 4%; in a contraction (probability 0.45) X returns −6% and Y returns 18%. What is the standard deviation of the portfolio's return, in % per annum?","answer":5.57,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.55*((0.7*16+0.3*4)-(0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)))**2+0.45*((0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)-(0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)))**2)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio return in expansion","value":12.4,"check":"(0.7*16+0.3*4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio return in contraction","value":1.2,"check":"(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected portfolio return","value":7.36,"check":"0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio standard deviation","value":5.57,"check":"sqrt(0.55*((0.7*16+0.3*4)-(0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)))**2+0.45*((0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)-(0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)))**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Expansion: 0.7 × 16 + 0.3 × 4 = 12.4%; contraction: 0.7 × (−6) + 0.3 × 18 = 1.2%","E[R_P] = 0.55 × 12.4 + 0.45 × 1.2 = 7.36%","σ_P² = 0.55 × (12.4 − 7.36)² + 0.45 × (1.2 − 7.36)² = 31.0464","σ_P = √31.0464 = 5.57%"],"keystrokes":["0.7 × 16 + 0.3 × 4 = 12.4; 0.7 × (−6) + 0.3 × 18 = 1.2","E = 0.55 × 12.4 + 0.45 × 1.2 = 7.36","0.55 × 5.04² + 0.45 × 6.16² = 31.0464; √ = 5.57"],"errors":[{"id":"weighted-sigmas","label":"Weighted the two stocks' standard deviations by 70/30","value":9.75,"check":"0.7*sqrt(0.55*(16-(0.55*16+0.45*(-6)))**2+0.45*((-6)-(0.55*16+0.45*(-6)))**2)+0.3*sqrt(0.55*(4-(0.55*4+0.45*18))**2+0.45*(18-(0.55*4+0.45*18))**2)","explain":"Portfolio σ is not the weighted average of the individual σ's; the stocks' returns do not move together in the two states, which lowers the portfolio's risk."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the portfolio variance","value":31.05,"check":"0.55*((0.7*16+0.3*4)-(0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)))**2+0.45*((0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)-(0.55*(0.7*16+0.3*4)+0.45*(0.7*(-6)+0.3*18)))**2","explain":"The weighted sum of squared deviations is σ_P²; take the square root."}],"explanation":"Find the portfolio return in each state first, then its mean and weighted squared deviations. σ_P = 5.57% is far below the 9.75% weighted average of the individual σ's.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-9, SM 13-10, S7 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-071","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"The variance of Okanagan Orchards' return, computed from forecast states, is 0.0196. What is the standard deviation of its return, in % per annum?","answer":14.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.0196)*100","steps":[{"label":"Standard deviation = √variance (decimal)","value":0.14,"check":"sqrt(0.0196)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"As a percentage","value":14.0,"check":"sqrt(0.0196)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["σ = √σ² = √0.0196 = 0.14","σ = 14.00% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.0196 → √x = 0.14","× 100 = 14"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Treated the variance as the standard deviation","value":1.96,"check":"0.0196*100","explain":"0.0196 is the variance; σ is its square root, 0.14 or 14%."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Left σ as a decimal","value":0.14,"check":"sqrt(0.0196)","explain":"The question asks for a percentage: 0.14 is 14%."}],"explanation":"σ = √0.0196 = 0.14 = 14%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-072","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock is equally likely to return 24% or −8%. What is the standard deviation of its return, in % per annum?","answer":16.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.5*(24-8)**2+0.5*(-8-8)**2)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return","value":8.0,"check":"0.5*24+0.5*(-8)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":16.0,"check":"sqrt(0.5*(24-8)**2+0.5*(-8-8)**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.5 × 24 + 0.5 × (−8) = 8%","Each outcome is 16 points from the mean: σ² = 0.5 × 16² + 0.5 × 16² = 256","σ = √256 = 16.00%"],"keystrokes":["0.5 × 24 + 0.5 × (−8) = 8","0.5 × 16² + 0.5 × 16² = 256; √ = 16"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance","value":256.0,"check":"0.5*(24-8)**2+0.5*(-8-8)**2","explain":"256 is σ² in %²; σ is 16%."},{"id":"forgot-half","label":"Used the full gap between the two returns","value":32.0,"check":"24-(-8)","explain":"The standard deviation is the typical distance from the mean (16 points), not the whole gap (32 points)."}],"explanation":"With two equally likely outcomes σ is half the gap between them: (24 − (−8))/2 = 16%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5, SM 13-7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-073","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Sterling Rail has three forecast states: strong (probability 0.25, return 20%), average (0.50, 8%) and weak (0.25, −4%). What is the standard deviation of its return?","options":["6.00%","8.49%","9.80%","72.00%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"E[R] = 8%; σ² = 0.25 × 12² + 0.50 × 0² + 0.25 × (−12)² = 72 (%²), so σ = √72 = 8.49%. Dividing 72 by N − 1 = 2 gives 36, so 6.00%; weighting the deviations equally gives √96 = 9.80%; stopping at 72 reports the variance, not σ.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-074","concept":"fin201-c12-variance-states","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock has a 50% chance of returning 14% and a 50% chance of returning 2%. What is its variance, written as a decimal?","options":["0.0036","0.0072","0.06","36"],"answer":0,"explanation":"E[R] = 8%. Each outcome is 6 points (0.06) from the mean, so σ² = 0.5 × 0.06² + 0.5 × 0.06² = 0.0036. Without the probabilities you get 0.0072; 0.06 is the standard deviation; 36 is the variance in %² (a percent-decimal mix).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.1"},{"id":"fin201-q12-075","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The historical mean (expected) return of N annual returns R_1, …, R_N is:","options":["[(1 + R_1)(1 + R_2)…(1 + R_N)]^(1/N) − 1","E[R] = (R_1 + R_2 + … + R_N)/N","(R_1 + R_2 + … + R_N)/(N − 1)","the middle return after ranking the N returns from lowest to highest"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The mean adds the returns and divides by N. The compound (geometric) average multiplies growth factors; N − 1 appears only in the sample variance.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-076","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following calls for the POPULATION standard deviation (divide by N)?","options":["Estimating one stock's future volatility from its last five annual returns","Judging a fund from ten years of returns in order to forecast its future risk","Measuring the spread of this month's returns across all nine stocks you hold, as the whole group","Using 20 randomly chosen TSX stocks to estimate the volatility of all TSX stocks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The professor's examples: population = all the stocks in your portfolio; sample = five years of your favourite stock. Whenever the data are only a sample used to estimate something larger, use N − 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-077","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"You have five years of annual returns on your favourite stock and want its standard deviation. Which formula applies?","options":["the sample standard deviation, dividing by N − 1","the population standard deviation, dividing by N","the probability-weighted formula Σ p_i (R_i − E[R])²","the geometric average of the five returns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Five years are only a sample of the stock's possible returns, so use the sample formula. The probability-weighted formula is for forecast states with given probabilities.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-078","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How do the population and sample standard deviations of the same set of returns differ?","options":["The sample version divides by N and the population version by N − 1, so the population version is larger.","They are always exactly equal once N is greater than 30, so the choice never matters.","The sample version divides by N − 1 instead of N, so it is slightly larger; use it when the data are a sample.","The population version squares the deviations, whereas the sample version uses absolute values instead."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Same numerator (sum of squared deviations); different divisor. Dividing by the smaller number N − 1 gives the larger σ. The difference shrinks as N grows.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, SM 12-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-079","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does the sample standard deviation divide by N − 1 rather than N?","options":["Because the first year of data is always discarded as a start-up observation.","Because dividing by N − 1 makes the answer come out as an exact whole-number percentage.","Because N years of prices give only N − 1 holding periods for which returns are measured.","Because the deviations around the sample's own mean are a little too small; dividing by N − 1 corrects that."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Deviations measured from the sample's own mean are on average too small, so dividing by N would understate the variance. Using N − 1 adjusts for this.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-080","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about a historical standard deviation is NOT correct?","options":["Dividing by N − 1 instead of N gives a smaller standard deviation.","The deviations from the mean always sum to zero.","It is measured in the same units as the returns.","A wider spread of returns around the mean gives a larger value."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dividing by N − 1 (the smaller number) makes the variance and standard deviation larger, not smaller.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-081","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over 1926-2016 (U.S. data in the textbook) large-company stocks had an average return of 12.0% and σ = 19.9%; Treasury bills averaged 3.4% with σ = 3.1%. What does this show?","options":["Stocks were less risky than T-bills because their average return was higher.","Stocks paid more on average but were far more volatile; T-bill returns stayed close to their average.","Treasury bills were riskier than stocks because their average return was lower.","The two asset classes had the same risk because both averages were positive."],"answer":1,"explanation":"σ measures how far returns typically fall from their average. 19.9% versus 3.1% shows far greater volatility for stocks, the price of their higher average return.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-082","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"For any set of historical returns, the deviations (R_i − mean) add up to zero, which gives a quick check on your arithmetic.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Deviations around the mean always cancel to zero (the textbook's 'the sum of the deviations is equal to zero'). If yours do not, the mean is wrong.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-083","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Hotels' annual returns over the last five years were 12%, −4%, 18%, 7% and 9%. Treat these as a sample of five years. What is the standard deviation of its annual returns, in % per annum?","answer":8.08,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(((12-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+((-4)-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(18-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(7-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(9-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2)/4)","steps":[{"label":"Mean return","value":8.4,"check":"(12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of squared deviations (in %²)","value":261.2,"check":"(12-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+((-4)-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(18-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(7-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(9-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":65.3,"check":"((12-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+((-4)-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(18-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(7-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(9-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2)/4","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":8.08,"check":"sqrt(((12-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+((-4)-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(18-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(7-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(9-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2)/4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Mean = (12 − 4 + 18 + 7 + 9)/5 = 8.40%","Deviations: 3.60, −12.40, 9.60, −1.40, 0.60","Squared deviations: 12.96, 153.76, 92.16, 1.96, 0.36; sum = 261.20","Variance = 261.20/4 = 65.3000   (sample, ÷(N − 1))","σ = √65.3000 = 8.08% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2nd [DATA]; 2nd [CLR WORK]; type each return and press ENTER ↓ ↓ (1-V mode); 2nd [STAT], then 2nd [SET] until 1-V shows; ↓ to read n, X̄ (mean), Sx (sample σ, ÷(N−1)) and σx (population σ, ÷N)","Read Sx = 8.0808"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided by N = 5 for a sample","value":7.23,"check":"sqrt(((12-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+((-4)-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(18-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(7-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(9-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2)/5)","explain":"For a sample of years the divisor is N − 1."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the variance","value":65.3,"check":"((12-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+((-4)-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(18-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(7-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2+(9-((12+(-4)+18+7+9)/5))**2)/4","explain":"That is σ² in %²; take the square root to get σ in percent."}],"explanation":"Sample formula: divide the sum of squared deviations (261.20) by N − 1 = 4 to get 65.3000, then take the square root: 8.08%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-6, L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-084","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A trust holds exactly four stocks. Last month they returned 9%, 15%, −3% and 11%. Treat the four as the whole population of interest. What is the (population) standard deviation of these returns, in %?","answer":6.71,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(((9-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(15-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+((-3)-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(11-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2)/4)","steps":[{"label":"Mean return","value":8.0,"check":"(9+15+(-3)+11)/4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of squared deviations (in %²)","value":180.0,"check":"(9-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(15-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+((-3)-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(11-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":45.0,"check":"((9-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(15-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+((-3)-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(11-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2)/4","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":6.71,"check":"sqrt(((9-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(15-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+((-3)-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(11-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2)/4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Mean = (9 + 15 − 3 + 11)/4 = 8.00%","Deviations: 1.00, 7.00, −11.00, 3.00","Squared deviations: 1.00, 49.00, 121.00, 9.00; sum = 180.00","Variance = 180.00/4 = 45.0000   (population, ÷N)","σ = √45.0000 = 6.71% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2nd [DATA]; 2nd [CLR WORK]; type each return and press ENTER ↓ ↓ (1-V mode); 2nd [STAT], then 2nd [SET] until 1-V shows; ↓ to read n, X̄ (mean), Sx (sample σ, ÷(N−1)) and σx (population σ, ÷N)","Read σx = 6.7082"],"errors":[{"id":"n-minus-1-for-population","label":"Divided by N − 1 = 3 for a population","value":7.75,"check":"sqrt(((9-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(15-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+((-3)-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(11-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2)/3)","explain":"When the data are the whole population the divisor is N, not N − 1."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the variance","value":45.0,"check":"((9-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(15-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+((-3)-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2+(11-((9+15+(-3)+11)/4))**2)/4","explain":"That is σ² in %²; take the square root to get σ in percent."}],"explanation":"Population formula: divide the sum of squared deviations (180.00) by N = 4 to get 45.0000, then take the square root: 6.71%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-085","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Whitecap Brewing's returns over six years were 21%, −8%, 14%, 3%, 17% and −5%. Treat the six years as a sample. What is the standard deviation of its annual returns, in % per annum?","answer":12.08,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(((21-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-8)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(14-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(3-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(17-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-5)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2)/5)","steps":[{"label":"Mean return","value":7.0,"check":"(21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of squared deviations (in %²)","value":730.0,"check":"(21-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-8)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(14-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(3-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(17-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-5)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":146.0,"check":"((21-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-8)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(14-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(3-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(17-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-5)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2)/5","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":12.08,"check":"sqrt(((21-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-8)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(14-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(3-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(17-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-5)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2)/5)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Mean = (21 − 8 + 14 + 3 + 17 − 5)/6 = 7.00%","Deviations: 14.00, −15.00, 7.00, −4.00, 10.00, −12.00","Squared deviations: 196.00, 225.00, 49.00, 16.00, 100.00, 144.00; sum = 730.00","Variance = 730.00/5 = 146.0000   (sample, ÷(N − 1))","σ = √146.0000 = 12.08% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2nd [DATA]; 2nd [CLR WORK]; type each return and press ENTER ↓ ↓ (1-V mode); 2nd [STAT], then 2nd [SET] until 1-V shows; ↓ to read n, X̄ (mean), Sx (sample σ, ÷(N−1)) and σx (population σ, ÷N)","Read Sx = 12.0830"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided by N = 6 for a sample","value":11.03,"check":"sqrt(((21-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-8)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(14-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(3-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(17-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-5)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2)/6)","explain":"For a sample of years the divisor is N − 1."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the variance","value":146.0,"check":"((21-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-8)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(14-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(3-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+(17-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2+((-5)-((21+(-8)+14+3+17+(-5))/6))**2)/5","explain":"That is σ² in %²; take the square root to get σ in percent."}],"explanation":"Sample formula: divide the sum of squared deviations (730.00) by N − 1 = 5 to get 146.0000, then take the square root: 12.08%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-7, L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-086","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Over five years a stock averaged 9.0% per annum. Four of its annual returns were 15%, −6%, 12% and 20%. Find the missing fifth return and then compute the sample standard deviation of the five returns, in % per annum.","answer":10.2,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(((15-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(4-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2)/4)","steps":[{"label":"Missing fifth return = 5 × 9.0 − (15 − 6 + 12 + 20)","value":4.0,"check":"5*9.0-(15-6+12+20)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Mean return","value":9.0,"check":"(15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of squared deviations (in %²)","value":416.0,"check":"(15-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(4-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":104.0,"check":"((15-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(4-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2)/4","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":10.2,"check":"sqrt(((15-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(4-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2)/4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Sum of five returns = 5 × 9.0 = 45, so the fifth = 45 − (15 − 6 + 12 + 20) = 45 − 41 = 4%","Mean = (15 − 6 + 12 + 20 + 4)/5 = 9.00%","Deviations: 6.00, −15.00, 3.00, 11.00, −5.00","Squared deviations: 36.00, 225.00, 9.00, 121.00, 25.00; sum = 416.00","Variance = 416.00/4 = 104.0000   (sample, ÷(N − 1))","σ = √104.0000 = 10.20% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2nd [DATA]; 2nd [CLR WORK]; type each return and press ENTER ↓ ↓ (1-V mode); 2nd [STAT], then 2nd [SET] until 1-V shows; ↓ to read n, X̄ (mean), Sx (sample σ, ÷(N−1)) and σx (population σ, ÷N)","Read Sx = 10.1980"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided by N = 5 for a sample","value":9.12,"check":"sqrt(((15-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(4-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2)/5)","explain":"For a sample of years the divisor is N − 1."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the variance","value":104.0,"check":"((15-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2+(4-((15+(-6)+12+20+4)/5))**2)/4","explain":"That is σ² in %²; take the square root to get σ in percent."},{"id":"omitted-year","label":"Computed σ from the four known returns only","value":11.32,"check":"sqrt(((15-((15+(-6)+12+20)/4))**2+((-6)-((15+(-6)+12+20)/4))**2+(12-((15+(-6)+12+20)/4))**2+(20-((15+(-6)+12+20)/4))**2)/3)","explain":"The fifth return (4%) must be recovered from the stated mean and included in the data."}],"explanation":"Sample formula: divide the sum of squared deviations (416.00) by N − 1 = 4 to get 104.0000, then take the square root: 10.20%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-087","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A fund returned −2%, 6%, 10% and 4% over four years. Treat the four years as a sample. What is the sample variance of its returns, as a decimal rounded to four places (for example 0.0123)?","answer":0.0025,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001},"check":"(((-0.02)-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.06-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.1-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.04-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2)/3","steps":[{"label":"Mean return (in %)","value":4.5,"check":"((-2)+6+10+4)/4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of squared deviations (in %²)","value":75.0,"check":"((-2)-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2+(6-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2+(10-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2+(4-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Sample variance (decimal)","value":0.0025,"check":"(((-0.02)-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.06-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.1-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.04-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2)/3","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}}],"solution":["Mean = (−2 + 6 + 10 + 4)/4 = 4.5%","Deviations: −6.5, 1.5, 5.5, −0.5; squares: 42.25, 2.25, 30.25, 0.25; sum = 75","Sample variance = 75/(4 − 1) = 25 (%²) = 0.0025 as a decimal"],"keystrokes":["2nd [DATA]; 2nd [CLR WORK]; type each return and press ENTER ↓ ↓ (1-V mode); 2nd [STAT], then 2nd [SET] until 1-V shows; ↓ to read n, X̄ (mean), Sx (sample σ, ÷(N−1)) and σx (population σ, ÷N)","Sx = 5.0000 (%); Sx² = 25 (%²) = 0.0025"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided by N = 4","value":0.001875,"check":"(((-0.02)-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.06-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.1-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2+(0.04-(((-0.02)+0.06+0.1+0.04)/4))**2)/4","explain":"A sample uses N − 1 = 3 as the divisor."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Left the variance in %²","value":25.0,"check":"(((-2)-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2+(6-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2+(10-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2+(4-(((-2)+6+10+4)/4))**2)/3","explain":"25 is in squared percent; as a decimal variance it is 0.0025."},{"id":"sigma-for-variance","label":"Reported σ as a decimal","value":0.05,"check":"sqrt(25)/100","explain":"0.05 is the standard deviation (5%), not the variance."}],"explanation":"Sample variance = 75/3 = 25 %² = 0.0025 (σ = 5.0%).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-6, L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-088","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":["fin201-c12-risk-aversion-premium"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock's yearly excess return over T-bills was 6%, −2%, 11% and 5% in four years. Treat the four years as a sample. What is the standard deviation of the stock's excess returns, in % per annum?","answer":5.35,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(((6-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+((-2)-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(11-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(5-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2)/3)","steps":[{"label":"Mean return","value":5.0,"check":"(6+(-2)+11+5)/4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of squared deviations (in %²)","value":86.0,"check":"(6-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+((-2)-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(11-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(5-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Variance (in %²)","value":28.67,"check":"((6-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+((-2)-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(11-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(5-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2)/3","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Standard deviation","value":5.35,"check":"sqrt(((6-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+((-2)-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(11-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(5-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2)/3)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Mean = (6 − 2 + 11 + 5)/4 = 5.00%","Deviations: 1.00, −7.00, 6.00, 0.00","Squared deviations: 1.00, 49.00, 36.00, 0.00; sum = 86.00","Variance = 86.00/3 = 28.6667   (sample, ÷(N − 1))","σ = √28.6667 = 5.35% per annum"],"keystrokes":["2nd [DATA]; 2nd [CLR WORK]; type each return and press ENTER ↓ ↓ (1-V mode); 2nd [STAT], then 2nd [SET] until 1-V shows; ↓ to read n, X̄ (mean), Sx (sample σ, ÷(N−1)) and σx (population σ, ÷N)","Read Sx = 5.3541"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided by N = 4 for a sample","value":4.64,"check":"sqrt(((6-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+((-2)-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(11-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(5-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2)/4)","explain":"For a sample of years the divisor is N − 1."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Stopped at the variance","value":28.67,"check":"((6-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+((-2)-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(11-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2+(5-((6+(-2)+11+5)/4))**2)/3","explain":"That is σ² in %²; take the square root to get σ in percent."}],"explanation":"Sample formula: divide the sum of squared deviations (86.00) by N − 1 = 3 to get 28.6667, then take the square root: 5.35%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-7c"},{"id":"fin201-q12-089","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A population standard deviation of 6.4% per annum was computed from five annual returns (dividing by N). What would the sample standard deviation (dividing by N − 1) of the same five returns be, in % per annum?","answer":7.16,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"6.4*sqrt(5/4)","steps":[{"label":"Sum of squared deviations = N × σ_pop²","value":204.8,"check":"5*6.4**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Sample standard deviation","value":7.16,"check":"6.4*sqrt(5/4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Sum of squared deviations = 5 × 6.4² = 204.8","Sample variance = 204.8/4 = 51.2","Sample σ = √51.2 = 7.16%   (= 6.4 × √(5/4))"],"keystrokes":["6.4 x² × 5 = 204.8","÷ 4 = 51.2","√ = 7.16"],"errors":[{"id":"n-minus-1-for-population","label":"Scaled the wrong way, by √(4/5)","value":5.72,"check":"6.4*sqrt(4/5)","explain":"The sample σ is larger than the population σ, so multiply by √(N/(N − 1))."},{"id":"factor-not-root","label":"Multiplied by N/(N − 1) without the square root","value":8.0,"check":"6.4*5/4","explain":"N/(N − 1) scales the variance; the standard deviation scales by its square root."}],"explanation":"Both formulas share the sum of squared deviations, so σ_sample = σ_pop × √(N/(N − 1)) = 6.4 × 1.118 = 7.16%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-090","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock's returns over the last four years were 8%, 2%, 14% and −4%. Treat the four years as a sample. What is the standard deviation of its returns?","options":["6.71%","7.75%","45.00%","60.00%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mean 5%; squared deviations 9 + 9 + 81 + 81 = 180. Sample: 180/3 = 60, σ = √60 = 7.75%. Dividing by N = 4 gives 6.71% (population formula); 45.00 and 60.00 are variances that were never square-rooted.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-091","concept":"fin201-c12-historical-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"The five stocks in Dana's portfolio returned 6%, 10%, 2%, 12% and 5% last month. Treat the five as the whole population. What is the (population) standard deviation of these returns?","options":["3.58%","4.00%","12.80%","16.00%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mean 7%; squared deviations 1 + 9 + 25 + 25 + 4 = 64. Population: 64/5 = 12.8, σ = √12.8 = 3.58%. Dividing by N − 1 = 4 gives 4.00%; 12.80 and 16.00 are unrooted variances.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13"},{"id":"fin201-q12-092","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A normal distribution is completely described by:","options":["its mean and its skewness","its mean and its standard deviation","its minimum and maximum","its median and its range"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is why the normal is so convenient: once you know μ and σ there is nothing else to know. All the probability ranges (68%, 95%, 99%) follow from these two numbers.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-093","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a normal distribution, approximately what share of outcomes lies within two standard deviations of the mean?","options":["about 68%","about 99%","about 50%","about 95%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The rule of thumb is 68% within one σ, 95% within two σ and 99% within three σ.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-094","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pair of statements about a normal distribution is correct?","options":["Within one σ lies about 68%; below the mean minus one σ lies about 32%.","Within one σ lies about 34%; below the mean minus one σ lies about 68%.","Within one σ lies about 68% of outcomes; below the mean minus one σ lies only about 16%.","Within one σ lies about 95%; below the mean minus one σ lies about 5%."],"answer":2,"explanation":"About 32% lies outside ±1σ, split equally between the two tails, so about 16% lies below μ − σ.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-095","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a property of the normal distribution?","options":["It is bell-shaped and symmetric around its mean.","It is skewed, with the mean above the median.","It is completely described by its mean and standard deviation.","About 68% of outcomes fall within one standard deviation of the mean."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The normal is symmetric, so its mean and median coincide; there is no skew.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-096","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can the normal distribution be used to describe yearly stock returns?","options":["Because stock returns are exactly normal in every sample that has been observed.","Because the normal distribution guarantees that no loss ever exceeds three standard deviations.","Because historical returns are roughly mound-shaped and symmetric, and a normal is fully described by μ and σ.","Because the normal distribution removes the need to estimate σ or the mean from data."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The normal is an approximation, not an exact description: actual distributions are at least roughly mound-shaped and symmetric, which is good enough for the 68/95/99 rules.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-097","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"U.S. large-company stocks averaged 12.0% with σ = 19.9% over 1926-2016. Treating returns as normal, what does the range −7.9% to 31.9% (12.0% ± 19.9%) tell an investor?","options":["About two years in three fall inside this range and about one year in three outside.","The return will fall inside this range in every single year, with no exceptions.","There is about a 95% chance that the annual return falls inside this range.","The return will be below −7.9% in about one year out of three."],"answer":0,"explanation":"±1σ covers about 68% (roughly 2/3) of outcomes. About 1/3 of years fall outside, split between above 31.9% and below −7.9% (about 16% each).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-098","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":3,"stem":"The cumulative normal table lists N(z) only for positive z. For a return X below the mean, z = (X − μ)/σ = −0.75. The probability that the return is below X is:","options":["N(0.75)","N(0.75) − 1","1 − N(0.75)","1 + N(0.75)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The table sheet says N(x) = 1 − N(−x) for x < 0. N(−0.75) = 1 − N(0.75) = 1 − 0.7734 = 0.2266.","source":"generated","ref":"Table sheet p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-099","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Annual returns on Tundra Utilities are roughly normal with a mean of 9.0% and a standard deviation of 14.0%. What is the upper end of the range that contains about 68% of annual returns, in % per annum?","answer":23.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"9.0+14.0","steps":[{"label":"Upper end = μ + 1σ","value":23.0,"check":"9.0+14.0","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["68% of outcomes lie within one standard deviation of the mean","Range = 9.0% ± 14.0% = −5.0% to 23.0%","Upper end = 23.00%"],"keystrokes":["9 + 14 = 23"],"errors":[{"id":"two-sigma-for-68","label":"Used two standard deviations","value":37.0,"check":"9.0+2*14.0","explain":"Two σ gives the 95% range; 68% is one σ."},{"id":"sigma-only","label":"Reported σ itself, forgetting the mean","value":14.0,"check":"14.0","explain":"The range is centred on the mean: add σ to μ."},{"id":"lower-end","label":"Gave the lower end of the range","value":-5.0,"check":"9.0-14.0","explain":"The upper end is μ + σ; μ − σ is the lower end."}],"explanation":"68% range = μ ± 1σ = 9.0% ± 14.0%; upper end 23.0%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4, SM 12-15"},{"id":"fin201-q12-100","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Returns on a Canadian equity fund are roughly normal with a mean of 11.4% and a standard deviation of 16.5% per year. What is the lower end of the range that contains about 95% of annual returns, in % per annum?","answer":-21.6,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"11.4-2*16.5","steps":[{"label":"Lower end = μ − 2σ","value":-21.6,"check":"11.4-2*16.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["95% of outcomes lie within two standard deviations of the mean","Lower end = 11.4 − 2 × 16.5 = 11.4 − 33.0 = −21.60%"],"keystrokes":["2 × 16.5 = 33","11.4 − 33 = −21.6"],"errors":[{"id":"one-sigma-for-95","label":"Used one standard deviation","value":-5.1,"check":"11.4-16.5","explain":"One σ gives the 68% range."},{"id":"three-sigma-for-95","label":"Used three standard deviations","value":-38.1,"check":"11.4-3*16.5","explain":"Three σ gives the 99% range."},{"id":"forgot-mean","label":"Forgot to start from the mean","value":-33.0,"check":"-2*16.5","explain":"The range is centred on the mean, 11.4%, not on zero."}],"explanation":"95% range = μ ± 2σ = 11.4% ± 33.0%, i.e. −21.6% to 44.4%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-101","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Annual returns on a Calgary pipeline stock are roughly normal with a mean of 9.0% and a standard deviation of 15.0%. Using the cumulative normal table, what is the probability that the return in a given year is below 0% (a loss), in %?","answer":27.43,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1-0.7257)*100","steps":[{"label":"z = (X − μ)/σ","value":-0.6,"check":"(0-9.0)/15.0","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"N(0.60) from the table","value":0.7257,"check":"0.7257","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"Probability of a loss = 1 − N(0.60)","value":27.43,"check":"(1-0.7257)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["z = (0 − 9.0)/15.0 = −0.60","From the table N(0.60) = 0.7257; N(−0.60) = 1 − N(0.60) = 0.2743","Probability of a loss = 27.43%"],"keystrokes":["(0 − 9) ÷ 15 = −0.60","Table: N(0.60) = 0.7257","1 − 0.7257 = 0.2743 (27.43%)"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-complement","label":"Used N(0.60) without 1 − ","value":72.57,"check":"0.7257*100","explain":"The table gives N(0.60) = P(Z < 0.60); a loss is the lower tail at z = −0.60, which is 1 − N(0.60)."},{"id":"one-sigma-rule","label":"Applied the 16% one-sigma tail","value":16.0,"check":"16","explain":"The 16% figure holds only at exactly one σ below the mean; a loss here is 0.6σ below."}],"explanation":"z = −0.60, so P(R < 0) = N(−0.60) = 1 − 0.7257 = 27.43%, about one year in four.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-15, Table sheet p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-102","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A Calgary REIT has annual returns that are roughly normal with a mean of 8.0% and a standard deviation of 12.0%. Using the cumulative normal table, what is the probability of earning more than 20% in a given year, in %?","answer":15.87,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1-0.8413)*100","steps":[{"label":"z = (20 − 8.0)/12.0","value":1.0,"check":"(20-8.0)/12.0","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"N(1.00) from the table","value":0.8413,"check":"0.8413","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"P(R > 20%) = 1 − N(1.00)","value":15.87,"check":"(1-0.8413)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["z = (20 − 8)/12 = 1.00","N(1.00) = 0.8413","P(R > 20%) = 1 − 0.8413 = 15.87%"],"keystrokes":["(20 − 8) ÷ 12 = 1","Table: N(1.00) = 0.8413","1 − 0.8413 = 0.1587"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-complement","label":"Reported N(1.00), the probability of being below","value":84.13,"check":"0.8413*100","explain":"N(z) is P(R < X). The question asks for the upper tail, so take 1 − N(z)."},{"id":"z-with-variance","label":"Divided by σ² instead of σ in the z-score","value":46.81,"check":"(1-0.5319)*100","explain":"z = (X − μ)/σ divides by the standard deviation (12), not the variance (144)."}],"explanation":"z = 1.00, so the upper-tail probability is 1 − 0.8413 = 15.87%, which matches the 16% one-sigma tail.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-16, Table sheet p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-103","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Returns on a Banff tourism stock are roughly normal with a mean of 9.0% and a standard deviation of 12.0%. Using the cumulative normal table, what is the probability that the annual return falls between 0% and 24%, in %?","answer":66.78,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(0.8944-(1-0.7734))*100","steps":[{"label":"z for 0%","value":-0.75,"check":"(0-9.0)/12.0","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"z for 24%","value":1.25,"check":"(24-9.0)/12.0","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"P(0% < R < 24%) = N(1.25) − N(−0.75)","value":66.78,"check":"(0.8944-(1-0.7734))*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["z(0%) = (0 − 9)/12 = −0.75; z(24%) = (24 − 9)/12 = 1.25","N(1.25) = 0.8944; N(−0.75) = 1 − N(0.75) = 1 − 0.7734 = 0.2266","P = 0.8944 − 0.2266 = 66.78%"],"keystrokes":["(0 − 9) ÷ 12 = −0.75; (24 − 9) ÷ 12 = 1.25","0.8944 − (1 − 0.7734) = 0.8944 − 0.2266 = 0.6678"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-lower-bound","label":"Reported only N(1.25)","value":89.44,"check":"0.8944*100","explain":"N(1.25) is P(R < 24%), which includes returns below 0%; subtract the lower tail."},{"id":"lower-z-sign","label":"Subtracted N(0.75) instead of N(−0.75)","value":12.1,"check":"(0.8944-0.7734)*100","explain":"The lower bound is below the mean: N(−0.75) = 1 − N(0.75), not N(0.75)."}],"explanation":"P(between) = N(upper z) − N(lower z) = 0.8944 − 0.2266 = 66.78%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-16, Table sheet p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-104","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Annual returns on a fund are roughly normal with a mean of 7.5% and a standard deviation of 11.0%. Which range contains about 95% of annual returns?","options":["−14.5% to 29.5%","−3.5% to 18.5%","−25.5% to 40.5%","−22.0% to 22.0%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"95% corresponds to two standard deviations: 7.5 ± 2 × 11.0 = 7.5 ± 22.0 = −14.5% to 29.5%. −3.5% to 18.5% is ±1σ (68%), −25.5% to 40.5% is ±3σ (99%), and −22.0% to 22.0% forgets to centre on the mean.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 12.4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-105","concept":"fin201-c12-normal-distribution","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Annual returns on a Canadian bond fund are roughly normal with a mean of 6.0% and a standard deviation of 12.0%. Using the cumulative normal table, what is the probability of a negative return in a year?","options":["16.00%","30.85%","48.40%","69.15%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"z = (0 − 6.0)/12.0 = −0.50 and N(−0.50) = 1 − N(0.50) = 1 − 0.6915 = 30.85%. Using N(0.50) directly gives 69.15%; dividing by the variance (z ≈ −0.04) gives 48.40%; 16% applies only at exactly one σ below the mean.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 12-15"},{"id":"fin201-q12-106","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"On the professor's risk-return diagram, what is plotted on each axis?","options":["Horizontal axis: the expected return E[R]. Vertical axis: the standard deviation σ.","Horizontal axis: the standard deviation σ (risk or volatility). Vertical axis: the expected return E[R].","Horizontal axis: beta. Vertical axis: the expected return E[R].","Horizontal axis: time. Vertical axis: the share price."],"answer":1,"explanation":"σ is on the x-axis and E[R] on the y-axis. Putting beta on the x-axis gives a different graph, the security market line, which belongs to the next chapter.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q12-107","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Each dot on a risk-return diagram is one stock plotted at its expected return E[R] on the vertical axis and its _____ on the horizontal axis.","options":["beta","correlation with the market","standard deviation","dividend yield"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The horizontal axis measures risk as volatility of returns, i.e. the standard deviation σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s14-15"},{"id":"fin201-q12-108","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Three stocks plot at (σ, E[R]): Tamarack (14%, 8%), Birchmount (14%, 11%) and Quill (10%, 8%). Which statement is correct?","options":["Tamarack dominates both Birchmount and Quill.","Birchmount dominates Quill because it has the higher expected return.","No stock dominates any other.","Birchmount and Quill each dominate Tamarack, but neither dominates the other."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Birchmount offers a higher return for the same risk (14%); Quill offers the same return for lower risk (10%). Birchmount versus Quill is a trade-off: more return but more risk, so no domination.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s16"},{"id":"fin201-q12-109","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does domination differ from the efficient frontier?","options":["Domination compares two points; the efficient frontier is the set of points that no other point dominates.","Domination applies only to bonds; the efficient frontier applies only to stocks.","The efficient frontier is the straight line through the risk-free asset; domination has no line.","They are the same idea described with different words, so no distinction is needed."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A single stock either is or is not dominated by another. Collecting all stocks that nothing dominates gives the upper-left boundary of the cloud, the efficient frontier.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s16-17"},{"id":"fin201-q12-110","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the risk-return diagram is NOT correct?","options":["Each dot shows one stock's expected return and standard deviation.","A stock directly above another has the same risk but a higher expected return.","Anywhere other than the efficient frontier a point can be dominated.","A stock plotted up and to the left of another has a higher σ and a lower E[R]."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Up means higher E[R] and left means lower σ, which is better on both counts: that is how one stock dominates another.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s14-17"},{"id":"fin201-q12-111","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can an investor ignore a stock that is not on the efficient frontier?","options":["Because another point offers more return for the same risk or less risk for the same return.","Because stocks that are off the frontier all have negative expected returns.","Because stocks that are off the frontier have no risk, so they add nothing.","Because the frontier is the only place where the risk-free asset can be found."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Points not on the frontier can be beaten on return, risk or both, so a rational investor would never choose them in preference to the dominating alternative.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s17"},{"id":"fin201-q12-112","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock A plots at (σ = 10%, E[R] = 6%) and Stock B at (σ = 20%, E[R] = 12%). What does the diagram tell you?","options":["B dominates A because it has the higher expected return, which outweighs the extra risk.","Neither dominates: B has more return but more risk, so the choice depends on risk preference.","A dominates B because it has the lower standard deviation, which outweighs the lower return.","A and B must be perfectly correlated, because both lie on one straight line from the origin."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Domination needs one stock to be better on return without being worse on risk (or vice versa). Here the two points trade off return against risk, so both can lie on the efficient frontier.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s16-17"},{"id":"fin201-q12-113","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Five stocks plot at (σ, E[R]): P (8%, 5%), Q (12%, 9%), R (12%, 7%), S (18%, 9%) and T (18%, 12%). Which stocks are NOT dominated by any other stock in the list?","options":["P, Q and T","P, Q, R and T","Q and T only","P, Q, S and T"],"answer":0,"explanation":"R is dominated by Q (same σ, higher E[R]); S is dominated by Q (same E[R], lower σ). No other stock in the list has both a return at least as high and a σ at least as low as P, Q or T, so they form the upper-left boundary.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s16-17"},{"id":"fin201-q12-114","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock with a higher expected return and a lower standard deviation than another stock dominates it.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. That is the definition of domination on the risk-return diagram: higher return for lower risk, or the same return for lower risk, or higher return for the same risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s16"},{"id":"fin201-q12-115","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"How are the points for individual stocks on the diagram estimated in the lecture?","options":["By computing E[R] and σ from historical returns over a period such as five years.","By reading each stock's beta from a regression of its returns on the market.","By using only the stock's closing price today, with no return history.","By assuming that every stock has the same σ as the market portfolio itself."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The lecture computes E[R] and σ for each stock over the historical period (e.g. five years) and plots them, with the warning that past performance does not guarantee future returns.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13-14"},{"id":"fin201-q12-116","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stocks M and N each have a 50% chance of a boom and a 50% chance of a bust. M returns 18% in the boom and 6% in the bust; N returns 30% in the boom and −6% in the bust. Which statement is correct?","options":["N dominates M because its best-case return is higher.","Both have E[R] = 12%, but σ_M = 6% and σ_N = 18%, so M dominates N.","Neither dominates: N has the higher σ, but M has the lower E[R].","M and N plot at the same point on the diagram."],"answer":1,"explanation":"E[R_M] = 0.5 × 18 + 0.5 × 6 = 12%; E[R_N] = 0.5 × 30 + 0.5 × (−6) = 12%. σ_M = 6% (both outcomes 6 points from the mean) and σ_N = 18%. Same return, lower risk: M dominates N.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-5, SM 13-7, L06 s16"},{"id":"fin201-q12-117","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock V returns 20%, 10% and −5% in states with probabilities 0.30, 0.50 and 0.20. Stock W returns 14%, 9% and 4% in the same states. Which statement is correct?","options":["V dominates W because its expected return of 10.0% is greater than W's 9.5%.","W dominates V because W has the lower σ of 3.50% against V's 8.66%.","Neither dominates: V has higher E[R] (10.0% vs 9.5%) but higher σ (8.66% vs 3.50%).","V and W have the same σ of 8.66%, so only expected returns differ."],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R_V] = 6 + 5 − 1 = 10.0%; σ_V² = 0.3 × 100 + 0.5 × 0 + 0.2 × 225 = 75, σ_V = 8.66%. E[R_W] = 4.2 + 4.5 + 0.8 = 9.5%; σ_W² = 0.3 × 4.5² + 0.5 × 0.5² + 0.2 × 5.5² = 12.25, σ_W = 3.50%. V has more return and more risk, so neither dominates.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-6, SM 13-7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-118","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Where are the best individual stocks on the risk-return diagram?","options":["On the upper-left boundary of the cloud of stocks, where no other stock dominates.","At the bottom of the cloud, where expected returns are lowest.","On the lower-right edge where σ is highest and E[R] is lowest.","Only on the vertical axis, at the risk-free rate with σ equal to zero."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The efficient frontier (the 'red curve' in the lecture) is the set of non-dominated points. Anywhere else can be dominated.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s17"},{"id":"fin201-q12-119","concept":"fin201-c12-risk-return-diagram","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Where would a Treasury bill (the risk-free asset) plot on the risk-return diagram?","options":["On the horizontal axis, at the market portfolio's σ and E[R] = 0.","At the origin, where both σ and E[R] are equal to zero.","On the vertical axis, at σ = 0 and a height of R_F.","At the same point as the market portfolio, with the same σ and E[R]."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The risk-free asset has return R_F with zero volatility, so its σ is 0 and its E[R] is R_F: it sits on the vertical axis.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s38"},{"id":"fin201-q12-401","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the professor's notation, the weight w_k of an asset in a portfolio is:","options":["the dollar amount invested in that asset divided by the total value of the portfolio","the asset's expected return divided by the portfolio's expected return","the asset's standard deviation divided by the portfolio's standard deviation","the number of shares held in the asset divided by the number of assets in the portfolio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A weight is a share of dollars: w_k = $k / total portfolio value, so the weights always sum to 1 (100%) because all of the money is invested somewhere (cash counts as an asset). The other options divide returns, risks or share counts, none of which describe how the money is split.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q12-402","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Laurel Ridge Capital holds $18,000 of Prairie Bank shares and $12,000 of Foothills Pipeline shares. Beside the two holdings the analyst writes 0.60 and 0.40. These numbers are called:","options":["portfolio weights","expected returns","correlation coefficients","betas"],"answer":0,"explanation":"$18,000 / $30,000 = 0.60 and $12,000 / $30,000 = 0.40: each is the share of total portfolio value held in that stock, which is a portfolio weight. They sum to 1. Expected returns, correlations and betas are properties of the stocks, not of how much money you put in each.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s21, TB 13.2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-403","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which expression gives the expected return of a two-stock portfolio with weights w_A and w_B?","options":["E[R_P] = √(w_A² E[R_A]² + w_B² E[R_B]² + 2ρ_AB w_A E[R_A] w_B E[R_B])","E[R_P] = w_A E[R_A] + w_B E[R_B]","E[R_P] = w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B","E[R_P] = (E[R_A] + E[R_B]) / 2 whatever the weights"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The portfolio's expected return is the dollar-weighted sum of the expected returns, E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k]. The square-root expression is the shape of the σ_P formula (wrongly applied to returns), and the simple average ignores how the money is actually split.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s22, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-404","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Consider two statements about a two-stock portfolio. (i) E[R_P] is the weighted average of the two stocks' expected returns. (ii) σ_P is the weighted average of the two stocks' standard deviations. Which is correct?","options":["Both are always true","(ii) is always true; (i) is true only when ρ_AB = 1","Neither is ever true","(i) is always true; (ii) is true only when ρ_AB = 1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Return is linear in the weights, so (i) holds for any correlation. Risk is not linear: σ_P = w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B only in the special case ρ_AB = 1 (and when a weight is zero); for ρ_AB < 1 the portfolio σ is below the weighted average.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, TB 13.2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-405","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT true of the expected return of a long-only two-stock portfolio?","options":["It is linear in the portfolio weights","It lies between the expected returns of the two stocks","It depends on the correlation between the two stocks","It can be computed by weighting each stock's expected return by its share of the dollars invested"],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R_P] = w_A E[R_A] + w_B E[R_B] contains no correlation term: correlation affects the risk of the portfolio, not its expected return. The other three statements are all true of a weighted average with non-negative weights summing to 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s19, s22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-406","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the correlation between two stocks not appear in the formula for a portfolio's expected return?","options":["Correlation only matters for stocks listed in different countries","E[R_P] is a weighted sum of the expected returns, so it is linear","Expected returns of the two stocks are always equal","The weights are always assumed to be equal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Expectation is linear, so E[w_A R_A + w_B R_B] = w_A E[R_A] + w_B E[R_B] regardless of how R_A and R_B move together. Correlation matters for the variance, where the cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B appears.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s19, s22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-407","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A long-only investor mixes Stock A (E[R] = 6%) and Stock B (E[R] = 12%) and reports a portfolio expected return of 13.5%. What does this figure tell you?","options":["There is an error: a long-only mix of A and B must have an expected return between 6% and 12%","Diversification has added a bonus return of 1.5% on top of the best stock","It is plausible if the correlation between A and B is very high","It is plausible if the correlation between A and B is negative"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A weighted average with non-negative weights summing to 1 cannot exceed the highest input, here 12%. Diversification lowers risk, not expected return, and correlation does not enter E[R_P] at all. The likely culprit is weights that sum to more than 1 (for example 0.25 and 1.00).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-CTQ5, L06 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-408","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The expected return of a portfolio is the _____ average of the expected returns of its assets, using the portfolio weights.","options":["simple","weighted","geometric","variance-weighted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k] is a weighted (arithmetic) average: bigger holdings count for more. A simple average is the special case of equal weights; a geometric average is for compounding past returns (Chapter 8), not for combining assets.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-409","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A long-only portfolio of two stocks can have an expected return above that of either stock if the correlation between them is sufficiently negative.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. E[R_P] is a weighted average, so with non-negative weights it must lie between the two expected returns; correlation never enters it. A very negative correlation lowers the portfolio's standard deviation, not its expected return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-CTQ5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-410","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"You know the dollar holdings and the expected return of each of two stocks. Which sequence gives the portfolio's expected return?","options":["Add the two expected returns and divide by two, ignoring the dollar amounts","Multiply each dollar amount by its expected return, add, and divide by the number of stocks","Find the weights, multiply the two weights together, and apply the product to the larger expected return","Find total value; divide each holding by it to get weights; multiply each weight by its expected return; add the products"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Weights come first (dollars / total), then each weight multiplies its own E[R], then the products are added. Averaging the returns while ignoring dollars is the equal-weights shortcut; dividing dollars-times-return by the number of stocks (instead of by total dollars) gives a number in dollars, not a return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-2, L06 s21-22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-411","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Trillium Fund puts 70% of its money in a Canadian bond index fund (E[R] = 4.5%) and 30% in a global equity fund (E[R] = 10.0%). What is its portfolio expected return?","options":["6.15%","7.25%","8.35%","13.15%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"E[R_P] = 0.70 × 4.5% + 0.30 × 10.0% = 3.15% + 3.00% = 6.15%. The 8.35% result swaps the weights, 7.25% is the simple average (equal weights assumed) and 13.15% leaves the equity fund unweighted so the weights add to more than 1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-3"},{"id":"fin201-q12-412","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"You own 400 shares of Bow Valley Utilities at $36.50 and 250 shares of Rideau Tech at $52.00. Their expected returns are 6.4% and 13.2%. What is the portfolio's expected return?","options":["9.02%","9.60%","9.80%","10.00%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Values: 400 × $36.50 = $14,600 and 250 × $52.00 = $13,000, total $27,600, so w = 0.5290 and 0.4710. E[R_P] = 0.5290 × 6.4% + 0.4710 × 13.2% = 9.60%. Using share counts (400 and 250) as the basis for weights gives 9.02%, swapping the weights 10.0%, and the simple average 9.80%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-1, 13-2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-413","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A cautious investor wants a portfolio expected return of 8% using a GIC fund (E[R] = 4%) and an equity fund (E[R] = 10%). What weight in the equity fund achieves this?","options":["33.33%","50.00%","66.67%","80.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Set 8 = w × 10 + (1 − w) × 4, so w = (8 − 4)/(10 − 4) = 66.67%. Dividing the target by the equity return (80%) ignores that part of the money earns 4%; 50% is the equal-weight guess; 33.33% is the weight in the GIC fund (the swapped answer).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-4, L06 s21-22"},{"id":"fin201-q12-414","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"You have $100,000 to invest. You invest $40,000 in a bank term deposit that is paying interest of 4% per annum, and $60,000 in a well-diversified portfolio of stocks that you think will return 9% per annum. What is your expected return?","answer":7.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"0.4*4 + 0.6*9","steps":[{"label":"Weight in the term deposit","value":0.4,"check":"40000/100000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Weight in the stock portfolio","value":0.6,"check":"60000/100000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_P]","value":7.0,"check":"0.4*4 + 0.6*9","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["w_deposit = 40,000 / 100,000 = 0.40 and w_stocks = 60,000 / 100,000 = 0.60 (they sum to 1)","E[R_P] = w_deposit × E[R_deposit] + w_stocks × E[R_stocks]","E[R_P] = 0.40 × 4% + 0.60 × 9% = 1.6% + 5.4% = 7.0% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.40 × 4 = 1.6","0.60 × 9 = 5.4","1.6 + 5.4 = 7.0"],"errors":[{"id":"equal-weights-assumed","label":"Averaged the two returns ignoring the dollars","value":6.5,"check":"(4+9)/2","explain":"The money is split 40/60, not 50/50; the simple average is only right for equal weights."},{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Swapped the weights","value":6.0,"check":"0.6*4 + 0.4*9","explain":"The 60% weight belongs to the 9% stock portfolio, not the 4% deposit."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Used weights of 1 on both assets","value":13.0,"check":"4 + 9","explain":"Weights must sum to 1; adding the two returns uses weights that sum to 2."}],"explanation":"E[R_P] = 0.40 × 4% + 0.60 × 9% = 7.0% per annum (the keyed answer on the sample exam). The result lies between the two returns, closer to 9% because 60% of the money is in stocks. The 6.5% answer is the simple average, the tempting error.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-415","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Heights Pension holds 2,400 units of Fund P priced at $18.75 and 1,800 units of Fund Q priced at $31.50. The expected returns are 7.8% for P and 11.4% for Q. What is the portfolio's expected return (% per annum)?","answer":9.81,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(2400*18.75)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)*7.8 + (1800*31.5)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)*11.4","steps":[{"label":"Total portfolio value","value":101700.0,"check":"2400*18.75+1800*31.5","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in Fund P","value":0.4425,"check":"(2400*18.75)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_P]","value":9.81,"check":"(2400*18.75)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)*7.8 + (1800*31.5)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)*11.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Values: P = 2,400 × $18.75 = $45,000 and Q = 1,800 × $31.50 = $56,700; total = $101,700","w_P = 45,000 / 101,700 = 0.4425 and w_Q = 0.5575","E[R_P] = 0.4425 × 7.8% + 0.5575 × 11.4% = 3.4513% + 6.3558% = 9.81%"],"keystrokes":["2400 × 18.75 = 45000 STO1; 1800 × 31.5 = 56700; + RCL1 = 101700","45000 ÷ 101700 = 0.4425 (w_P)","0.4425 × 7.8 + (1 − 0.4425) × 11.4 = 9.81"],"errors":[{"id":"shares-not-dollars","label":"Weighted by unit counts instead of dollar values","value":9.34,"check":"2400/4200*7.8 + 1800/4200*11.4","explain":"Weights are shares of dollars invested, not of units held; the two funds have different prices."},{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Swapped the weights","value":9.39,"check":"(1800*31.5)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)*7.8 + (2400*18.75)/(2400*18.75+1800*31.5)*11.4","explain":"Fund Q is the larger holding, so its (higher) return gets the larger weight."},{"id":"equal-weights-assumed","label":"Simple average of the two returns","value":9.6,"check":"(7.8+11.4)/2","explain":"The holdings are not equal in value, so a simple average misstates the portfolio return."}],"explanation":"Weights come from dollar values (units × price), not unit counts: w_P = 45,000/101,700 = 0.4425. E[R_P] = 0.4425 × 7.8% + 0.5575 × 11.4% = 9.81%, above the simple average of 9.6% because the higher-return fund is the larger holding.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-1, 13-2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-416","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Pinecrest Savings Club allocates 25% of its money to a money-market fund (E[R] = 3.2%), 40% to Canadian equities (E[R] = 9.6%) and 35% to US equities (E[R] = 11.8%). What is the portfolio's expected return (% per annum)?","answer":8.77,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.25*3.2 + 0.40*9.6 + 0.35*11.8","steps":[{"label":"Contribution of money market","value":0.8,"check":"0.25*3.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Contribution of Canadian equities","value":3.84,"check":"0.40*9.6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Contribution of US equities","value":4.13,"check":"0.35*11.8","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_P]","value":8.77,"check":"0.25*3.2 + 0.40*9.6 + 0.35*11.8","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Check the weights: 0.25 + 0.40 + 0.35 = 1.00","E[R_P] = Σ w_k E[R_k] = 0.25 × 3.2% + 0.40 × 9.6% + 0.35 × 11.8%","= 0.80% + 3.84% + 4.13% = 8.77% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.25 × 3.2 = 0.80","0.40 × 9.6 = 3.84","0.35 × 11.8 = 4.13","0.80 + 3.84 + 4.13 = 8.77"],"errors":[{"id":"equal-weights-assumed","label":"Equal weights of 1/3 on each fund","value":8.2,"check":"(3.2+9.6+11.8)/3","explain":"The weights are 25/40/35, not equal."},{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Attached the weights in the wrong order","value":7.91,"check":"0.35*3.2 + 0.40*9.6 + 0.25*11.8","explain":"Each weight must be paired with its own fund's return; here the 35% belongs to US equities."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Used 45% instead of 35% on US equities (weights sum to 1.10)","value":9.95,"check":"0.25*3.2 + 0.40*9.6 + 0.45*11.8","explain":"Always confirm the weights sum to 1 before multiplying."}],"explanation":"E[R_P] = 0.25(3.2) + 0.40(9.6) + 0.35(11.8) = 8.77%. The formula extends to any number of assets: Σ w_k E[R_k] with Σ w_k = 1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-3, 13-8"},{"id":"fin201-q12-417","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You have $25,000 to invest in Stock X (E[R] = 8.2%) and Stock Y (E[R] = 12.6%) and want a portfolio expected return of 9.8%. How many dollars should you put in Stock Y?","answer":9090.91,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(9.8-8.2)/(12.6-8.2)*25000","steps":[{"label":"Weight in Y","value":0.3636,"check":"(9.8-8.2)/(12.6-8.2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Dollars in Y","value":9090.91,"check":"(9.8-8.2)/(12.6-8.2)*25000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Dollars in X","value":15909.09,"check":"25000-(9.8-8.2)/(12.6-8.2)*25000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["With w_X = 1 − w_Y: 9.8 = w_Y(12.6) + (1 − w_Y)(8.2) = 8.2 + 4.4 w_Y","w_Y = (9.8 − 8.2)/(12.6 − 8.2) = 1.6/4.4 = 0.3636","Dollars in Y = 0.3636 × $25,000 = $9,090.91; dollars in X = $15,909.09"],"keystrokes":["(9.8 − 8.2) ÷ (12.6 − 8.2) = 0.363636","× 25000 = 9090.91"],"errors":[{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Reported the dollars in X instead","value":15909.09,"check":"25000-(9.8-8.2)/(12.6-8.2)*25000","explain":"That is the amount in X; the question asks for Y."},{"id":"target-over-asset","label":"Divided the target by Y's return","value":19444.44,"check":"9.8/12.6*25000","explain":"That treats the rest of the money as earning 0%, but X earns 8.2%."},{"id":"denominator-not-spread","label":"Forgot the 8.2% earned on the rest: used (9.8 − 8.2)/12.6","value":3174.6,"check":"(9.8-8.2)/12.6*25000","explain":"Solving w × 12.6 + (1 − w) × 8.2 = 9.8 gives a denominator of 12.6 − 8.2, not 12.6."}],"explanation":"Because the weights sum to 1, w_Y = (target − E[R_X]) / (E[R_Y] − E[R_X]) = 0.3636, so $9,090.91 goes in Y and $15,909.09 in X. Check: 0.6364 × 8.2% + 0.3636 × 12.6% = 9.8%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-4"},{"id":"fin201-q12-418","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A portfolio is 60% Stock A and 40% Stock B. Boom (probability 0.30): A returns 24%, B returns 4%. Normal (0.50): A 10%, B 6%. Recession (0.20): A −12%, B 12%. What is the portfolio's expected return (% per annum)?","answer":8.52,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.30*(0.6*24+0.4*4) + 0.50*(0.6*10+0.4*6) + 0.20*(0.6*(-12)+0.4*12)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio return in Boom","value":16.0,"check":"0.6*24+0.4*4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio return in Normal","value":8.4,"check":"0.6*10+0.4*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio return in Recession","value":-2.4,"check":"0.6*(-12)+0.4*12","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_P]","value":8.52,"check":"0.30*(0.6*24+0.4*4) + 0.50*(0.6*10+0.4*6) + 0.20*(0.6*(-12)+0.4*12)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Portfolio return in each state = 0.6 R_A + 0.4 R_B: Boom 14.4 + 1.6 = 16.0%; Normal 6.0 + 2.4 = 8.4%; Recession −7.2 + 4.8 = −2.4%","E[R_P] = Σ p R_P = 0.30(16.0) + 0.50(8.4) + 0.20(−2.4) = 4.80 + 4.20 − 0.48 = 8.52%","Check: E[R_A] = 9.8%, E[R_B] = 6.6%, so 0.6(9.8) + 0.4(6.6) = 8.52% (linearity)"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 24 + 0.4 × 4 = 16.0","0.6 × 10 + 0.4 × 6 = 8.4","0.6 × (−12) + 0.4 × 12 = −2.4","0.30 × 16 + 0.50 × 8.4 + 0.20 × (−2.4) = 8.52"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Averaged the three state returns with equal weight","value":7.33,"check":"(16.0+8.4-2.4)/3","explain":"The states are not equally likely; each state's return must be multiplied by its probability."},{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Used 40% in A and 60% in B","value":7.88,"check":"0.30*(0.4*24+0.6*4) + 0.50*(0.4*10+0.6*6) + 0.20*(0.4*(-12)+0.6*12)","explain":"A is 60% of the money, B is 40%."},{"id":"dropped-state","label":"Left out the recession state (probabilities add to 0.80)","value":9.0,"check":"0.30*16 + 0.50*8.4","explain":"Probabilities must sum to 1: the recession state (probability 0.20) cannot be dropped."}],"explanation":"Compute the portfolio return state by state, then take the probability-weighted average: 8.52%. Linearity means the same answer comes from weighting the stocks' own expected returns (9.8% and 6.6%).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.2, SM 13-9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-419","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You invest your own $50,000 plus $20,000 borrowed at the risk-free rate of 3.5% per annum, all in an index fund with an expected return of 9.5%. What is the expected return on your own $50,000 (% per annum)?","answer":11.9,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"1.4*9.5 + (-0.4)*3.5","steps":[{"label":"Weight in the index fund","value":1.4,"check":"70000/50000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Weight in the risk-free asset (borrowing)","value":-0.4,"check":"-20000/50000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_P]","value":11.9,"check":"1.4*9.5 + (-0.4)*3.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Your own money is the base: w_fund = 70,000 / 50,000 = 1.40 and w_F = −20,000 / 50,000 = −0.40 (borrowing is a negative weight); 1.40 − 0.40 = 1","E[R_P] = 1.40 × 9.5% + (−0.40) × 3.5% = 13.3% − 1.4% = 11.9% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.4 × 9.5 = 13.3","−0.4 × 3.5 = −1.4","13.3 − 1.4 = 11.9"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Ignored the borrowing cost","value":13.3,"check":"1.4*9.5","explain":"The loan costs 3.5% on 40% of your capital; it must enter with a negative weight."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Gave the loan a positive weight of +0.40","value":14.7,"check":"1.4*9.5 + 0.4*3.5","explain":"Borrowing is a negative position in the risk-free asset; weights then sum to 1.00, not 1.80."},{"id":"ignored-leverage","label":"Reported the fund's own return","value":9.5,"check":"9.5","explain":"Borrowing magnifies the expected return on your own money: you earn 9.5% on $70,000 but pay interest on $20,000."}],"explanation":"With borrowing, weights are measured against your own money: 1.40 in the fund and −0.40 in the risk-free asset (still summing to 1). E[R_P] = 1.40(9.5) − 0.40(3.5) = 11.9%, higher than 9.5% because leverage adds expected return (and risk).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s38-39, SM 13-19"},{"id":"fin201-q12-420","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A portfolio's weights are +1.25 in an equity index fund and −0.25 in the risk-free asset. What does the negative weight mean?","options":["The investor has invested in an asset with a negative expected return","The weights are invalid because portfolio weights must lie between 0 and 1","The investor has borrowed at the risk-free rate and invested more than 100% of her own money in the fund","The investor holds fewer dollars in the fund than in the risk-free asset"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Weights must sum to 1 (1.25 − 0.25 = 1) but individual weights can be negative: a negative risk-free weight is borrowing, and the borrowed money lets her hold 125% of her own money in the fund. This is the w_F < 0, w_P > 1 end of the capital allocation line.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q12-421","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"You hold $30,000 of Stock A (E[R] = 5%) and $20,000 of Stock B (E[R] = 11%). What is the portfolio's expected return?","options":["8.00%","8.60%","16.00%","None of the above"],"answer":3,"explanation":"w_A = 30,000/50,000 = 0.60 and w_B = 0.40, so E[R_P] = 0.60 × 5% + 0.40 × 11% = 3.0% + 4.4% = 7.40%, which is not listed. 8.00% is the simple average of 5% and 11%, 8.60% swaps the weights (0.4 × 5% + 0.6 × 11%), and 16.00% adds the returns (weights of 1 on both).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-422","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which expression gives the standard deviation of a two-stock portfolio in terms of the weights, the standard deviations and the correlation?","options":["σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B)","σ_P = w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B","σ_P = w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B","σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B²)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The formula adds the two weighted variances and the correlation cross-term, then takes the square root. Without the square root the result is a variance; w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B is right only when ρ_AB = 1; dropping the cross-term assumes ρ_AB = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s23-24, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-423","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"An analyst evaluates √(0.5² × 20² + 0.5² × 30² + 2 × 0.4 × 0.5 × 20 × 0.5 × 30) and gets 21.1 (in percent). Which quantity has she computed?","options":["The expected return of a 50/50 portfolio of the two stocks","The standard deviation of a 50/50 portfolio of the two stocks","The covariance between the two stocks","The beta of the portfolio against the market"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The expression has the exact shape of σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B) with w = 0.5, σ = 20% and 30%, ρ = 0.4. Expected return would be a plain weighted sum, covariance has no weights, and beta needs the market's variance.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s23"},{"id":"fin201-q12-424","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-covariance-correlation"],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A text gives the portfolio SD formula with ρ_AB in the cross-term; a case study gives cov(A,B) instead. Which statement about the two forms is correct?","options":["With a covariance you still write 2 ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B, putting the covariance in the ρ slot","The covariance form has no cross-term because covariance already includes the weights","They are the same formula because cov(A,B) = ρ_AB σ_A σ_B: use 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B) when the covariance is given","The covariance form applies only when ρ_AB = 0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Since cov(A,B) = ρ_AB σ_A σ_B, the cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B equals 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B). Putting a covariance (for example 0.021) where a correlation belongs is the classic cov-as-ρ error and nearly deletes the cross-term.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-425","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT needed to compute the standard deviation of a two-stock portfolio?","options":["The expected returns of the two stocks","The portfolio weights","The standard deviation of each stock","The correlation (or covariance) between the two stocks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"σ_P depends on weights, individual risks and the correlation or covariance. Expected returns affect E[R_P] but never enter the risk formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q12-426","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the standard deviation of a two-stock portfolio normally smaller than w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B?","options":["Because variances are added after weighting, which always makes the total smaller","Because the portfolio's expected return is a weighted average","Because the weights are squared in the formula, and squares are always smaller","Because the stocks do not move in lockstep, so one's bad outcome is partly offset by the other's"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The weighted average of SDs is exactly what the formula gives at ρ = 1, where the cross-term is as large as it can be. For ρ < 1 the cross-term shrinks, so σ_P falls below the weighted average. Squared weights alone do not explain the gap: at ρ = 1 they still give the weighted average.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s18, s24"},{"id":"fin201-q12-427","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 50/50 mix of Stock A (σ = 24%) and Stock B (σ = 12%) is reported to have σ_P = 18%. What does this tell you about the correlation?","options":["ρ_AB = 0, because the stocks are independent","ρ_AB = −1, because the risks offset each other","ρ_AB = +1, because 18% equals the weighted average 0.5 × 24% + 0.5 × 12%","Nothing: σ_P always equals the weighted average of the two SDs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The weighted average of the SDs is 0.5(24) + 0.5(12) = 18%. σ_P reaches that ceiling only when the returns are perfectly positively correlated, so ρ_AB = +1 and there is no diversification benefit. For ρ = 0 the SD would be 13.4% and for ρ = −1 it would be 6%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-428","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A 40/60 mix of Stock A (σ = 30%) and Stock B (σ = 20%) has a portfolio standard deviation of exactly zero. What must be true?","options":["ρ_AB = 0, because the two stocks are independent","ρ_AB = −1, so the weighted risks 0.4 × 30% = 12% and 0.6 × 20% = 12% cancel exactly","ρ_AB = +1, because the weights add up to one","Any correlation produces this result because the weights are well chosen"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With ρ = −1 the formula is a perfect square: σ_P = |w_A σ_A − w_B σ_B| = |12% − 12%| = 0. Only perfect negative correlation allows the risk to cancel completely; at ρ = 0 this mix has σ_P = 16.97%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-429","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"The weights and standard deviations of two stocks are held fixed while the correlation between them falls from 0.6 to 0.1. What happens to σ_P?","options":["σ_P falls, because the cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B shrinks","σ_P rises, because lower correlation means more uncertainty","σ_P is unchanged, because the weights and σ's are unchanged","σ_P cannot be determined without the expected returns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ρ appears only in the cross-term, which is proportional to ρ (positive weights). Lowering ρ lowers the variance and therefore σ_P; the expected returns play no role in the risk formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-430","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"A portfolio of 50% in an equity fund with σ = 20% and 50% in Treasury bills has a standard deviation of 10%.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. A risk-free asset has σ_F = 0 and no correlation with anything, so the formula collapses to σ_P = w_P σ_P = 0.50 × 20% = 10%. The capital allocation line is straight for this reason.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s40"},{"id":"fin201-q12-431","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"The variance of a two-stock portfolio equals the weighted average w_A σ_A² + w_B σ_B² of the two stocks' variances.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The weights are squared (w_A² σ_A² + w_B² σ_B²) and a covariance cross-term 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B) is added. Risk is not linear in the weights, unlike expected return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q12-432","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"If the correlation ρ_AB between two stocks is 0, the cross-term in the formula for the portfolio variance _____.","options":["doubles","becomes negative","equals w_A w_B","disappears"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The cross-term 2ρ_AB w_A σ_A w_B σ_B is proportional to ρ_AB, so it is zero when ρ_AB = 0 and σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B²). It is negative only when ρ_AB < 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-433","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence correctly computes the standard deviation of a two-stock portfolio from weights, SDs and a correlation?","options":["Find the weights; compute w_A²σ_A² and w_B²σ_B²; add the cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B; sum to get the variance; take the square root","Find the weights; take the square root of each term; then add the terms together","Sum the two squared terms; take the square root; then add the cross-term","Find the weights; multiply the two SDs together; take the square root; weight the result"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The square root is the last step and applies to the whole variance. Taking square roots term by term (or before adding the cross-term) breaks the formula and tends to reproduce a weighted average of SDs.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s23-24, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-434","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 20% and Stock B has σ = 30%. Their correlation is 0.3. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio with 60% in A and 40% in B?","options":["3.74%","16.97%","19.35%","24.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Variance terms: 0.6² × 20² = 144.0; 0.4² × 30² = 144.0; cross-term 2(0.3)(0.6)(20)(0.4)(30) = 86.4. Sum = 374.4, so σ_P = √374.4 = 19.35%. The 24.00% answer is the ρ = 1 weighted average, 16.97% omits the correlation cross-term, and 3.74% forgets the square root (a decimal variance of 0.0374).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-435","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-covariance-correlation"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 15% and Stock B has σ = 25%; cov(A,B) = 0.0150 (returns in decimals). What is the standard deviation of an equally weighted portfolio of the two?","options":["14.58%","14.67%","16.96%","20.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Using the covariance form: variance = 0.5²(0.15²) + 0.5²(0.25²) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(0.0150) = 0.005625 + 0.015625 + 0.0075 = 0.028750, so σ_P = 16.96%. Putting 0.0150 in the ρ slot of the correlation formula gives 14.67%; omitting the covariance gives 14.58%; the weighted average is 20.00% (the ρ = 1 result; here ρ = 0.4).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-436","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two uncorrelated stocks (ρ = 0) have σ = 16% and σ = 28%. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio with 70% in the first and 30% in the second?","options":["1.96%","14.00%","19.60%","20.36%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With ρ = 0 the cross-term vanishes: variance = 0.7²(16²) + 0.3²(28²) = 125.44 + 70.56 = 196.00, so σ_P = 14.00%. The 19.60% answer is the weighted average (ρ = 1 shortcut), 20.36% forgets to square the weights, and 1.96% stops at the variance.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-437","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"You hold $12,000 in the Bay Street Equity Fund (σ = 18%) and $8,000 in Treasury bills. What is the standard deviation of the whole portfolio?","options":["6.48%","7.20%","10.80%","18.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The T-bills are risk-free (σ = 0), so σ_P = w × σ_fund with w = 12,000/20,000 = 0.60: 0.60 × 18% = 10.80%. Using the T-bill weight (7.20%) uses the wrong weight; 18.00% ignores the T-bills; 6.48% squares the weight without squaring σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s40, SM 13-19"},{"id":"fin201-q12-438","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 22% and Stock B has σ = 34%; their correlation is 0.35. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio with 60% in A and 40% in B (% per annum)?","answer":22.02,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*22**2 + 0.4**2*34**2 + 2*0.35*0.6*22*0.4*34)","steps":[{"label":"Variance of the A component, w_A²σ_A²","value":174.24,"check":"0.6**2*22**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Variance of the B component, w_B²σ_B²","value":184.96,"check":"0.4**2*34**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B","value":125.66,"check":"2*0.35*0.6*22*0.4*34","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":22.02,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*22**2 + 0.4**2*34**2 + 2*0.35*0.6*22*0.4*34)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["w_A²σ_A² = 0.36 × 484 = 174.24; w_B²σ_B² = 0.16 × 1,156 = 184.96","Cross-term = 2 × 0.35 × 0.6 × 22 × 0.4 × 34 = 125.664","Variance = 174.24 + 184.96 + 125.664 = 484.864 (in %²)","σ_P = √484.864 = 22.02% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.6 x² × 22 x² = 174.24","0.16 × 1156 = 184.96","2 × 0.35 × 0.6 × 22 × 0.4 × 34 = 125.664","sum = 484.864, then 2nd √x = 22.02"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance (as a decimal) instead of its square root","value":4.85,"check":"(0.6**2*22**2 + 0.4**2*34**2 + 2*0.35*0.6*22*0.4*34)/100","explain":"σ_P is the square root of the portfolio variance; stopping at the variance gives a number in squared units."},{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Used the weighted average of the two SDs (the ρ = 1 shortcut)","value":26.8,"check":"0.6*22 + 0.4*34","explain":"w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B is the answer only when ρ = 1; for ρ < 1 the portfolio SD is smaller."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Left out the correlation cross-term (treated ρ as 0)","value":18.95,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*22**2 + 0.4**2*34**2)","explain":"The cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B carries the correlation; dropping it assumes the stocks are uncorrelated."}],"explanation":"σ_P = √(0.36·22² + 0.16·34² + 2·0.35·0.6·22·0.4·34) = 22.02%. This is below the 26.80% weighted average because ρ = 0.35 < 1 – the diversification benefit – and above the 18.95% you get by wrongly ignoring correlation.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s23"},{"id":"fin201-q12-439","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-covariance-correlation"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 20% and Stock B has σ = 30%; the covariance of their returns is 0.0210 (returns in decimals). What is the standard deviation of a portfolio with 60% in A and 40% in B (% per annum)?","answer":19.72,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*0.20**2 + 0.4**2*0.30**2 + 2*0.6*0.4*0.0210)*100","steps":[{"label":"Weighted variance of A, w_A²σ_A²","value":0.0144,"check":"0.6**2*0.20**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"Weighted variance of B, w_B²σ_B²","value":0.0144,"check":"0.4**2*0.30**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"Cross-term 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B)","value":0.01008,"check":"2*0.6*0.4*0.0210","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Portfolio variance","value":0.03888,"check":"0.6**2*0.20**2 + 0.4**2*0.30**2 + 2*0.6*0.4*0.0210","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":19.72,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*0.20**2 + 0.4**2*0.30**2 + 2*0.6*0.4*0.0210)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Use cov directly: variance = w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B)","= 0.36 × 0.04 + 0.16 × 0.09 + 2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 0.0210 = 0.0144 + 0.0144 + 0.01008 = 0.03888","σ_P = √0.03888 = 19.72% per annum (the implied ρ is 0.0210/(0.20 × 0.30) = 0.35)"],"keystrokes":["0.36 × 0.04 = 0.0144","0.16 × 0.09 = 0.0144","2 × 0.6 × 0.4 × 0.021 = 0.01008","sum = 0.03888, √x = 0.1972 → 19.72%"],"errors":[{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Put the covariance in the ρ slot of the correlation formula","value":17.15,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*0.20**2 + 0.4**2*0.30**2 + 2*0.0210*0.6*0.20*0.4*0.30)*100","explain":"Covariance already contains σ_A σ_B; the correlation formula needs ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B) = 0.35, not 0.0210."},{"id":"forgot-factor-2","label":"Forgot the 2 on the cross-term","value":18.4,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*0.20**2 + 0.4**2*0.30**2 + 0.6*0.4*0.0210)*100","explain":"The cross-term appears twice (A with B and B with A), hence the 2."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Dropped the covariance term","value":16.97,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*0.20**2 + 0.4**2*0.30**2)*100","explain":"A covariance of 0.0210 is positive and adds to the portfolio variance; leaving it out understates the risk."}],"explanation":"With a covariance given, use σ_P² = w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B). Work in decimals throughout (cov = 0.0210 is in decimal squared units) and convert σ_P back to percent at the end: 19.72%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-440","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Stock A (σ = 24%) and Stock B (σ = 18%) are uncorrelated (ρ = 0). What is the standard deviation of an equally weighted portfolio of the two (% per annum)?","answer":15.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2 + 0.5**2*18**2)","steps":[{"label":"Variance of the A component, w_A²σ_A²","value":144.0,"check":"0.5**2*24**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Variance of the B component, w_B²σ_B²","value":81.0,"check":"0.5**2*18**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":15.0,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2 + 0.5**2*18**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["ρ = 0, so the cross-term is zero: σ_P² = w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B²","= 0.25 × 576 + 0.25 × 324 = 144 + 81 = 225","σ_P = √225 = 15.00% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.25 × 576 = 144","0.25 × 324 = 81","144 + 81 = 225, √x = 15.00"],"errors":[{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Used the weighted average of the SDs","value":21.0,"check":"0.5*24 + 0.5*18","explain":"That is the ρ = 1 answer; with ρ = 0 the portfolio SD is lower."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance as a percent (decimal 0.0225)","value":2.25,"check":"(0.5**2*24**2 + 0.5**2*18**2)/100","explain":"225 is the variance in %²; the SD is its square root."},{"id":"weights-ignored","label":"Took √(σ_A² + σ_B²) without weights","value":30.0,"check":"sqrt(24**2 + 18**2)","explain":"Each variance must be multiplied by the square of its weight."}],"explanation":"For uncorrelated stocks σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B²) = √225 = 15.00%, well below the 21.00% weighted average (the ρ = 1 value).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-441","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 25% and Stock B has σ = 15%, and the two are perfectly positively correlated (ρ = +1). What is the standard deviation of a portfolio with 40% in A and 60% in B (% per annum)?","answer":19.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.4**2*25**2 + 0.6**2*15**2 + 2*1*0.4*25*0.6*15)","steps":[{"label":"Weighted risk of A, w_A σ_A","value":10.0,"check":"0.4*25","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weighted risk of B, w_B σ_B","value":9.0,"check":"0.6*15","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":19.0,"check":"sqrt(0.4**2*25**2 + 0.6**2*15**2 + 2*1*0.4*25*0.6*15)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["With ρ = 1 the formula is a perfect square: σ_P = √(w_Aσ_A + w_Bσ_B)² = w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B","= 0.4 × 25% + 0.6 × 15% = 10% + 9% = 19.00%","Check with the full formula: √(100 + 81 + 2 × 1 × 10 × 9) = √361 = 19.00%"],"keystrokes":["0.4 × 25 = 10","0.6 × 15 = 9","10 + 9 = 19.00"],"errors":[{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Left out the correlation cross-term (treated ρ as 0)","value":13.45,"check":"sqrt(0.4**2*25**2 + 0.6**2*15**2)","explain":"The cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B carries the correlation; dropping it assumes the stocks are uncorrelated."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance (361) scaled to a decimal percent","value":3.61,"check":"(0.4**2*25**2 + 0.6**2*15**2 + 2*1*0.4*25*0.6*15)/100","explain":"361 is the variance; the SD is its square root."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Added the stand-alone SDs with no weights","value":40.0,"check":"25 + 15","explain":"Each SD is multiplied by its weight; the weights sum to 1."}],"explanation":"ρ = +1 is the one case where risk is linear: σ_P = 0.4(25) + 0.6(15) = 19.00%. Any ρ < 1 would give less than this, which is why 19% is the upper limit for every mix of these two stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-442","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 30% and Stock B has σ = 20%, and the two are perfectly negatively correlated (ρ = −1). What is the standard deviation of a portfolio with 60% in A and 40% in B (% per annum)?","answer":10.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*30**2 + 0.4**2*20**2 + 2*(-1)*0.6*30*0.4*20)","steps":[{"label":"Weighted risk of A, w_A σ_A","value":18.0,"check":"0.6*30","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weighted risk of B, w_B σ_B","value":8.0,"check":"0.4*20","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P = |w_Aσ_A − w_Bσ_B|","value":10.0,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*30**2 + 0.4**2*20**2 + 2*(-1)*0.6*30*0.4*20)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["With ρ = −1 the formula is the perfect square (w_Aσ_A − w_Bσ_B)², so σ_P = |w_A σ_A − w_B σ_B|","= |0.6 × 30% − 0.4 × 20%| = |18% − 8%| = 10.00%","Check: √(324 + 64 + 2 × (−1) × 18 × 8) = √(388 − 288) = √100 = 10.00%"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 30 = 18","0.4 × 20 = 8","|18 − 8| = 10.00"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance (as a decimal) instead of its square root","value":1.0,"check":"(0.6**2*30**2 + 0.4**2*20**2 + 2*(-1)*0.6*30*0.4*20)/100","explain":"σ_P is the square root of the portfolio variance; stopping at the variance gives a number in squared units."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Left out the correlation cross-term (treated ρ as 0)","value":19.7,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*30**2 + 0.4**2*20**2)","explain":"The cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B carries the correlation; dropping it assumes the stocks are uncorrelated."},{"id":"sign-of-rho","label":"Used ρ = +1 in the cross-term (added the two risks)","value":26.0,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*30**2 + 0.4**2*20**2 + 2*0.6*30*0.4*20)","explain":"The cross-term is 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B with ρ = −1: it subtracts 288, it does not add it."}],"explanation":"At ρ = −1 the two risks offset: σ_P = |18% − 8%| = 10.00%, far below the 26.00% weighted average. Changing the weights to 40/60 would give 12% − 12% = 0, a riskless portfolio.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-443","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Of a $40,000 portfolio, $25,000 is invested in a Canadian equity fund with σ = 19.2% and the rest in Treasury bills. What is the portfolio's standard deviation (% per annum)?","answer":12.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"25000/40000*19.2","steps":[{"label":"Weight in the equity fund","value":0.625,"check":"25000/40000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0005}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P = w σ_fund","value":12.0,"check":"25000/40000*19.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["T-bills have σ = 0 and ρ = 0 with the fund, so the formula reduces to σ_P = w_P σ_P","w = 25,000 / 40,000 = 0.625","σ_P = 0.625 × 19.2% = 12.00% per annum"],"keystrokes":["25000 ÷ 40000 = 0.625","0.625 × 19.2 = 12.00"],"errors":[{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Used the T-bill weight (0.375)","value":7.2,"check":"15000/40000*19.2","explain":"The risk comes from the equity fund, so its weight (0.625) multiplies σ."},{"id":"ignored-rf-weight","label":"Reported the fund's own SD","value":19.2,"check":"19.2","explain":"Holding part of the money in T-bills scales the risk down in proportion."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Squared the weight and σ without a square root (decimal variance)","value":1.44,"check":"(25000/40000*19.2)**2/100","explain":"σ_P = w σ already; no squaring is needed for the risk-free mix."}],"explanation":"With a risk-free asset the cross-term and the risk-free variance both vanish, so σ_P is simply the risky weight times the risky σ: 0.625 × 19.2% = 12.00%. This straight-line scaling is why a capital allocation line is a straight line.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s40"},{"id":"fin201-q12-444","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You use $40,000 of your own money and borrow $20,000 at the risk-free rate of 3% per annum, investing all $60,000 in a fund with E[R] = 9% and σ = 14%. What is the standard deviation of the return on your own $40,000 (% per annum)?","answer":21.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"1.5*14","steps":[{"label":"Weight in the fund","value":1.5,"check":"60000/40000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P = w σ_fund","value":21.0,"check":"1.5*14","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Weights on your own money: w_fund = 60,000/40,000 = 1.50 and w_F = −20,000/40,000 = −0.50","σ_P = w_fund × σ_fund (the risk-free asset adds no variance) = 1.50 × 14% = 21.00%","Expected return for reference: 1.5 × 9% − 0.5 × 3% = 12.0%"],"keystrokes":["60000 ÷ 40000 = 1.5","1.5 × 14 = 21.00"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-leverage","label":"Reported the fund's own SD","value":14.0,"check":"14","explain":"Borrowing to hold 150% of your capital in the fund multiplies the risk by 1.5."},{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Used the borrowing weight (0.5)","value":7.0,"check":"0.5*14","explain":"The fund weight is 1.5, not 0.5; the risk-free weight is −0.5."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Counted the loan as a positive 0.5 weight: (1.5 + 0.5) × 14","value":28.0,"check":"(1.5+0.5)*14","explain":"Borrowing is a negative weight; the weights must sum to 1 (1.5 − 0.5), and the risk-free asset adds no risk."}],"explanation":"Leverage scales risk linearly: σ_P = 1.5 × 14% = 21.00%. The loan has no variance (it is risk-free), so all of the risk comes from the 150% position in the fund.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39-40"},{"id":"fin201-q12-445","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"You hold $30,000 of Stock A (σ = 28%) and $20,000 of Stock B (σ = 16%). The correlation between them is 0.2. What is the standard deviation of the portfolio (% per annum)?","answer":19.14,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*28**2 + 0.4**2*16**2 + 2*0.2*0.6*28*0.4*16)","steps":[{"label":"Weight in A","value":0.6,"check":"30000/50000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.001}},{"label":"Portfolio variance (%²)","value":366.21,"check":"0.6**2*28**2 + 0.4**2*16**2 + 2*0.2*0.6*28*0.4*16","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":19.14,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*28**2 + 0.4**2*16**2 + 2*0.2*0.6*28*0.4*16)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Weights: w_A = 30,000/50,000 = 0.60 and w_B = 0.40","Variance = 0.36 × 784 + 0.16 × 256 + 2 × 0.2 × 0.6 × 28 × 0.4 × 16 = 282.240 + 40.960 + 43.008 = 366.208","σ_P = √366.208 = 19.14% per annum"],"keystrokes":["30000 ÷ 50000 = 0.6","0.36 × 784 + 0.16 × 256 + 2 × 0.2 × 0.6 × 28 × 0.4 × 16 = 366.208","2nd √x = 19.14"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance (as a decimal) instead of its square root","value":3.66,"check":"(0.6**2*28**2 + 0.4**2*16**2 + 2*0.2*0.6*28*0.4*16)/100","explain":"σ_P is the square root of the portfolio variance; stopping at the variance gives a number in squared units."},{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Used the weighted average of the two SDs (the ρ = 1 shortcut)","value":23.2,"check":"0.6*28 + 0.4*16","explain":"w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B is the answer only when ρ = 1; for ρ < 1 the portfolio SD is smaller."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Left out the correlation cross-term (treated ρ as 0)","value":17.98,"check":"sqrt(0.6**2*28**2 + 0.4**2*16**2)","explain":"The cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B carries the correlation; dropping it assumes the stocks are uncorrelated."}],"explanation":"Convert dollars to weights first (0.60, 0.40), then apply σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B) = 19.14%, below the 23.20% weighted average.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-1, 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-446","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Invest 75% of your funds in Asset X (E[R] = 9%, σ = 14%) and 25% in Asset Y (E[R] = 15%, σ = 20%). The correlation between X and Y is 0.3. What is the standard deviation of the portfolio (% per annum)?","answer":12.91,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.75**2*14**2 + 0.25**2*20**2 + 2*0.3*0.75*14*0.25*20)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio expected return E[R_P]","value":10.5,"check":"0.75*9 + 0.25*15","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio variance (%²)","value":166.75,"check":"0.75**2*14**2 + 0.25**2*20**2 + 2*0.3*0.75*14*0.25*20","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":12.91,"check":"sqrt(0.75**2*14**2 + 0.25**2*20**2 + 2*0.3*0.75*14*0.25*20)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["E[R_P] = 0.75 × 9% + 0.25 × 15% = 10.50% (linear, no ρ needed)","Variance = 0.5625 × 196 + 0.0625 × 400 + 2 × 0.3 × 0.75 × 14 × 0.25 × 20 = 110.25 + 25.00 + 31.50 = 166.75","σ_P = √166.75 = 12.91% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.5625 × 196 = 110.25","0.0625 × 400 = 25","2 × 0.3 × 0.75 × 14 × 0.25 × 20 = 31.5","sum 166.75, 2nd √x = 12.91"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance (as a decimal) instead of its square root","value":1.67,"check":"(0.75**2*14**2 + 0.25**2*20**2 + 2*0.3*0.75*14*0.25*20)/100","explain":"σ_P is the square root of the portfolio variance; stopping at the variance gives a number in squared units."},{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Used the weighted average of the two SDs (the ρ = 1 shortcut)","value":15.5,"check":"0.75*14 + 0.25*20","explain":"w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B is the answer only when ρ = 1; for ρ < 1 the portfolio SD is smaller."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Left out the correlation cross-term (treated ρ as 0)","value":11.63,"check":"sqrt(0.75**2*14**2 + 0.25**2*20**2)","explain":"The cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B carries the correlation; dropping it assumes the stocks are uncorrelated."}],"explanation":"Return is a weighted average (10.50%) but risk is not: σ_P = 12.91% is less than the weighted average of 15.5% because ρ = 0.3 < 1. Report the pair (10.50%, 12.91%) as the portfolio's point on the risk-return diagram.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s11-13, L06 s22-24"},{"id":"fin201-q12-447","concept":"fin201-c12-portfolio-sd","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return","fin201-c12-covariance-correlation"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A portfolio is 50% Stock A and 50% Stock B. Boom (probability 0.25): A 20%, B −4%. Normal (0.50): A 8%, B 6%. Recession (0.25): A −10%, B 14%. What is the standard deviation of the portfolio's return (% per annum)?","answer":2.35,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.25*(8-6)**2 + 0.50*(7-6)**2 + 0.25*(2-6)**2)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio return in Boom","value":8.0,"check":"0.5*20+0.5*(-4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio return in Normal","value":7.0,"check":"0.5*8+0.5*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio return in Recession","value":2.0,"check":"0.5*(-10)+0.5*14","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_P]","value":6.0,"check":"0.25*8 + 0.50*7 + 0.25*2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio variance (%²)","value":5.5,"check":"0.25*(8-6)**2 + 0.50*(7-6)**2 + 0.25*(2-6)**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":2.35,"check":"sqrt(0.25*(8-6)**2 + 0.50*(7-6)**2 + 0.25*(2-6)**2)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Portfolio return in each state: Boom 0.5(20) + 0.5(−4) = 8%; Normal 0.5(8) + 0.5(6) = 7%; Recession 0.5(−10) + 0.5(14) = 2%","E[R_P] = 0.25(8) + 0.50(7) + 0.25(2) = 6.00%","σ_P² = 0.25(8 − 6)² + 0.50(7 − 6)² + 0.25(2 − 6)² = 1.00 + 0.50 + 4.00 = 5.50","σ_P = √5.50 = 2.35% per annum (the formula route gives the same: σ_A = 10.71%, σ_B = 6.38%, cov = −66.75 %² = −0.006675 in decimals, ρ = −0.98)"],"keystrokes":["0.5 × 20 + 0.5 × (−4) = 8; 0.5 × 8 + 0.5 × 6 = 7; 0.5 × (−10) + 0.5 × 14 = 2","0.25 × 8 + 0.5 × 7 + 0.25 × 2 = 6","0.25 × 4 + 0.5 × 1 + 0.25 × 16 = 5.5, 2nd √x = 2.35"],"errors":[{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Reported the variance (5.50) instead of its square root","value":5.5,"check":"0.25*(8-6)**2 + 0.50*(7-6)**2 + 0.25*(2-6)**2","explain":"5.50 is a variance in %²; the SD is its square root."},{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Weighted average of the two stocks' SDs (10.71% and 6.38%)","value":8.55,"check":"0.5*sqrt(0.25*(20-6.5)**2+0.5*(8-6.5)**2+0.25*(-10-6.5)**2) + 0.5*sqrt(0.25*(-4-5.5)**2+0.5*(6-5.5)**2+0.25*(14-5.5)**2)","explain":"The two stocks are almost perfectly negatively correlated (ρ ≈ −0.98), so averaging their SDs overstates the risk by a wide margin."},{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Gave the three states equal weight","value":2.62,"check":"sqrt(((8-17/3)**2 + (7-17/3)**2 + (2-17/3)**2)/3)","explain":"States have probabilities 0.25/0.50/0.25; equal weighting changes both the mean and the variance."}],"explanation":"When the data are given by state, the quickest route is to compute the portfolio's return in every state and then treat it as a single asset: E[R_P] = 6.00%, σ_P² = 5.50, σ_P = 2.35%. The formula route (σ's, cov, weights) gives the same answer; A and B hedge each other so risk falls from about 8.5% (the weighted average) to 2.35%.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s4-6, TB 13.2, SM 13-9"},{"id":"fin201-q12-448","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The correlation coefficient ρ_AB between the returns of stocks A and B is:","options":["cov(A,B) / σ_B², the slope of A's returns on B's returns","cov(A,B) / (σ_A σ_B), a unit-free number between −1 and +1","cov(A,B) × σ_A × σ_B, a number in squared percent","σ_A σ_B / cov(A,B), which is always above 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ρ_AB = cov(A,B)/(σ_A σ_B) standardizes the covariance by the two standard deviations, so it has no units and lies in [−1, +1]. Dividing by one variance gives a regression slope (beta-type measure), not a correlation.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27, S7 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-449","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which pair of stocks is most likely to have the highest correlation of returns?","options":["Two Canadian chartered banks","A Calgary oil producer and a Vancouver software company","A Canadian bank and a Japanese automaker","A gold miner and a regulated electric utility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stocks in the same industry and the same country respond to the same news (interest rates, regulation, the Canadian economy), so their returns move together and ρ is high and positive. Different industries or different countries have lower correlations, and lower correlation means a bigger diversification benefit.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q12-450","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A scatter plot puts Stock A's yearly returns on the horizontal axis and Stock B's on the vertical axis. The points sit tightly around a straight line sloping downward from left to right. The correlation is closest to:","options":["0","+0.9","−0.1","−0.9"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A downward-sloping tight cloud means that when A is high B is low: strong negative correlation, near −1. A formless blob is near 0, and an upward-sloping tight line is near +1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s28, S7 s8-10"},{"id":"fin201-q12-451","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts covariance and correlation?","options":["Covariance is bounded by ±1, whereas correlation is measured in squared percent","Covariance and correlation always have opposite signs for the same pair of stocks","Correlation measures the average return of two stocks, covariance measures their risk","Correlation is covariance scaled by σ_A σ_B: unit-free and bounded by ±1, unlike covariance"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Both share the same sign (the sign of the co-movement). Dividing the covariance by σ_A σ_B removes the units and caps the result at ±1 so strengths are comparable across pairs; the raw covariance grows with the stocks' volatility.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27, S7 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-452","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following could NOT be the correlation coefficient between two stocks?","options":["−0.85","+1.20","0.00","+0.62"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Correlation is always between −1 and +1, so +1.20 is impossible; it signals an arithmetic or units error, for example mixing percent and decimals.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-453","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do we divide the covariance by σ_A σ_B to get the correlation?","options":["It converts the covariance from percent into dollars","It makes the covariance equal to the portfolio's expected return","It removes the units, so ρ shows only the direction and strength of co-movement","It ensures that the correlation always comes out positive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A covariance of 0.03 could be strong for two calm stocks or weak for two volatile ones. Dividing by σ_A σ_B standardizes the relationship so that +1, 0 and −1 mean the same thing for every pair.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-454","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student computes cov(A,B), σ_A and σ_B from a five-year sample, but divides all three sums by N = 5 instead of N − 1 = 4. What happens to the correlation?","options":["It becomes too high by a factor of 5/4","It is unchanged, because the same factor cancels","It becomes too low by a factor of 4/5","It is no longer bounded by ±1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each of cov, σ_A² and σ_B² is a sum divided by N or N − 1. In ρ = cov/(σ_Aσ_B) the denominators are the same factor 1/(N−1) in numerator and in √(σ_A² σ_B²), so it cancels. (Mixing N for one term and N − 1 for another would, however, bias ρ.)","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-455","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"The correlation between two Canadian bank stocks is estimated at 0.85. What does this imply for combining them in a portfolio?","options":["Some benefit remains because ρ < 1, but it is small since the stocks move together","There is no diversification benefit at all, since the correlation is positive","The portfolio will be riskless, because stocks that move together offset each other","The portfolio's expected return will be lower than either stock's"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Any ρ below 1 gives some benefit (σ_P lower than the weighted average of the σ's), but at 0.85 the cross-term is almost at its maximum so the gain is modest. The benefit comes at no cost in expected return because E[R_P] is a weighted average.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s30, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-456","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"cov(A,B) = −0.0150. Which interpretation is correct?","options":["The two stocks are perfectly negatively correlated","The stocks are uncorrelated because the number is close to zero","One of the stocks must have a negative expected return","The returns tend to move in opposite directions; strength needs ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The sign shows direction (negative: when A is above its mean B tends to be below its mean). The size depends on the stocks' volatilities, so −0.0150 could be anything from weak to perfect negative correlation until it is converted to ρ.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s5-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-457","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A student reports a correlation of 1.18 between two stocks. What is the best interpretation?","options":["The stocks are more than perfectly correlated, so diversification will increase risk","It is valid if the covariance is large enough","The value is impossible: ρ must lie in [−1, +1], so there is a units or arithmetic error to find","It indicates leveraged stocks with betas above 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A correlation outside [−1, +1] is always a mistake: typically a wrong denominator (for example dividing by one variance, or by σ_A σ_B in different units), a unit mix of percent and decimals, or an arithmetic slip. Recheck before using the number.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s7, L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-458","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Every return on Stock A is doubled (a 2× leveraged version, A' = 2A) while Stock B is unchanged. What happens to cov(A',B) and to ρ(A',B) compared with cov(A,B) and ρ(A,B)?","options":["Both double","The covariance doubles; the correlation is unchanged","The covariance is unchanged; the correlation doubles","Both are unchanged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"cov(2A, B) = 2 cov(A,B), but σ_(2A) = 2σ_A as well, so ρ = 2cov/(2σ_A σ_B) is unchanged. This is why correlation, not covariance, describes the pattern of co-movement independent of scale.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-459","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"Returns of two stocks over five years are given. The sample covariance is the sum of products of deviations from the means divided by:","options":["N = 5","N + 1 = 6","N − 1 = 4","The sum of the probabilities"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The professor uses the sample version (÷(N − 1)) for a short history such as five years of one stock, and the population version (÷N) only when the data are the whole population. Probabilities are used instead of a divisor when returns are given by state of the economy.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-460","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Returns of two stocks are listed for three states of the economy with probabilities 0.20, 0.50 and 0.30. How is cov(A,B) computed?","options":["Σ p_i (R_A,i − E[R_A])(R_B,i − E[R_B]), with no division by N − 1","Σ (R_A,i − E[R_A])(R_B,i − E[R_B]) / (N − 1) with N = 3","Σ p_i R_A,i R_B,i, without subtracting the means","Σ p_i (R_A,i − R_B,i)², the squared difference between the two stocks' returns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With probabilities given, each state's deviation product is weighted by its probability: cov = Σ p (R_A − E_A)(R_B − E_B). Dividing by N − 1 is for historical samples; skipping the deviations gives E[R_A R_B], which is not the covariance.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s5, FS p2"},{"id":"fin201-q12-461","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"If cov(A,B) = 0, then the correlation between A and B is 0.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B), so a zero numerator gives ρ = 0 (as long as both σ are positive). Uncorrelated stocks have no cross-term in the portfolio variance.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-462","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A correlation of −0.9 between two stocks indicates a stronger linear relationship than a correlation of +0.3.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Strength is the magnitude |ρ| (0.9 versus 0.3); the sign only gives the direction. −0.9 means the stocks move almost exactly opposite to each other, the best case for diversification.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-463","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A correlation of 0.5 between A and B means that B's return is always exactly half of A's return.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. A correlation of 0.5 describes a loose tendency to move together; it says nothing about a fixed proportion. Only |ρ| = 1 means an exact linear relationship.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-464","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Two stocks in the same industry and the same country tend to have a _____ correlation.","options":["low positive","negative","high positive","zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Common industry and country factors drive both stocks, so their returns rise and fall together; stocks in different industries or countries have lower correlations and offer more diversification.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s30"},{"id":"fin201-q12-465","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 24% and Stock B has σ = 15%; cov(A,B) = 0.0180 (decimals). What is the correlation between A and B?","options":["0.02","0.31","0.50","0.80"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B) = 0.0180/(0.24 × 0.15) = 0.0180/0.0360 = 0.50. Dividing by σ_A² (0.31) or σ_B² (0.80) gives a beta-type slope, and reporting 0.02 simply quotes the covariance as if it were ρ.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-466","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"In three equally likely states of the economy, Stock X returns 15%, 6% and −3%, and Stock Y returns 4%, 10% and 10%. What is cov(X,Y), with returns as decimals?","options":["−0.0054","−0.0027","−0.0018","0.0030"],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R_X] = 6%, E[R_Y] = 8%. Deviation products (decimals): 0.09 × (−0.04) = −0.0036, 0 × 0.02 = 0, (−0.09)(0.02) = −0.0018. With probability 1/3 each, cov = (−0.0036 + 0 − 0.0018)/3 = −0.0018. Dividing by N − 1 gives −0.0027, leaving the sum undivided gives −0.0054, and skipping the means (E[XY]) gives 0.0030.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-467","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 18% and Stock B has σ = 30%; their correlation is −0.5. What is cov(A,B), with returns as decimals?","options":["−0.1200","−0.0900","−0.0270","0.0270"],"answer":2,"explanation":"cov(A,B) = ρ σ_A σ_B = (−0.5)(0.18)(0.30) = −0.0270. The positive 0.0270 loses the sign, −0.0900 omits σ_B, and −0.1200 uses the average of the two σ's in place of their product.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27, S7 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-468","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Strong economy (probability 0.30): Stock A returns 18%, Stock B 6%. Moderate (0.50): A 9%, B 8%. Weak (0.20): A −6%, B 20%. What is the correlation between A and B?","answer":-0.95,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)))/(sqrt((0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2))*sqrt((0.3*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.2*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2)))","steps":[{"label":"Expected return E[R_A]","value":8.7,"check":"(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06))*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_B]","value":9.8,"check":"(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Standard deviation σ_A","value":8.32,"check":"sqrt((0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2))*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Standard deviation σ_B","value":5.17,"check":"sqrt((0.3*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.2*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2))*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Covariance cov(A,B), in decimals","value":-0.00409,"check":"(0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Correlation ρ_AB","value":-0.95,"check":"(0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)))/(sqrt((0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2))*sqrt((0.3*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.2*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2)))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R_A] = 0.3(18) + 0.5(9) + 0.2(−6) = 8.7%; E[R_B] = 0.3(6) + 0.5(8) + 0.2(20) = 9.8%","Deviations (A): +9.3, +0.3, −14.7; (B): −3.8, −1.8, +10.2 (in %)","σ_A² = 0.3(9.3)² + 0.5(0.3)² + 0.2(14.7)² = 69.21 → σ_A = 8.32%; σ_B² = 0.3(3.8)² + 0.5(1.8)² + 0.2(10.2)² = 26.76 → σ_B = 5.17%","cov = 0.3(9.3)(−3.8) + 0.5(0.3)(−1.8) + 0.2(−14.7)(10.2) = −40.86 %² = −0.004086","ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B) = −40.86/(8.319 × 5.173) = −0.95"],"keystrokes":["Use the statistics (2nd DATA) or compute by hand: 0.3 × 9.3 × (−3.8) = −10.602","0.5 × 0.3 × (−1.8) = −0.27","0.2 × (−14.7) × 10.2 = −29.988; sum = −40.86","√69.21 = 8.319; √26.76 = 5.173; −40.86 ÷ (8.319 × 5.173) = −0.95"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-probabilities","label":"Summed the deviation products without weighting by the probabilities","value":-0.97,"check":"((0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)))/sqrt(((0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2)*((0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2))","explain":"Each state's deviation product must be multiplied by its probability; an unweighted sum treats the states as equally likely."},{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Reported the covariance (in decimals) as the correlation","value":-0.0041,"check":"(0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)))","explain":"The covariance still carries the units of σ_A σ_B; divide by σ_A σ_B to get ρ."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Divided the covariance in %² by σ's in decimals","value":-9494.5,"check":"((0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20)))*10000)/(sqrt((0.3*(0.18-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2+0.2*(-0.06-(0.3*0.18+0.5*0.09+0.2*(-0.06)))**2))*sqrt((0.3*(0.06-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2+0.2*(0.20-(0.3*0.06+0.5*0.08+0.2*0.20))**2)))","explain":"Mixing percent and decimal units gives a nonsense ρ far outside [−1, 1]."}],"explanation":"ρ_AB = −0.95: the two stocks move almost exactly opposite across the three states, so a portfolio of both would remove most of the risk. Always compute E first, then deviations, then probability-weighted squares and products; a ρ outside [−1, 1] means a units slip.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s4-6"},{"id":"fin201-q12-469","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In three equally likely states, Stock A returns 14%, 6% and −2%, while Stock B returns 3%, 7% and 11%. What is the correlation between A and B?","answer":-1.0,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(((0.14-0.06)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.06-0.06)*(0.07-0.07)+(-0.02-0.06)*(0.11-0.07))/3)/(sqrt(((0.14-0.06)**2+(0.06-0.06)**2+(-0.02-0.06)**2)/3)*sqrt(((0.03-0.07)**2+(0.07-0.07)**2+(0.11-0.07)**2)/3))","steps":[{"label":"Expected return E[R_A]","value":6.0,"check":"(0.14+0.06-0.02)/3*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Standard deviation σ_A","value":6.53,"check":"sqrt(((0.14-0.06)**2+(0.06-0.06)**2+(-0.02-0.06)**2)/3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Standard deviation σ_B","value":3.27,"check":"sqrt(((0.03-0.07)**2+(0.07-0.07)**2+(0.11-0.07)**2)/3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Covariance cov(A,B), in decimals","value":-0.00213,"check":"((0.14-0.06)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.06-0.06)*(0.07-0.07)+(-0.02-0.06)*(0.11-0.07))/3","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Correlation ρ_AB","value":-1.0,"check":"(((0.14-0.06)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.06-0.06)*(0.07-0.07)+(-0.02-0.06)*(0.11-0.07))/3)/(sqrt(((0.14-0.06)**2+(0.06-0.06)**2+(-0.02-0.06)**2)/3)*sqrt(((0.03-0.07)**2+(0.07-0.07)**2+(0.11-0.07)**2)/3))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R_A] = (14 + 6 − 2)/3 = 6%; E[R_B] = (3 + 7 + 11)/3 = 7%. Deviations: A = +8, 0, −8; B = −4, 0, +4","σ_A = √((64 + 0 + 64)/3) = 6.53%; σ_B = √((16 + 0 + 16)/3) = 3.27%","cov = (8 × (−4) + 0 + (−8)(4))/3 = −64/3 = −21.33 %² = −0.002133","ρ = −21.33/(6.532 × 3.266) = −1.00: B is an exact decreasing straight-line function of A (B falls 0.5 for every 1 that A rises)"],"keystrokes":["Deviations: A = 8, 0, −8; B = −4, 0, 4","cov = (−32 + 0 − 32) ÷ 3 = −21.33","σ_A = 6.532, σ_B = 3.266; −21.33 ÷ 21.33 = −1.00"],"errors":[{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Reported the covariance (in decimals) as the correlation","value":-0.0021,"check":"((0.14-0.06)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.06-0.06)*(0.07-0.07)+(-0.02-0.06)*(0.11-0.07))/3","explain":"A covariance of −0.0021 is not a correlation; divide by σ_A σ_B."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Divided by σ_A² only (the slope of B on A)","value":-0.5,"check":"(((0.14-0.06)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.06-0.06)*(0.07-0.07)+(-0.02-0.06)*(0.11-0.07))/3)/(((0.14-0.06)**2+(0.06-0.06)**2+(-0.02-0.06)**2)/3)","explain":"Dividing by one variance gives a regression slope (−0.50), not the correlation, which divides by σ_A σ_B."},{"id":"forgot-minus-mean","label":"Used Σ p R_A R_B instead of deviation products","value":0.97,"check":"(((0.14*0.03+0.06*0.07-0.02*0.11)/3)/(sqrt(((0.14-0.06)**2+(0.06-0.06)**2+(-0.02-0.06)**2)/3)*sqrt(((0.03-0.07)**2+(0.07-0.07)**2+(0.11-0.07)**2)/3)))","explain":"Covariance is built from deviations from the means; E[R_A R_B] alone is not the covariance."}],"explanation":"ρ = −1.00 exactly: B is a perfect decreasing straight-line function of A. A perfectly negatively correlated pair is the strongest diversifier: the right weights make σ_P zero.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s5-10, L06 s26"},{"id":"fin201-q12-470","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":["fin201-c12-diversification-benefit"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Over five years, Stock A returned 12%, −4%, 18%, 6% and 8%, and Stock B returned 5%, 9%, 1%, 7% and 3% (same years, same order). Using sample statistics (divide by N − 1), what is the correlation between A and B?","answer":-0.9,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(((12-8)*(5-5)+(-4-8)*(9-5)+(18-8)*(1-5)+(6-8)*(7-5)+(8-8)*(3-5))/4)/(sqrt(((12-8)**2+(-4-8)**2+(18-8)**2+(6-8)**2+(8-8)**2)/4)*sqrt(((5-5)**2+(9-5)**2+(1-5)**2+(7-5)**2+(3-5)**2)/4))","steps":[{"label":"Sample covariance cov(A,B), in decimals","value":-0.0023,"check":"(((12-8)*(5-5)+(-4-8)*(9-5)+(18-8)*(1-5)+(6-8)*(7-5)+(8-8)*(3-5))/4)/10000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Sample standard deviation σ_A","value":8.12,"check":"sqrt(((12-8)**2+(-4-8)**2+(18-8)**2+(6-8)**2+(8-8)**2)/4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Sample standard deviation σ_B","value":3.16,"check":"sqrt(((5-5)**2+(9-5)**2+(1-5)**2+(7-5)**2+(3-5)**2)/4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Correlation ρ_AB","value":-0.9,"check":"(((12-8)*(5-5)+(-4-8)*(9-5)+(18-8)*(1-5)+(6-8)*(7-5)+(8-8)*(3-5))/4)/(sqrt(((12-8)**2+(-4-8)**2+(18-8)**2+(6-8)**2+(8-8)**2)/4)*sqrt(((5-5)**2+(9-5)**2+(1-5)**2+(7-5)**2+(3-5)**2)/4))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Means: A = 8.0%, B = 5.0%. Deviations: A = +4, −12, +10, −2, 0; B = 0, +4, −4, +2, −2","Sum of products = 0 − 48 − 40 − 4 + 0 = −92, so cov = −92/4 = −23 %² = −0.0023 in decimals","σ_A² = (16 + 144 + 100 + 4 + 0)/4 = 66 → σ_A = 8.124%; σ_B² = (0 + 16 + 16 + 4 + 4)/4 = 10 → σ_B = 3.162%","ρ = −23/(8.124 × 3.162) = −0.895, about −0.90"],"keystrokes":["2nd DATA, enter X and Y pairs, 2nd STAT (LIN mode): Sx = 8.124, Sy = 3.162, r = −0.895","or by hand: −92 ÷ 4 = −23; −23 ÷ (8.124 × 3.162) = −0.895"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided the covariance by N = 5 but the standard deviations by N − 1 = 4","value":-0.72,"check":"((((12-8)*(5-5)+(-4-8)*(9-5)+(18-8)*(1-5)+(6-8)*(7-5)+(8-8)*(3-5))/5))/(sqrt(((12-8)**2+(-4-8)**2+(18-8)**2+(6-8)**2+(8-8)**2)/4)*sqrt(((5-5)**2+(9-5)**2+(1-5)**2+(7-5)**2+(3-5)**2)/4))","explain":"All three quantities must use the same divisor; mixing N and N − 1 distorts ρ (here it shrinks it)."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Divided by σ_A² only (the slope of B on A)","value":-0.35,"check":"(((12-8)*(5-5)+(-4-8)*(9-5)+(18-8)*(1-5)+(6-8)*(7-5)+(8-8)*(3-5))/4)/((sqrt(((12-8)**2+(-4-8)**2+(18-8)**2+(6-8)**2+(8-8)**2)/4))**2)","explain":"Dividing by one variance gives a regression slope, not the correlation."},{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Reported the covariance in decimals as the correlation","value":-0.0023,"check":"(((12-8)*(5-5)+(-4-8)*(9-5)+(18-8)*(1-5)+(6-8)*(7-5)+(8-8)*(3-5))/4)/10000","explain":"−0.0023 is a covariance; ρ needs the division by σ_A σ_B."}],"explanation":"With sample data use N − 1 throughout (and the same factor for cov, σ_A and σ_B): ρ ≈ −0.90. A strongly negative correlation means these two stocks are excellent diversifiers.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, s27, SM 13-32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-471","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over five years, Stock X returned 8%, 2%, 14%, 6% and 10%, and Stock Y returned 9%, 3%, 12%, 6% and 5% (same years, same order). Compute the sample covariance of their returns, entering returns as decimals (for example 8% = 0.08).","answer":0.0013,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05},"check":"((0.08-0.08)*(0.09-0.07)+(0.02-0.08)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.14-0.08)*(0.12-0.07)+(0.06-0.08)*(0.06-0.07)+(0.10-0.08)*(0.05-0.07))/4","steps":[{"label":"Mean of X","value":8.0,"check":"(0.08+0.02+0.14+0.06+0.10)/5*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Mean of Y","value":7.0,"check":"(0.09+0.03+0.12+0.06+0.05)/5*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sum of deviation products","value":0.0052,"check":"(0.08-0.08)*(0.09-0.07)+(0.02-0.08)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.14-0.08)*(0.12-0.07)+(0.06-0.08)*(0.06-0.07)+(0.10-0.08)*(0.05-0.07)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Sample covariance cov(X,Y)","value":0.0013,"check":"((0.08-0.08)*(0.09-0.07)+(0.02-0.08)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.14-0.08)*(0.12-0.07)+(0.06-0.08)*(0.06-0.07)+(0.10-0.08)*(0.05-0.07))/4","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}}],"solution":["Means: X = 8%, Y = 7%. Deviations (decimals): X = 0, −0.06, +0.06, −0.02, +0.02; Y = +0.02, −0.04, +0.05, −0.01, −0.02","Products: 0 + 0.0024 + 0.0030 + 0.0002 − 0.0004 = 0.0052","Sample covariance = 0.0052/(5 − 1) = 0.0013"],"keystrokes":["Means: 8 and 7 (in %)","Deviation products in decimals: 0, 0.0024, 0.0030, 0.0002, −0.0004; sum = 0.0052","0.0052 ÷ 4 = 0.0013"],"errors":[{"id":"n-with-sample","label":"Divided by N = 5 instead of N − 1 = 4","value":0.00104,"check":"((0.08-0.08)*(0.09-0.07)+(0.02-0.08)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.14-0.08)*(0.12-0.07)+(0.06-0.08)*(0.06-0.07)+(0.10-0.08)*(0.05-0.07))/5","explain":"Five years of one stock's history is a sample: divide by N − 1."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Worked in percent units: 52 %² ÷ 4","value":13.0,"check":"((8-8)*(9-7)+(2-8)*(3-7)+(14-8)*(12-7)+(6-8)*(6-7)+(10-8)*(5-7))/4","explain":"The question asks for decimals; 13 %² equals 0.0013 in decimal form."},{"id":"omitted-division","label":"Did not divide the sum of products","value":0.0052,"check":"(0.08-0.08)*(0.09-0.07)+(0.02-0.08)*(0.03-0.07)+(0.14-0.08)*(0.12-0.07)+(0.06-0.08)*(0.06-0.07)+(0.10-0.08)*(0.05-0.07)","explain":"A covariance is an average of the deviation products, so divide by N − 1."}],"explanation":"The sample covariance is Σ(R_X − mean)(R_Y − mean)/(N − 1) = 0.0052/4 = 0.0013 in decimals (13 in %² units). It is positive because the two stocks tended to be above or below their averages in the same years.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s13, s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-472","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 20% and Stock B has σ = 25%, and the correlation between them is 0.4. What is the covariance of their returns, as a decimal?","answer":0.02,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05},"check":"0.4*0.20*0.25","steps":[{"label":"Covariance cov(A,B) = ρ σ_A σ_B","value":0.02,"check":"0.4*0.20*0.25","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}}],"solution":["cov(A,B) = ρ_AB σ_A σ_B = 0.4 × 0.20 × 0.25 = 0.0200 (using decimals)"],"keystrokes":["0.4 × 0.2 × 0.25 = 0.02"],"errors":[{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Multiplied the percentages directly (20 × 25 × 0.4)","value":200.0,"check":"0.4*20*25","explain":"σ must be written as decimals (0.20, 0.25); 200 is the covariance in %² units."},{"id":"inverted-formula","label":"Divided ρ by σ_A σ_B","value":8.0,"check":"0.4/(0.20*0.25)","explain":"ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B), so cov = ρ σ_A σ_B, not ρ ÷ (σ_A σ_B)."},{"id":"forgot-sigma","label":"Forgot one σ: ρ × σ_A","value":0.08,"check":"0.4*0.20","explain":"The covariance needs both standard deviations."}],"explanation":"cov(A,B) = ρ σ_A σ_B = 0.0200. This is the quantity you can drop straight into the covariance form of the portfolio variance: 2 w_A w_B cov(A,B).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-473","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 14% and Stock B has σ = 10%; cov(A,B) = 0.0105 (decimals). What is the correlation between A and B?","answer":0.75,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"0.0105/(0.14*0.10)","steps":[{"label":"Product σ_A σ_B (decimals)","value":0.014,"check":"0.14*0.10","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Correlation ρ_AB","value":0.75,"check":"0.0105/(0.14*0.10)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["σ_A σ_B = 0.14 × 0.10 = 0.0140","ρ_AB = cov/(σ_A σ_B) = 0.0105/0.0140 = 0.75"],"keystrokes":["0.14 × 0.10 = 0.014","0.0105 ÷ 0.014 = 0.75"],"errors":[{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Reported the covariance as the correlation","value":0.0105,"check":"0.0105","explain":"The covariance is not bounded by ±1 and keeps squared-return units; divide by σ_A σ_B."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Divided by σ_A² only","value":0.54,"check":"0.0105/(0.14**2)","explain":"That is the slope of B on A (a beta-type number), not the correlation."},{"id":"pct-decimal-mix","label":"Divided a decimal covariance by the σ product in percent (14 × 10)","value":7.5e-05,"check":"0.0105/(14*10)","explain":"Use decimals for both cov and σ (or percent for both)."}],"explanation":"ρ = 0.0105/(0.14 × 0.10) = 0.75: a strong positive correlation, so the pair offers only a modest diversification benefit.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s6, L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-474","concept":"fin201-c12-covariance-correlation","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 16%. The covariance of A and B is 0.0156 (decimals) and their correlation is 0.65. What is Stock B's standard deviation (% per annum)?","answer":15.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.0156/(0.65*0.16)*100","steps":[{"label":"Product σ_A σ_B = cov/ρ","value":0.024,"check":"0.0156/0.65","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Stock B's standard deviation σ_B","value":15.0,"check":"0.0156/(0.65*0.16)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B) rearranges to σ_A σ_B = cov/ρ = 0.0156/0.65 = 0.0240","σ_B = 0.0240/σ_A = 0.0240/0.16 = 0.15 = 15.00%"],"keystrokes":["0.0156 ÷ 0.65 = 0.024","0.024 ÷ 0.16 = 0.15 → 15.00%"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rho","label":"Ignored the correlation: cov ÷ σ_A","value":9.75,"check":"0.0156/0.16*100","explain":"cov = ρ σ_A σ_B, so ρ must be divided out as well."},{"id":"inverted-formula","label":"Multiplied by ρ instead of dividing","value":6.34,"check":"0.0156*0.65/0.16*100","explain":"ρ = cov/(σ_Aσ_B), so σ_B = cov/(ρ σ_A); ρ belongs in the denominator."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Divided by ρ σ_A² (variance of A)","value":93.75,"check":"0.0156/(0.65*0.16**2)*100","explain":"σ_A σ_B is a product of two standard deviations; only one is known, so divide by the known σ_A (not its square)."}],"explanation":"Rearranging ρ = cov/(σ_A σ_B): σ_B = cov/(ρ σ_A) = 0.0156/(0.65 × 0.16) = 0.15, or 15.00%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-475","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The diversification benefit of combining two stocks is best described as:","options":["the amount by which σ_P falls below the weighted average of the stocks' σ's, available whenever ρ < 1","the higher expected return earned by holding two stocks instead of one","the complete elimination of risk once at least ten stocks are held","the risk reduction that occurs only when the stocks are negatively correlated"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The benefit is the gap between w_A σ_A + w_B σ_B (the ρ = 1 risk) and the actual σ_P. It exists for every ρ below 1, not only negative ones, it costs no expected return, and it never removes systematic risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s18, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-476","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A 50/50 mix of two stocks with σ = 15% and σ = 19% has a portfolio standard deviation of 13.1%. The gap between 13.1% and the weighted average of 17.0% is an illustration of:","options":["a risk premium","domination","the diversification benefit","systematic risk"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The weighted average (17.0%) is what the portfolio would have if the stocks were perfectly correlated; the actual 13.1% is lower because ρ < 1. That shortfall is the diversification benefit.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q12-477","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"On a risk-return diagram (σ on the horizontal axis, E[R] on the vertical axis), the curve traced by all mixes of two stocks bows to the left of the straight line joining them. What does this tell you about ρ_AB?","options":["ρ_AB = 1: there is no diversification benefit","ρ_AB > 1: the portfolios are riskier than the stocks themselves","Nothing: the curve's shape depends only on the expected returns","ρ_AB < 1: the mixes are less risky than the weighted average of the two risks"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The straight line is the ρ = 1 case (σ_P is the weighted average of the σ's). Any curve bowed to the left means σ_P is below that line for the same expected return, so ρ < 1. The lower the correlation, the stronger the bow.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s32-33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-478","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly describes the set of two-stock portfolios on a risk-return diagram (σ on the horizontal axis)?","options":["ρ = 1: a curve bowed to the left; ρ = 0: a straight line; ρ = −1: a straight line","ρ = 1: a straight line; ρ = 0: a curve bowed to the left; ρ = −1: two straight segments touching σ_P = 0","ρ = 1: two straight segments touching σ_P = 0; ρ = 0: a straight line; ρ = −1: a straight line","The shape is the same for every ρ; only the length of the curve changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At ρ = 1 risk is linear in the weights, so the mixes lie on a straight line. At ρ = 0 the curve bows to the left. At ρ = −1 the σ_P formula is a perfect square |w_A σ_A − w_B σ_B|, giving two straight segments that touch σ_P = 0 at the hedging weights.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s32-33, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-479","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"In which of the following cases is there NO diversification benefit from mixing two stocks?","options":["ρ_AB = +0.95","ρ_AB = 0","ρ_AB = −0.30","ρ_AB = +1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A benefit exists whenever ρ < 1; it is just small when ρ is close to 1 (such as 0.95). Only perfect positive correlation makes σ_P exactly equal to the weighted average of the σ's.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-480","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-weights-return"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does diversification reduce risk without reducing expected return?","options":["Because diversified portfolios are always invested in the stock with the highest expected return","Because a lower correlation between the stocks raises the portfolio's expected return","Because E[R_P] is just a weighted average, while σ_P falls when the stocks do not move in lockstep","Because risk and return are unrelated for portfolios"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Return is linear in the weights (no correlation term) while risk is not. Lower correlation lowers σ_P but leaves E[R_P] untouched, so diversification is called the only 'free lunch' in finance (risk falls with no loss of expected return).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s19, s22-24"},{"id":"fin201-q12-481","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why can adding a riskier stock (higher σ) to a portfolio lower the portfolio's standard deviation?","options":["Because it cannot: adding a stock with a higher σ always raises σ_P","Because a low correlation lets its swings offset the rest of the portfolio's","Because a riskier stock always has a higher expected return that offsets the risk","Because the weights of the original stock increase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"For a small weight w in the new stock, the variance changes by roughly 2w times (ρ σ_new σ_old − σ_old²) plus an O(w²) term, so the change is negative when ρ < σ_old/σ_new. With σ_old = 10%, σ_new = 20% and ρ = 0.2 this holds, and σ_P falls.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s35, SM 13-7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-482","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"You hold a portfolio and want to add one more stock. Candidates X and Y have the same expected return and the same σ. The correlation of X with your portfolio is 0.9 and of Y is 0.1. Which should you add, and why?","options":["X, because a higher correlation means the stock behaves like what you know","Y, because its lower correlation gives a bigger fall in σ_P at the same expected return","Either, because stocks with equal E[R] and σ are interchangeable","X, because a stock that moves with the portfolio lowers its volatility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At equal E[R] and σ, the better partner is the one with the lower ρ: its cross-term is smaller, so σ_P is lower. The professor's rule is 'for your next few stocks you want low ρ'.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q12-483","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Weights and standard deviations of two stocks are fixed, and the correlation between them rises from 0.2 to 0.8. What happens to the diversification benefit (weighted-average σ minus σ_P)?","options":["It shrinks, because σ_P rises towards the unchanged weighted average","It grows, because a higher correlation means more co-movement","It is unchanged, because the weights and σ's are fixed","It becomes negative"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The weighted average of the σ's does not depend on ρ. σ_P increases with ρ, so the gap between them narrows; it reaches zero at ρ = 1. It never becomes negative for ρ ≤ 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-484","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"With many stocks to choose from, the efficient frontier lies to the left of every individual stock on the risk-return diagram. What does this tell you?","options":["Combinations achieve a lower σ for the same expected return than any single stock can","Individual stocks carry no risk of their own","The frontier is where expected returns are highest, whatever the risk","All stocks on the frontier are perfectly correlated with each other"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Individual stocks sit to the right of (or on) the frontier, so each is dominated by some combination: the same E[R] with lower σ. That is the diversification benefit seen across a universe of stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s37"},{"id":"fin201-q12-485","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"On the two-stock curve (σ_P on the horizontal axis, E[R_P] on the vertical axis) with ρ_AB low enough for the curve to bend back, a point on the lower branch below the minimum-variance portfolio is:","options":["efficient, because it has the highest weight in the riskier stock","the minimum-variance portfolio","the market portfolio","inefficient, because another mix has the same σ_P and a higher E[R_P]"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Points on the lower branch are dominated: moving up the curve to the other mix with the same σ_P raises E[R_P] at no extra risk. The lecture's point is that 'some weights are not efficient'.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s35"},{"id":"fin201-q12-486","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A two-stock portfolio can have a standard deviation lower than that of either stock held alone.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. If ρ is low enough, mixing the stocks produces a minimum-variance portfolio whose σ_P is below both σ_A and σ_B (for example, ρ = −0.5 with equal σ's gives σ_P = 0.5 σ). It cannot happen at ρ = 1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-CTQ7, L06 s33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-487","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"Diversification only reduces risk when the correlation between the stocks is negative.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Any ρ below +1 produces a diversification benefit (σ_P less than the weighted average of the σ's); negative correlation just makes it larger. At ρ = 0 and even at ρ = 0.5 risk still falls.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27"},{"id":"fin201-q12-488","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"A two-stock portfolio gets a diversification benefit whenever the correlation between the stocks is _____ one.","options":["less than","equal to","greater than","exactly zero or below"],"answer":0,"explanation":"σ_P equals the weighted average of the σ's only when ρ = 1, and it is lower for every ρ < 1. The benefit does not require ρ ≤ 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s27, s33"},{"id":"fin201-q12-489","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two stocks, fixed 50/50 weights and fixed σ's, are combined under different correlations. Which ordering of σ_P, from largest to smallest, is correct?","options":["ρ = −0.6 > ρ = 0 > ρ = +0.3 > ρ = +1","ρ = 0 > ρ = +0.3 > ρ = +1 > ρ = −0.6","ρ = +1 > ρ = +0.3 > ρ = 0 > ρ = −0.6","All equal, because the weights and σ's are the same"],"answer":2,"explanation":"σ_P rises with ρ because only the cross-term 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B changes. The largest value is at ρ = +1, then 0.3, 0, and the smallest at −0.6.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s32-33, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-490","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A 50/50 portfolio combines Stock A (σ = 20%) and Stock B (σ = 30%) with a correlation of 0.25. By how many percentage points is the portfolio's standard deviation below the weighted average of the two standard deviations?","options":["5.00 pp","6.97 pp","20.00 pp","25.00 pp"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weighted average = 0.5(20) + 0.5(30) = 25%. σ_P = √(100 + 225 + 75) = √400 = 20%. Benefit = 25 − 20 = 5.00 percentage points. 20.00 is σ_P itself, 25.00 is the weighted average, and 6.97 uses ρ = 0 instead of 0.25.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q12-491","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A portfolio holds 60% of Stock A (σ = 28%) and 40% of Stock B (σ = 18%) with a correlation of 0.3. What percentage of the weighted-average risk is removed by diversification?","options":["3.83%","15.98%","19.01%","84.02%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weighted average = 0.6(28) + 0.4(18) = 24.0%. σ_P = √(282.24 + 51.84 + 72.576) = √406.656 = 20.17%. Risk removed = (24.0 − 20.17)/24.0 = 15.98%. The alternatives give the share remaining (84.02%), the reduction in percentage points (3.83) and the reduction relative to σ_P (19.01%).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s18, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-492","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock C has σ = 26% and Stock D has σ = 14%; their correlation is 0.1. A portfolio holds 45% in C and 55% in D. How many percentage points below the weighted average of the two standard deviations is the portfolio's standard deviation?","answer":4.76,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(0.45*26+0.55*14) - sqrt(0.45**2*26**2 + 0.55**2*14**2 + 2*0.1*0.45*26*0.55*14)","steps":[{"label":"Weighted average of the SDs","value":19.4,"check":"0.45*26+0.55*14","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio SD σ_P","value":14.64,"check":"sqrt(0.45**2*26**2 + 0.55**2*14**2 + 2*0.1*0.45*26*0.55*14)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Diversification benefit (percentage points)","value":4.76,"check":"(0.45*26+0.55*14) - sqrt(0.45**2*26**2 + 0.55**2*14**2 + 2*0.1*0.45*26*0.55*14)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Weighted average = 0.45 × 26% + 0.55 × 14% = 19.4%","σ_P = √(0.2025 × 676 + 0.3025 × 196 + 2 × 0.1 × 0.45 × 26 × 0.55 × 14) = √(136.89 + 59.29 + 18.018) = √214.198 = 14.64%","Benefit = 19.4% − 14.64% = 4.76 percentage points"],"keystrokes":["0.45 × 26 + 0.55 × 14 = 19.4","0.2025 × 676 + 0.3025 × 196 + 2 × 0.1 × 0.45 × 26 × 0.55 × 14 = 214.198, 2nd √x = 14.636","19.4 − 14.636 = 4.76"],"errors":[{"id":"reported-sigma","label":"Reported σ_P itself instead of the gap","value":14.64,"check":"sqrt(0.45**2*26**2 + 0.55**2*14**2 + 2*0.1*0.45*26*0.55*14)","explain":"The question asks for the reduction relative to the weighted average, not the level of σ_P."},{"id":"reported-average","label":"Reported the weighted average","value":19.4,"check":"0.45*26+0.55*14","explain":"That is the risk at ρ = 1; the benefit is how far σ_P falls below it."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Computed σ_P with ρ = 0 and took the gap","value":5.39,"check":"(0.45*26+0.55*14) - sqrt(0.45**2*26**2+0.55**2*14**2)","explain":"ρ = 0.1, not 0, so the cross-term must be included; ignoring it overstates the benefit."}],"explanation":"Diversification benefit = (w_C σ_C + w_D σ_D) − σ_P = 19.40% − 14.64% = 4.76 percentage points. The low ρ = 0.1 makes it large; at ρ = 1 it would be zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s18, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-493","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two stocks each have σ = 24% and are held 50/50. By how many percentage points does the portfolio's standard deviation fall if the correlation between them drops from +0.5 to −0.5?","answer":8.78,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*0.5*0.5*24*0.5*24) - sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*(-0.5)*0.5*24*0.5*24)","steps":[{"label":"σ_P when ρ = +0.5","value":20.78,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*0.5*0.5*24*0.5*24)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"σ_P when ρ = −0.5","value":12.0,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*(-0.5)*0.5*24*0.5*24)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Fall in σ_P (percentage points)","value":8.78,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*0.5*0.5*24*0.5*24) - sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*(-0.5)*0.5*24*0.5*24)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["ρ = +0.5: σ_P = √(0.25 × 576 + 0.25 × 576 + 2 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 24 × 0.5 × 24) = √(144 + 144 + 144) = √432 = 20.78%","ρ = −0.5: σ_P = √(144 + 144 − 144) = √144 = 12.00%","Fall = 20.78% − 12.00% = 8.78 percentage points"],"keystrokes":["√432 = 20.785","√144 = 12.00","20.785 − 12.00 = 8.78"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-of-rho","label":"Compared with ρ = 0 instead of −0.5","value":3.81,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*0.5*0.5*24*0.5*24) - sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2)","explain":"The question asks about ρ = −0.5, where the cross-term is negative (−72), not zero."},{"id":"reported-sigma","label":"Reported σ_P at ρ = −0.5","value":12.0,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*24**2+0.5**2*24**2+2*(-0.5)*0.5*24*0.5*24)","explain":"That is the level of risk at ρ = −0.5, not the fall from the ρ = +0.5 level."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Subtracted the variances (432 − 144) and scaled","value":2.88,"check":"(432-144)/100","explain":"Take the square root of each variance before subtracting."}],"explanation":"Moving ρ from +0.5 to −0.5 lowers σ_P from 20.78% to 12.00%, a fall of 8.78 percentage points. Each step down in ρ shrinks the cross-term (+144, 0, −144) and with it σ_P.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-494","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"You hold only Stock A (σ = 10%, E[R] = 6%). You move 20% of your money into the riskier Stock B (σ = 20%, E[R] = 11%). The correlation between A and B is 0.2. What is the new portfolio's standard deviation (% per annum)?","answer":9.63,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"sqrt(0.8**2*10**2+0.2**2*20**2+2*0.2*0.8*10*0.2*20)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio variance (%²)","value":92.8,"check":"0.8**2*10**2+0.2**2*20**2+2*0.2*0.8*10*0.2*20","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"New portfolio SD σ_P","value":9.63,"check":"sqrt(0.8**2*10**2+0.2**2*20**2+2*0.2*0.8*10*0.2*20)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Change in risk versus holding A alone (percentage points)","value":-0.37,"check":"sqrt(0.8**2*10**2+0.2**2*20**2+2*0.2*0.8*10*0.2*20)-10","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Variance = 0.64 × 100 + 0.04 × 400 + 2 × 0.2 × 0.8 × 10 × 0.2 × 20 = 64 + 16 + 12.8 = 92.8","σ_P = √92.8 = 9.63%, which is 0.37 percentage points below A's own 10%","Expected return rises too: 0.8 × 6% + 0.2 × 11% = 7.0% > 6.0%"],"keystrokes":["0.64 × 100 = 64; 0.04 × 400 = 16","2 × 0.2 × 0.8 × 10 × 0.2 × 20 = 12.8","64 + 16 + 12.8 = 92.8; 2nd √x = 9.63"],"errors":[{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Weighted average of the SDs (12.0%)","value":12.0,"check":"0.8*10+0.2*20","explain":"That is the ρ = 1 answer and would wrongly suggest that adding a riskier stock raises risk."},{"id":"no-correlation","label":"Dropped the cross-term (ρ = 0)","value":8.94,"check":"sqrt(0.8**2*10**2+0.2**2*20**2)","explain":"ρ = 0.2 adds 12.8 to the variance; ignoring it gives 8.94%, too low."},{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Used 20% in A and 80% in B","value":16.52,"check":"sqrt(0.2**2*10**2+0.8**2*20**2+2*0.2*0.2*10*0.8*20)","explain":"The 80% weight belongs to Stock A (the stock you already hold)."}],"explanation":"Even though B is twice as risky as A, a 20% slice lowers σ_P from 10.00% to 9.63% (and raises E[R_P] from 6% to 7%), because ρ = 0.2 is below σ_A/σ_B = 0.5. This is why holding only the lower-risk stock is inefficient.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s35, SM 13-11"},{"id":"fin201-q12-495","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-benefit","also":["fin201-c12-portfolio-sd"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Stock A (σ = 24%) and Stock B (σ = 16%) are perfectly negatively correlated (ρ = −1). What percentage of the portfolio should be in Stock A for the portfolio to have zero standard deviation?","answer":40.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"16/(24+16)*100","steps":[{"label":"Sum of the two SDs, σ_A + σ_B","value":40.0,"check":"24+16","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in A for σ_P = 0 (in %)","value":40.0,"check":"16/(24+16)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["With ρ = −1: σ_P = |w_A σ_A − w_B σ_B|, which is 0 when w_A σ_A = w_B σ_B","24 w_A = 16 (1 − w_A), so w_A = 16/(24 + 16) = 0.40","Check: 0.40 × 24 = 9.6 and 0.60 × 16 = 9.6, so σ_P = 0"],"keystrokes":["16 ÷ (24 + 16) = 0.40","× 100 = 40.00%"],"errors":[{"id":"swapped-weights","label":"Gave the weight to the other stock","value":60.0,"check":"24/(24+16)*100","explain":"The riskier stock needs the smaller weight so that the two weighted risks are equal."},{"id":"equal-weights-assumed","label":"Used equal weights","value":50.0,"check":"50","explain":"Equal dollars give 0.5 × 24 = 12 against 0.5 × 16 = 8, which does not cancel."},{"id":"ratio-not-share","label":"Used the ratio of the SDs","value":66.67,"check":"16/24*100","explain":"The weights must sum to 1, so w_A = σ_B/(σ_A + σ_B), not σ_B/σ_A."}],"explanation":"At ρ = −1 the risks cancel completely when w_A σ_A = w_B σ_B, giving w_A = σ_B/(σ_A + σ_B) = 40% (and 60% in B). This is the extreme diversification benefit: a riskless portfolio built from two risky stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-11, L06 s32"},{"id":"fin201-q12-496","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"When the number of securities in a portfolio is increased, what typically happens to the standard deviation of the portfolio returns?","options":["It decreases at an increasing rate","It increases at an increasing rate","It is unchanged","It decreases but at a decreasing rate","It increases but at a decreasing rate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adding securities averages out company-specific (diversifiable) risk, so the portfolio standard deviation falls; but each extra security removes less than the one before, and the fall levels off at the systematic (market) level σ_M. So σ_P decreases at a decreasing rate, not at an increasing rate and not all the way to zero.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-497","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Systematic risk is best defined as:","options":["risk from economy-wide factors that diversification cannot remove","risk particular to one company or industry that disappears in a large portfolio","the standard deviation of a single stock's returns","the risk of holding fewer than ten securities"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Systematic (market, non-diversifiable) risk comes from economy-wide factors such as interest rates, inflation and GDP growth. Company-specific risk is the diversifiable part. Total σ of a stock includes both.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46, s49"},{"id":"fin201-q12-498","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Equally weighted portfolios of randomly chosen Canadian stocks have an average standard deviation of 44% with 1 stock, 24% with 10, 20% with 100 and 19% with 1,000. The 19% or so that remains however many stocks are added is:","options":["unsystematic (company-specific) risk","systematic (market) risk","diversifiable risk","the risk-free rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Diversification strips out company-specific risk, so the floor that remains is the market's own volatility σ_M: non-diversifiable, systematic risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47, TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-499","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a source of systematic risk?","options":["A strike shuts down one company's only plant","A mining firm's chief executive resigns unexpectedly","The Bank of Canada unexpectedly raises its policy interest rate","A single restaurant chain recalls a contaminated product"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A policy-rate change affects the discount rates, borrowing costs and profits of almost every firm, so it cannot be diversified away. The other three events hit one firm only and tend to wash out in a diversified portfolio.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-CTQ3, L06 s49"},{"id":"fin201-q12-500","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing of risk terms is correct?","options":["Company-specific = non-diversifiable = systematic; market = diversifiable = non-systematic","Diversifiable = systematic; non-diversifiable = unsystematic","Company-specific = diversifiable = non-systematic; market = non-diversifiable = systematic","All of these terms are different names for total risk"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The professor's terminology: company-specific = diversifiable = non-systematic; market = non-diversifiable (undiversifiable) = systematic. Total risk = systematic + unsystematic.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s49"},{"id":"fin201-q12-501","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT another name for systematic risk?","options":["Market risk","Non-diversifiable risk","Undiversifiable risk","Company-specific risk"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Systematic risk is also called market risk, non-diversifiable and undiversifiable risk. Company-specific risk is its opposite: the diversifiable, non-systematic part.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s49"},{"id":"fin201-q12-502","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can't holding more and more stocks eliminate systematic risk?","options":["Because large portfolios have higher expected returns than small ones","Because systematic factors move almost all stocks the same way at once, so nothing offsets","Because systematic risk only affects the stocks that are not in the portfolio","Because adding stocks makes the correlation between them rise to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Company-specific events offset because good news at one firm is unrelated to bad news at another. Economy-wide events (rates, recession) move nearly all firms together, so no number of stocks cancels them: σ_P levels off at the market volatility σ_M.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46, TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-503","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the portfolio standard deviation fall at a decreasing rate as the number of stocks grows?","options":["Because each added stock removes less of the specific risk that is left","Because later stocks have lower expected returns","Because each added stock has a larger weight than the previous one","Because the standard deviation falls at a constant rate until it reaches zero"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The second stock removes a large share of the diversifiable risk; by the tenth or thirtieth most of it is already gone, so extra stocks help little. What remains is the market volatility floor. In the textbook data, 10 stocks capture most of the benefit and 30 almost all of it.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47, TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-504","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Average standard deviations of equally weighted portfolios: 1 stock 44%, 2 stocks 33%, 5 stocks 27%, 10 stocks 24%, 30 stocks 21%, 100 stocks 20%. What does this pattern show?","options":["Most of the reduction comes from the first few stocks; about 20% remains","Risk falls by about the same amount each time one more stock is added","Beyond 30 stocks the portfolio's risk starts to rise again","With 100 stocks all of the risk has been eliminated"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The drops shrink: 11 points from 1 to 2 stocks, but about 1 point from 30 to 100. The floor near 20% is the systematic risk that no number of stocks removes.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5, L06 s47"},{"id":"fin201-q12-505","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"For a portfolio of randomly chosen stocks, as the number of stocks N becomes very large the standard deviation of the portfolio approaches:","options":["zero","the average standard deviation of the individual stocks","σ_M, the volatility of the market (the systematic level)","the risk-free rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unsystematic risk washes out, leaving the systematic component: σ_P tends to σ_M, not to zero and not to the average stock's σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47"},{"id":"fin201-q12-506","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"By holding enough stocks, an investor can reduce the standard deviation of the portfolio's return to zero.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Diversification removes only the company-specific part; the market-wide (systematic) risk remains, so σ_P bottoms out near the market volatility σ_M (about 19-20% in the textbook data).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5, L06 s46"},{"id":"fin201-q12-507","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"In a well-diversified portfolio, essentially all of the remaining risk is systematic.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. A well-diversified portfolio has almost no unsystematic risk left, so its total risk is essentially systematic; this is why β, not σ, becomes the relevant risk measure for an added stock in such a portfolio.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5, L06 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q12-508","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"An investor holds all her savings in one Calgary oil producer. Which step reduces her total risk the most without necessarily lowering her expected return?","options":["Buying a second Alberta oil producer with the same σ","Moving all of the money into the stock with the highest σ","Waiting for the oil price to rise before diversifying","Spreading the money over many stocks in different industries and countries"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Low-correlation holdings reduce company- and industry-specific risk. A second oil producer is highly correlated with the first, so it adds little; this follows the professor's rule: after the first stock, look for low ρ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59, s30"},{"id":"fin201-q12-509","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"As more stocks are added, the portfolio standard deviation falls at a _____ rate towards the systematic level.","options":["increasing","decreasing","constant","negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fall is steep at first, then flattens: each additional stock removes less risk than the one before.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s47, PRAC q7"},{"id":"fin201-q12-510","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which ordering of typical standard deviations, from largest to smallest, is correct for equally weighted portfolios of randomly chosen stocks?","options":["One stock > 5 stocks > 30 stocks > broad market index","Broad market index > 30 stocks > 5 stocks > one stock","30 stocks > 5 stocks > one stock > broad market index","All are about equal because diversification cannot reduce σ"],"answer":0,"explanation":"σ falls as stocks are added and bottoms out at the market's volatility (the broad index is the limiting, fully diversified portfolio). A single stock carries all of its company-specific risk and has the largest σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s47, TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-511","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"With two stocks at ρ = −1 the portfolio risk can be driven to zero, yet no large portfolio of real stocks reaches zero. Why?","options":["Real stocks all have the same expected return","Large portfolios always have weights larger than 1","The formula σ_P = w σ stops applying beyond two stocks","Real stocks share economy-wide factors, so a systematic floor remains"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ρ = −1 is an extreme two-stock case that real stocks never reach: nearly all stocks rise and fall with the market, so their correlations are positive on average. The economy-wide (systematic) part of their risk does not cancel, and σ_P levels off near the market's volatility σ_M.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s32, s46"},{"id":"fin201-q12-512","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A portfolio of 25 randomly chosen TSX stocks has σ = 20.3%, while the broad TSX index has σ = 18.9%. What does the 1.4-point gap suggest?","options":["The 25-stock portfolio must already lie on the efficient frontier","Adding another 25 stocks would roughly halve the portfolio's σ","Little more risk can be removed: what remains of the 25-stock risk is mostly systematic","The index must contain more unsystematic risk than the portfolio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The portfolio is already within 1.4 points of the market's own volatility, so the only risk left to remove is the small unsystematic slice. Doubling the number of stocks would shave off at most a fraction of that 1.4 points.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5, L06 s47"},{"id":"fin201-q12-513","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A single randomly chosen stock has a standard deviation of 48.0%, while an equally weighted portfolio of 10 such stocks has a standard deviation of 26.4%. What percentage of the single stock's standard deviation is removed by holding the 10-stock portfolio?","options":["26.40","45.00","55.00","181.82"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reduction = (48.0 − 26.4)/48.0 = 21.6/48.0 = 45.00%. The 55.00% option is the share that remains, 26.40 is the level of σ_P rather than a reduction, and 181.82% inverts the ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-515","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Typical standard deviations of equally weighted portfolios of Canadian large caps are 40.0% for one stock, 22.0% for ten stocks, 18.4% for forty stocks, and 16.0% for the whole market. What percentage of the maximum possible risk reduction (from one stock down to the market level) is achieved by holding ten stocks?","answer":75.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(40-22)/(40-16)*100","steps":[{"label":"Maximum possible reduction (one stock to market)","value":24.0,"check":"40-16","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Reduction achieved by ten stocks","value":18.0,"check":"40-22","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Share of the possible reduction achieved","value":75.0,"check":"(40-22)/(40-16)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Total reduction available = 40.0 − 16.0 = 24.0 percentage points","Reduction with ten stocks = 40.0 − 22.0 = 18.0 points","Share achieved = 18.0/24.0 = 75.00%"],"keystrokes":["40 − 16 = 24","40 − 22 = 18","18 ÷ 24 × 100 = 75.00"],"errors":[{"id":"share-of-single","label":"Divided by the single stock's σ instead of the reducible amount","value":45.0,"check":"(40-22)/40*100","explain":"The market floor (16%) cannot be diversified away, so the benchmark is the 24-point reducible part, not all 40 points."},{"id":"remaining-share","label":"Gave the share still to be removed","value":25.0,"check":"(22-16)/(40-16)*100","explain":"That is the remaining part (25%); the question asks what has been achieved."},{"id":"level-not-share","label":"Divided the portfolio's σ by the single stock's σ","value":55.0,"check":"22/40*100","explain":"That is the ratio of risks (55%), not the share of the reducible risk achieved."}],"explanation":"Ten stocks remove 18 of the 24 points that diversification can ever remove: 75%. This is the textbook's point that 'by the time we have 10 securities, most of the effect is already realized'. The last 6 points need dozens more stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5, L06 s47"},{"id":"fin201-q12-516","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A portfolio's standard deviation is 49.0% with 1 stock, 37.0% with 2, 30.0% with 4 and 25.2% with 8. How many times larger is the reduction from adding the second stock than the average reduction per added stock when going from 4 to 8 stocks?","answer":10.0,"unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"(49-37)/((30-25.2)/4)","steps":[{"label":"Reduction from adding the 2nd stock (points)","value":12.0,"check":"49-37","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Average reduction per stock from 4 to 8 (points)","value":1.2,"check":"(30-25.2)/4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Ratio","value":10.0,"check":"(49-37)/((30-25.2)/4)","unit":"x","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Adding the 2nd stock: 49.0 − 37.0 = 12.0 points for one stock","From 4 to 8 stocks: (30.0 − 25.2)/4 = 1.2 points per added stock","Ratio = 12.0/1.2 = 10.00"],"keystrokes":["49 − 37 = 12","(30 − 25.2) ÷ 4 = 1.2","12 ÷ 1.2 = 10.00"],"errors":[{"id":"total-not-per-stock","label":"Used the whole 4-to-8 reduction (4.8) instead of the per-stock average","value":2.5,"check":"(49-37)/(30-25.2)","explain":"Four stocks were added between 4 and 8, so divide the 4.8-point fall by 4."},{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":0.1,"check":"((30-25.2)/4)/(49-37)","explain":"The question asks how many times larger the early gain is, not the reverse."},{"id":"wrong-interval","label":"Used the 2-to-4 average (3.5) as the comparison","value":3.43,"check":"(49-37)/((37-30)/2)","explain":"The comparison interval is 4 to 8 stocks."}],"explanation":"The second stock cuts σ by 12 points, but each of the stocks added between 4 and 8 cuts it by only 1.2 on average: ten times less. That is 'a decreasing rate' in numbers.","source":"generated","ref":"PRAC q7, TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q12-518","concept":"fin201-c12-diversification-limit","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the textbook's data, a randomly chosen single stock has an average σ of 49.24%, a 30-stock portfolio 20.87%, and a 1,000-stock portfolio 19.21%. What percentage of the total diversification achieved by 1,000 stocks is already achieved by 30 stocks?","answer":94.47,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(49.24-20.87)/(49.24-19.21)*100","steps":[{"label":"Total reduction with 1,000 stocks","value":30.03,"check":"49.24-19.21","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Reduction with 30 stocks","value":28.37,"check":"49.24-20.87","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Share achieved by 30 stocks","value":94.47,"check":"(49.24-20.87)/(49.24-19.21)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Total reduction (1 stock → 1,000 stocks) = 49.24 − 19.21 = 30.03 points","Reduction by 30 stocks = 49.24 − 20.87 = 28.37 points","Share = 28.37/30.03 = 94.47%"],"keystrokes":["49.24 − 19.21 = 30.03","49.24 − 20.87 = 28.37","28.37 ÷ 30.03 × 100 = 94.47"],"errors":[{"id":"share-of-single","label":"Divided the 30-stock reduction by the single stock's σ","value":57.62,"check":"(49.24-20.87)/49.24*100","explain":"That ignores that 19.21 points of σ can never be diversified away."},{"id":"level-not-share","label":"Divided the 30-stock σ by the single-stock σ","value":42.38,"check":"20.87/49.24*100","explain":"That is the ratio of risk levels (42%), not the share of the reduction achieved."},{"id":"wrong-base","label":"Compared the 30-stock σ with the 1,000-stock σ","value":92.05,"check":"19.21/20.87*100","explain":"That gives 92%, the ratio of the two floors, not the share of the diversification achieved."}],"explanation":"Thirty stocks already deliver 94.47% of everything diversification can do; the other 970 stocks add the last 5.53%. This is the 'decreasing rate' and 'limit at σ_M' pattern: 'by the time we get to 30 or so, there is little remaining benefit' (Ross).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5 (Table 13.7), L06 s47"}],"color":"#7A4A1E","short":"Risk & return","tag":"σ, ρ and diversification","province":"Cappadocia","numeral":"XII"},{"chapter":13,"title":"CML, Sharpe ratio, CAPM and the SML","topics":[{"id":"fin201-t13-1","order":1,"title":"Adding the risk-free asset","summary":"Once a risk-free asset exists, an investor can mix any risky portfolio with it. The mix lies on a straight capital allocation line whose slope is the Sharpe ratio, and the steepest such line, tangent to the efficient frontier, is the capital market line through the market portfolio M.","concepts":["fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","fin201-c13-market-portfolio"]},{"id":"fin201-t13-2","order":2,"title":"Systematic risk and beta","summary":"Total risk splits into a diversifiable (unsystematic) part and a non-diversifiable (systematic) part. Beta measures the systematic part as a regression slope against the market, and the beta of a portfolio is simply the weighted average of its betas, with a zero for the risk-free asset.","concepts":["fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","fin201-c13-beta","fin201-c13-portfolio-beta"]},{"id":"fin201-t13-3","order":3,"title":"CAPM and the SML","summary":"Only systematic risk is rewarded: the CAPM says expected return = risk-free rate + beta × market risk premium. The security market line draws this, alpha shows which assets are under- or overpriced, and the course ends by separating σ from β, building portfolios stage by stage, and linking the required return to a project's cost of capital.","concepts":["fin201-c13-capm","fin201-c13-sml-alpha","fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","fin201-c13-portfolio-building","fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link"]}],"concepts":[{"id":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","topic":"fin201-t13-1","term":"Risk-free asset and the capital allocation line (CAL)","definition":"The risk-free asset pays a certain return R_F with σ_F = 0. Mixing it with a risky portfolio P (weights w_F + w_P = 1) gives E[R] = w_P E[R_P] + w_F R_F and σ = w_P σ_P, so every mix lies on a straight line from R_F through P: the capital allocation line (CAL).","explain":"Because σ_F = 0, the variance and correlation terms for the risk-free asset drop out of the two-asset σ formula and only w_P σ_P is left: risk rises in direct proportion to the weight in P, and return is linear in the weights, hence a straight line. Lending (w_F > 0) puts you between R_F and P; borrowing at R_F (w_F < 0, so w_P > 1) puts you beyond P. The professor assumes the lending and borrowing rates are equal.","example":"R_F = 3%, P has E[R_P] = 11% and σ_P = 20%. With w_P = 0.6 and w_F = 0.4: E[R] = 0.6 × 11 + 0.4 × 3 = 7.8% and σ = 0.6 × 20 = 12%. Borrowing half your wealth (w_P = 1.5, w_F = −0.5): E[R] = 1.5 × 11 − 0.5 × 3 = 15% and σ = 1.5 × 20 = 30%.","contrast":"CAL: one risky portfolio P plus R_F, a straight line. Efficient frontier: a curve of risky assets only. CML: the special CAL tangent to the frontier (through M). SML: a different picture (E[R] against β, not σ). Two risky assets give a curved σ_P; one risky portfolio plus R_F gives a straight line.","trap":"Forgetting the risk-free weight in E[R] (using w_P E[R_P] alone); entering borrowing as a positive weight so the weights no longer sum to 1; trying to use the full two-asset σ formula with ρ. Dollars are not weights: divide by your own wealth, so $60,000 in P on $40,000 of wealth is w_P = 1.5.","mnemonic":"σ_F = 0 wipes out the correlation: σ is just w_P × σ_P.","formula":"E[R] = w_P E[R_P] + (1 − w_P) R_F;  σ = w_P σ_P  (w_F = 1 − w_P; w_F < 0 means borrowing)","given":true,"archetypes":["A29"],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"One CAL, four positions (R_F = 3%, P: E[R_P] = 11%, σ_P = 20%)","head":["w_P","w_F","E[R] (%)","σ (%)","Where on the CAL"],"rows":[["0.0","1.0","3.0","0.0","All in R_F (the intercept)"],["0.5","0.5","7.0","10.0","Lending: between R_F and P"],["1.0","0.0","11.0","20.0","All in P"],["1.5","−0.5","15.0","30.0","Borrowing: beyond P"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","topic":"fin201-t13-1","term":"Sharpe ratio and the capital market line (CML)","definition":"Sharpe ratio = tan φ = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P: the excess return (risk premium) per unit of total risk, equal to the slope of P's CAL. The best CAL is tangent to the efficient frontier at the market portfolio M and is the capital market line (CML): E[R_A] = R_F + [(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M] σ_A for any efficient portfolio A.","explain":"A steeper CAL dominates a flatter one: on the steeper line you can find a point with more return at the same σ. So the portfolio with the higher Sharpe ratio is better, even if its raw return is lower. Mixing a portfolio with R_F never changes its Sharpe ratio, you only slide along the same CAL. The CML turns the market's Sharpe ratio into a price list: the required E[R] of any efficient portfolio given its σ.","example":"R_F = 3%. Fund P: E[R] = 10%, σ = 14% gives Sharpe 7/14 = 0.50. Fund Q: E[R] = 12%, σ = 20% gives 9/20 = 0.45. P has the steeper CAL, so P wins despite Q's higher return. If M has E[R_M] = 9% and σ_M = 12% (slope 0.50), an efficient portfolio with σ = 18% should earn 3 + 0.50 × 18 = 12%.","contrast":"Sharpe divides by σ (total risk) and ranks whole portfolios; the SML's reward-to-risk divides by β (systematic risk) and prices individual assets. CML: E[R] against σ, efficient portfolios only. SML: E[R] against β, every asset and portfolio.","trap":"Putting β (or σ_M) in the denominator; forgetting to subtract R_F in the numerator; using the CML to price a single stock (it carries diversifiable risk, so it plots below the CML); choosing the fund with the higher return instead of the higher Sharpe ratio.","mnemonic":"Sharpe = slope = excess return per unit of σ.","formula":"Sharpe = tan φ = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P;  CML: E[R_A] = R_F + [(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M] σ_A","given":true,"archetypes":["A29"],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Three lines, three jobs","head":["Line","Axes (y against x)","Slope","Applies to"],"rows":[["CAL","E[R] against σ","Sharpe ratio of P: (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P","Mixes of one risky portfolio P with R_F"],["CML","E[R] against σ","(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M","Efficient portfolios only (the CAL tangent at M)"],["SML","E[R] against β","E[R_M] − R_F (market risk premium)","Every asset and portfolio"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","topic":"fin201-t13-1","term":"Market portfolio and the index proxy","definition":"The market portfolio M is the risky portfolio at the point where the CAL from R_F is tangent to the efficient frontier: it has the highest Sharpe ratio of any risky portfolio, and the tangent line is the CML. In practice we proxy M with a broad index portfolio (S&P 500 / SPDR trust; the S&P/TSX Composite in Canada). By definition β_M = 1.","explain":"To find M, start at R_F and rotate a straight line upward until it just touches the frontier: the single point of contact is M. In theory every investor holds M plus R_F and chooses only the weight w_F, according to risk tolerance. M is hard to find in advance (it depends on future expected returns and σ's, and the best past portfolio need not be the best future one), so we use a broad index as a stand-in.","example":"R_F = 2%. Three frontier portfolios: P1 has E[R] = 8%, σ = 10% (Sharpe 0.60); P2 has 10%, 14% (0.571); P3 has 13%, 20% (0.55). P1 has the steepest CAL of the three, so it is the best of these candidates to mix with R_F (M's own Sharpe ratio is at least 0.60). A timid and a bold investor would both hold M; the timid one adds T-bills, the bold one borrows.","contrast":"M is the maximum-Sharpe (tangency) portfolio. It is not the minimum-variance portfolio (lowest σ, far left of the frontier) and not the highest-expected-return portfolio (far right). An index fund is a proxy for M, not M itself.","trap":"'Tangent' is not 'highest return' or 'lowest σ'. The review question 'How do we find the market portfolio?' wants: draw the CAL from R_F tangent to the efficient frontier. When asked which candidate portfolio is best to combine with R_F, compute each Sharpe ratio and pick the highest. Remember the index is only a proxy.","mnemonic":"Touch once, tangent: that touch is M.","formula":"Sharpe_M = (E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M is the maximum of all risky portfolios;  β_M = 1","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","topic":"fin201-t13-2","term":"Systematic vs unsystematic risk","definition":"Total risk (measured by σ) = systematic risk + unsystematic risk. Systematic (market, non-diversifiable) risk affects all assets; unsystematic (company-specific, diversifiable, idiosyncratic) risk affects one asset or a small group. Diversification removes only the unsystematic part, so a well-diversified portfolio's σ falls to a limit of σ_M.","explain":"Systematic: surprises that hit almost every firm, such as GDP, inflation and interest rates. Unsystematic: a strike, lawsuit, fire, new product or CEO change at one company. In a big portfolio the good and bad company-specific surprises cancel, but a market-wide surprise moves everything the same way, so it stays. Only unexpected news is risk: news already expected is already in prices. Because unsystematic risk can be removed almost free, the market pays no premium for it (the systematic risk principle).","example":"In the textbook's classic study of randomly chosen NYSE stocks (Table 13.7), one stock has an average σ of about 49%; with 10 stocks σ falls to about 24%, with 100 to about 20% and with 1,000 to about 19%. That floor is systematic risk, roughly the σ of the market (the lecture's picture of σ_P falling to σ_M). A fire at one packaging plant is unsystematic; an unexpected Bank of Canada rate hike is systematic.","contrast":"Systematic = market = non-diversifiable. Unsystematic = company-specific = diversifiable = non-systematic = unique = idiosyncratic. Do not confuse either with total risk σ (the sum of both parts) or with β (the measure of the systematic part only).","trap":"Cue words: 'affects the whole economy / all firms' means systematic; 'a single company / small group' means unsystematic. Do not say diversification removes all risk, and do not say the market rewards total risk. Only the surprise part of news is risk, so a fully expected rate change does not move prices.","mnemonic":"System-wide = Systematic. Diversify away the Unique.","formula":"Total risk = systematic risk + unsystematic risk;  σ_P → σ_M as the number of stocks grows","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Systematic vs unsystematic risk","head":["","Systematic risk","Unsystematic risk"],"rows":[["Other names","Market risk; non-diversifiable (undiversifiable) risk","Company-specific; diversifiable; non-systematic; unique; idiosyncratic risk"],["Typical sources","GDP, inflation, interest rates: surprises that hit almost all assets","Strike, lawsuit, fire, new product, CEO change: surprises at one asset or a small group"],["Diversification","Cannot be removed; σ_P falls only to σ_M","Washes out as stocks are added"],["Rewarded?","Yes, through the risk premium (measured by β)","No: it can be removed almost free"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-beta","topic":"fin201-t13-2","term":"Beta: a measure of systematic risk","definition":"β_i = cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M² = ρ_iM σ_i/σ_M: the slope of the regression of the asset's returns on the market's returns, measuring systematic risk. β_M = 1 and β_F = 0. Unlike the correlation ρ, β is not limited to the range −1 to +1.","explain":"Beta says how much the stock moves, on average, for a 1% market move. In the regression R_i = α + β R_M + ε the slope is β. Divide by the market's variance σ_M², not by σ_M. Because β = ρ × (σ_i/σ_M), a stock can be highly correlated with the market and still have a small β if it swings much less than the market, or a β above 1 if it swings more. Providers publish different betas because they choose different market indexes, time windows, return frequencies and adjustments.","example":"cov(R_X, R_M) = 0.0300, σ_X = 30% and σ_M = 20%: β = 0.0300/0.20² = 0.0300/0.0400 = 0.75. Check with ρ: ρ = 0.0300/(0.30 × 0.20) = 0.50, and β = 0.50 × 30/20 = 0.75.","contrast":"β vs ρ: ρ (−1 to +1) measures how tightly two assets move together; β also scales for relative size, σ_i/σ_M. β vs σ: β is systematic risk only, σ is total risk. β vs α: slope versus intercept of the same regression.","trap":"Dividing the covariance by σ_M instead of σ_M² (the most common slip); putting σ_i in the denominator (inverting the σ ratio); reporting ρ as β; mixing percent and decimals when squaring σ_M (18% means 0.18² = 0.0324). A beta of 0 does not mean σ = 0.","mnemonic":"β = ρ × (stock σ ÷ market σ); divide by the market's VARIANCE.","formula":"β_i = cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M² = ρ_iM σ_i/σ_M","given":false,"archetypes":["A26"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"Beta cheat table","head":["Beta","Meaning","Typical example"],"rows":[["β = 1","Same systematic risk as the market","Market index portfolio (SPDR trust, S&P 500)"],["β = 0","No systematic risk (can still have σ)","Treasury bills (R_F); casino winnings"],["0 < β < 1","Defensive: less systematic risk than the market","Hygiene products and other defensive stocks"],["β > 1","Aggressive: more systematic risk than the market","High tech"],["β < 0","Moves opposite to the market (rare)","Short an index; gold stocks in a fear spike"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","topic":"fin201-t13-2","term":"Portfolio beta and target-beta portfolios","definition":"β_P = Σ w_i β_i: the weighted average of the asset betas, with β_F = 0 for the risk-free asset and β_M = 1. The weights, including the risk-free weight, must sum to 1; a negative risk-free weight means borrowing at R_F, which pushes β_P above the beta of the risky holdings.","explain":"Unlike portfolio σ, portfolio beta needs no correlations: it is a weighted average, like portfolio return. To build a target beta, put the known holdings in, solve for the unknown stock's weight, and let the risk-free asset (β = 0) absorb the rest: w_F = 1 − all other weights. If the target beta is higher than the betas you can hold, you must borrow (w_F < 0).","example":"A $60,000 portfolio: $20,000 in a utility (β = 0.60), $30,000 in a bank (β = 1.10) and $10,000 in T-bills. Weights 1/3, 1/2 and 1/6, so β_P = 0.3333 × 0.60 + 0.5 × 1.10 + 0.1667 × 0 = 0.20 + 0.55 = 0.75. To lift the beta to 1.5 with the bank stock alone you would need w = 1.5/1.10 = 1.364 and w_F = −0.364 (borrowing).","contrast":"β_P is linear in the weights; σ_P is not (it needs ρ). A risk-free holding has β = 0 but still has a weight. A long-only stock portfolio with no risk-free asset cannot have a beta below its lowest stock beta or above its highest.","trap":"Dropping the T-bill (or the borrowing) from the weights so they no longer sum to 1; using dollars instead of weights; entering borrowing as a positive weight; giving the T-bill a beta of 1; taking the simple average of the betas.","mnemonic":"Weights × betas; the risk-free asset is a zero, not a nobody.","formula":"β_P = Σ w_i β_i  (β_F = 0, β_M = 1, Σ w_i = 1 including w_F)","given":false,"archetypes":["A28"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c13-capm","topic":"fin201-t13-3","term":"Capital asset pricing model (CAPM)","definition":"CAPM: E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F), equivalently E[R_i] − R_F = β_i (E[R_M] − R_F): the stock's risk premium is its β times the market risk premium. Expected return = time value of money + (amount of systematic risk × price of systematic risk).","explain":"Three parts: R_F pays for waiting; E[R_M] − R_F is the market risk premium (the price of one unit of β); β is how many units you carry. Only systematic risk is priced, so σ never appears. Because E[R] is also the required return, it is the discount rate for an asset of that risk. You can rearrange for β, R_F or E[R_M].","example":"R_F = 4.5%, E[R_M] = 10%, β = 0.8 (the style of the sample exam). Market risk premium = 10 − 4.5 = 5.5%, so E[R] = 4.5 + 0.8 × 5.5 = 8.9%.","contrast":"Market return E[R_M] vs market risk premium E[R_M] − R_F (if the question gives the premium, do not subtract R_F again). CAPM vs SML: the same equation; the SML is its graph with β on the horizontal axis. CAPM vs CML: the CML uses σ and efficient portfolios only.","trap":"Writing R_F + β × E[R_M] (no subtraction of R_F); treating the market risk premium as the market return or the reverse; percent and decimal mix-ups; solving for R_F, which appears twice in the equation.","mnemonic":"Wait-pay + (risk amount × risk price).","formula":"E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F)","given":true,"archetypes":["A27"],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"list","title":"The three parts of the CAPM","items":["Time value of money: the risk-free rate R_F (the reward for waiting)","Amount of systematic risk: the asset's β","Price of systematic risk: the market risk premium E[R_M] − R_F (the reward per unit of β)"]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","topic":"fin201-t13-3","term":"Security market line, alpha and mispricing","definition":"The security market line (SML) plots E[R] against β: intercept R_F, slope E[R_M] − R_F, passing through the market at β = 1. Alpha α = the E[R] an asset offers − the E[R] CAPM requires for its β. Above the SML (α > 0) means undervalued; below (α < 0) means overvalued. In equilibrium every asset has the same reward-to-risk ratio (E[R_i] − R_F)/β_i.","explain":"An asset above the SML pays more than its β justifies: investors buy, its price rises, and its expected return falls back onto the line. Below the line the reverse happens. Equal reward-to-risk lets you solve for R_F or the market risk premium from two assets: set (E[R_a] − R_F)/β_a = (E[R_b] − R_F)/β_b and cross-multiply. A positive-NPV investment plots above the SML.","example":"R_F = 3% and market risk premium 6%. A stock with β = 1.2 offering 11%: CAPM says 3 + 1.2 × 6 = 10.2%, so α = 11 − 10.2 = +0.8%, above the SML, undervalued. Reward-to-risk: (11 − 3)/1.2 = 6.67% > 6%.","contrast":"Above the SML = undervalued (cheap, price will rise), not overvalued. Alpha is a gap in return; beta is risk. SML (β axis, all assets) vs CML (σ axis, efficient portfolios).","trap":"Alpha sign reversed; saying a stock above the SML will fall in price; comparing reward-to-risk using σ instead of β; algebra slips when solving for R_F (cross-multiply, collect the R_F terms).","mnemonic":"Above the line = a bargain: price up, return falls back to the line.","formula":"α = E[R_i] − [R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F)];  (E[R_i] − R_F)/β_i = E[R_M] − R_F = SML slope","given":false,"archetypes":["A27"],"weight":3,"figure":null},{"id":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","topic":"fin201-t13-3","term":"σ ≠ β: which risk measure to use when","definition":"σ measures total risk (systematic + unsystematic); β measures only systematic risk. The risk that earns a premium, and that matters to a well-diversified investor, is β; σ matters to someone holding a single stock and to the ranking of whole portfolios by Sharpe ratio.","explain":"The professor uses two measures of risk: σ for volatility and β for systematic risk. A stock can be very volatile yet have a low β (much of its risk is unsystematic and can be diversified away), so its expected return is low. Which stock is 'riskier' depends on who is asking: an investor with all her money in one stock cares about σ; a diversified investor cares about β.","example":"Stock A: σ = 40%, β = 0.5. Stock B: σ = 20%, β = 1.5. A has the greater total risk, so also more unsystematic risk; B has three times the systematic risk, so B has the higher risk premium and expected return under CAPM.","contrast":"σ vs β: total vs systematic. Sharpe (σ in the denominator, ranks portfolios) vs reward-to-risk (β in the denominator, prices assets). E[R]-σ diagram (CML) vs SML.","trap":"Ranking expected returns by σ; calling the high-σ stock 'riskier' for a diversified investor; plugging σ into the CAPM. 'Which has the higher expected return?' means look at β. 'Which has more total risk?' means look at σ.","mnemonic":"Alone, look at σ. In a crowd, look at β.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":3,"figure":{"type":"table","title":"σ vs β: which to use when","head":["Situation","Use","Why"],"rows":[["You hold one stock, or judge a stock on its own","σ","All of its risk, systematic and unsystematic, lands on you"],["Ranking whole portfolios mixed with R_F (CAL, Sharpe ratio)","σ","The Sharpe ratio and the CML are built on total risk"],["Adding a stock to an already well-diversified portfolio","β","Unsystematic risk washes out; only systematic risk changes your risk"],["Setting a stock's required or expected return (CAPM, SML)","β","The market pays only for systematic risk"],["Choosing the discount rate for a project","β","The required return depends on the project's systematic risk"]]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","topic":"fin201-t13-3","term":"Building a portfolio: σ, then ρ, then β","definition":"The professor's recipe: for your first stock choose low σ; for your next few stocks choose low ρ with what you already hold (diversification benefit); when adding to an already well-diversified portfolio choose low β and positive α.","explain":"Each stage uses the risk measure that matters at that stage. With one stock all its risk is yours, so look at σ. With a few stocks the biggest gain comes from risk that cancels, so look at ρ with your existing holdings. Once the portfolio is diversified, only systematic risk is left, so only β changes your risk; a positive α adds return without extra systematic risk.","example":"Maya owns no stocks, so she starts with a low-σ utility. Next she adds a Saskatchewan potash miner and a Vancouver software firm because their ρ with the utility is low. With 30 holdings she adds a new stock only if its β is low (to hold down systematic risk) and its α is positive.","contrast":"σ is for the first stock, ρ for the next few, β (with α) for a diversified portfolio. Do not choose the first stock by low β (a low-β stock can still be very volatile), and do not use ρ with the market when ρ among your own holdings is what matters.","trap":"Using σ when the portfolio is already diversified; using ρ at stage 3; ignoring α (a low β with a negative α hurts return); saying β matters for a one-stock investor.","mnemonic":"σ, ρ, β: the stage decides the measure.","formula":"","given":false,"archetypes":[],"weight":2,"figure":{"type":"list","title":"How to build a portfolio","items":["First stock: low σ (all of its risk is yours)","Next few stocks: low ρ with what you already hold (diversification benefit)","Additions to a well-diversified portfolio: low β and positive α (only systematic risk is left)"]}},{"id":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","topic":"fin201-t13-3","term":"Link to the cost of capital","definition":"From the investor's side E[R] is the required return; from the firm's side the same number is the cost of capital for an investment of that risk. CAPM gives it: k = R_F + β_project (E[R_M] − R_F). A project is worth doing when its expected return (the IRR, for conventional cash flows) exceeds k, i.e. when it plots above the SML (positive NPV).","explain":"All investments of similar risk should offer similar returns. A firm should discount a project at the return investors can earn elsewhere at the same β, not at the interest rate on the loan used to pay for it. On this exam the idea shows up as: compute the CAPM rate, then decide.","example":"R_F = 3.5% and market risk premium 6.2%. A project with β = 1.3 should be discounted at 3.5 + 1.3 × 6.2 = 11.56%. If its IRR is 13%, IRR > k: it plots above the SML, so accept (NPV > 0).","contrast":"Required return k (from CAPM, depends on the project's risk) vs expected return IRR (what the project promises). Project β vs the firm's overall β: use the project's own. Cost of capital vs the cost of the particular financing source.","trap":"Using the firm's average rate for a riskier project; comparing the IRR with R_F instead of the CAPM rate; thinking a positive-NPV project plots below the SML.","mnemonic":"Required return = what the SML pays for that β.","formula":"k = R_F + β_project (E[R_M] − R_F);  accept if IRR > k","given":true,"archetypes":["A27"],"weight":1,"figure":null}],"activities":[{"id":"fin201-a13-01","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","title":"Systematic or unsystematic?","prompt":"Sort each event by the type of risk it creates for a stock investor.","buckets":["Systematic (market) risk","Unsystematic (company-specific) risk"],"items":[{"text":"Bank of Canada surprises with a 0.75-point rate hike","bucket":0},{"text":"Canadian inflation comes in far above expectations","bucket":0},{"text":"A recession cuts consumer spending in every sector","bucket":0},{"text":"A global credit crunch hits all lenders and borrowers","bucket":0},{"text":"Ottawa unexpectedly changes the corporate tax rate for all firms","bucket":0},{"text":"A fire closes the only plant of one manufacturer","bucket":1},{"text":"The CEO of a single retailer resigns suddenly","bucket":1},{"text":"One airline's pilots go on strike","bucket":1},{"text":"A brewer's new product launch is a hit","bucket":1},{"text":"A pipeline company loses a lawsuit","bucket":1}],"explanation":"Surprises that hit nearly every firm (rates, inflation, GDP, tax policy) are systematic and survive diversification. Surprises specific to one company or a small group wash out in a diversified portfolio, so they are unsystematic."},{"id":"fin201-a13-02","type":"match","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","title":"Symbol and meaning","prompt":"Match each symbol with its meaning in the CML / CAPM framework.","pairs":[{"left":"β_i","right":"Systematic risk of asset i relative to the market (regression slope)"},{"left":"σ_M²","right":"Market variance: the denominator of beta"},{"left":"cov(R_i, R_M)","right":"How the asset's return moves with the market's, in squared-return units"},{"left":"ρ_iM","right":"Correlation with the market, always between −1 and +1"},{"left":"E[R_M] − R_F","right":"Market risk premium: the price of one unit of beta"},{"left":"w_F < 0","right":"Borrowing at the risk-free rate"},{"left":"tan φ","right":"Sharpe ratio: slope of the capital allocation line"},{"left":"α","right":"Return above or below what CAPM requires for the asset's beta"}],"explanation":"β scales the covariance by the market variance; ρ is the bounded cousin; the market risk premium is the SML slope; tan φ is the CAL slope in the σ diagram; α is the vertical distance from the SML."},{"id":"fin201-a13-03","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","title":"The three parts of the CAPM","prompt":"Put the three parts of E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F) in the order they appear in the formula.","items":["Time value of money: the risk-free rate R_F","Amount of systematic risk: the asset's β","Price of systematic risk: the market risk premium E[R_M] − R_F"],"explanation":"Expected return = constant (R_F) + systematic risk (β) × constant (market risk premium). Only the middle term is asset-specific."},{"id":"fin201-a13-04","type":"sort","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","title":"Beta cheat sort","prompt":"Sort each asset or portfolio by its beta.","buckets":["β = 0","0 < β < 1 (defensive)","β = 1 (market)","β > 1 (aggressive)"],"items":[{"text":"A 91-day Government of Canada T-bill","bucket":0},{"text":"Casino winnings, unrelated to the stock market","bucket":0},{"text":"A maker of hygiene products","bucket":1},{"text":"A portfolio with 50% in an index fund and 50% in T-bills","bucket":1},{"text":"An index fund tracking the S&P/TSX Composite","bucket":2},{"text":"The SPDR trust on the S&P 500","bucket":2},{"text":"A high-growth software company","bucket":3},{"text":"A portfolio of 150% in the index, borrowed at R_F","bucket":3},{"text":"A high-tech start-up with volatile earnings","bucket":3}],"explanation":"Index portfolios are the market (β = 1); T-bills and unrelated cash flows have β = 0; defensive stocks sit between 0 and 1; high tech and leverage push beta above 1. Mixing the index with T-bills scales beta by the weight in the index."},{"id":"fin201-a13-05","type":"order","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","title":"Build a target-beta portfolio","prompt":"Put the steps for building a portfolio with a target beta in order.","items":["Convert each known dollar holding into a weight (dollars ÷ total portfolio value)","Write β_P = Σ w β with β_F = 0 and set it equal to the target beta","Solve that equation for the unknown stock's weight","Set the risk-free weight equal to 1 minus all the other weights (negative means borrowing)","Convert the weights back into dollars and check that Σ w = 1 and β_P equals the target"],"explanation":"The unknown stock's weight comes from the beta equation; only then does the risk-free asset take whatever weight remains, which may be negative."}],"formulas":[{"id":"fin201-f13-cal-mix","name":"Mix of a risky portfolio and the risk-free asset (CAL)","formula":"E[R] = w_P E[R_P] + (1 − w_P) R_F;  σ = w_P σ_P","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c13-risk-free-cal"],"when":"Any combination of one risky portfolio P with the risk-free asset; w_P > 1 means borrowing at R_F.","trap":"Forgetting the (1 − w_P) R_F term, or letting the weights sum to more than 1 when borrowing."},{"id":"fin201-f13-sharpe","name":"Sharpe ratio","formula":"Sharpe = tan φ = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","fin201-c13-market-portfolio"],"when":"Compare portfolios or CALs; the best CAL (highest Sharpe ratio) is tangent to the frontier at M.","trap":"Uses σ, not β, in the denominator, and the excess return over R_F in the numerator."},{"id":"fin201-f13-cml","name":"Capital market line","formula":"E[R_A] = R_F + [(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M] σ_A","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","fin201-c13-market-portfolio"],"when":"Required return of an efficient portfolio with total risk σ_A, or the σ needed for a target return.","trap":"Applies to efficient portfolios only, never to a single stock."},{"id":"fin201-f13-beta-cov","name":"Beta from covariance","formula":"β_i = cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M²","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-beta"],"when":"Beta from a state table (cov = Σ p (R_i − E[R_i])(R_M − E[R_M])) or from a stated covariance.","trap":"The denominator is the market VARIANCE, not σ_M."},{"id":"fin201-f13-beta-rho","name":"Beta from correlation","formula":"β_i = ρ_iM σ_i/σ_M","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-beta"],"when":"Beta from the correlation and the two standard deviations.","trap":"σ_i on top, σ_M below; β is not bounded by ±1 like ρ."},{"id":"fin201-f13-regression","name":"Market-model regression","formula":"R_i = α + β_i R_M + ε","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-beta"],"when":"Interpret beta as the slope when stock returns are regressed on market returns.","trap":"Beta is the slope, alpha the intercept; both come from the same line."},{"id":"fin201-f13-portfolio-beta","name":"Portfolio beta","formula":"β_P = Σ w_i β_i  (β_F = 0, β_M = 1, Σ w_i = 1)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-portfolio-beta"],"when":"Beta of any portfolio, including a risk-free weight; solve for an unknown weight to hit a target beta.","trap":"Weights must include the risk-free (possibly negative) weight and add up to 1."},{"id":"fin201-f13-capm","name":"CAPM / SML","formula":"E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F)","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c13-capm","fin201-c13-sml-alpha"],"when":"Expected or required return of any asset from its beta; also the equation of the SML.","trap":"Subtract R_F from the market return first (or use the stated market risk premium directly)."},{"id":"fin201-f13-capm-solve","name":"CAPM rearranged for beta and the market return","formula":"β_i = (E[R_i] − R_F)/(E[R_M] − R_F);  E[R_M] = R_F + (E[R_i] − R_F)/β_i","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-capm"],"when":"Back out the beta or the market return when the other inputs are given.","trap":"A stated market risk premium is not E[R_M]; add R_F back to get the market return."},{"id":"fin201-f13-reward-to-risk","name":"Reward-to-risk ratio and SML slope","formula":"(E[R_i] − R_F)/β_i = E[R_M] − R_F  (all assets)","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-sml-alpha"],"when":"Test whether an asset is on the SML; set two assets equal to solve for R_F or the market risk premium.","trap":"A higher ratio than the market's means above the SML (undervalued)."},{"id":"fin201-f13-alpha","name":"Alpha","formula":"α = E[R_i] − [R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F)]","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-sml-alpha"],"when":"Mispricing: α > 0 plots above the SML (undervalued, price rises); α < 0 plots below it (overvalued).","trap":"Offered return minus required return, in that order."},{"id":"fin201-f13-total-risk","name":"Total risk decomposition","formula":"Total risk = systematic risk + unsystematic risk;  σ_P → σ_M as N grows","given":false,"concepts":["fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic"],"when":"Explain what diversification removes and what remains.","trap":"Diversification removes only the unsystematic part."},{"id":"fin201-f13-project-rate","name":"Project required return from CAPM","formula":"k = R_F + β_project (E[R_M] − R_F);  accept if IRR > k","given":true,"concepts":["fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","fin201-c13-capm"],"when":"Discount rate for a project of a given systematic risk, then compare with the project's IRR.","trap":"Use the project's own beta, not the firm's overall beta."}],"questions":[{"id":"fin201-q13-001","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the capital allocation line framework, which description fits the risk-free asset?","options":["A certain return R_F with σ = 0, so it adds no variance or covariance term","A certain return R_F, but with a beta of 1 because it is part of the market","An asset whose expected return is zero and whose σ is also zero","An asset paying R_F with a small σ that diversification can remove"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The risk-free asset pays R_F for certain, so σ_F = 0. That is why the w_F², σ_F and correlation terms vanish from the portfolio σ formula. Its beta is 0, not 1, and R_F itself is positive, not zero.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s38"},{"id":"fin201-q13-002","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cypress Credit Union keeps one diversified equity fund P (not claimed to be the tangent portfolio) and only changes how much of its money sits in 90-day Government of Canada T-bills. The straight line of all the E[R] and σ combinations it can reach is called the:","options":["capital market line (CML)","capital allocation line (CAL)","security market line (SML)","efficient frontier of risky assets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mixing one risky portfolio with the risk-free asset traces a straight capital allocation line. It would be the CML only if P were the tangent portfolio M. The SML plots E[R] against β, and the frontier is a curve of risky assets only.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q13-003","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tamarack Capital puts 70% of its money in a risky portfolio P (E[R_P] = 9.5%, σ_P = 16.0%) and 30% in T-bills yielding 2.5%. What is the expected return of the combined portfolio?","options":["6.65%","8.40%","6.00%","7.40%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"E[R] = w_P E[R_P] + w_F R_F = 0.70 × 9.5 + 0.30 × 2.5 = 6.65 + 0.75 = 7.40%. Dropping the risk-free term gives 6.65%; using 0.70 for both weights (they then add to 1.4) gives 8.40%; a simple average ignores the 70/30 split and gives 6.00%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q13-004","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Prairie Endowment has $2,000,000. It places $1,400,000 in a diversified equity portfolio P (E[R_P] = 10.5%, σ_P = 18.0%) and the rest in T-bills paying 3.2%. Find both weights and the σ of the combined portfolio, then give its expected return (% per annum).","answer":8.31,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.7*10.5+0.3*3.2","steps":[{"label":"Weight in P (w_P)","value":70.0,"check":"1400000/2000000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Weight in T-bills (w_F)","value":30.0,"check":"600000/2000000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"σ of the mix = w_P σ_P","value":12.6,"check":"0.7*18","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Expected return of the mix","value":8.31,"check":"0.7*10.5+0.3*3.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["w_P = 1,400,000/2,000,000 = 0.70 and w_F = 1 − 0.70 = 0.30","σ = w_P σ_P = 0.70 × 18.0% = 12.6% (σ_F = 0, so no correlation term)","E[R] = w_P E[R_P] + w_F R_F = 0.70 × 10.5% + 0.30 × 3.2% = 7.35% + 0.96% = 8.31%"],"keystrokes":["1,400,000 ÷ 2,000,000 = 0.70 (w_P); 1 − 0.70 = 0.30 (w_F)","0.70 × 18 = 12.6 (σ)","0.70 × 10.5 = 7.35; 0.30 × 3.2 = 0.96; add → 8.31"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Left out the T-bill term","value":7.35,"check":"0.7*10.5","explain":"The risk-free asset still earns R_F on its 30% weight: add w_F R_F."},{"id":"equal-weights","label":"Averaged the two returns","value":6.85,"check":"(10.5+3.2)/2","explain":"The weights come from the dollars (70/30), not 50/50."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Used dollars in millions as weights","value":16.62,"check":"1.4*10.5+0.6*3.2","explain":"1.4 and 0.6 add to 2.0. Divide each amount by total wealth to get weights that sum to 1."}],"explanation":"Weights come from dollars over total wealth; σ = w_P σ_P because σ_F = 0; E[R] is the weighted average of E[R_P] and R_F.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39-40"},{"id":"fin201-q13-005","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hanna has $40,000 of her own money. She borrows another $20,000 at the T-bill rate of 3.0% (borrowing and lending rates are equal) and invests all $60,000 in portfolio P with E[R_P] = 10.0% and σ_P = 15.0%. What is the σ of her position (% per annum)?","answer":22.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.5*15","steps":[{"label":"Weight in P (w_P)","value":150.0,"check":"60000/40000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Weight in the risk-free asset (w_F)","value":-50.0,"check":"(40000-60000)/40000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Expected return on her own money","value":13.5,"check":"1.5*10+(-0.5)*3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"σ of her position = w_P σ_P","value":22.5,"check":"1.5*15","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["w_P = 60,000/40,000 = 1.50 and w_F = 1 − 1.50 = −0.50 (borrowing)","E[R] = 1.5 × 10.0% + (−0.5) × 3.0% = 15.0% − 1.5% = 13.5%","σ = w_P σ_P = 1.50 × 15.0% = 22.5% (beyond P on the same CAL)"],"keystrokes":["60,000 ÷ 40,000 = 1.5 (w_P)","1 − 1.5 = −0.5 (w_F)","1.5 × 15 = 22.5"],"errors":[{"id":"ignored-borrowing","label":"Used σ_P without scaling for leverage","value":15.0,"check":"15","explain":"Investing 150% of her wealth in P scales σ by 1.5: σ = w_P σ_P."},{"id":"weights-swapped","label":"Used the risk-free weight as the weight on P","value":7.5,"check":"0.5*15","explain":"The weight on P is 1.50 (60,000/40,000), not 0.50."}],"explanation":"Leverage multiplies P's risk: with w_P = 1.5 the position sits 50% further out on the CAL, so σ = 1.5 × 15% = 22.5%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39-40, SM 13-19"},{"id":"fin201-q13-006","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rocky View Pension can mix a risky portfolio P (E[R_P] = 11.5%, σ_P = 20.0%) with T-bills at 2.5%. The board wants the overall σ to be 14.0%. What expected return (% per annum) does the mix earn?","answer":8.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.7*11.5+0.3*2.5","steps":[{"label":"Weight in P: w_P = σ_target/σ_P","value":70.0,"check":"14/20*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Weight in T-bills","value":30.0,"check":"(1-14/20)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Expected return of the mix","value":8.8,"check":"0.7*11.5+0.3*2.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Because σ = w_P σ_P, w_P = 14.0/20.0 = 0.70 and w_F = 0.30","E[R] = 0.70 × 11.5% + 0.30 × 2.5% = 8.05% + 0.75% = 8.80%"],"keystrokes":["14 ÷ 20 = 0.70 (w_P); 1 − 0.70 = 0.30","0.70 × 11.5 = 8.05; 0.30 × 2.5 = 0.75; add → 8.80"],"errors":[{"id":"inverted-weight","label":"Inverted the weight: w_P = σ_P/σ_target","value":15.36,"check":"20/14*11.5+(1-20/14)*2.5","explain":"A target σ below σ_P means w_P < 1; the weight is σ_target/σ_P, not the reverse."},{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Left out the T-bill term","value":8.05,"check":"0.7*11.5","explain":"Add w_F R_F = 0.30 × 2.5% for the T-bill portion."}],"explanation":"On a CAL σ is proportional to w_P, so w_P = σ_target/σ_P. The return follows from the weighted average.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s40, SM 13-19"},{"id":"fin201-q13-007","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is a capital allocation line (P mixed with the risk-free asset) a straight line?","options":["Because R_F and P are perfectly positively correlated, so the usual two-asset curve collapses to a line","Because investors can lend at R_F but cannot borrow at it, so the line simply stops at portfolio P","Because the efficient frontier of risky assets is itself nearly straight near the point of tangency with the line","Because σ_F = 0, the σ formula collapses to σ = w_P σ_P, so σ and E[R] both move in proportion to w_P"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The two-asset σ formula has w_F²σ_F², and a correlation term times σ_F. With σ_F = 0 only w_P²σ_P² is left, so σ = w_P σ_P. Correlation is irrelevant because it multiplies zero; it is not that R_F is 'perfectly correlated' with P.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s40"},{"id":"fin201-q13-008","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts a capital allocation line with the efficient frontier of risky stocks?","options":["The CAL is a straight line using R_F; the frontier is a curve of risky assets only","Both are straight lines, but the CAL is steeper because it includes R_F","The frontier contains the risk-free asset, while the CAL contains only stocks","The CAL is curved because of correlation, while the frontier is straight because of diversification"],"answer":0,"explanation":"R_F has σ = 0, so mixing it with P gives a straight line. Mixing risky stocks gives a curve whose bend depends on correlation. The risk-free asset belongs to the CAL, not to the frontier of stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s37-39"},{"id":"fin201-q13-009","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"A CAL built from a risky portfolio P and the risk-free asset has all of the following properties EXCEPT:","options":["it passes through the point σ = 0, E[R] = R_F","its slope is the beta of P","points beyond P require borrowing at R_F (w_F < 0)","σ at any point equals w_P σ_P"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slope of the CAL is the Sharpe ratio (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P = tan φ, not a beta. The other three statements are true: the intercept is R_F, w_F < 0 means borrowing, and σ = w_P σ_P.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39-42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-010","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Zainab likes portfolio P but wants a higher expected return than P offers, using only P and the risk-free asset (lending and borrowing rates equal). What should she do?","options":["Lend more at R_F so that w_F rises above its current value","Switch to a single stock with a higher β but the same σ as P","Borrow at R_F and put more than 100% of her money in P (w_F < 0)","Hold P alone, since no mix of P and R_F can beat P itself"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Points beyond P on its CAL have w_P > 1, which means borrowing at R_F. E[R] rises because the borrowed money earns E[R_P] − R_F > 0 on top of her own, though σ rises too. Lending moves her toward R_F, lowering return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q13-011","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"An investor holds w_F = −0.40 in the risk-free asset and w_P = 1.40 in portfolio P. Which interpretation is correct?","options":["She borrowed 40% of her wealth at R_F and holds 140% in P, so σ = 1.40 σ_P","She short-sold P and lent the proceeds at R_F, so her σ is 0.40 σ_P","She lent 40% of her wealth at R_F and holds 60% in P, so her σ is 0.60 σ_P","She holds 140% in P, which is possible only if R_F is higher than E[R_P]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A negative risk-free weight is borrowing. The weights still sum to 1 (1.40 − 0.40), and σ = w_P σ_P = 1.40 σ_P. Lending would be a positive w_F.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s39, SM 13-19"},{"id":"fin201-q13-012","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Because the risk-free asset has σ_F = 0, the standard deviation of any mix of P and R_F simplifies to σ = _____.","options":["w_P² σ_P²","w_P σ_P","w_P σ_P + w_F R_F","(w_P + w_F) σ_P"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In σ = √(w_F²σ_F² + w_P²σ_P² + 2ρ w_F σ_F w_P σ_P) the F terms are zero, leaving √(w_P²σ_P²) = w_P σ_P. The squared version is a variance; (w_P + w_F) σ_P would equal σ_P because the weights sum to 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s40"},{"id":"fin201-q13-013","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sofia has $50,000. She borrows $30,000 at the risk-free rate of 3.0% and invests the full $80,000 in portfolio P (E[R_P] = 10.4%, σ_P = 17.0%). What is the expected return on her own $50,000?","options":["16.64%","14.84%","18.44%","10.40%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"w_P = 80/50 = 1.6 and w_F = −0.6, so E[R] = 1.6 × 10.4% − 0.6 × 3.0% = 16.64% − 1.80% = 14.84%. Ignoring the interest on the loan gives 16.64%; entering the borrowing as +0.6 (weights then add to 2.2) gives 18.44%; treating her as if she held P alone gives 10.40%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-19, L06 s39"},{"id":"fin201-q13-014","concept":"fin201-c13-risk-free-cal","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"If lending and borrowing rates are equal, the CAL continues as the same straight line beyond portfolio P.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Beyond P the weight on the risk-free asset is negative (borrowing at R_F), and σ = w_P σ_P and E[R] stay linear in w_P. In reality the borrowing rate is higher, which would bend the line, but the professor assumes equal rates.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s38-39"},{"id":"fin201-q13-015","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Sharpe ratio of a portfolio P is:","options":["E[R_P]/σ_P, the raw return per unit of total risk","(E[R_P] − R_F)/β_P, the excess return per unit of systematic risk","(E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P, the excess return per unit of total risk","(E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P², the excess return per unit of variance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sharpe = tan φ = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P: a risk premium divided by σ. Leaving out R_F gives a ratio that is not the CAL slope; dividing by β is the SML's reward-to-risk; dividing by variance mixes units.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42, FS"},{"id":"fin201-q13-016","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"On a diagram with σ on the horizontal axis and E[R] on the vertical axis, the slope of the straight line from R_F to portfolio P (tan φ) is called the:","options":["Sharpe ratio of P","beta of P","market risk premium","correlation between P and the market"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The rise (E[R_P] − R_F) over the run σ_P is the Sharpe ratio. A beta would be the slope on a diagram with β on the horizontal axis (the SML).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-017","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Evergreen Growth Fund has an expected return of 10.4% per annum and σ = 15.2%; its beta is 1.25. Three-month T-bills yield 3.1%. What is the fund's Sharpe ratio?","answer":0.48,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(10.4-3.1)/15.2","steps":[{"label":"Excess return E[R_P] − R_F","value":7.3,"check":"10.4-3.1","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Sharpe ratio","value":0.48,"check":"(10.4-3.1)/15.2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Excess return = 10.4% − 3.1% = 7.3%","Sharpe = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P = 7.3/15.2 = 0.48"],"keystrokes":["10.4 − 3.1 = 7.3","÷ 15.2 = 0.4803 → 0.48"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Did not subtract R_F","value":0.68,"check":"10.4/15.2","explain":"The numerator is the excess return over the risk-free rate."},{"id":"sharpe-with-beta","label":"Divided by beta instead of σ","value":5.84,"check":"(10.4-3.1)/1.25","explain":"Sharpe uses total risk σ. Dividing by β gives the SML's reward-to-risk ratio, a different measure."},{"id":"no-square-root","label":"Divided by the variance","value":3.16,"check":"0.073/0.152**2","explain":"The Sharpe ratio divides the excess return by σ, not by the variance σ²."}],"explanation":"Sharpe ratio = excess return per unit of σ: (10.4 − 3.1)/15.2 = 0.48. The beta of 1.25 is not needed; dividing by it gives the SML's reward-to-risk ratio (5.84), not a Sharpe ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-018","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"T-bills yield 2.5%. Fund X has E[R] = 12.5% and σ = 21.0%. Fund Y has E[R] = 9.6% and σ = 12.0%. Either fund can be mixed with T-bills. What is the Sharpe ratio of the fund whose CAL is better?","answer":0.59,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(9.6-2.5)/12","steps":[{"label":"Sharpe ratio of X","value":0.48,"check":"(12.5-2.5)/21","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Sharpe ratio of Y (the steeper CAL)","value":0.59,"check":"(9.6-2.5)/12","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Sharpe X = (12.5 − 2.5)/21.0 = 10.0/21.0 = 0.476","Sharpe Y = (9.6 − 2.5)/12.0 = 7.1/12.0 = 0.592","Y has the higher Sharpe ratio, so its CAL is steeper: 0.59"],"keystrokes":["(12.5 − 2.5) ÷ 21 = 0.4762","(9.6 − 2.5) ÷ 12 = 0.5917 → 0.59 (the larger)"],"errors":[{"id":"picked-higher-return","label":"Reported the Sharpe ratio of the higher-return fund","value":0.48,"check":"(12.5-2.5)/21","explain":"The better CAL belongs to the fund with the higher Sharpe ratio, not the higher raw return."},{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Did not subtract R_F for Y","value":0.8,"check":"9.6/12","explain":"Subtract R_F: (9.6 − 2.5)/12, not 9.6/12."}],"explanation":"A steeper CAL dominates a flatter one, so rank by Sharpe ratio. Y earns less than X but more excess return per unit of σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s41-42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-019","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"T-bills yield 2.6%. The market portfolio has E[R_M] = 9.8% and σ_M = 16.0%. According to the capital market line, what expected return (% per annum) should an efficient portfolio with σ = 21.0% offer?","answer":12.05,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"2.6+(9.8-2.6)/16*21","steps":[{"label":"Slope of the CML = Sharpe ratio of M","value":0.45,"check":"(9.8-2.6)/16","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Risk premium of the efficient portfolio","value":9.45,"check":"(9.8-2.6)/16*21","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Expected return E[R_A]","value":12.05,"check":"2.6+(9.8-2.6)/16*21","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["Slope = (E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M = (9.8 − 2.6)/16.0 = 0.45","Risk premium = 0.45 × 21.0% = 9.45%","E[R_A] = R_F + 9.45% = 2.6% + 9.45% = 12.05%"],"keystrokes":["9.8 − 2.6 = 7.2; ÷ 16 = 0.45","0.45 × 21 = 9.45","+ 2.6 = 12.05"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-add-rf","label":"Stopped at the risk premium","value":9.45,"check":"(9.8-2.6)/16*21","explain":"The CML gives the excess return; add R_F back to get E[R_A]."},{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Scaled the market return, not its premium","value":12.86,"check":"21/16*9.8","explain":"Scale the premium: (σ_A/σ_M)(E[R_M] − R_F), then add R_F."}],"explanation":"CML: E[R_A] = R_F + [(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M] σ_A. It applies to efficient portfolios only.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43, SM 13 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q13-020","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Wetaskiwin Pension Plan can mix T-bills (R_F = 3.0%) with the market portfolio (E[R_M] = 10.0%, σ_M = 16.0%). It wants an expected return of 8.5% per annum. Using the CML, what σ (% per annum) will the plan bear?","answer":12.57,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(8.5-3)/((10-3)/16)","steps":[{"label":"Target risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":5.5,"check":"8.5-3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in M = target premium ÷ market premium","value":78.57,"check":"(8.5-3)/(10-3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Required σ = w_M × σ_M","value":12.57,"check":"(8.5-3)/(10-3)*16","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["CML: E[R] = R_F + [(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M] σ, so σ = σ_M × (E[R] − R_F)/(E[R_M] − R_F)","Target premium = 8.5% − 3.0% = 5.5%; market premium = 10.0% − 3.0% = 7.0%","w_M = 5.5/7.0 = 0.7857, so σ = 0.7857 × 16.0% = 12.57% (equivalently 5.5 ÷ CML slope 0.4375)"],"keystrokes":["8.5 − 3 = 5.5; ÷ (10 − 3) = 0.785714","× 16 = 12.5714 → 12.57"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Scaled the raw returns","value":13.6,"check":"8.5/10*16","explain":"Work with premiums: (8.5 − 3.0)/(10.0 − 3.0) of σ_M."},{"id":"slope-inverted","label":"Multiplied by the slope instead of dividing","value":2.41,"check":"(8.5-3)*((10-3)/16)","explain":"σ = premium ÷ slope. Multiplying gives a number with the wrong units."}],"explanation":"On the CML the premium is the slope times σ, so σ = premium ÷ slope. The plan holds 78.6% M and 21.4% T-bills.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43, SM 13-31"},{"id":"fin201-q13-021","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the capital market line (CML) from the security market line (SML)?","options":["The CML plots E[R] against β for all assets; the SML plots E[R] against σ for efficient portfolios only","The CML plots E[R] against σ for efficient portfolios only; the SML plots E[R] against β for every asset","Both plot E[R] against σ, but the SML starts at R_F while the CML starts at the origin","The CML has slope E[R_M] − R_F, while the SML has slope (E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CML: horizontal axis σ, slope = Sharpe ratio of M = (E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M, efficient portfolios only. SML: horizontal axis β, slope = market risk premium E[R_M] − R_F, all assets. Giving the CML the slope E[R_M] − R_F and the SML the slope (E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M swaps the two.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43, s56"},{"id":"fin201-q13-022","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"R_F = 3%. The market portfolio has E[R_M] = 10% and σ_M = 14%. A balanced fund has E[R] = 7% and σ = 12%. What follows?","options":["It lies on the CML, because its 7% return exceeds R_F and its σ is below the market's σ","It beats the market portfolio, because its σ of 12% is lower than the market's 14%","Its Sharpe ratio is 0.33 against the market's 0.50, so it plots below the CML (M plus T-bills earns 9% at σ = 12%)","It is undervalued, because it plots below the CML and so offers a high return for its risk"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sharpe_fund = (7 − 3)/12 = 0.33; Sharpe_M = (10 − 3)/14 = 0.50. At σ = 12% the CML gives 3 + 0.50 × 12 = 9%, so the fund is dominated. Below the CML means inefficient, not 'undervalued': that vocabulary belongs to the SML.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s41-43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-023","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about the Sharpe ratio is NOT correct?","options":["It equals the slope (tan φ) of the CAL through the portfolio","Its denominator is the portfolio's standard deviation","The portfolio with the highest Sharpe ratio has the best CAL","It measures the excess return earned per unit of beta"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Excess return per unit of β is the SML's reward-to-risk ratio. The Sharpe ratio divides the excess return by σ, equals tan φ, and ranks CALs: steeper is better.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-024","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why is the best CAL the one tangent to the efficient frontier?","options":["It touches the frontier at the lowest-σ portfolio, so it carries the least risk of any CAL","It has the steepest slope (highest Sharpe ratio) of any line from R_F that still touches an attainable portfolio","It is the only CAL that is a straight line, because it meets the frontier at just a single point","It has the highest intercept on the vertical axis, so it pays the most when σ = 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every CAL starts at R_F, so the intercept is the same. A steeper line gives more return for each unit of σ; any line steeper than the tangent one misses all attainable portfolios. Tangency is about the highest slope, not the lowest σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-025","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":["fin201-c13-capm","fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta"],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"R_F = 3%, E[R_M] = 9% and σ_M = 15%. Stock Q has σ_Q = 30% and β_Q = 0.8. An analyst plugs σ_Q into the CML and gets 15%. What is Stock Q's required (expected) return under the right model?","options":["7.80%","15.00%","4.80%","10.20%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A single stock carries diversifiable risk, so the CML (efficient portfolios only) does not apply; its reward depends on β through the CAPM: 3% + 0.8 × (9% − 3%) = 7.80%. The CML gives 15.00%; forgetting to add R_F gives 4.80%; using β × E[R_M] instead of β × MRP gives 10.20%.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43, s55, SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-026","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"The CML gives the expected return of an efficient portfolio A as E[R_A] = R_F + _____ × σ_A.","options":["(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M","(E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M²","E[R_M]/σ_M","(E[R_M] − R_F) × β_A"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The CML slope is the Sharpe ratio of the market portfolio, (E[R_M] − R_F)/σ_M. Dividing by σ_M² mixes units; omitting R_F uses the raw market return instead of its premium; multiplying by β_A turns it into the SML.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43, SM 13 App A1"},{"id":"fin201-q13-027","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Banff Balanced Fund has E[R] = 8.6% and σ = 11.0%; its beta is 0.90. The market portfolio has σ_M = 14.0% and T-bills yield 2.0%. What is the fund's Sharpe ratio?","options":["0.78","7.33","0.60","0.47"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sharpe = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P = (8.6 − 2.0)/11.0 = 0.60. Skipping R_F gives 0.78; dividing by β gives 7.33 (that is a reward-to-risk ratio, not a Sharpe ratio); dividing by the market's σ gives 0.47.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-028","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"R_F = 3%. Portfolio P has E[R_P] = 11% and σ_P = 20%. An investor puts 60% in P and 40% in T-bills. What is the Sharpe ratio of her mixed position?","options":["0.65","0.24","0.67","0.40"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The mix has E[R] = 0.6 × 11 + 0.4 × 3 = 7.8% and σ = 0.6 × 20 = 12%, so Sharpe = (7.8 − 3)/12 = 0.40, the same as P's own (11 − 3)/20 = 0.40: mixing with R_F slides you along the same CAL. Skipping R_F gives 0.65; using P's σ gives 0.24; mixing P's premium with the mix's σ gives 0.67.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s41-42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-029","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"R_F rises from 2% to 3% while E[R_P] and σ_P stay the same. What happens to the Sharpe ratio of P?","options":["It rises, because R_F is part of the numerator","It is unchanged, because σ_P is unchanged","It falls only if β_P is greater than 1","It falls, because the numerator E[R_P] − R_F shrinks"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Sharpe = (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P. A higher R_F reduces the excess return while σ_P stays put, so the ratio falls (the CAL starts higher but is flatter). β plays no part in the Sharpe ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42"},{"id":"fin201-q13-030","concept":"fin201-c13-sharpe-cml","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The market portfolio has the highest Sharpe ratio of all risky portfolios on the efficient frontier.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. M is where the CAL from R_F is tangent to the frontier, which is the steepest attainable line, so its Sharpe ratio is the maximum.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-031","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The market portfolio M is best described as:","options":["the portfolio at the far left of the frontier, with the lowest σ of all","the tangency portfolio, where the CAL from R_F touches the frontier (highest Sharpe ratio)","the single stock with the highest expected return, at the far right of the diagram","the risk-free asset combined 50/50 with the best-performing single stock"],"answer":1,"explanation":"M is the tangency portfolio: it has the steepest attainable CAL, i.e. the highest Sharpe ratio. The lowest-σ portfolio is the minimum-variance portfolio at the left end of the frontier, and the highest-return asset sits at the far right.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-032","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Starting at R_F on the vertical axis, an analyst rotates a straight line upward until it just touches the efficient frontier at a single point. The point of contact is the:","options":["minimum-variance portfolio","risk-free asset","market portfolio M","portfolio with the highest expected return"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The first point the rotating line touches is the tangency point, and the line is the CML. The minimum-variance portfolio is the leftmost point of the frontier; the line from R_F does not touch it at its steepest.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-033","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why do practitioners use a broad index such as the S&P 500 (SPDR trust) as a proxy for the market portfolio?","options":["An index has σ = 0, so it works as a risk-free substitute for the true M","An index has been proven to lie exactly on the efficient frontier at the point of tangency","Investors are legally barred from holding M directly, so a proxy is required","M cannot be observed in advance, and the best past portfolio need not be the best future one"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It is hard enough to find the best portfolio from past data and even harder to predict where M will be, so we use a broad market index. An index is diversified but not risk-free, and nobody has proven it sits exactly at the tangency.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s44"},{"id":"fin201-q13-034","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly separates the market portfolio from the minimum-variance portfolio?","options":["M maximizes the Sharpe ratio; the minimum-variance portfolio minimizes σ","M minimizes σ; the minimum-variance portfolio maximizes the Sharpe ratio","Both describe the same point, the leftmost one on the efficient frontier","M is the highest-return frontier portfolio; the minimum-variance portfolio has the best Sharpe ratio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Two different criteria pick two different points: tangency (best slope from R_F) picks M, lowest σ picks the minimum-variance portfolio. By construction no frontier portfolio has a higher Sharpe ratio than M.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s37, s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-035","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT true of the market portfolio M?","options":["It lies on the efficient frontier at the point where the CML touches it","Its beta is 1, so it carries the market's systematic risk","It has the lowest σ of any portfolio on the efficient frontier","It has the highest Sharpe ratio of any risky portfolio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lowest σ describes the minimum-variance portfolio. M is the tangency portfolio: on the frontier, β_M = 1, and the highest Sharpe ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-036","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Two Calgary investors, one cautious and one bold, both accept the CML theory. How will their portfolios differ?","options":["The cautious investor holds a lower-σ stock portfolio, while the bold one holds M","Both hold M; the cautious one holds more R_F (w_F > 0), the bold one less or borrows (w_F < 0)","The cautious investor holds only R_F and the bold investor holds only M itself","Each holds a different tangent portfolio, matched to the investor's own risk tolerance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every point on the CML is a mix of M and R_F, so risk tolerance only changes the weight w_F. There is one tangent portfolio, not one per investor.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-037","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Three portfolios on the efficient frontier have Sharpe ratios of 0.52, 0.61 and 0.58. M is the tangency portfolio. What can we conclude?","options":["M's Sharpe ratio is 0.52, the lowest of the three, because M carries the least risk","M's Sharpe ratio is exactly 0.58, the middle of the three values, as the market is average","M's Sharpe ratio is at least 0.61, and the 0.61 portfolio offers the steepest CAL of the three","M's Sharpe ratio is below all three, because the index used to proxy M is imperfect"],"answer":2,"explanation":"M has the highest Sharpe ratio of all risky portfolios, so its ratio is at least as high as each of these three; of the three, the 0.61 portfolio gives the steepest CAL. M is not the minimum-risk portfolio, and an imperfect index proxy does not lower M's own ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42-43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-038","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence describes how the market portfolio is found?","options":["Plot the frontier → mark R_F → draw the steepest line from R_F that still touches the frontier → read off the tangency point as M","Mark R_F → pick the single stock with the highest expected return → draw a line from R_F to it → call that stock M","Plot the frontier → find its leftmost point (lowest σ) → draw a horizontal line across to R_F → call the point M","Mark R_F → draw the flattest line that touches the frontier → read off the contact point as M → plot the frontier afterwards"],"answer":0,"explanation":"M is found by tangency: first the frontier, then R_F, then the steepest CAL that still touches an attainable portfolio. The flattest line has the lowest Sharpe ratio and the leftmost point is the minimum-variance portfolio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43, REV p3"},{"id":"fin201-q13-039","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The market portfolio is the portfolio on the efficient frontier with the lowest σ.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The lowest-σ portfolio on the frontier is the minimum-variance portfolio. The market portfolio is the tangency portfolio, with the highest Sharpe ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43"},{"id":"fin201-q13-040","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In practice we proxy the market portfolio with a broad market _____, such as the S&P 500 (SPDR trust).","options":["index portfolio","Treasury bill","dividend stock","bond ladder"],"answer":0,"explanation":"M cannot be observed in advance, so the course uses a broad market index portfolio (S&P 500, SPDR trust; in Canada the S&P/TSX Composite) as the stand-in.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s44"},{"id":"fin201-q13-041","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does the market portfolio have a beta of exactly 1?","options":["Because ρ = 1 and any perfectly correlated asset has β = 1","Because β is defined by setting the market's σ equal to 100%","Because β is measured relative to the risk-free asset, whose gross return is 1","β_M = cov(R_M, R_M)/σ_M² = σ_M²/σ_M² = 1: the market has the same risk as itself"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Beta is cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M². Put i = M and the covariance of a variable with itself is its variance, so β_M = 1. A perfectly correlated asset has β = σ_i/σ_M, which equals 1 only if its σ matches the market's.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51"},{"id":"fin201-q13-042","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A Calgary advisor tells every client, cautious or bold: 'You will all hold the same low-cost broad index fund; we only change how much T-bill you hold alongside it.' Which idea underlies this advice?","options":["Every investor holds the market portfolio plus the risk-free asset and differs only in w_F","Every investor should hold the minimum-variance portfolio, whatever their risk tolerance","Diversification eliminates systematic risk, so an index fund is risk-free","Each investor should pick a different single stock whose β matches their risk tolerance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is the CML idea: the best risky portfolio is the same for all (M, proxied by an index), and risk tolerance is expressed through the risk-free weight. An index fund still carries systematic risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s43-44"},{"id":"fin201-q13-043","concept":"fin201-c13-market-portfolio","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A broad index fund such as the SPDR trust is only a proxy for the market portfolio, not M itself.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. The theoretical M uses expected returns and σ's we cannot observe in advance, so a broad index is used as a practical stand-in.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s44"},{"id":"fin201-q13-044","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Systematic risk is risk that:","options":["is unique to one company and disappears in a large portfolio","arises only from choosing poor weights for the stocks in a portfolio","affects almost all assets and cannot be removed by diversification","is borne only by investors who hold the risk-free asset"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Systematic (market, non-diversifiable) risk comes from surprises that hit nearly every asset, such as GDP, inflation and interest rates. Risk unique to a company is unsystematic.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-49, TB 13.4"},{"id":"fin201-q13-045","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The Bank of Canada unexpectedly raises its policy rate by 0.75 percentage points. For a stock portfolio this surprise is:","options":["unsystematic risk, because only one institution made the decision","systematic risk, because it affects almost every firm","unsystematic risk, because only banks are affected","no risk at all, because the decision was announced publicly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Interest rates are a classic market-wide factor, so a surprise change is systematic. Announcement does not remove risk; only the expected part of news is already priced.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13 CTQ 3, 4"},{"id":"fin201-q13-046","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A fire shuts down the only plant of Foothills Packaging Ltd. for six months. For an investor who also owns 40 other stocks, this event is mainly:","options":["systematic risk, because the stock trades on the TSX","systematic risk, because every investor in the stock is affected","no risk at all, because the 40 other stocks are unaffected","unsystematic risk, because it is specific to one company"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A single-company event is company-specific (unsystematic, diversifiable) risk: with 40 other stocks it barely moves the portfolio. It is still a risk for that one stock, so 'no risk at all' overstates it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.4, SM 13 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q13-047","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A sudden pandemic lockdown cuts sales in nearly every Canadian industry at once. Which type of risk does this represent?","options":["unsystematic risk, since it started with one virus","company-specific risk, since each firm is affected separately","systematic (market) risk, because it hits almost every firm at once","diversifiable risk, since it could be avoided by holding many stocks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The shock hits almost all assets in the same direction, so holding many stocks does not cancel it: systematic, non-diversifiable risk.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-49"},{"id":"fin201-q13-048","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which of these events is mostly SYSTEMATIC risk?","options":["The CEO of Sunrise Dairy resigns suddenly","A union strike halts one airline's flights","A data breach hits one retailer","Canadian inflation unexpectedly jumps to 8%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An inflation surprise affects wages, costs and prices across the whole economy. The other three events are unique to one company, so they are unsystematic.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.4, SM 13 CTQ 3"},{"id":"fin201-q13-049","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pair gives two names for the same type of risk?","options":["Company-specific risk and diversifiable risk","Market risk and diversifiable risk","Systematic risk and company-specific risk","Unsystematic risk and non-diversifiable risk"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Company-specific = unsystematic = diversifiable = idiosyncratic. Market = systematic = non-diversifiable. The wrong pairs each mix one term from each family.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s49"},{"id":"fin201-q13-050","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which type of risk earns a risk premium in the CAPM framework?","options":["Both types, with the premium proportional to the asset's total risk σ","Only unsystematic risk, because it is the risk investors choose to take","Only systematic risk, since unsystematic risk can be removed almost free","Neither type; the premium compensates investors only for expected inflation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The systematic risk principle: the reward for bearing risk depends only on systematic risk. Unsystematic risk is avoidable by diversifying, so the market pays nothing for it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.6, L06 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q13-051","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT another name for unsystematic risk?","options":["Idiosyncratic risk","Market risk","Diversifiable risk","Company-specific risk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Market risk is a synonym for systematic risk. Company-specific, diversifiable and idiosyncratic risk all describe the unsystematic part.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s49, S7 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q13-052","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does diversification fail to remove systematic risk?","options":["Because investors cannot afford enough different stocks to cover the whole market","A systematic shock moves almost all assets the same way, so it does not cancel","Diversification only works when a portfolio holds fewer than ten stocks","Systematic risk is always smaller than unsystematic risk, so it never matters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unsystematic surprises are good for some stocks and bad for others, so they average out. A market-wide surprise moves everything together, so the portfolio σ bottoms out at about σ_M.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47, TB 13.5"},{"id":"fin201-q13-053","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why does the market pay no risk premium for unsystematic risk?","options":["It is far too small to measure reliably, so the market simply ignores it","It is already built into the risk-free rate that every investor receives","Investors can remove it almost free by diversifying, so bearing it earns nothing","It is too uncertain to price, so the market leaves it out of expected returns"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because unsystematic risk can be diversified away for free, the market does not reward risk borne unnecessarily. Only systematic risk, which cannot be removed without giving up expected return, earns a premium.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.6, SM 13 CTQ 1"},{"id":"fin201-q13-054","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Moraine Holdings owns 8 Alberta energy stocks and adds 40 stocks spread across Canadian sectors. Which part of its risk falls the most?","options":["Unsystematic risk; what remains is mostly systematic, close to σ_M","Systematic risk, because more stocks mean less market exposure","Both parts fall by the same proportion as stocks are added","Neither: adding stocks changes only expected return"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spreading across sectors cancels company- and industry-specific surprises (unsystematic). The market-wide part stays, so σ_P falls toward the limit σ_M.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47"},{"id":"fin201-q13-055","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"In the textbook's classic study of randomly chosen NYSE stocks (Table 13.7), equally weighted portfolios have an average σ of about 49% with 1 stock, 24% with 10, 20% with 100 and 19% with 1,000. What does the roughly 19% floor represent?","options":["Unsystematic risk left over because 1,000 stocks is still too few","The risk-free rate, which is the return when all risk is removed","Systematic risk: about the market's own σ, which cannot be diversified away","Estimation error from using historical data to measure σ"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The drop from 49% to 19% is mostly unsystematic risk being diversified away; the benefit shrinks as stocks are added and flattens at the market's own volatility. A floor that does not fall further is systematic.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5 Table 13.7, L06 s47"},{"id":"fin201-q13-056","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Total risk can be split into systematic risk plus unsystematic risk, and diversification removes only the unsystematic part.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"True. Total risk = systematic + unsystematic. Diversifying cancels company-specific surprises, leaving the systematic part.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.5, S7 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q13-057","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":1,"stem":"If a portfolio holds enough stocks, diversification eliminates both unsystematic and systematic risk.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. Diversification eliminates only unsystematic risk. A very large portfolio still has σ close to σ_M because systematic risk affects all stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47"},{"id":"fin201-q13-058","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"As more stocks are added, the σ of a portfolio falls toward a limit of _____, the volatility of the market.","options":["R_F","zero","σ_M","the σ of the lowest-σ stock in the portfolio"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The limit is σ_M, the systematic risk that cannot be diversified away. It does not reach zero, and it has nothing to do with R_F.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-47"},{"id":"fin201-q13-059","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"The textbook splits a stock's actual return into its expected return plus a systematic (market-wide) surprise and an unsystematic (company-specific) surprise. Fraser Foods is expected to return 7.0% this year. A market-wide surprise adds 2.0% and a company-specific surprise subtracts 3.5%. What is its actual return?","options":["9.00%","5.50%","12.50%","3.50%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R = E(R) + m + ε, where m is the systematic surprise and ε the unsystematic one: 7.0 + 2.0 − 3.5 = 5.50%. Ignoring the company-specific surprise gives 9.00%; adding the negative surprise as a positive gives 12.50%; ignoring the market-wide surprise gives 3.50%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.4"},{"id":"fin201-q13-060","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"The Bank of Canada raised its policy rate by exactly the 0.25 percentage points that every analyst had expected, and Canadian stock prices barely moved. Why?","options":["Interest rate changes are unsystematic, so they should not move market prices","Stock prices do not depend on interest rates, only on company earnings","Diversified portfolios are immune to systematic risk, so index prices cannot react","Only the surprise part of news moves prices; the expected part was already priced in"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Risk comes from surprises, the unanticipated part of the return. An expected change has already been discounted. Interest rates are systematic, and diversified portfolios still carry systematic risk.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13 CTQ 2, TB 13.3"},{"id":"fin201-q13-061","concept":"fin201-c13-systematic-unsystematic","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"Which statement about variance is correct?","options":["Both a stock's and a diversified portfolio's variance measure only systematic risk","A single stock's variance measures total risk; a diversified portfolio's mostly systematic risk","Both a stock's and a diversified portfolio's variance measure only unsystematic risk","A diversified portfolio's variance reflects unsystematic risk; a stock's reflects systematic risk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The variance of an individual asset includes both parts. In a well-diversified portfolio the unsystematic parts have washed out, so what is left is essentially the systematic risk.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13 CTQ 6, S7 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q13-062","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The beta of stock i is defined as:","options":["cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M, the covariance per unit of market standard deviation","cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M², the slope of the regression of stock returns on market returns","cov(R_i, R_M)/(σ_i σ_M), the correlation between stock and market","σ_i/σ_M, the ratio of the stock's volatility to the market's volatility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"β_i = cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M² = ρ_iM σ_i/σ_M. Dividing by σ_M alone is the classic slip; dividing by σ_i σ_M gives the correlation ρ; σ_i/σ_M is only part of the ρ-version.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53-54, S7 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q13-063","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"A regression of Cobalt Tech's monthly returns on the S&P/TSX Composite's monthly returns gives a slope of 1.6. How is the stock classified?","options":["Aggressive: β > 1, more systematic risk than the market","Defensive: 0 < β < 1, less systematic risk than the market","Market-neutral: β = 0, no systematic risk at all","Opposite to the market: β < 0, moves against it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The regression slope is β. A slope of 1.6 means the stock tends to move 1.6% for each 1% market move: more systematic risk than the market, so aggressive (typical of high tech).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-54"},{"id":"fin201-q13-064","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which asset would you expect to have a beta closest to zero?","options":["A broad S&P/TSX Composite index fund","A high-growth software stock","A 91-day Government of Canada T-bill","A leveraged fund on the S&P 500"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The risk-free asset has no systematic risk, so β_F = 0. The broad index fund has β ≈ 1; growth and leveraged funds have β above 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-52"},{"id":"fin201-q13-065","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Aurora Aerospace's return depends on the economy. Strong (probability 0.30): Aurora 30%, S&P/TSX Composite 22%. Moderate (0.50): Aurora 8%, market 9%. Weak (0.20): Aurora −16%, market −15%. Treating the index as the market, what is Aurora's beta?","answer":1.23,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)))/(0.3*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.2*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2)","steps":[{"label":"Expected market return E[R_M]","value":8.1,"check":"(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Expected stock return E[R_i]","value":9.8,"check":"(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Covariance cov(R_i, R_M)","value":0.02026,"check":"(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Market variance σ_M²","value":0.01651,"check":"(0.3*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.2*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Beta = cov/σ_M²","value":1.23,"check":"(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)))/(0.3*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.2*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R_M] = 0.30 × 22% + 0.50 × 9% + 0.20 × (−15%) = 8.1%;  E[R_i] = 0.30 × 30% + 0.50 × 8% + 0.20 × (−16%) = 9.8%","cov = 0.30 × (0.202)(0.139) + 0.50 × (−0.018)(0.009) + 0.20 × (−0.258)(−0.231) = 0.02026","σ_M² = 0.30 × (0.139)² + 0.50 × (0.009)² + 0.20 × (−0.231)² = 0.01651","β = cov/σ_M² = 0.02026/0.01651 = 1.23"],"keystrokes":["Means: 0.3 × 22 + 0.5 × 9 + 0.2 × (−15) = 8.1 and 0.3 × 30 + 0.5 × 8 + 0.2 × (−16) = 9.8","Cov: sum of p × (deviation of stock) × (deviation of market) = 0.020262","Var: sum of p × (deviation of market)² = 0.016509; ÷ → 1.2273 → 1.23"],"errors":[{"id":"beta-sigma-m","label":"Divided the covariance by σ_M instead of σ_M²","value":0.16,"check":"(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)))/sqrt((0.3*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.2*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2))","explain":"The denominator is the market VARIANCE σ_M². Dividing by σ_M leaves a number that is not a beta."},{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Computed the correlation instead of beta","value":0.98,"check":"(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)))/(sqrt((0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))**2+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))**2+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))**2))*sqrt((0.3*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.5*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2+0.2*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))**2)))","explain":"Dividing the covariance by σ_i σ_M gives ρ, which cannot exceed 1. Beta divides by σ_M²."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Divided the covariance by the stock's variance","value":0.79,"check":"(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.22-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(0.09-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15))+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))*(-0.15-(0.3*0.22+0.5*0.09+0.2*-0.15)))/(0.3*(0.3-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))**2+0.5*(0.08-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))**2+0.2*(-0.16-(0.3*0.3+0.5*0.08+0.2*-0.16))**2)","explain":"Beta measures sensitivity to the market, so the denominator is the market's variance, not the stock's."}],"explanation":"Beta is the covariance of the stock with the market divided by the market's variance; Aurora has 23% more systematic risk than the market.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s5, s14, SM 13-32"},{"id":"fin201-q13-066","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Over five years, Muskoka Brewing's returns have a correlation of 0.72 with the S&P/TSX Composite. Muskoka's σ is 31% and the index's σ is 19%. What is Muskoka's beta?","answer":1.17,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"0.72*31/19","steps":[{"label":"Volatility ratio σ_i/σ_M","value":1.63,"check":"31/19","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Beta = ρ σ_i/σ_M","value":1.17,"check":"0.72*31/19","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["σ_i/σ_M = 31/19 = 1.63","β = ρ_iM σ_i/σ_M = 0.72 × 1.63 = 1.17"],"keystrokes":["31 ÷ 19 = 1.6316","× 0.72 = 1.1747 → 1.17"],"errors":[{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Reported the correlation as the beta","value":0.72,"check":"0.72","explain":"Correlation is bounded by ±1 and ignores relative size. Beta multiplies ρ by σ_i/σ_M."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Inverted the volatility ratio","value":0.44,"check":"0.72*19/31","explain":"The stock's σ goes in the numerator and the market's in the denominator."},{"id":"forgot-rho","label":"Used σ_i/σ_M without ρ","value":1.63,"check":"31/19","explain":"Beta = ρ × σ_i/σ_M; the ratio alone ignores how tightly the stock follows the market."}],"explanation":"β = ρ σ_i/σ_M: Muskoka swings 1.63 times as much as the market but is only 72% correlated with it, giving β = 1.17.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53, S7 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q13-067","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Kootenay Foods' returns have a covariance of 0.0240 with the market's returns. The market's σ is 18%. What is Kootenay's beta?","answer":0.74,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"0.0240/0.18**2","steps":[{"label":"Market variance σ_M² (decimals)","value":0.0324,"check":"0.18**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.0001}},{"label":"Beta = cov/σ_M²","value":0.74,"check":"0.0240/0.18**2","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["σ_M² = 0.18² = 0.0324","β = cov(R_i, R_M)/σ_M² = 0.0240/0.0324 = 0.74"],"keystrokes":["0.18 x² = 0.0324","0.0240 ÷ 0.0324 = 0.7407 → 0.74"],"errors":[{"id":"beta-sigma-m","label":"Divided by σ_M instead of σ_M²","value":0.13,"check":"0.0240/0.18","explain":"Divide by the variance 0.0324, not the standard deviation 0.18."},{"id":"inverted-beta","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":1.35,"check":"0.18**2/0.0240","explain":"The covariance goes on top: β = cov/σ_M²."}],"explanation":"Beta is covariance over market variance; Kootenay is a defensive stock (β < 1).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53, SM 13-32"},{"id":"fin201-q13-068","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Yukon Gold Corp. is a mining stock. In four equally likely states the market returns 20%, 8%, −4% and −16%, while Yukon Gold returns −6%, 0%, 6% and 18% respectively. What is Yukon Gold's beta?","answer":-0.65,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(0.25*(-0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.0-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.18-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16)))/(0.25*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2)","steps":[{"label":"Expected market return","value":2.0,"check":"(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Expected stock return","value":4.5,"check":"(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Covariance cov(R_i, R_M)","value":-0.0117,"check":"(0.25*(-0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.0-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.18-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16)))","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Market variance σ_M²","value":0.018,"check":"(0.25*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":5e-05}},{"label":"Beta = cov/σ_M²","value":-0.65,"check":"(0.25*(-0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.0-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.18-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16)))/(0.25*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2)","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R_M] = 2.0%; E[R_i] = 4.5%","Deviations: stock −10.5%, −4.5%, 1.5%, 13.5%; market 18%, 6%, −6%, −18%","cov = 0.25 × (−0.0189 − 0.0027 − 0.0009 − 0.0243) = −0.0117;  σ_M² = 0.25 × (0.0324 + 0.0036 + 0.0036 + 0.0324) = 0.0180","β = −0.0117/0.0180 = −0.65: the stock tends to move against the market"],"keystrokes":["Means: ÷ 4 of each column → 2.0 and 4.5","Cov: ¼ × Σ(dev stock × dev market) = −0.0117","Var: ¼ × Σ(dev market)² = 0.0180; ÷ → −0.65"],"errors":[{"id":"beta-sigma-m","label":"Divided the covariance by σ_M instead of σ_M²","value":-0.09,"check":"(0.25*(-0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.0-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.18-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16)))/sqrt((0.25*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2))","explain":"The denominator is the variance 0.0180, not the standard deviation 0.1342."},{"id":"cov-as-rho","label":"Computed the correlation instead of beta","value":-0.98,"check":"(0.25*(-0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.0-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))+0.25*(0.18-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16)))/(sqrt((0.25*(-0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))**2+0.25*(0.0-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))**2+0.25*(0.06-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))**2+0.25*(0.18-(0.25*-0.06+0.25*0.0+0.25*0.06+0.25*0.18))**2))*sqrt((0.25*(0.2-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(0.08-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.04-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2+0.25*(-0.16-(0.25*0.2+0.25*0.08+0.25*-0.04+0.25*-0.16))**2)))","explain":"Dividing by σ_i σ_M gives ρ (here −0.98). Beta divides by σ_M²."}],"explanation":"A negative covariance with the market gives a negative beta: the stock does well when the market does badly (a rare, diversifying profile, like gold stocks in a scare).","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s18, L06 s51-52"},{"id":"fin201-q13-069","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A regression of 60 monthly returns gives Cobalt Tech: R_stock = 0.3% + 1.32 × R_market. If the market returns +4.0% next month, what return does the regression predict for Cobalt Tech (% per month)?","answer":5.58,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02},"check":"0.3+1.32*4","steps":[{"label":"Systematic part: β × market return","value":5.28,"check":"1.32*4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}},{"label":"Predicted return = α + β R_M","value":5.58,"check":"0.3+1.32*4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.02}}],"solution":["The slope of the regression line is β = 1.32; the intercept is α = 0.3%","Predicted R = α + β R_M = 0.3% + 1.32 × 4.0% = 0.3% + 5.28% = 5.58%"],"keystrokes":["1.32 × 4 = 5.28","+ 0.3 = 5.58"],"errors":[{"id":"dropped-intercept","label":"Left out the intercept α","value":5.28,"check":"1.32*4","explain":"The regression line is α + β R_M; the stock's own average adds 0.3%."},{"id":"inverted-beta","label":"Divided by beta instead of multiplying","value":3.33,"check":"0.3+4/1.32","explain":"Beta is the slope: the stock moves β units per unit of market move, so multiply."}],"explanation":"Beta is the slope of the regression of stock returns on market returns: each 1% market move predicts a 1.32% move in the stock, plus the intercept.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s54, S7 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q13-070","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Glacier Mining has σ = 36% and a correlation of 0.55 with the S&P/TSX Composite, whose σ is 22%. What is Glacier's beta?","options":["0.55","0.34","0.90","1.64"],"answer":2,"explanation":"β = ρ σ_i/σ_M = 0.55 × 36/22 = 0.90. Reporting ρ as beta gives 0.55; inverting the σ ratio gives 0.34; dropping ρ gives 1.64.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53, S7 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q13-071","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Willow Utilities has a covariance of 0.0192 with the market. Willow's σ is 24% and the market's σ is 16%. What is Willow's beta?","options":["0.75","0.12","0.50","1.33"],"answer":0,"explanation":"β = cov/σ_M² = 0.0192/0.0256 = 0.75. Dividing by σ_M gives 0.12; dividing by σ_i σ_M gives the correlation, 0.50; inverting the ratio gives 1.33.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53, SM 13-32"},{"id":"fin201-q13-072","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Gold-Rush Royalty has σ = 45% and a correlation of −0.60 with the market, whose σ is 18%. What is its beta?","options":["−0.60","−0.24","1.50","−1.50"],"answer":3,"explanation":"β = ρ σ_i/σ_M = −0.60 × 45/18 = −1.50. Beta is not bounded by −1: it can be below −1 when the stock's σ exceeds the market's. Reporting ρ gives −0.60; inverting the σ ratio gives −0.24; dropping the sign gives 1.50.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53, S7 s18"},{"id":"fin201-q13-073","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about beta and the correlation coefficient ρ is correct?","options":["ρ is bounded by −1 and +1, but β is not, since β also depends on σ_i/σ_M","Both are bounded by −1 and +1, since both measure co-movement with the market","β measures the strength of the linear association; ρ measures sensitivity to the market","They are always equal whenever the benchmark is the market portfolio itself"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Correlation only measures how tightly two return series move together. Beta rescales by σ_i/σ_M, so a stock twice as volatile as the market with ρ = 0.8 has β = 1.6.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-074","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stock A has σ = 38% and β = 0.6. Stock B has σ = 21% and β = 1.4. Which statement is correct?","options":["A has more systematic risk because its σ is higher","They have equal systematic risk because both trade on the same exchange","B has both more systematic risk and more total risk","B has more systematic risk; A has more total risk"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Beta measures systematic risk and σ measures total risk. A's extra volatility must be unsystematic, which a diversified investor can remove.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s50, s58, SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-075","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement about beta is NOT correct?","options":["The beta of any asset must lie between −1 and +1","The beta of the market portfolio is 1","The beta of a Treasury bill is 0","Beta is the slope of the regression of a stock's returns on the market's returns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Only the correlation coefficient is bounded by ±1. Beta = ρ σ_i/σ_M can be 2.5 or −1.5 if the asset is much more volatile than the market.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-54"},{"id":"fin201-q13-076","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why can different information providers publish different betas for the same stock?","options":["Beta reflects each investor's own risk aversion, which providers measure differently","Providers use different formulas, some dividing by σ_M and others by σ_M²","Beta is set by each company's management, not estimated from market returns","Each picks its own market index, estimation window, return frequency and adjustments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Beta is estimated by regression from past returns, so every choice in the regression changes the answer. The formula cov/σ_M² is the same for all; beta is a property of the stock, not of the investor.","source":"generated","ref":"REV p3, TB 13.6"},{"id":"fin201-q13-077","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A fund has a beta of 1.4. If the market is expected to fall 10% in a sell-off, what does the beta imply (all else equal)?","options":["A fall of about 7%","A fall of about 14% from the systematic component","A fall of 10%, since all diversified funds move with the market","A rise of 14%, since β > 1 means outperformance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Beta is the sensitivity to the market: expected move ≈ β × market move = 1.4 × (−10%) = −14% (plus the fund's own α and noise).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51, s54"},{"id":"fin201-q13-078","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Hollis Mining has a correlation of 0.95 with the market yet a beta of only 0.40. How is this possible?","options":["It is impossible, because a stock with a correlation that high must have a beta close to 0.95","It has a very large unsystematic component that lowers its beta well below its correlation","Its σ is only about 0.42 times the market's, so it follows the market closely but swings far less","It has a large negative alpha, which pulls its beta below its correlation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Solve 0.40 = 0.95 × σ_i/σ_M, so σ_i/σ_M = 0.42. High correlation says the stock follows the market; beta also scales for size. Alpha and unsystematic risk do not enter the formula.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53, S7 s14"},{"id":"fin201-q13-079","concept":"fin201-c13-beta","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Beta can be written β_i = ρ_iM × σ_i / _____.","options":["σ_M²","σ_i","E[R_M] − R_F","σ_M"],"answer":3,"explanation":"β = cov/σ_M² and cov = ρ σ_i σ_M, so β = ρ σ_i σ_M/σ_M² = ρ σ_i/σ_M. The denominator is σ_M (not squared) in the ρ version.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-080","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The beta of a portfolio is:","options":["the weighted average of the asset betas, with β = 0 for the risk-free asset","the square root of the weighted sum of the squared asset betas","the beta of the largest holding, since it dominates the portfolio","the simple, equal-weighted average of the risky assets' betas"],"answer":0,"explanation":"β_P = Σ w_i β_i with the same dollar weights used for the portfolio return, risk-free asset included at β_F = 0. An equal-weighted average ignores the actual weights.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s52, S7 s19-20, TB 13.6"},{"id":"fin201-q13-081","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"What beta should a Government of Canada T-bill be given when you compute the beta of a portfolio that holds it?","options":["1, because it is part of the market","−1, because it offsets stocks","Its yield, expressed as a decimal","0, because it has no systematic risk"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The risk-free asset has β_F = 0, but its weight must still be included so that all weights sum to 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-52, S7 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-082","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Family Office holds $12,000 in Nordic Utilities (β = 0.85), $20,000 in Harbour Bank (β = 1.15) and $8,000 in Aurora Aerospace (β = 1.55). What is the portfolio beta?","answer":1.14,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(12000*0.85+20000*1.15+8000*1.55)/40000","steps":[{"label":"Weight in Nordic Utilities","value":30.0,"check":"12000/40000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in Harbour Bank","value":50.0,"check":"20000/40000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in Aurora Aerospace","value":20.0,"check":"8000/40000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio beta","value":1.14,"check":"(12000*0.85+20000*1.15+8000*1.55)/40000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Total = $40,000, so the weights are 0.30, 0.50 and 0.20","β_P = 0.30 × 0.85 + 0.50 × 1.15 + 0.20 × 1.55 = 0.255 + 0.575 + 0.310 = 1.14"],"keystrokes":["12,000 ÷ 40,000 = 0.30; 20,000 ÷ 40,000 = 0.50; 8,000 ÷ 40,000 = 0.20","0.3 × 0.85 = 0.255; 0.5 × 1.15 = 0.575; 0.2 × 1.55 = 0.31; add → 1.14"],"errors":[{"id":"equal-weights","label":"Took the simple average of the betas","value":1.18,"check":"(0.85+1.15+1.55)/3","explain":"The portfolio is not equally weighted: the bank holds half the money."},{"id":"weights-swapped","label":"Paired the weights with the wrong stocks","value":1.21,"check":"0.2*0.85+0.5*1.15+0.3*1.55","explain":"Each beta must be multiplied by its own stock's weight."}],"explanation":"Portfolio beta is the dollar-weighted average of the stock betas: 1.14, more systematic risk than the market.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20, SM 13-13, TB 13.6"},{"id":"fin201-q13-083","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Parkland Wealth holds $22,000 in Stock A (β = 1.30), $18,000 in Stock B (β = 0.70) and $10,000 in T-bills. What is the portfolio beta?","answer":0.82,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(22000*1.30+18000*0.70+10000*0)/50000","steps":[{"label":"Weight in Stock A","value":44.0,"check":"22000/50000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in Stock B","value":36.0,"check":"18000/50000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in T-bills","value":20.0,"check":"10000/50000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio beta","value":0.82,"check":"(22000*1.30+18000*0.70+10000*0)/50000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Total = $50,000: weights 0.44, 0.36 and 0.20","β_P = 0.44 × 1.30 + 0.36 × 0.70 + 0.20 × 0 = 0.572 + 0.252 + 0 = 0.82"],"keystrokes":["22,000 ÷ 50,000 = 0.44; 18,000 ÷ 50,000 = 0.36; 10,000 ÷ 50,000 = 0.20","0.44 × 1.3 = 0.572; 0.36 × 0.7 = 0.252; + 0 → 0.824 → 0.82"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Dropped the T-bills and re-weighted the stocks over $40,000","value":1.03,"check":"(22000*1.30+18000*0.70)/40000","explain":"T-bills have β = 0 but still take 20% of the weights; leaving them out overstates β_P."},{"id":"rf-beta-one","label":"Gave the T-bills a beta of 1","value":1.02,"check":"0.44*1.30+0.36*0.70+0.20*1","explain":"The risk-free asset has no systematic risk: β_F = 0."}],"explanation":"The T-bills dilute the portfolio's beta: β_P = 0.82, a defensive portfolio.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s52, SM 13-19"},{"id":"fin201-q13-084","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Corvus Capital has $120,000 and wants a portfolio with a beta of 1.20. It already holds $30,000 in Stock X (β = 0.90) and $42,000 in Stock Y (β = 1.60). Stock Z has β = 1.25, and the rest of the money goes into a risk-free asset. How many dollars go into the risk-free asset?","answer":8160.0,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1},"check":"(1-0.25-0.35-(1.2-0.25*0.9-0.35*1.6)/1.25)*120000","steps":[{"label":"Weight in X","value":25.0,"check":"30000/120000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Weight in Y","value":35.0,"check":"42000/120000*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Weight in Z from β_P = 1.20","value":33.2,"check":"(1.2-0.25*0.9-0.35*1.6)/1.25*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Weight in the risk-free asset","value":6.8,"check":"(1-0.25-0.35-(1.2-0.25*0.9-0.35*1.6)/1.25)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Dollars in the risk-free asset","value":8160.0,"check":"(1-0.25-0.35-(1.2-0.25*0.9-0.35*1.6)/1.25)*120000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1}}],"solution":["w_X = 30,000/120,000 = 0.25 and w_Y = 42,000/120,000 = 0.35","1.20 = 0.25 × 0.90 + 0.35 × 1.60 + w_Z × 1.25 + w_F × 0, so w_Z = (1.20 − 0.225 − 0.560)/1.25 = 0.332","w_F = 1 − 0.25 − 0.35 − 0.332 = 0.068, which is 0.068 × $120,000 = $8,160 (and Z gets $39,840)"],"keystrokes":["0.25 × 0.9 = 0.225; 0.35 × 1.6 = 0.56","(1.2 − 0.225 − 0.56) ÷ 1.25 = 0.332","1 − 0.25 − 0.35 − 0.332 = 0.068; × 120,000 = 8,160"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Set the risk-free weight to 1 − w_Z, ignoring X and Y","value":80160.0,"check":"(1-(1.2-0.25*0.9-0.35*1.6)/1.25)*120000","explain":"The risk-free weight is what remains after X, Y and Z: 1 − w_X − w_Y − w_Z."},{"id":"wrong-target","label":"Solved for a beta of 1.00 instead of 1.20","value":27360.0,"check":"(1-0.25-0.35-(1-0.25*0.9-0.35*1.6)/1.25)*120000","explain":"The target is β_P = 1.20, not the market's 1.00."}],"explanation":"Set Σ w β equal to the target with β_F = 0, solve for the unknown weight, and let the risk-free asset take the rest: it absorbs $8,160.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20, SM 13-26"},{"id":"fin201-q13-085","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A client of Ridgeline Advisors has $60,000 and wants a portfolio with a beta of 1.8. Ridgeline may use only one stock with β = 1.2 and a risk-free asset (equal lending and borrowing rates). What weight (%) goes into the risk-free asset? A negative answer means borrowing.","answer":-50.0,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(1-1.8/1.2)*100","steps":[{"label":"Weight in the stock: w_S = 1.8/1.2","value":150.0,"check":"1.8/1.2*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Weight in the risk-free asset","value":-50.0,"check":"(1-1.8/1.2)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}},{"label":"Dollars borrowed","value":30000.0,"check":"(1.8/1.2-1)*60000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1}},{"label":"Dollars invested in the stock","value":90000.0,"check":"1.8/1.2*60000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":1}}],"solution":["β_P = w_S × 1.2 + w_F × 0 = 1.8, so w_S = 1.8/1.2 = 1.50","w_F = 1 − 1.50 = −0.50, i.e. −50%","She borrows 0.5 × $60,000 = $30,000 and invests $90,000 in the stock"],"keystrokes":["1.8 ÷ 1.2 = 1.5 (w_S)","1 − 1.5 = −0.5 → −50%"],"errors":[{"id":"borrowing-sign","label":"Entered the borrowing as a positive weight","value":50.0,"check":"(1.8/1.2-1)*100","explain":"A weight on the risk-free asset is negative when you borrow."},{"id":"inverted-weight","label":"Inverted the ratio: w_S = 1.2/1.8","value":33.33,"check":"(1-1.2/1.8)*100","explain":"A target beta above the stock's beta needs w_S > 1, so divide the target by the stock's beta."},{"id":"rf-weight-from-target","label":"Subtracted the target beta from 1","value":-80.0,"check":"(1-1.8)*100","explain":"The weights come from 1 − w_S, with w_S = target ÷ stock beta, not from the beta target itself."}],"explanation":"To reach a beta above any single stock's beta you must borrow at R_F: w_S = 1.5 and w_F = −0.5.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-19d, S7 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-086","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Delta Ridge Fund can mix Stock S (E[R] = 11.5%, β = 1.25) with T-bills yielding 3.5%. It wants the mix to have an expected return of 9.0%. What is the beta of the mix?","answer":0.86,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(9.0-3.5)/(11.5-3.5)*1.25","steps":[{"label":"Weight in S: (9.0 − 3.5)/(11.5 − 3.5)","value":68.75,"check":"(9.0-3.5)/(11.5-3.5)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Weight in T-bills","value":31.25,"check":"(1-(9.0-3.5)/(11.5-3.5))*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio beta","value":0.86,"check":"(9.0-3.5)/(11.5-3.5)*1.25","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["9.0 = w_S × 11.5 + (1 − w_S) × 3.5, so w_S = (9.0 − 3.5)/(11.5 − 3.5) = 0.6875","β_P = 0.6875 × 1.25 + 0.3125 × 0 = 0.86"],"keystrokes":["(9.0 − 3.5) ÷ (11.5 − 3.5) = 0.6875","0.6875 × 1.25 = 0.8594 → 0.86"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Solved for the weight as 9.0/11.5","value":0.98,"check":"9.0/11.5*1.25","explain":"Work with premiums: w_S = (9.0 − 3.5)/(11.5 − 3.5)."},{"id":"weights-swapped","label":"Used the T-bill weight as the weight on S","value":0.39,"check":"(1-(9.0-3.5)/(11.5-3.5))*1.25","explain":"The weight on S is 0.6875; the T-bills get the remaining 0.3125."}],"explanation":"Return and beta are both linear in the weights, so the target return fixes w_S and then β_P = w_S β_S.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-19, S7 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q13-087","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Yardley Fund holds four equal parts (25% each): T-bills, Stock Q (β = 1.10), Stock W (β = 0.70) and Stock X. The portfolio is exactly as risky as the market. What is Stock X's beta?","answer":2.2,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"1/0.25-1.10-0.70","steps":[{"label":"Sum of betas needed: β_P ÷ 0.25","value":4.0,"check":"1/0.25","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Beta of Stock X","value":2.2,"check":"1/0.25-1.10-0.70","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["β_P = 1.00 = 0.25 × (0 + 1.10 + 0.70 + β_X)","0 + 1.10 + 0.70 + β_X = 4.00, so β_X = 4.00 − 1.80 = 2.20"],"keystrokes":["1 ÷ 0.25 = 4","4 − 1.10 − 0.70 = 2.20"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Treated it as three equal parts, ignoring the T-bills' share","value":1.2,"check":"3-1.10-0.70","explain":"The T-bills occupy 25% of the weights with β = 0; it is a four-asset portfolio."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Ignored the 25% weights altogether","value":-0.8,"check":"1-1.10-0.70","explain":"Each beta is multiplied by its 0.25 weight before the sum equals 1.00."}],"explanation":"As risky as the market means β_P = 1. The T-bills contribute zero beta but take a quarter of the weights, so X needs a high beta.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-14, S7 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-088","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Qualico Trust holds 40% in Stock A (β = 1.10), 35% in Stock B (β = 0.60) and 25% in T-bills. What is its portfolio beta?","options":["0.87","0.57","0.65","0.90"],"answer":2,"explanation":"β_P = 0.40 × 1.10 + 0.35 × 0.60 + 0.25 × 0 = 0.44 + 0.21 = 0.65. Re-weighting over the stocks alone (forgetting the T-bill weight) gives 0.87; an equal-weight average gives 0.57; giving the T-bills a beta of 1 gives 0.90.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20, TB 13.6"},{"id":"fin201-q13-089","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Orchard Capital has $75,000 of its own money, borrows $25,000 at the risk-free rate and invests the full $100,000 in stocks whose average beta is 1.20. What is the portfolio beta?","options":["1.60","1.20","0.90","1.50"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Weights are measured on its own $75,000: w_stocks = 100/75 = 1.333 and w_F = −0.333, so β_P = 1.333 × 1.20 + (−0.333) × 0 = 1.60. Ignoring the leverage gives 1.20; using own money over total invested gives 0.90; adding the loan as 25% extra gives 1.50.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-19d, S7 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-090","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Brightwater Capital has a stock portfolio with β = 1.20. To cut its overall beta to 0.90 without changing the mix of its stocks, it moves part of the money into T-bills. What percentage of the portfolio goes into T-bills?","options":["33.33%","25.00%","30.00%","75.00%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"β_P = w_S × 1.20 = 0.90, so w_S = 0.75 and the T-bills take 1 − 0.75 = 25.00%. Dividing the beta gap by the new beta gives 33.33%; using the raw beta gap gives 30.00%; reporting the stock weight kept gives 75.00%.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20, SM 13-19b"},{"id":"fin201-q13-091","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts portfolio beta with portfolio σ?","options":["Both are simple weighted averages, so neither depends on correlations between assets","Portfolio beta is a weighted average of the betas; portfolio σ also depends on correlations","Portfolio σ is a simple weighted average, but portfolio beta needs correlations","Neither depends on the portfolio weights, only on the assets' own risks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"β_P = Σ w β is linear, like E[R_P]. σ_P is non-linear: the two-asset formula includes 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B, so diversification can push σ_P below the weighted average of the σ's.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, S7 s19"},{"id":"fin201-q13-092","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"To compute the beta of a portfolio of three stocks and a T-bill position, you do NOT need:","options":["the correlations between the three stocks","each stock's beta","the portfolio weights, including the T-bill's","the fact that the T-bill has β = 0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Beta is linear in the weights, so each asset's beta and weight is enough. Correlations among the holdings matter only for σ_P.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-093","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why can a portfolio's beta be found as a weighted average of the betas, while its σ cannot?","options":["Betas are always between 0 and 1, so they average neatly into a portfolio value","Beta ignores the risk-free asset, whereas σ_P includes it in the calculation","Beta is a covariance with the market and so linear in the weights; σ_P has cross terms","Beta is a pure number, while σ is measured in percent, so only beta can be averaged"],"answer":2,"explanation":"cov(R_P, R_M) = Σ w_i cov(R_i, R_M), so β_P = Σ w_i β_i. The portfolio variance has cross terms between every pair of assets, which is why σ_P is not a weighted average.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s24, s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-094","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"Orchard's stocks have an average beta of 1.00, but it holds 130% in stocks and −30% in the risk-free asset (borrowed). How should its portfolio beta be interpreted?","options":["β_P = 1.00, because the borrowed money has β = 0 and changes nothing","β_P = 0.70, because borrowing 30% offsets 30% of the market risk","β_P cannot be computed because a negative weight has no beta","β_P = 1.30: borrowing makes the portfolio more aggressive than the market"],"answer":3,"explanation":"β_P = 1.30 × 1.00 + (−0.30) × 0 = 1.30. The loan itself has β = 0, but it allows 130% of own capital in stocks, which scales the systematic risk up.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-19d, TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-095","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"A portfolio of risky stocks held with positive weights (no risk-free asset, no borrowing) can have a beta lower than the lowest beta among its stocks.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"False. The portfolio beta is a weighted average of the stock betas, so it lies between the smallest and largest. Only by adding the risk-free asset (β = 0) or short positions could it go below.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13 CTQ 7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-096","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"In β_P = Σ w_i β_i, the risk-free asset enters with a beta of _____.","options":["0","1","−1","its yield R_F"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The risk-free asset has no systematic risk, so β_F = 0. Its weight still counts in Σ w = 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s52, S7 s20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-097","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which sequence correctly builds a portfolio with a target beta from some known holdings, one unknown stock and the risk-free asset?","options":["Set w_F = 0 → solve for the unknown stock's weight → convert to dollars → check that Σ w β equals the target","Dollars to weights → Σ w β = target with β_F = 0 → solve for the unknown weight → w_F = 1 − the rest → back to dollars","Dollars to weights → set w_F = 1 − the unknown weight → solve for the unknown stock's beta → back to dollars","Solve for w_F first from E[R_P] → set β_F = 1 → average all the betas → convert weights to dollars"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The unknown stock's weight comes from the beta equation, and only then does the risk-free asset take the remainder (possibly negative). Fixing w_F first or giving it β = 1 breaks the logic.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20"},{"id":"fin201-q13-401","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"If you invest in a stock which has a beta of 0.8, when the risk-free rate is 4.5% per annum and you expect the broad S&P 500 market index this year will return 10% per annum, what do you expect your stock return will be?","answer":8.9,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"4.5+0.8*(10-4.5)","steps":[{"label":"Market risk premium E[R_M] − R_F","value":5.5,"check":"10-4.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Stock risk premium β × MRP","value":4.4,"check":"0.8*(10-4.5)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return R_F + β × MRP","value":8.9,"check":"4.5+0.8*(10-4.5)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R_stock] = R_F + β (E[R_M] − R_F)","= 4.5 + 0.8 × (10 − 4.5)","= 4.5 + 0.8 × 5.5 = 4.5 + 4.4 = 8.9% per annum"],"keystrokes":["10 − 4.5 = 5.5","× 0.8 = 4.4","+ 4.5 = 8.9"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Multiplied β by the whole 10% market return","value":12.5,"check":"4.5+0.8*10","explain":"10% is the market's total return, not its premium. Subtract R_F first: the market risk premium is 10 − 4.5 = 5.5%."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left out the risk-free rate","value":4.4,"check":"0.8*(10-4.5)","explain":"0.8 × 5.5 = 4.4% is only the reward for bearing risk. CAPM adds the time value of money R_F back."},{"id":"beta-times-market","label":"Applied β to the market return and dropped R_F","value":8.0,"check":"0.8*10","explain":"Two slips in one: the premium is E[R_M] − R_F, and R_F is then added to it."}],"explanation":"The market is expected to return 10% and T-bills pay 4.5%, so the market risk premium is 5.5%. With β = 0.8 the stock carries 80% of an average amount of systematic risk, so its premium is 4.4%. Add the risk-free rate: 8.9% per annum. The common trap is multiplying β by the 10% market return.","source":"official","ref":"PRAC q12"},{"id":"fin201-q13-402","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Government of Canada T-bills yield 3.4% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.2% per annum. Prairie Grid Utilities has a beta of 0.55. Using CAPM, what is its expected return? Answer in % per annum.","answer":6.81,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"3.4+0.55*6.2","steps":[{"label":"Stock risk premium β × MRP","value":3.41,"check":"0.55*6.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Expected return R_F + premium","value":6.81,"check":"3.4+0.55*6.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R] = R_F + β × (market risk premium)","= 3.4 + 0.55 × 6.2","= 3.4 + 3.41 = 6.81% per annum"],"keystrokes":["0.55 × 6.2 = 3.41","+ 3.4 = 6.81"],"errors":[{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.2% as the market return and subtracted R_F again","value":4.94,"check":"3.4+0.55*(6.2-3.4)","explain":"The question gives the market risk premium, which already is E[R_M] − R_F. Subtracting R_F a second time understates the premium."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left out the risk-free rate","value":3.41,"check":"0.55*6.2","explain":"3.41% is only the reward for risk. CAPM adds the time value of money R_F back."},{"id":"forgot-beta","label":"Added the full market risk premium without scaling by β","value":9.6,"check":"3.4+6.2","explain":"β = 0.55 means the stock carries only 55% of an average amount of systematic risk, so it earns only 55% of the premium."}],"explanation":"The market risk premium is given directly, so use it as is: E[R] = 3.4 + 0.55 × 6.2 = 6.81% per annum. A defensive utility (β < 1) earns less than the market.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-15"},{"id":"fin201-q13-403","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Tundra Brewing is expected to return 9.1% per annum. T-bills yield 3.6% per annum and the market risk premium is 5.8% per annum. If CAPM holds, what is Tundra's beta? Give beta to 2 decimals.","answer":0.95,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(9.1-3.6)/5.8","steps":[{"label":"Stock risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":5.5,"check":"9.1-3.6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Beta = stock premium ÷ MRP","value":0.95,"check":"(9.1-3.6)/5.8","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R] = R_F + β × MRP, so β = (E[R] − R_F)/MRP","= (9.1 − 3.6)/5.8 = 5.5/5.8 = 0.9483","β ≈ 0.95: a little less systematic risk than the market"],"keystrokes":["9.1 − 3.6 = 5.5","÷ 5.8 = 0.9483"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the whole expected return by the premium","value":1.57,"check":"9.1/5.8","explain":"The stock's risk premium is E[R] − R_F = 5.5%, not 9.1%. Beta compares premiums, not total returns."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Subtracted R_F from a premium that already excludes it","value":2.5,"check":"(9.1-3.6)/(5.8-3.6)","explain":"5.8% is already E[R_M] − R_F. Subtracting 3.6% again shrinks the denominator and inflates beta."},{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Inverted the ratio (MRP ÷ stock premium)","value":1.05,"check":"5.8/(9.1-3.6)","explain":"Beta = stock premium ÷ market premium. A stock earning a smaller premium than the market must have a beta below 1."}],"explanation":"Strip out the time value first: the stock earns a 5.5% premium while the market premium is 5.8%. Beta is the ratio of the two premiums: 5.5/5.8 = 0.95. Using the total return 9.1% in the numerator (forgot-minus-rf) inflates beta to 1.57.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-404","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Northern Lights Software has a beta of 1.25 and an expected return of 12.4% per annum. The risk-free rate is 3.9% per annum. If CAPM holds, what is the expected return on the market portfolio? Answer in % per annum.","answer":10.7,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"3.9+(12.4-3.9)/1.25","steps":[{"label":"Stock risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":8.5,"check":"12.4-3.9","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Market risk premium = stock premium ÷ β","value":6.8,"check":"(12.4-3.9)/1.25","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"E[R_M] = R_F + MRP","value":10.7,"check":"3.9+(12.4-3.9)/1.25","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R] − R_F = β (E[R_M] − R_F)","E[R_M] − R_F = (12.4 − 3.9)/1.25 = 8.5/1.25 = 6.8","E[R_M] = 3.9 + 6.8 = 10.7% per annum"],"keystrokes":["12.4 − 3.9 = 8.5","÷ 1.25 = 6.8","+ 3.9 = 10.7"],"errors":[{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Reported the market risk premium as the market return","value":6.8,"check":"(12.4-3.9)/1.25","explain":"6.8% is E[R_M] − R_F. The market's expected return is that premium plus R_F."},{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the whole expected return by β","value":9.92,"check":"12.4/1.25","explain":"The premium is E[R] − R_F = 8.5%, so divide 8.5, not 12.4, by β."},{"id":"multiplied-by-beta","label":"Multiplied the stock premium by β instead of dividing","value":14.53,"check":"3.9+(12.4-3.9)*1.25","explain":"The stock premium equals β times the market premium, so the market premium is the stock premium divided by β."}],"explanation":"Divide the stock's 8.5% premium by its beta of 1.25 to get the market risk premium (6.8%), then add the risk-free rate: E[R_M] = 10.7%. Stopping at 6.8% answers a different question (the premium, not the market return).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-17"},{"id":"fin201-q13-405","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Cascade Pipelines has a beta of 1.30 and an expected return of 11.38% per annum. The market portfolio is expected to return 9.4% per annum. If CAPM holds, what is the risk-free rate? Answer in % per annum.","answer":2.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(11.38-1.3*9.4)/(1-1.3)","steps":[{"label":"β × E[R_M]","value":12.22,"check":"1.3*9.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"E[R] − β × E[R_M]","value":-0.84,"check":"11.38-1.3*9.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Divide by (1 − β) to get R_F","value":2.8,"check":"(11.38-1.3*9.4)/(1-1.3)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R] = R_F + β (E[R_M] − R_F) = R_F (1 − β) + β E[R_M]","11.38 = R_F (1 − 1.3) + 1.3 × 9.4 = −0.3 R_F + 12.22","R_F = (11.38 − 12.22)/(−0.3) = −0.84/−0.3 = 2.8% per annum"],"keystrokes":["1.3 × 9.4 = 12.22","11.38 − 12.22 = −0.84","÷ (1 − 1.3) = ÷ (−0.3) = 2.8"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Forgot that R_F also sits inside the bracket","value":-0.84,"check":"11.38-1.3*9.4","explain":"R_F appears twice: E[R] = R_F + β E[R_M] − β R_F. Collect both R_F terms to get R_F (1 − β) and divide by (1 − β)."},{"id":"sign-slip","label":"Divided by (β − 1) instead of (1 − β)","value":-2.8,"check":"(11.38-1.3*9.4)/(1.3-1)","explain":"R_F (1 − β) = E[R] − β E[R_M]. With β > 1, (1 − β) is negative; flipping it flips the sign of the answer."},{"id":"wrong-divisor","label":"Divided by β instead of (1 − β)","value":-0.65,"check":"(11.38-1.3*9.4)/1.3","explain":"The coefficient on R_F after collecting terms is (1 − β), not β."}],"explanation":"Solving for R_F is the awkward rearrangement because R_F appears twice. Expand and collect: R_F (1 − β) = E[R] − β E[R_M], so R_F = (11.38 − 12.22)/(1 − 1.3) = 2.8%. Check: 2.8 + 1.3 × (9.4 − 2.8) = 2.8 + 8.58 = 11.38.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-18"},{"id":"fin201-q13-406","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Two stocks lie on the same security market line. Stock P has beta 1.20 and expected return 10.8% per annum; Stock Q has beta 0.70 and expected return 7.8% per annum. Because reward-to-risk (E[R] − R_F)/β is equal for both, what is the risk-free rate? Answer in % per annum.","answer":3.6,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"10.8-1.2*(10.8-7.8)/(1.2-0.7)","steps":[{"label":"Reward-to-risk = SML slope (return gap ÷ beta gap)","value":6.0,"check":"(10.8-7.8)/(1.2-0.7)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"R_F = E[R_P] − β_P × slope","value":3.6,"check":"10.8-1.2*(10.8-7.8)/(1.2-0.7)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Equal reward-to-risk: (10.8 − R_F)/1.20 = (7.8 − R_F)/0.70","Cross-multiply: 0.70 (10.8 − R_F) = 1.20 (7.8 − R_F) → 7.56 − 0.70 R_F = 9.36 − 1.20 R_F → 0.50 R_F = 1.80 → R_F = 3.6","Shortcut: slope = (10.8 − 7.8)/(1.20 − 0.70) = 6.0, so R_F = 10.8 − 1.20 × 6.0 = 3.6% per annum"],"keystrokes":["10.8 − 7.8 = 3.0","÷ (1.2 − 0.7) = 6.0","10.8 − 1.2 × 6.0 = 3.6"],"errors":[{"id":"swapped-betas","label":"Cross-multiplied with the betas on the wrong sides","value":15.0,"check":"(1.2*10.8-0.7*7.8)/(1.2-0.7)","explain":"(E_P − R_F)/β_P = (E_Q − R_F)/β_Q gives β_Q (E_P − R_F) = β_P (E_Q − R_F). Pairing each beta with its own return flips the numerator."},{"id":"slope-as-rf","label":"Reported the SML slope as the risk-free rate","value":6.0,"check":"(10.8-7.8)/(1.2-0.7)","explain":"6.0% is the slope (the market risk premium). The risk-free rate is the intercept at β = 0."},{"id":"added-betas","label":"Used β_P + β_Q instead of the beta gap for the slope","value":8.91,"check":"10.8-1.2*(10.8-7.8)/(1.2+0.7)","explain":"Slope = rise over run, and the run is the difference in betas, 1.20 − 0.70."}],"explanation":"In equilibrium every asset earns the same premium per unit of beta, so the two stocks sit on one straight line. Its slope is (10.8 − 7.8)/(1.20 − 0.70) = 6.0, and extending the line back to β = 0 gives R_F = 3.6%. Check Q: 3.6 + 0.70 × 6.0 = 7.8.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-22"},{"id":"fin201-q13-407","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Under CAPM, Stock S (beta 1.40) is expected to return 12.5% per annum and Stock T (beta 0.80) is expected to return 8.3% per annum. Both are fairly priced. What return do you expect on the market portfolio? Answer in % per annum.","answer":9.7,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"8.3-0.8*(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)+(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)","steps":[{"label":"Market risk premium = SML slope","value":7.0,"check":"(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Risk-free rate = E[R_T] − β_T × slope","value":2.7,"check":"8.3-0.8*(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"E[R_M] = R_F + MRP","value":9.7,"check":"8.3-0.8*(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)+(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Fairly priced stocks lie on the SML, so slope = (12.5 − 8.3)/(1.40 − 0.80) = 4.2/0.6 = 7.0 = market risk premium","R_F = 8.3 − 0.80 × 7.0 = 2.7","E[R_M] = R_F + MRP = 2.7 + 7.0 = 9.7% per annum (the SML at β = 1)"],"keystrokes":["12.5 − 8.3 = 4.2","÷ (1.4 − 0.8) = 7.0","8.3 − 0.8 × 7.0 = 2.7","2.7 + 7.0 = 9.7"],"errors":[{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Reported the SML slope as the market return","value":7.0,"check":"(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)","explain":"The slope of the SML is the market risk premium, E[R_M] − R_F. The market return is R_F plus that slope."},{"id":"averaged-returns","label":"Averaged the two stocks' returns","value":10.4,"check":"(12.5+8.3)/2","explain":"The average of two expected returns is the return at the average of their betas (1.10), not at the market's beta of 1.00."},{"id":"rf-as-market","label":"Reported the risk-free rate as the market return","value":2.7,"check":"8.3-0.8*(12.5-8.3)/(1.4-0.8)","explain":"R_F is the intercept at β = 0. The market portfolio sits at β = 1."}],"explanation":"Two points fix the SML. Slope 7.0% is the market risk premium; the intercept is R_F = 2.7%; at β = 1 the line gives E[R_M] = 2.7 + 7.0 = 9.7%. Check S: 2.7 + 1.40 × 7.0 = 12.5.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-29"},{"id":"fin201-q13-408","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":["fin201-c13-portfolio-beta"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Priya holds $40,000 in an S&P/TSX 60 index fund (beta 1.00), $30,000 in Coast Mountain Rail (beta 1.60) and $30,000 in T-bills. T-bills pay 3.0% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.0% per annum. Using CAPM, what is the expected return on her whole portfolio? Answer in % per annum.","answer":8.28,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"3+(0.4*1+0.3*1.6+0.3*0)*6","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio beta Σ w β (T-bill β = 0)","value":0.88,"check":"0.4*1+0.3*1.6+0.3*0","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"E[R_P] = R_F + β_P × MRP","value":8.28,"check":"3+(0.4*1+0.3*1.6+0.3*0)*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Weights: 40%, 30%, 30%. β_P = 0.40 × 1.00 + 0.30 × 1.60 + 0.30 × 0 = 0.88","E[R_P] = 3.0 + 0.88 × 6.0 = 3.0 + 5.28 = 8.28% per annum","Check by weights: 0.40 × 9.0 + 0.30 × 12.6 + 0.30 × 3.0 = 3.6 + 3.78 + 0.9 = 8.28"],"keystrokes":["0.4 × 1 + 0.3 × 1.6 + 0.3 × 0 = 0.88","× 6 = 5.28","+ 3 = 8.28"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-rf-weight","label":"Ignored the T-bills when weighting betas","value":10.54,"check":"3+((40*1+30*1.6)/70)*6","explain":"T-bills are 30% of the portfolio with β = 0. Dropping them re-weights the risky assets to 100% and overstates beta."},{"id":"equal-weight-beta","label":"Averaged the three betas with equal weights","value":8.2,"check":"3+((1+1.6+0)/3)*6","explain":"Portfolio beta uses dollar weights (40/30/30), not a simple average."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.0% as the market return","value":5.64,"check":"3+0.88*(6-3)","explain":"The question gives the market risk premium, so do not subtract R_F again."}],"explanation":"CAPM works for portfolios exactly as for stocks, using the portfolio beta. The T-bills count with weight 30% and β = 0, so β_P = 0.88 and E[R_P] = 3.0 + 0.88 × 6.0 = 8.28%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-13"},{"id":"fin201-q13-409","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Foothills Steel has a beta of 1.60 and an expected return of 14.0% per annum. T-bills yield 3.6% per annum. What market risk premium does CAPM imply? Answer in % per annum.","answer":6.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(14-3.6)/1.6","steps":[{"label":"Stock risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":10.4,"check":"14-3.6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"MRP = stock premium ÷ β","value":6.5,"check":"(14-3.6)/1.6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R] − R_F = β × MRP, so MRP = (E[R] − R_F)/β","= (14.0 − 3.6)/1.60 = 10.4/1.60 = 6.5% per annum"],"keystrokes":["14 − 3.6 = 10.4","÷ 1.6 = 6.5"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the whole expected return by β","value":8.75,"check":"14/1.6","explain":"The stock's premium is 14.0 − 3.6 = 10.4%. Dividing the total return by β does not remove R_F."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Reported the market return (R_F + MRP) instead of the premium","value":10.1,"check":"3.6+(14-3.6)/1.6","explain":"6.5% is the premium. Adding R_F back gives 10.1%, which is E[R_M], a different quantity."},{"id":"forgot-divide-beta","label":"Stopped at the stock's premium","value":10.4,"check":"14-3.6","explain":"10.4% is the premium for β = 1.60. Divide by β to get the premium for β = 1."}],"explanation":"The stock earns a 10.4% premium for carrying 1.60 units of market risk, so each unit of beta is paid 10.4/1.60 = 6.5%. That per-unit reward is the market risk premium (the SML slope).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-410","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Coast Mountain Rail has a beta of 1.5. The risk-free rate is 3% per annum and the market is expected to return 9% per annum. What is Coast Mountain's CAPM expected return?","options":["16.50%","9.00%","12.00%","13.50%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Market risk premium = 9 − 3 = 6%, so E[R] = 3 + 1.5 × 6 = 12.0%. 16.5% = 3 + 1.5 × 9 multiplies β by the whole market return (forgot to subtract R_F). 9.0% = 1.5 × 6 is the premium alone with no time value. 13.5% = 1.5 × 9 uses the market return and forgets R_F as well.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s17"},{"id":"fin201-q13-411","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Riverbend Dairy has a beta of 0.85. The risk-free rate is 3.5% per annum and the market risk premium is 6% per annum. What is Riverbend's CAPM expected return?","options":["8.60%","5.63%","5.10%","9.50%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 6% is the premium, so use it directly: 3.5 + 0.85 × 6 = 8.60%. 5.63% = 3.5 + 0.85 × (6 − 3.5) treats 6% as the market return and subtracts R_F again (mrp-as-market). 5.10% = 0.85 × 6 leaves out R_F. 9.50% = 3.5 + 6 forgets to scale by β.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-412","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Okanagan Orchards has a beta of 0.85. The risk-free rate is 3.5% per annum and the market portfolio is expected to return 6% per annum. What is Okanagan's CAPM expected return?","options":["8.60%","5.10%","6.00%","5.63%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The 6% is the market return, not the premium, so the premium is 6 − 3.5 = 2.5% and E[R] = 3.5 + 0.85 × 2.5 = 5.625%, or 5.63%. 8.60% = 3.5 + 0.85 × 6 mistakes the market return for the premium. 5.10% = 0.85 × 6 makes that mistake and also drops R_F. 6.00% is just the market return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-15"},{"id":"fin201-q13-413","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rocky Mountain Telecom is expected to return 14.5% per annum. T-bills yield 4% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.5% per annum. If CAPM holds, what is its beta?","options":["2.23","1.62","4.20","0.62"],"answer":1,"explanation":"β = (E[R] − R_F)/MRP = (14.5 − 4)/6.5 = 10.5/6.5 = 1.615, or 1.62. 2.23 = 14.5/6.5 forgets to subtract R_F. 4.20 = 10.5/(6.5 − 4) subtracts R_F from a premium that already excludes it. 0.62 = 6.5/10.5 inverts the ratio.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-414","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"A pension fund wants a portfolio with an expected return of 10.5% per annum. T-bills yield 3.0% per annum and the market is expected to return 9.0% per annum. What portfolio beta does CAPM require?","options":["1.75","0.83","1.25","1.17"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Required premium = 10.5 − 3.0 = 7.5%; market risk premium = 9.0 − 3.0 = 6.0%; β = 7.5/6.0 = 1.25. 1.75 = 10.5/6.0 skips subtracting R_F from the target. 0.83 = 7.5/9.0 divides by the market return instead of the premium. 1.17 = 10.5/9.0 ignores R_F in both places.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-415","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which statement best describes the capital asset pricing model (CAPM)?","options":["A stock's expected return equals the risk-free rate plus its beta times the market risk premium, so only systematic risk is rewarded.","A stock's expected return rises with its standard deviation, because investors are paid for total risk.","Every stock earns the expected return of the market portfolio, whatever its risk.","A stock's expected return equals the risk-free rate plus its standard deviation times the market risk premium."],"answer":0,"explanation":"CAPM: E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F). Only systematic risk, measured by β, is rewarded because company-specific risk can be diversified away. The versions that use σ confuse total risk with systematic risk, and the claim that every stock earns the market return ignores risk altogether.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s55"},{"id":"fin201-q13-416","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"You must find the required return on one individual stock from its beta, the risk-free rate and the market's expected return. Which relationship do you use?","options":["The Sharpe ratio, (E[R_P] − R_F)/σ_P","The capital allocation line, E[R] = w E[R_P] + (1 − w) R_F","The two-asset portfolio standard deviation formula","The CAPM, E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A required return for a single asset from its beta is the CAPM (the equation of the SML). The Sharpe ratio scores a portfolio's reward per unit of total risk, the CAL mixes a portfolio with the risk-free asset, and the portfolio formula measures risk, not required return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s55"},{"id":"fin201-q13-417","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"In E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F), which part is the reward for merely waiting, with no risk taken?","options":["β_i (E[R_M] − R_F), the reward for risk","R_F, the time value of money","E[R_M] − R_F, the market risk premium","β_i, the amount of systematic risk"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CAPM has three parts: the time value of money (R_F, the reward for waiting), the market risk premium (the reward for bearing an average amount of systematic risk) and beta (how much systematic risk this asset has).","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-418","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A tutor describes expected return as: (1) a reward for waiting, (2) the reward the market pays for an average amount of systematic risk, (3) how much systematic risk this particular stock carries. Which CAPM terms match (1), (2) and (3) in that order?","options":["R_F; β_i; E[R_M] − R_F","E[R_M]; E[R_M] − R_F; σ_i","R_F; E[R_M] − R_F; β_i","R_F; σ_i; E[R_M]"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The professor's three parts are the time value of money (R_F), the market risk premium (E[R_M] − R_F) and the amount of systematic risk (β_i). Standard deviation is total risk and is not a CAPM input.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s55"},{"id":"fin201-q13-419","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A question states: 'the market risk premium is 6%.' For a stock with beta β and risk-free rate R_F, which CAPM expression is correct?","options":["R_F + β × 6%","R_F + β × (6% − R_F)","β × 6%","6% + β × R_F"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The market risk premium is E[R_M] − R_F, so 6% replaces the whole bracket: E[R_i] = R_F + β × 6%. Writing R_F + β × (6% − R_F) subtracts R_F a second time, the mrp-as-market error.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-420","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A question states: 'the market is expected to return 6%.' For a stock with beta β and risk-free rate R_F, which CAPM expression is correct?","options":["R_F + β × 6%","β × (6% − R_F)","6% + β × R_F","R_F + β × (6% − R_F)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"6% is E[R_M], the market's total expected return, so the premium is 6% − R_F and E[R_i] = R_F + β × (6% − R_F). Multiplying β by the 6% directly treats the market return as if it were the premium.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-17"},{"id":"fin201-q13-421","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Rearranging E[R_i] = R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F) for beta gives β_i =","options":["E[R_i] ÷ (E[R_M] − R_F)","(E[R_i] − R_F) ÷ (E[R_M] − R_F)","(E[R_M] − R_F) ÷ (E[R_i] − R_F)","E[R_i] ÷ E[R_M]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Subtract R_F from both sides, then divide by the market risk premium: β = (E[R_i] − R_F)/(E[R_M] − R_F), the ratio of the stock's premium to the market's premium. Skipping the R_F subtraction in the numerator is the forgot-minus-rf error; flipping the fraction inverts beta.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-422","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock's expected return, beta and the risk-free rate are known. The market risk premium that CAPM implies is","options":["E[R_i] ÷ β_i","(E[R_i] − R_F) × β_i","(E[R_i] − R_F) ÷ β_i","E[R_i] − R_F"],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R_i] − R_F = β_i × MRP, so MRP = (E[R_i] − R_F)/β_i. This is the stock's reward-to-risk ratio, which in equilibrium equals the market risk premium. Multiplying by β gives the stock's own premium instead.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-423","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":3,"stem":"From a stock's E[R_i], β_i and the risk-free rate R_F, the market's expected return is E[R_M] =","options":["R_F + (E[R_i] − R_F) ÷ β_i","(E[R_i] − R_F) ÷ β_i","R_F + β_i (E[R_i] − R_F)","E[R_i] ÷ β_i"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The market return is R_F plus the market risk premium, and the premium is the stock's premium divided by β. (E[R_i] − R_F) ÷ β_i alone stops at the premium (mrp-as-market); R_F + β_i (E[R_i] − R_F) multiplies by β instead of dividing.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-17"},{"id":"fin201-q13-424","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock has beta 1.4. If the market risk premium rises by 1 percentage point and nothing else changes, its CAPM expected return rises by","options":["1.0 percentage point","0.4 percentage point","2.4 percentage points","1.4 percentage points"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The expected return is R_F + β × MRP, so a 1-point rise in MRP adds β × 1 = 1.4 points. Only a stock with β = 1 moves one for one with the premium.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-425","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"CAPM: E[R_i] = R_F + β_i × (_____)","options":["E[R_M]","E[R_M] − R_F","E[R_M] + R_F","E[R_i] − R_F"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bracket is the market risk premium, E[R_M] − R_F: the extra return the market portfolio offers over the risk-free asset. Multiplying β by E[R_M] alone ignores the time value of money already in the market return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s55"},{"id":"fin201-q13-426","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"A classmate writes E[R_stock] = R_F + (σ_stock ÷ σ_M)(E[R_M] − R_F) for one individual stock. What is wrong with it?","options":["Nothing: σ and β are interchangeable once you divide by σ_M.","The market's σ_M should be squared to σ_M² for a single stock.","It is the CML, valid only for efficient portfolios; a single stock needs β, not σ.","The risk-free rate should not appear in a single stock's formula."],"answer":2,"explanation":"The CML, E[R_P] = R_F + (σ_P/σ_M)(E[R_M] − R_F), applies to efficient portfolios, whose risk is all systematic. A single stock also carries diversifiable risk inside its σ, so its required return depends on β only (CAPM/SML). This is the cml-on-single-stock error.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s42-57"},{"id":"fin201-q13-427","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a component of a stock's CAPM expected return?","options":["A premium for the company's own unsystematic (diversifiable) risk","The risk-free rate (time value of money)","The market risk premium","The stock's beta (amount of systematic risk)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CAPM has exactly three parts: the time value of money, the market risk premium and the amount of systematic risk. Company-specific risk can be diversified away, so the market pays nothing for it.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-428","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does CAPM pay no extra expected return for a stock's company-specific risk?","options":["It is always much smaller than market risk, so it is not worth pricing.","It cannot be measured for individual stocks, so no premium can be set.","It is already included in the risk-free rate that every investor earns.","Investors can diversify it away almost free, so nobody is paid to bear it."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Unsystematic risk washes out in a diversified portfolio, so no investor needs to bear it. Only systematic risk, which diversification cannot remove, earns a premium. The size of the risk and our ability to measure it are beside the point.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-50"},{"id":"fin201-q13-429","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A mining stock has a standard deviation of 45% but a beta of 0. T-bills yield 3.5% and the market is expected to return 9.5%. What does CAPM give as the stock's expected return, and why?","options":["9.5%: every stock earns the market return.","3.5%: it has no systematic risk, so it earns only the time value of money.","Well above 9.5%: a 45% standard deviation demands a large premium.","0%: zero beta means the stock earns nothing."],"answer":1,"explanation":"With β = 0, E[R] = R_F + 0 × (9.5 − 3.5) = 3.5%. The stock may be very volatile, but none of that volatility moves with the market, so a diversified investor is not paid for it.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-52"},{"id":"fin201-q13-430","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"A gold stock has a beta of −0.3. T-bills yield 4.0% and the market risk premium is 6.2% per annum. What does CAPM imply?","options":["Expected return −1.86%, the negative risk premium alone.","Expected return 4.00%, since negative betas earn the risk-free rate.","Expected return 2.14%, below R_F, because the stock hedges market risk.","Nothing: CAPM cannot be used with a negative beta."],"answer":2,"explanation":"E[R] = 4.0 + (−0.3)(6.2) = 4.0 − 1.86 = 2.14%. A negative beta moves against the market, so holding it hedges systematic risk and investors accept less than R_F. Quoting −1.86% forgets the time value of money.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-52"},{"id":"fin201-q13-431","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"The risk-free rate rises by 1 percentage point and the market risk premium is unchanged. According to CAPM, what happens to expected returns?","options":["Every stock's expected return rises by 1 percentage point, whatever its beta.","High-beta stocks rise by more than 1 point.","Expected returns stay the same; only the market portfolio changes.","Low-beta stocks rise by 1 point and high-beta stocks do not change."],"answer":0,"explanation":"E[R_i] = R_F + β_i × MRP. If MRP is unchanged, only the R_F term moves, so every expected return shifts up by the same 1 point and the SML moves up in parallel. A stock's beta multiplies the premium, not R_F.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-432","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"The market risk premium and the expected return on the market portfolio are two names for the same number.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The market risk premium is E[R_M] − R_F. With E[R_M] = 9.5% and R_F = 3.5%, the premium is 6.0%, a different number. Mixing the two is the most common CAPM trap.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16"},{"id":"fin201-q13-433","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"With R_F = 4% and a market risk premium of 6%, doubling a stock's beta from 0.8 to 1.6 doubles its risk premium (4.8% to 9.6%) but does not double its expected return (8.8% to 13.6%).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The premium is β × MRP, so it scales with β: 0.8 × 6 = 4.8 and 1.6 × 6 = 9.6. The expected return adds R_F = 4% to the premium, so it moves from 8.8% to 13.6%, not to 17.6%.","source":"generated","ref":"TB Ex 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-434","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"CAPM can be applied to a portfolio by using the portfolio beta, β_P = Σ w_i β_i (with β = 0 for the risk-free asset), in place of β_i.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Portfolio beta is the weighted average of the betas, and the portfolio's expected return is R_F + β_P × MRP. The risk-free asset counts in the weights with β = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-435","concept":"fin201-c13-capm","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"To compute a stock's CAPM expected return from R_F, E[R_M] and β, which sequence of steps is correct?","options":["Multiply β by E[R_M]; then add R_F.","Add R_F to E[R_M]; then multiply by β.","Multiply E[R_M] − R_F by β; then subtract R_F.","Subtract R_F from E[R_M]; multiply the result by β; add R_F."],"answer":3,"explanation":"First find the market risk premium (E[R_M] − R_F), then scale it by the amount of systematic risk (β), then add back the time value of money (R_F). Each wrong sequence mishandles R_F.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s17"},{"id":"fin201-q13-501","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Bluewater Biotech is expected to return 10.6% per annum. Its beta is 1.10, T-bills yield 2.8% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.4% per annum. Alpha is the expected return minus the return the SML requires for the stock's beta. What is Bluewater's alpha, in % per annum?","answer":0.76,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"10.6-(2.8+1.1*6.4)","steps":[{"label":"SML return R_F + β × MRP","value":9.84,"check":"2.8+1.1*6.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Alpha = E[R] − SML return","value":0.76,"check":"10.6-(2.8+1.1*6.4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["SML return = R_F + β × MRP = 2.8 + 1.10 × 6.4 = 2.8 + 7.04 = 9.84%","α = E[R] − SML return = 10.6 − 9.84 = +0.76% per annum","Positive alpha: the stock plots above the SML, so it is undervalued"],"keystrokes":["1.1 × 6.4 = 7.04","+ 2.8 = 9.84","10.6 − 9.84 = 0.76"],"errors":[{"id":"alpha-sign","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order (SML return − E[R])","value":-0.76,"check":"(2.8+1.1*6.4)-10.6","explain":"Alpha is what the stock offers minus what the SML requires. Reversing the order turns a bargain into an apparent overpriced stock."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.4% as the market return and subtracted R_F again","value":3.84,"check":"10.6-(2.8+1.1*(6.4-2.8))","explain":"The question gives the market risk premium, already E[R_M] − R_F. Subtracting R_F a second time lowers the SML return and inflates alpha."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left R_F out of the SML return","value":3.56,"check":"10.6-1.1*6.4","explain":"The SML return is R_F plus the risk premium, not the premium alone."}],"explanation":"The SML says a stock with β = 1.10 should offer 9.84%. Bluewater offers 10.6%, so alpha is +0.76%. It plots above the line: undervalued, and buying pressure should push its price up until the expected return falls to 9.84%.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-502","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeshore Hydro has a beta of 0.70 and is expected to return 6.4% per annum. T-bills yield 3.0% per annum and the market risk premium is 5.5% per annum. What is Lakeshore's alpha (expected return minus SML return), in % per annum?","answer":-0.45,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"6.4-(3+0.7*5.5)","steps":[{"label":"SML return R_F + β × MRP","value":6.85,"check":"3+0.7*5.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Alpha = E[R] − SML return","value":-0.45,"check":"6.4-(3+0.7*5.5)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["SML return = 3.0 + 0.70 × 5.5 = 3.0 + 3.85 = 6.85%","α = 6.4 − 6.85 = −0.45% per annum","Negative alpha: the stock plots below the SML, so it is overvalued"],"keystrokes":["0.7 × 5.5 = 3.85","+ 3 = 6.85","6.4 − 6.85 = −0.45"],"errors":[{"id":"alpha-sign","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":0.45,"check":"6.85-6.4","explain":"Alpha = expected return − SML return. The stock offers less than its β requires, so alpha is negative."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 5.5% as the market return","value":1.65,"check":"6.4-(3+0.7*(5.5-3))","explain":"5.5% is already the premium, so do not subtract R_F again."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left R_F out of the SML return","value":2.55,"check":"6.4-0.7*5.5","explain":"The SML return includes the 3.0% risk-free rate."}],"explanation":"A beta of 0.70 earns 6.85% on the SML, but Lakeshore offers only 6.4%. Alpha is −0.45%: below the line, overvalued. Its price should fall until the expected return rises to 6.85%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-503","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Peace River Gas has a beta of 1.30 and is expected to return 11.5% per annum. T-bills yield 3.0% per annum. What is its reward-to-risk ratio, (E[R] − R_F)/β, in % per unit of beta?","answer":6.54,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(11.5-3)/1.3","steps":[{"label":"Risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":8.5,"check":"11.5-3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Reward-to-risk (premium ÷ β)","value":6.54,"check":"(11.5-3)/1.3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Reward-to-risk = (E[R] − R_F)/β","= (11.5 − 3.0)/1.30 = 8.5/1.30 = 6.54% per unit of beta"],"keystrokes":["11.5 − 3 = 8.5","÷ 1.3 = 6.54"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the total return by β","value":8.85,"check":"11.5/1.3","explain":"Reward-to-risk uses the premium E[R] − R_F in the numerator, not the total return."},{"id":"multiplied-by-beta","label":"Multiplied the premium by β","value":11.05,"check":"(11.5-3)*1.3","explain":"The ratio divides the premium by β, giving premium per unit of systematic risk."},{"id":"forgot-divide-beta","label":"Stopped at the risk premium","value":8.5,"check":"11.5-3","explain":"8.5% is the premium for 1.30 units of beta. Divide by β to get the premium per unit."}],"explanation":"Peace River earns 8.5% over T-bills for 1.30 units of systematic risk, or 6.54% per unit. If the market risk premium is 6.0%, this ratio is higher than the market's, so the stock plots above the SML (undervalued).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-504","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Chinook Brewing trades at $50.00. You expect a $1.20 dividend and a price of $53.50 in one year. Its beta is 1.20, T-bills yield 3.0% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.5% per annum. What price today would put Chinook exactly on the SML? Answer in $.","answer":49.37,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"(53.5+1.2)/(1+(3+1.2*6.5)/100)","steps":[{"label":"Expected return at $50.00","value":9.4,"check":"((53.5+1.2)/50-1)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"SML (required) return","value":10.8,"check":"3+1.2*6.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Fair price = payoff ÷ (1 + SML return)","value":49.37,"check":"(53.5+1.2)/(1+(3+1.2*6.5)/100)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["Expected return at $50.00 = (53.50 + 1.20)/50 − 1 = 9.4%","SML return = 3.0 + 1.20 × 6.5 = 10.8%, so alpha = 9.4 − 10.8 = −1.4% (below the SML: overvalued)","Fair price = (53.50 + 1.20)/1.108 = 54.70/1.108 = $49.37"],"keystrokes":["(53.5 + 1.2) = 54.7","3 + 1.2 × 6.5 = 10.8","54.7 ÷ 1.108 = 49.37"],"errors":[{"id":"discounted-at-expected-return","label":"Discounted at the stock's own expected return","value":50.0,"check":"(53.5+1.2)/(1+((53.5+1.2)/50-1))","explain":"Discounting at the 9.4% the stock currently offers just reproduces today's $50.00. The fair price uses the 10.8% the SML requires."},{"id":"mrp-as-discount-rate","label":"Discounted at the market risk premium","value":51.36,"check":"(53.5+1.2)/(1+6.5/100)","explain":"6.5% is the premium, not the required return for β = 1.20. Required return = R_F + β × MRP."},{"id":"forgot-dividend","label":"Left the dividend out of the payoff","value":48.29,"check":"53.5/(1+(3+1.2*6.5)/100)","explain":"The payoff next year is price plus dividend, $54.70."}],"explanation":"At $50.00 the stock offers 9.4% but the SML requires 10.8%, so it is overvalued. The price must fall until the payoff yields 10.8%: $54.70/1.108 = $49.37. Once priced there, its alpha is zero.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s24 / L04"},{"id":"fin201-q13-505","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock is expected to return 10.4% per annum. T-bills yield 3.0% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.5% per annum. Up to what beta does the stock plot on or above the SML (alpha at least zero)? Give beta to 2 decimals.","answer":1.14,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(10.4-3)/6.5","steps":[{"label":"Stock risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":7.4,"check":"10.4-3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Break-even beta = premium ÷ MRP","value":1.14,"check":"(10.4-3)/6.5","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Alpha = 0 when E[R] = R_F + β × MRP, so β* = (E[R] − R_F)/MRP","= (10.4 − 3.0)/6.5 = 7.4/6.5 = 1.138","Below β = 1.14 the stock plots above the SML (alpha > 0); above it, below the SML"],"keystrokes":["10.4 − 3 = 7.4","÷ 6.5 = 1.14"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the total return by the premium","value":1.6,"check":"10.4/6.5","explain":"Subtract R_F from the expected return first."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Subtracted R_F from the premium","value":2.11,"check":"(10.4-3)/(6.5-3)","explain":"6.5% is already E[R_M] − R_F."},{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":0.88,"check":"6.5/(10.4-3)","explain":"Break-even beta is the stock's premium over the market's premium."}],"explanation":"At β* the stock's reward-to-risk equals the market premium and its alpha is zero. Any stock offering 10.4% is a bargain if its true β is below 1.14 and overpriced if above, which is why a beta estimate matters.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-16 / S7 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-506","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Stocks A and B both lie on the SML. Stock A has beta 1.40 and expected return 10.9% per annum; Stock B has beta 0.70 and expected return 7.05% per annum. What is the slope of the SML, in % per unit of beta?","answer":5.5,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(10.9-7.05)/(1.4-0.7)","steps":[{"label":"Rise (return gap)","value":3.85,"check":"10.9-7.05","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Slope = rise ÷ run (beta gap)","value":5.5,"check":"(10.9-7.05)/(1.4-0.7)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Slope = rise/run = (E[R_A] − E[R_B])/(β_A − β_B)","= (10.9 − 7.05)/(1.40 − 0.70) = 3.85/0.70 = 5.5% per unit of beta","Since β_M = 1 and β_F = 0, the slope is the market risk premium E[R_M] − R_F = 5.5%"],"keystrokes":["10.9 − 7.05 = 3.85","1.4 − 0.7 = 0.7","3.85 ÷ 0.7 = 5.5"],"errors":[{"id":"inverted-slope","label":"Computed run over rise","value":0.18,"check":"(1.4-0.7)/(10.9-7.05)","explain":"Expected return is on the vertical axis, so slope = return gap divided by beta gap."},{"id":"forgot-beta-gap","label":"Forgot to divide by the beta gap","value":3.85,"check":"10.9-7.05","explain":"3.85% is the return gap for a beta gap of 0.70. Divide to get the premium per unit of beta."},{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided one stock's total return by its beta","value":7.79,"check":"10.9/1.4","explain":"That ratio still contains R_F. The slope uses differences, which cancel R_F."}],"explanation":"Two points on a line fix its slope. The SML runs from (β = 0, R_F) to (β = 1, E[R_M]), so its slope equals the market risk premium: 5.5% per unit of beta.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-30 / S7 s23"},{"id":"fin201-q13-507","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":["fin201-c13-portfolio-beta"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Aria holds 60% of her stock portfolio in Stock 1 (beta 1.30, expected return 11.2% per annum) and 40% in Stock 2 (beta 0.80, expected return 8.4% per annum). T-bills yield 3.0% and the market risk premium is 6.0% per annum. What is the portfolio's alpha, in % per annum?","answer":0.48,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(0.6*11.2+0.4*8.4)-(3+(0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8)*6)","steps":[{"label":"Portfolio expected return Σ w E[R]","value":10.08,"check":"0.6*11.2+0.4*8.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Portfolio beta Σ w β","value":1.1,"check":"0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"SML return for β_P","value":9.6,"check":"3+(0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8)*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Alpha = E[R_P] − SML return","value":0.48,"check":"(0.6*11.2+0.4*8.4)-(3+(0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8)*6)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R_P] = 0.6 × 11.2 + 0.4 × 8.4 = 6.72 + 3.36 = 10.08%","β_P = 0.6 × 1.30 + 0.4 × 0.80 = 0.78 + 0.32 = 1.10","SML return = 3.0 + 1.10 × 6.0 = 9.6%; α_P = 10.08 − 9.6 = +0.48% per annum","Check: stock alphas are 11.2 − 10.8 = +0.4 and 8.4 − 7.8 = +0.6, so 0.6 × 0.4 + 0.4 × 0.6 = 0.48"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 11.2 + 0.4 × 8.4 = 10.08","0.6 × 1.3 + 0.4 × 0.8 = 1.10","3 + 1.10 × 6 = 9.6","10.08 − 9.6 = 0.48"],"errors":[{"id":"alpha-sign","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":-0.48,"check":"(3+(0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8)*6)-(0.6*11.2+0.4*8.4)","explain":"Alpha is the portfolio's expected return minus the SML return for its beta."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.0% as the market return","value":3.78,"check":"(0.6*11.2+0.4*8.4)-(3+(0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8)*(6-3))","explain":"The 6.0% is the premium, so do not subtract R_F again."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left R_F out of the SML return","value":3.48,"check":"(0.6*11.2+0.4*8.4)-(0.6*1.3+0.4*0.8)*6","explain":"The SML return for the portfolio is R_F + β_P × MRP."}],"explanation":"A portfolio plots on the SML at its weighted-average beta, so its alpha is the weighted average of the stock alphas. The portfolio earns 10.08% against the 9.6% required: +0.48%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-13 / S7 s19-24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-508","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cedar Valley Foods has a beta of 0.60 and is expected to return 7.5% per annum. T-bills yield 3.0% and the market risk premium is 5.5% per annum. What is its alpha?","options":["−1.20%","+1.20%","+3.00%","+4.20%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SML return = 3.0 + 0.60 × 5.5 = 6.3%, so alpha = 7.5 − 6.3 = +1.20% (above the SML, undervalued). −1.20% reverses the subtraction (alpha-sign). +3.00% subtracts R_F from the premium first (mrp-as-market: 7.5 − 4.5). +4.20% leaves R_F out of the SML return (7.5 − 3.3).","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-509","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"T-bills yield 4% and the market is expected to return 10% per annum. Stock X has beta 1.2 and expected return 11.0%; Stock Y has beta 0.5 and expected return 7.5%; Stock Z has beta 1.0 and expected return 10.0%. Which stock is undervalued?","options":["Stock X","Stock Y","Stock Z","Stocks X and Y"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SML returns: X = 4 + 1.2 × 6 = 11.2% (offers 11.0%, alpha −0.2); Y = 4 + 0.5 × 6 = 7.0% (offers 7.5%, alpha +0.5); Z = 4 + 1.0 × 6 = 10.0% (offers 10.0%, alpha 0). X has the highest raw return but also the highest beta, so it still plots below the line; Z sits on it.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-510","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Alderbrook Mills trades at $40.00. You expect a $1.00 dividend and a price of $42.60 in one year. The SML return for its beta is 8.0% per annum. What is its alpha?","options":["−1.00%","−1.50%","−5.50%","+1.00%"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Expected return = (42.60 + 1.00)/40.00 − 1 = 9.0%, so alpha = 9.0 − 8.0 = +1.0%. −1.00% reverses the order. −1.50% leaves out the dividend (6.5 − 8.0). −5.50% uses only the 2.5% dividend yield (2.5 − 8.0).","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s24 / L04 s6"},{"id":"fin201-q13-511","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the SML framework, a stock's alpha is","options":["its expected return minus the return the SML requires for its beta","its beta minus the market's beta of 1","its expected return minus the market's expected return","the slope of the SML"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alpha = E[R_i] − [R_F + β_i (E[R_M] − R_F)]: the vertical distance between the stock and the SML. It is not a beta difference, a comparison with the market's return, or the line's slope (which is the market risk premium).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s57"},{"id":"fin201-q13-512","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Zenith Rail has a beta of 0.90 and is expected to return 8.9% per annum. The SML return for a beta of 0.90 is 7.8%. Where does Zenith plot, and what does that mean?","options":["Above the SML: overvalued, alpha +1.1%","Below the SML: undervalued, alpha −1.1%","Above the SML: undervalued, alpha +1.1%","On the SML: fairly priced"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Zenith offers 1.1 points more than the SML pays for its beta, so it plots above the line: alpha +1.1%, undervalued, and its price should rise. Above the SML never means overvalued.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s57"},{"id":"fin201-q13-513","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Ironwood Steel is expected to return 11.2% per annum, but the SML return for its beta is 12.0%. This stock is","options":["undervalued: it plots below the SML","overvalued: it plots below the SML with alpha −0.8%","overvalued: it plots above the SML","fairly priced, because 11.2% is far above the risk-free rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The stock offers 0.8 points less than the SML requires for its risk, so alpha = −0.8%, it plots below the line and is overvalued; selling pressure should lower the price until the expected return rises to 12.0%.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-514","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which pairing is correct?","options":["Plots above the SML = overvalued = positive alpha = price expected to fall","Plots below the SML = undervalued = negative alpha = price expected to rise","Plots below the SML = overvalued = positive alpha = price expected to fall","Plots above the SML = undervalued = positive alpha = price expected to rise"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Above the SML a stock offers more than its risk requires: positive alpha, undervalued, buyers push the price up. Below the SML it offers too little: negative alpha, overvalued, price falls. The other pairings mix up the signs.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-515","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which line plots expected return against beta and applies to every individual asset?","options":["The security market line","The capital market line","The capital allocation line","The efficient frontier"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The SML has β on the horizontal axis and holds for any asset or portfolio. The CML and CAL put σ on the horizontal axis (CML only for efficient portfolios) and the efficient frontier plots portfolios by σ.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s56-57"},{"id":"fin201-q13-516","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which statement is NOT true of a stock that plots exactly on the SML?","options":["Its alpha is zero.","Its reward-to-risk ratio equals the market risk premium.","Its price is too low for its risk.","Its expected return equals R_F + β (E[R_M] − R_F)."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A stock on the SML offers exactly what its beta requires: alpha zero, reward-to-risk equal to the market's, and expected return given by CAPM. A price that is too low would show up as a return above the line.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-517","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why must every fairly priced asset have the same reward-to-risk ratio (E[R] − R_F)/β?","options":["Because all assets have the same beta once they are held in a diversified portfolio.","If one paid more per unit of beta, buyers would bid its price up and its return down until the ratios matched.","Because the risk-free rate is the same for every asset, so the premiums per unit of beta must match.","Because unsystematic risk is the same for every asset, so only beta is left to differ."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Competition removes any difference in premium per unit of systematic risk: a better ratio attracts buyers, bidding the price up and the expected return down. That equality is exactly what makes all assets lie on one straight line, the SML.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-518","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock is expected to return 9.0% per annum, while the SML return for its beta is 8.1%. What should happen over time?","options":["Supply pushes its price down, raising its expected return toward 8.1%.","Nothing: expected returns do not depend on today's price.","Its beta must rise until the SML return reaches 9.0%.","Demand pushes its price up, lowering its expected return toward 8.1%."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Above the SML means undervalued. Buyers bid the price up; a higher price today with the same future payoffs means a lower expected return, until the stock sits on the line. Prices adjust, not betas.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-519","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Peace River Gas has a reward-to-risk ratio of 6.54% per unit of beta, while the market risk premium is 6.0%. What does this tell you?","options":["It plots above the SML: undervalued with positive alpha.","It plots below the SML: overvalued with negative alpha.","It plots on the SML: fairly priced.","Its beta must be above 1."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The market's reward-to-risk ratio is the premium 6.0% (β_M = 1). A ratio above 6.0% means more reward per unit of beta than the market pays, so the stock is above the line and undervalued. The ratio says nothing directly about whether beta exceeds 1.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-520","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"The slope of the security market line equals","options":["the risk-free rate, R_F","the expected market return, E[R_M]","the market risk premium, E[R_M] − R_F","the market's beta, β_M"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rise over run between the points (β = 0, R_F) and (β = 1, E[R_M]): (E[R_M] − R_F)/(1 − 0) = the market risk premium. The intercept is R_F; the market's beta of 1 is just one of the x-values.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s23"},{"id":"fin201-q13-521","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"If the market risk premium rises while the risk-free rate is unchanged, the SML","options":["shifts up in parallel","gets steeper, pivoting around its intercept R_F","gets flatter","keeps its slope but its intercept falls"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The slope of the SML is the market risk premium, so a larger premium makes it steeper. The intercept is R_F, which did not change, so the line pivots at R_F. A parallel shift would come from a change in R_F alone.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7"},{"id":"fin201-q13-522","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"The SML passes through the points (β = 0, _____) and (β = 1, E[R_M]).","options":["E[R_M]","the market risk premium","alpha","R_F"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A zero-beta asset has no systematic risk and earns only the risk-free rate, so the SML starts at R_F on the vertical axis. At β = 1 the line gives the market's expected return.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s23"},{"id":"fin201-q13-523","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock with a positive alpha is overvalued.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Positive alpha means the stock offers more than the SML requires, so it plots above the line and is undervalued. Overvalued stocks have negative alpha.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s24"},{"id":"fin201-q13-524","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock that plots below the SML offers an expected return that is too low for its beta, so its price is expected to fall.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Below the SML means overvalued (negative alpha). Selling pressure lowers the price, which raises the expected return until the stock is back on the line.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-525","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"T-bills yield 3% and the market risk premium is 6%. Stock A (beta 1.8) is expected to return 12.0%; Stock B (beta 0.9) is expected to return 9.0%. Which is the better buy, and why?","options":["Stock B: its alpha is +0.6% versus −1.8% for A.","Stock A: it has the higher expected return.","Neither: both plot on the SML.","Stock A: its higher beta means a higher reward."],"answer":0,"explanation":"SML returns: A = 3 + 1.8 × 6 = 13.8% (alpha −1.8); B = 3 + 0.9 × 6 = 8.4% (alpha +0.6). Compare each stock with the return its own beta requires, not with the other stock's raw return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-526","concept":"fin201-c13-sml-alpha","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Chloe's analysis finds that a stock has a positive alpha. What should she conclude?","options":["It is overvalued and should be sold.","Nothing, because alpha only matters when beta equals 1.","It is undervalued: it plots above the SML, so its price should rise.","It must have a lower beta than the market."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Positive alpha means the stock plots above the SML, offering extra return for its level of systematic risk. That is a bargain, and buyers should lift the price. Alpha matters at any beta, and says nothing alone about whether beta is below 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s57-59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-601","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which pair of statements correctly describes the two risk measures we use?","options":["σ measures systematic risk; β measures total risk.","σ measures company-specific risk; β measures market risk.","Both measure systematic risk, in different units.","σ measures total risk; β measures systematic (market) risk only."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Standard deviation captures all the variability of a stock's return: systematic plus unsystematic. Beta captures only the part that moves with the market, which diversification cannot remove.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q13-602","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which risk measure feeds into the CAPM when we compute a stock's expected return?","options":["σ, the total risk","β, the amount of systematic risk","ρ, the correlation with the market","The variance of the stock's returns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CAPM prices only systematic risk, so beta is the input: E[R] = R_F + β (E[R_M] − R_F). Standard deviation and variance include diversifiable risk, and correlation alone ignores how volatile the stock is relative to the market.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s58"},{"id":"fin201-q13-603","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Quebec Gold Corp. has σ = 52% and β = 0.2. Metro Grocer has σ = 18% and β = 1.1. Which stock is riskier for a well-diversified investor, and why?","options":["Metro Grocer: it has the higher β, so it contributes more systematic risk.","Quebec Gold: it has the higher σ, so it contributes more risk.","They are equally risky because both are single stocks.","Quebec Gold: a low β means the stock is hard to diversify."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A diversified investor has already removed company-specific risk, so what matters is how much each stock adds to the portfolio's market risk: β. Quebec Gold's large σ is mostly diversifiable; Metro Grocer's β of 1.1 means more systematic risk and a higher required return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-604","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How does σ differ from β as a measure of a stock's risk?","options":["β includes the diversifiable part of the risk; σ captures only the part that cannot be diversified away.","σ is used for stocks and β for bonds.","σ includes the diversifiable part of the risk; β captures only the part that cannot be diversified away.","β is bounded between −1 and 1 like ρ, but σ is not."],"answer":2,"explanation":"Total risk (σ) = systematic risk + unsystematic risk. Beta isolates the systematic part by measuring sensitivity to the market. Beta is not bounded between −1 and 1 (that is ρ), and both measures apply to any asset.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s50"},{"id":"fin201-q13-605","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following statements is NOT true?","options":["A stock with β = 0 can still be volatile.","A stock's σ includes risk that diversification can remove.","Total risk is made up of systematic risk plus unsystematic risk.","A stock with a higher σ must have a higher CAPM expected return."],"answer":3,"explanation":"CAPM ties expected return to β, not σ. A high-σ stock with a low β can have a low expected return, because most of its risk is diversifiable. The other three statements are true.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s58"},{"id":"fin201-q13-606","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does CAPM use β rather than σ to set a stock's expected return?","options":["β is much easier to estimate from market data than σ is.","Diversified investors have removed company-specific risk, so only systematic risk is rewarded.","σ is always smaller than β, so it would understate the reward for risk.","σ can only be defined for portfolios, not for single stocks."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Investors can diversify away the unsystematic part of σ for free, so the market does not pay for it. What remains is systematic risk, measured by β. Ease of computation and the relative size of σ and β are irrelevant.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s58-59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-607","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A lawsuit specific to Prairie Lumber doubles the stock's standard deviation but leaves its beta unchanged. What happens to its CAPM required return?","options":["It is unchanged: the extra risk is diversifiable and unpriced.","It doubles.","It rises, but by less than double.","It falls, because the stock became less correlated with the market."],"answer":0,"explanation":"CAPM required return depends on β only. A company-specific event changes unsystematic risk, which diversified investors do not need to bear, so the required return stays where it was (the stock's price may still fall on the bad news).","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-608","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"T-bills yield 3% and the market risk premium is 6%. Rank these stocks by CAPM expected return, highest first: Wolf Creek Energy (σ 40%, β 0.9), Muskeg Rail (σ 25%, β 1.4), Foothills Dairy (σ 12%, β 0.5).","options":["Wolf Creek Energy, Muskeg Rail, Foothills Dairy","Foothills Dairy, Muskeg Rail, Wolf Creek Energy","Muskeg Rail, Wolf Creek Energy, Foothills Dairy","Muskeg Rail, Foothills Dairy, Wolf Creek Energy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CAPM returns: Muskeg = 3 + 1.4 × 6 = 11.4%; Wolf Creek = 3 + 0.9 × 6 = 8.4%; Foothills = 3 + 0.5 × 6 = 6.0%. Ranking by σ (Wolf, Muskeg, Foothills) puts them in the wrong order because σ is not what the market rewards.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-609","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":["fin201-c13-beta","fin201-c13-capm"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Athabasca Mining has a standard deviation of 38% per annum and a correlation of 0.35 with the market portfolio, whose standard deviation is 16%. T-bills yield 3.5% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.0% per annum. What is Athabasca's CAPM expected return? Answer in % per annum.","answer":8.49,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"3.5+(0.35*38/16)*6","steps":[{"label":"Beta = ρ σ_i / σ_M","value":0.83,"check":"0.35*38/16","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"E[R] = R_F + β × MRP","value":8.49,"check":"3.5+(0.35*38/16)*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["β = ρ σ_i/σ_M = 0.35 × 38/16 = 0.8313","E[R] = R_F + β × MRP = 3.5 + 0.8313 × 6.0 = 3.5 + 4.9875","= 8.49% per annum (about 8.48% with β rounded to 0.83)"],"keystrokes":["0.35 × 38 ÷ 16 = 0.8313","× 6 = 4.9875","+ 3.5 = 8.4875"],"errors":[{"id":"rho-for-beta","label":"Used the correlation as the beta","value":5.6,"check":"3.5+0.35*6","explain":"ρ is bounded between −1 and 1 and ignores how volatile the stock is relative to the market. Beta = ρ × σ_i/σ_M."},{"id":"sigma-for-beta","label":"Used σ_i/σ_M as the beta","value":17.75,"check":"3.5+(38/16)*6","explain":"Dividing σ's gives the ratio of total risks, not systematic risk. Multiply by ρ to keep only the part that moves with the market."},{"id":"beta-sigma-i","label":"Inverted the σ ratio (ρ σ_M/σ_i)","value":4.38,"check":"3.5+(0.35*16/38)*6","explain":"Beta scales by the stock's σ over the market's σ, not the reverse."}],"explanation":"Athabasca is far more volatile than the market (38% vs 16%) but only 0.35 correlated with it, so its beta is 0.35 × 38/16 = 0.83: less systematic risk than the market. CAPM then gives a modest 8.49%, well below what its σ alone might suggest.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s14 / L06 s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-610","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"T-bills yield 3.2% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.4% per annum. Yukon Royalties has σ = 42% and β = 0.60. Metro Grocer has σ = 24% and β = 1.30. By how many percentage points is Metro Grocer's CAPM required return above Yukon Royalties'?","answer":4.48,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(3.2+1.3*6.4)-(3.2+0.6*6.4)","steps":[{"label":"Yukon required return","value":7.04,"check":"3.2+0.6*6.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Metro required return","value":11.52,"check":"3.2+1.3*6.4","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Difference (Metro − Yukon)","value":4.48,"check":"(3.2+1.3*6.4)-(3.2+0.6*6.4)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Yukon: 3.2 + 0.60 × 6.4 = 3.2 + 3.84 = 7.04%","Metro: 3.2 + 1.30 × 6.4 = 3.2 + 8.32 = 11.52%","Difference = 11.52 − 7.04 = 4.48 percentage points (equivalently (1.30 − 0.60) × 6.4)"],"keystrokes":["0.6 × 6.4 + 3.2 = 7.04","1.3 × 6.4 + 3.2 = 11.52","11.52 − 7.04 = 4.48"],"errors":[{"id":"sign-slip","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":-4.48,"check":"(3.2+0.6*6.4)-(3.2+1.3*6.4)","explain":"Metro has the higher beta, so its required return is the higher one."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.4% as the market return","value":2.24,"check":"(1.3-0.6)*(6.4-3.2)","explain":"6.4% is already the premium; do not subtract R_F again."},{"id":"sigma-for-beta","label":"Plugged each σ (as a decimal) into CAPM in place of β","value":-1.15,"check":"(24-42)/100*6.4","explain":"Yukon's larger σ is mostly company-specific risk. CAPM pays for β, and Metro's β is higher."}],"explanation":"The lower-σ stock earns the higher required return because it carries more systematic risk: the gap is simply the beta difference times the premium, 0.70 × 6.4 = 4.48 points. Total risk (σ) plays no role.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-611","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":["fin201-c13-capm"],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Frontier Mining earns 50% in a boom (probability 0.25), 10% in a normal economy (0.50) and −30% in a recession (0.25). T-bills yield 3.5% and the market risk premium is 6.0% per annum. If CAPM holds, what beta is consistent with Frontier's expected return? Give beta to 2 decimals.","answer":1.08,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(0.25*50+0.5*10+0.25*(-30)-3.5)/6","steps":[{"label":"Expected return Σ p R","value":10.0,"check":"0.25*50+0.5*10+0.25*(-30)","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Stock risk premium E[R] − R_F","value":6.5,"check":"0.25*50+0.5*10+0.25*(-30)-3.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Beta = premium ÷ MRP","value":1.08,"check":"(0.25*50+0.5*10+0.25*(-30)-3.5)/6","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["E[R] = 0.25 × 50 + 0.50 × 10 + 0.25 × (−30) = 12.5 + 5 − 7.5 = 10.0%","Premium = 10.0 − 3.5 = 6.5%","β = 6.5/6.0 = 1.083, so about 1.08"],"keystrokes":["0.25 × 50 + 0.5 × 10 + 0.25 × (−30) = 10","− 3.5 = 6.5","÷ 6 = 1.08"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the total expected return by the premium","value":1.67,"check":"10/6","explain":"Beta compares premiums: subtract R_F from the expected return first."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Subtracted R_F from the market risk premium","value":2.6,"check":"(10-3.5)/(6-3.5)","explain":"6.0% is already E[R_M] − R_F."},{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":0.92,"check":"6/(10-3.5)","explain":"Beta = stock premium ÷ market premium."}],"explanation":"Frontier's σ is huge (about 28%), but CAPM only needs its expected return: 10.0% implies a 6.5% premium, so β = 6.5/6.0 = 1.08. Most of its volatility must therefore be company-specific.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-612","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Steady Rail has σ = 7.07% and expected return 12.0%; Frontier Mining has σ = 28.28% and expected return 10.0%. T-bills yield 3.5% and the market risk premium is 6.0%. Assume CAPM holds. For a well-diversified investor, which stock is riskier?","options":["Frontier Mining: its σ is four times larger.","Frontier Mining: its lower expected return signals higher risk.","They are equally risky because both exceed the risk-free rate.","Steady Rail: its implied β is 1.42 versus 1.08."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Implied betas: Steady Rail (12.0 − 3.5)/6.0 = 1.42; Frontier Mining (10.0 − 3.5)/6.0 = 1.08. Frontier has more total and more unsystematic risk, but the diversifiable part is not rewarded, so Steady Rail is the riskier stock to hold in a diversified portfolio and earns the higher return.","source":"generated","ref":"SM 13-28"},{"id":"fin201-q13-613","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"A stock has σ = 12% while the market's σ_M is 16%. Why can its beta not exceed 1?","options":["Because beta can never exceed 1 for any stock.","β = ρ σ_i/σ_M and ρ ≤ 1, so β ≤ 12/16 = 0.75.","Because β = σ_i/σ_M exactly, which is 0.75.","Because a stock with a lower σ than the market has a negative correlation with it."],"answer":1,"explanation":"β = ρσ_i/σ_M. With ρ at most 1, β is at most σ_i/σ_M = 0.75 here (reached only if ρ = 1). Beta is not capped at 1 in general: a stock with σ_i = 40% and ρ = 0.8 has β = 2.0. The ratio of σ's is the ceiling, not the value.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-614","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A stock with a beta of 0 must have a standard deviation of 0.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Beta measures only systematic risk. A stock unrelated to the market (casino winnings in the lecture's example) has β = 0 but can still be very volatile; only the risk-free asset has both σ = 0 and β = 0.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s51-52"},{"id":"fin201-q13-615","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"A stock's total risk, measured by its standard deviation, is made up of systematic risk plus unsystematic risk.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Total risk = systematic (market, non-diversifiable) + unsystematic (company-specific, diversifiable). Diversification removes only the second part.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s22"},{"id":"fin201-q13-616","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"If the correlation ρ and the market's σ_M stay the same, doubling a stock's own σ_i changes its beta β = ρ σ_i/σ_M by","options":["doubling it","leaving it unchanged","halving it","quadrupling it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Beta is proportional to σ_i when ρ and σ_M are fixed, so doubling σ_i doubles β. Note that in practice a rise in σ_i from company-specific news would not change β, because ρ would fall by the same factor.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-617","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"T-bills yield 3.0% and the market risk premium is 6.0%. Stock A: expected return 9.5%, σ 15%, β 1.2. Stock B: expected return 9.5%, σ 30%, β 0.7. Which is the better addition to a well-diversified portfolio?","options":["Stock A: its lower σ makes it safer.","Neither: both offer the same expected return.","Stock B: its alpha is +2.3% (A's is −0.7%), despite the higher σ.","Stock A: its higher β means a higher reward."],"answer":2,"explanation":"SML returns: A = 3 + 1.2 × 6 = 10.2% (alpha −0.7); B = 3 + 0.7 × 6 = 7.2% (alpha +2.3). With the same expected return, the lower-β stock earns the bigger alpha. B's high σ is largely diversifiable.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s58-59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-618","concept":"fin201-c13-sigma-vs-beta","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":3,"stem":"How does beta differ from the correlation coefficient ρ with the market?","options":["β is bounded between −1 and 1; ρ is not.","They are the same number measured in different units.","ρ measures systematic risk; β measures total risk.","ρ is bounded between −1 and 1; β is not, because it also scales by σ_i/σ_M."],"answer":3,"explanation":"β = ρσ_i/σ_M: correlation times the stock's volatility relative to the market. A volatile stock with a high ρ can have β = 2 or more, whereas ρ can never leave [−1, 1].","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s53"},{"id":"fin201-q13-701","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"Which sequence summarizes the professor's guide to building a portfolio?","options":["First stock: low β. Next few stocks: high ρ. Adding to a diversified portfolio: low σ and negative α.","First stock: low σ. Next few stocks: low ρ. Adding to a diversified portfolio: low β and positive α.","First stock: high α. Next few stocks: low β. Adding to a diversified portfolio: low ρ.","Always pick the lowest σ at every stage."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each stage uses the risk measure that is still relevant. One stock: all risk is yours, so low σ. A few stocks: low correlation with what you hold diversifies fastest. Many stocks: only systematic risk remains, so look at β and reward it with positive α.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-702","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Marcus is buying the first stock he will ever own. Which feature should he favour?","options":["Low σ","Low β","Low ρ","Positive α"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a single stock, none of its risk is diversified away, so total risk (σ) is what he bears. Correlation needs a second stock to be defined, and β and α matter later once only systematic risk is left.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-703","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"Marcus owns Calgary Oilfield Services and now chooses his second to fourth stocks. Which feature should he favour?","options":["High σ","High β","Low ρ with the stocks he already owns","Low β only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"While the portfolio is small, diversifiable risk remains, and it falls fastest when new stocks move differently from existing holdings: low correlation. This is the diversification benefit whenever ρ < 1.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-704","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Marcus now owns 40 stocks. Which features should he look for in a new addition?","options":["Low σ and low ρ","High β and positive α","Low β and negative α","Low β and positive α"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A well-diversified portfolio's remaining risk is about σ_M, so a new stock matters through its β. Among low-β candidates, positive alpha means it pays more than the SML requires.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-705","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"How do the roles of ρ and β differ when building a portfolio?","options":["β matters for the first few stocks and ρ for the later ones.","ρ with your holdings matters while diversifiable risk remains; β matters once you are diversified.","ρ and β always give the same ranking of candidate stocks.","ρ is used for the risk-free asset and β for the risky stocks."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Early on, the question is how a stock co-moves with the stocks you already hold (ρ). Later, those stocks already span the market, so the question is how it co-moves with the market itself (β).","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-706","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which rule is NOT part of the professor's guide to building a portfolio?","options":["Add the highest-σ stock to a well-diversified portfolio because it offers the most return.","For the first stock, look for low σ.","For the next few stocks, look for low ρ.","For a diversified portfolio, look for low β and positive α."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Return is not driven by total risk, and a well-diversified portfolio is indifferent to a stock's σ except through β. The other three are the guide's three stages.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-707","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why does ρ matter less once a portfolio holds many stocks?","options":["Because correlations between stocks become zero in large portfolios.","Because σ keeps rising as more stocks are added.","The diversifiable risk is already gone, so a new stock matters mainly through its β.","Because β has no meaning for small portfolios."],"answer":2,"explanation":"As stocks are added, portfolio σ falls toward the market's σ_M and stops falling. The marginal stock cannot remove more risk by having a low ρ with the holdings, since the holdings already behave like the market.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-48"},{"id":"fin201-q13-708","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Why look for a positive α when adding to a well-diversified portfolio?","options":["It lowers the portfolio's standard deviation below σ_M.","It guarantees a profit on the stock over the holding period.","It raises the number of stocks, which always adds diversification.","It pays more than the SML requires for the systematic risk it adds."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Alpha is the gap between a stock's expected return and its SML return. Once diversification is exhausted, the way to improve the risk-reward trade-off is to add undervalued stocks. Alpha is not a risk reducer and is only an expectation, not a guarantee.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s57-59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-709","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"A well-diversified investor wants one more stock. T-bills yield 3.0% and the market risk premium is 6.0%. Candidate P: β 0.55, expected return 7.4%. Candidate Q: β 0.45, expected return 5.4%. Candidate R: β 1.20, expected return 10.6%. Which should she choose?","options":["Q: it has the lowest β.","P: low β and the largest positive alpha (+1.1%)","R: it has the highest expected return.","R: it has the largest alpha."],"answer":1,"explanation":"SML returns: P = 3 + 0.55 × 6 = 6.3% (alpha +1.1); Q = 3 + 0.45 × 6 = 5.7% (alpha −0.3); R = 3 + 1.2 × 6 = 10.2% (alpha +0.4). P has low β and the largest positive alpha. Q has the lowest β but negative alpha; R's alpha is smaller and its β is higher.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-710","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maya owns Calgary Oilfield Services (σ 30%). For her second stock she compares three candidates, each with σ = 30%: Edmonton Grocer (ρ with Calgary Oilfield 0.15), Alberta Bank (ρ 0.78) and Rocky Mountain Pipeline (ρ 0.85). Which should she pick for diversification?","options":["Edmonton Grocer","Alberta Bank","Rocky Mountain Pipeline","Any of them, because the σ's are equal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With equal σ's, the lowest correlation with her existing holding gives the biggest drop in portfolio σ. A high-ρ stock such as the pipeline moves with her oil-services holding and adds little diversification.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-711","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maya holds Stock A (σ = 30%) and will put half her money in a second stock with σ = 30%. Candidate Low has a correlation of 0.20 with A; candidate High has a correlation of 0.85. By how many percentage points is the two-stock portfolio's σ lower with Low than with High?","answer":5.62,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.85*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100-sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.2*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100","steps":[{"label":"σ_P with ρ = 0.20","value":23.24,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.2*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"σ_P with ρ = 0.85","value":28.85,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.85*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Difference (High − Low)","value":5.62,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.85*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100-sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.2*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["σ_P = √(w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2ρ w_A σ_A w_B σ_B)","ρ = 0.20: √(0.0225 + 0.0225 + 2 × 0.2 × 0.0225) = √0.054 = 23.24%","ρ = 0.85: √(0.0225 + 0.0225 + 2 × 0.85 × 0.0225) = √0.08325 = 28.85%; difference ≈ 5.6 points (5.62 from unrounded σ's, 5.61 from the rounded ones)"],"keystrokes":["ρ = 0.2: √0.054 = 0.2324","ρ = 0.85: √0.08325 = 0.2885","28.85 − 23.24 ≈ 5.6"],"errors":[{"id":"rho-one-shortcut","label":"Averaged the σ's and ignored ρ","value":0.0,"check":"30-30","explain":"Weighted-average σ (30% for both) only holds when ρ = 1. For ρ < 1 the portfolio σ is lower, and lower still when ρ is lower."},{"id":"sign-slip","label":"Subtracted in the wrong order","value":-5.62,"check":"sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.2*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100-sqrt(0.5**2*0.3**2+0.5**2*0.3**2+2*0.85*0.5*0.3*0.5*0.3)*100","explain":"The question asks how much lower the low-correlation portfolio's σ is, so take High minus Low."}],"explanation":"Choosing the stock with ρ = 0.20 instead of 0.85 cuts the two-stock portfolio's σ from 28.85% to 23.24%, about 5.6 points. This is why the second to fourth stocks should have low ρ with what you already hold.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59 / S7 s11"},{"id":"fin201-q13-712","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":["fin201-c13-portfolio-beta"],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Dev's portfolio of Canadian stocks is worth $240,000 and has a beta of 0.95. He adds $60,000 of new cash in a utility stock with a beta of 0.40. What is the new portfolio beta? Give beta to 2 decimals.","answer":0.84,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(240000*0.95+60000*0.4)/300000","steps":[{"label":"Weight on the old portfolio","value":0.8,"check":"240000/300000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"New portfolio beta Σ w β","value":0.84,"check":"(240000*0.95+60000*0.4)/300000","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Weights: 240,000/300,000 = 0.80 and 60,000/300,000 = 0.20","β_new = 0.80 × 0.95 + 0.20 × 0.40 = 0.76 + 0.08 = 0.84"],"keystrokes":["0.8 × 0.95 = 0.76","0.2 × 0.4 = 0.08","0.76 + 0.08 = 0.84"],"errors":[{"id":"simple-average-beta","label":"Averaged the two betas ignoring dollar weights","value":0.68,"check":"(0.95+0.4)/2","explain":"The old portfolio is four times the size of the new position, so it gets four times the weight."},{"id":"weights-not-one","label":"Added the betas","value":1.35,"check":"0.95+0.4","explain":"Betas are weighted by portfolio weights that sum to 1, not added."}],"explanation":"Adding a low-beta stock to a diversified portfolio lowers its systematic risk in proportion to its weight: 80% × 0.95 + 20% × 0.40 = 0.84.","source":"generated","ref":"S7 s19-20 / SM 13-13"},{"id":"fin201-q13-713","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":3,"stem":"Portfolio σ is 38% with one stock, 27% with two, 21% with five, 17.5% with 20 and 16.2% with 60 stocks, while the market's σ_M is 16%. What does this tell an investor about choosing the 61st stock?","options":["Keep choosing the lowest-σ stock, since σ is still falling quickly.","Keep choosing the lowest-ρ stock, since each one cuts σ sharply.","Little diversifiable risk is left, so the choice should be driven by β and α rather than σ or ρ.","Stop investing: further stocks will raise σ."],"answer":2,"explanation":"σ_P falls toward σ_M and then flattens: the portfolio is already as diversified as the market allows. What is left is systematic risk, so the guide is low β and positive α.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s46-48"},{"id":"fin201-q13-714","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"apply","difficulty":2,"stem":"For the very first stock in a portfolio, low beta is the best guide.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single stock leaves all its risk undiversified, so total risk (σ) is what the investor bears. Beta becomes the right guide only after the portfolio is already diversified.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-715","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":3,"stem":"Once a portfolio is well diversified, a stock with a negative alpha should still be added if it has the lowest σ available.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"σ no longer matters much because the portfolio's remaining risk is systematic. A negative alpha means the stock is overvalued, offering less than the SML pays for its β, so it hurts the risk-reward trade-off.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-716","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"order","difficulty":1,"stem":"Put the selection criteria in the order the guide uses them as a portfolio grows.","options":["Low β; then low σ; then low ρ","Low ρ; then low σ; then positive α","Positive α; then low β; then low σ","Low σ; then low ρ; then low β with positive α"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Start with a single low-σ stock, add a few low-ρ stocks to diversify, and once diversified (σ_P ≈ σ_M) pick low-β, positive-α stocks.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-717","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"formula","difficulty":1,"stem":"To add to an already well-diversified portfolio, look for stocks with low _____ and positive alpha.","options":["σ","β","ρ","dividend yield"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Once the portfolio holds the market's risk, a stock's contribution to systematic risk (β) is what counts, and positive α means it pays more than the SML requires for that β.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-718","concept":"fin201-c13-portfolio-building","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"A well-diversified investor looks at Fraser Valley Berries: σ = 45%, β = 0.4, α = +1.5%. What is the best verdict?","options":["A good addition: low β and positive α; most of its high σ is diversifiable.","Reject it: a σ of 45% is too risky.","Reject it: a β below 1 means it earns less than the risk-free rate.","Neutral: only ρ matters at this stage."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a diversified portfolio, σ is largely irrelevant; the stock adds little systematic risk (β = 0.4) and offers more than the SML pays (α > 0). A β below 1 still earns more than R_F as long as the market risk premium is positive.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s58-59"},{"id":"fin201-q13-751","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":1,"stem":"Dragon Pass Rail Terminals is evaluating a new project with a beta of 1.35. T-bills yield 3.2% per annum and the market risk premium is 5.8% per annum. Using CAPM, what return must the project earn to be acceptable (its required return)? Answer in % per annum.","answer":11.03,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"3.2+1.35*5.8","steps":[{"label":"Project risk premium β × MRP","value":7.83,"check":"1.35*5.8","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Required return R_F + β × MRP","value":11.03,"check":"3.2+1.35*5.8","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Required return = R_F + β_project × (market risk premium)","= 3.2 + 1.35 × 5.8 = 3.2 + 7.83 = 11.03% per annum","The project must earn at least 11.03% (its cost of capital) to beat what the market offers at the same beta"],"keystrokes":["1.35 × 5.8 = 7.83","+ 3.2 = 11.03"],"errors":[{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 5.8% as the market return","value":6.71,"check":"3.2+1.35*(5.8-3.2)","explain":"The market risk premium is already E[R_M] − R_F; subtracting R_F again understates the hurdle."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left out the risk-free rate","value":7.83,"check":"1.35*5.8","explain":"7.83% is only the reward for systematic risk. The hurdle also includes the time value of money."},{"id":"risk-free-only","label":"Used the risk-free rate as the hurdle","value":3.2,"check":"3.2","explain":"A risky project cannot be discounted at the T-bill rate: shareholders demand a premium for its beta."}],"explanation":"The SML gives the going rate for bearing risk. A project with β = 1.35 must offer at least 3.2 + 1.35 × 5.8 = 11.03%, otherwise shareholders do better investing in the market at the same systematic risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-752","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Keystone Logistics is considering a project that costs $480,000 and returns $128,000 at the end of each of the next 5 years. The company's overall beta is 0.90, but this project's beta is 1.40. T-bills yield 3.4% and the market risk premium is 5.5% per annum. What is the project's NPV at its CAPM required return? Answer in $.","answer":-8112.82,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":5},"check":"128000*(1-(1+0.111)**(-5))/0.111-480000","steps":[{"label":"Project required return R_F + β × MRP","value":11.1,"check":"3.4+1.4*5.5","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Annuity factor, 5 years","value":3.6866,"check":"(1-(1+0.111)**(-5))/0.111","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.005}},{"label":"PV of inflows","value":471887.18,"check":"128000*(1-(1+0.111)**(-5))/0.111","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":5}},{"label":"NPV = PV of inflows − cost","value":-8112.82,"check":"128000*(1-(1+0.111)**(-5))/0.111-480000","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":5}}],"solution":["Required return = 3.4 + 1.40 × 5.5 = 3.4 + 7.7 = 11.10% (use the project's beta, not the firm's)","Annuity factor = (1 − 1.111⁻⁵)/0.111 = 3.6866; PV of inflows = 128,000 × 3.6866 = $471,887.18","NPV = 471,887.18 − 480,000 = −$8,112.82: reject (the project's IRR of about 10.4% is below 11.10%)"],"keystrokes":["CF0 = −480000, C01 = 128000, F01 = 5, NPV I = 11.1, CPT → −8,112.82","or N = 5, I/Y = 11.1, PMT = 128000, CPT PV = 471,887.18"],"errors":[{"id":"firm-wide-beta","label":"Discounted at the firm's overall beta (0.90)","value":26386.95,"check":"128000*(1-(1+0.0835)**(-5))/0.0835-480000","explain":"Using β = 0.90 gives an 8.35% rate and a positive NPV, so the project is wrongly accepted. The discount rate must reflect the project's own, higher beta."},{"id":"mrp-as-discount-rate","label":"Discounted at the market risk premium (5.5%)","value":66596.41,"check":"128000*(1-(1+0.055)**(-5))/0.055-480000","explain":"5.5% is only the premium; the required return adds R_F and scales the premium by β."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 5.5% as the market return","value":54231.81,"check":"128000*(1-(1+0.0634)**(-5))/0.0634-480000","explain":"That gives a rate of 3.4 + 1.40 × (5.5 − 3.4) = 6.34%, far too low."}],"explanation":"The discount rate comes from the SML at the project's own beta: 11.10%. At that rate the PV of the inflows ($471,887) is below the $480,000 cost, so NPV ≈ −$8,113. Using the firm's lower beta (8.35%) would give +$26,387 and accept a project that destroys value.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8 / L05A"},{"id":"fin201-q13-753","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"multistep","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"Sundre Utilities will pay a dividend of $1.85 per share next year, and dividends are expected to grow 4% per year forever. The stock's beta is 0.90, T-bills yield 3.1% and the market risk premium is 6.2% per annum. Using CAPM for the required return, what is the share price? Answer in $.","answer":39.53,"unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05},"check":"1.85/((3.1+0.9*6.2)/100-0.04)","steps":[{"label":"Required return R = R_F + β × MRP","value":8.68,"check":"3.1+0.9*6.2","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Price P0 = D1/(R − g)","value":39.53,"check":"1.85/((3.1+0.9*6.2)/100-0.04)","unit":"$","tolerance":{"abs":0.05}}],"solution":["R = R_F + β × MRP = 3.1 + 0.90 × 6.2 = 3.1 + 5.58 = 8.68%","P0 = D1/(R − g) = 1.85/(0.0868 − 0.04) = 1.85/0.0468 = $39.53"],"keystrokes":["0.9 × 6.2 + 3.1 = 8.68","8.68 ÷ 100 − 0.04 = 0.0468","1.85 ÷ 0.0468 = 39.53"],"errors":[{"id":"r-plus-g","label":"Added g to R in the denominator","value":14.59,"check":"1.85/((3.1+0.9*6.2)/100+0.04)","explain":"Gordon growth divides by R − g, not R + g."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.2% as the market return","value":97.88,"check":"1.85/((3.1+0.9*(6.2-3.1))/100-0.04)","explain":"6.2% is already the premium, so the required return is 8.68%, not 5.89%."},{"id":"dropped-time-value","label":"Left R_F out of the required return","value":117.09,"check":"1.85/((0.9*6.2)/100-0.04)","explain":"The required return on equity includes the risk-free rate."}],"explanation":"CAPM supplies the required return that investors use to discount a stock's dividends: the cost of equity is 8.68%. The Gordon growth model then values the stock at $1.85/(0.0868 − 0.04) = $39.53.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8 / L04 s21"},{"id":"fin201-q13-754","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Maple Ridge Foods has a packaged-foods division (beta 0.70) and a new e-commerce venture (beta 1.80). T-bills yield 3.0% and the market risk premium is 6.0% per annum. What hurdle rate should the e-commerce venture's projects face? Answer in % per annum.","answer":13.8,"unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"3+1.8*6","steps":[{"label":"Venture risk premium β × MRP","value":10.8,"check":"1.8*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Hurdle rate R_F + β × MRP","value":13.8,"check":"3+1.8*6","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Use the beta of the project's own risk class: e-commerce, β = 1.80","Hurdle = 3.0 + 1.80 × 6.0 = 3.0 + 10.8 = 13.8% per annum","(Packaged foods would face 3.0 + 0.70 × 6.0 = 7.2%.)"],"keystrokes":["1.8 × 6 = 10.8","+ 3 = 13.8"],"errors":[{"id":"firm-wide-beta","label":"Used the market's own rate (β = 1.00) as a one-size-fits-all hurdle","value":9.0,"check":"3+1.0*6","explain":"A single 9.0% hurdle (the market's return) is too low for the risky venture and too high for the safe division."},{"id":"wrong-division-beta","label":"Used the packaged-foods beta","value":7.2,"check":"3+0.7*6","explain":"The venture's projects carry e-commerce risk, so use its beta, not the other division's."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Treated 6.0% as the market return","value":8.4,"check":"3+1.8*(6-3)","explain":"6.0% is already the premium."}],"explanation":"Required return depends on the risk of the project, not on the company that owns it. The e-commerce venture has β = 1.80, so its hurdle is 13.8%. Applying a blended 9.0% would accept risky projects that earn less than the market offers for that beta.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-755","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"numeric","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project is expected to earn an IRR of 10.1% per annum with conventional cash flows. T-bills yield 3.0% and the market risk premium is 6.5% per annum. Up to what project beta is it acceptable (IRR at least the SML return)? Give beta to 2 decimals.","answer":1.09,"unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01},"check":"(10.1-3)/6.5","steps":[{"label":"Excess over the risk-free rate","value":7.1,"check":"10.1-3","unit":"%","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}},{"label":"Break-even beta = excess ÷ MRP","value":1.09,"check":"(10.1-3)/6.5","unit":"","tolerance":{"abs":0.01}}],"solution":["Acceptable if IRR ≥ R_F + β × MRP, so β ≤ (IRR − R_F)/MRP","= (10.1 − 3.0)/6.5 = 7.1/6.5 = 1.092","Accept if the project's beta is below about 1.09; reject if above"],"keystrokes":["10.1 − 3 = 7.1","÷ 6.5 = 1.09"],"errors":[{"id":"forgot-minus-rf","label":"Divided the whole IRR by the premium","value":1.55,"check":"10.1/6.5","explain":"Subtract R_F first: the hurdle is R_F + β × MRP."},{"id":"mrp-as-market","label":"Subtracted R_F from the premium","value":2.03,"check":"(10.1-3)/(6.5-3)","explain":"6.5% is already the market risk premium."},{"id":"inverted-ratio","label":"Inverted the ratio","value":0.92,"check":"6.5/(10.1-3)","explain":"The break-even beta is the project's excess return divided by the market's."}],"explanation":"Setting IRR equal to the SML return gives the break-even beta, 1.09. Any project with this IRR and a lower beta plots above the SML and has a positive NPV; with a higher beta it plots below and should be rejected.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-756","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"A new project has a beta of 1.15. T-bills yield 2.9% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.1% per annum. What is the project's CAPM required return?","options":["6.58%","7.02%","9.92%","9.00%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Required return = 2.9 + 1.15 × 6.1 = 2.9 + 7.015 = 9.92%. 6.58% = 2.9 + 1.15 × (6.1 − 2.9) subtracts R_F from a premium that already excludes it. 7.02% = 1.15 × 6.1 drops the risk-free rate. 9.00% = 2.9 + 6.1 forgets to scale the premium by beta.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-757","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":3,"stem":"T-bills yield 3% and the market risk premium is 6%. Project A has beta 0.5 and IRR 8.5%; Project B has beta 1.2 and IRR 11.0%; Project C has beta 1.8 and IRR 13.0%. All have conventional cash flows. Which projects should be accepted?","options":["A and B only","B and C only","C only","A, B and C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hurdles: A = 3 + 0.5 × 6 = 6.0% (8.5 beats it); B = 3 + 1.2 × 6 = 10.2% (11.0 beats it); C = 3 + 1.8 × 6 = 13.8% (13.0 falls short). 'B and C only' is what a single firm-wide hurdle at the market's 9% (β = 1) would give; 'C only' picks the highest IRR; 'A, B and C' compares every IRR with R_F alone.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-758","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"In the Chapter 13 preview of the cost of capital, the appropriate discount rate for a new project is","options":["the interest rate the firm pays on its bank loan","the risk-free rate, because shareholders can always buy T-bills","the firm's average accounting return on equity","the expected return that financial markets offer on investments with the same systematic risk"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Shareholders can always invest for themselves in the market, so a project must offer at least what the market offers for the same beta: the SML return. That minimum required return is the project's cost of capital.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-759","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":1,"stem":"The return R_F + β (E[R_M] − R_F) is called what from an investor's perspective, and what from the firm's financing perspective?","options":["Cost of capital; required rate of return","Required rate of return; cost of capital","Historical return; dividend yield","Internal rate of return; payback period"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same number looks different from each side of the market: investors require it as compensation for risk, and the firm must pay (earn) it on the capital it raises (invests). All investments of similar risk should offer a similar return.","source":"generated","ref":"L06 s45"},{"id":"fin201-q13-760","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"identify","difficulty":2,"stem":"Comparing a project's IRR with the SML return for its beta is effectively which Chapter 9 decision rule?","options":["The payback rule with a cutoff chosen by management","The accounting rate of return rule","The IRR rule, with the SML return for the project's beta as the hurdle rate","The profitability index rule with R_F as the discount rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Accepting a project when its expected return beats what the market offers for the same risk is the IRR criterion, now with a principled hurdle rate from the SML. Payback, ARR and a risk-free PI do not use the market's price of risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-761","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"Cascade Energy's overall beta is 1.0, but a proposed LNG terminal is much riskier than its average business. Which discount rate is appropriate for the terminal?","options":["The SML return for the terminal's own beta, which is above the firm's average rate","The SML return for the firm's overall beta of 1.0","The risk-free rate","The firm's average return on past projects"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Required return depends on the risk of the project, not on who owns it. A higher-beta project must meet a higher hurdle. Using the firm-wide rate understates the hurdle for risky projects and overstates it for safe ones.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-762","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"not","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which of the following does NOT affect a project's CAPM required return?","options":["The risk-free rate","The market risk premium","The project's beta","The project's unique, diversifiable risk"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The required return is R_F + β (E[R_M] − R_F). Diversifiable risk is not rewarded, so it does not enter the hurdle rate.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.7-13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-763","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"why","difficulty":2,"stem":"Why must a new project beat the SML return for its beta to benefit shareholders?","options":["The firm must first cover the interest on its bank loans before shareholders are paid.","Shareholders can earn that return in the market at the same beta, so the project must offer more.","Accounting profit must exceed the risk-free rate for the project to add any value.","The SML return is guaranteed by the market, so it is the least any project can earn."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The SML return is the opportunity cost of the firm's capital: what investors forgo by leaving money in the project instead of the market. Only a project that earns more than that has a positive NPV.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-764","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A project with conventional cash flows has beta 1.35 and an IRR of 12.4%. The SML return for beta 1.35 is 11.03%. What does this tell you?","options":["Reject: an IRR well above the required return signals too much risk.","Accept only if the IRR exceeds the risk-free rate of 3.2%.","Accept: it plots above the SML, so its NPV is positive.","No decision is possible without the payback period."],"answer":2,"explanation":"A project plotting above the SML offers more than financial markets pay for the same beta, so discounting at 11.03% gives NPV > 0. The risk is already in the hurdle rate; comparing IRR with R_F alone would accept almost anything.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8a"},{"id":"fin201-q13-765","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"interpret","difficulty":2,"stem":"A division proposes a project with conventional cash flows and an IRR of 8.6%. The SML return for the project's beta is 9.3%. What is the right decision, and why?","options":["Reject: it plots below the SML, so its NPV is negative.","Accept: 8.6% is well above the risk-free rate.","Accept: any positive IRR adds value.","Reject only if the payback period is long."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The project earns 0.7 points less than the market offers for its risk. Discounting at 9.3% gives NPV < 0, so it would reduce shareholder wealth.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8a"},{"id":"fin201-q13-766","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"apply","difficulty":3,"stem":"Maple Ridge Foods has a stable packaged-foods division (beta 0.7) and a new e-commerce unit (beta 1.8) but uses one firm-wide hurdle rate based on a beta of 1.0. What is the likely result?","options":["It will tend to accept good low-beta projects and reject bad high-beta ones.","It will accept and reject the same projects as using each division's own beta.","It will reject every project, because the hurdle is too high for both divisions.","It will tend to reject good low-beta projects and accept bad high-beta ones."],"answer":3,"explanation":"Against a single hurdle, safe projects (whose true hurdle is lower) look worse than they are, and risky projects (whose true hurdle is higher) look better than they are. Over time the firm drifts toward risk.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-767","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"formula","difficulty":2,"stem":"Which expression gives the required return for a project?","options":["R_F + β_firm × (E[R_M] − R_F)","R_F + β_project × (E[R_M] − R_F)","E[R_M], for every project","R_F + σ_project × (E[R_M] − R_F)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The project's own systematic risk goes into the formula. The firm's beta is right only for projects with the firm's average risk, E[R_M] is right only when β = 1, and σ is not the risk measure the market rewards.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-768","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"fill","angle":"define","difficulty":1,"stem":"The minimum required return on a new investment is called its _____.","options":["internal rate of return","profitability index","cost of capital","payback period"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cost of capital is the return the firm must earn on a project just to break even; it is the opportunity cost of the capital invested, and CAPM tells us how to estimate it from the project's beta.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-769","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"mc","angle":"calc","difficulty":2,"stem":"Lakeland Shipping is valuing a low-risk project with a beta of 0.80. T-bills yield 3.4% per annum and the market risk premium is 6.2% per annum. What discount rate should it use?","options":["8.36%","5.64%","4.96%","9.60%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Required return = 3.4 + 0.80 × 6.2 = 3.4 + 4.96 = 8.36%. 5.64% = 3.4 + 0.80 × (6.2 − 3.4) subtracts R_F from a premium that already excludes it. 4.96% = 0.80 × 6.2 drops the risk-free rate. 9.60% = 3.4 + 6.2 forgets to scale the premium by beta.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"},{"id":"fin201-q13-770","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"why","difficulty":1,"stem":"A project with a positive NPV would plot above the security market line.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Positive NPV means the project earns more than the market offers for its risk, so its expected return exceeds the SML return for its beta and it plots above the line.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8a"},{"id":"fin201-q13-771","concept":"fin201-c13-cost-of-capital-link","also":[],"type":"tf","angle":"contrast","difficulty":2,"stem":"A risky project should be discounted at the risk-free rate because T-bills are the cheapest source of money.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The discount rate reflects the project's risk, not the cheapest funding available. A risky project must be discounted at R_F + β × MRP; the risk-free rate would accept almost every project.","source":"generated","ref":"TB 13.8"}],"color":"#2F8F8A","short":"CAPM","tag":"β, the SML and the market line","province":"Syria","numeral":"XIII"}],"stats":{"questions":1718,"concepts":104,"activities":33,"numeric":378},"version":"09cfb97f24"};
